/* Psychiatry for Residents: practical layer (objectives, responses, management, facts). Original content. */
window.TPR_X={"c-0-1-1":{"obj":["Cleared the onlookers and agreed one lead voice before speaking to him","Named his frustration out loud and offered at least two real choices (quiet space, oral PRN, a phone call)","Screened for a physical driver: intoxication, withdrawal, delirium, akathisia, low glucose","Checked every PRN dose in the last 24 hours against the BNF ceiling before offering more","Agreed and recorded what the team will do if talking fails"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Get away from me! You lot locked me up for nothing.\"","r":"I can see you're furious, and I'm staying back. I'm not here to argue about the section. Tell me what happened tonight and what would help right now."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I run a business. I need to leave now, I've got deals to close.\"","r":"Sounds like a lot is riding on things outside. I can't agree to you leaving tonight, but I can get you your phone to call someone, and make sure your consultant hears about it tomorrow."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"They're all staring at me. They're going to jump me.\"","r":"That sounds frightening. Nobody is going to rush you. I've asked everyone else to move away. Let's stand over here where there's more room."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll calm down if you give me my leave back.\"","r":"I hear how much leave matters to you. Only your consultant can change it, not me, but I'll write down that you asked and that you settled. That will count tomorrow."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking your tablets. They slow me down.\"","r":"Fair enough, you get a say. Your choice: a tablet now to take the edge off, or quiet time in your room with a nurse close by. Which feels better?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I haven't slept for days. My head's buzzing.\"","r":"That sounds exhausting, and no sleep makes everything louder. Let's get you somewhere quiet, and I'll look at whether something to help you sleep tonight is right."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Nurse: \"Just write him up for IM, Doctor. He's been like this all shift.\"","r":"You've had a hard shift, I can see that. He's stopped throwing and he's talking, so let's give oral a proper try first. I'll write the IM plan now so we're ready if he escalates."}],"bps":{"bio":["Oral lorazepam 1–2 mg PRN for agitation; add up all doses in the past 24 h first (BNF daily ceiling usually 4 mg)","If his antipsychotic is not yet optimised, RC to consider olanzapine 10 mg at night, titrating to 15–20 mg; baseline ECG, weight, HbA1c, lipids","NEWS2 after any PRN; capillary glucose; urine drug screen only if he agrees"],"psych":["Named nurse debriefs him within 72 h: what set it off, what helped","Triggers and calming strategies written into his positive behaviour support plan","Low-stimulus sleep plan tonight; no caffeine after 16:00"],"social":["Nicotine replacement offered by the ward team if he smokes","Named nurse supports one call to family or work to ease worry about outside commitments","RC reviews Section 17 leave at the next ward round"],"safety":["Nurse in charge sets observation level (e.g. intermittent, every 15 min) with a review time at handover","Response team on standby out of sight; IM plan written per trust RT policy","Incident report, and use-of-force record if any hands-on contact","Night coordinator and day team told at handover"]},"facts":[{"h":"Two arm-lengths, side-on, exit behind","x":"Stand angled, hands open and below the waist, out of kicking range. If he tells you to back off, do it. Never let him stand between you and the only door."},{"h":"One voice, everyone else quiet","x":"Several staff talking at once raises arousal. Agree the lead before you go in; the rest move bystanders and keep out of his eyeline."},{"h":"PRN totals cover the whole day","x":"Count oral and IM doses from the last 24 hours before offering more. Going above the BNF ceiling needs a senior decision and closer physical observations."},{"h":"Agitation can be a medical problem","x":"Check glucose, temperature, pupils and gait. Delirium, alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal, stimulants and akathisia all look like 'kicking off' but need different treatment."},{"h":"Spell out choices before acting","x":"Patients often describe force they did not see coming as an assault. Saying plainly what happens next, and what the options are, often makes holding unnecessary."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Lead verbal de-escalation with nursing staff, clear exits and roles, and agree a fallback plan if talking fails."},"c-0-1-2":{"obj":["Confirmed legal authority for IM treatment (status, T2/T3 wording, or s62) before prescribing","Chosen a drug that fits her ECG status and 24-hour totals, and written down why","Set NEWS2 frequency, a breathing-rate trigger and who to call","Prescribed flumazenil and procyclidine at the same time as the IM dose","Arranged a debrief for her and support for the injured nurse"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Don't you dare stick a needle in me!\"","r":"I hear you, and I'd much rather you didn't need one. You can still take a tablet now instead. If not, the injection is only to calm you, and a nurse will stay right beside you."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"You're poisoning me. You want me dead.\"","r":"That sounds terrifying. It's lorazepam, a calming medicine. I'll tell you exactly what's happening before anything is done, and someone will check your breathing all night."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Just leave me alone. I want it to stop.\"","r":"Something is really hurting you right now. We won't leave you on your own. Can you tell me what the voices or thoughts are saying? Let's help it stop."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Nurse: \"Give her haloperidol, it always works better for her.\"","r":"I know it's worked before. There's no ECG on file, and haloperidol can upset the heart rhythm, so lorazepam is safer tonight. Once she's settled, let's get an ECG so it's an option next time."},{"tag":"Question","p":"Next morning: \"Why did you hold me down? I would have taken it.\"","r":"I'm sorry it felt like that. You were hurting your head and had said no to the tablet twice. Let's agree now what you'd want us to try first, and put it in your plan."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"Injured nurse: \"I should have seen it coming.\"","r":"That's a really normal reaction, and it isn't your fault. You've been hurt. Please get checked and let occupational health know, and we'll talk properly at the debrief."}],"bps":{"bio":["IM lorazepam 1–2 mg (no ECG on file); may repeat after 30–60 min if partial response, within 24-h total including oral doses","Once ECG is normal: IM haloperidol 5 mg with IM promethazine 25–50 mg becomes an option; procyclidine 5–10 mg IM PRN for dystonia","NEWS2 every 15 min for at least 1 h, then hourly until alert and walking; ECG, U&E, CK, FBC when safe","Flumazenil on PRN chart: give if breathing rate falls below 10"],"psych":["Named nurse debriefs her within 72 h: what she noticed, what helped","Her preferences for future emergencies recorded as an advance statement","RC reviews regular antipsychotic and the voices or distress that drove the head-banging"],"social":["Family told with her agreement","IMHA offered"],"safety":["Neuro obs after head-banging; A&E if drowsier than the drugs explain or new focal signs","Injured nurse: occupational health, incident report; police only if the nurse chooses","On-call consultant told if BNF maximum reached or a second IM needed","Use of force recorded; RT reviewed at the next ward round"]},"facts":[{"h":"No ECG, no haloperidol","x":"NICE NG10 asks for an ECG before IM haloperidol. Without one, IM lorazepam alone is the safer choice."},{"h":"Flumazenil wears off first","x":"200 micrograms IV over 15 seconds, then 100 micrograms every 60 seconds, max 1 mg. Its effect fades within about an hour, so she can resedate: keep watching."},{"h":"Asleep means sedated","x":"Obs at least hourly; every 15 minutes if she was held, had high doses or used drugs. If she refuses, record breathing, colour and responsiveness by sight."},{"h":"Totals include oral PRN","x":"Add tablets given earlier in the day to IM doses when checking BNF limits. Forgotten day-shift doses are the commonest totalling error."},{"h":"Procyclidine for acute dystonia","x":"Procyclidine 5–10 mg IM works within minutes for neck spasm or oculogyric crisis. Prescribe it PRN with any IM antipsychotic."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Choose a lawful rapid tranquillisation drug and route, oral first, and set up post-dose physical monitoring."},"c-0-1-3":{"obj":["Found out how long he was held, in what position, and whether weight was on his back or chest","Recorded a full NEWS2, temperature and a head-to-toe injury check","Sent CK, U&E, glucose and done an ECG, with repeat CK booked at 24 h","Agreed explicit transfer triggers with the nurse in charge","Explained to him what was done and why, and offered a debrief"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Get off me. Nobody's touching me after what they did.\"","r":"That makes sense after being held down. I won't touch you without asking. Could I watch your breathing and count your pulse from here first, then we'll decide the next step together?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I couldn't breathe. They were on my back.\"","r":"That sounds really frightening, and I'm taking it seriously. I'll check your chest and breathing now, and what you've told me will be written down and looked into."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"It wasn't just weed. Someone gave me a pill as well.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me, it really helps. Do you know what it looked like or what it was called? Some pills push up heart rate and temperature, so I'll test for that now."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm fine. Leave me alone to sleep.\"","r":"I'm glad you feel OK. Your temperature and heart rate are high after the hold, so I need a few quick checks. Ten minutes, then you can rest."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Am I in trouble? Are the police coming?\"","r":"Right now my job is making sure you're physically OK. What happened will be talked through with you and the team. If police get involved, we'll tell you and you'll have support."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Nurse: \"He's just tired from fighting. He's settled now.\"","r":"He might be, but sweating, fast breathing and 38.2 after a hold are warning signs. Let's do obs every 15 minutes and bloods now so we catch anything early."}],"bps":{"bio":["NEWS2 every 15 min for at least 1 h, then hourly if stable","CK, U&E, creatinine, FBC, glucose, urine dip; ECG now; repeat CK at 24 h","Oral fluids encouraged and charted; paracetamol 1 g if febrile (max 4 g/24 h)","Avoid further IM antipsychotic while hot and CK unknown"],"psych":["Debrief within 72 h with named nurse: his account of the hold","Triggers (cannabis, conflict with the other patient) added to his care plan","Dual diagnosis worker offered for cannabis"],"social":["Nearest relative told with his consent","Ward manager makes sure the assaulted patient gets separate support and a safeguarding review"],"safety":["999 to A&E if temperature rises, he becomes drowsy, NEWS2 reaches 7, chest pain, or dark urine","Use of force recorded with duration and position","On-call consultant and RC informed","Post-incident review arranged by ward manager"]},"facts":[{"h":"Get him off his front","x":"Avoid prone holds; if one happens, move to side or seated as soon as possible. No weight on chest, back, neck or abdomen."},{"h":"Danger signs after restraint","x":"Hot, sweating, breathing fast, confused or suddenly quiet: think acute behavioural disturbance, rhabdomyolysis or positional asphyxia. Treat as a medical emergency, not as 'calming down'."},{"h":"CK peaks a day or two later","x":"Muscle breakdown peaks around 24–72 hours, so one early normal CK is not reassuring. Repeat at 24 h and check urine for blood without red cells."},{"h":"Fever plus antipsychotic plus stiffness","x":"Check for rigidity and confusion. Hyperthermia with high CK may be NMS or serotonin toxicity: stop the antipsychotic and involve the medical team."},{"h":"Every use of force is recorded","x":"The Mental Health Units (Use of Force) Act 2018 requires a record of each use: who, technique, duration, position and any injury."}],"type":"Physical examination + ECG and investigations","do":"Examine him after restraint for positional asphyxia signs, then order CK and ECG and arrange review."},"c-0-1-4":{"obj":["Seen him face to face within the hour and decided, with reasons, whether seclusion continues","Recorded fluid intake, urine output and signs of lithium toxicity","Sent lithium level, U&E and creatinine, and agreed which results mean transfer","Told him the specific behaviour that will end seclusion, and set the next review time","Informed the RC or on-call consultant"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Let me out of this box now!\"","r":"I hear you, it's awful in here. I want you out as soon as it's safe. If you can sit and chat with me quietly for a few minutes, that's the first step. What's going on for you?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not drinking anything you lot give me.\"","r":"OK, your choice. Would a sealed bottle you open yourself be better? You're very dry and on lithium, so water really matters for your kidneys tonight."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How long am I going to be stuck in here?\"","r":"It's checked regularly, and it ends as soon as you're calm enough to be safe on the ward. Keep talking with the nurses like this and it can end well before morning."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"My hands won't stop shaking. What have you given me?\"","r":"That shake may be your lithium, especially when you're dry. You haven't been given anything new. I'd like to check your blood level now so we know you're safe. Can I take a sample?"},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll stay calm if you take me off the lithium.\"","r":"I can hear you've had enough of it. I'm holding tonight's dose until we know your level. Your consultant can talk through the longer-term options with you tomorrow."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Nurse: \"Can't you just sign it off? He's still threatening people.\"","r":"I need to see him myself; the review only counts if I do. I'll come in with three of you, look at him from the doorway first, and we'll decide together."}],"bps":{"bio":["Hold lithium tonight; lithium level, U&E and creatinine now (note hours since last dose; standard level is 12 h post-dose)","Offer about 200 ml every 30 min in sealed containers; fluid balance chart","NEWS2 at each nursing review, or by sight if refused","Lithium above 1.5 mmol/L, vomiting, unsteadiness or confusion: end seclusion and transfer to A&E"],"psych":["Short contact at every review: what he needs to be able to come out","Exit criteria given in plain words (e.g. sitting, talking calmly, no threats for 30 min)","Morning MDT reviews antimanic treatment and lithium dose"],"social":["Family told with his consent","IMHA informed if he wishes"],"safety":["Continuous observation by a trained nurse outside the room","Medical reviews 4-hourly until the first MDT review; independent MDT review if over 8 h","RC or on-call consultant told tonight","Room checked: toilet access, mattress, blanket, temperature"]},"facts":[{"h":"Review clock: 1 hour, then every 4","x":"Medical review within an hour of starting, then every 4 hours until the first MDT review. Two staff, one a registered nurse, review every 2 hours."},{"h":"Independent review after 8 hours","x":"Over 8 hours continuously, or 12 hours across 48, triggers an independent MDT review with senior staff not involved in his care."},{"h":"Dry plus lithium means danger","x":"Dehydration pushes lithium up fast. Toxicity: coarse tremor, vomiting, diarrhoea, unsteadiness, slurred speech, confusion. Usual target 0.6–0.8 mmol/L; above 1.5 is toxic."},{"h":"Last in, first out","x":"Enter the seclusion room after staff and leave before them, so you never block an exit or get trapped. Leave the discussion of why he was secluded until last."},{"h":"Not a holding pen till morning","x":"End seclusion once he is no longer an immediate danger to others, or if he needs medical care. Waiting for the day team is not a reason."}],"type":"Mental Health Act process","do":"Complete the first medical seclusion review within the hour, recording ongoing need, physical safety and plan."},"c-0-1-5":{"obj":["Checked airway, breathing, consciousness and neck within the first minute and called 999","Given A&E a phone handover with legal status, ligature details and current risk","Agreed an observation level and when it will next be reviewed","Made sure the staff who found her were supported and stood down","Rebuilt her safety plan with her, in her own words, on return"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm fine. I don't need hospital, it was nothing.\"","r":"I'm really glad you're talking to me. Neck injuries can swell hours later even when you feel OK, so I need you checked properly. A nurse will be with you the whole time."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I've caused everyone so much trouble. I'm so sorry.\"","r":"You don't need to say sorry. You were in a lot of pain, and we want to help with that. Right now the only thing that matters is that you're safe."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I took some tablets earlier as well. I'd been saving them.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me, that's really important. Do you know which tablets, roughly how many, and when? The hospital needs that to treat you properly."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Why is someone watching me all the time? I can't even go to the toilet!\"","r":"I understand it feels intrusive. It's because we want you alive and safe. Let's talk about how to make it less invasive, and it'll be reviewed at a set time every day."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Are you going to tell my mum?\"","r":"I won't share details without asking you first. I'd like her to know you're in hospital so she can support you. What would you be OK with her knowing?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"Nurse: \"I checked her ten minutes before. I should have stopped this.\"","r":"You found her, and you got that ligature off. That's what counts. This isn't about blame. Take a break now, and we'll make sure you get a proper debrief."}],"bps":{"bio":["ABC, oxygen, SpO2, GCS; protect the neck if she was suspended or fell","Paracetamol level and overdose screen if any tablets possible","RC reviews antidepressant: under-25s have higher risk of suicidal thoughts early on; review within 1 week of any start or increase, limit quantities"],"psych":["Collaborative safety plan with named nurse: warning signs, coping steps, people, crisis numbers","Ward psychologist offers a formulation; DBT-informed skills if self-harm keeps recurring","Daily one-to-one time with named nurse while on enhanced observation"],"social":["Family involved with her consent; carer support offered","Ward social worker checks home, study and money pressures"],"safety":["Within arm's length observation on return; MDT reviews every 24 h","Room and belongings checked with her for cords and stored tablets","Incident report; ligature and room kept as found","Staff debrief within 72 h; ward manager leads the incident review"]},"facts":[{"h":"Any suspension or blackout goes to A&E","x":"A ligature with possible loss of consciousness can cause delayed airway swelling, carotid injury or hypoxic brain injury. Send her even if she looks well."},{"h":"No risk scores to decide care","x":"NICE NG225: risk tools and scales should not be used to predict suicide or to decide who gets treatment. Base care on her needs and a full assessment."},{"h":"Know where the ligature cutters are","x":"Every ward keeps them in a fixed place. Find them on your first shift; seconds matter."},{"h":"Section 5(2) stays on the ward","x":"It applies only to inpatients. Once she is in A&E and wants to leave, use liaison psychiatry, the MCA or an MHA assessment instead."},{"h":"Observation means engagement","x":"Close observation works when staff talk with her, not just watch. Set a daily review and step down once a clear plan is in place."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Lead the first five minutes after self-harm, decide on A&E transfer, and set observation levels afterwards."},"c-0-1-6":{"obj":["Graded his risk using the relapse history and the named neighbour","Reported him to police with a reference number, photo and description","Informed the on-call consultant, nearest relative and MHA office","Stated how long Section 18 allows him to be retaken","Planned a return review covering physical health, missed medicines and leave"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"Sister: \"How did you lose him? You were meant to be looking after him!\"","r":"I understand why you're angry, and I'm sorry you're worried. He had an hour out, which is part of getting better. Right now we're all focused on finding him safely."},{"tag":"Question","p":"Sister: \"Is he going to be arrested?\"","r":"No, he's not in trouble. The police help us bring people back to hospital safely. When he's back, we'll check he's well and look again at his leave."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"Sister: \"He rang me. He said the neighbour's been spying on him again.\"","r":"Thank you, that's really important. Do you know where he was, and did he say what he might do? I'll pass this to the police straight away."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"Sister: \"I can't cope if something happens to him.\"","r":"That's a frightening thought, and you've done the right thing calling. Here's the ward number to ring any time tonight, and we'll phone you as soon as we hear."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"Patient on the phone: \"I'm fine, I'm at the pub. I'll come back tomorrow.\"","r":"I'm glad you called and that you're OK. You're still on a section, so we need you back tonight. Can we send someone or a taxi? What's the address?"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Police: \"Is he really high risk? We're flat out tonight.\"","r":"I understand you're stretched. He's detained, his last relapse involved threats to a neighbour, and he's missed his evening medicine. That's why we graded him high. The neighbour's address is on the form."}],"bps":{"bio":["On return: NEWS2, injury check, breath alcohol, urine drug screen if he agrees","Give missed doses as charted; if clozapine missed over 48 h, do not restart at the old dose: retitrate from 12.5 mg","Check antipsychotic adherence; plasma level if available"],"psych":["RC and named nurse explore why he left: boredom, beliefs about the neighbour, substances","Early warning signs and relapse signature added to care plan"],"social":["Sister updated; carer's assessment offered","Care coordinator informed next morning"],"safety":["Police missing-person report with risk grade and reference; neighbour named to police","Section 18: police, AMHP or ward staff can retake him","Leave on hold pending RC review, not as punishment","AWOL form, incident report, MHA office notified"]},"facts":[{"h":"Section 18 time limits","x":"On Section 3 he can be retaken within 6 months of going AWOL or before the section would end, whichever is later. On Section 2, only within its 28 days."},{"h":"Away over 28 days: report needed","x":"If retaken after more than 28 days absent, the RC must reassess and report to the managers within a week, or the detention lapses."},{"h":"Police need a clear picture","x":"Give status, risk to self and others, named person at risk, missed medicines, likely places and a photo. Vague calls get low priority."},{"h":"Locked door: think warrant","x":"If he is home and won't come out, an AMHP or authorised officer can apply to a magistrate for a Section 135(2) warrant to enter and retake him."},{"h":"Sharing with family","x":"You can always listen. Share only what helps find him and keep people safe; that is justified when risk is high."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Grade the AWOL risk with the nurse in charge, start Section 18 actions, and inform police and family."},"c-0-2-1":{"obj":["Heard why she wants to leave and offered something to make staying more bearable","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts, plan, means, timing and what has held her back","Made and recorded a decision with reasons: discharge with a safety plan, or Section 5(2)","Handed Form H1 to the managers' delegate and written the 72-hour expiry time","Told the on-call consultant and asked for an MHA assessment"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You said I could leave whenever I wanted. That was a lie.\"","r":"You're right that informal usually means you can leave, and I understand why this feels like a betrayal. I'm worried about your safety tonight, and I'd rather be honest about that."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not talking to anyone. Just open the door.\"","r":"I won't keep you without a proper reason. Give me ten minutes to understand what's happened today. If I can't see a safe way for you to go, I'll tell you exactly why."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"If I go home, I'm going to finish it. The tablets are in the drawer.\"","r":"Thank you for being honest with me. It tells me how much pain you're in. Because of what you've said, I can't let you leave tonight, and I'll explain what happens next."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just one night at home. I'll come back tomorrow, I promise.\"","r":"I can hear how much you need a break from here. A night alone with tablets in the house worries me. Could we make tonight here more bearable instead? What would help?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What's a 5(2)? Am I sectioned now?\"","r":"It's a holding power for up to 72 hours while a doctor and a social worker decide if you need to stay. It isn't a full section. A nurse will explain your rights and give them to you in writing."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"It's so noisy here, I can't think. I need to get out.\"","r":"It is noisy, and that's hard when you're already overwhelmed. Let's find you a quieter room. Would something to help you sleep tonight make it easier?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Short-term sleep aid if wanted: zopiclone 7.5 mg at night (3.75 mg if older or frail), check chart first","RC reviews antidepressant dose (e.g. sertraline 50 mg in the morning, rising in 50 mg steps to 200 mg)","Means removed: tablets at home dealt with via family; room checked with her consent"],"psych":["Named nurse one-to-one tonight on what would make staying tolerable","Collaborative safety plan written with her before any discharge (NICE NG225)","Psychology formulation; CBT for depression after discharge via community or talking therapies"],"social":["Ward social worker explores housing, money and who is at home","If discharged: home treatment team contact within 72 h, GP letter next working day","Trusted person involved with her consent"],"safety":["Observation level agreed with nurse in charge (e.g. within eyesight); reviewed at morning handover","H1 handed over; 72 h runs from 21:50 when the nurse's holding power began","AMHP and two doctors (one Section 12 approved) booked for the morning","Section 132 rights explained and given in writing"]},"facts":[{"h":"72 hours starts at the nurse's hold","x":"When 5(4) is followed by 5(2), the 72 hours run from when the nurse's holding power began, not from when you sign H1. Here: 21:50."},{"h":"Section 5(4): six hours, nurse only","x":"A mental health or learning disability nurse can stop an informal inpatient leaving for a maximum of 6 hours, using Form H2. It ends when the doctor arrives and decides."},{"h":"Section 5(2) cannot be renewed","x":"Use it once, then an MHA assessment follows. It does not authorise treatment without consent, and it ends as soon as the assessment decides."},{"h":"Who can sign 5(2)","x":"The doctor or AC responsible for her inpatient care, or one person they nominate (usually the on-call doctor). Always assess her yourself first."},{"h":"Letting her go needs a real plan","x":"Safety plan, means removed, home treatment team, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, GP letter and a named person at home."}],"type":"Mental Health Act process","do":"Assess her within minutes and decide between letting her leave and a Section 5(2), within time limits."},"c-0-2-2":{"obj":["Found the first-dose date and read exactly what the certificate authorises (class, route, dose)","Asked him why he is refusing and offered at least one alternative he could accept","Declined IM tonight with a stated legal reason, and told the nurse what would change that","Handed over so the RC requests a SOAD next working day","Offered him an IMHA"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"They're putting something in my tablets. I've watched them do it.\"","r":"That sounds really frightening. Nobody will force tablets on you tonight. Would it help to open a sealed pack yourself and check the name against your chart?"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You've got no right to drug me.\"","r":"You're right to ask what gives us the right. Tonight nobody will give you medicine against your will. Your consultant will go through the rules and your choices with you tomorrow."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What happens if I keep saying no?\"","r":"Then your consultant asks an independent doctor to review your treatment, and they'll talk to you too. You can have an advocate and a solicitor, and your views get written down."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"It makes me restless all the time, and I've put on two stone.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me, those are real problems. Restlessness and weight gain can both be treated, or the medicine changed. I'll make sure the team looks at it tomorrow."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'd take the melt one, but not that tablet.\"","r":"That sounds like a good way forward. Let me check the melt is the same medicine and allowed on your form, and we'll get it from pharmacy if we can."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Nurse: \"He's on a 3. Just give the IM, it's what we always do.\"","r":"I get the frustration. He's past three months and the T3 covers oral only, so IM tonight would be unlawful unless it's a true emergency. Offer again later, and call me if he escalates."}],"bps":{"bio":["Offer olanzapine orodispersible at the same dose if the certificate covers oral olanzapine","Akathisia: rate on the Barnes scale; propranolol 10 mg two to three times daily is an option; RC to consider dose reduction","Weight, BMI, HbA1c and lipids if not done in the last 3 months"],"psych":["Named nurse explores the poisoning belief without arguing; sealed packs opened in front of him","RC shares the medication decision with him; long-acting injection discussed once trust is better","CBT for psychosis via the psychology team when settled"],"social":["Ward staff make the IMHA referral","Family involved with his consent to support adherence"],"safety":["Observation level unchanged unless agitation rises","Doctor called at once if violence; s62 only for genuine urgency","RC requests SOAD via the CQC; MHA office checks certificates next working day"]},"facts":[{"h":"The clock starts at the first dose","x":"Three months runs from the first dose of any drug given for his mental disorder during this unbroken detention, including time on Section 2. Not from the date of the Section 3."},{"h":"Read the T3 like a prescription","x":"Check drug class, route and the BNF dose limit it lists. Oral only means oral only. Anything outside it needs a new certificate or a genuine s62 emergency."},{"h":"A capacitous refusal ends the T2","x":"A T2 rests on capacity and consent. Once he says no with capacity, it no longer covers him; tell the RC and MHA office."},{"h":"One missed dose is rarely urgent","x":"Missing an evening antipsychotic dose carries little risk. Rushing to IM on a technicality carries legal and relationship cost."},{"h":"SOAD consults two people","x":"A nurse and one other professional involved in his care who is neither a doctor nor a nurse. Have their names ready when the RC makes the request."}],"type":"Mental Health Act process","do":"Check his T2/T3 status and three-month rule, decide if Section 62 applies, and record the authority."},"c-0-2-3":{"obj":["Assessed capacity separately for medication and for ECT, recording which part of the test is not met","Checked for an advance decision, a health and welfare LPA or a deputy","Named the right forms: T4 or T6 for ECT, T2 or T3 for medicines","Set up fluid balance, NEWS2 and bloods for her poor intake","Explained the SOAD visit to her and, with consent, to family"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I don't deserve treatment. Give it to someone who matters.\"","r":"I can hear how strongly you feel that, and I'm sorry you're carrying it. Thoughts like this are often part of severe depression. You matter to us, and we want to help lift it."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"ECT is electric shocks. You'll fry my brain.\"","r":"That fear is very common, and films don't help. It's done under a short general anaesthetic with a muscle relaxant. Some people lose memories around the treatment time. Can I explain what actually happens?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not signing anything.\"","r":"That's fine, you don't have to sign anything today. I want to understand what you think about the treatment. Your view will be written down and shared with the independent doctor."},{"tag":"Question","p":"Daughter: \"Can she be made to have ECT?\"","r":"Only in narrow circumstances. If she lacks capacity and hasn't validly refused it in advance, an independent doctor must agree. If she has capacity and says no, it can't happen outside an emergency."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Years ago I wrote down that I never wanted ECT.\"","r":"Thank you, that's really important. Do you know where it is or who has a copy? If it's a valid advance decision refusing ECT, we must respect it, so I need to see it today."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not depressed. I'm wicked, and this is what I deserve.\"","r":"I hear that it feels completely true. What I see is someone very unwell, not wicked. Can we agree to look after your body for now, starting with something to drink?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Antidepressant at full dose (e.g. sertraline 50 mg in the morning, up in 50 mg steps to 200 mg) plus an antipsychotic (e.g. olanzapine 5–10 mg at night)","ECT twice weekly, usually 6–12 sessions; anaesthetic review, ECG, FBC, U&E before the first; capacity rechecked before each session","Fluid and food chart, daily weight, U&E every 48 h, NEWS2 4-hourly; medical review if AKI or intake stays low"],"psych":["Named nurse: daily one-to-one, gentle prompting to eat and drink","Written ECT information for her and family","After recovery: CBT or behavioural activation and a relapse prevention plan"],"social":["Family involved with consent; carer's assessment offered","IMHA referral for the ECT decision","Office of the Public Guardian register searched for an LPA or deputy"],"safety":["Suicide risk: within eyesight observation, reviewed daily","If life-threatening before the SOAD visit: RC and ECT team consider s62 emergency ECT","RC requests SOAD via the CQC today; MHA office tracks the 3-month date"]},"facts":[{"h":"ECT has its own forms","x":"T4: she consents with capacity, signed by the AC or a SOAD. T6: SOAD certifies she lacks capacity and ECT is appropriate. T2 and T3 never cover ECT."},{"h":"A capacitous no to ECT stands","x":"Outside an emergency, ECT cannot be given to a detained adult who has capacity and refuses. Section 63 does not apply to ECT."},{"h":"Advance refusal blocks a T6","x":"A SOAD cannot certify ECT that conflicts with a valid, applicable advance decision, or a refusal by an attorney, deputy or the Court of Protection."},{"h":"Emergency ECT: two grounds only","x":"Under s62, ECT without a certificate is limited to saving life or preventing serious deterioration. The suffering and violence grounds do not apply."},{"h":"Name the part of the test she fails","x":"Record understand, retain, use or weigh, and communicate separately for each decision. Psychotic guilt usually blocks using and weighing, not understanding."}],"type":"Capacity assessment + Mental Health Act process","do":"Assess her capacity to consent to medication and ECT, then complete T2 or request a SOAD."},"c-0-2-4":{"obj":["Checked him for head injury and intoxication before any talk about leave","Told him leave is off tonight and that only the RC can change it","Heard his mother's concerns and confirmed home leave won't happen without her agreement","Offered his mother a carer's assessment and carer support","Recorded the late return and substance use and flagged it for the RC"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"This is a joke. I was only a bit late.\"","r":"I get that it feels unfair. Three hours late after drinking means I can't let you out again tonight. Your consultant reviews leave, and I'll make sure your side is written down."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Give me overnight tomorrow and I'll be perfect, I swear.\"","r":"I hear how much you want time out. Only your consultant can grant leave. Staying off alcohol and weed on the ward is what will help your case most."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not drunk, it was two beers. Weed helps me sleep.\"","r":"Thanks for being straight with me. Alcohol and weed both tend to push mania up and wreck sleep over time. Let's look at other ways to help you sleep."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So am I being punished now?\"","r":"No. Coming back late and intoxicated tells us leave needs more support, not punishment. We'll talk through what went wrong and plan leave that works."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"Mother: \"I feel terrible saying he can't come home.\"","r":"That's a really hard thing to say about your son, and it's OK to say it. You know your limits. No for now doesn't mean no for ever."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"Mother: \"Last time he was home he smashed the TV and scared his sister.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me; that matters for planning. I'll record it and make sure the team knows. Is anyone at home at risk right now?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"Mother: \"Will you tell him it was me who said no?\"","r":"We'll agree with you first how it's shared. We can tell him home leave isn't possible right now without saying it came from you, if that's safer."}],"bps":{"bio":["Hourly GCS and neuro obs while intoxicated; scalp and pupils checked; breath alcohol","Ask about daily drinking; CIWA-Ar and a chlordiazepoxide reducing regimen only if withdrawal expected; thiamine 100 mg three times daily if drinking heavily","Urine drug screen with consent; RC reviews antimanic dose"],"psych":["Dual diagnosis worker: motivational interviewing about alcohol and cannabis","Psychoeducation on substances and manic relapse"],"social":["Mother offered a Care Act 2014 carer's assessment from the local authority; carers' group details","Ward social worker explores other leave destinations"],"safety":["Nurse in charge does not facilitate leave tonight and records why; RC decides on Monday","Search on return per trust policy","Incident report for late return and intoxication","Risk assessment updated: mania plus substances, family conflict"]},"facts":[{"h":"Only the RC grants leave","x":"Section 17 leave is granted, changed or cancelled by the RC. Staff can delay a planned leave on the day if it looks unsafe, but must record why and tell the RC."},{"h":"Late back is AWOL, not recall","x":"Recall is a written notice from the RC to a patient still on leave. Failing to return on time makes him absent without leave under Section 18."},{"h":"Drunk hides head injuries","x":"Unsteady and intoxicated: check scalp, pupils and GCS. Falling GCS, vomiting, seizure or worsening headache means A&E for CT."},{"h":"Home leave needs the household","x":"Before granting leave to a family home, ask who lives there and record their agreement. A carer's refusal is a valid reason to change the plan."},{"h":"Over 7 days' leave: consider a CTO","x":"When the RC considers more than 7 consecutive days of leave, they must also consider whether a community treatment order would suit better."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Assess him after intoxicated return, decide tonight's Section 17 leave, and agree next steps with his mother."},"c-0-2-5":{"obj":["Recorded the start time of the 24 hours and when a 12-hour extension is allowed","Put the head wound and GCS ahead of any mental health assessment","Booked the AMHP and a Section 12 doctor for when he can engage","Agreed who stays with him: police, A&E staff or security","Had a safety plan and follow-up ready in case he is discharged"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I haven't done anything. Why am I under arrest?\"","r":"You're not under arrest, and you haven't committed a crime. The police were worried about your safety by the road. You're here so we can check you're OK and talk when you're ready."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm walking out. You can't keep me here.\"","r":"I understand you want to go. The law says you need to stay until you've been seen, up to 24 hours. Let's sort your head and get you a drink, then we'll talk."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"What's the point? Nobody would notice if I was gone.\"","r":"That sounds like a really lonely place to be. I want to hear what led to tonight. First let's look after the cut on your head, then I'll come back and sit with you."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I was just drunk. I didn't mean any of it.\"","r":"That may be right, and drink can make everything feel worse. We'd still like to talk properly once you're sober, so we're sure you're safe going home."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I took a load of paracetamol before I went out.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me, that changes things. How many, and what time? I need to tell the A&E doctor now so they can check your blood and treat you."},{"tag":"Question","p":"Police officer: \"Can we go now? We've been here two hours.\"","r":"I know you're needed elsewhere. He's settled, A&E agree and security know, so you can hand over. If he turns violent, our agreed protocol is to call you back."}],"bps":{"bio":["GCS and neuro obs; CT head per NICE head injury guidance; intoxication is not a reason to delay imaging","Breath alcohol and CIWA-Ar; oral thiamine, or IV Pabrinex if confusion, unsteadiness or eye signs; avoid sedation until head injury cleared","Paracetamol and salicylate levels if overdose possible; ECG"],"psych":["Assess once he is coherent and breath alcohol is falling; do not wait for zero","Safety plan with him; brief alcohol intervention"],"social":["AMHP identifies nearest relative and checks housing","Alcohol care team referral from A&E"],"safety":["One-to-one A&E staff or security; police stay if violent, per local protocol","24 h from 01:20 arrival; assessing doctor may extend by 12 h only if his condition prevents assessment","Bed search started early if admission likely","If discharged: crisis team follow-up within 72 h, GP letter, NHS 111 option 2"]},"facts":[{"h":"The clock keeps running","x":"24 hours from arrival at the first place of safety. The assessing doctor can extend by up to 12 hours only if his physical or mental condition made assessment impractical."},{"h":"Coherent is enough, zero isn't needed","x":"Assess once he can engage meaningfully and alcohol is falling. Waiting for a zero reading can burn most of the 24 hours."},{"h":"Moving between places of safety","x":"He can be transferred, e.g. from A&E to a 136 suite, while still detained. The original 24-hour clock carries on."},{"h":"No disorder found: release him","x":"If the doctor finds no mental disorder, he must be released even if the AMHP has not seen him. Otherwise both doctor and AMHP assess."},{"h":"Head injury before psychiatry","x":"Intoxication lowers GCS and hides bleeding. Falling GCS, vomiting or worsening headache needs urgent CT before anyone calls it 'just drink'."}],"type":"Mental Health Act process","do":"Confirm the 24-hour Section 136 clock, decide when he is fit for assessment, and agree police roles."},"c-0-2-6":{"obj":["Checked the RC report against the notes and written a signed, dated addendum for anything out of date","Prepared short, first-hand answers on nature, degree, risk and why hospital is needed now","Confirmed who attends: covering RC, named nurse, care coordinator","Explained the hearing to him and checked he has a solicitor","Agreed the Section 117 aftercare plan in case he is discharged"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You're going to tell them I'm mad so I stay locked up.\"","r":"I can hear you're worried about that. I'll tell them honestly what I've seen, including how much better you've got. They decide, not me, and your solicitor puts your side."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Solicitor: \"Isn't it right he's had no incidents for four weeks?\"","r":"Yes, that's right, and it's real progress. My concern is that yesterday he still told me his neighbours are watching him, and he's unsure he'd take medication at home."},{"tag":"Question","p":"Panel: \"What would happen if he were discharged today?\"","r":"From my six weeks with him, I think he would likely stop medication and the beliefs about his neighbours could grow. The care coordinator can speak better to community support."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Panel: \"Why can't this be managed in the community?\"","r":"His understanding of the illness is still limited, and he hasn't yet had overnight leave. That's the next step. If it goes well, discharge to the early intervention team is the aim."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I don't know what to say in there. I'll mess it up.\"","r":"That's very normal. You only need to say what's true for you. Your solicitor will guide you, and you can ask for a break at any point."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Honestly, I only take the tablets because I have to in here.\"","r":"Thank you for being honest. It's useful to know, and it may come up tomorrow. Can we talk about what would make the medicine feel worth taking at home?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Report states current antipsychotic, dose, adherence, side effects and date of last change","Physical monitoring listed with dates: weight, HbA1c, lipids, ECG"],"psych":["Early intervention team: CBT for psychosis (at least 16 sessions) and family intervention (NICE CG178)","Relapse prevention plan with early warning signs agreed with him"],"social":["Care coordinator leads Section 117 aftercare: housing, benefits, IPS employment support","Family involvement and carer's assessment"],"safety":["Risk summary specific and dated, not copied forward","If discharged: follow-up within 72 h, crisis numbers, GP letter","Supervisor debrief after the hearing"]},"facts":[{"h":"The panel must discharge unless...","x":"For Section 3 the hospital must show: a disorder whose nature or degree makes hospital treatment appropriate, treatment necessary for his health, safety or others' protection, and appropriate treatment available."},{"h":"Nature versus degree","x":"Nature is the illness's pattern over time (e.g. relapse after stopping medication). Degree is how unwell he is now. Know which you rely on and why."},{"h":"Speak only to what you saw","x":"Say 'I don't know' or 'the care coordinator can answer that' rather than guess. Panels lose trust in overstated or second-hand evidence."},{"h":"Section 117 aftercare is free","x":"After Section 3, the ICB and local authority must provide free aftercare until both agree it is no longer needed. Name the actual services in your evidence."},{"h":"Quote dates, not impressions","x":"Give dates of the last incident, last leave and last medication change. An old risk event presented as current undermines the whole report."}],"type":"Documentation and reporting","do":"Update the tribunal report to be accurate and current, and rehearse answers to likely questions."},"c-0-2-7":{"obj":["Named NMS as the likely cause and stopped haloperidol","Explained the danger to her in plain words and sought her agreement first","Recorded a decision-specific capacity assessment for the blood test","Arranged CK, U&E and transfer criteria with the medical team","Reported the reaction via Yellow Card and an incident form"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"No needles. You're not taking my blood.\"","r":"I hear you, and you're allowed to say no. I'm worried because fever and stiffness after a new medicine can be dangerous. One small sample could tell us if you need the general hospital. Can I explain?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"You'll use my blood for experiments. You're working with them.\"","r":"That sounds frightening. The blood goes only to our lab to check your muscles and kidneys. You can watch me label it, and I'll tell you the results myself."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Nurse: \"She's on a Section 2. Can't we just hold her?\"","r":"The section covers treating her mental disorder, not this physical test. If she lacks capacity for it, the MCA lets us act in her best interests, least restrictively. Let me assess first."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not peeing in a pot for you.\"","r":"That's fine, it's your choice and we won't force it. It would help us know if anything else is affecting you. If you change your mind, just tell a nurse."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it the medicine making me feel this bad?\"","r":"It might well be. We've stopped it straight away. A blood test will show how your muscles are coping, and the medical team will look after you."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll do the bloods if you let me go home.\"","r":"I understand you want to be home. I can't promise that, but the test helps you get better faster. Let's get through this first, then talk about leave with your consultant."}],"bps":{"bio":["Stop haloperidol and all antipsychotics now","CK, U&E, creatinine, LFT, FBC, CRP, glucose; urine for blood; ECG; NEWS2 hourly","Medical team: IV fluids and cooling; dantrolene or bromocriptine only on their advice","Agitation meanwhile: lorazepam 1–2 mg oral or IM, not antipsychotics"],"psych":["Explain NMS to her once well enough; record it as an adverse drug reaction","Mania managed meanwhile with low stimulation and sleep support"],"social":["Family told with her consent; offer a meeting","IMHA offered"],"safety":["If she lacks capacity: least restrictive best-interests bloods, recorded on the MCA form","Transfer to the general hospital if CK markedly raised, NEWS2 rising or she worsens","Incident form, Yellow Card, RC informed","Duty of candour conversation if harm confirmed"]},"facts":[{"h":"NMS has four features","x":"Fever, rigidity, autonomic instability (fast pulse, labile BP, sweating) and altered consciousness, usually within days of starting or raising an antipsychotic. CK is often very high."},{"h":"Detention treats the mind, not this","x":"Part 4 covers treatment for mental disorder. Tests for this physical problem need her consent, or a best-interests decision under the MCA if she lacks capacity."},{"h":"Never force a urine drug screen","x":"It is rarely urgent enough to justify holding someone. Record the refusal and treat clinically as if substances are possible."},{"h":"Restarting after NMS","x":"Wait at least two weeks after full recovery, start low and go slow with a different, lower-potency antipsychotic, and watch temperature and CK."},{"h":"Yellow Card every suspected NMS","x":"Report suspected serious reactions to the MHRA through the Yellow Card website or app, even if you are not sure of the cause."}],"type":"Mental Health Act process","do":"Persuade her first, then decide whether MHA or MCA lawfully authorises the blood test and drug screen."},"c-0-2-8":{"obj":["Checked with the RC that both recall tests are met, not just the missed depot","Explained recall to him with a reason he could repeat back","Planned safe conveyance: AMHP, police and a Section 135(2) warrant if he refuses","Written his arrival time and the 72-hour deadline where the team will see it","Asked about the side effects behind his refusal"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going anywhere. That injection turns me into a zombie.\"","r":"I hear that it knocks you out, and that matters. Coming in lets us sort the dose or try something different. If you come with us now, it'll be calmer than if others have to get involved."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"There are cameras in the walls. If I leave, they'll take my flat.\"","r":"That sounds terrifying. Your flat stays yours; a hospital stay doesn't change your tenancy. We'll make sure it's locked and safe while you're away."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You said the CTO would keep me out of hospital!\"","r":"You're right, it was meant to keep you well at home, and I'm sorry it's come to this. You've done well for a long time. A short stay now is to get you back on track."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How long will you keep me?\"","r":"Up to 72 hours to start with. In that time your doctor decides whether you go home on the CTO or need to stay longer. You can have an advocate and a solicitor."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll take tablets instead. Just don't make me come in.\"","r":"That's worth talking about. Your consultant decides, and I'll pass it on now. If they think tablets could work, the plan may change, but we'd still need to see you soon."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"CPN: \"We can't recall him just for missing a depot, can we?\"","r":"Right, a missed dose alone isn't enough. But he's frightened, has blacked out his windows and was yelling at the neighbour. That's relapse with risk, which meets the test. Let's call the RC now."}],"bps":{"bio":["On recall, check the CTO11 certificate lists the depot before giving it without consent","Side-effect review: EPSE rating, prolactin, weight; RC may switch (e.g. aripiprazole long-acting injection 400 mg monthly)","Admission bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, lipids, HbA1c; ECG"],"psych":["RC agrees depot choice with him after the side-effect review","CPN updates relapse signature and crisis plan"],"social":["Flat secured and keys logged; housing officer told if the neighbour dispute continues","Nearest relative informed","IMHA offered"],"safety":["CTO3 served by hand where possible; time and method recorded","If he refuses: AMHP applies for a Section 135(2) warrant; police assist with conveyance","Within 72 h of arrival: release back on the CTO, or revoke with AMHP agreement (CTO5)","MHA office informed of revocation so the tribunal referral is made"]},"facts":[{"h":"Recall needs two things","x":"He needs hospital treatment for his mental disorder, and without recall there would be risk to his health, safety or others. Breaching a mandatory CTO condition is the other route."},{"h":"How CTO3 is served matters","x":"Handed to him: effective at once. Delivered to his usual address: effective the next day. Sent first class: effective the second working day after posting."},{"h":"72 hours from arrival","x":"He can be held for up to 72 hours from arrival at hospital. In that time he is treated if authorised, released back on the CTO, or the CTO is revoked."},{"h":"Revocation needs an AMHP","x":"RC completes CTO5 and an AMHP must agree. He returns to his original section with a fresh 6-month period, and the hospital refers him to the tribunal."},{"h":"Record what you saw","x":"A missed depot alone does not justify recall. Document the relapse and risk: blacked-out windows, suspiciousness, yelling at the neighbour, refusal to engage."}],"type":"Mental Health Act process","do":"Agree recall with the RC and plan the 72-hour period and possible CTO revocation."},"c-0-2-9":{"obj":["Screened her with the 4AT, named delirium as the working diagnosis and listed three likely causes for the medical team to chase tonight","Recorded a capacity assessment for one decision (leaving hospital and refusing IV treatment tonight), stating which of the four abilities failed","Told the nurse in charge in one sentence that the MCA, not the MHA, covers keeping her on the ward tonight, and what force is proportionate","Agreed 1:1 enhanced observation and an urgent DoLS authorisation started by the ward before you leave","Phoned the daughter, asked about an LPA for health and welfare, and asked her to come in tomorrow to help orient her mother"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I have to go, he'll be waiting for his tea!\"","r":"\"You've always looked after him. Tell me about him while we sit a minute. It's the middle of the night and your drip is still running, so I'll ring your daughter now.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Get out of my way! You can't keep me prisoner!\"","r":"\"I can see you're frightened and cross. Nobody wants to trap you. An infection is making you unwell. Walk with me to the day room and we'll sort it out together.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not having that needle back in my arm.\"","r":"\"Fair enough, it's uncomfortable. It was giving you water because you were very dry. Could you drink a big glass with me now instead, and the nurse checks again in an hour?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Medical registrar: \"Can't you just section her so we can keep her?\"","r":"\"Thanks for calling early. This is delirium from a physical cause and she needs medical treatment, so the MHA isn't the tool. She lacks capacity to decide to leave tonight; the MCA covers it. I'll document it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Nurse: \"If she pushes past us, are we allowed to hold her?\"","r":"\"Yes, if it's needed to stop her coming to harm and is proportionate: least force, shortest time. Try distraction and walking with her first, then write down exactly what you did and why.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"Daughter: \"She's been forgetting things for a year. She left the gas on twice.\"","r":"\"That's really helpful, thank you. It suggests a memory problem may sit underneath this infection. We'll treat the infection first, then ask her GP for a memory assessment once she's clear-headed.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Delirium screen tonight by the medical team: FBC, U&E (sodium), calcium, glucose, CRP, LFT, urine culture (no dipstick over 65), bladder scan, bowel chart","Drug chart review: stop or reduce anticholinergics, opioids and sedatives where possible; continue antibiotics and fluids with a fluid balance chart","Only if she is a danger to herself or others and de-escalation fails: haloperidol 0.5 mg orally, lowest dose, no longer than a week (NICE CG103); avoid in Parkinson's or Lewy body dementia"],"psych":["Reorientation every shift: clock and calendar in view, glasses and hearing aids in, same staff, lights low at night","Repeat 4AT daily; GP to arrange memory assessment once the delirium has cleared"],"social":["Daughter visits to reorient and brings familiar photos; check for registered LPA (health and welfare)","IMCA referral if there is no family or friend to consult on serious decisions","Ward discharge coordinator checks home support before discharge"],"safety":["1:1 enhanced observation tonight; falls bundle: low bed, call bell in reach, non-slip socks","Ward (managing authority) grants an urgent DoLS authorisation and applies to the local authority for a standard one on the same day, per trust policy","Reassess capacity in the morning when she is more alert and record it; liaison review next working day"]},"facts":[{"h":"Capacity is decision- and time-specific","x":"Assess the exact decision: leaving tonight and refusing IV antibiotics. In delirium ability fluctuates, so reassess when she is brighter and record both findings."},{"h":"4AT takes under two minutes","x":"Alertness, AMT4 (age, date of birth, place, year), months backwards, and acute change or fluctuation. Score 4 or more suggests delirium; 1–3 suggests possible cognitive impairment."},{"h":"MCA restraint has two tests","x":"Holding her is lawful only if you reasonably believe it prevents harm to her, and the force matches how likely and how serious that harm is (MCA s6). Record both."},{"h":"Urgent DoLS lasts 7 days","x":"The hospital authorises itself for up to 7 days; the local authority may extend this once by 7 more while it assesses the standard application."},{"h":"Quiet delirium is missed most","x":"Hypoactive delirium (sleepy, withdrawn, slow) is often mislabelled as depression or tiredness and carries a worse outlook than the agitated form."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"Assess her capacity to stay, choose between MCA, DoLS and MHA, and advise the medical team."},"c-0-2-10":{"obj":["Explained the infection, the treatment and the risk of sepsis in plain words and asked him to repeat them back","Tested all four parts of capacity for this one decision and recorded which part failed and the evidence for it","Told the nurse that Section 3 does not authorise treatment of the cellulitis, and that the MCA applies if he lacks capacity","Agreed a least restrictive plan with the medical team (trusted escort, oral antibiotics if clinically safe) and a time to re-check","Recorded family or IMCA views and a time to reassess his capacity"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"My leg is fine. I'm not going anywhere.\"","r":"\"I'm not here to force anything. Can I show you what I'm seeing on your leg? Then tell me what you think is going on, and I'll tell you what worries me.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"They'll poison me over there. They're all in on it.\"","r":"\"That sounds really frightening. You don't have to trust them yet. What if a nurse you know stays with you throughout, and you watch the antibiotic come out of a sealed pack?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You can't make me. I know my rights.\"","r":"\"You're right that you have rights, and your section doesn't cover your leg. I need to understand how you're weighing this up. What do you think could happen if the infection spreads?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll take tablets if I can stay here.\"","r":"\"That's a really helpful offer, thank you. I'll ask the medical doctor now whether tablets are safe enough for your leg. If it spreads or you feel worse, we'll need to talk about hospital again.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Nurse: \"He's on a Section 3, so can't we just treat him?\"","r":"\"Section 3 covers treatment for his mental disorder, not cellulitis. For his leg it's the Mental Capacity Act. I'll assess and document his capacity now, then we'll agree the plan together.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"Sister: \"Last time he was like this he only trusted Mum.\"","r":"\"Thank you, that's really useful. Could your mum call or visit today? She may make treatment feel safer for him, and I'd like her view on what he would want.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Medical review today; if oral treatment is clinically safe: flucloxacillin 500 mg–1 g four times a day for 5–7 days (NICE NG141); penicillin allergy: clarithromycin 500 mg twice a day (check QT with his antipsychotic)","Mark the erythema edge with time and date; NEWS2 4-hourly; lactate and blood cultures if NEWS2 reaches 5","Elevate the leg; paracetamol 1 g up to four times a day"],"psych":["Do not argue with the persecutory belief; work through a trusted nurse and offer concrete reassurances","Continue antipsychotic as authorised on his T2 or T3"],"social":["Mother or nearest relative consulted for best-interests views","IMCA if no appropriate family and serious medical treatment is proposed"],"safety":["RC grants escorted s17 leave for any transfer; section papers travel with him","Reassess capacity within 24 hours or sooner if he changes his mind or deteriorates","999 or A&E if NEWS2 rises, systolic falls below 90, new confusion or a rapidly spreading margin"]},"facts":[{"h":"s63 covers the mental disorder only","x":"Part 4 treatment without consent is for the mental disorder and its direct consequences. An unrelated infection needs his consent or the MCA."},{"h":"Unwise is not incapacity","x":"He may refuse if he can understand, retain, use and weigh the information and communicate a choice. A diagnosis of schizophrenia alone never proves incapacity."},{"h":"Name the link to the delusion","x":"If a fixed belief that antibiotics are poison stops him weighing the risks, write that explicitly: it is the evidence that capacity fails for this decision."},{"h":"IMCA for serious treatment","x":"If nobody appropriate (other than paid staff) can be consulted about serious medical treatment, an IMCA must be instructed unless the treatment is urgent."},{"h":"NEWS2 triggers","x":"5 or more, or 3 in any single parameter, needs urgent medical review; 7 or more is an emergency. On a mental health site that usually means 999."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"Assess his capacity to refuse cellulitis treatment, explain the legal position to the nurse, and agree a plan."},"c-0-3-1":{"obj":["Gained consent for the physical assessment, offered a chaperone, and recorded both","Checked and documented heart, lungs, abdomen, thyroid, cranial and peripheral nerves and both fundi, normal or not","Requested baseline bloods, ECG and urine drug screen, and named who chases each result","Listed the organic red flags you looked for and the action if any is present","Handed the night team a plan for observations and any parts left to finish, before 22:00"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking my top off. Why do you need to touch me?\"","r":"\"That's a fair question. Some physical illnesses cause symptoms like yours, and I want to rule them out. We can go one part at a time, with a nurse present, and you can say stop whenever you like.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"What are you going to do with my blood?\"","r":"\"Fair worry. It goes only to the hospital lab, to check things like your thyroid, sugar and salts so any medicine is safe for you. You can watch me label it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do you think I'm on drugs or something?\"","r":"\"I ask everyone, it's routine. Cannabis and other drugs can bring on experiences like yours, so knowing helps us treat you properly. This is about your health, not getting you in trouble.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I just want to sleep. Can't this wait till tomorrow?\"","r":"\"You look exhausted, and I'll be quick. The heart and blood checks matter before any medicine tonight. Anything we don't finish, I'll hand to the morning team.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've had headaches every morning for weeks.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me, that's important. I'd like to look carefully at the back of your eyes and test your nerves now. Depending on what I find, I may ask the medical team about a scan.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Nurse: \"He'll need something to sleep tonight. Can you write him up?\"","r":"\"Yes. I'll chart a short-term sleeping tablet once I've seen his ECG. Please let me know if he becomes more unsettled, and record his sleep overnight.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, glucose or HbA1c, lipids, calcium, prolactin, CRP; B12, folate, HIV and syphilis serology per trust protocol","12-lead ECG before any antipsychotic if cardiac history, family history of sudden death, abnormal pulse, or if the drug's SPC requires it","Record weight, BMI, waist, BP and pulse as the cardiometabolic baseline","Sleep tonight if needed: promethazine 25 mg or zopiclone 7.5 mg at bedtime, a few nights only"],"psych":["Leave the antipsychotic choice for the ward round unless distress is severe; discuss options with him (NICE CG178)","Refer to the Early Intervention in Psychosis team (2-week access standard)"],"social":["Collateral history from family with his consent","Note housing, work and money worries for the ward social worker"],"safety":["NEWS2 4-hourly overnight; observation level set by the nurse in charge after the risk assessment","VTE, falls and MUST completed on the record","Focal signs or papilloedema: same-night call to the medical registrar about CT"]},"facts":[{"h":"Fundoscopy is the most skipped step","x":"Papilloedema means raised intracranial pressure and changes tonight's plan. Dim the lights, look at both discs and write what you saw."},{"h":"Date every refusal","x":"If he declines part of the assessment, record what was offered, why he declined and when it will be offered again. An undated 'declined' gets lost."},{"h":"Prolactin before the first dose","x":"A baseline prolactin lets you interpret later sexual or breast side effects. NICE lists it with weight, waist, BP, glucose and lipids before starting."},{"h":"Drug screens miss a lot","x":"Standard urine screens miss synthetic cannabinoids and most novel substances. A negative result does not exclude drug-induced psychosis: ask directly."},{"h":"Organic clues in first psychosis","x":"Late onset, visual hallucinations, fluctuating confusion, focal signs, seizures, headache or fever all call for a medical work-up before calling it primary psychosis."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Complete an admission clerking with full system examination, fundoscopy and baseline bloods and ECG."},"c-0-3-2":{"obj":["Measured the QT by hand and recalculated the QTc rather than trusting the machine figure","Held the noon haloperidol and written the reason on the drug chart","Requested potassium, magnesium and calcium and checked the chart for other QT-prolonging drugs","Presented the ECG systematically to the consultant in under a minute, ending with a clear SBAR request","Named the ECG signs of STEMI and the 999 route to primary PCI"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"There's nothing wrong with my heart. I feel amazing!\"","r":"\"I'm glad you feel well. This change on the tracing usually causes no symptoms at first, which is why we check. It's easy to put right by changing the medicine.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I still get something to calm me down and help me sleep?\"","r":"\"Yes. Pausing one medicine doesn't mean stopping treatment. The consultant and I will choose one that's kinder to your heart today, and you won't be left without anything.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Am I going to have a heart attack?\"","r":"\"I can see that's scary. This isn't a heart attack. It's a timing change in the heart's electrical rhythm that can become risky if ignored, so we're acting now while you feel well.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"No more sticky pads and wires on me.\"","r":"\"Fair enough, you've had a lot done. The repeat tracing takes two minutes and stops us guessing. Could we do it after lunch, with the nurse you get on with?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Nurse: \"Can I give the noon dose and we sort it out later?\"","r":"\"Please hold it and mark the chart. Over 500 means we stop the drug now. I'll send bloods and repeat the ECG. Tell me straight away about fainting, palpitations or chest pain.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Consultant: \"Did you just read the machine's number?\"","r":"\"No. I measured it by hand in lead II: QT 450 ms at a rate of 85 gives a Fridericia QTc of about 505. I've held haloperidol and sent electrolytes.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Hold haloperidol; check the chart for other QT drugs (citalopram, escitalopram, erythromycin, clarithromycin, methadone, ondansetron)","Urgent potassium (keep above 4.0), magnesium and calcium; correct with the medical team","Repeat ECG within 24 hours and after any correction","Alternative for mania, agreed with the consultant: aripiprazole 15 mg once daily (15–30 mg) or olanzapine 15 mg at night (5–20 mg); ECG again at steady state"],"psych":["Explain the change to her simply and repeat it later: mania limits how much she retains","Low-stimulus area and consistent nursing while the drug is switched"],"safety":["Medical or cardiology advice if QTc stays over 500, potassium is low, or she has syncope or palpitations","Record the reaction on the drug chart and the electronic record; Yellow Card report","STEMI on any ECG: 999 to the primary PCI centre, aspirin 300 mg unless contraindicated"]},"facts":[{"h":"QTc thresholds","x":"Prolonged above about 450 ms in men and 470 ms in women. Above 500 ms the risk of torsades de pointes is real: stop the likely drug and check electrolytes."},{"h":"Use Fridericia at fast rates","x":"Bazett overestimates QTc when the heart rate is high, as in agitated patients. Fridericia (QT divided by the cube root of RR) is more reliable."},{"h":"Measure QT in lead II or V5","x":"From the start of QRS to the end of the T wave, ignoring U waves; average three beats. Machines misread flat T waves and noisy traces."},{"h":"STEMI criteria","x":"New ST elevation in two contiguous leads (at least 1 mm in limb leads, more in V2–V3) with symptoms: 999 for primary PCI and aspirin 300 mg."},{"h":"Haloperidol needs a baseline ECG","x":"The product licence advises an ECG before starting. Risk rises with high or IV doses, low potassium or magnesium, and other QT drugs."}],"type":"ECG and investigations","do":"Interpret and present the admission ECG, act on QTc 510 ms on haloperidol, and recognise MI."},"c-0-3-3":{"obj":["Completed an ABCDE assessment and a 12-lead ECG within 10 minutes of the bleep","Called 999 stating 'suspected STEMI' before any MHA paperwork","Given aspirin 300 mg chewed (no allergy or active bleeding) and recorded the time","Secured s17 escorted leave from the on-call consultant by phone, with section papers travelling with him","Given the paramedics an SBAR handover in under a minute"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"It's crushing. Am I dying?\"","r":"\"This is frightening, and you did the right thing telling us. We're treating it as your heart. An ambulance is coming, I'm staying with you, and this tablet thins your blood.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going to that hospital. You're trying to get rid of me.\"","r":"\"I can see why it feels like that. You're not being sent away. Your heart needs tests we can't do here. A nurse you know comes with you, and you come back here afterwards.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It's just my nerves. I get panicky at night.\"","r":"\"It might be anxiety, but with your diabetes and smoking we can't assume that. I'd rather check your heart and be wrong. Let's do this tracing now.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Nurse: \"Should we wait for the consultant before calling an ambulance?\"","r":"\"No. Call 999 now and say possible heart attack. I'll ring the consultant about leave while we wait. The medical emergency comes first.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Paramedic: \"Is he going to be a risk in the back of the ambulance?\"","r":"\"He's been calm and cooperative tonight, with no violence this admission. Two nurses are escorting him with his section papers, and they know what to do should he try to leave.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"Wife, next morning: \"Why did nobody ring me last night?\"","r":"\"I'm sorry you heard this so late. Our focus was getting him to the heart unit quickly. He's stable now. Here is the cardiac ward's number, and ours, so you can get updates.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Aspirin 300 mg chewed now; GTN 400 micrograms sublingual if systolic over 90 and no right ventricular involvement","Oxygen only if SpO2 below 94% (target 94–98%); capillary glucose","Repeat ECG every 15–20 minutes while waiting; IV access if a trained person is on site"],"psych":["Send a brief psychiatric summary and risk history with him","Liaison psychiatry to review him on the cardiac ward within 24 hours"],"social":["Tell next of kin with his consent, tonight if he wishes","Ward staff phone the cardiac ward each shift for updates and to plan his return"],"safety":["999 as suspected STEMI; paramedics transmit the ECG ahead to the PCI centre","RC grants escorted s17 leave by phone; copies of section papers and the s17 form go with the escort","Nurse in charge adjusts ward staffing after the escort leaves","Datix and a timed record of every step"]},"facts":[{"h":"Inferior STEMI: II, III, aVF","x":"ST elevation in II, III and aVF usually means right coronary artery. If hypotensive, check V4R: right ventricular infarct means avoid GTN."},{"h":"Primary PCI window","x":"NICE: offer primary PCI if symptoms began within 12 hours and it can be delivered within 120 minutes of when fibrinolysis could have been given."},{"h":"NEWS2 of 7 is an emergency","x":"A score of 7 or more needs an emergency response and continuous monitoring. On a stand-alone unit that means 999, not waiting for a doctor."},{"h":"Diabetes blunts chest pain","x":"People with diabetes, older adults and women may present with breathlessness, sweating or nausea only. Keep a low threshold for ECG and troponin."},{"h":"Emergencies never wait for paperwork","x":"The RC can grant s17 leave by phone; the escort carries the papers. If the RC is unreachable, call 999 anyway and document why."}],"type":"Emergency management + Handover and escalation","do":"Assess his chest pain using NEWS2, stabilise him, arrange urgent transfer and give an SBAR handover."},"c-0-3-4":{"obj":["Recognised NMS from fever, rigidity, autonomic instability and altered consciousness, and stopped all antipsychotics with the time recorded","Sent CK, U&E, LFT, FBC, CRP and glucose and arranged 999 transfer","Named at least two differentials (sepsis, serotonin syndrome, lethal catatonia) and why NMS fits best","Given the receiving team an SBAR including the dose timeline and the IM haloperidol","Explained to the family (with consent) what happened, honestly and without jargon, and gave a named contact"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't move properly. What's happening to me?\"","r":"\"Your muscles are stiff because of a reaction to your medicine, and we've stopped it. You're safe. We're taking you to the main hospital to cool you down and protect your kidneys.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"Mother: \"You've poisoned my son! Why did nobody warn us?\"","r":"\"I'm so sorry; this must be terrifying. You deserve a clear explanation. It's a rare reaction to his medicine. We stopped it straight away and he's being treated now. Let me tell you what we know.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Mother: \"Will he get better?\"","r":"\"Most people recover fully when it's caught early and the medicine is stopped, often within one to two weeks. He's in the right place, and I'll be honest with you if anything changes.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can he ever take antipsychotics again?\"","r":"\"Most people can, safely. We'd wait until he's fully recovered, use a different medicine, start low and go slowly, checking temperature and blood tests closely. We'll plan that with you both.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Nurse: \"He's agitated. Shall I give his PRN haloperidol?\"","r":"\"No antipsychotics at all, please. If he's very distressed, lorazepam is fine and I'll prescribe it. Cool him with fans, give sips if he can swallow safely, and do obs every 15 minutes.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Mother: \"Who do I ring to find out how he is?\"","r":"\"Here's my name and the ward number. The medical ward will update you directly too, and I'll ask his consultant to call you tomorrow.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Stop all antipsychotics, including any depot; mark the chart with the time","Bloods before transfer: CK, renal and liver function, full blood count, CRP, glucose, calcium, magnesium; CK repeated daily in hospital","In hospital: IV fluids, external cooling, VTE prophylaxis; dantrolene or bromocriptine in severe cases (medical decision)","Lorazepam 1 mg orally or IM for agitation while awaiting transfer"],"psych":["Explain the reaction to him and his family; written information on NMS","Consultant plans rechallenge at least 2 weeks after full recovery: different antipsychotic, low dose, slow titration"],"social":["Family updated with his consent; named contact on the ward","Ward social worker supports the family with travel and visiting the general hospital"],"safety":["999 transfer with escort, drug chart, observation chart and MHA papers","NEWS2 every 15 minutes until transfer","Record as an adverse drug reaction everywhere; Yellow Card; duty of candour conversation documented"]},"facts":[{"h":"Four core features","x":"Fever, lead-pipe rigidity, altered consciousness and autonomic instability (fast pulse, swinging BP, sweating). Raised CK supports it, often into the thousands."},{"h":"Highest risk early and with IM","x":"Rapid dose rises, IM or depot antipsychotics, dehydration, exhaustion and restraint raise the risk. Most cases start within two weeks of a change."},{"h":"NMS is slow, serotonin syndrome fast","x":"NMS evolves over days with bradykinesia and lead-pipe rigidity. Serotonin syndrome comes on within hours with clonus, hyperreflexia and diarrhoea."},{"h":"Protect the kidneys","x":"Muscle breakdown releases myoglobin and can cause acute kidney injury. Watch CK, creatinine and urine output; IV fluids are the cornerstone."},{"h":"Rechallenge: wait and switch","x":"Wait at least two weeks after recovery, choose a different antipsychotic, start low, increase slowly, and monitor temperature, pulse, BP and CK."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Recognise neuroleptic malignant syndrome, stop the antipsychotic, check CK and arrange urgent medical transfer."},"c-0-3-5":{"obj":["Identified serotonin syndrome with the Hunter criteria after checking for inducible, spontaneous and ocular clonus","Advised stopping sertraline and tramadol and asked about every other serotonergic drug and substance","Distinguished it from NMS and anticholinergic toxicity in one sentence each to the A&E doctor","Agreed admission under the medical team with CK, U&E and TOXBASE advice","Asked about intent (accidental or deliberate overload) and planned a liaison review in 24–48 hours"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So is this just my anxiety?\"","r":"\"It makes sense to wonder, but no. Your body is reacting to two medicines together. It's treatable, and most people improve within a day of stopping them.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have told the GP I was on sertraline.\"","r":"\"Please don't blame yourself. This combination is easy to miss, and checking is our job. What matters is we've spotted it and stopped both.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will I have to come off antidepressants forever?\"","r":"\"No. Once you're well, we'll review your mood together and decide when and what to restart, usually at a low dose. You won't be left unsupported.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can I at least keep the tramadol? My back is agony.\"","r":"\"Your pain is real and we'll treat it. Tramadol is part of the problem, so it has to stop. There are painkillers, like paracetamol, that don't affect serotonin.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've also been taking St John's wort from the chemist.\"","r":"\"Thank you, that really helps. It raises serotonin too, so please stop it. Anything else, like cough mixtures, migraine tablets or recreational drugs?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"A&E doctor: \"Can I give her diazepam for the jerking?\"","r":"\"Yes, a benzodiazepine is the right first step. Avoid antipsychotics and physical restraint, keep her cool and give IV fluids. Ask TOXBASE about cyproheptadine if she doesn't settle.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Stop sertraline, tramadol and any other serotonergic drug or supplement","Diazepam 5–10 mg orally for agitation and clonus, repeated as the medical team advises","IV fluids, cooling, hourly temperature and NEWS2; CK, U&E, LFT, coagulation if temperature is high","Cyproheptadine 12 mg orally then 2 mg every 2 hours if still symptomatic, only on TOXBASE or specialist advice"],"psych":["Liaison review in 24–48 hours: mood, risk and when to restart an antidepressant","Restart only when symptom-free, at a low dose, never alongside tramadol"],"social":["GP letter: tramadol listed as an adverse interaction; GP to arrange physiotherapy and non-serotonergic pain relief","Community pharmacist told via the discharge summary so future prescriptions are flagged"],"safety":["Ask directly whether extra tablets were taken on purpose; if so, full self-harm assessment","Record the adverse reaction; Yellow Card report","Temperature above 39.5 or rising rigidity: critical care review"]},"facts":[{"h":"Hunter criteria need clonus","x":"With a serotonergic drug: spontaneous clonus; inducible or ocular clonus plus agitation or sweating; tremor plus hyperreflexia; or hypertonia, temperature over 38 and clonus."},{"h":"Look at the legs first","x":"Clonus and hyperreflexia are usually worse in the legs. Test ankle clonus and watch for slow side-to-side ocular clonus."},{"h":"Hidden serotonergic culprits","x":"Tramadol, pethidine, fentanyl, methadone, linezolid, triptans, St John's wort, dextromethorphan, MDMA and cocaine. Any of these with an MAOI is the most dangerous."},{"h":"Fast in, fast out","x":"Most cases start within 24 hours of a new drug or dose rise and settle within 24 hours of stopping, longer with fluoxetine or MAOIs."},{"h":"Pupils, skin and bowels split it","x":"Serotonin syndrome: big pupils, sweating, diarrhoea. Anticholinergic: big pupils but dry hot skin, silent bowel, retention. NMS: rigidity and slow onset."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Recognise serotonin syndrome, stop serotonergic drugs, agree treatment with the medical team, and plan follow-up."},"c-0-3-6":{"obj":["Held the 18:00 lithium and the ibuprofen, with the reason written on the chart for the night team","Checked for neurological signs of toxicity (coarse tremor, ataxia, slurred speech, confusion) and recorded NEWS2","Confirmed the time of the blood sample against the last dose","Requested U&E, creatinine, eGFR, calcium, a repeat level and an ECG, and named who chases them","Stated the criteria for referring her to the acute medical team and acted on them"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to stop lithium? It's the only thing that keeps me well.\"","r":"\"I know how much it helps you. This is a pause, not stopping for good. Once your kidneys and level are back to normal, we'll restart safely, probably at a lower dose.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is it damaging my kidneys?\"","r":"\"That's a fair worry. A high level with sickness can strain the kidneys, which is why I'm checking them now. Caught early, they usually recover. Fluids and stopping the ibuprofen help a lot.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've hardly drunk anything. It all comes back up.\"","r":"\"Thanks for telling me, that matters. Being dry pushes lithium up. Let's try small sips every few minutes. If you can't keep them down, you'll need a drip at the medical hospital.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"The ibuprofen was my idea. I asked for it.\"","r":"\"You weren't to know. Asking for pain relief was sensible; catching the interaction is our job. Paracetamol is safe with lithium, so we'll use that for your back.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Nurse: \"Shall I just give an antiemetic and recheck tomorrow?\"","r":"\"Not on its own. Hold lithium, start a fluid chart and regular obs. I want bloods and an ECG now. Call me if she gets confused, more unsteady or keeps vomiting.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Medical registrar: \"It's only 1.6. Can't psychiatry manage it?\"","r":"\"I understand, but she has vomiting, diarrhoea and a coarse tremor and can't keep fluids down. She needs IV fluids and renal monitoring we can't give here. Could you see her this evening?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Hold lithium and ibuprofen; chart the reason","Now: lithium level, U&E, creatinine, eGFR, calcium; ECG for bradycardia, T-wave change and QTc","Oral fluids 2–3 litres a day if tolerated; IV sodium chloride 0.9% via the medical team if not","Paracetamol 1 g up to four times a day for back pain instead of NSAIDs","Repeat level and renal function as the medical team or TOXBASE advise"],"psych":["Daily mood check while lithium is held; watch for early relapse signs","Restart only after consultant review, often at a lower dose; level 12 hours post-dose about a week after restarting"],"social":["With consent, tell family what to watch for: vomiting, diarrhoea, unsteadiness, confusion","Care coordinator reinforces fluids and sick-day rules after discharge: pause lithium and seek advice if vomiting"],"safety":["NEWS2 and fluid balance chart overnight, 1- to 4-hourly by severity","Refer to acute medicine if neurological signs, falling GCS, AKI, persistent vomiting or a rising level","Datix; GP letter: avoid NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, ARBs and thiazides; update her lithium record book"]},"facts":[{"h":"Target 0.6–0.8 mmol/L","x":"NICE: aim for 0.6–0.8 for most people; up to 1.0 if relapse occurred at lower levels. Take the sample 12 hours after the last dose."},{"h":"Routine monitoring rhythm","x":"Level weekly after each dose change until stable, then 3-monthly in year one, then 6-monthly; U&E, eGFR, calcium and TFTs 6-monthly; weight annually."},{"h":"NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, thiazides","x":"All reduce renal lithium clearance, thiazides most of all; levels can climb within days. Warn patients against over-the-counter ibuprofen."},{"h":"Coarse tremor is a warning sign","x":"A fine tremor is a common side effect. A coarse tremor, unsteadiness, slurred speech or new confusion means toxicity, whatever the number says."},{"h":"Lithium and the ECG","x":"T-wave flattening is common and harmless. Toxicity can cause bradycardia, sinus node dysfunction and QT prolongation, so get an ECG with any high level."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Assess for lithium toxicity, withhold lithium, check renal function, and decide on acute medical referral."},"c-0-3-7":{"obj":["Refused a full-dose restart after more than 48 hours missed, and explained why to the nurse and the patient","Sent urgent FBC with neutrophils, CRP and troponin plus an ECG, and reported the FBC to the monitoring service","Screened for neutropenia, myocarditis and gut hypomotility with targeted questions and an abdominal check","Agreed a written re-titration schedule with the on-call pharmacist","Checked that clozapine appears on his T2 or T3 before any dose is given"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why can't I have my normal dose? I feel fine.\"","r":"\"I'm really glad you want it back. After three days off your body loses its tolerance, and the full dose could drop your blood pressure or cause a fit. Building up again keeps you safe.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"My throat's killing me and I'm shivery.\"","r":"\"That sounds miserable. On clozapine, a sore throat with fever needs a blood test today to check your infection-fighting cells. I'll arrange it now and get you something for the pain.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I stopped because of the drooling. It's embarrassing.\"","r":"\"Thanks for telling me. That's really common and it matters. Sleeping propped up and some medicines can help. Let's tackle it so it doesn't put you off again.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is the constipation a big deal? I'm used to it.\"","r":"\"With clozapine it can be serious, because the gut can stop moving altogether. I'd like to feel your tummy and start laxatives today. Tell us straight away about pain or vomiting.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not having another blood test.\"","r":"\"I understand, you've had loads. This one can't wait: fever on clozapine can mean your white cells are low. It's one tube, and it decides whether restarting is safe.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've stopped smoking since I came in.\"","r":"\"Well done, that's great for your health. It also matters for your dose, because stopping smoking raises clozapine levels a lot. We'll build that into the new plan and check a blood level.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Withhold clozapine; urgent FBC with differential, CRP, troponin and ECG today","Re-titrate per pharmacy: typically 12.5 mg once or twice on day 1, then rises of 25–50 mg a day as tolerated; check BP (lying and standing), pulse and temperature before doses","Constipation: senna 15 mg at night plus docusate 100 mg twice daily (or macrogol); never bulk-forming laxatives alone; daily bowel chart","Hypersalivation: hyoscine hydrobromide 300 micrograms up to three times daily via pharmacy if severe","Plasma clozapine level once on a stable dose, especially now he has stopped smoking"],"psych":["Explore why he stopped (drooling, sedation, beliefs); agree a plan he can live with","Named nurse gives written information on side effects to report"],"safety":["Red FBC (WBC below 3.0 or neutrophils below 1.5 ×10⁹/L): stop clozapine, same-day medical advice","Raised troponin or CRP, tachycardia with chest pain, or an acute abdomen: acute medical team or A&E today","Tell the consultant on Monday; record missed doses for the monitoring service"]},"facts":[{"h":"48 hours resets the clock","x":"Missed more than 48 hours: re-titrate from 12.5 mg. A full-dose restart risks hypotension, collapse, seizures and, rarely, cardiac arrest."},{"h":"Fever means FBC today","x":"Any sign of infection on clozapine needs an urgent FBC. Fever with a sore throat may be the first sign of agranulocytosis."},{"h":"Traffic-light blood results","x":"Green: continue. Amber (WBC 3.0–3.5 or neutrophils 1.5–2.0 ×10⁹/L): continue with twice-weekly tests. Red (WBC under 3.0 or neutrophils under 1.5): stop."},{"h":"Constipation can be fatal","x":"Clozapine slows the whole gut; ileus, obstruction and perforation have caused deaths. Ask about bowels at every review and treat early."},{"h":"Smoke, not nicotine, lowers levels","x":"Tobacco smoke induces CYP1A2. Clozapine levels can rise by about half after stopping smoking within a week or two; patches and vapes do not prevent this."},{"h":"Myocarditis comes early","x":"Mostly in the first two months of titration: fever, persistent tachycardia, chest pain, raised troponin and CRP. Re-titration warrants the same vigilance."}],"type":"Medication review","do":"Review missed clozapine doses, decide on retitration, and screen for neutropenia, myocarditis and constipation."},"c-0-3-8":{"obj":["Checked airway, GCS, pupils, neck, hips and limbs before she was moved","Recognised apixaban as the reason for CT head within 8 hours and arranged transfer to A&E","Started neurological observations at the NICE frequency","Screened for delirium with the 4AT and listed the causes to rule out","Arranged s17 escorted leave and a written handover, and informed her family"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Where am I? Who are you?\"","r":"\"You're safe. You're in hospital, it's the middle of the night and you've had a fall. I'm a doctor. Lie still for a moment while I check you're not hurt.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"My hip hurts.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Please don't try to move it. I'm going to look at your leg now; if it looks shortened or turned out, you'll need an X-ray at the hospital.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to go to another hospital.\"","r":"\"I understand, it's late and frightening. The scan is quick and checks for bleeding, because of your blood thinner. A nurse from here will stay with you the whole time.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Healthcare assistant: \"Can we just pop her back into bed?\"","r":"\"Not yet please. Keep her warm and still while I check her head, neck and hips. If nothing's broken we'll use the hoist or flat lifting device, never lift under her arms.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"Daughter: \"Why wasn't anyone watching her?\"","r":"\"I'm sorry this happened, and I understand you're upset. Falls can happen even with checks. We'll look carefully at how this happened and what to change, and you'll get an update first thing.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"A&E registrar: \"Couldn't this wait until the morning?\"","r":"\"She's on apixaban, which alone means CT within 8 hours under NICE, and she's GCS 14 against a usual 15. I'd like her seen now, please.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Neuro obs to NICE NG232: every 30 minutes until GCS is 15, then every 30 minutes for 2 hours, every hour for 4 hours, every 2 hours after that","CT head within 8 hours of injury; within 1 hour if any one-hour trigger appears; anticoagulant reversal is an A&E decision if there is a bleed","Delirium screen: FBC, U&E, calcium, glucose, CRP, LFT, TFT; urine culture only with symptoms; bladder scan; bowel chart","Review sedatives, antipsychotics, antihypertensives and anticholinergics; lying and standing BP"],"psych":["4AT now and daily against her baseline cognition","Reorientation, familiar staff and her own glasses and hearing aids"],"social":["Daughter informed tonight or first thing, as she prefers","Physio and OT for a full falls risk assessment (NICE NG249)"],"safety":["RC authorises s17 escorted leave; section papers, drug chart, ReSPECT form and baseline cognition go with her","Datix and post-fall huddle on the ward","Falls care plan: low bed, sensor mat, non-slip footwear, regular toileting rounds"]},"facts":[{"h":"Anticoagulant means CT within 8 hours","x":"An adult on an anticoagulant with a head injury and no other CT indication should have a CT head within 8 hours of the injury (NICE NG232)."},{"h":"One-hour CT triggers","x":"GCS under 13 at first check or under 15 at two hours, suspected skull fracture, post-injury seizure, focal deficit, or more than one episode of vomiting."},{"h":"Check hips before lifting","x":"A shortened, externally rotated leg or pain on hip movement means do not stand her up. Lift flat and arrange an X-ray."},{"h":"Dipsticks mislead over 65","x":"Bacteria in the urine is common and harmless in older adults. Do not diagnose UTI from a dipstick; send culture only with urinary symptoms or signs of infection."},{"h":"Lying and standing BP","x":"Lie her for 5 minutes, then measure at 1 and 3 minutes standing. A systolic drop of 20 or diastolic of 10 is orthostatic hypotension: review her drugs."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Assess her fall and head injury, apply CT head criteria given anticoagulants, and screen for delirium."},"c-0-3-9":{"obj":["Acknowledged his distress about the weight and agreed he will not stop olanzapine abruptly","Offered three options (lifestyle programme, switch to a lower-risk antipsychotic, metformin) and recorded his choice","Interpreted the HbA1c of 51 as above the diabetes threshold and planned a confirmatory repeat","Written to both GPs within a week with results, the plan and who monitors what","Calculated QRISK3 and booked dates for weight, waist, glucose and lipid checks"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I look disgusting. These pills have ruined me.\"","r":"\"I can hear how much this hurts. The weight gain is real and the medicine is causing it. You were right to tell us. Let's look at what we can change today.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm stopping them. I'd rather hear voices than be fat.\"","r":"\"That feeling makes sense. But stopping suddenly makes relapse much more likely. Give me a chance to change things first: some medicines cause far less weight gain.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If I switch, will the weight come off?\"","r":"\"Many people lose some weight after switching, though it's not guaranteed and takes months. Adding diet and activity support gives the best chance. We'll weigh you every month.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can I just take half the dose?\"","r":"\"I'd rather change things safely than guess. Half may not protect you from relapse and may not shift much weight. Let's agree a proper plan with the consultant this week.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"Diabetes? But I feel absolutely fine.\"","r":"\"Most people with early type 2 diabetes feel well, so it's easy to miss. One result just over the line needs repeating to confirm. If confirmed, your GP will start treatment.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will I have to come off quetiapine? It's the only thing that's worked.\"","r":"\"Not necessarily. It's done a lot for you, and that matters. Often the right choice is to treat the diabetes and keep the quetiapine. We'll decide together.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Option A: switch to aripiprazole 10 mg once daily in the morning (usual 10–15 mg, max 30 mg), cross-tapering olanzapine over 2–4 weeks","Option B: add metformin 500 mg with breakfast, raising by 500 mg every 1–2 weeks to 1 g twice daily; check eGFR first, warn about stomach upset","Monitoring (NICE CG178): weigh weekly for the first 6 weeks, again at 12 weeks, at one year, then annually; waist, BP, HbA1c and lipids at 12 weeks, one year, then annually","Woman: GP repeats HbA1c to confirm; if confirmed, metformin first line (NICE NG28); QRISK3 and lipids"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing on diet and activity in clinic","Dietitian or early intervention physical health worker; structured lifestyle programme"],"social":["Care coordinator books monthly weight and waist checks","GP exercise referral; NHS stop smoking service"],"safety":["Agree early warning signs of relapse before any switch; care coordinator contact weekly during cross-taper","Crisis line number given in case the voices return"]},"facts":[{"h":"HbA1c 48 is the line","x":"48 mmol/mol or more indicates diabetes; 42–47 is high risk. Without symptoms, confirm with a repeat. Unreliable in anaemia, haemoglobin variants, pregnancy or recent blood loss."},{"h":"Act on early weight gain","x":"A rise of 7% or more from baseline is clinically significant. Most olanzapine weight gain happens in the first months, so intervene then."},{"h":"Metabolic risk league table","x":"Highest with clozapine and olanzapine, moderate with quetiapine and risperidone, lowest with aripiprazole, lurasidone and amisulpride."},{"h":"Quitting smoking raises olanzapine","x":"Tobacco smoke induces CYP1A2. Stopping can raise olanzapine levels, adding sedation and weight gain. Tell the prescriber; the dose may need cutting."},{"h":"Annual SMI health check","x":"Everyone with severe mental illness should have a yearly check: BMI, BP, glucose or HbA1c, lipids, smoking and alcohol, shared with the GP."}],"type":"Medication review","do":"Review antipsychotic metabolic effects, agree a weight plan, and act on the raised HbA1c on quetiapine."},"c-0-4-1":{"obj":["Delivered the handover in under 3 minutes, highest risk first","Stated each patient's legal status and any change to leave","Named an owner and a deadline for every outstanding task","Recorded the handover in the notes and confirmed each incident has a Datix","Answered a challenge from the consultant without becoming defensive"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Consultant: \"Why wasn't she put on closer observations?\"","r":"\"Fair question. After the cut she was calm, denied ongoing intent and agreed a safety plan, so the nurse in charge and I chose 15-minute checks. I'd value your review of that today.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Consultant: \"That's a lot of detail. What do you need from me?\"","r":"\"Sorry, straight to it: please review Mr B's leave and Miss A's observation level today. Everything else is in hand.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Ward manager: \"Who's chasing Mr C's repeat obs and bloods?\"","r":"\"I will, by 12:00, and I'll update the nurse in charge. If anything is abnormal, I'll call the medical registrar straight away.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Consultant: \"Has anyone told Mr B's family about the AWOL?\"","r":"\"Not yet, thanks for picking that up. The nurse in charge will call his mother this morning and record it. Police were informed at the time and the Datix is done.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"Miss A, later: \"Everyone's talking about me now.\"","r":"\"You're right that we discussed what happened; keeping you safe is our job, not gossip. I'd like to hear how the weekend felt for you and what would help now.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"Mr B: \"Why can't I go out? I came back, didn't I?\"","r":"\"I know being stuck on the ward feels unfair. Your leave is paused until the consultant reviews it today. Tell me what happened on Sunday, so I can pass on your side.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Mr C: repeat NEWS2 today; if chest pain recurs, repeat ECG and medical review","Miss A: wound check and dressing; tetanus status recorded"],"psych":["Miss A: risk review with the consultant and updated safety plan with her named nurse today","Mr B: explore what led to the AWOL (drugs, distress, unmet need)"],"social":["Mr B: family informed; where he went and with whom recorded","Mr B: ward social worker checks whether housing or money worries drove the AWOL"],"safety":["Mr B: s17 leave suspended pending RC review; AWOL documented and Datix complete","Miss A: observation level confirmed by the consultant; room search per policy","Every outstanding task on the ward list with an owner and a time"]},"facts":[{"h":"Lead with risk, not chronology","x":"Open with how many issues, then go highest risk first: self-harm, absconsion, physical deterioration. A chronological story buries the action."},{"h":"Every task needs an owner","x":"'Bloods pending' is not a handover. Say who chases what and by when, and hear them accept it."},{"h":"Legal status in every line","x":"Say informal, s2, s3 or CTO each time, plus any leave change. Leave for detained patients is the responsible clinician's decision."},{"h":"AWOL on s2","x":"A patient absent without leave can be taken into custody and returned while the section is still in force, under s18 of the MHA."},{"h":"SBAR keeps it tight","x":"Situation in one line, only relevant background, your assessment, then a clear request: 'I need you to review his leave today.'"}],"type":"Handover and escalation","do":"Give a prioritised SBAR handover of weekend events to the consultant in about three minutes."},"c-0-4-2":{"obj":["Let her speak uninterrupted at first, then summarised her worries back to her","Recorded the risk information she gave about his leave in the notes the same evening","Explained confidentiality without hiding behind it: what you can share now and what needs his consent","Asked the son that evening about sharing information and recorded his answer","Arranged a consultant or care coordinator call within 2 working days and given carers' support details"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Nobody ever tells me anything! I'm his mother!\"","r":"\"You're clearly worried, and it sounds like you've felt shut out. I want to hear what's been happening. Tell me everything; I can always take in what you share and record it.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"When he came home he said the TV was sending him messages. He didn't sleep.\"","r":"\"Thank you, that's exactly what we need to know. I'll write it in his notes tonight and tell the nurse in charge so his leave is reviewed before he goes out again.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So you won't even tell me what tablets he's on?\"","r":"\"I know that's frustrating. He's asked us not to share details for now, and I have to respect that. I'll ask him again tonight, and if he agrees, we'll talk.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"What if he kills himself? Would you tell me then?\"","r":"\"That's a frightening thought, and I'm glad you said it. If there were a serious risk to his life, we could share what's needed to keep him safe. Has he said anything that worries you?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm going to put in a complaint about all of you.\"","r":"\"That's your right, and I'll give you the PALS details. I'd also like to fix what's gone wrong now: I'll ask his consultant to call you within two working days.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm exhausted. I can't keep doing this on my own.\"","r":"\"That sounds really hard, and you've been carrying a lot. You're able to ask for a carer's assessment, and there's a local carers' service. You don't have to do this alone.\""}],"bps":{"psych":["Ask him why he doesn't want his family told; revisit consent regularly","Offer family intervention once he agrees: at least 10 sessions over 3 months to a year (NICE CG178)"],"social":["Carer's assessment by the local authority (Care Act 2014); local carers' centre details","General information she can have now: psychosis leaflets, visiting times, how leave decisions are made","PALS contact details"],"safety":["Her report recorded tonight; nurse in charge informed; consultant reviews leave before the next leave","His consent decision recorded: what may be shared, with whom","If serious risk to him or others emerges, share the minimum necessary and record the reasoning"]},"facts":[{"h":"Listening never breaches confidentiality","x":"You can always receive information from relatives. Hearing it, recording it and acting on it needs no consent."},{"h":"General information is shareable","x":"Without consent you can still explain what psychosis is, how the ward works, how leave is decided and how carers get support."},{"h":"Consent can change","x":"Refusal to share is often tied to paranoia or a bad day. Ask again, and ask what he would allow: medication only, leave plans, or nothing."},{"h":"Risk can override refusal","x":"Disclosure without consent is justified to prevent serious harm to him or others (GMC guidance). Share the minimum and record why."},{"h":"Triangle of Care","x":"Six standards: identify carers early, train staff, agreed practice on confidentiality, a named carer lead, an introduction to the service, and carer support."}],"type":"Handling a complaint","do":"Listen to the mother's concerns, protect her son's confidentiality, and agree clear next steps."},"c-0-4-3":{"obj":["Checked what she already understands and how much she wants to know before naming the diagnosis","Given a warning shot, used the word 'schizophrenia' clearly, then paused","Responded to her emotional reaction and asked about thoughts of self-harm before ending","Explained treatment (medication, CBT for psychosis, family intervention) and a realistic, hopeful outlook","Agreed follow-up within a week and given written information and crisis numbers"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"No. I'm not schizophrenic. I'm not crazy.\"","r":"\"It's a heavy word, and many people react that way. It doesn't make you 'crazy' or define who you are. It's a name for what you've been going through, so we can treat it.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Does this mean I'll be like this forever?\"","r":"\"Not necessarily. Many people recover well, especially with early treatment like this. Some have one episode, others need longer support. We'll give you the best chance.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I have to take tablets for the rest of my life?\"","r":"\"Not necessarily. We usually advise continuing for one to two years after you're well, then reviewing together. Stopping too early makes it more likely to come back.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"Mother: \"Did I cause this? Was it how I brought her up?\"","r":"\"No, parenting doesn't cause schizophrenia. It comes from a mix of genes, brain development and stress. Being here and supporting her is exactly the right thing.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I still finish uni and have kids one day?\"","r":"\"Yes, many people do. The team can help you plan around your studies. If you're thinking about pregnancy later, we'll choose medicines with that in mind.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"(Starts crying) \"I just can't deal with this.\"","r":"\"Take your time; there's no rush. This is a lot to hear, and we don't have to cover everything today. What's going through your mind right now?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Antipsychotic chosen with her after discussing side effects (NICE CG178), e.g. aripiprazole 10 mg once daily in the morning (range 10–30 mg)","Baseline weight, waist, BP, pulse, HbA1c, lipids and prolactin; ECG if indicated; weight weekly for 6 weeks"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, at least 16 one-to-one sessions, EIP psychologist","Family intervention with her mother, at least 10 sessions over 3 months to a year","Psychoeducation group run by the EIP team"],"social":["EIP care coordinator follow-up within 1 week","Individual placement and support (IPS) worker to protect her studies","Carer's assessment for her mother by the local authority"],"safety":["Ask about suicidal thoughts today and record the answer","Written crisis plan: crisis team phone number, plus NHS 111 (choose the mental health option) out of hours","GP letter the same week with diagnosis, plan and risk summary"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask before you tell","x":"Start with 'What have you made of things so far?' and 'How much detail would you like today?' Then pitch the diagnosis at her level."},{"h":"Warning shot, then the word","x":"Signal difficult news, say 'schizophrenia' plainly, then stop talking. Silence lets her react; rushing to reassure shuts her down."},{"h":"Suicide risk is highest early","x":"The first years after a psychosis diagnosis carry raised suicide risk, especially in young people with good insight. Always ask before she leaves."},{"h":"Talk to her, not her mother","x":"Keep eye contact and questions with the patient. Bring her mother in by inviting her questions at the end, with her daughter's agreement."},{"h":"NICE psychological offer","x":"Offer CBTp (16 or more sessions) to everyone with first-episode psychosis, and family intervention (10 or more) to families in close contact."}],"type":"Breaking bad news","do":"Break the schizophrenia diagnosis to her with her mother present, check understanding and plan next steps."},"c-0-4-4":{"obj":["Reported the incident on the trust system before the end of your shift, with times and facts only, no opinions","Agreed with your consultant who opens the candour conversation with the family, and by when today","Left yesterday's entry untouched and added any new note dated, timed and marked as written after the event","Drafted a first-person factual statement and had it read by your supervisor or defence organisation before sending","Booked a debrief and named one person you will talk to this week about how you are coping"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"He was supposed to be checked every fifteen minutes. How did this happen on your ward?\"","r":"You are right to be angry, and I am sorry this happened while he was in our care. I do not have all the answers yet. The trust is reviewing it properly, and we will share what we find with you."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Are you going to be straight with us, or just close ranks?\"","r":"That is a fair worry. We have a legal duty to be open with you. You will get an apology and a written account, and you can ask questions at every stage. Who should we keep updated?"},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have known. He sounded odd when I rang him on Sunday.\"","r":"That sounds heavy to carry, and many families feel this afterwards. It is not your fault. Would you tell me what he said on Sunday? It may help us understand what was going on for him."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Someone saw him yesterday and said he was fine. Was that you?\"","r":"Yes, I saw him yesterday afternoon, and I am truly sorry. I wrote down what he told me then. The review will look at everything, including my assessment, and I will be honest with them and with you."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I'd planned it for days. I just told you what you wanted to hear.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me now; that takes courage, and I am glad you are still here. What made it hard to say yesterday? I want us to find a way you can tell us next time."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will someone lose their job over this?\"","r":"The review is about learning, not blaming one person. It looks at the whole picture: observations, the room, how we communicated. If something needs to change, it will, and we will tell you what changed."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Don't tell my family anything about what I did.\"","r":"I hear you. Can you tell me what worries you about them knowing? We must be open with you about what happened. You can decide who else is told, and how much, and I will respect that where I can."}],"bps":{"bio":["Medical team documents neuro observations, airway and neck injury findings and any hypoxic injury before transfer back.","Consultant reviews his medication chart before he returns; no new PRN added without that review."],"psych":["Consultant or senior reassesses suicidal intent on the medical ward within 24 hours, from his own account, not yesterday's entry.","Ward manager runs a hot debrief today including night staff; after-action review or learning response agreed by patient safety team.","You: meet educational supervisor this week; self-refer to Practitioner Health or occupational health if sleep or mood suffer beyond two weeks."],"social":["Consultant leads the candour conversation; trust sends written follow-up with apology and named contact.","Ward manager names one family contact with a direct phone number and agreed update times."],"safety":["Estates make the ligature point safe today; ward ligature risk assessment reviewed by ward manager.","Observation level on his return set by the MDT (eyesight or arm's length), with reasons recorded.","MHA office and patient safety lead informed; they decide on regulatory notification.","Nurse in charge checks patients who witnessed it; watch for copycat self-harm over the next week."]},"facts":[{"h":"Candour is a legal duty","x":"Regulation 20: for moderate harm, severe harm or death, tell the patient (or family acting for him) as soon as reasonably practicable, apologise, explain what is known, then confirm in writing."},{"h":"Sorry is not an admission","x":"The Compensation Act 2006 says an apology is not in itself an admission of negligence. GMC and NMC guidance expects you to apologise when things go wrong."},{"h":"Never edit the old entry","x":"New notes carry today's date and time and 'written retrospectively'. Changing records after an incident is a probity issue that ends careers, even if the added line is true."},{"h":"PSIRF replaced serious incident reports","x":"NHS trusts now respond under the Patient Safety Incident Response Framework: proportionate learning responses focused on systems. Ask which response type applies and what is needed from you."},{"h":"How to write a statement","x":"First person, dated, your role, sources (notes or memory), times, what you saw, did and said. No opinions about colleagues, no guesses. Keep a copy; call your defence organisation before any inquest."},{"h":"Practitioner Health is free","x":"Confidential NHS service for doctors in England; refer yourself online. Distress after a serious incident is common, and early support speeds recovery."}],"type":"Documentation and reporting","do":"Submit a Datix, start duty of candour with the family, write a factual statement, and seek support."},"c-0-4-5":{"obj":["Checked the officer's identity and asked for the request in writing, with its legal basis, before confirming anything","Asked Mr C what he is willing to share, with whom, and recorded his answer in his own words","Listened to his mother's account and offered a carer's assessment, without confirming or ruling out a diagnosis","Recorded each request, your decision, the reason and who you consulted (consultant, information governance or Caldicott Guardian)"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I'm his mother! I have a right to know what's wrong with my own son.\"","r":"I can hear how frightened you are, and how much you care. He is an adult, so I need his permission to share details. What I can do is listen now, and ask him today about all of us talking together."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"You don't have to say it. Just don't say no if I'm right.\"","r":"I wish I could make this easier. I cannot confirm or rule anything out without his agreement. I can explain how the ward works and what support there is for families. Would that help?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He's been messaging his ex saying she'll pay. I'm scared he'll hurt her.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me; that is important and you were right to say it. I will pass it to the team today, and if there is a risk to her we may need to act. Can you show us the messages?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I don't know what it is, how can I help him when he comes home?\"","r":"That is a fair point. If he agrees, you will be part of planning his discharge. Even if not, you can tell us your worries, and we can arrange a carer's assessment and give you crisis numbers."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want the police knowing I'm in a mental hospital.\"","r":"That is your right, and I will respect it unless the law requires otherwise. They want to speak to you as a witness. Would you be willing to meet them here, without us sharing anything medical?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can the police just read my notes?\"","r":"No. They need your agreement or a legal order, such as from a court. If that ever happens, we share only what is needed, and I would tell you unless the law prevented it."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"My mum keeps ringing. Just tell her to back off.\"","r":"It sounds like her calls are hard for you. She is worried about you. We will not share anything you do not agree to. Would it help if we told her only that you are safe?"}],"bps":{"social":["Ward social worker or nurse in charge offers his mother a Care Act carer's assessment via the local authority and local carers' group details.","Named nurse revisits consent to share with family at each ward round; his wishes recorded in the care plan.","If he agrees to see police: interview on the ward at a time the responsible clinician confirms he is well enough, with a trusted nurse present if he wishes."],"safety":["Mother's report of threats: tell the consultant today, update the risk plan and decide whether public-interest disclosure to police or the ex-partner is justified.","Information governance logs the police request and replies in writing; copy of outcome filed in notes.","Review consent and risk at the next ward round (within 48 hours)."]},"facts":[{"h":"Police have no automatic right","x":"Data protection law permits disclosure to police for preventing or detecting crime but never obliges it. Without consent, a court order or a serious-harm reason, say you cannot confirm."},{"h":"Public interest needs serious harm","x":"GMC: disclosure without consent can be justified to prevent or detect serious crime (murder, rape, serious assault) or serious harm. A witness inquiry rarely meets that bar."},{"h":"Confirming admission is disclosure","x":"Even 'he is on this ward' reveals mental health care. Phone identity cannot be verified: take a number and call back through a known switchboard."},{"h":"Listening is never a breach","x":"You can always take information from relatives, give general information about illnesses and services, and explain how the ward works. Say what you can do, not only what you cannot."},{"h":"AWOL detained patients differ","x":"If a detained patient goes missing, sharing what police need to find and return him (description, risks, legal status) is lawful. Not the full history."},{"h":"Use the Caldicott Guardian","x":"Every NHS trust has one. For non-urgent police requests, involve them or information governance, and record their advice alongside your reasons."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"Handle the police and relative requests within GMC confidentiality guidance, recording what you share and why."},"c-0-4-6":{"obj":["Accepted the card, declined the scarf and personal number warmly, and gave one clear reason","Explored her fear about leaving and turned it into a concrete plan for the first 72 hours","Given her a written crisis plan with the crisis team number, NHS 111 option 2 and her care coordinator's name","Left the social media request unanswered, tightened your privacy settings and told your supervisor the same day","Recorded the offer and your response in the notes and, if required, the trust gifts register"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Please. You're the only one who actually gets me. What if I need you at 3am?\"","r":"I am really glad our talks helped. At 3am you need someone who can act straight away, and that cannot be me. Let us write down who will answer, and what you can do while you wait."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So it was all fake? You were just doing your job?\"","r":"I can see that hurts. My care about how you are doing is real. Keeping things professional is what lets me and the team help you safely, and that does not stop because you are going home."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just accept me on Instagram. I won't message you, I promise.\"","r":"I believe you mean that. I keep my social media separate from work for every patient, so it is not about you. I will not accept it, and I hope you can see why."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"If you won't take the scarf, I'm not taking it back.\"","r":"That is generous of you. I still cannot accept it. Please keep it or give it to someone close to you. The card I will keep, and it means a lot."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Everyone leaves me. The team will forget about me once I'm out.\"","r":"That fear makes sense after what you have been through. You will not be forgotten: your care coordinator will see you within 72 hours. Let us put the day and time on your plan right now."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I come back to the ward just to say hi to you?\"","r":"I understand wanting to keep a link. Coming back to the ward will not help your recovery. Your care coordinator is your link now, and they can pass any message to the team."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Another doctor gave his number to someone. Why are you different?\"","r":"I cannot speak for others, and I will mention it to the team. My answer is the same for everyone: no personal numbers. What matters is that you can reach help quickly, so here is how."}],"bps":{"bio":["Discharge medicines in small supplies (7 days) if overdose risk, with GP told; no new psychotropic started on discharge day.","Crisis sedation only short term: NICE advises no longer than 1 week (eg promethazine 25 mg at night), reviewed by GP or care coordinator."],"psych":["CMHT personality disorder pathway: DBT (individual plus skills group, about 12 months) or MBT; referral sent before discharge.","Three distress-tolerance skills she has used on the ward written onto her crisis card."],"social":["Care coordinator: follow-up within 72 hours, then agreed weekly contact; responds consistently if she contacts staff personally.","Ward manager logs the gift offer under trust policy."],"safety":["Tell your educational supervisor today; note entry records what was offered and what you said.","Crisis plan: crisis team, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, plus two people she trusts.","If personal contact continues: care coordinator addresses it in a planned way; you never reply."]},"facts":[{"h":"Gifts must not influence care","x":"GMC: do not accept gifts that could affect, or be seen to affect, how you treat patients. Cards and small tokens are usually fine; follow the trust register threshold."},{"h":"Personal number: never","x":"Once given it cannot be taken back, and crisis calls land on someone off duty and unable to act. Always route out-of-hours need to services that can respond."},{"h":"Social media: no response","x":"Do not accept, reply or message. Set accounts private, remove your workplace from profiles and tell your supervisor. GMC boundary guidance covers online contact."},{"h":"Boundaries outlast discharge","x":"GMC guidance applies to former patients. Psychiatric patients are seen as especially vulnerable, so any personal relationship after discharge is very likely to be improper."},{"h":"72-hour follow-up after discharge","x":"Suicide risk peaks in the first days after leaving hospital. The NHS standard is contact within 72 hours; write the date on her plan before she leaves."},{"h":"Crossings drift into violations","x":"Warning signs: extra time for one patient, secrecy, feeling special, sharing your own problems. If you would hide it from the team, stop and take it to supervision."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"Decline the number, gift and online contact kindly but clearly, and document and plan follow-up."},"c-0-4-7":{"obj":["Triaged all five calls by risk within five minutes and told each caller when you will arrive","Instructed Ward 2 to call 999 (or the medical emergency team) and start ABCDE before you got there","Escalated to the registrar or consultant in one SBAR call, stating exactly what help you need","Confirmed the Section 136 arrival time, physical health check and AMHP contact, and noted the 24-hour deadline","Recorded times and actions for every call and handed over at the end of the shift"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Three hours I've been sat here. Nobody cares if I live or die.\"","r":"I am sorry you have waited so long; that is hard when you are struggling. I do care. Are you safe right now? I will make sure someone sees you, and I will tell you roughly when."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking any tablets. Get away from me!\"","r":"OK, I am stepping back. You look really angry, and I want to understand. Nobody is going to rush you. What has happened tonight? There is medicine that can help you feel calmer, if you want it."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Last time you lot held me face down and jabbed me. I'll fight.\"","r":"That sounds frightening, and I am sorry it happened. We do not want to hold anyone face down. If you can take something by mouth, we can avoid an injection. What would help you feel safer now?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"I just need a sleeping tablet. Why does that need a doctor?\"","r":"Fair question. Sleeping tablets need a prescription, and I want to check it is safe with your other medicines. I am at an emergency now; the nurse will try a few things and I will see you within the hour."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not mad. The police had no right to bring me here.\"","r":"I hear you, and you are not in trouble. Police can bring someone here when they are worried. Our job is to listen and check how you are. First, are you hurt, or have you taken anything tonight?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"If you send me home tonight, I'll do it.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me straight; I am taking it seriously. Nobody will send you home before a full assessment. Can you stay with the nurse while I arrange it? Do you have anything on you that you could use to hurt yourself?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"The nurse said Dad's gone to A&E. Is he dying?\"","r":"I understand this is frightening. He became physically unwell tonight and needed more medical care than we can give here, so he went quickly. He is in the right place, and I will make sure they have your number."}],"bps":{"bio":["Ward 2: 999 on a standalone site; nurse stays with him, ABCDE, oxygen to target saturations, capillary glucose, observations repeated at least every 15 minutes.","PICU: offer oral first (lorazepam 1-2 mg or promethazine 25-50 mg). If rapid tranquillisation needed: IM lorazepam 1-2 mg, or IM haloperidol 5 mg plus IM promethazine 50 mg only with a known normal ECG.","After IM medicine: pulse, BP, respiratory rate, temperature, SpO2 and consciousness at least hourly; every 15 minutes if asleep, intoxicated or above BNF maximum.","Ward 4: no new hypnotic by phone; check PRN chart and caffeine. If needed after review, zopiclone 3.75-7.5 mg at bedtime, short course only."],"psych":["PICU: one staff member leads verbal de-escalation, reduce noise and audience, offer choices.","A&E: brief phone risk review with the A&E nurse; liaison or the senior on call takes the full assessment."],"social":["136 suite: AMHP and Section 12 doctor contacted on arrival; AMHP identifies nearest relative.","Ward 2: nurse in charge informs next of kin and sends a transfer letter with medicines list."],"safety":["Ring the registrar or consultant as soon as you have triaged, not after you are stuck.","A&E woman on continuous observation in a safe room while she waits.","136: record arrival time; 24-hour limit, extendable by up to 12 hours only if his condition prevents assessment.","End of shift: written handover; exception report if hours overran or breaks were missed; debrief with your supervisor."]},"facts":[{"h":"NEWS2 7+ means emergency response","x":"A score of 7 or more needs emergency assessment by a team with critical care skills and continuous monitoring. On a standalone psychiatric site that means 999 now."},{"h":"Rapid tranquillisation needs monitoring","x":"Lorazepam or haloperidol plus promethazine IM, per NICE. Never leave someone unobserved afterwards; have flumazenil available after lorazepam and an ECG before haloperidol."},{"h":"The Section 136 clock","x":"24 hours from arrival at the place of safety. A doctor can extend by up to 12 hours if his condition prevents assessment. Rule out physical problems before the mental health assessment."},{"h":"Call the senior early","x":"Seniors expect the call. One SBAR call: who you are, the problems ranked, what you have done, and what you need: advice, attendance or taking over a task."},{"h":"New confusion is delirium first","x":"Older adult, new confusion, high NEWS2: sepsis, hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, retention or drug toxicity until proven otherwise. Do not sedate before finding the cause."},{"h":"Give every caller a time","x":"'I will be there in about 40 minutes; bleep me if anything changes' reduces repeat bleeps and keeps staff watching for deterioration."}],"type":"Handover and escalation","do":"Prioritise all five bleeps by clinical urgency and escalate to the senior on call at the right time."},"c-1-1-1":{"obj":["Built a day-by-day timeline from the flatmates, anchored to the last day he was completely himself","Screened for catatonia with a structured scale and recorded his fluid intake and urine output","Had bloods (including CK, U&E, glucose, TFT, calcium), a urine drug screen and an ECG sent before agreeing he is medically fit","Listed in the notes what is still unknown, who will find it out and by when","Agreed tonight's plan with the on-call senior: where he stays, how he is observed and the legal framework if he tries to leave"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why is everyone looking at me? Did someone send you?\"","r":"I can see that feels unsettling. Nobody sent me except the hospital team; I am a doctor here to help. People are looking because they are worried about you. What has this week been like?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I can't eat that. You don't know what's in it.\"","r":"That sounds frightening, not knowing if food is safe. Thank you for telling me. Would a sealed bottle of water that you open yourself feel OK? I want your body to get what it needs."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"Nothing's wrong with me. I just need to work things out.\"","r":"It sounds like a lot is going on in your head, and I want to help you work it out. I would also like to rule out anything physical that makes thinking harder. Can I take some blood and do a heart tracing?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is he going to get sectioned? We don't want to get him in trouble.\"","r":"You did the right thing bringing him; he is not in trouble. We will try to agree a plan with him first. What you tell me about the last week helps us get that plan right."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He's been smoking a lot of that spice stuff since term ended.\"","r":"Thank you, that is really useful. Standard urine tests often miss synthetic cannabinoids, so what you saw matters. Do you know when he last used, and how much?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"His mum's on the phone from home. What do we tell her?\"","r":"It is good she knows. If he agrees, I would like to speak to her myself; she knows his history best. Could you ask for her number and whether she can stay reachable tonight?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods now: FBC, U&E, LFT, CRP, glucose, TFT, calcium, CK; urine drug screen; ECG with QTc; fluid balance chart started.","Catatonia screen (Bush-Francis). If positive: lorazepam 1-2 mg oral or IM, re-rate at 30 minutes; continue regular lorazepam (off-label) with senior advice, watching for over-sedation.","Hold antipsychotics until catatonia, NMS risk and organic causes are excluded. Before any start: weight, BP, pulse, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, ECG.","Raised CK, fever or rigidity: medical team leads; IV fluids per A&E."],"psych":["EIP referral tonight: CBT for psychosis (at least 16 sessions, EIP psychologist) and family intervention (at least 10 sessions) offered within 2 weeks.","Low-stimulus observed room; one consistent clinician; short, concrete sentences."],"social":["With consent, call his parents tonight for history; if he refuses, still take information from them and record why.","Next working day, with consent: university wellbeing or disability service to pause deadlines."],"safety":["Stays in an observed A&E area (within eyesight) until results are back and the plan is agreed with the on-call registrar.","Capacity for bloods and fluids assessed and recorded; if he lacks it, physical care given under the MCA in his best interests.","If admission is needed and he refuses: on-call senior and AMHP arrange MHA assessment."]},"facts":[{"h":"Catatonia: test, do not guess","x":"Use the Bush-Francis screening items. Two or more positive signs: lorazepam challenge 1-2 mg, re-rate at 30 minutes. Clear improvement supports catatonia."},{"h":"Antipsychotics can tip catatonia into NMS","x":"Check CK, temperature, rigidity and autonomic signs first. Fever, rigidity and raised CK together are a medical emergency: stop antipsychotics, involve the medical team."},{"h":"Spice hides from urine screens","x":"Most synthetic cannabinoids are missed by standard drug screens. Ask flatmates what they have seen: packets, vapes, behaviour after use."},{"h":"EIP within two weeks","x":"NHS standard: NICE-recommended care for first-episode psychosis starts within 2 weeks of referral, ages 14-65. Refer even when the diagnosis is still uncertain."},{"h":"'Medically fit' needs results","x":"Normal observations an hour ago do not exclude dehydration, low sodium, raised CK or intoxication. Write which results are pending and who reviews them."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Build a dated timeline of the change, list unknowns, and agree a safe plan for tonight."},"c-1-1-2":{"obj":["Written her baseline (when well) and the current change as two separate, specific statements in the clerking","Confirmed the date, drug and dose of her last depot from the depot clinic record","Named at least one specific reason for this relapse and linked it to an action","Completed medicines reconciliation within 24 hours and booked bloods, ECG and physical observations","Recorded her relapse signature in the care plan and invited her care coordinator to the first review"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm fine. The home team overreacted. I want to go home.\"","r":"I am glad you feel OK in yourself. The team was worried enough to bring you in, so let us check together. What is the first thing that would tell you, or your family, you were getting unwell?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why another blood test? I had loads last time.\"","r":"Fair question. Your injection and other medicines can affect sugar, cholesterol and heart rhythm, and some changes since you left could be physical. It is a quick check that keeps you safer."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I stopped the injection. It made me put on so much weight.\"","r":"Thank you for being honest; that really helps. Weight gain is a real and upsetting side effect. Let us look at options together, such as a different medicine or weight support, so treatment works for you."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Every time I come in, you lot read the old notes and decide what's wrong with me.\"","r":"You are right that notes do not tell the whole story, which is why I want to hear it from you. What do people get wrong about you when they only read the file?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I say the voices are worse, will you keep me in for months?\"","r":"I understand that worry. Telling me helps us treat it sooner, which usually means a shorter stay. You are here informally, so you have a say. What are the voices saying now?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"My neighbour keeps knocking at night and taking my money.\"","r":"That sounds frightening, and thank you for telling me. Nobody should take your money. I will tell the team today, and our social worker can help keep you safe. Has this been happening since you got home?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Depot: confirm last dose from the depot card. Monthly paliperidone more than 6 weeks late usually needs re-starting with two injections a week apart; check the SPC before prescribing.","Within 24-72 hours: weight, BMI, waist, BP, pulse; FBC, U&E (sodium), LFT, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, TFT; ECG for QTc.","If on lithium, level 12 hours post-dose now; if on clozapine, trough level and ask about any change in smoking.","Medicines reconciliation within 24 hours with the ward pharmacist, using GP record, Summary Care Record and depot clinic."],"psych":["Named nurse completes relapse signature work with her: three early signs, each with an action.","If side effects drove non-adherence: rate them (eg GASS) and agree a switch of depot or oral option with her."],"social":["Care coordinator attends the first ward review and owns the discharge plan.","Ward social worker: housing, benefits and money check; adult safeguarding referral if financial exploitation is confirmed.","Depot venue and reminder agreed (text reminder or CMHT nurse home visit)."],"safety":["Informal: capacity to agree to admission recorded; if she tries to leave while at risk, nurse or doctor holding powers (s5(4), s5(2)).","Observation level set at admission, reviewed daily by the MDT.","Discharge: follow-up within 72 hours; crisis plan shared with home treatment team."]},"facts":[{"h":"Baseline is not 'normal'","x":"Write 'When well:' (lives alone, cooks, quiet voices daily, day centre twice a week), then 'Now:'. The gap between the two lines is what you are treating."},{"h":"Paliperidone missed-dose rule","x":"Monthly paliperidone 4-6 weeks late: give as soon as possible. Over 6 weeks: re-start with two injections a week apart. Over 6 months: full loading again. Confirm with the SPC."},{"h":"Low sodium mimics relapse","x":"Polydipsia, SSRIs and carbamazepine can drop sodium, causing confusion, unsteadiness or fits that look like relapse. Check U&E on every readmission."},{"h":"Reconcile within 24 hours","x":"NICE expects medicines reconciliation within 24 hours of admission from at least two sources: GP record, Summary Care Record, depot clinic or her own supply."},{"h":"A quick readmission is a clue","x":"Back within a month means part of the plan failed. Name the cause in one line (missed depot, no 72-hour contact, money, substances) and fix it before discharge."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Clerk her readmission, stating her usual baseline, what has changed, and why."},"c-1-1-3":{"obj":["Taken a dated timeline from his wife covering sleep, spending, speech, plans and the episode at 25","Asked about family history, substances, steroids and thyroid problems, and recorded each answer","Escalated to the consultant the same day with a revised working diagnosis and the antidepressant question","Asked him for consent to share with his wife and recorded his answer, while still taking her information","Offered his wife a carer's assessment and practical help to protect their money"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You put him on antidepressants without even asking me what he's like!\"","r":"I understand why you are upset. What you are telling me is really important and may change his treatment today. I will speak to the consultant this morning. Can you tell me more about the episode at 25?"},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Is this my fault? I should have told someone years ago.\"","r":"No, it is not your fault. Many people do not see a high as an illness, especially the person having it. You are telling us now, and that is what matters. How have you been coping?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He's taken out a loan in my name. We could lose the house.\"","r":"That sounds really frightening; thank you for telling me. Our social worker can help, and there is specialist debt advice. Shall we talk about protecting your accounts while he is unwell?"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not ill. My idea will make us millions. She's just jealous.\"","r":"It sounds like this plan really matters to you, and I am not here to judge it. I have noticed you are sleeping very little, which affects anyone's thinking. How many hours did you sleep last night?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Don't tell my wife anything.\"","r":"I will respect that and not share your details without permission. She may still tell us what she has noticed, and we will listen. Can I ask what worries you about her being involved?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should he even be driving? He's been going really fast.\"","r":"That is a real concern. While someone is this unwell they should not drive, and certain conditions must be reported to the DVLA. I will discuss it with him and the team and record the advice."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So what is it? Bipolar?\"","r":"What you have described may well change how we understand this. I cannot discuss his diagnosis without his agreement, but I will ask him today if the three of us can meet."}],"bps":{"bio":["Stop sertraline today after consultant review; two days' use needs no taper.","Antipsychotic for mania: olanzapine 10-15 mg at night (max 20 mg), or quetiapine 100 mg day 1 rising to 400 mg by day 4 in two doses, or risperidone 2 mg daily. Warn: sedation, weight gain, dizziness.","Baseline before long-term choice: weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids, U&E, eGFR, TFT, calcium, ECG; urine drug screen with consent.","Long term: lithium first line, level 0.6-0.8 mmol/L 12 hours post-dose. Do not start valproate without two-specialist agreement (MHRA, under 55)."],"psych":["Nursing staff chart sleep and mood every shift; Young Mania Rating Scale weekly.","After the acute phase: structured psychological relapse-prevention work for bipolar disorder, ideally including his wife."],"social":["Ward social worker: debt advice; wife contacts the bank's vulnerable-customer team about joint accounts and the loan.","Carer's assessment for his wife via the local authority; ward carers' information pack today."],"safety":["Raise observation level; mixed states carry high suicide risk: nurses ask directly about suicidal thoughts each shift.","Advise him not to drive; record that advice and his duty to tell the DVLA.","Ask his wife privately about threats, violence or coercion; IDVA or safeguarding referral if needed."]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask about need for sleep","x":"'Do you feel tired after so little sleep?' Energised points to mania; exhausted points to depressive insomnia. One question with big diagnostic weight."},{"h":"Antidepressants in emerging mania","x":"NICE: if mania or hypomania develops on an antidepressant, consider stopping it and offer an antipsychotic. Never add a second antidepressant for 'agitated depression' before excluding bipolarity."},{"h":"Valproate: two specialists","x":"MHRA (2024): nobody under 55 should start valproate unless two specialists independently agree no other treatment is suitable. Pregnancy prevention rules still apply to women."},{"h":"Lithium level timing","x":"12 hours after the dose. Target 0.6-0.8 mmol/L. Check weekly after starting and after each dose change until stable, then every 3 months in the first year."},{"h":"Confidentiality limits telling, not hearing","x":"You can always listen to relatives. Record the source of collateral, and tell the patient where safe that family has been in touch."}],"type":"Collateral history","do":"Take a focused collateral history from his wife and decide whether the working diagnosis should change."},"c-1-2-1":{"obj":["Mapped the length, triggers and pattern of her mood changes, separating hours from days or weeks","Asked directly about at least four days of elevated mood with less need for sleep, and about first-rank symptoms","Asked about trauma without requiring details, and checked whether anyone, including children, is at risk now","Agreed a provisional formulation in plain words with her, and the three steps to firm it up","Agreed a written safety plan with her warning signs, two coping steps, two named contacts and crisis numbers"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I've had three diagnoses already. Why should I believe yours?\"","r":"I would feel the same. Different labels may mean nobody had the whole picture. I would rather understand your pattern than add a fourth label. Can we map out how your moods actually go?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So have I got bipolar or not?\"","r":"Honestly, I do not think so yet. Bipolar usually means separate highs and lows lasting days or weeks. Yours sound shorter and linked to people. A month of mood diary will help us both be sure."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I tell you about the voices, you'll say I'm schizophrenic.\"","r":"Many people hear voices, especially under stress or after hard experiences, and most do not have schizophrenia. Knowing what yours are like helps me understand. Where do they seem to come from?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"My stepdad... things happened when I was little. I've never told anyone.\"","r":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. You do not need to give details today, and it was not your fault. Is he still in your life, and could anyone, including children, be at risk from him now?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want a personality disorder label. People stop helping you.\"","r":"I understand; that label has been used unfairly. A diagnosis should open help, not close it. Whatever we call it, there are therapies that work well. What kind of help matters most to you?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Sometimes I cut just to feel something. Last night was deeper.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. It sounds like the pain inside gets unbearable. Can I look at the wound to check it is healing? Then let us talk about last night and what might help next time."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just give me something to stop the mood swings. Anything.\"","r":"I can hear how exhausting this is. Tablets alone rarely settle swings like these, and I do not want to give you side effects without benefit. Therapy does help. Meanwhile, let us plan for the worst days."}],"bps":{"bio":["No mood stabiliser or antipsychotic for the label alone (NICE); review current medicines and stop any without clear benefit.","Crisis only: short-term sedative (eg promethazine 25 mg at night) for no longer than 1 week, agreed with her GP.","Small prescription supplies (weekly) if overdose risk; drug screen and bloods only if substances or a physical cause of voices is suspected."],"psych":["Mood diary rated twice daily for 4-6 weeks, reviewed at her next appointment.","CMHT psychologist: structured personality and trauma assessment (eg International Trauma Questionnaire).","If EUPD confirmed: DBT (individual plus skills group, about 12 months) or MBT. If complex PTSD: phased trauma-focused therapy."],"social":["Named CMHT worker with fortnightly contact while assessment completes.","With consent, collateral from someone who knew her as a teenager.","Check housing, work and current relationships; IDVA referral if there is current abuse."],"safety":["Written safety plan: triggers, her warning signs, two coping steps, two contacts, crisis team, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123.","If non-recent abuse is disclosed and children may be at risk: tell the trust safeguarding lead today.","No risk score to decide care; record specific needs and risks. Review within 2 weeks."]},"facts":[{"h":"Time course beats symptom lists","x":"Hours, triggered by people: emotional instability. Days to weeks with sleep and energy change, then recovery: bipolar. Steady decline with first-rank symptoms: psychosis."},{"h":"Hypomania needs four days","x":"Ask for at least 4 consecutive days of high or irritable mood with less need for sleep and more activity. A good afternoon or a big night out does not count."},{"h":"No drugs for EUPD itself","x":"NICE: medicines are not recommended for personality disorder or its individual symptoms. Treat coexisting depression or psychosis in their own right."},{"h":"Voices are not a diagnosis","x":"Voices occur in EUPD, PTSD and dissociation. Voices tied to stress with preserved insight point away from schizophrenia but do not exclude it."},{"h":"Risk tools do not predict","x":"NICE (2022): do not use risk scales to predict self-harm or to decide who gets treatment. Assess needs and agree a safety plan instead."},{"h":"Complex PTSD in ICD-11","x":"PTSD plus lasting problems with emotional control, negative self-view and relationships. Often follows repeated or childhood trauma; overlaps closely with EUPD."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Ask discriminating questions separating mood disorder, personality disorder and psychosis, then agree a working formulation."},"c-1-2-2":{"obj":["Confirmed fluctuating attention with a bedside tool (4AT) and listed the organic red flags in the notes","Spoken directly to the medical registrar with a clear request for admission and named tests (MRI, EEG, lumbar puncture with autoimmune antibodies)","Escalated to the medical consultant and told your own consultant if the registrar declined","Assessed and recorded her capacity for investigations, and agreed who decides in her best interests if she lacks it","Explained to her partner in plain words why a physical cause is suspected and what happens next"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"They said she's medically cleared. Why are you saying it's physical?\"","r":"Good question. 'Cleared' meant her first blood tests were normal. Her fever, fast heart rate, the funny turn and her drifting attention suggest something may be affecting the brain, so she needs more tests."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is it a brain tumour? Is she going to die?\"","r":"I understand that fear. I cannot say yet what is causing this, but several possible causes are very treatable, especially when found early. That is why I am pushing for tests tonight. I will update you as we learn more."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"We've been here six hours and everyone just says she's mad.\"","r":"I am sorry; that must be awful. I do not think this is simply a mental illness, and I have told the medical team so. I will stay involved until there is a plan."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"No needles. Get off me. You're trying to poison me.\"","r":"You sound very frightened, and nobody wants to hurt you. You have a temperature and we need to find out why. Can your partner sit with you while we talk about it?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"There are insects on the wall. Can't you see them?\"","r":"I cannot see them, but I believe they look real to you, and that sounds scary. You are in hospital and you are safe. A fever can make the brain see things, and we are working on it."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"She started steroids for her asthma last week. Does that matter?\"","r":"Yes, thank you, that really helps. Steroids can affect mood and thinking, so it goes on the list. We still need the other tests, because the fever and the fit need explaining too."}],"bps":{"bio":["Medical team: FBC, U&E, LFT, CRP, calcium, magnesium, glucose, TFT, CK, blood cultures, HIV, syphilis; pregnancy test; urine drug screen.","MRI brain, EEG, lumbar puncture (cells, protein, glucose, HSV PCR, oligoclonal bands, NMDA receptor antibodies in CSF and serum), with neurology advice.","If herpes encephalitis is possible: IV aciclovir 10 mg/kg every 8 hours, started without waiting for the LP, adjusted for renal function.","Avoid antipsychotics. If sedation is essential for safety: lorazepam 0.5-1 mg orally, with observations.","NMDA receptor antibodies positive: pelvic imaging for ovarian teratoma."],"psych":["Liaison psychiatry reviews daily on the medical ward: delirium, agitation, capacity.","Delirium care by ward nurses: quiet side room, clock and orientation cues, glasses, partner present, consistent staff."],"social":["Named member of the medical team updates her partner daily; liaison contact number given.","Partner supported to arrange cover for home and work commitments."],"safety":["Capacity for LP and MRI assessed by the treating medical team; if lacking, MCA best-interests decision with her partner consulted. The MHA does not authorise physical treatment.","Enhanced or 1:1 observation on the medical ward if wandering or pulling at lines.","Escalation log in notes: names, times, outcomes; tell your consultant in the morning; hand over to day liaison at 09:00."]},"facts":[{"h":"4AT in two minutes","x":"Alertness, four-item orientation test, months backwards, acute change or fluctuation. A score of 4 or more suggests delirium and gives you objective words for the medical team."},{"h":"Anti-NMDAR encephalitis pattern","x":"Often a young woman: flu-like prodrome, then anxiety, insomnia and psychosis, then seizures, abnormal movements, autonomic swings. Linked to ovarian teratoma; early immunotherapy improves outcome."},{"h":"Normal MRI does not exclude it","x":"Imaging is often normal in autoimmune encephalitis. EEG changes and CSF antibodies carry more weight; ask for CSF and serum testing."},{"h":"Antipsychotics can harm here","x":"Encephalitis patients tolerate antipsychotics poorly: rigidity, NMS-like reactions and masked signs. Prefer low-dose lorazepam if sedation is unavoidable."},{"h":"Escalate up, not around","x":"If the registrar declines: 'I remain concerned and will discuss with your consultant.' Then do it, and record names and times."}],"type":"ECG and investigations","do":"Decide whether the presentation is organic and persuade the medical team to investigate and admit."},"c-1-2-3":{"obj":["Explained to the team how cannabis and cocaine drive his relapses and why NICE NG58 keeps him under mental health services","Asked about his drug use in a motivational way and recorded amount, cost, potency and his readiness to change","Screened her for PTSD with a validated tool (PCL-5) and asked whether she is safe from the perpetrator now","Proposed one integrated plan for each patient naming both conditions and who treats each part"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Weed is the only thing that calms the voices. Why would I stop?\"","r":"It makes sense to use what seems to help. Can we look at it together? Some people find it calms them at first but makes the voices worse the next day. What have you noticed?"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not an addict. Loads of people smoke.\"","r":"You are right that lots of people use it, and I am not calling you an addict. I am curious how it fits with your health. How much would you use on a normal day?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If I keep smoking, will you lot kick me off your books?\"","r":"No. Your care stays with us; using cannabis does not mean losing support. We would like the drug and alcohol team to work alongside us, if you are willing."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll cut down if you stop the injection.\"","r":"I appreciate you thinking about cutting down; that is a real step. The injection protects you from relapse, so let us not swap one for the other. What is it about the injection that bothers you?"},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should be over it by now. It was five years ago.\"","r":"There is no timetable for recovering from an assault. What you describe is a common, treatable reaction, not weakness. The right therapy helps, even years later."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I can't talk about it. Last time I did, I didn't sleep for a week.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. You will not have to talk about it until you are ready; therapy starts by building ways to cope. You set the pace. Would that feel manageable?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He still messages me. He knows where I live.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me; your safety comes first. Can I ask a few questions about how risky things are now? There are specialist workers who can help keep you safe."}],"bps":{"bio":["Him: continue his antipsychotic. If he stops smoking tobacco or cannabis, clozapine and olanzapine levels rise; check level and review dose within a week.","Him: ECG (QTc, ischaemia) and BP because of cocaine; urine drug screen with consent; offer NRT or varenicline.","Her: check dose and adherence of the current antidepressant before any third switch. NICE drug options for PTSD: sertraline (50 mg morning, up to 200 mg) or venlafaxine.","Her: avoid benzodiazepines for sleep; ask about night-time alcohol."],"psych":["Him: motivational interviewing by his care coordinator; joint working with the community drug and alcohol service.","Her: trauma-focused CBT or EMDR, 8-12 sessions, psychology team; grounding and stabilisation first if she dissociates."],"social":["Him: care coordinator stays; CPA review with the drug service worker; money and housing check for drug debt.","Her: IDVA referral; DASH checklist, MARAC referral if high risk (14 or more yes, or professional judgement)."],"safety":["Him: relapse plan naming drug use as an early warning sign; crisis plan; advise not driving and the duty to tell the DVLA.","Her: safety plan covering the perpetrator: blocked numbers, safe address, refuge and police contact. Police with her consent unless others are at risk.","Both: MDT review in 4 weeks."]},"facts":[{"h":"NG58: no exclusion for substances","x":"Drug or alcohol use is never a reason to discharge someone with severe mental illness from mental health care. Mental health services lead; drug services join, not replace."},{"h":"Smoking changes drug levels","x":"Tobacco smoke induces CYP1A2. Stopping smoking, eg on admission, can raise clozapine and olanzapine levels sharply within days: check levels and watch for sedation."},{"h":"Potency matters","x":"Daily high-potency cannabis carries the highest psychosis risk. Any reduction, or switching to lower potency, is a worthwhile first target."},{"h":"PTSD: therapy before tablets","x":"NICE: trauma-focused CBT or EMDR first line. Sertraline or venlafaxine if she prefers medication or therapy is unavailable."},{"h":"Screen with the PCL-5","x":"20 items scored 0-80; around 31-33 or above suggests probable PTSD. Takes five minutes and can change the whole plan."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Propose an integrated plan treating both comorbid conditions together for each patient."},"c-1-3-1":{"obj":["Opened with one sentence: age, sex, legal status, presenting problem and why now","Presented a 5P formulation in under one minute, with each P linked to an action","Stated current risk in words (what, how likely, what makes it worse, what protects) without a score","Finished within three minutes with a plan and one clear question for the consultant","Checked the plan with her after the round and recorded her agreement"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I lost my job and him in the same week. There's no point any more.\"","r":"That is an enormous amount of loss at once, and it makes sense you feel this way. I am glad you are here. What does 'no point' mean for you right now?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do I have to see all those people on the ward round?\"","r":"Fair question; it can feel like a lot. Each person helps with something different: medicine, money, therapy. I will give them the key points so you do not repeat everything. Is there anything you want me to leave out?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want antidepressants. I just need to sort the money out.\"","r":"Money worries are a big part of this, and we will get help with them. Medicine is one option, not the only one. Can I explain what it might help with, and then you decide?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've been drinking a bottle of wine a night since he left.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me honestly. That much can deepen low mood and disturb sleep. Stopping suddenly on the ward could cause withdrawal, so I want to check that now. When was your last drink?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Who's going to look after my dog? She's all I've got.\"","r":"She clearly matters a lot to you. Let us sort that today. Could your sister take her? If not, our social worker can look at options, and you can keep in touch with them about her."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I feel stupid being here. Other people have real problems.\"","r":"You do have a real problem, and coming in was the right thing. Depression after big losses is common and treatable. You deserve help like anyone else."}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg in the morning; increase in 50 mg steps (max 200 mg) if needed after 2-4 weeks. Warn: nausea, anxiety and sleep upset in the first 2 weeks. Or mirtazapine 15-45 mg at night if sleep is poor (warn weight gain).","Alcohol: AUDIT today; if dependent, withdrawal scale and chlordiazepoxide reducing regimen per trust protocol; oral thiamine, or IV Pabrinex if Wernicke's risk is high.","Bloods: FBC (MCV), LFT, GGT, U&E, TFT, glucose; weight and BP."],"psych":["Individual CBT for depression (around 16 sessions), psychology team or NHS Talking Therapies after discharge.","Brief alcohol intervention on the ward; community alcohol service referral if AUDIT is 16 or more."],"social":["Ward social worker: debt advice referral and Mental Health Crisis Breathing Space; benefits check after redundancy; fit note from the ward doctor.","Sister as named contact; dog care arranged today."],"safety":["Observation level agreed today and reviewed daily; leave only with a written safety plan.","Sister checks home for stockpiled tablets before any leave; limited supplies at discharge.","Discharge via the home treatment team; follow-up within 72 hours; antidepressant review within 1 week of starting."]},"facts":[{"h":"One-line opener sets the frame","x":"Age, sex, legal status, when admitted, why now, working diagnosis. If the consultant stops you after one sentence, they should still know what they are dealing with."},{"h":"Formulation is a story","x":"Link each P to an action: 'debt keeps her low, so social work today'. A P with no action is wasted airtime."},{"h":"Breathing Space for crisis care","x":"People receiving mental health crisis treatment get creditor protection for its length plus 30 days. A debt adviser applies; an AMHP provides the evidence."},{"h":"Review high-risk starts in a week","x":"NICE: see people at increased suicide risk, or aged 18-25, within 1 week of starting an antidepressant. Prescribe small quantities on discharge."},{"h":"Alcohol first, then reassess mood","x":"NICE: treat alcohol misuse first, as depression often lifts after 3-4 weeks of abstinence. Severe depression with high risk may still need treating now: your question for the consultant."},{"h":"Risk in words, not numbers","x":"Say what might happen, how likely, what makes it worse and what protects. NICE advises against risk scores to decide care."}],"type":"Handover and escalation","do":"Present a new patient in three minutes with a 5P formulation and a linked plan."},"c-1-3-2":{"obj":["Presented a 5P formulation in under two minutes that places alcohol as both trigger and maintaining driver of his low mood","Established whether low mood came before or after heavy drinking, using his longest alcohol-free spell as the test","Agreed a timed plan: detox with thiamine now, mood reassessment at 3–4 weeks alcohol-free, antidepressant decision then","Documented suicide risk linked to intoxication, DVLA advice and whether he cares for children"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"“I'm not an alcoholic. I only drink because she left. Anyone would.”","r":"Losing your marriage is a huge blow, and I'm not here to label you. A bottle a day does change how the brain handles mood and sleep, though. Can we look at what the drink does for you, and what it costs?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"“So which is it, doc? Am I depressed or is it the booze?”","r":"Honestly, possibly both, and we can find out. Alcohol alone can push mood down, and that often lifts within a month of stopping. If you're still low at three or four weeks dry, we treat depression in its own right."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"“Just give me antidepressants now and I'll cut down to a few cans.”","r":"I can see you want to feel better fast. Tablets rarely work while drinking stays heavy, and cutting down after dependence usually slides back. Let's finish the detox first, then decide on tablets with a clearer picture."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"“My kids saw me drunk at Christmas. They're better off without me.”","r":"That sounds painful to carry, and thank you for saying it. When you say they're better off without you, have you been thinking of ending your life? I'd like to understand that properly."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"“Will I have a fit if I stop? Last time I saw spiders on the wall.”","r":"That sounds frightening, and it's useful to know. Seeing things means your withdrawal can be severe, so we're giving a reducing sedative and vitamin B1, and nurses will check you regularly. Tell them straight away if you feel shaky."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"“His wife's gone and he's lost it. You lot just dry him out and send him back.”","r":"I hear how fed up and worried you are. We're doing more than drying him out: protecting his memory with vitamins, checking his liver, and watching his mood closely. How was he before the drinking took off?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Chlordiazepoxide reducing regime: start up to 30 mg four times daily by severity, taper to zero over 7–10 days; CIWA-Ar four-hourly guides extra doses; hold if drowsy","Pabrinex IV one pair daily for 3–5 days; if confusion, ataxia or eye signs, two pairs three times daily; then oral thiamine 100 mg three times daily","Bloods now: FBC (MCV), LFTs, GGT, U&Es, magnesium, phosphate, clotting; liver ultrasound if abnormal","After detox: acamprosate 666 mg three times daily (if 60 kg or more) for up to 6 months, or naltrexone 25 mg day one then 50 mg daily once LFTs checked","No antidepressant yet; repeat PHQ-9 at 3–4 weeks abstinent and decide then"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing on the ward by the addictions liaison nurse to build his own reasons to stop","Relapse-prevention group or 1:1 with the community drug and alcohol service, starting before discharge","If low mood persists at 4 weeks dry: CBT via NHS Talking Therapies alongside the alcohol work"],"social":["Addictions liaison nurse links him to the community alcohol service and mutual aid (AA, SMART Recovery)","Ward social worker: housing and money after the separation; debt advice referral","With consent, involve a sibling or friend in the plan; signpost Al-Anon for family"],"safety":["Suicide risk reviewed daily on the ward round during detox; observation level set with the nurse in charge","Advise no driving; he must tell the DVLA about alcohol dependence; record the advice","If he sees or looks after children, discuss with the trust safeguarding lead","Discharge with crisis numbers and 72-hour follow-up; warn that tolerance falls after detox"]},"facts":[{"h":"Low mood often lifts when drinking stops","x":"Many alcohol-related depressive symptoms improve within 3–4 weeks of abstinence. Low mood that persists beyond that, or that came before heavy drinking, points to an independent depression worth treating."},{"h":"Thiamine before glucose","x":"In a malnourished drinker, glucose without thiamine can tip him into Wernicke's. Give Pabrinex first. The full triad of confusion, ataxia and eye signs shows in a minority: treat on suspicion."},{"h":"Withdrawal timeline","x":"Symptoms start 6–12 hours after the last drink; seizures cluster at 24–48 hours; delirium tremens usually at 48–72 hours. Past fits or DTs predict a severe withdrawal."},{"h":"Acamprosate or naltrexone","x":"Acamprosate starts straight after detox, 666 mg three times daily if 60 kg or more; check renal function. Naltrexone 50 mg daily: check LFTs; it blocks opioid painkillers."},{"h":"Alcohol stacks suicide risk","x":"Dependence, a recent loss, midlife male and drinking when suicidal thoughts peak all add up. Ask whether the thoughts come mainly when drunk, and plan for the weeks after discharge."},{"h":"DVLA: the duty is his","x":"Alcohol dependence must be declared to the DVLA; a Group 1 licence is usually refused until a year free of dependence. Advise him, document it, and act if he keeps driving."}],"type":"Documentation and reporting","do":"Present a 5P formulation showing how alcohol fits his presentation and shapes the plan."},"c-1-3-3":{"obj":["Drafted a formulation that names trauma, possible autism and personality difficulties as working hypotheses, not verdicts","Replaced 'non-engagement' with three concrete barriers and a reasonable adjustment for each","Made a neurodevelopmental assessment referral with a support plan for the wait","Agreed a written crisis plan with her, shared with A&E liaison and her GP"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"“Everyone's going to discharge me anyway. That's what you lot always do.”","r":"After being let down so many times, I'd expect you to think that. I'm not here to discharge you. I want to understand what makes appointments hard, so we can change how we work with you."},{"tag":"Question","p":"“Are you saying I'm autistic now? I thought I had BPD.”","r":"I'm not changing any label today. Some of what you describe, like noise, sudden changes and taking words literally, fits autism too, and the two can sit together. A proper assessment would tell us. Would you want one?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"“The waiting room is unbearable. Buzzing lights, people staring. I just walk out.”","r":"That makes complete sense, and it isn't you being difficult. We can book you the first slot of the day and use a quiet room. Would a text the day before help too?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"“Sometimes I go blank and lose an hour. I find cuts and don't remember doing them.”","r":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that; it must feel scary. Losing time under stress often links to what happened when you were young. I'd like to understand it more and build it into your safety plan."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"“I'm not doing group therapy. I can't cope with groups.”","r":"That's fine, and it tells us something important. Let's think about what you could manage: one-to-one, shorter sessions, written material. What has helped, even a little, before?"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"“She's manipulative. Nine A&E visits and she won't come to clinic.”","r":"Nine visits tells us she's in crisis and our clinic isn't reachable for her. If noise and change overwhelm her, A&E becomes the only door. Let's test adjustments for three months before any discharge decision."}],"bps":{"bio":["No medication for the personality difficulties themselves (NICE CG78); any crisis sedative for no more than one week","Weekly pharmacy dispensing of any medicines risky in overdose","If ADHD is confirmed: medication started by the adult ADHD service after baseline BP, pulse, weight and height"],"psych":["Structured therapy via the trust personality disorder service (DBT where self-harm dominates, or MBT), adapted for sensory needs","Phase-based trauma work for possible complex PTSD once stabilisation skills are in place","Shared formulation drawn with her and her care coordinator; revisited every 3 months"],"social":["Care coordinator: one named clinician, first appointment of the day, quiet room, text reminders, written summaries","Record reasonable adjustments (Equality Act 2010) as an alert on the electronic record","Signpost local autism support; involve her sister if she wishes"],"safety":["Co-produced crisis plan sent to A&E liaison and GP, including what helps her in a noisy department","Missed appointment triggers a phone call from the care coordinator, never automatic discharge","Crisis line, NHS 111 option 2 and Samaritans 116 123 on the plan","MDT review of formulation and engagement in 3 months"]},"facts":[{"h":"Eye contact doesn't rule out autism","x":"Many autistic women camouflage: they learn eye contact and social scripts and pay for it in exhaustion. Ask about childhood, sensory load and coping with change, not just how she presents."},{"h":"No drugs for the disorder itself","x":"NICE advises against medication for borderline personality disorder or its individual symptoms. In crisis, a sedative for no more than a week is acceptable; treat co-occurring conditions on their merits."},{"h":"Complex PTSD is in ICD-11","x":"Core PTSD plus problems with emotion control, a deeply negative self-view and relationship difficulty. It overlaps with EUPD; formulate both rather than choosing one too early."},{"h":"Reasonable adjustments are a legal duty","x":"Under the Equality Act 2010, services must adjust for disability. A quiet waiting space, a fixed clinician and written plans cost little and change engagement."},{"h":"Discharging for DNAs repeats the harm","x":"For someone shaped by neglect, discharge after missed appointments feels like abandonment and pushes crises back to A&E. Find out why she misses first."}],"type":"Documentation and reporting","do":"Draft a formulation covering personality, trauma and neurodevelopmental factors, and change the plan accordingly."},"c-1-4-1":{"obj":["Separated her long-standing risk from today's new triggers (eviction, losing her dog) in one clear sentence in the notes","Established intent, planning and how she feels about surviving, without asking for method detail","Co-produced a written safety plan in her own words with two named people and three crisis numbers","Secured crisis team contact within 24 hours and a GP letter by the next working day"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"“I've done this a hundred times. Just let me go home.”","r":"I hear that you're exhausted with all of this, and I won't keep you longer than needed. Something sounds different this week, though, and I'd rather understand it with you than send you out with nothing in place."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"“They took Bella. She was the only reason I got up in the morning.”","r":"Losing her sounds heartbreaking; she was clearly central to your days. When she went, did the thoughts of dying change? I'm asking because it helps us plan what you need now."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"“You lot only care when I'm in A&E. Nobody rings when I'm drowning.”","r":"That's a fair criticism, and I'm sorry it's felt that way. Let's change it now: the crisis team will call you within 24 hours, and we'll write down who you can reach before then."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"“Honestly? I'm not sure I'm glad I woke up.”","r":"Thank you for being honest; that matters. Feeling unsure is common after an overdose. Tell me about the part of you that is still here. What has kept you going on other bad nights?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"“If I tell you how bad it is, you'll section me.”","r":"I understand that worry. Telling me helps me give you the right help, and most people go home after an overdose with support in place. I'll be open with you about what I'm thinking at each step."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"“She's known to you lot, chronic high risk. Can you just sign her off?”","r":"She is well known, but this week is different: she's lost her home and her dog. I need twenty more minutes to finish a safety plan and arrange crisis team follow-up. I'll write it up before she leaves."}],"bps":{"bio":["Ask the GP for 7-day dispensing of any psychotropics; review need for medicines dangerous in overdose","No new psychotropic started in A&E; prescribing review passed to her CMHT"],"psych":["Psychosocial assessment and formulation documented in line with NICE NG225 and shared with her","CMHT to restart or begin structured therapy for self-harm (DBT where available)"],"social":["Care coordinator told next working day; social worker to start a homelessness application with the council housing team","Help contacting the RSPCA about her dog if she wants it","Named person to be with her or reachable tonight, with her agreement"],"safety":["Crisis resolution and home treatment team contact within 24 hours","Copy of the safety plan in her hand: crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, Shout (text 85258)","Agree removal of stockpiled tablets at home; returned to a pharmacy","GP letter next working day; CMHT review within a week"]},"facts":[{"h":"No risk scores for decisions","x":"NICE NG225: don't use risk tools or labels like 'low' or 'high' to decide treatment or discharge. Assess needs, formulate, and plan."},{"h":"Repeat self-harm still predicts suicide","x":"Self-harm is the strongest single predictor of later suicide, and the raised risk lasts years. Familiarity breeds under-reaction; every episode needs fresh eyes."},{"h":"Ask about intent, not technique","x":"What she hoped would happen, whether she planned, told anyone or left a note tells you far more than which tablets. Don't rehearse methods in detail."},{"h":"Losses are acute triggers","x":"Eviction, debt, losing a pet or partner, or recent discharge from services shift the picture fast. Ask 'what is different this time?' every time."},{"h":"Safety plans must be hers","x":"A plan in her words, with warning signs she recognises and people she'll actually call, gets used. A staff-written plan she never saw does not."},{"h":"NHS 111 option 2","x":"In England, dialling 111 and choosing option 2 reaches the local mental health crisis service, 24/7. Put it on every safety plan with Samaritans 116 123."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Safety plan","do":"Complete a psychosocial assessment separating chronic from acute risk, and co-produce a written safety plan."},"c-1-4-2":{"obj":["Written a risk formulation naming the risk (violence to his neighbour), its triggers and early warning signs in plain words","Linked the formulation to tomorrow's Section 17 leave, with conditions matched on the leave form","Given the weekend doctor and nurse in charge an if–then action list for late return, cannabis use or talk of the neighbour","Agreed with the patient, and with consent his brother, what to watch for on leave"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"“Why are you writing about me hurting people? That was years ago.”","r":"I know it's uncomfortable to see in writing. When you were unwell, things went badly, and I want whoever is on duty to spot early signs and help you sooner. Can we also write what helps you?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"“Can I go to my flat on leave? My neighbour's still there.”","r":"I understand wanting to go home. Given what happened before, I'd suggest your brother's house this first time, then build up to the flat. What do you think?"},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"“One joint on leave isn't a big deal, right?”","r":"I appreciate you being honest. Cannabis was part of what made you unwell last time, so it would put your leave at risk. If you do use, tell staff when you get back; that's far better than us finding out."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"“He's still putting stuff in my food. I can taste it.”","r":"Thank you for telling me; that must be frightening. I'm going to hold tomorrow's leave until we've talked more, because I want you safe and well when you go out. Can we talk about the neighbour?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"“What if he turns up at mine and he's not right? What do I do?”","r":"That's a sensible question. If he seems suspicious, isn't sleeping or mentions the neighbour, ring the ward on this number straight away. If anyone's in danger, call 999. You won't be on your own."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"“He's been settled for weeks. The old entry says low risk. Why change it?”","r":"He has been settled, and that's good. But 'low' gives the night doctor nothing to act on. I'm adding who could be harmed, what triggers it and what to do. Then leave can go ahead safely."}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue his depot at the prescribed interval; put the next due date on the leave plan so leave doesn't clash","Urine drug screen with consent if he returns smelling of cannabis or changed in behaviour"],"psych":["Relapse signature work with his named nurse before longer leave","Brief cannabis intervention; dual-diagnosis worker if he wants it"],"social":["Brother given leave times, ward number and warning signs, with the patient's consent","First leave to his brother's house, not the flat near the neighbour; graded plan back to home","Care coordinator to review housing near the neighbour before discharge"],"safety":["RC completes the s17 form: destination, duration, return time, no contact with the neighbour; copy to patient","Late return: ward follows AWOL policy; s18 allows return; police informed by risk grade; consider the neighbour's safety","Risk entry updated on the electronic record, not only progress notes; nurse in charge and weekend doctor briefed at handover","Leave reviewed at Monday's ward round"]},"facts":[{"h":"'Low risk' is not a formulation","x":"A formulation says what might happen, to whom, in what circumstances, how soon, and what prevents it. If a stranger can't act on it at 3am, rewrite it."},{"h":"Only the RC grants s17 leave","x":"The responsible clinician authorises Section 17 leave and sets its conditions. You can't add or extend it, but you can advise, and leave can be suspended if risk changes."},{"h":"AWOL patients can be brought back","x":"Under Section 18, a detained patient who is absent without leave can be returned by staff or police. Grade the risk by trust policy; record who you told and when."},{"h":"Past behaviour, present state","x":"The best guide to violence is what he did before and under which conditions. Check whether those conditions (no medication, cannabis, contact with the neighbour) are returning now."},{"h":"New depot, partial cover","x":"Many depots take weeks to months to reach steady levels. Three weeks in, protection against relapse may still be incomplete; the depot alone shouldn't reassure you."}],"type":"Documentation and reporting","do":"Write a risk formulation in the notes that an unfamiliar doctor could act on at 3am."},"c-1-5-1":{"obj":["Asked her own explanation first, then shared a tentative formulation in under three minutes with no jargon","Answered 'is my brain broken?' directly and explored the fear behind it","Linked each part of the formulation to one treatment step she agreed to","Confirmed understanding with teach-back and asked about suicidal thoughts before she left"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"“Is something wrong with my brain? Am I going to be like this forever?”","r":"That's a frightening thought to carry, and I'm glad you asked. Nothing is broken. Some people react more strongly to stress, often because of genes and early losses. Depression is treatable, and each episode teaches us something."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"“I stopped the tablets early last time. So this is my fault.”","r":"Lots of people stop when they feel better; it makes sense. This isn't about blame. It tells us something useful: staying on treatment longer after you recover protects you. Shall we plan that together?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"“Isn't it just a chemical imbalance? My friend said that.”","r":"It's a common way of putting it, but too simple. Brain chemistry plays a part, along with genes, losing your mum young, and how you cope with pressure. That's good news: it gives us more ways in."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"“I don't want to be on medication for life.”","r":"That's a reasonable worry, and nobody is deciding 'for life' today. After three episodes, staying on it for about two years once you're well cuts the chance of another. We'll review it together regularly."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"“Sometimes I think everyone would cope better if I wasn't here.”","r":"Thank you for telling me; that's important. When those thoughts come, have you thought of ending your life, or made any plans? I want to understand so we can keep you safe."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"“I push myself so hard at work, then I just crash. Every time.”","r":"That's a really clear description, and it fits what I'm seeing. Pushing harder and pulling away from friends seems to keep the low mood going. That's exactly the pattern therapy can help you change."}],"bps":{"bio":["If she chooses medication: sertraline 50 mg each morning; if partial response after 2–4 weeks, increase in 50 mg steps (max 200 mg daily)","Warn about early nausea, restlessness and sexual side effects; ask about them at review","Continue for at least 2 years after remission, given recurrent depression (NICE NG222); taper over weeks when stopping","Review within 2 weeks of starting; PHQ-9 at baseline and each review"],"psych":["Individual CBT or behavioural activation via NHS Talking Therapies (around 16 sessions)","Once well: mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (8-week group) for relapse prevention","Written formulation diagram given to her to revisit"],"social":["Work: GP fit note for phased hours; occupational health referral if she agrees","Plan one contact a week with her close friend"],"safety":["Crisis plan with NHS 111 option 2 and Samaritans 116 123","Follow-up within 2 weeks, sooner if suicidal thoughts increase","Letter written to her, copied to GP, with the plan"]},"facts":[{"h":"Three episodes: think maintenance","x":"After recurrent depression, NICE advises continuing an effective antidepressant for at least two years after recovery. Stopping early, as she did last time, is a common route to relapse."},{"h":"Drop 'chemical imbalance'","x":"It oversimplifies and leaves patients feeling passive. Explain vulnerability plus stress plus what keeps it going: it gives her levers to pull."},{"h":"Write the letter to her","x":"Clinic letters addressed to the patient in plain English, copied to the GP, are recommended practice. Include her formulation diagram and the agreed plan."},{"h":"Teach-back, framed kindly","x":"Say 'I want to check I explained that well; how would you put it?' It tests your explanation, not her intelligence, and catches misunderstandings early."},{"h":"Sertraline: start low, then wait","x":"50 mg once daily; benefit builds over 2–4 weeks. Early nausea and restlessness usually settle. Ask directly about sexual side effects; patients rarely mention them and quietly stop."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis","do":"Share your formulation in plain English, check her understanding with teach-back, and agree next steps."},"c-1-5-2":{"obj":["Let both parents say what they fear before explaining, and named their guilt directly","Explained the stress–vulnerability model and cannabis risk in plain words without blaming them","Stayed within his consent: shared diagnosis and plan, kept his relationship private, and still listened fully","Offered family intervention and a Care Act carer's assessment, and gave the team's crisis contact"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"“We divorced when he was 14. Did we do this to him?”","r":"I can hear how much you've been carrying that question. Divorce doesn't cause psychosis. It comes from a mix of how the brain develops and stresses like cannabis and sleep loss. You being here today helps him."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"“He's 19 and you won't tell us anything? We're his parents!”","r":"I understand how frustrating that is when you're this worried. He's agreed I can share his diagnosis and plan, and I'll do that now. A few things he's asked to keep private, and I must respect that. I'm always happy to listen."},{"tag":"Question","p":"“Was it the cannabis? He's been smoking skunk since sixteen.”","r":"Strong cannabis, especially daily from a young age, does raise the risk and can worsen symptoms. It's probably one piece among several. The hopeful part: stopping really helps recovery, and the team can support him with that."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"“Will he ever be normal again? Go back to uni?”","r":"That's what every parent wants to know. Many young people recover well after a first episode, especially with early treatment and family behind them. Some have further episodes. The team includes help to get back into study."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"“He keeps saying his girlfriend controls his thoughts. Is she in danger?”","r":"Thank you; that's really useful, and I'll pass it to the team. I can't discuss his relationship, but anything you tell us helps. Has he said anything about harming himself or anyone else?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"“I haven't slept in weeks. I keep checking on him at night.”","r":"That sounds exhausting, and it's very common for parents in your position. Your health matters too. A carer's assessment looks at what support you need; can I refer you today?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Antipsychotic chosen with him, started at the lower end of the licensed range","Baseline weight, BMI, waist, BP, pulse, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; ECG if indicated","Weight weekly for 6 weeks, then at 12 weeks and 1 year; HbA1c and lipids at 12 weeks and 1 year"],"psych":["Family intervention via EIP: at least 10 planned sessions over 3 months to a year","CBT for psychosis, at least 16 sessions, EIP psychologist","Cannabis work with his EIP care coordinator or the local drug service"],"social":["Carer's assessment by the local authority under the Care Act 2014 (or the carers' centre on its behalf)","Carer information: Rethink Mental Illness, local carers' centre, Triangle of Care pack","EIP vocational worker (IPS) to plan a return to study"],"safety":["Parents given the EIP duty number in hours and the crisis line / NHS 111 option 2 out of hours","His consent decisions recorded (what can and can't be shared) and reviewed regularly","Parents taught the early warning signs he agrees to share"]},"facts":[{"h":"Listening never breaches confidentiality","x":"You can always hear what relatives tell you and give general information about psychosis. What you share about him is limited by his consent; record exactly what he agreed."},{"h":"High-potency cannabis raises risk","x":"Daily use of strong, high-THC cannabis is linked to a several-fold higher risk of psychosis and to more relapses. Quitting improves outcome: say it as hope, not blame."},{"h":"Family intervention is NICE-backed","x":"Offer it to families in close contact: at least 10 planned sessions over 3 months to a year, including him where possible. It reduces relapse and admissions."},{"h":"Carers have their own legal right","x":"Under the Care Act 2014, any adult carer who appears to need support is able to ask for a carer's assessment from the local authority, whatever the patient decides."},{"h":"Warmth, not criticism","x":"High criticism, hostility or over-involvement at home goes with more relapse. Frame it as a skill families learn together in sessions, never as a judgement."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis","do":"Explain his illness to his parents within his consent limits and signpost carer support."},"c-1-5-3":{"obj":["Presented a formulation and plan in two minutes: who, why now, what keeps it going, strengths, risks, plan","Spoke to the patient, not about her, and gave her the last word on the summary","Left the meeting with named people, dates and the Section 117 aftercare plan agreed","Wrote her early relapse signs and crisis route into a plan she and her sister both hold"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"“Weight gain's why I stopped. If it comes back, I'm stopping again.”","r":"Thank you for being straight with us; that helps. Let's make weight part of the plan: monthly weigh-ins, dietitian support, and a promise that if it climbs, we review the medicine with you before you stop."},{"tag":"Question","p":"“Who do I call if she starts going downhill again?”","r":"Good question, and it's going in the plan. Her care coordinator is your first call in office hours; after that, the crisis line. If you notice poor sleep or her stopping tablets, ring early. It's never a nuisance."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"“You're all talking about me like I'm not here.”","r":"You're right, and I'm sorry. This is your plan. Let me check with you: did I get the reasons for this admission right? What do you want everyone here to know?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"“If I go home and it falls apart, will I lose the flat?”","r":"That's a real worry. Your care coordinator and the ward social worker will check rent and benefits before you leave. Section 117 aftercare means the support you need to stay well at home is arranged and free."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"“Can I skip the home treatment visits if I come to clinic?”","r":"I'd like the first week of visits, because the days after discharge are when people most often struggle. If it's going well, we can step down quickly. Would that feel fair?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"“I just want my job back. Nobody will hire me now.”","r":"That matters a lot, and wanting to work is one of your strengths. An employment specialist can help with applications and talking to employers. Let's add that referral to the plan today."}],"bps":{"bio":["14 days of discharge medication; GP letter within 24 hours stating drug, dose and who prescribes next","Weight and waist monthly for 3 months, then 3-monthly; HbA1c and lipids 12 weeks after the switch, then yearly","Physical health clinic: BP, smoking status, annual health check"],"psych":["Relapse prevention with her care coordinator: written early warning signs (stopping tablets, sleep loss, isolation)","Continue art group; CBT for psychosis via CMHT psychology if residual symptoms"],"social":["Section 117 aftercare plan: joint NHS and local authority duty, free of charge, led by the care coordinator","Employment specialist (IPS) referral","Ward social worker checks benefits and tenancy before discharge","Sister invited to CPA reviews with consent and offered a carer's assessment"],"safety":["Home treatment team follow-up within 72 hours of discharge","Crisis plan copy to patient and sister: care coordinator, crisis line, NHS 111 option 2","Self-neglect and exploitation risk when unwell written into the plan; CPA review date set at the meeting"]},"facts":[{"h":"Section 117 is free aftercare","x":"After Section 3, the NHS and local authority must jointly provide aftercare for needs arising from her illness, free of charge, until both agree it is no longer needed."},{"h":"72 hours after discharge","x":"The days straight after inpatient discharge carry high suicide and relapse risk. National standards expect follow-up within 72 hours; book it before she leaves."},{"h":"Two-minute structure","x":"Who she is; why now; what keeps it going; strengths; risks; plan with names and dates. Then ask her: 'Have I got that right?' Stop there."},{"h":"Weight drives non-adherence","x":"Weight gain is one of the commonest reasons people stop antipsychotics. Treat it as a clinical problem with measured monitoring and options, not a cosmetic complaint."},{"h":"Strengths belong in the formulation","x":"Naming her sister, art group and wish to work shows the team what to build on, and tells her she is more than her risks."}],"type":"Family meeting","do":"Present a structured formulation and plan to the MDT, patient and family within two minutes."},"c-2-1-1":{"obj":["Elicited and described his persecutory beliefs, any hallucinations and first-rank symptoms in his own words","Ruled out delirium and requested a full organic screen with a reason for each test","Took a non-judgemental substance history: cannabis type, amount, recent change, other drugs and alcohol","Assessed risk to himself and others and agreed a plan with an EIP referral tonight, crisis team contact within 24 hours and driving advice"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"“I'm not mental. The neighbours really are filming me. Go and check the alarm.”","r":"I believe this feels completely real and frightening, and I'm not here to argue. I am worried you've barely slept for three weeks; that strain can make the world feel threatening. Can we look at what would help you feel safer?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"“No blood tests. You'll put something in me.”","r":"That's an understandable worry if you feel watched. The tests only check things like thyroid and infection that can cause experiences like these. You can watch every step and ask what each tube is for."},{"tag":"Question","p":"“It's only weed. Everyone smokes. Why does it matter?”","r":"Fair question. Strong daily cannabis is one of the clearest links we know to experiences like yours, and it can make them last longer. I'm not judging you; I want to know how much and what kind, so we get this right."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"“Are you going to lock me up?”","r":"That's not my plan. If you're willing to go home with your flatmate and see a specialist team for young people, that's the safest route. I'll be honest with you about every step."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"“He bought a knife last week. Said it was for protection from them.”","r":"Thank you, that's really important and it changes our safety plan. Do you know where the knife is now? Has he threatened anyone, or talked about confronting the neighbours?"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"“Can't you just call it drug-induced and send him to his GP?”","r":"Even if cannabis is involved, three weeks of persecutory beliefs needs early intervention, not a GP letter. EIP takes cannabis-associated psychosis. I'll refer tonight and arrange crisis team contact tomorrow; please send the bloods."}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, calcium, glucose/HbA1c, lipids, CRP, B12, folate; urine drug screen; ECG; HIV and syphilis serology with consent","CT head and medical review if focal signs, fits, confusion or fluctuating attention","No antipsychotic tonight; if EIP starts one, begin at the bottom of the licensed range after baseline weight, BP, bloods and ECG"],"psych":["EIP package: CBT for psychosis (at least 16 sessions) and family intervention","Brief cannabis intervention now; ongoing support from EIP or the local drug and alcohol service"],"social":["Flatmate told the plan with his consent and given the crisis number","EIP care coordinator to check college or work, money and housing"],"safety":["Crisis resolution and home treatment team contact within 24 hours","Ask directly about weapons and plans to confront neighbours; if he intends to act, discuss admission or MHA assessment with the on-call consultant","Advise no driving; he must tell the DVLA; record the advice","Safety plan with crisis line and NHS 111 option 2; GP letter same day listing bloods to chase"]},"facts":[{"h":"EIP takes cannabis cases","x":"Early intervention teams accept first-episode psychosis whatever the suspected cause, usually ages 14–65. 'Drug-induced' is not a reason to bypass them."},{"h":"Two-week EIP standard","x":"In England, people with first-episode psychosis should start a NICE-recommended package of care within two weeks of referral. Send the referral tonight, not Monday."},{"h":"The four-week abstinence test","x":"Psychotic symptoms persisting well beyond about four weeks after stopping cannabis point towards a primary psychotic illness rather than a purely drug-induced one."},{"h":"Delirium first in A&E","x":"Fluctuating attention, disorientation, visual hallucinations or abnormal observations mean a medical cause until proven otherwise: synthetic cannabinoids, stimulants, withdrawal, infection, encephalitis."},{"h":"Psychosis and the DVLA","x":"Acute psychosis must be notified to the DVLA. Group 1 driving can usually resume after three months well and adherent, if medication doesn't impair driving. Document your advice."},{"h":"Suicide risk is high early","x":"The first year after a first episode carries a raised suicide risk, especially with depression, emerging insight or command voices. Ask directly at every contact."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Mental state examination","do":"Take a first-episode psychosis history and MSE, screen organic causes, assess risk, and refer to early intervention."},"c-2-1-2":{"obj":["Elicited each first-rank symptom with an open question first and a precise follow-up, without leading her","Recorded at least three verbatim quotes showing her beliefs and any thought disorder","Rated her conviction and tested for other explanations without arguing","Checked what she does in response to the voices and passed new risks to the nurse in charge before ward round"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"“Why are you asking me these weird questions? You think I'm crazy.”","r":"I can see why they sound odd. I ask everyone these, because people sometimes have experiences they don't know how to describe. If a question doesn't fit, just tell me and we'll move on."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"“The cars are being controlled. I'm not ill; I'm the only one who sees it.”","r":"I hear that you're certain, and it must be lonely when nobody believes you. Can you tell me how you first realised? What made the traffic lights stand out that day?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"“They talk about me all the time. 'She's walking now, she's sitting now.'”","r":"That sounds exhausting to live with. Thank you for telling me exactly what they say. Do the voices ever tell you to do things? And what do you do when they do?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"“If I say they can read my thoughts, you'll keep me here longer.”","r":"I understand that worry. What you tell me helps us choose the right treatment so you get better sooner. You have rights on this section, including appeal, and staff will explain how."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"“I was just shouting. People shout. Let me out.”","r":"Being detained when you don't feel ill must be really frustrating. You can appeal to a Tribunal, and I'll make sure you know how. Meanwhile, I'd like to understand what was happening for you yesterday."},{"tag":"Question","p":"“Do other people hear their thoughts out loud too?”","r":"Some people with experiences like yours do, and it can be very distressing. Is that something that happens to you? Tell me what it's like."}],"bps":{"bio":["Before any antipsychotic: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, glucose/HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, ECG, weight/BMI, BP, urine drug screen","Delirium screen and medical review if attention fluctuates or observations are abnormal"],"social":["MHA office: s132 rights given and repeated if not understood; IMHA offered","Help to apply to the Tribunal within 14 days if she wishes","Ask her permission to contact family for collateral"],"safety":["Command hallucinations or passivity with intent to act: tell the nurse in charge now and review observation level","Risk assessment updated on the electronic record the same day","Written MSE handed over before the 11:30 ward round"]},"facts":[{"h":"First-rank symptoms aren't diagnostic","x":"They occur in mania, organic states and other psychoses as well as schizophrenia. Record them precisely and let the diagnosis follow the whole picture."},{"h":"Delusional perception has two stages","x":"A normal perception (the traffic light changes) followed by a sudden, unconnected delusional meaning (I am being controlled). Capture both parts in her words."},{"h":"Third person means 'she', not 'you'","x":"Voices discussing her as 'she', arguing about her or commenting on her actions are first-rank. Voices speaking to her as 'you' are second-person and less specific."},{"h":"Quote, don't label","x":"Write her exact words for beliefs and thought disorder. 'Derailment' means little at a Tribunal; a quoted sentence shows the panel what you saw."},{"h":"Section 2 appeal window","x":"A patient on Section 2 can apply to the Tribunal within the first 14 days. Explain rights under Section 132 as soon as practicable and repeat if not understood."},{"h":"Conviction and insight","x":"Ask how sure she is out of ten and whether anything else could explain it. Any doubt is a good sign and shapes how you discuss treatment."}],"type":"Mental state examination","do":"Elicit and accurately describe delusions, hallucinations, passivity, thought interference and formal thought disorder."},"c-2-1-3":{"obj":["Gathered premorbid personality, a dated timeline of decline and family history of mental illness from the brother","Asked specifically about threats, weapons and named targets, and logged the threat to a neighbour","Kept the drug use confidential while still receiving anything the brother volunteered","Offered a carer's assessment, carer information and a family meeting with the patient's consent"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"“Nobody tells us anything! We found out he was here from his landlord.”","r":"That must have been awful, finding out like that, and I'm sorry. I'd like to put it right. He's agreed I can talk with you about how he's doing, and I'd really value what you know about him."},{"tag":"Question","p":"“Is he on drugs? Just tell me straight.”","r":"I can see why you'd want to know. Some things he's asked to keep between him and us, and I need to respect that. I can hear anything you're worried about, though. Have you noticed anything?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"“He said he'd stab the bloke next door if he kept beaming things at him.”","r":"Thank you for telling me; that's really important and you've done the right thing. When did he say this? Does he have any knives or weapons, or has he gone near the neighbour since?"},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"“I should've seen it coming. I told him to snap out of it.”","r":"It's very hard to spot from the inside, and many families say the same. You're here now and helping. What you've seen these past months is exactly what we need."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"“I've been sleeping on his sofa to watch him. I can't keep doing this.”","r":"That sounds exhausting, and you clearly care a great deal. You have a right to support for yourself. A carer's assessment looks at what you need; can I refer you today?"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"“So what's the plan? How long is he in for?”","r":"Fair to ask. He's here informally, so he's agreed to stay while we assess and treat. I can't give a date yet. With his agreement, I'll invite you to a family meeting where we go through the plan together."}],"bps":{"psych":["Family intervention once he is more settled (NICE CG178: at least 10 planned sessions)","General psychoeducation on psychosis for the brother, nothing patient-specific beyond consent"],"social":["Carer's assessment via local authority adult social care (Care Act 2014)","Carer information pack, local carers' centre, Rethink Mental Illness","With consent, invite the brother to the next ward round or a family meeting"],"safety":["Update the risk assessment with the threat to the neighbour; discuss at MDT today","With the consultant, decide whether the threat needs sharing with police or the neighbour, and the plan if he tries to leave","Record what was shared, what was withheld, and why"]},"facts":[{"h":"Receiving isn't disclosing","x":"Taking information from relatives never breaches confidentiality; only sharing does. GMC guidance expects you to listen to people close to the patient."},{"h":"Keep a neutral face","x":"If the brother mentions drugs, don't nod, flinch or say 'I can't comment'. Try: 'Thanks, tell me what you've noticed.' Then move on."},{"h":"Relatives hold the risk history","x":"Threats, weapons and named targets often come only from family. Ask every carer directly: has he said or done anything that frightened you?"},{"h":"Disclosure to protect others","x":"Where there is a serious risk of harm to a named person, you may share without consent in the public interest. Discuss with your consultant and document the reasoning."},{"h":"Triangle of Care","x":"A national standard for involving carers in acute mental health care: identify carers early, give information and a named contact, and offer support."}],"type":"Collateral history","do":"Take a collateral history from the brother within the patient's confidentiality limits and address his concerns."},"c-2-1-4":{"obj":["Engaged him at the door with his care coordinator, without forcing entry or arguing about beliefs","Built a mental state picture from limited contact: appearance, speech, beliefs, the flat, food, utilities","Assessed risk to self and others, self-neglect and exploitation, including the knife by the door","Escalated the same day: consultant informed, AMHP contacted for an MHA assessment, and a Section 135(1) route planned if access is refused"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"“Go away. I don't need you. I'm fine in here.”","r":"Okay, I hear you, and we won't come in without your say. We've been worried because we haven't seen you. Can we just talk here at the door for a few minutes?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"“They're beaming radiation through the walls. The foil keeps it out.”","r":"That sounds really frightening to live with, and I can see you've worked hard to protect yourself. How are you sleeping and eating with all this going on?"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"“You're going to stab me with that needle again. It poisons me.”","r":"I'm sorry the injections have felt like that. We're not giving anything today. I'd like to hear what happened after the last one; we may be able to change the medicine or the dose."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"“Are you here to section me?”","r":"We're here because we care how you are. I can't promise nothing will change; if we're very worried, others may need to see you. Talking with us now gives you the biggest say in what happens."},{"tag":"Question","p":"“Can't you just break the door down and get him?”","r":"I understand how desperate this feels. We can't legally force entry without a warrant. We're arranging a proper assessment today, and if he won't open up, there's a court process to get in safely."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"“I haven't eaten in days. There's no money left.”","r":"Thank you for telling me; that worries me, and we can help. Can we get you some food today? Sam can sort an urgent benefits check. Is your electricity still on?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Restart antipsychotic after assessment: same depot if it worked, once the side effects he blames on it are explored","Physical check at first chance: hydration, weight, BP, glucose/HbA1c, U&Es, signs of infection"],"psych":["Short, low-pressure visits from the care coordinator; no challenging of delusions","Explore what put him off the depot (pain, side effects, beliefs) and agree one change he would accept"],"social":["Care coordinator: food today (food bank voucher), urgent benefits review, check gas and electricity","Mother updated with consent and offered a carer's assessment","Look for cuckooing: unknown visitors, other people's belongings, new locks"],"safety":["Lone working: never alone, tell base before and after, keep the exit behind you","AMHP hub contacted today; two doctors, one s12-approved, one who knows him","If entry refused: AMHP seeks a s135(1) warrant from a magistrate; police attend with the AMHP and a doctor","Safeguarding concern for self-neglect if criteria met; inform GP; update crisis plan"]},"facts":[{"h":"Nobody can just push the door","x":"Doctors, nurses and AMHPs have no power to force entry. Section 136 applies only outside a private home. Use a Section 135(1) warrant."},{"h":"How Section 135(1) works","x":"An AMHP lays information before a magistrate. The warrant lets police enter, with an AMHP and a doctor, and remove him to a place of safety, where he can be held for up to 24 hours."},{"h":"MHA assessment line-up","x":"An AMHP plus two doctors, at least one Section 12-approved and ideally one who already knows him. Request via the AMHP hub with the risk details ready."},{"h":"Foil and barricades mean relapse","x":"Covered windows, missed depots and not going out are classic relapse signs. Look past the door: smell, food, piled-up post, utilities."},{"h":"Lone-working basics","x":"Go in pairs, tell base your plan and call-back time, keep the exit behind you, and leave if you feel unsafe. A knife by the door changes the plan."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"Engage him at the door, complete an MSE and risk assessment, and decide next steps."},"c-2-1-5":{"obj":["Mapped a relapse timeline with her: when tablets stopped, what changed first, what others noticed","Asked directly about akathisia, weight, sedation and sexual side effects as reasons for stopping","Identified her personal early warning signs and wrote them into a relapse prevention plan she owns","Offered realistic options including a long-acting injection without coercion, and set up dual-diagnosis input and a named care coordinator before discharge"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"“I know, I stopped the tablets. It's my fault I'm back.”","r":"Lots of people stop at some point, and there's usually a good reason. I'm not here to blame you. Tell me what made them hard to take; that's what we need to fix."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"“They made me pace all night. I couldn't sit still. So I stopped.”","r":"That restless feeling sounds horrible, and it's a real side effect called akathisia, not you being difficult. It can be treated, with a lower dose or another medicine. I'm glad you told me."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"“Cannabis helps me. It's the only thing that calms me down.”","r":"I can see why it feels that way, especially after the break-up. It also seems to bring the voices back quicker. Would you be open to seeing a worker who helps people cut down, without judging?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"“I'm not having a depot. That's for people who are forced.”","r":"I hear that. An injection isn't a punishment, and nobody is deciding it for you today. Some people choose it so they don't have to remember tablets. Shall I explain the options, and you decide?"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"“My care coordinator left and nobody replaced her. Two months!”","r":"You're right, and that gap shouldn't have happened. I'm sorry. Before you leave, I'll make sure you have a named care coordinator and a visit within three days of discharge."},{"tag":"Question","p":"“How will I know it's starting again next time?”","r":"That's the heart of this. Let's list what you and others noticed first: maybe sleep, feeling people were against you, staying in your room. We'll write them down with who to call."}],"bps":{"bio":["Rate akathisia with the Barnes scale; first reduce the aripiprazole dose, or add propranolol 10 mg two to three times daily (off-label; check pulse and BP; avoid in asthma)","If she chooses an LAI: aripiprazole 400 mg IM monthly with 14 days of oral aripiprazole after the first injection; 300 mg if side effects","Weight, HbA1c and lipids at baseline and 12 weeks; urine drug screen with consent"],"psych":["Relapse prevention work with her named nurse and care coordinator: early warning signs, triggers, action plan","CBT for psychosis via CMHT psychology (at least 16 sessions)","Family or partner involved in the plan, with consent"],"social":["Dual-diagnosis worker referral before discharge","CMHT confirms a named care coordinator; s117 aftercare meeting with social care","Support after the break-up: housing, benefits, daytime activity"],"safety":["Follow-up within 72 hours of discharge","Written crisis plan with early warning signs and who to call","GP letter: medication plan, monitoring, what to do if she misses doses or an injection"]},"facts":[{"h":"Akathisia makes people quit","x":"Inner restlessness and pacing are often mistaken for anxiety or agitation. Aripiprazole is a common cause. Ask directly; patients rarely name it and simply stop."},{"h":"Aripiprazole LAI needs oral overlap","x":"400 mg IM monthly. Continue oral aripiprazole for 14 days after the first injection so levels stay covered. Drop to 300 mg if side effects."},{"h":"Propranolol for akathisia","x":"Low-dose propranolol, 10 mg two or three times daily, can help. Check pulse and BP; avoid in asthma. Reducing the antipsychotic dose comes first."},{"h":"Relapse is usually multi-causal","x":"Stopped tablets, cannabis, a break-up and a service gap often stack up. Ask about all four; fixing adherence alone repeats the cycle."},{"h":"LAIs aren't only for detained patients","x":"Offer a long-acting injection to anyone who prefers it or has relapsed after stopping oral treatment. It's a choice, and it shows missed doses early."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Explore adherence, cannabis and stress behind the relapse non-judgementally, and agree a relapse prevention plan."},"c-2-1-6":{"obj":["Explored what they already understand and fear about 'schizophrenia' before explaining anything","Explained the diagnosis, causes and treatments in small chunks with no jargon","Gave balanced genetic risk for the sister and an honest, hopeful outlook","Addressed the mother's guilt and violence stigma, checked understanding with teach-back, and gave written information"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"“Is this our fault? Did I do something wrong when he was little?”","r":"No, it isn't your fault. Parenting doesn't cause schizophrenia. It comes from a mix of genes, how the brain develops, and stresses like cannabis. You being here is one of the best things for his recovery."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"“Will his sister get it too?”","r":"Her risk is higher than average, but most brothers and sisters never develop it: roughly one in ten, against one in a hundred generally. Avoiding cannabis and getting help early if things change both help."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"“He's not schizophrenic. He was just stressed from his job.”","r":"Stress definitely played a part, and I understand this word is hard to hear. We use it because the voices and beliefs have lasted eight months. Naming it means we can offer treatments that work."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"“Does this mean he's dangerous? You read about it in the papers.”","r":"That's a very common fear, and the news makes it worse. Most people with schizophrenia are never violent, and they're more likely to be victims of crime. The bigger risks are to his own health."},{"tag":"Question","p":"“Will I have to take tablets for the rest of my life?”","r":"Not necessarily. We usually advise at least a year or two once you're well, then review together. Some people come off slowly with support; others stay on because it keeps them well. You'll be part of every decision."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"“So my life's over. No job, no girlfriend, nothing.”","r":"I can hear how heavy this feels. It isn't over. Many people with this diagnosis work, have relationships and live full lives. The team can help you back into work when you're ready. What matters most to you?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Antipsychotic chosen with him; weight weekly for 6 weeks, then 12 weeks, 1 year and yearly; HbA1c and lipids at 12 weeks and yearly","Advise stopping cannabis; offer smoking cessation support"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis via EIP, at least 16 sessions","Family intervention: at least 10 sessions over 3 months to a year, mother and sister invited"],"social":["Individual Placement and Support (IPS) employment specialist referral","Carer's assessment for his mother (Care Act 2014); Rethink Mental Illness information","He notifies the DVLA; record the advice given"],"safety":["Crisis plan with EIP duty number and NHS 111 option 2","Ask about suicidal thoughts today; review at next contact","Follow-up meeting in 1–2 weeks to revisit questions"]},"facts":[{"h":"Genetic risk in rough numbers","x":"General population: about 1 in 100. Sibling: about 1 in 10. Identical twin: around 1 in 2. Say 'roughly', and stress that most relatives stay well."},{"h":"Course varies widely","x":"Some recover after one episode, some have recurring episodes with good recovery between, and some have lasting symptoms. Early, sustained treatment improves the odds."},{"h":"Signs of a better outlook","x":"Sudden onset, a clear trigger, prominent mood symptoms, good functioning beforehand, early treatment and a supportive family all predict a better course."},{"h":"Physical health is the big killer","x":"People with schizophrenia die around 15–20 years earlier, mostly from heart disease, diabetes and smoking. Annual physical checks and stopping smoking matter as much as the antipsychotic."},{"h":"Leaflet and a second meeting","x":"People retain little after hearing a diagnosis. Written information and a follow-up to answer questions are expected and appreciated."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis","do":"Explain schizophrenia, its causes, genetics, treatment and prognosis in plain English, and answer family questions."},"c-2-1-7":{"obj":["Found out what matters most to her in a medicine before comparing options","Compared two or three antipsychotics on weight, prolactin, sedation and movement effects in plain words","Explained a long-acting injection: how often, where, oral overlap, side effects, and that she can change her mind","Agreed a switch plan with a cross-taper, a contraception check and a dated metabolic monitoring schedule"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"“I've put on 8 kilos. I hate how I look. I'm stopping it.”","r":"That's a big change in a short time, and it's completely reasonable to be upset. Please don't stop suddenly; that's when you've relapsed before. Let's look at options that are kinder on weight."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"“An injection? That feels like you're controlling me.”","r":"I understand why it might feel like that. It's your choice; we wouldn't force it while you're informal and able to decide. Some people like it because they're free of daily tablets. Can I explain how it works?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"“How often would I need it, and does it hurt?”","r":"Depending on the medicine, every month or even every two or three months. It goes into the arm or buttock, like a vaccine; it may be sore for a day or two. We swap sides each time."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"“Can I just take olanzapine when I feel bad?”","r":"I see why that's tempting. It works by preventing symptoms, so taking it on and off is when people relapse, as happened twice before. A medicine you can live with every day is the goal."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"“We're thinking about trying for a baby next year.”","r":"Thank you for telling me; that matters. Let's choose a medicine with that in mind and plan it with your GP before you try. Until then, keep using contraception."},{"tag":"Question","p":"“Will the weight come off if I switch?”","r":"Often some does, especially with diet and activity support, but not always all of it. Switching to aripiprazole gives you the best chance. We'll weigh you regularly so we can see what's working."}],"bps":{"bio":["Cross-taper over 2–4 weeks: start aripiprazole 10 mg each morning (usual range 10–30 mg) while reducing olanzapine stepwise; pharmacist writes the plan","Once oral aripiprazole is tolerated: aripiprazole LAI 400 mg monthly (deltoid or gluteal) with 14 days' oral cover","Watch for akathisia, insomnia and nausea during the switch","Weight weekly for 6 weeks; weight, BP, HbA1c and lipids at 12 weeks, then yearly via GP or CMHT","If weight keeps rising after the switch: metformin on specialist advice, with renal function checked"],"psych":["Dietitian and activity programme; weekly ward weights","Shared decision documented, with a Choice and Medication leaflet; her decision by Friday"],"social":["Depot clinic or CMHT nurse books the LAI; date and site agreed with her","GP letter with the plan and monitoring schedule"],"safety":["Capacity and the shared decision recorded in the notes","Missed injection: care coordinator contacts her the same week","Contraception and pregnancy plans recorded; preconception review with GP before trying"]},"facts":[{"h":"Olanzapine LAI: 3 hours' watch","x":"After each olanzapine pamoate injection, observe for at least 3 hours in a healthcare setting for post-injection delirium/sedation syndrome: drowsiness, confusion, ataxia."},{"h":"Paliperidone LAI loading","x":"150 mg on day 1 and 100 mg on day 8, both deltoid, then monthly (usually 75 mg; range 25–150 mg). It raises prolactin: ask about periods and breast milk leakage."},{"h":"Aripiprazole: low metabolic burden","x":"Little weight gain and no prolactin rise. Watch early for akathisia, insomnia and nausea. Start 10 mg each morning; usual range 10–30 mg."},{"h":"Metabolic monitoring timetable","x":"Baseline weight, waist, BP, HbA1c, lipids. Weight weekly for 6 weeks, then at 12 weeks and 1 year; HbA1c and lipids at 12 weeks and 1 year, then yearly."},{"h":"Prolactin and fertility","x":"Prolactin-raising drugs (risperidone, paliperidone, amisulpride) can stop periods and lower fertility. Switching to a prolactin-sparing drug can restore fertility quickly: discuss contraception."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Agree an antipsychotic through shared decision-making and explain long-acting injectable treatment."},"c-2-1-8":{"obj":["Named akathisia to her as a medication side effect and rated it on the Barnes scale before any PRN decision","Checked for dystonia, parkinsonism, tardive movements and NMS signs, and recorded a baseline","Sent prolactin, hCG, TFTs, U&E and CK, with pregnancy excluded before blaming the drug","Agreed with her a dose cut or prolactin-sparing switch, plus propranolol if safe","Asked directly about self-harm and aggression linked to the restlessness and set her observation level"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't stay in my own skin. I'd rather be dead than feel like this.\"","r":"\"That sounds unbearable, and I'm glad you told me. This is a known side effect and we can ease it today. Have you had thoughts of acting on that, of hurting yourself?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"The nurses keep saying I'm agitated and giving me more of the same stuff.\"","r":"\"You're right to question that. I think this is the medicine causing restlessness, not your illness. I'll ask them not to give extra haloperidol for it and write a different plan.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking that tablet again. Not tonight, not ever.\"","r":"\"I understand why. Let's not just push on with what's hurting you. I'd like to lower the dose or change to one that's gentler on restlessness and hormones. Can we plan that together?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Why am I leaking milk? Is something seriously wrong with me?\"","r":"\"That must be frightening. The medicine can raise a hormone called prolactin, which does exactly this. It usually settles when we change the drug. I'll check a blood test and a pregnancy test to be sure.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I ever get my periods back? I want kids one day.\"","r":"\"Yes, in most women periods return once prolactin falls after a switch. Your wish for children matters to how we choose your medicine, so I'll make sure the team plans with that in mind.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've been spitting the tablets out for two days.\"","r":"\"Thanks for being honest, that helps me. It tells me how bad this feels. Let's fix the side effects so the treatment works for you rather than against you.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Propranolol 10 mg two to three times daily, up to 30–80 mg/day; check pulse and BP before each dose; avoid in asthma","Reduce haloperidol, or switch to aripiprazole (start 10 mg once daily, range 10–30 mg) with pharmacist cross-taper plan","Avoid procyclidine for akathisia; keep it only for dystonia or parkinsonism","Repeat Barnes scale daily and prolactin 6–8 weeks after the change"],"psych":["Explain akathisia and raised prolactin in plain words and give a written side-effect leaflet","Agree with her a signal to staff when restlessness peaks so she is not labelled agitated"],"social":["Named nurse to tell family or carer (with consent) why her medicine is changing","Ward pharmacist gives a medicines counselling session on the new drug before discharge"],"safety":["Raise observation level while akathisia is severe; nurse in charge informed","No PRN antipsychotic for restlessness; call duty doctor if distress escalates","Escalate to medical team if fever, rigidity, confusion or raised CK appear (possible NMS)","Review on next consultant ward round"]},"facts":[{"h":"Akathisia raises suicide risk","x":"Inner restlessness can drive impulsive self-harm or aggression. Always ask about it directly and record the answer, rather than assuming the cause is low mood."},{"h":"Look while she stands still","x":"Ask her to sit, then stand still for two minutes. Watch for shifting weight, rocking or marching on the spot. The Barnes scale needs both what she feels and what you see."},{"h":"Anticholinergics do little here","x":"Procyclidine helps dystonia and parkinsonism, not akathisia. First-line is lowering the dose or switching; propranolol or low-dose mirtazapine (15 mg at night) can help."},{"h":"Prolactin-sparing choices","x":"Aripiprazole, quetiapine and clozapine barely raise prolactin. Risperidone, amisulpride and haloperidol raise it most. Long-term high prolactin thins bone."},{"h":"Pregnancy test before prolactin blame","x":"Missed periods plus breast milk can mean pregnancy. Check hCG first. Very high prolactin, headache or visual field loss: think pituitary tumour and discuss imaging."}],"type":"Medication review + Physical examination","do":"Examine for akathisia and EPSE, explain likely hyperprolactinaemia, and agree a medication plan."},"c-2-1-9":{"obj":["Explained why clozapine is offered after two failed antipsychotics, with honest odds of benefit","Named the life-threatening risks (low neutrophils, myocarditis, severe constipation, seizures) and the red-flag symptoms for each","Set out the blood test schedule and the red, amber and green results in plain words","Agreed with him and his mother whether titration happens at home or on the ward, with daily observations arranged","Gave the smoking and missed-dose rules and checked they could repeat them back"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I've read it can kill you. Why would I take that?\"","r":"\"It's right to ask. The serious risks are rare and we watch for them closely with blood tests and checks. For voices that haven't shifted with two medicines, it's the one most likely to help.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Weekly needles for months? I'm not a pin cushion.\"","r":"\"It is a lot, I agree. It's weekly for the first 18 weeks, then every two weeks, then monthly. Some clinics use a finger-prick test. The checks are what make it safe.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I still have a smoke?\"","r":"\"Yes, but tell us before you cut down or stop, even for a hospital stay or switching to vaping. Stopping makes clozapine levels rise fast, so we'd lower the dose.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I've watched him suffer for years. Nothing works.\" (mother)","r":"\"That has clearly been exhausting for you. Clozapine is different from what he's had; many people who haven't responded before do improve. Your help spotting problems early will matter a lot.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't we just try a third normal tablet first?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting something familiar. The evidence says a third ordinary antipsychotic rarely helps when two have failed, while clozapine often does. Waiting longer means more time with the voices.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if I forget a couple of days?\"","r":"\"If you miss more than two days, don't restart at your usual dose. Call us, because we need to restart from a low dose and build up again to stay safe.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Clozapine 12.5 mg once or twice on day 1, then 25–50 mg day 2, up by 25–50 mg/day to 300 mg/day over 2–3 weeks; usual range 200–450 mg/day","Pulse, BP and temperature before each dose during titration; troponin and CRP weekly for the first 4 weeks per local protocol","FBC weekly for 18 weeks, fortnightly to week 52, then 4-weekly; weight, lipids and HbA1c at baseline, 3 months, then yearly","Laxative from day 1 (senna 7.5–15 mg at night plus a softener); hyoscine hydrobromide 300 micrograms at night if drooling","Plasma level if poor response or smoking changes; aim at least 350 micrograms/L"],"psych":["Written clozapine booklet and red-flag card; teach-back with him and his mother","Continue CBT for psychosis for residual voices via the psychology team"],"social":["Care coordinator arranges clinic or home treatment visits for titration and blood tests","Mother offered a carer's assessment through the local authority","GP letter so clozapine appears on the primary care record"],"safety":["Same-day contact for fever, sore throat, chest pain, breathlessness or no bowel movement for 3 days","Missed more than 48 hours: retitrate from 12.5 mg","Clozapine clinic and pharmacy registered before first dose"]},"facts":[{"h":"Constipation kills more than neutropenia","x":"Clozapine slows the gut; obstruction can be fatal. Ask about bowels at every contact and prescribe a laxative early. Ask specifically, as patients rarely volunteer it."},{"h":"Myocarditis peaks in month one","x":"Watch for fever, tachycardia above 120, chest pain or breathlessness in the first 8 weeks. Rising troponin or CRP: stop and get a cardiology opinion."},{"h":"Smoking change shifts levels","x":"Tobacco smoke speeds clozapine breakdown. Stopping smoking can push levels up by about half within days. Reduce dose and check a level. Vaping does not have this effect."},{"h":"48-hour missed-dose rule","x":"More than 48 hours without clozapine means restarting at 12.5 mg and titrating again, because collapse and fits can occur on full-dose restart."},{"h":"Benign fever and tachycardia are common","x":"Mild fever and fast pulse often happen early in titration. Check FBC, troponin and CRP before calling it benign."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain clozapine benefits, risks and blood monitoring, and agree a safe community or ward start."},"c-2-1-10":{"obj":["Screened for depression, antipsychotic side effects, sedation and ongoing cannabis use before calling it negative symptoms","Explained negative symptoms to the father in plain words while keeping the son's confidences","Offered CBT for psychosis (at least 16 sessions) and family intervention (at least 10 sessions) with named referrals","Agreed one concrete goal with the son, such as a supported return to college","Arranged a carer's assessment and support for the father"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You lot are doing nothing. He just rots in his room.\"","r":"\"I can hear how frustrated and worried you are, and you're right that the voices were only part of it. Let me explain what we can do next, because there is more on offer.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is he just being lazy? Should I push him harder?\"","r":"\"It isn't laziness; low drive is part of the illness. Pushing hard often backfires. Small, regular steps that he chooses work better, and family sessions can show you how.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Can't you just tell me what he's said to you?\"","r":"\"I can't share what he's told me without his agreement, but I can listen to everything you've noticed, explain the illness and the treatments, and ask him if he'll let us talk together.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to talk to some therapist. What's the point?\"","r":"\"Fair enough to feel that. It isn't just talking about the past; it's working on things you want, like getting back to college. Would you try a first session and decide after?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I used to be good at stuff. Now I'm nothing.\"","r":"\"That sounds really painful. Losing what you were good at is a huge loss. I want to check whether low mood is part of this, because that is very treatable too.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will he ever be like he was?\" (father)","r":"\"Many people recover a lot of function, especially with early help. It takes months, not weeks. The therapies and support for work or study improve his chances.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Check aripiprazole dose; if sedated or akathisic, review dose with consultant (range 10–30 mg/day)","Screen depression (PHQ-9) and treat if present: sertraline 50 mg once daily","Annual physical health check by EIP: weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids, smoking; stop cannabis advice"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, at least 16 sessions, EIP psychology team","Family intervention, at least 10 sessions over 3–12 months, trained EIP clinicians, with son included","Consider arts therapies or social skills group run by the occupational therapist"],"social":["Individual Placement and Support worker or supported education for return to college","Care coordinator to plan a graded weekly activity timetable","Carer's assessment by the local authority; carer support group details for the father"],"safety":["Check suicidal thoughts; post-psychotic depression is a high-risk period","Crisis line number given to son and father; review at next EIP appointment"]},"facts":[{"h":"Rule out secondary negative symptoms","x":"Depression, akathisia or parkinsonism, sedation, cannabis and untreated positive symptoms all mimic negative symptoms. Treat these first before calling it primary."},{"h":"NICE session numbers to quote","x":"CBT for psychosis: at least 16 planned sessions. Family intervention: at least 10 sessions over 3 to 12 months, with the patient involved where possible."},{"h":"Confidentiality works both ways","x":"You can always listen to a relative and give general information. Only sharing his personal details needs his consent."},{"h":"Work and study protect recovery","x":"Individual Placement and Support gets more people into jobs than training-first schemes. Offer it early, not when the person is 'fully better'."}],"type":"Therapy assessment","do":"Assess negative symptoms, exclude secondary causes, and discuss CBT for psychosis and family intervention."},"c-2-1-11":{"obj":["Screened for re-experiencing, avoidance and hyperarousal without asking for details of the assault","Took a non-judgemental cannabis history including amount, cost, route and what it does for her","Put trauma-informed measures in the ward care plan, including female staff for night checks","Agreed referrals to psychology for trauma-focused therapy and to the dual diagnosis worker","Checked for ongoing risk from the perpetrator and offered reporting and support options"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"When men come in at night I think it's happening again.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying, and it makes complete sense after what happened. We'll arrange for women to do your night checks where we can, and staff will knock and say who they are.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've never told anyone about it. I don't want to go into it.\"","r":"\"Thank you for trusting me. You don't need to tell me any details. I just want to know how it affects you now, like sleep, nightmares and feeling on edge.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Weed is the only thing that lets me sleep.\"","r":"\"I can see why you'd use it then. It may help sleep tonight but seems to bring the voices back. Can we look at other ways to sleep and help you cut down?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Maybe it was my fault. I was out of it that night.\"","r":"\"It was not your fault. Nobody is to blame for being assaulted, whatever state they were in. The person who did it is responsible.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will therapy make me relive it all?\"","r":"\"Trauma therapy is paced and you stay in control. It usually starts with feeling safer and coping skills, and it works for people with psychosis too.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want the police involved.\"","r":"\"That's your decision, and I respect it. You can change your mind at any time. Let me give you details of support services that you can contact without reporting it.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue antipsychotic; review dose once cannabis stops, as sedation may rise","Short-term sleep plan: sleep hygiene first; promethazine 25 mg at night if needed rather than benzodiazepines","Urine drug screen with consent; monitor cannabis withdrawal (irritability, poor sleep, peaks days 2–6)"],"psych":["Trauma-focused CBT or EMDR via ward or CMHT psychology once stable; psychosis is not a bar","Motivational interviewing for cannabis by the dual diagnosis worker","Grounding and stabilisation skills taught by named nurse before therapy starts"],"social":["Sexual Assault Referral Centre and Rape Crisis details if she wishes","Check for ongoing contact with the perpetrator, housing safety and finances funding cannabis"],"safety":["Female staff for night checks where possible; knock and introduce; avoid male-led restraint","Safeguarding team referral if abuse or exploitation is ongoing","Suicide risk check, as PTSD plus psychosis raises it","CMHT and drug service follow-up within 72 hours of discharge"]},"facts":[{"h":"Don't ask for the story","x":"You need symptoms, not the narrative. Ask about nightmares, flashbacks, avoidance, startle and sleep. Forcing details can re-traumatise and break trust."},{"h":"PTSD treatment first-line","x":"Trauma-focused CBT or EMDR, usually 8–12 sessions. Psychosis and substance use are not reasons to exclude her."},{"h":"Ask what cannabis does for her","x":"People use for sleep, fear or boredom. Naming the function lets you replace it. Moralising shuts the conversation down."},{"h":"High-potency cannabis raises relapse","x":"Daily use of strong cannabis increases psychotic relapse and admission. Cutting down, not just stopping, is still worth it."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Assess PTSD symptoms and cannabis use alongside psychosis, and agree one integrated plan."},"c-2-1-12":{"obj":["Asked directly about intent, plan, timing, weapons and past violence towards the named neighbour","Gave the on-call consultant a clear SBAR and secured an MHA assessment via the AMHP","Agreed and recorded a minimum, justified disclosure to police to protect the neighbour","Checked suicide risk and physical causes alongside the risk to others","Set an immediate safety plan in A&E: room, security, observation and medication"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"He's poisoning my water. I can taste it. Nobody believes me.\"","r":"\"That sounds really frightening. I can see you believe you're in danger. I want to help you feel safe tonight. Can you tell me what you're planning to do about him?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I'll sort him out myself. I've got knives at home.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being straight with me. I'm worried someone could get badly hurt, including you. That's why I think you need to stay in hospital tonight while we help with these fears.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So you're locking me up and he gets away with it?\"","r":"\"I get why it feels unfair. Being here is about keeping you and others safe while we treat the fear driving this. It isn't a punishment.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not having the injection again. It made me a zombie.\"","r":"\"I hear you. Feeling like that is a fair reason to stop. Let's talk about a lower dose or a different medicine, but right now I'm worried about your safety and your neighbour's.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Let me go home and I promise I won't do anything.\"","r":"\"I appreciate you saying that. But you've told me you planned to act tonight and you have knives at home. I can't safely agree to that, so I'm asking colleagues to assess you now.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Are you going to tell the police?\"","r":"\"I may need to share limited information to keep someone safe, and I'll tell you if I do. I'll only share what is needed to prevent harm.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Oral lorazepam 1–2 mg if agitated; if refused and risk escalates, IM per rapid tranquillisation policy with ECG and obs after","Check physical causes: glucose, drug screen, NEWS2","Restart previous antipsychotic after consultant review; check last depot date and whether a test dose is needed"],"psych":["Structured risk formulation using HCR-20 headings, recorded in the risk assessment","Once settled, explore why he stopped the depot and what side effects he had"],"social":["Duty AMHP coordinates MHA assessment (s2 or s3) with a Section 12 doctor","Care coordinator informed same night or next morning; nearest relative identified by AMHP","Bed manager asked for acute or PICU bed with risk level flagged"],"safety":["Safe room near exit, security nearby, never alone with door blocked","Police informed (999 if imminent); disclosure reason and content recorded","Continuous observation in A&E until assessed; ask about suicide too","Forensic or MAPPA lead informed next working day if relevant"]},"facts":[{"h":"Section 5(2) does not work in A&E","x":"A&E is not an inpatient ward. Use a full MHA assessment via the AMHP. Common law can briefly stop someone leaving in an emergency."},{"h":"Specific, named, imminent equals high risk","x":"A named victim, a plan for tonight, access to weapons and past violence together demand urgent action whatever his scores say."},{"h":"Disclosure in the public interest","x":"GMC guidance allows sharing without consent to prevent serious harm. Share the minimum, tell him if safe, and record what, to whom and why."},{"h":"Persecutory delusions plus anger","x":"Feeling threatened and angry about it drives most psychotic violence. Ask how he plans to protect himself."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Assess violence risk to the named neighbour, decide immediate management, and plan protection of others."},"c-2-1-13":{"obj":["Explored his beliefs, checking and accusations without confronting him with his wife's report","Saw his wife alone and completed a DASH risk checklist with her","Made a same-day children's social care referral through the MASH","Asked about alcohol, weapons, past partners and threats to any supposed rival","Agreed a treatment and safety plan, including IDVA and MARAC where indicated"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I barely touched her. She's exaggerating to cover what she's doing.\"","r":"\"I can see you feel very hurt by what you believe is happening. Whatever happened last week, pushing her is something we need to talk about. How did it come to that?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I've got proof. Marks on her underwear, texts deleted. Don't tell me I'm mad.\"","r":"\"I'm not calling you mad. I can see how sure you feel and how much it torments you. I'm worried about what these thoughts are doing to you and your family.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"If I find out who he is, he's finished.\"","r":"\"That sounds like a lot of anger. When you say finished, what do you picture doing? I need to know whether anyone might get hurt.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If social services come, they'll take my kids.\" (wife)","r":"\"That's a common fear. Their aim is to support you to keep the children safe, not to remove them. Sharing this helps you get protection rather than going through it alone.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Maybe if I hadn't worked late he wouldn't think this.\" (wife)","r":"\"None of this is your fault. His beliefs come from an illness, and his behaviour is his responsibility. You deserve to feel safe at home.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't need tablets. I need her to tell the truth.\"","r":"\"I understand you feel the problem is her. Treatment can ease how much these thoughts take over your day and your sleep. Would you try it for that?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Antipsychotic for delusional disorder after consultant review, such as risperidone 1–2 mg/day, titrate slowly to 4 mg/day; check prolactin","Alcohol history with AUDIT; refer to alcohol services; no benzodiazepine prescribing in the community"],"psych":["Engagement-focused work by CMHT psychology once safe; CBT for delusions may help some","Separate support for the wife via IDVA; no couples therapy while abuse is ongoing"],"social":["Children's social care referral via MASH today, written follow-up within 48 hours","IDVA referral for the wife; MARAC if DASH is high risk","School safeguarding lead informed through children's services as needed"],"safety":["Wife given National Domestic Abuse Helpline 0808 2000 247 and 999 advice","Trust safeguarding lead informed; risk assessment and crisis plan updated","Consider admission or MHA assessment if threats to wife or rival escalate","Duty review within one week; GP informed"]},"facts":[{"h":"Morbid jealousy can kill","x":"It carries a real risk of serious violence and homicide, mostly to the partner, sometimes to the imagined rival. Checking and interrogating often come before attacks."},{"h":"Never confront him with her report","x":"Quoting the wife can put her at once in danger. Ask open questions about the relationship and let him describe the checking."},{"h":"Children make it safeguarding","x":"Children living with domestic abuse are at risk of harm. Refer the same day. Usually tell parents first, but don't seek consent if it would raise risk."},{"h":"Separation is a key safety measure","x":"Physical separation reduces the risk most. Ask where she could stay and offer help with emergency accommodation through the IDVA."},{"h":"Alcohol multiplies the risk","x":"Heavy drinking increases jealousy and disinhibits violence. Always check it."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Safety plan","do":"Assess his morbid jealousy and risk to his wife and children, then make a safety plan."},"c-2-2-1":{"obj":["Covered core depressive symptoms, bipolar and psychosis screens, and a full MSE","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts, plans, preparation and protective reasons","Agreed with her what may and may not be shared before speaking to her partner","Used the partner's account to revise risk and recorded any mismatch","Agreed a written safety plan and next contact date, and checked for children at home"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm just tired. Work's been mad. He's overreacting.\"","r":"\"Work stress is real and wears people down. Can I ask a few more questions about sleep, appetite and how you've felt in yourself? That helps me see the full picture.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Are you going to tell him everything I've said?\"","r":"\"No, not without your agreement. I'd like to hear what he's noticed. What would you be happy for me to share, and is there anything you'd rather keep private?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"She's been giving her things away and wrote letters to the kids.\" (partner)","r":"\"Thank you, that's really important and I'm glad you told me. I need to speak with her again about this today, and we will make a plan to keep her safe.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm a terrible mum. They'd be better off without me.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry you feel that way. Depression makes people believe things like this. Are you having thoughts about ending your life? I'd like to understand how strong they are.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I don't know what to do anymore. She won't talk to me.\" (partner)","r":"\"It's hard watching someone you love struggle. You're doing the right thing by speaking to us. Here's what you can do and who to call if you're worried.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want tablets. I'm not that kind of person.\"","r":"\"That's fine to feel. Talking therapy is just as valid a choice. Let's look at the options together and what has helped you before.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["If moderate or severe and she chooses medication: sertraline 50 mg once daily, increase by 50 mg steps no sooner than weekly, max 200 mg","Warn of early nausea, headache, anxiety; review at 1 week if raised risk, otherwise 2 weeks","Bloods via GP: FBC, TFT, U&E, LFT, glucose"],"psych":["CBT, behavioural activation or interpersonal therapy via NHS Talking Therapies or CMHT psychology","Collaborative safety plan written with her"],"social":["Partner (with consent) told the plan and crisis contacts; offer carer's assessment","Ask about childcare, work and finances; fit note if needed"],"safety":["Children at home: consider their welfare and refer to safeguarding if at risk","Crisis line and NHS 111 mental health option given to both","Discuss with consultant if collateral raises risk; consider home treatment team","Follow-up within 1 week if risk raised"]},"facts":[{"h":"Listening to relatives is always allowed","x":"Consent limits what you share, not what you hear. Take the partner's account even if she declines sharing, and record it."},{"h":"Collateral trumps self-report","x":"When a relative describes preparation, such as letters, giving things away or stockpiling, act on it even if she denies intent."},{"h":"Don't rely on risk scores","x":"NICE advises against using scales to predict suicide or decide care. Use the conversation and a formulation instead."},{"h":"Under-25s need earlier review","x":"Antidepressants can increase suicidal thoughts early on in under-25s. Review at 1 week for them or anyone at higher risk."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Collateral history","do":"Take a depression history, MSE and risk assessment, then obtain consented collateral from her partner."},"c-2-2-2":{"obj":["Explored her nihilistic and guilty beliefs, voices and suicidal thoughts without arguing or colluding","Recorded fluid intake, urine output, NEWS2 and named the urgent bloods, including refeeding markers","Assessed her capacity to refuse food, fluids and treatment, specific to each decision","Escalated to the consultant the same evening with a plan for combined drug treatment and an urgent ECT referral","Agreed observation level, fluid chart and medical triggers with the nurse in charge"],"snip":[{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"My insides are rotten. There's nothing left in there to feed.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying to live with. I believe it feels completely real. I think a serious depression is making your body feel this way. Will you try a few sips with me now?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I've done terrible things. I deserve whatever comes.\"","r":"\"I can hear how heavy that guilt is. Depression often makes people feel they deserve punishment. Do you ever have thoughts of punishing yourself, or ending your life?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"No food, no water. There's no point.\"","r":"\"I hear that it feels pointless. Without fluids your kidneys could fail within days, and I can't let that happen without trying to help. Could you manage a drink if a nurse sits with you?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is she going mad? She's never been like this.\" (husband)","r":"\"She's very unwell with a severe depression that has affected her beliefs. It's treatable, often very well, but it is urgent. We're starting treatment and checking her physical health tonight.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Don't let them give me electric shocks.\"","r":"\"No one will do anything without talking it through with you first. It's one option we may discuss because it works quickly for severe depression like this. For now, let's focus on fluids.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why can't she just go home? She came in voluntarily.\" (husband)","r":"\"I understand. Right now she isn't drinking and her beliefs are putting her at risk. If she tried to leave, we might have to use the Mental Health Act to keep her safe.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods tonight: U&E, FBC, LFT, glucose, calcium, TFT, CK, phosphate and magnesium; ECG","Medical review for IV or subcutaneous fluids if no oral intake for 24 hours or AKI","Sertraline 50 mg once daily plus olanzapine 5 mg at night (range 5–20 mg); check ECG and glucose","Urgent ECT referral if intake stays poor or she is at high suicide risk; anaesthetic pre-assessment"],"psych":["Short, calm contacts; don't argue about the delusions; give hope that treatment works","Explain the plan to her husband and ask what has worked before"],"social":["Husband updated (with consent or in her best interests); nearest relative recorded","Ward dietitian review for weight and refeeding risk"],"safety":["1:1 observation tonight; nurse in charge informed of suicide and self-neglect risk","Fluid chart, NEWS2 4-hourly, daily weight","AMHP and MHA assessment if she lacks capacity and refuses, or tries to leave (s5(2) if needed)","Consultant review the same evening, then daily"]},"facts":[{"h":"Days, not weeks","x":"A woman refusing fluids can develop acute kidney injury in 48–72 hours. Psychotic depression is a medical and psychiatric emergency."},{"h":"Refeeding risk after 5 days","x":"Little or no intake for 5 days or more means refeeding risk. Check phosphate, potassium and magnesium before feeding, then daily; involve the dietitian."},{"h":"Combine, don't monotherapy","x":"NICE: offer an antidepressant plus an antipsychotic for psychotic depression. An antidepressant alone works less well. ECT is first-line when life is at risk."},{"h":"ECT rules under the MHA","x":"A capacitous refusal of ECT stands, except under s62 when ECT is immediately necessary to save life or prevent serious deterioration. If she lacks capacity and is detained, a SOAD must certify it."},{"h":"Look for catatonia","x":"Mutism, staring, posturing or resisting movement suggest catatonia. A lorazepam challenge (1–2 mg) may help both diagnosis and treatment."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Take a psychotic depression history and MSE, judge urgency, and agree an immediate plan with nursing."},"c-2-2-3":{"obj":["Found the real reason for refusal (emotional blunting) using open questions","Assessed capacity to refuse sertraline using the four MCA steps and recorded it","Offered at least two real alternatives, including a different drug and psychological therapy","Reassessed suicide risk and agreed observation level and review triggers with nursing staff","Explained his informal status accurately, without threats"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Those pills just numb you. I'd rather feel something.\"","r":"\"That's a real side effect, and wanting to feel things makes sense. There are medicines less likely to do that, and therapy that doesn't involve tablets. Can I tell you about them?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Just leave me alone. You lot keep hassling me.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry it feels like hassling. I'll keep it short. I need to understand how you are, because people care about your safety. Can I sit for ten minutes?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can you force me to take them?\"","r":"\"No. You're here informally and you can refuse treatment. I'd rather find something you're willing to try. If your safety became seriously at risk, we'd talk honestly about the options.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I lost my job. I've got nothing left to get better for.\"","r":"\"Losing your job is a huge blow, and I'm sorry. When you say nothing left, do you have thoughts about ending your life? I need to ask so we can keep you safe.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If I take one, can I go home this weekend?\"","r":"\"I'd like to get you home too. Let's base leave on how you're feeling and staying safe, not on one tablet. What would show us you're ready?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Therapy's a waste of time. Talking won't bring my job back.\"","r":"\"No, it won't. But it can help with the hopelessness and planning next steps, and a work adviser can help with jobs and money too.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Offer mirtazapine 15 mg at night, increase to 30 mg after 1–2 weeks (max 45 mg); warn of sedation, weight gain; less blunting and sexual side effects","If he prefers to retry an SSRI, start low (sertraline 25 mg) and review blunting at 2 weeks","Physical health baseline: bloods, weight, ECG if indicated"],"psych":["Behavioural activation with ward psychologist or OT; CBT for depression after discharge via CMHT or NHS Talking Therapies","Shared decision-making leaflet (Choice and Medication) and a second conversation the next day"],"social":["Ward social worker or welfare adviser: benefits, Universal Credit, debt advice","Fit note and employment support referral","With his consent, involve family and offer a carer's assessment"],"safety":["Observation level agreed with nurse in charge; review triggers: sudden calm, giving things away, asking to leave","Doctor's holding power s5(2) if he tries to leave and is at serious risk","Consultant review at next ward round; 72-hour follow-up after discharge"]},"facts":[{"h":"Disagreeing is not lacking capacity","x":"He can refuse if he can understand, retain, weigh and communicate the decision. An unwise choice is not proof of incapacity. Test hopelessness affecting weighing."},{"h":"Informal patients with capacity decide","x":"You cannot treat a capacitous informal patient against his will under the MCA. The only compulsory route is the MHA if its criteria are met."},{"h":"Never use sectioning as a threat","x":"Using the MHA to force agreement is coercive and damages trust. Explain the legal facts honestly if asked."},{"h":"Emotional blunting is common on SSRIs","x":"Many people on SSRIs report feeling flat. Mirtazapine, vortioxetine or a lower dose may help. Ask directly; patients often call it numb."},{"h":"72-hour follow-up after discharge","x":"Suicide risk peaks in the first days after discharge. NHS standard: contact within 72 hours."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"Explore his reasons for refusing medication, assess capacity for this decision, and agree a safe plan."},"c-2-2-4":{"obj":["Asked sensitively about the type and impact of her sexual side effects","Explained discontinuation symptoms, how they differ from relapse, and why paroxetine is worst","Checked pregnancy plans, contraception, other medicines and current mood and risk","Agreed a written cross-taper or taper plan she can slow down","Sent the GP a letter the same week stating dose steps and who prescribes"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I want off it today. I'm sick of it.\"","r":"\"I understand, and you're entitled to stop. Stopping paroxetine suddenly is what caused those shocks last month. If we do it in steps over a few weeks, you're far less likely to feel that again.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Am I addicted? Those shocks scared me.\"","r":"\"No. You don't crave it or need more. Your body has adapted to it, so sudden stopping causes withdrawal symptoms. Going slowly avoids that.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sex just doesn't work any more. It's wrecking my relationship.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; lots of people find it hard to raise. It's a common side effect and a good reason to change. There are options that affect sex much less.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will the depression come back if I stop?\"","r":"\"It might, so we'll keep an eye on it. Changing to a different medicine keeps you protected. If you'd rather stop, we'll go slowly and agree warning signs to look out for.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't I just take it every other day?\"","r":"\"I wouldn't advise that. Paroxetine leaves the body quickly, so skipping days causes those shocks again. Smaller daily doses work much better.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will the sexual side effects go once I'm off it?\"","r":"\"For most people, yes, within weeks. Rarely they last longer, so tell us if they don't improve. We'll check how you are at your review.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Cross-taper: paroxetine 30 to 20 mg and start mirtazapine 15 mg at night; after 1–2 weeks, paroxetine 10 mg and mirtazapine 30 mg; then stop paroxetine","If stopping, use smaller steps near the end (paroxetine 10 mg/5 mL oral suspension allows 5 mg or smaller steps) over weeks to months","Check pregnancy intention; paroxetine is best avoided in pregnancy; advise contraception during changes","Check interacting medicines and avoid adding another serotonergic drug during the switch"],"psych":["Written taper schedule and symptom diary to tell withdrawal from relapse","Offer CBT or mindfulness-based cognitive therapy via NHS Talking Therapies to cut relapse risk"],"social":["GP letter the same week: dose steps, who prescribes, review date","With her consent, invite her partner to a joint review to discuss the sexual side effects and plan"],"safety":["Phone review at 1–2 weeks; crisis line or NHS 111 if mood drops or suicidal thoughts appear","Pause or go back a step if discontinuation symptoms appear"]},"facts":[{"h":"Paroxetine is the hardest to stop","x":"Short half-life and strong anticholinergic effect cause the worst withdrawal. Fluoxetine is easiest. Never stop paroxetine abruptly."},{"h":"Withdrawal versus relapse","x":"Withdrawal starts within days, includes dizziness, 'brain zaps', flu-like feelings, and settles when a dose is restarted. Relapse builds over weeks with core depressive symptoms."},{"h":"Slow at the bottom","x":"Final dose cuts cause the biggest change in effect. Use smaller steps and longer gaps near the end; liquid forms help."},{"h":"Sexual side effects: options","x":"Switch to mirtazapine or vortioxetine, or lower the dose. Ask every time; few patients mention it unprompted."},{"h":"MAOI switches need a washout","x":"Never cross-taper to or from an MAOI. Seek pharmacist advice; washout periods are needed to avoid serotonin syndrome."}],"type":"Medication review","do":"Review her antidepressant side effects, explain discontinuation symptoms, and agree a safe switch or taper."},"c-2-2-5":{"obj":["Checked diagnosis, dose, duration and adherence of past trials before calling it treatment-resistant","Screened for bipolarity, substance use, physical causes and ongoing stressors","Offered at least three further options, including lithium, other combinations and ECT","Explained lithium monitoring, toxicity signs and interacting medicines in plain words","Set up baseline tests, first level date and shared care with the GP"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I've tried everything. I'm just broken.\"","r":"\"I can hear how worn down you are after so long. You're not broken. This kind of depression is common, and there are several treatments we haven't tried yet that often help.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Isn't lithium for people with bipolar? Are you saying I'm bipolar?\"","r":"\"No, it's a fair worry. Lithium is also used in smaller doses to boost an antidepressant when depression hasn't lifted. I'll ask a few questions to check, but this is about helping the antidepressant work.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why all the blood tests?\"","r":"\"The useful level of lithium in the blood is narrow. Too low does nothing, too high can make you ill. Tests also check your kidneys and thyroid, which lithium can affect.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I take ibuprofen for my back. Do I have to stop?\"","r":"\"It's best to avoid it, because it can push lithium to toxic levels. Paracetamol is safer. If you really need an anti-inflammatory, speak to us first so we can check levels.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Honestly, I didn't take the venlafaxine every day.\"","r":"\"Thanks for telling me. It matters, because it means venlafaxine may not have had a fair chance. Let's think about that before deciding on lithium.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How long before I know if it works?\"","r":"\"Usually within a few weeks of reaching the right level. We'll judge at about 4 to 6 weeks.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Baseline: U&E and eGFR, TFT, calcium, weight, ECG if cardiac risk","Lithium carbonate (prescribe by brand, e.g. Priadel) 200–400 mg at night; level 12 hours post-dose after 1 week and after each dose change","Augmentation target usually 0.4–1.0 mmol/L as agreed with consultant; levels 3-monthly in the first year, U&E, TFT and calcium 6-monthly","Other options: add mirtazapine, aripiprazole or quetiapine augmentation; ECT if severe"],"psych":["Different high-intensity therapy, such as IPT or CBT with a new focus, via CMHT psychology","Lithium information pack and record book; teach-back of toxicity signs"],"social":["Shared care with GP using local lithium protocol","Check work, finances and support; fit note if needed"],"safety":["Sick-day rules: stop lithium and get a level the same day if vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness or confusion","Suicide risk check; lithium is toxic in overdose, so limit supply if at risk","Review response at 4–6 weeks; consider specialist affective disorders service if no response"]},"facts":[{"h":"Check before labelling resistance","x":"Ask about dose, at least 4–6 weeks at therapeutic dose, and daily adherence. Many 'failed' trials were too low, too short or not taken."},{"h":"Lithium levels: 12 hours after","x":"Take blood 12 hours after the last dose, so night dosing means a morning test. Recheck 1 week after any dose change."},{"h":"Three drugs that raise lithium","x":"NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors or ARBs, and diuretics (especially thiazides) raise levels. Dehydration and vomiting do too."},{"h":"Toxicity signs to teach","x":"Coarse tremor, vomiting, diarrhoea, unsteadiness, slurred speech, confusion. Stop the dose and get a same-day level."},{"h":"Lithium cuts suicide","x":"Lithium has good evidence for reducing suicide, which helps in long-term depression."}],"type":"Medication review","do":"Review reasons for treatment resistance, explain options, and explain lithium augmentation with monitoring."},"c-2-2-6":{"obj":["Asked what she believes about ECT and named her fears before explaining it","Explained the ECT procedure, course length, benefits and memory risks in plain words","Compared rTMS honestly, including lower effect in severe depression and limited NHS access","Checked her understanding using teach-back and confirmed she can withdraw consent at any time","Arranged pre-assessment, baseline memory testing and a written leaflet"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"In films they strap you down and you're screaming.\"","r":"\"That's a scary image, and it's not how it's done now. You're fully asleep under anaesthetic, with a relaxant so your body barely moves. You won't feel the treatment.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will it wipe my memory?\"","r":"\"Memory problems are the main side effect. Many people forget events around the treatment period, and most memory returns within weeks. A few have lasting gaps. We'll test your memory throughout.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Wouldn't the magnet one be better? It sounds gentler.\"","r":"\"It is gentler, with fewer side effects. But for depression as severe as yours, especially when you're not eating, ECT works faster and more often. rTMS is also harder to get on the NHS.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if I start and want to stop?\"","r":"\"You can stop at any time, even after signing the consent form. We'd talk about other options, and it wouldn't affect the rest of your care.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can I try one more tablet first?\"","r":"\"That's a fair question. Another tablet might take 6 weeks or more, and you're not eating. Let's weigh that together; ECT often helps within a couple of weeks.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm too old for all this. Just let me be.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry you feel so worn down. ECT is often very effective in older people with severe depression. I'd hate for you to go on suffering when there's a good chance of getting better.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Anaesthetic pre-assessment, ECG, FBC, U&E, and dental check; review medicines that affect seizures, such as benzodiazepines","Twice-weekly ECT, usually 6–12 sessions; review after each session; nothing to eat for about 6 hours before","Baseline MoCA or similar memory test, repeated during the course","Weight, food and fluid chart while intake is poor"],"psych":["ECT leaflet and option to visit the ECT suite with a family member","Teach-back of key points before consent"],"social":["Arrange transport and an escort for outpatient treatments; no driving during the course","Family involvement with her consent"],"safety":["Capacity and consent recorded before each course; she may withdraw at any time","If she declines, revisit medication and consider rTMS referral; review weekly"]},"facts":[{"h":"ECT works fast in severe depression","x":"Most effective acute treatment for severe or psychotic depression and when eating stops. Older adults often respond well."},{"h":"Memory risk honestly","x":"Gaps around the treatment period are common; most memory returns within weeks. Some lasting gaps occur. Don't minimise it."},{"h":"rTMS in brief","x":"Awake, magnetic coil to the scalp, daily on weekdays for 4–6 weeks. Mild headache or scalp discomfort; rare seizure. Less effective than ECT in severe depression."},{"h":"Consent can be withdrawn","x":"Consent covers a course but can be withdrawn before any session. Check capacity and understanding again during the course."},{"h":"No driving during the course","x":"Avoid driving during the course and for at least 24–48 hours after each anaesthetic, per local policy. Arrange an escort home."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain ECT and rTMS, answer her concerns, and check understanding for informed consent."},"c-2-2-7":{"obj":["Excluded delirium and medical causes of low mood using a quick cognitive screen","Asked about the 'no point' comment and suicidal thoughts, plans and access to tablets","Recommended sertraline at a named dose with the antiplatelet bleeding risk and stomach protection addressed","Linked mood treatment to cardiac rehab and spoke directly to the cardiology team","Wrote a clear advice note and discharge recommendations for the GP"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"Anyone would be fed up in here. I'm not depressed.\"","r":"\"You're right, hospital is grim. But crying daily, not sleeping and feeling there's no point sounds like more than being fed up. Can we look at it together?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there any point to rehab? My heart's knackered.\"","r":"\"I understand why you feel that. Your cardiologists say your heart is recovering, and rehab is what gets you back to normal life. Low mood can drain motivation, which is something we can treat.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will antidepressants strain my heart?\"","r":"\"A sensible question. Sertraline has been studied in people after heart attacks and is safe for most. We'll check for interactions with your heart tablets and add stomach protection.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I brought this on myself. Smoking, drinking, all of it.\"","r":"\"Lots of people feel that after a heart attack. Blaming yourself won't help your recovery. What helps is the next step, and you're already in the right place for it.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Some nights I think it'd be easier if I didn't wake up.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Have you thought about doing anything to end your life, or have you planned anything? I want to make sure you're safe.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I just want to go home. Sign me out.\"","r":"\"I understand. Let's talk to your cardiology team today. Going home is fine if it's safe, but leaving with no plan for your mood could set back your recovery.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg once daily (range 50–200 mg); start low, review at 2 weeks","With aspirin plus clopidogrel or ticagrelor, add a PPI such as lansoprazole 15–30 mg; avoid omeprazole with clopidogrel","Check sodium at baseline and 2–4 weeks (hyponatraemia risk in older adults)","Avoid tricyclics; avoid citalopram above 20 mg if QT is a concern","Screen for delirium (4AT), TFT, calcium, glucose; review drugs such as beta-blockers"],"psych":["Cardiac rehab with its psychological support component","CBT or behavioural activation via NHS Talking Therapies long-term conditions service"],"social":["Fit note for employer; smoking cessation and alcohol advice","Involve partner or family with consent; peer support through cardiac rehab"],"safety":["Ask about access to stockpiled tablets; agree crisis contacts","Cardiology team to contact liaison if he asks to self-discharge","GP or liaison review within 2 weeks of discharge"]},"facts":[{"h":"Sertraline is the post-MI choice","x":"Best evidence for safety after heart attack. Mirtazapine is an alternative. Tricyclics are contraindicated after recent MI."},{"h":"SSRI plus antiplatelets bleed","x":"SSRIs reduce platelet serotonin, increasing GI bleeds. With aspirin or clopidogrel, add a PPI. Lansoprazole is preferred; omeprazole weakens clopidogrel."},{"h":"Depression worsens cardiac outcomes","x":"Depression after MI raises death and readmission risk and reduces rehab attendance. Treating it helps him engage."},{"h":"Rule out hypoactive delirium","x":"Quiet, withdrawn and sleepy can mean delirium, not depression. Do a 4AT and check attention before diagnosing."},{"h":"Citalopram has a QT ceiling","x":"Max 40 mg in adults, 20 mg over 65; prolongs QT dose-dependently. Avoid after recent MI if QT is long."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Assess his mood and risk after MI, exclude organic causes, and advise cardiology on safe treatment."},"c-2-2-8":{"obj":["Asked about his wife by name and let him describe the loss before assessment questions","Separated normal grief features from depressive features with at least three specific points","Clarified the voice he hears as a grief perception and checked for other psychotic symptoms","Asked about suicidal thoughts, wish to join his wife, alcohol and access to means","Agreed a plan with him and, with consent, his daughter: bereavement support, depression treatment and safety plan"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I hear her call my name at night. Am I going mad?\"","r":"\"No, you're not. Many bereaved people hear or sense the person they've lost, especially as they drift off or wake. It's part of grief, not madness.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Forty-one years. I don't know who I am without her.\"","r":"\"That's a huge part of your life. It's no surprise you feel lost. Tell me a bit about her, if you'd like to.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I'd like to be with her. I don't see the point of carrying on.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you told me. When you say you'd like to be with her, have you thought of ending your life? Have you made any plans?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I don't need pills. Grief is grief. I'll get through it.\"","r":"\"Grief is natural, and I won't try to take it away. But losing weight and feeling hopeless all day suggests depression on top, which makes grieving harder. Treating it can help you grieve.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have noticed she was ill. I could have saved her.\"","r":"\"Many people go over and over what they could have done. From what you've told me, it came out of the blue. Blaming yourself is part of the pain, not the truth.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is Dad going to be alright?\" (daughter)","r":"\"With support, most people do improve. He's agreed to a plan. Keep in touch, eat together when you can, and call the crisis line or 999 if he talks about ending his life.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Mirtazapine 15 mg at night, up to 30–45 mg after 2 weeks; helps sleep and appetite; warn of drowsiness and weight gain","GP bloods for weight loss: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, glucose, calcium; weigh at each visit","Alcohol: AUDIT; brief advice; refer if harmful use"],"psych":["Cruse Bereavement Support (helpline 0808 808 1677) or local bereavement counselling","CBT for depression via NHS Talking Therapies; grief-focused therapy if grief remains intense after 6 months"],"social":["Daughter (with consent) to help with practical tasks and regular contact","Age UK or local befriending; Men's Sheds or community groups","Help with probate, finances and Tell Us Once via the GP practice social prescriber"],"safety":["Written safety plan; Samaritans 116 123, crisis line and NHS 111 mental health option","Ask about firearms, stored tablets and anniversaries; limit medication supply if risk","Review in 2 weeks; sooner if suicidal thoughts increase"]},"facts":[{"h":"Grief versus depression","x":"Grief comes in waves tied to reminders, with self-esteem intact. Depression is constant low mood, worthlessness beyond the loss, and loss of all pleasure. Both can coexist."},{"h":"Hearing the dead is common","x":"Sensing or hearing a dead spouse, often while falling asleep or waking, is common in normal grief. He knows it isn't real. Don't call it psychosis."},{"h":"Prolonged grief needs 6 months","x":"ICD-11 prolonged grief disorder needs intense longing or preoccupation lasting at least 6 months after the death and impairing function."},{"h":"Older bereaved men: highest risk","x":"Widowed older men have high suicide rates, especially in the first year. Ask about wish to join the deceased, alcohol and means."},{"h":"Mirtazapine fits weight loss","x":"Sedating and appetite-boosting, so useful with insomnia and weight loss. Start 15 mg at night; check sodium in older adults."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Distinguish grief from a depressive episode, assess risk, and agree a plan."},"c-2-2-9":{"obj":["Asked directly, in steps, about suicidal thoughts, plans, preparations, past attempts and access to means, using his own words","Built a shared safety formulation naming his triggers, what keeps him going and what would make tonight worse","Agreed a written safety plan with two named people, the local crisis line and Samaritans 116 123, and handed him a copy","Secured his consent to share the plan with his sister and briefed her on warning signs and when to call 999","Arranged home treatment team contact within 24 hours and sent a same-day GP letter"],"snip":[{"tag":"Shame","p":"\"I'm a grown man crying in a mental health office. This is pathetic.\"","r":"\"It isn't pathetic. You've lost your job and your partner in two weeks; anyone would be struggling. Coming here took strength. Can you tell me what the worst moments have been like?\""},{"tag":"Minimising","p":"\"I said that to the GP, but I wouldn't actually do anything. I'm fine.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you'd tell me if you weren't. People often play it down once they're in front of a doctor. When you said you couldn't see the point, what was going through your mind?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've looked up how many paracetamol it would take. I've got a few boxes at home.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest; that matters. Looking it up tells me how bad it's got. Would you be willing for your sister to take those boxes away tonight while we get you more support?\""},{"tag":"Hopelessness","p":"\"What's the point of a plan? Nothing on it is going to get my job back.\"","r":"\"You're right, a plan won't fix the job. It's for the worst hours, so you get through them. What has helped you get through even one bad night recently?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Don't tell my sister anything. She'll tell our mum and make it worse.\"","r":"\"That's your choice and I'll respect it. Could we agree what she does hear, just the warning signs and who to call, and nothing about the details you've told me?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to section me if I tell you everything?\"","r":"\"Telling me how you feel doesn't mean you'll be sectioned. My aim is to keep you safe at home with support if we can. I'd rather know the full picture so the plan fits you.\""},{"tag":"Alcohol","p":"\"A few cans helps me sleep. It's the only thing that switches my head off.\"","r":"\"I can see why it feels like it helps. Drink also makes thoughts of suicide more likely to turn into action, especially late at night. Could we put cutting down on your plan, with help from the alcohol service?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Rate depression with PHQ-9 and drinking with AUDIT; check for psychotic symptoms and severe insomnia","If an antidepressant is agreed: sertraline 50 mg each morning, review at 2 weeks; warn of early agitation, nausea, sleep change","Prescribe small amounts (1–2 weeks' supply) while risk is raised; ask the GP to do the same","No benzodiazepines or zopiclone to take home while drinking daily"],"psych":["Safety plan written with him, in his words, before he leaves","HTT daily visits for support and problem-solving during the crisis","Refer to NHS Talking Therapies (CBT or behavioural activation) once the crisis settles"],"social":["Sister holds a copy of the plan (with consent) and removes stockpiled tablets tonight","Signpost debt advice (StepChange or Citizens Advice) and Jobcentre Plus support","Refer to the local drug and alcohol service; give the number today"],"safety":["Means: tablets removed, no stockpiling; ask about ligature points and firearms","Crisis contacts: local crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123, 999","Inform GP and duty consultant today; HTT contact within 24 hours","If he stops responding: call sister and GP, then ask police for a welfare check"]},"facts":[{"h":"Asking does not plant the idea","x":"Direct questions about suicide do not increase risk; most people feel relief. Ask stepwise: life not worth living, thoughts of death, plans, preparations, rehearsal."},{"h":"No low, medium or high","x":"NICE NG225 says risk scales and categories must not decide care. Write a safety formulation: triggers, what keeps him alive, what would raise risk tonight."},{"h":"Men, losses and drink","x":"Middle-aged men are the highest-risk group in UK suicide data. Recent job loss, separation, debt and alcohol each add risk; together they need same-day action."},{"h":"Means restriction works","x":"Removing stockpiled tablets and limiting supply cuts deaths. Paracetamol is sold in packs of 16 (pharmacy up to 32) for this reason."},{"h":"Safety plan has six parts","x":"Warning signs, things he can do alone, people and places for distraction, people to ask for help, professionals and crisis lines, making his space safer."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Safety plan","do":"Assess his suicidal thinking, build a shared safety formulation, and agree a written safety plan and follow-up."},"c-2-2-10":{"obj":["Checked with the A&E team the drugs, amounts and timing, the paracetamol level and the treatment plan, and that TOXBASE was used","Assessed and recorded her capacity to refuse acetylcysteine, using all four parts of the test","Explored intent before, during and after the overdose, including concealment, final acts and how she feels now","Asked about the boyfriend and home safety in private, and identified any children or dependants","Agreed a plan: complete treatment, psychosocial assessment when fit, safeguarding referral and same-day GP letter"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I feel completely fine. Take this drip out, I want to go home.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you feel well, and I know hospital is the last place you want to be. Paracetamol damage often doesn't show for a day or two. Can we talk while the drip finishes, then plan your going home?\""},{"tag":"Minimising","p":"\"It was stupid. I just wanted to sleep and not deal with him for a while.\"","r":"\"It sounds like you were desperate to escape how you felt. That's important to understand. At the time, did any part of you think you might not wake up?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Why does everyone keep asking me the same questions? Just leave me alone.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry, it must be exhausting. I'm the one who'll help plan what happens next, so I need to hear it once properly. We can go at your pace and take breaks.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He checks my phone and takes my bank card. He said if I left he'd tell everyone I'm mental.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. What you describe is controlling behaviour, and it's not your fault. Are you safe to go back tonight? There are people who help with exactly this, and I'd like to involve them.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Please don't tell him I'm here. He'll go mad.\"","r":"\"I won't tell him anything without your agreement. Your safety comes first. Would you like us to make sure he can't come onto the ward, and talk about where you could stay?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"My mum's going to be so ashamed of me.\"","r":"\"Lots of people worry about that, and most families are mainly relieved and want to help. Would you like me to be there when you talk to her, or help you work out what to say?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Medical team decides fitness: end-of-treatment bloods (INR, ALT, creatinine, paracetamol level) per TOXBASE","Ask her GP to limit prescriptions to small amounts and review her regular medicines","Screen for depression (PHQ-9) and alcohol or drug use (AUDIT)"],"psych":["Psychosocial assessment once medically fit and sober, with a written safety plan","Offer follow-up with the crisis team or CMHT; consider referral for talking therapy"],"social":["Refer to the IDVA service and give the National Domestic Abuse Helpline (0808 2000 247)","Ward social worker or hospital safeguarding lead to complete DASH risk checklist; MARAC if high risk","Agree a safe place to go on discharge (friend, family or refuge)"],"safety":["Nurse observation and security aware if she tries to leave before capacity is reassessed","Raise adult safeguarding concern today; children's social care via MASH if children are involved","Crisis numbers in writing; same-day discharge letter to GP"]},"facts":[{"h":"Looking well means nothing early","x":"Paracetamol liver injury peaks at 72–96 hours. Early symptoms may be absent. Medical fitness depends on the end-of-treatment bloods, not on how she looks."},{"h":"Section 5(2) never in A&E","x":"5(2) applies only to a patient already admitted to a hospital ward. In the emergency department, urgent physical treatment for someone lacking capacity rests on the MCA (best interests)."},{"h":"Capacity test has four parts","x":"Can she understand, retain, use and weigh, and communicate the decision? Record the exact risks you explained (liver failure, death) and her answers in her words."},{"h":"Intent is not the tablet count","x":"Ask what she expected to happen. A small overdose she believed would kill her is more worrying than a large one she knew was safe."},{"h":"Coercive control is a crime","x":"Coercive or controlling behaviour in an intimate relationship is an offence in England and Wales. Ask about it alone, never in front of the partner."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Confirm medical fitness, assess overdose intent and current risk, and decide admission, home treatment or discharge."},"c-2-3-1":{"obj":["Recorded each MSE domain with at least one direct quote of her speech or beliefs","Checked for mixed features, suicidal thoughts and ideas of harming others","Screened for intoxication, delirium and pregnancy before any medicine is given","Given her Section 2 rights and recorded that she was told about the IMHA","Handed over an observation level, investigation list and morning review to the nurse in charge"],"snip":[{"tag":"Grandiosity","p":"\"I don't need a doctor. I've been chosen. I'm going to fix the whole NHS by Christmas.\"","r":"\"That sounds like a big mission. Tell me how you came to know you'd been chosen. And while we talk, can I check how your body is coping after no sleep?\""},{"tag":"Irritability","p":"\"You're boring me. Stop with the questions or I'm walking out.\"","r":"\"Fair enough, I'll keep it short. Three more questions, then you can rest. How many hours have you slept this week?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You can't keep me here. I know my rights. I'll sue all of you.\"","r":"\"You do have rights, and I want you to know them. You're on Section 2 for assessment. The nurse will give you a leaflet, and you can appeal to a tribunal and have an advocate. Shall I explain more?\""},{"tag":"Distractible","p":"\"Is that your real watch? I love that colour. Did you hear that bird? Blue birds mean luck.\"","r":"\"It's a lot to notice at once. Let's come back to you: are you having any thoughts of self-harm or anyone else?\""},{"tag":"Disinhibition","p":"\"You're gorgeous, doctor. Come and sit closer to me.\"","r":"\"I'll stay where I am, thank you. I'm here to check how you are. Can you tell me what you've had to eat and drink today?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Are you going to drug me? I won't take anything that changes who I am.\"","r":"\"No medicine tonight unless we agree or there's an emergency. I'd like to talk tomorrow about what's helped before. For now, would you take some food and water?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods tonight or first thing: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, calcium, glucose/HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; urine drug screen; pregnancy test","ECG before any antipsychotic; NEWS2 at least 4-hourly at first; food, fluid and sleep chart","PRN only per trust policy: lorazepam 1 mg oral (max 4 mg/24 h) for agitation, after pregnancy status known"],"psych":["Low-stimulus room; short, calm conversations; one staff member speaking at a time","Full history and collateral from partner over the next 24–48 hours"],"social":["Partner (with consent): finances, debts, children at home, who has her keys and cards","MHA administrator checks section papers; IMHA referral made"],"safety":["Observation level agreed with nurse in charge (e.g. intermittent, every 15 minutes) and reviewed daily","Watch for sexual vulnerability, absconding and conflict with other patients","Consultant review at morning ward round"]},"facts":[{"h":"Quote her exact words","x":"Write what she actually said, e.g. 'I've been chosen to fix the NHS.' A label like 'grandiose' alone cannot be checked by the next doctor or a tribunal."},{"h":"Mixed states hide suicide risk","x":"Elated speech can sit beside despair. Ask about suicide in every manic patient; mixed episodes carry some of the highest suicide risk in bipolar disorder."},{"h":"Section 2 timings","x":"Up to 28 days. She may apply to the Tribunal within the first 14 days. Rights under s132 must be given as soon as practicable, verbally and in writing."},{"h":"Rule out the body first","x":"Stimulants, steroids, thyroid disease, head injury and delirium mimic mania. Check orientation, UDS, TFTs and a neurological assessment before calling it bipolar."}],"type":"Mental state examination","do":"Complete and present a structured mania MSE, naming immediate risks and needed investigations."},"c-2-3-2":{"obj":["Tried calm negotiation first and recorded his answer when asked to stay voluntarily","Applied Section 5(2) correctly with Form H1 delivered to the MHA office, and gave his rights","Requested an AMHP-led MHA assessment the same day, not at the end of the 72 hours","Gave clear advice not to drive and his duty to tell the DVLA, and agreed where his keys are kept","Put a plan in place to protect his money, with consent where possible"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm here voluntarily. You can't stop me leaving. That's the law.\"","r":"\"You're right that informal patients can usually leave. I'm worried you're not well enough today, and there is a legal power for that. Before using it, would you stay and talk with me for ten minutes?\""},{"tag":"Grandiosity","p":"\"This deal will make me a millionaire. If I don't buy the car today, I lose it.\"","r":"\"I can see how exciting it feels. You haven't slept for days, and big decisions made like that often look different later. What would be lost by waiting one week?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You're locking me up for buying a car? This is ridiculous.\"","r":"\"I hear how angry you are. It isn't about the car. It's that you're unwell, not sleeping and at risk of harm. I'm holding you for up to 72 hours while specialists assess you. Here are your rights.\""},{"tag":"Driving","p":"\"I'm a brilliant driver. Better than ever, actually. My reactions are amazing.\"","r":"\"Feeling sharper is part of the illness, but judgement is affected. The law says you mustn't drive while unwell like this, and you must tell the DVLA. Can the ward keep your keys for now?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Fine, I'll stay if you let me phone the dealer and put a deposit down.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you'll stay. I'd ask you not to commit to any big purchases until you're sleeping again. Could we call your brother together to help look after your cards for a few days?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will the DVLA take my licence forever?\"","r":"\"No. Most people get it back. Once you've been well and stable, usually for about three months, you can reapply, and your doctor gives a report. Telling them now keeps you legal and insured.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Review sleep: offer zopiclone 7.5 mg at night or promethazine 25 mg PRN if he consents","Restart or review antimanic medication once the legal position is clear; check UDS and alcohol"],"psych":["Low-stimulus space; one consistent nurse; short, clear explanations","Once calmer, agree with him an early-warning sign list (sleep loss, spending) for future relapses"],"social":["Keys and bank cards stored in the ward safe with his agreement","With consent, family helps contact his bank about spending limits or card freeze","If capacity for money remains in doubt later: ward social worker discusses appointeeship or Court of Protection deputy"],"safety":["Form H1 handed to hospital managers; nurse in charge and on-call consultant told","AMHP hub called now for MHA assessment; observation level raised while he is held","DVLA advice recorded in notes and in discharge letter to GP"]},"facts":[{"h":"5(4) nurse, 5(2) doctor","x":"Under 5(4) a registered MH or LD nurse can hold for 6 hours. Under 5(2) the responsible doctor or AC, or a single named deputy, can hold for 72 hours. Neither renews."},{"h":"Clock starts at H1 delivery","x":"The 72 hours run from when Form H1 is given to the hospital managers. If 5(4) was used first, the time counts from the start of the 5(4)."},{"h":"5(2) gives no treatment power","x":"A holding power does not allow medication without consent. Only common law emergency action or the MCA applies until a treatment section is in place."},{"h":"Patient tells DVLA, not you","x":"He must notify the DVLA. You advise and record it. Tell the DVLA yourself only if he keeps driving, after telling him you are going to."},{"h":"Mania and money","x":"Banks offer spending blocks and third-party help. When well, a Health and welfare LPA finance lets a trusted person step in at the next episode."}],"type":"Mental Health Act process + Risk assessment","do":"Assess his safety to leave, apply a holding power if needed, and address driving and financial risks."},"c-2-3-3":{"obj":["Confirmed her consent to involve her husband and checked her capacity to consent to treatment","Agreed to stop sertraline and start one oral antipsychotic, with dose, timing and side effects explained","Checked pregnancy status and explained why valproate is not a first choice for her","Set out baseline and 12-week physical monitoring","Gave the husband a realistic time frame and a plan for long-term prevention"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How long will she be in here? I've got work and two kids to sort out.\"","r":"\"That's a lot to carry. Most people improve over a few weeks, but I can't promise a date. We'll review progress every week. Would a carers' assessment or the ward social worker help with the children?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not ill. I've never felt better. You're the ones who need treatment.\"","r":"\"I believe you feel great. What worries us is four nights without sleep and the spending. This medicine helps sleep and calms racing thoughts. Can I tell you what to expect?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Olanzapine? My friend took that and got fat. I'm not having that.\"","r":"\"That's a real concern. Olanzapine can increase appetite and weight. We'll weigh you weekly and act early. Quetiapine or another option is possible too. Which matters most to you?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"The GP said sertraline was for her depression. Why are you stopping it?\"","r":"\"Good question. Antidepressants can push mood too high in bipolar disorder, which may have happened here. Stopping it now is safer. We'll treat low mood differently if it comes back.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Is she going to be like this forever? I don't recognise her.\"","r":"\"That must be frightening. Manic episodes do settle with treatment, and the person you know comes back. The next step is a medicine to reduce future episodes. You'll be part of that plan.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'll take tablets, but not that blood test every few months stuff.\"","r":"\"Fair enough for now. Today we only need a few baseline bloods for safety. We can talk about long-term options, and what each needs, when you're feeling more settled.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Stop sertraline now; watch for discontinuation symptoms (dizziness, irritability) in the first week","Olanzapine 10–15 mg at night (range 5–20 mg); warn about sedation, appetite and weight gain","Lorazepam 1 mg oral PRN for agitation or sleep (max 4 mg/24 h); review daily and stop within 2 weeks","Monitoring: weight weekly for 6 weeks; HbA1c, lipids and weight at 12 weeks; ECG if indicated","Plan lithium for long-term prevention once settled; no valproate unless MHRA conditions are met"],"psych":["Psychoeducation for her and husband on early warning signs once she can engage","Ward psychology and OT as she improves; later, structured group psychoeducation for bipolar disorder"],"social":["Carers' assessment by local authority for the husband; ward social worker to check childcare","Care coordinator allocated under CPA before discharge"],"safety":["PRN plan with maximum doses on chart; rapid tranquillisation per NICE NG10 if needed","Pregnancy test before prescribing; perinatal team if positive","Follow-up within 72 hours of discharge"]},"facts":[{"h":"Antipsychotic first for mania","x":"NICE: haloperidol, olanzapine, quetiapine or risperidone first. If ineffective at full dose, try another. Then add lithium. Lithium alone is slow to work in acute mania."},{"h":"Valproate under 55: two signatures","x":"MHRA 2024: no new valproate under age 55 unless two specialists separately sign that nothing else is suitable. Women who could become pregnant also need the Pregnancy Prevention Programme."},{"h":"Olanzapine goes at night","x":"Give it at bedtime to use the sedation for sleep. Expect weight gain in the first weeks; check weight weekly and give diet advice early."},{"h":"Do not promise a date","x":"Say 'most people improve over weeks' and give a weekly review point. A fixed discharge date that slips damages trust with families."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Agree an evidence-based acute mania management plan with the patient and her husband."},"c-2-3-4":{"obj":["Asked directly about suicidal thoughts and plans and agreed a written safety plan","Checked for mixed features and recent lithium adherence","Ordered a 12-hour lithium level, U&Es, eGFR, TFTs and calcium","Explained why citalopram alone is risky and agreed a NICE option for bipolar depression","Gave DVLA, work and carer advice and booked a review within 2 weeks"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"My GP said citalopram would help. Why can't I just have that?\"","r":"\"Your GP was right that you need treatment. In bipolar disorder, an antidepressant on its own can tip mood into a high. There are options that lift low mood without that risk. Can I explain them?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Some days I think everyone would be better off if I wasn't here.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. That's an important thing to say. How often do these thoughts come, and have you thought about how you might end your life?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I've been skipping lithium a few days a week. It makes me feel flat.\"","r":"\"I appreciate your honesty; that helps us. Missed doses can bring on relapse. Let's check your level, and talk about what flatness feels like. We may be able to adjust things.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I've let my family down again. My wife's carrying everything.\"","r":"\"Depression makes people feel like a burden, but this is an illness, not a failing. Your wife came with you because she cares. Can we include her in the plan?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will I lose my job? I've been off six weeks already.\"","r":"\"It's a real worry. I can give you a fit note, and with your consent, write to occupational health about a phased return. Many people go back to work after episodes like this.\""},{"tag":"Relative","p":"\"He sleeps all day. Should I be pushing him to get up?\"","r":"\"You're clearly trying to help. Gentle routine helps more than pushing: a fixed getting-up time and one small activity a day. Tell us straight away if he talks about wishing you weren't here.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Lithium level 12 hours post-dose; if below 0.6 mmol/L and adherent, increase by 200 mg and recheck in 1 week","If TSH raised: start levothyroxine (GP) and continue lithium","Add quetiapine: 50 mg at bedtime day 1, 100 mg day 2, 200 mg day 3, 300 mg day 4; warn about sedation, dizziness, weight gain","Alternatives: olanzapine plus fluoxetine, or lamotrigine titrated slowly (25 mg daily for 2 weeks) with rash warning","Weight, HbA1c and lipids at baseline and 12 weeks"],"psych":["Offer high-intensity psychological therapy for bipolar depression (CBT or IPT, psychology team)","Behavioural activation steps: fixed wake time, daily activity plan"],"social":["Fit note issued; occupational health letter with consent for phased return","Carers' assessment by local authority for his wife; signpost Bipolar UK groups"],"safety":["Written safety plan; crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123","Must not drive while severely depressed with suicidal thoughts; notify DVLA","Review within 2 weeks; care coordinator informed"]},"facts":[{"h":"No antidepressant alone in bipolar I","x":"Antidepressant monotherapy risks switching into mania or rapid cycling. NICE options: fluoxetine with olanzapine, quetiapine, olanzapine alone or lamotrigine."},{"h":"Check lithium before blaming it","x":"Low levels from missed doses, or high TSH from lithium-induced hypothyroidism, often drive bipolar depression. Fix these first."},{"h":"Quetiapine for bipolar depression","x":"Taken at bedtime, built up to 300 mg by day 4. Sleepiness and dizziness on standing are common early; warn about driving and alcohol."},{"h":"Lamotrigine rash rule","x":"Titrate slowly from 25 mg daily. Any rash, especially with fever or mouth sores, means stop and seek urgent advice; risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome."},{"h":"Lithium protects against suicide","x":"Lithium reduces suicide risk in bipolar disorder. Stopping it suddenly raises relapse and suicide risk; never stop abruptly."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Assess his bipolar depression and risk, and agree a safe evidence-based treatment plan."},"c-2-3-5":{"obj":["Explained why lithium is used and the 0.6–0.8 mmol/L target in plain words, and checked she understood","Taught her five toxicity warning signs and the sick-day rule, and she repeated them back","Listed drugs to avoid (NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, ARBs, thiazides) and the need for steady fluid intake","Agreed baseline bloods, the first level at day 7 and the long-term monitoring schedule","Gave the lithium pack and record book and sent a shared care plan to the GP"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I read online that lithium wrecks your kidneys. Is that true?\"","r":"\"It's a fair worry. A small number of people get kidney changes over many years. That's why we check your kidneys every six months, so we can act early. For most, the benefits clearly outweigh this.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Every week a needle? I really hate needles. Is that going to be forever?\"","r":"\"I understand. Lithium only works within a narrow range: too low does nothing, too high is harmful. At first it's weekly, then every three months. We can use numbing cream if needles bother you.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Isn't lithium a really old drug? There must be something better now.\"","r":"\"It is old, but it's still the most effective medicine for preventing manic and depressive episodes, and it lowers suicide risk. Newer isn't always better here.\""},{"tag":"Relative","p":"\"How will I know if she's getting too much?\"","r":"\"Watch for being sick, diarrhoea, a bad shake, wobbly walking, slurred speech or confusion. If you see these, she should stop the next dose and get seen urgently, saying she takes lithium.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I still have a drink, and take ibuprofen for my period pains?\"","r":"\"Small amounts of alcohol are OK, but avoid binges and dehydration. Avoid ibuprofen, as it raises lithium levels. Paracetamol is fine. Always tell a pharmacist you take lithium.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"What if I want a baby one day?\"","r":"\"Many women on lithium have healthy babies. The key is planning: talk to us before trying, so a perinatal psychiatrist can help you weigh options. Please don't stop it suddenly if you become pregnant; call us.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Before the first dose: renal function (U&Es, eGFR), thyroid, calcium, FBC and BMI; ECG if heart disease or risk","Priadel 400 mg at night (200 mg if eGFR reduced or under 50 kg); prescribe by brand","Level 12 hours post-dose at day 7 and 1 week after each change, until 0.6–0.8 mmol/L is reached","Then levels every 3 months for the first year; U&Es, eGFR, TFTs and calcium every 6 months; weight yearly","Common early effects: fine tremor, thirst, passing more urine, metallic taste, mild nausea"],"psych":["Psychoeducation on relapse signs and the importance of steady adherence","Agree a relapse signature with her and her mother"],"social":["Mother (with consent) knows toxicity signs and where the record book is kept","Register with the local lithium monitoring service or GP recall system"],"safety":["Sick-day rule: stop lithium and seek advice if vomiting, diarrhoea or unable to drink","Shared care plan to GP; flag NSAID and ACE inhibitor interaction on GP record","Follow-up within 72 hours of discharge and CMHT review"]},"facts":[{"h":"Lithium goes at night","x":"One dose at bedtime makes the 12-hour morning level easy to time. Take bloods in the morning, about 12 hours after the bedtime dose."},{"h":"Keep to one brand","x":"Lithium brands are not interchangeable. Liquid lithium citrate and tablet lithium carbonate differ mg for mg. Prescribe and dispense by brand name."},{"h":"Toxicity can happen in range","x":"Levels above 1.5 mmol/L are usually toxic, but older or dehydrated patients can be toxic at normal levels. Treat the symptoms, not just the number."},{"h":"Three drug classes to avoid","x":"NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors/ARBs and thiazide diuretics all raise lithium levels. If one must start, recheck the level within a week."},{"h":"Never stop suddenly","x":"Abrupt stopping causes rebound mania. If stopping, taper over at least 4 weeks, ideally up to 3 months."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain lithium, its blood monitoring and toxicity warning signs, and agree a safe start plan."},"c-2-4-1":{"obj":["Taken a timeline of the last attack: first sign, peak within minutes, duration and what she feared would happen","Confirmed that ECG, troponin, glucose and a thyroid test have been checked, and asked about caffeine, alcohol and drugs","Asked one direct question about suicidal thoughts and recorded the answer","Drawn the panic cycle with her and checked she can explain it back","Given the NHS Talking Therapies self-referral details and a written safety-net before she leaves"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It's not anxiety. Something's wrong with my heart. They've missed it.\"","r":"\"Your symptoms are real and frightening. Your ECG and blood tests show your heart is healthy tonight. What you felt fits a panic attack: the body's alarm going off without danger. Can I explain how that happens?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I really thought I was dying. What if next time I actually do?\"","r":"\"That feeling of dying is one of the most common parts of panic. It's terrifying but not dangerous: the attack peaks and passes, usually within 10 to 20 minutes. Nobody dies from a panic attack.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't even take my kids to school. I feel like a useless mum.\"","r":"\"That sounds really hard, and it isn't your fault. Avoiding places is how panic protects you in the short term, but it makes your world smaller. Therapy helps people get those places back, step by step.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't you just give me something to calm me down when it happens?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting fast relief. Tablets like diazepam help briefly but make panic worse long term and are addictive. CBT works better and lasts. A daily SSRI is an option if you want medication.\""},{"tag":"Embarrassment","p":"\"I'm so sorry for wasting A&E's time again.\"","r":"\"You're not wasting anyone's time. You were frightened and came for help. Tonight we can give you a plan so you know what's happening next time and what to do.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I have a few glasses of wine every night. It's the only thing that settles me.\"","r":"\"Thanks for telling me. Wine calms you at first, but as it wears off it can bring on panic, often at night. Could we look at cutting down alongside therapy?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Confirm TFT result; check caffeine, alcohol and stimulant use","If medication wanted: sertraline 25 mg daily for 1 week, then 50 mg; increase by 50 mg every 1–2 weeks to max 200 mg","Warn: early jitteriness may briefly worsen anxiety; nausea; sexual side effects; review at 2 weeks","No benzodiazepines; no sedating antihistamines or antipsychotics for panic"],"psych":["CBT for panic via NHS Talking Therapies (self-referral); 7–14 hours over about 4 months","Self-help meanwhile: NHS website panic leaflet, slow breathing, staying in the situation until anxiety falls"],"social":["Partner to encourage short, planned trips out rather than taking over every task","Fit note or work adjustments via GP if needed"],"safety":["Safety-net: chest pain on exertion, fainting or pain that feels different needs urgent review","Same-day GP letter; liaison record updated in case she returns"]},"facts":[{"h":"Panic peaks within 10 minutes","x":"Attacks build to a peak within minutes and usually settle within 20–30. Persistent chest pain or symptoms on exertion point away from panic."},{"h":"No benzodiazepines for panic","x":"NICE CG113: benzodiazepines worsen long-term outcomes in panic disorder. Do not prescribe 'just for now' from A&E."},{"h":"SSRIs start low in panic","x":"Begin at half the usual dose (e.g. sertraline 25 mg) because early jitteriness can trigger attacks. If no response after 12 weeks, consider imipramine or clomipramine."},{"h":"Avoidance keeps it going","x":"Escaping or avoiding places teaches the brain the danger was real. Staying until anxiety falls is the core of CBT for panic."},{"h":"Self-referral is allowed","x":"In England, adults can refer themselves to NHS Talking Therapies online or by phone. Give her the local link before she leaves."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Take a panic and agoraphobia history, confirm physical causes were considered, and agree a discharge plan."},"c-2-4-2":{"obj":["Spoken to him directly first and agreed with him how his mother would be involved","Separated fear of being judged from general worry using targeted questions","Identified at least two of his safety behaviours and asked about alcohol, drugs and mood","Checked the sertraline dose and length of trial against NICE expectations","Agreed a plan: individual CBT for social anxiety, an SSRI review and a letter to student support"],"snip":[{"tag":"Third party","p":"Mother: \"He won't tell you properly. He's always been shy. Let me explain.\"","r":"\"Thank you; your view is useful and I'll ask you shortly. First I'd like to hear from him in his own words. Is that OK with you both?\""},{"tag":"Shame","p":"\"I just freeze in seminars. Everyone can see me going red. They think I'm stupid.\"","r":"\"That sounds horrible. Many people feel exactly that. What goes through your mind just before you have to speak?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I have a few drinks before I go out, otherwise I can't face anyone.\"","r":"\"That's really common, and thank you for telling me. Alcohol helps for an evening but makes anxiety worse the next day. How much, and how often?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"The sertraline didn't work. I want to try something stronger, like pregabalin.\"","r":"\"I understand you want something that works. Fifty milligrams for a short time isn't a full trial. A higher dose plus CBT works best. Pregabalin isn't the first choice and it can be habit-forming.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I go to therapy, will they make me stand up and give a speech?\"","r":"\"Nobody will force you. You and the therapist plan small steps together, starting with things you feel you could manage. You stay in control of the pace.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this going to stop me finishing my degree?\"","r":"\"Not if we act now. With your consent I'll write to student support about deadlines and seminar adjustments. Treatment works well for this, and many people finish their degree.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline: increase from 50 mg to 100 mg each morning; review at 4–6 weeks; up to 200 mg if partial response","Allow 10–12 weeks at an effective dose before judging; continue 6–12 months after response","Avoid pregabalin as a first switch; no benzodiazepines; check for bought online diazepam"],"psych":["Individual CBT for social anxiety (up to 14 sessions), NHS Talking Therapies","Work on dropping safety behaviours and video feedback in therapy","If GAD also confirmed: CBT or applied relaxation"],"social":["With consent, letter to university disability or student support service for adjustments and extensions","Mother: encourage him to attend and answer for himself; signpost carer information"],"safety":["Screen mood and suicide risk (PHQ-9); AUDIT for alcohol","Clear re-referral route in the GP letter"]},"facts":[{"h":"Fear of judgement vs worry","x":"Social anxiety is fear of being judged or humiliated by others. GAD is worry about many everyday things. Ask: 'Is it people watching you, or everything going wrong?'"},{"h":"CBT beats tablets here","x":"NICE CG159: individual CBT specific to social anxiety is first-line. Offer an SSRI (escitalopram or sertraline) if he declines therapy or as an add-on."},{"h":"Safety behaviours keep it going","x":"Rehearsing lines, avoiding eye contact, sitting at the back or drinking first stop him learning that the feared outcome doesn't happen."},{"h":"Pregabalin is a controlled drug","x":"Pregabalin has been Schedule 3, Class C since 2019 because of misuse and dependence. Warn about sedation and breathing risk with opioids or alcohol."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Clarify whether this is GAD, social anxiety disorder or both, and agree a treatment plan."},"c-2-4-3":{"obj":["Asked about the actual content of her intrusive thoughts in a calm, non-judgemental way","Measured time spent on checking each day, her distress and the impact on caring for the baby","Distinguished unwanted harm thoughts from intent, psychosis and severe depression, and recorded the reasoning","Explained ERP in plain words and agreed first steps with her","Told her honestly that OCD thoughts alone are not a reason for social services, and recorded the safeguarding judgement"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to take my baby away?\"","r":"\"I can hear how frightened you are. Having unwanted thoughts like these is a well-known symptom of OCD, and it doesn't mean you'll harm your baby. My job is to help you get well and look after her.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I get pictures of her drowning in the bath. That's why I can't bathe her alone.\"","r":"\"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. Those images horrify you because you love her. That's exactly how OCD works: it attacks what matters most. Do you ever want them to happen?\""},{"tag":"Reassurance-seeking","p":"\"Just tell me she's safe. Promise me I won't hurt her.\"","r":"\"I understand why you want to hear it. Reassurance only helps for a moment, then the doubt comes back stronger. What I can tell you is that this is OCD, and it's treatable.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"What kind of mother thinks things like that? I'm disgusting.\"","r":"\"You're not disgusting. Most new parents have intrusive thoughts. With OCD, they get stuck because you fight them so hard. The guilt you feel shows how much you care.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will tablets get into my breast milk?\"","r":"\"It's important to ask. Sertraline is one of the most used options when breastfeeding, as very little gets into milk. We'd check the baby is feeding and settling normally. You don't have to stop breastfeeding.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I can't stop checking. If the house burns down it'll be my fault.\"","r":"\"That sense of responsibility is part of OCD. We won't ask you to stop everything at once. In ERP you pick one small check to delay, and notice the anxiety falls on its own.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg once daily; increase in 50 mg steps every 1–2 weeks as needed, up to 200 mg","Allow 12 weeks at a good dose; continue at least 12 months after remission","Warn: nausea, headache, sleep change, early anxiety; check baby's feeding and weight with health visitor"],"psych":["CBT with ERP via perinatal psychology (prioritised for perinatal patients); weekly sessions","Measure with Y-BOCS at start and each review","Partner and family stop giving reassurance and join in checking only as agreed in therapy"],"social":["Health visitor (with consent) supports bathing and practical routines","Partner support; consider childcare help from family while therapy starts"],"safety":["Discuss with consultant; record why no child protection referral is needed","Screen for postnatal depression and psychosis; ask about suicidal thoughts","Re-assess if thoughts become acceptable, planned or psychotic"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ego-dystonic thoughts are not intent","x":"Unwanted harm thoughts in OCD are resisted and horrify the person. They do not raise the risk of harm. Avoidance of the baby is the protective behaviour, not a warning sign."},{"h":"Reassurance is a compulsion","x":"Each reassurance briefly lowers anxiety, then the doubt returns. Clinicians and family must stop repeating it; this is part of treatment."},{"h":"OCD needs higher SSRI doses","x":"OCD often responds only at upper doses and after 12 weeks. Keep going for at least 12 months after remission to prevent relapse."},{"h":"Ask the actual thought","x":"Say 'What exactly comes into your mind?' Vague questions miss the obsession. Hearing it calmly reduces shame and helps tell OCD from psychosis."},{"h":"Y-BOCS tracks severity","x":"Score 0–40. 16–23 moderate, 24–31 severe, 32 or more extreme. Repeat it at reviews to show change."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a treatment","do":"Elicit obsessions and compulsions, rate severity and risk, then explain exposure and response prevention."},"c-2-4-4":{"obj":["Explored his preoccupation, time spent checking, camouflage and how strongly he holds the belief","Asked directly about suicide and about DIY procedures or buying procedures abroad","Screened for depression, OCD, social anxiety and eating disorders","Explained, without arguing about his nose, why surgery is unlikely to help","Agreed CBT with ERP for BDD and an SSRI plan, with a written safety plan"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Just look at it. It's obviously crooked. Don't tell me you can't see it.\"","r":"\"I won't argue about how it looks. I can see how much distress it causes you, and that's what I want to help with. How many hours a day does it take up?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If you write the letter for surgery, I'll do whatever therapy you want afterwards.\"","r":"\"I understand how much you want this. Most people with BDD stay unhappy after surgery, or the worry moves somewhere else. Treating the preoccupation first gives you the best chance of feeling better.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've watched videos on how to break and reset your own nose.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. That could seriously injure you. How close have you come to trying? Do you have anything at home you'd use? I need to understand that to keep you safe.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Nobody will ever want to be with me looking like this.\"","r":"\"That sounds lonely and painful. Have those feelings ever led to thoughts about ending your life?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"So you think I'm mad? That it's all in my head?\"","r":"\"No. BDD is a real, common condition where the brain focuses on one feature and magnifies it. It's not about vanity, and it does respond to treatment.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will tablets change how my nose looks to me?\"","r":"\"Not overnight. An SSRI like fluoxetine can reduce the pull to check and the distress, usually over 8 to 12 weeks. Most people do best with tablets plus CBT.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Fluoxetine 20 mg each morning; increase towards 40–60 mg over weeks if partial response","Allow 12 weeks at an effective dose; warn about early agitation, nausea, sleep change and sexual side effects","Under 25: review within 1 week of starting for agitation or suicidal thoughts"],"psych":["CBT with ERP for BDD (specialist psychology); target mirror checking, camouflage and comparing","Measure severity with BDD-YBOCS at start and reviews"],"social":["With consent, college student support for a planned return","BDD Foundation information and peer support"],"safety":["Written safety plan; NHS 111 option 2 and Samaritans 116 123","Ask about DIY surgery or overseas procedures; remove tools if planning","GP to keep declining cosmetic referrals; review in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Surgery does not cure BDD","x":"NICE CG31: do not offer cosmetic procedures as treatment for BDD. Most remain dissatisfied, and the focus often shifts to another body part."},{"h":"High suicide risk","x":"BDD carries one of the highest rates of suicidal thinking among psychiatric disorders. Ask directly at every review, not just the first."},{"h":"Poor insight is still BDD","x":"Absolute conviction about the defect is classed as BDD with absent insight, not a psychotic disorder. Treat with SSRI and CBT, not antipsychotics alone."},{"h":"Don't argue, don't agree","x":"Neither confirm the defect nor debate it. Focus on time lost, distress and what he's stopped doing."},{"h":"National service for severe cases","x":"Severe BDD not responding to standard treatment can be referred to the NHS England national OCD/BDD specialist service."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Assess for body dysmorphic disorder and risk, and respond honestly to his request for surgery."},"c-2-4-5":{"obj":["Seen her alone with a professional interpreter and explained confidentiality","Covered re-experiencing, avoidance, sense of current threat and complex PTSD features without asking for trauma detail","Asked about suicidal thoughts, alcohol, current safety at home and children","Explained PTSD and the trauma-focused treatment options in plain words","Made a referral that states the interpreter language, and linked her to refugee and housing support"],"snip":[{"tag":"Third party","p":"Husband: \"I speak perfect English. I'll translate. The phone interpreter is a waste of time.\"","r":"\"Thank you for offering. We always use a professional interpreter, so it's fair for everyone and you don't have to carry that. Could you wait outside? I'll invite you back at the end.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will what I say go to the Home Office? Will it hurt my asylum case?\"","r":"\"What you tell me stays within your health care. I don't share it with the Home Office unless you ask me to. You choose what you tell me.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Every night I am back there. I hear the shooting. I wake up screaming.\"","r":"\"That sounds exhausting and terrifying. You're safe in this room. You don't need to tell me what happened today. Can you tell me how often the nightmares come?\""},{"tag":"Shame","p":"\"I shout at my children for nothing. I am not the mother I was.\"","r":"\"Feeling on edge and irritable is a very common part of trauma. It doesn't make you a bad mother. Treatment helps with this too.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is something wrong with my mind? In my country, people would say I am crazy.\"","r":"\"No. PTSD is the mind's normal reaction to terrible events. Your brain is still on alert as if the danger is happening now. Many people recover with the right therapy.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to talk about what happened. I can't.\"","r":"\"That's okay. You don't have to. Therapy goes at your pace, and you decide when. Today I only need to know how it affects you now.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["If therapy declined or as add-on: sertraline 25 mg daily for 1 week, then 50 mg; max 200 mg","Sleep hygiene advice; avoid long-term hypnotics","Screen for chronic pain and physical health problems often missed in refugees"],"psych":["Trauma-focused CBT (8–12 sessions, more if complex) or EMDR, with a professional interpreter","Grounding techniques for flashbacks while waiting","Assess for complex PTSD; phase-based therapy if present"],"social":["Refugee support (Refugee Council or local charity); housing officer if unstable housing","Specialist services: Freedom from Torture or Helen Bamber Foundation if torture is disclosed","Any medicolegal report request: discuss with consultant; route via specialist services"],"safety":["Ask about domestic abuse when alone; IDVA if disclosed","Children: check their wellbeing; safeguarding referral if concerns","Crisis numbers in her language where possible; review in 4 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Never use family to interpret","x":"NHS England guidance: use professional interpreters, not family or friends. Family may filter, be the abuser or silence disclosure."},{"h":"Don't dig for detail","x":"Diagnosis needs only enough to know the event was threatening. Detailed retelling belongs in therapy, not first assessment."},{"h":"ICD-11 three clusters","x":"Re-experiencing in the present, avoidance, and sense of current threat. Complex PTSD adds emotion dysregulation, negative self-concept and relationship problems."},{"h":"Trauma therapy first, not drugs","x":"NICE NG116: trauma-focused CBT or EMDR first. Drugs (sertraline or venlafaxine) are second line or for preference."},{"h":"Flashbacks are not psychosis","x":"Hearing gunfire during a flashback is re-experiencing. Check it's linked to trauma memories before diagnosing psychosis."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Take a trauma-informed PTSD history through an interpreter and agree a safe plan."},"c-2-4-6":{"obj":["Agreed one consistent plan with the medical consultant before speaking to the patient","Explored his fear of illness, checking, searching online and reassurance-seeking, and what triggered it","Screened for depression and suicidal thoughts","Explained the reassurance cycle without implying his pain is imaginary","Recorded red-flag symptoms that would justify new tests, and shared the plan with the GP"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So you think I'm making it up? The pain is real.\"","r":"\"I believe your pain is real. The tests have been careful and thorough. What I want to help with is the worry, which is making everything harder for you.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just one more scan and I'll stop worrying. I promise.\"","r":"\"I understand. But after the last scan, the relief lasted how long? Each test gives a few days of peace, then the worry comes back. Let's try something that lasts longer.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"My dad died of bowel cancer at 50. They missed it with him.\"","r":"\"I'm so sorry. That explains a lot of your fear. Your colonoscopy last month was clear. Your GP will still check any new symptoms. Can we talk about how that loss affected you?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I spend hours every night on Google checking symptoms.\"","r":"\"Thanks for telling me. Searching is like checking: it feels like it helps but keeps the worry going. Cutting it down is part of the therapy.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Every doctor says something different. I don't trust any of you.\"","r":"\"That's frustrating, and I understand why trust is hard. That's why we've agreed one plan with the medical team, and your GP will be your one main doctor for this.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if I really do get cancer one day and you all ignore me?\"","r":"\"A fair worry. Having health anxiety doesn't stop you getting checked. If you have bleeding, weight loss or yellow skin, you'll be seen properly. That's written into your plan.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No repeat imaging without new objective findings","If depression or anxiety needs medication: sertraline 50 mg daily, review at 4 weeks","Screen PHQ-9 and GAD-7"],"psych":["CBT for health anxiety via NHS Talking Therapies (self-referral)","Reduce online checking and body checking as first homework"],"social":["Fit note if needed; occupational health if off work","Explore bereavement support if linked to loss"],"safety":["GP is named clinician; planned regular reviews (e.g. monthly)","Red flags in discharge summary: rectal bleeding, weight loss, jaundice","Ask about suicidal thoughts"]},"facts":[{"h":"Reassurance gives short relief","x":"Each normal result brings relief for days, then doubt returns. More tests strengthen the cycle and expose him to radiation and incidental findings."},{"h":"One clinician, planned visits","x":"Scheduled GP reviews at fixed intervals, not on demand, reduce emergency attendances and unnecessary tests."},{"h":"Health anxiety vs somatic symptoms","x":"Health anxiety: fear of having a disease. Somatic symptom disorder: distress about the symptoms themselves. Both respond to CBT."},{"h":"Health anxiety doesn't protect","x":"Anxious patients still get physical illness. New, objective red flags always need proper assessment."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Assess for health anxiety, explain it without dismissing symptoms, and agree a plan with the medical team."},"c-2-4-7":{"obj":["Explored where the belief came from, how strongly he holds it and whether others in his church share it","Tested conviction gently, asking if any other explanation is possible","Screened for other psychotic features, mood change and functional decline","Obtained collateral from his pastor with consent","Agreed practical solutions for prescriptions and appointments, and clear re-referral signs for the GP"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"My GP thinks I'm crazy. Are you going to put me on medication?\"","r":"\"No, I'm not here to judge your faith. I'd like to understand your beliefs and how they affect your daily life. Then we'll decide together if anything is needed.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"It's in Revelation. 666 is in every bar code. That's just a fact.\"","r":"\"Thank you for explaining. Many people in your church share this, I understand. Is there any chance some bar codes are harmless?\""},{"tag":"Collateral","p":"Pastor: \"Several of our members believe the same. He's devout, but not unusual for us.\"","r":"\"That's very helpful. Has anything about him changed recently: how he talks, sleeps, or acts with others?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How can I get my medicine if everything has a bar code?\"","r":"\"Let's solve that. You can ask for a paper prescription and phone appointments. Is there anyone at church who could help with the practical side?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I feel the beast is sending me messages through the TV.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. That sounds different from the shared belief. Tell me more about these messages: when did they start, and what do they say?\""},{"tag":"Relief","p":"\"So you don't think I'm mentally ill?\"","r":"\"From today, no. Your belief is part of your faith, which many share. If anything changes, like new or frightening experiences, please come back.\""}],"bps":{"psych":["No antipsychotic; no diagnosis of psychosis","Review if new or personal beliefs emerge"],"social":["GP to issue paper FP10 prescriptions; booking by phone or post; cash payment where shops allow it","Pastor and church support with practical tasks, with consent"],"safety":["GP letter: re-refer if belief becomes personal, spreads or other symptoms appear","EIP referral if psychosis develops; NHS standard: treatment within 2 weeks","CMHT duty number given"]},"facts":[{"h":"Shared belief is not a delusion","x":"A belief held by others in his religious or cultural group is not delusional. Ask: 'Do others in your church believe this?'"},{"h":"Overvalued idea defined","x":"Understandable, emotionally charged, dominates life, but not held against all evidence. Common in anorexia, BDD and some religious beliefs."},{"h":"Watch for spread","x":"Red flags: belief becomes personal, new unusual ideas, hallucinations, thought disorder or decline in work and relationships."},{"h":"Test conviction gently","x":"Ask 'Is there any other explanation?' A person with an overvalued idea can usually consider doubt; a delusion cannot."}],"type":"Mental state examination","do":"Classify his belief as overvalued idea, culturally shared belief or delusion, and plan accordingly."},"c-2-5-1":{"obj":["Completed a full psychosocial assessment that names tonight's trigger and any change from her usual pattern","Asked directly about children at home, domestic abuse, substances and current suicidal intent","Explained in one or two sentences why an open-ended admission is unlikely to help, without sounding rejecting","Agreed a written crisis plan with her, including a timed call from the crisis team tomorrow and the 24/7 crisis number","Told the nurse in charge, the care coordinator and the GP the plan before she leaves"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You lot always send me home. Nobody actually listens until I do something worse.\"","r":"\"It sounds like you've felt brushed off before, and I'm sorry. I'm not rushing tonight. Tell me what happened before you cut, and then we'll decide together what will actually help.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just admit me for one night. If you don't, I can't promise I'll be safe.\"","r":"\"I hear how frightened you are of tonight. Let's look at that fear properly: what would make tonight survivable? If something has truly changed, I'll consider a short, planned stay, but let's work it out together.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I know I'm wasting your time. The nurses think I'm attention-seeking.\"","r":"\"You're not wasting my time. Coming here tells me you were in a lot of pain. My job is to understand it, so let's start with how you're feeling right now.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He grabbed my wrist before I did it. He's done it before.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; I know that wasn't easy to say. You don't deserve to be hurt. I'd like to ask a few more questions about your safety at home, and we'll plan where you can sleep tonight.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not doing another safety plan. They're pointless bits of paper.\"","r":"\"Fair enough if the old ones didn't fit. Let's make this one yours: what has got you through a night like this before? We'll write down only things you'd really use.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I just want the feelings to stop. I don't care how.\"","r":"\"That sounds unbearable. When you say you don't care how, are you thinking about ending your life tonight? I'm asking because I want to keep you safe.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No new psychotropic for EUPD itself; if sleep is the crisis, promethazine 25 mg at night for up to 7 nights only","Prescribe any medicines in small (7-day) supplies; ask GP to stop stockpiled paracetamol and opioids","Check paracetamol level if any doubt about co-ingestion; wound check by practice nurse in 5–7 days"],"psych":["Collaborative safety plan written with her: triggers, own coping, two named contacts, crisis number","Crisis resolution and home treatment team (CRHT) phone contact by 12:00 tomorrow","Care coordinator to fast-track her to the personality disorder pathway for DBT or MBT"],"social":["If abuse disclosed: DASH risk checklist tonight; refer to IDVA; MARAC referral if 14+ ticks or high concern","Agree where she sleeps tonight (friend, family or refuge via the National Domestic Abuse Helpline)","Care coordinator emailed tonight; GP discharge letter the same day"],"safety":["Children at home: children's social care referral and trust safeguarding team informed","Give local 24/7 crisis line and NHS 111 option 2; document both on the plan she takes home","Liaison team to add a shared A&E care plan for future attendances; review at next liaison meeting"]},"facts":[{"h":"Every attendance gets a full assessment","x":"Repeat visits never justify a shortened review. Ask what is different this time: new method, new plan, final acts or lost protective ties raise concern."},{"h":"Risk scores do not decide discharge","x":"Do not use risk scales or 'low/medium/high' labels to decide who gets treated or sent home. Write a formulation: what drives risk, what protects, what changes it."},{"h":"Admission: short, planned, with an end date","x":"Unplanned or long stays tend to increase self-harm and dependence in EUPD. If admitting, write the goal and the discharge date on day one."},{"h":"DASH 14+ ticks means MARAC","x":"After a domestic abuse disclosure, complete the DASH checklist. 14 or more 'yes' answers, or strong professional concern, triggers a MARAC referral and an IDVA."},{"h":"Promethazine, not benzodiazepines","x":"For a crisis, a sedating antihistamine for up to one week is acceptable. Avoid benzodiazepines and large supplies: overdose and dependence risk."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Safety plan","do":"Complete a focused psychosocial assessment, agree a safe crisis plan, and decide whether admission helps."},"c-2-5-2":{"obj":["Found out what she already knows about DBT and what she hopes a tablet would do","Explained the four DBT parts (weekly individual, weekly skills group, phone coaching, about 12 months) in plain words","Answered honestly that no medicine treats EUPD itself, and named what medication can help with","Checked her consent to involve her mother and what may be shared","Agreed a safety plan for the wait and confirmed she wants the referral to go ahead"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Talking doesn't work. I've had counselling and it just made me cry every week.\"","r":"\"That sounds exhausting. DBT is different from counselling: it's practical skills training, with homework, coaching calls and a plan for crises. What was it about counselling that didn't help?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So why can't I just have a tablet that stops the feelings?\"","r":"\"I wish there were one. No medicine treats EUPD itself, and tablets can add side effects or overdose risk. If you develop depression or can't sleep in a crisis, we'd treat that separately.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"A group? I can't talk about self-harm in front of strangers.\"","r":"\"Lots of people worry about that. The skills group is more like a class: you learn and practise skills, and you don't share details of self-harm there. That goes to your individual therapist.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"A whole year? What happens if I mess up and cut during it?\"","r":"\"Slips are expected. DBT treats them as information: you and your therapist look at what led up to it and which skill could help next time. You won't be thrown out for one setback.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Mother: \"Can you just tell me everything so I can keep an eye on her?\"","r":"\"I can see how much you care. She's an adult, so I'll share what she's happy for me to share. Let's ask her now what would help her most from you.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"What do I do while I'm waiting? I can't hold on for months.\"","r":"\"That's a fair worry. Your care coordinator will keep weekly contact, we'll write a crisis plan today, and I'll tell you the realistic wait rather than guess.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No new psychotropic for EUPD; review and reduce any drugs started in past crises","Treat a separate depressive episode on its own merits (sertraline 50 mg each morning; review at 2 and 4 weeks)","Crisis sleep only: promethazine 25 mg at night, maximum 7 nights"],"psych":["Standard DBT via personality disorder service: weekly individual therapy, weekly 2-hour skills group, phone coaching, about 12 months","Start a diary card now with her care coordinator to track urges and skills","If DBT not acceptable, discuss MBT as an alternative structured therapy"],"social":["Care coordinator: weekly contact during the wait; liaise with DBT service about start date","Offer her mother a carer's assessment from adult social care (Care Act 2014) and carer support group details"],"safety":["Written safety plan with two named contacts, local crisis line and NHS 111 option 2","GP letter: plan, no new medication, small prescription supplies","Review in clinic in 4 weeks or sooner if self-harm escalates"]},"facts":[{"h":"DBT has four parts","x":"Individual therapy weekly, skills group weekly, phone coaching between sessions, and a therapist consultation team. Usually about a year. Missing any part waters it down."},{"h":"Four skills modules","x":"Mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness. Say them as: notice, survive the moment, calm the storm, ask for what you need."},{"h":"No drug treats EUPD itself","x":"Medication is not recommended for the disorder or its core symptoms. Treat comorbid depression, PTSD or psychosis on their own merits."},{"h":"Life-threatening behaviour goes first","x":"DBT ranks targets: self-harm and suicidal behaviour, then anything that blocks therapy, then quality of life. Use this to reassure her that slips are worked on, not punished."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain DBT in plain language, address her medication concerns, and confirm she wants to proceed."},"c-2-5-3":{"obj":["Named splitting to the nurse in charge in neutral, non-blaming language","Arranged a same-day team meeting and one written plan for leave, observations and PRN used on every shift","Told the patient, warmly, that information is shared within the team","Passed the complaint to the ward manager and safeguarding lead the same day and recorded it","Set admission goals and an expected discharge date with her and the care coordinator"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You're the only doctor here who gets me. Promise you won't tell the others what I said.\"","r":"\"I'm glad our talks help. I can't keep secrets from the team, because we all look after you together. That way you get the same answer whoever's on shift.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"The night staff hate me. They make me wait for everything on purpose.\"","r":"\"That sounds really upsetting. I'd like to understand what's happening at night. I'll make sure every shift works from the same written plan, so it's fair and predictable.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"That healthcare assistant grabbed my arm and laughed at me.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. I'm taking this seriously. I'll pass it to the ward manager and safeguarding lead today, and nobody will treat you differently for raising it.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Nurse: \"She's manipulating you. You can't see it because you're only here in the day.\"","r":"\"You may well be seeing things I miss. Let's get everyone's view in one room today and agree one plan, so none of us is being played off against another.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You betrayed me. I trusted you and you told them everything.\"","r":"\"I can see you feel let down, and that matters. I shared it because the team needs one plan to keep you safe. I'm still here, and the plan stays the same.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"When am I going home? Nobody tells me anything.\"","r":"\"That's a fair question. Let's agree what this admission is for, write it down, and set a date to aim for with your care coordinator this week.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["One PRN plan on the drug chart: drug, dose, maximum in 24 hours and the trigger for use, the same on every shift","Review need for any regular sedatives started during admission"],"psych":["Psychology-led team formulation session within 1 week","Named nurse with a fixed daily 1:1 time; protected weekly reflective practice for staff","Refer to personality disorder pathway for structured therapy after discharge"],"social":["Care coordinator attends a CPA or discharge planning meeting within 2 weeks","Ward manager logs complaint; patient given PALS details"],"safety":["Allegation about staff: safeguarding lead informed same day; trust allegations policy followed","Written leave and observation plan in the care plan; reviewed at weekly ward round","Document behaviour as observed, not labels such as 'manipulative'"]},"facts":[{"h":"Splitting splits staff, not just patients","x":"Staff fall into 'good' and 'bad' camps. Sign: strong disagreements about one patient. Fix: one written plan, one named nurse, shared decisions."},{"h":"Being 'the only one' is a warning","x":"Idealisation feels flattering but often flips to devaluation. Stay warm, share information and keep the same boundaries as colleagues."},{"h":"Complaints never change her care","x":"She must not be treated less well for complaining. Allegations about staff go to the safeguarding lead the same day."},{"h":"Write what you saw","x":"'Shouted and threw a cup at 14:10' helps the next shift. 'Manipulative' or 'attention-seeking' damages care and can be read by her later."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"Name the splitting, help the team agree one consistent plan, and take the complaint seriously."},"c-2-6-1":{"obj":["Obtained a clear pre-injury personality picture and specific changes from his wife","Estimated injury severity from loss of consciousness and post-traumatic amnesia","Assessed risk to his wife, children, the public and himself, including driving and money","Explained the brain-behaviour link to his wife in plain words","Made referrals for imaging, neuropsychology, carer support and safeguarding before they leave"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"Wife: \"He's not the man I married. Sometimes I think he hates me.\"","r":"\"That must be heartbreaking. The injury affected the part of the brain that controls temper and judgement. It isn't about how he feels about you. Can I ask how safe you feel at home?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"There's nothing wrong with me. The neighbours wind me up, that's all.\"","r":"\"It sounds like the neighbours really get to you. Your wife has noticed some changes since the accident, which is common after a head injury. Would you be willing to do some memory and thinking tests?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"Wife: \"Will he get back to normal?\"","r":"\"Honestly, it's hard to predict. Some improvement can continue for a year or two, but some changes may stay. Specialist rehabilitation can help both of you manage what remains.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"Wife: \"He's never hit me, but he punched the wall next to my head last week.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. That's frightening and it matters. I'd like to go through some safety questions with you and put support in place, including for the children.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not giving up my car. I need it for everything.\"","r":"\"I understand how much it matters. After a head injury the law says you must tell the DVLA if it could affect driving. Let's check what applies to you, and I'll write it down.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Wife: \"He's spending thousands. Can I stop him?\"","r":"\"That's a real worry. We can check whether he can make money decisions. If he can't, there are legal routes such as a deputy through the Court of Protection. Meanwhile, talk to your bank about protections.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["MRI brain (better than CT for contusions and diffuse axonal injury) if none since the injury","EEG if episodic rage, staring spells or blank periods suggest seizures","Avoid routine benzodiazepines and high-dose antipsychotics: worsen cognition and lower seizure threshold; specialist advice before any drug for aggression","Screen alcohol: AUDIT; bloods FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT"],"psych":["Neuropsychology assessment of executive function by clinical neuropsychologist","Referral to neuropsychiatry or community brain injury rehabilitation team for behaviour plan","Headway support groups for him and his wife"],"social":["Carer's assessment for his wife by adult social care (Care Act 2014)","Capacity assessment for finances; discuss deputyship with adult social care if he lacks capacity","Occupational health or Jobcentre disability employment adviser if still working"],"safety":["DASH checklist with wife; IDVA referral; MARAC if high risk","Children's social care referral via trust safeguarding team","Advise him to stop driving and notify DVLA; document advice given","Inform police liaison and diversion team if charged; review in clinic within 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"PTA length tells you severity","x":"Post-traumatic amnesia over 24 hours means a severe injury; over 7 days, very severe. Ask: 'What is the first thing you remember after the crash?'"},{"h":"Talks well, acts badly","x":"Frontal injury can leave conversation intact while judgement fails. Test capacity by asking him to explain and weigh specific decisions, such as spending."},{"h":"Frontal change has a pattern","x":"Disinhibition, impulsivity, irritability, apathy, poor planning and loss of empathy. Get the wife's 'before and after' with concrete instances."},{"h":"Sedate low, slow, short","x":"Brain-injured patients are sensitive to sedation and seizures. Use the lowest effective dose and seek neuropsychiatry advice before regular medication."},{"h":"Not a personality disorder","x":"New personality change after injury in a previously settled adult is organic. A personality disorder label blocks access to brain injury services."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Take a history of personality change after brain injury, assess risk, and plan further assessment."},"c-2-6-2":{"obj":["Taken an eyewitness account and asked for any phone video of an attack","Listed at least three features favouring dissociative and three favouring epileptic seizures","Agreed with A&E to continue lamotrigine and to avoid further benzodiazepine unless clearly epileptic","Explained dissociative seizures to her as real, common and treatable, without saying 'pseudo' or 'not real'","Arranged neurology review for video EEG and given driving advice in writing"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So you think I'm faking it? That's what the last doctor said.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry you were made to feel that. These attacks are real and not under your control. My job is to work out which type they are, because each has a treatment that works.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If it's not epilepsy, does that mean I'm going mad?\"","r":"\"No. It means the brain's 'software' is temporarily switching off under strain, a bit like a computer freezing. It's very common in neurology clinics and many people improve with therapy.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should I stop my epilepsy tablets then?\"","r":"\"Please don't. You have a past diagnosis of epilepsy and both types can happen in the same person. Neurology will decide after the video EEG.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not seeing a psychiatrist about seizures. That's ridiculous.\"","r":"\"That's understandable. Neurology and psychiatry work together on this. The therapy teaches you to spot early warning feelings and stop attacks. Would you like written information to read first?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"A&E registrar: \"Just give her more lorazepam and we'll discharge her.\"","r":"\"I'd hold off. The features look dissociative, and repeated benzodiazepines risk breathing problems. If an attack looks epileptic and lasts over five minutes, follow the seizure protocol. Can we ask neurology about video EEG?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Mother: \"What do we do if she has one at home?\"","r":"\"Keep her safe from injury, stay calm, talk to her quietly and time it. Don't restrain her. If it looks like her usual epileptic seizure or goes past five minutes, follow her rescue plan and call 999.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue lamotrigine at current dose; check adherence; note oestrogen-containing contraception lowers lamotrigine levels","No further benzodiazepine for attacks with dissociative features; buccal midazolam 10 mg only for clearly epileptic seizures lasting 5 minutes or more","Neurology referral for video EEG telemetry to capture typical attacks"],"psych":["Once confirmed: psychoeducation, then CBT-informed therapy for dissociative seizures via neuropsychiatry or FND service","Screen for anxiety, depression, PTSD and current stressors; treat comorbidity"],"social":["Written information for her and family (neurosymptoms.org)","Ask gently about trauma or current abuse; workplace or college adjustments if attacks continue"],"safety":["Advise no driving and to notify DVLA; document advice","Written attack plan for family and A&E: when to use rescue medication, when not","GP letter with plan and neurology follow-up date"]},"facts":[{"h":"Eyes shut favour dissociative","x":"Eyes closed and resisting opening, long duration (over 2 minutes), side-to-side head shaking, waxing and waning, pelvic thrusting and crying favour dissociative. Eyes open and stereotyped short events favour epileptic."},{"h":"Video EEG is the gold standard","x":"Capturing a typical attack on video with simultaneous EEG gives the diagnosis. A normal routine EEG between attacks proves nothing either way."},{"h":"Both can coexist","x":"A meaningful minority of people with dissociative seizures also have epilepsy. Never stop antiepileptics until neurology has confirmed the picture."},{"h":"Too much benzodiazepine harms","x":"Dissociative attacks treated as status epilepticus lead to respiratory depression and ICU admissions. Time the attack and check features before giving rescue medication."},{"h":"Words change outcomes","x":"Say 'dissociative' or 'functional' seizures. Patients who accept the diagnosis do better; 'pseudo' or 'not real' makes them disengage."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Distinguish dissociative from epileptic seizures, keep her safe, and explain the plan without blame."},"c-2-6-3":{"obj":["Checked hydration, nutrition, pressure areas and VTE prophylaxis are covered today","Screened for catatonia with the Bush-Francis scale and proposed a monitored lorazepam challenge","Asked the medical team for EEG, MRI and to discuss LP with encephalitis antibodies","Recorded an MCA best interests decision and consulted her family","Spoken to her directly and respectfully, explaining each step, even though she is mute"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Medical registrar: \"Her bloods are normal. It's psych. Can you take her today?\"","r":"\"I agree something psychological may be going on after her brother's death. Before transfer, though, we need EEG for non-convulsive status and MRI. Let's share her care and review daily.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"Brother's partner: \"Is she going to die like this?\"","r":"\"I can see how frightening this is. She's safe, and we're giving fluids and checking for every treatable cause. I'll update you each day.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Mother: \"Can she hear us when we talk to her?\"","r":"\"Very possibly, yes. Please keep talking to her normally, tell her you're here and what's happening. It may help her feel safe.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Nurse: \"She's just being stubborn. Why don't we leave her until she's hungry?\"","r":"\"I understand it's frustrating. She can't currently choose to respond, and she's at real risk of dehydration and clots. Please keep the fluid chart and turns going while we investigate.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Mother: \"Why give her a sedative if she's already not moving?\"","r":"\"Good question. If this is catatonia, a small dose of lorazepam often wakes people up within minutes. It's both a test and a treatment, and we'll monitor her breathing closely.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["IV fluids with fluid balance chart; daily U&E, CK, glucose; NEWS2 observations at least 4-hourly","Enoxaparin 40 mg SC once daily unless contraindicated (adjust for renal function), plus mechanical prophylaxis assessment","Lorazepam challenge 1–2 mg IV, re-rate Bush-Francis after 10–30 minutes; oxygen saturations monitored, flumazenil available","Avoid antipsychotics until catatonia and NMS are excluded; dietitian to plan NG feeding if no intake by day 3"],"psych":["Daily liaison psychiatry review; talk to her and explain care at every contact","Once responsive: gentle exploration of the bereavement; later bereavement or trauma-focused therapy"],"social":["Collateral from family on recent stress, prior episodes, substances and medication","Offer family Cruse bereavement support details"],"safety":["MCA best interests record for fluids, feeding and investigations; family consulted","Urgent DoLS authorisation if she is under continuous supervision and not free to leave","Pressure area care with 2-hourly turns; Waterlow score; mouth and eye care"]},"facts":[{"h":"Rule out NCSE with an EEG","x":"Non-convulsive status epilepticus can look exactly like stupor. Only an EEG excludes it, so ask for one before calling it psychological."},{"h":"Lorazepam challenge tests catatonia","x":"Give 1–2 mg IV and re-score Bush-Francis. A clear improvement supports catatonia and guides regular lorazepam. Monitor saturations; have flumazenil ready."},{"h":"Young woman, mute: think encephalitis","x":"Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis can start with psychiatric features then mutism, dyskinesias and autonomic instability. Fever or orofacial movements: same-day neurology, LP and antibodies."},{"h":"Stupor kills through the body","x":"Dehydration, AKI, pressure sores, aspiration and PE are the real dangers. Fluids, turns and VTE prophylaxis start on day one, whatever the cause."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Assess the stupor, exclude organic causes and catatonia, and plan safe physical care."},"c-2-6-4":{"obj":["Explained the purpose of the tests and gained his consent before starting","Completed at least six frontal tests: letter and category fluency, Luria sequence, go/no-go, similarities or proverbs, cognitive estimates and primitive reflexes","Reported findings to the consultant in one structured summary with a differential that includes a structural lesion","Requested MRI brain, a dementia blood screen and neuropsychology","Advised him and his wife about driving, lasting power of attorney and carer support"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't see why I need puzzles. My memory's perfect. It's my wife who has the problem.\"","r":"\"You're right that your memory seems good. These tests look at a different part of thinking, like planning and switching tasks. It helps us understand what's happened at work. Shall we try?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"This is childish. Tap once, tap twice, what's the point?\"","r":"\"I know it feels simple. It checks how well the brain holds back an automatic response, which can explain the trouble at work. It takes two minutes.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So do I have dementia or not?\"","r":"\"I can't say yet. Some tests were harder for you, so we need a brain scan and more detailed testing. There are several possible causes, including some that are treatable.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"Wife: \"He laughed at a funeral last week. I'm so embarrassed I've stopped going out.\"","r":"\"That sounds really hard, and it isn't his fault or yours. Changes like this can come from the front of the brain. Let's also look at support for you.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I only eat sweets because I'm bored. Lots of people do.\"","r":"\"That's possible. Your wife noticed it's new for you, and new changes in appetite can be a clue. That's why we're checking carefully.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["MRI brain to look for frontal or temporal atrophy, tumour, vascular change","Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, calcium, glucose, TFT, B12, folate; syphilis and HIV with consent","Do not start cholinesterase inhibitors or memantine if frontotemporal dementia is confirmed"],"psych":["Neuropsychology for detailed executive and social cognition testing","Memory clinic MDT review; specialist cognitive neurology for under-65s","Carer education on behaviour change and practical strategies"],"social":["Lasting power of attorney (finances and health) while he has capacity","Carer's assessment for his wife by adult social care","Occupational health and Equality Act advice; benefits check (PIP)"],"safety":["Advise him to notify DVLA; document advice","Capacity check for big financial decisions; bank alert with consent","Results and next steps in a joint appointment within 4 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Frontal signs, memory spared","x":"Disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy, compulsive acts, sweet craving and poor planning with good recall point towards behavioural-variant FTD. Exclude a frontal lesion first."},{"h":"Fluency is your fastest test","x":"Ask for words starting with F in one minute, then animals. Interpret against age and education; perseverations and rule breaks matter as much as the count."},{"h":"Do three hand sequences","x":"Fist-edge-palm (Luria): demonstrate, then ask for five unaided runs. Breakdown or getting stuck on one position is perseveration."},{"h":"Check primitive reflexes","x":"Grasp, palmomental and pout reflexes. A clear grasp reflex in an adult is significant and often forgotten."},{"h":"Tumours can mimic dementia","x":"A frontal meningioma or glioma can cause exactly this picture with normal memory. That is why MRI comes before a dementia label."}],"type":"Cognitive assessment","do":"Perform and explain a structured frontal lobe examination, recording each test result."},"c-2-6-5":{"obj":["Set the agenda early: a careful assessment today, no same-day prescription, without dismissing his request","Established symptoms before age 12 and impairment in at least two settings","Screened for mood disorder (including bipolar), substance use and cardiac risk","Gained consent to contact a parent or sibling for childhood history","Given driving and 'do not use other people's medication' advice and sent a clear referral letter"],"snip":[{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just give me what my mate takes. It worked straight away, so I obviously have it.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you felt calmer, and it makes sense to look into ADHD. But stimulants help many people focus, so that doesn't confirm a diagnosis. Let's get the assessment right so any treatment is safe.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I've waited months for this. Now you're telling me to wait longer?\"","r":"\"That's really frustrating, and I'm sorry. I'll be honest about wait times and explain Right to Choose, which may be quicker. Meanwhile we can work on sleep, work and anything else today.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do you need to talk to my mum? I'm 29.\"","r":"\"Good question. ADHD has to start in childhood, and most of us don't remember our early school years well. Your mum's view, or old school reports, make the assessment much stronger. Only with your permission.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I use a bit of coke at weekends. It helps me focus too.\"","r":"\"Thanks for being straight with me. Cocaine can cause or mask attention problems, and mixing it with stimulants strains the heart. Let's talk about cutting down, and I'll note it so the specialist can plan safely.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will I lose my job if they find out I've got ADHD?\"","r":"\"It's a common worry. ADHD can count as a disability, so your employer must consider reasonable adjustments. Access to Work can help too. What you share with work is your choice.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I still drive for work if I'm on tablets?\"","r":"\"Usually yes, if the medication is prescribed and you're not impaired. Tell the DVLA if your condition affects safe driving, and never drive if you feel unwell or drowsy.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Baseline before any stimulant: BP, pulse, weight, height, cardiac history; ECG if heart disease, syncope, family sudden death or BP high","If diagnosed: lisdexamfetamine (Elvanse) 30 mg each morning, increase by 20 mg weekly, max 70 mg; or methylphenidate XL 18 mg each morning, adjust weekly, max 108 mg","Warn: poor appetite, insomnia (take before about 10:00), raised pulse and BP, anxiety, headache","Monitor pulse and BP after each dose change and every 6 months; weight every 6 months; annual specialist review"],"psych":["ASRS screener today; DIVA-5 structured interview by the adult ADHD service","Address substances first: refer to drug and alcohol service if use is regular","After diagnosis: group psychoeducation or ADHD-focused CBT if available"],"social":["Access to Work and workplace adjustments; GP fit note only if needed","Explain Right to Choose (England) for an approved provider"],"safety":["Advise not to use others' stimulants: illegal supply and cardiac risk","Driving advice documented; DVLA notification if condition or medication impairs driving","Shared care with GP only after specialist titration is stable"]},"facts":[{"h":"Childhood onset is non-negotiable","x":"Symptoms must start before 12 and impair at least two settings. No childhood history from someone who knew him means no diagnosis yet."},{"h":"Stimulant response proves nothing","x":"Stimulants sharpen attention in most people. Feeling calmer on a friend's tablets is a misuse warning, not a diagnostic test."},{"h":"Lisdexamfetamine goes in the morning","x":"Start 30 mg on waking; step up 20 mg weekly to 70 mg max. Late doses cause insomnia. Check pulse and BP at each step."},{"h":"Schedule 2: 30-day supply","x":"Stimulants are Schedule 2 controlled drugs. Prescribe no more than 30 days at a time and watch for early requests or lost scripts."},{"h":"Screen for bipolar before treating","x":"Ask about spells of reduced sleep need, high energy and overspending. Stimulants can trigger mania; episodic symptoms suggest a mood disorder."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Take a focused adult ADHD history, explain how diagnosis is made, and agree a safe next step."},"c-2-7-1":{"obj":["Asked what she already knows and fears before giving any numbers","Given an honest prognosis with at least two good and two poor prognostic features that apply to her","Named her treatment: one NICE-recommended therapy, GP physical monitoring and the red flags for urgent help","Checked what she consents to share with her mother before inviting her in","Agreed a next appointment date and given crisis and Beat helpline details"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"My mum read that people die from this. Am I going to die?\"","r":"\"That's a frightening thing to read. Anorexia is serious, and I won't hide that. But most people who get treatment early, like you are now, improve, and many recover completely.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not even that thin. Other girls on my course are smaller than me.\"","r":"\"It can be hard to see from the inside. Your BMI and blood tests tell us your body is under real strain. Can we focus on what you've noticed, like tiredness or feeling cold?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'll do therapy, but I'm not gaining weight.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you'll try therapy. Weight is part of recovery because a starved brain makes anorexia's thoughts louder. We'll go at a pace you can manage, and your therapist will help with the fear.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I have to drop out of uni?\"","r":"\"Not necessarily. Many students carry on with treatment and support from student services. If your body becomes unsafe, health comes first, but that's something we'd decide together.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Please don't tell my mum anything. She'll freak out.\"","r":"\"That's your choice, and I'll respect it. Families often help recovery, so let's agree what you're comfortable sharing. If your life were ever in danger, I'd have to involve others, and I'd tell you first.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"Mother: \"Is this my fault? Did I do something wrong?\"","r":"\"No. Eating disorders have many causes and parents don't cause them. You can be a big part of her recovery, and we'll give you support too.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GP monitoring: weight, pulse, lying and standing BP, temperature; bloods (FBC, U&E, phosphate, magnesium, LFT, glucose) and ECG at frequency set by MEED risk","No medication as sole treatment; bone density scan if underweight for 2 years or more","Same-day medical review if pulse under 40, fainting, chest pain or temperature under 35 °C"],"psych":["CBT-ED (up to 40 sessions over 40 weeks), MANTRA or SSCM (about 20 sessions) via community eating disorder service","FREED early-intervention pathway if available (age 16–25, illness under 3 years)"],"social":["With consent: university health service and disability adviser for study adjustments","Mother: carer information, Beat carer support and skills sessions"],"safety":["MEED risk rating recorded with escalation plan in GP letter","Ask about self-harm and suicidal thoughts; crisis line and NHS 111 option 2 given","Review in clinic within 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"The honest numbers","x":"Long-term studies suggest roughly half recover fully, about a third improve, and about one in five stay unwell. Early treatment shifts the odds."},{"h":"Good and poor signs","x":"Better: young onset, short illness, early treatment, family support. Worse: long duration, very low BMI, purging, comorbid depression or substance use."},{"h":"Anorexia has the highest death rate","x":"It carries the highest mortality of any psychiatric illness, from cardiac events and suicide. Say it calmly, then pivot to what reduces risk."},{"h":"Pulse under 40 = same day","x":"Bradycardia below 40, fainting, chest pain or hypothermia need same-day medical assessment. A healthy-looking student can still be medically unsafe."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis","do":"Explain anorexia nervosa, its prognosis and treatment plan plainly, and address her fears honestly."},"c-2-7-2":{"obj":["Named her high refeeding risk using at least two criteria (BMI under 16, little intake over 10 days)","Ordered bloods including phosphate, magnesium, potassium, glucose and an ECG before the first meal","Prescribed thiamine, vitamin B compound strong and a multivitamin to start before feeding","Left a written weekend plan with blood frequency, obs and escalation thresholds","Agreed with the medical registrar how electrolytes will be replaced and when to transfer"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"If you make me eat a full plate tonight I'm leaving. I can't do it.\"","r":"\"I hear how scary that is. We won't start with full meals. We'll begin carefully with a set plan, and the nurses will support you at each meal. Can we try the first snack together?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why are you taking so much blood? I'm fine.\"","r":"\"Your body has been short of food, and when eating restarts, salts like phosphate can drop fast and affect your heart. Daily checks let us top them up before that happens.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can I just have fruit and water until Monday?\"","r":"\"I understand that feels safer. But too little food is risky too: low blood sugar and more weight loss. The plan gives just enough, and the dietitian will review it Monday.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Nurse: \"Her phosphate is low this morning. Should we stop feeding?\"","r":"\"Please keep feeding. Low phosphate means we replace it, not stop. I'll prescribe replacement now, recheck this afternoon and speak to the medical registrar.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"My hands and feet are swelling. Is my heart failing?\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me straight away. Some swelling is common when eating restarts, but I'll check you now, including your heart, bloods and fluid chart.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Everyone's watching me eat. It's humiliating.\"","r":"\"That sounds really hard. Supervision is to support you, not judge you. Let's ask the nurses to sit with you quietly, away from others, and agree what helps.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Thiamine 200–300 mg orally daily, vitamin B compound strong 1–2 tablets three times daily and a multivitamin once daily, starting before the first meal, for 10 days","Feed per trust/MEED protocol; MEED supports starting around 1400–2000 kcal/day with close monitoring; avoid underfeeding","Bloods (U&E, phosphate, magnesium, glucose) daily for the first week; capillary glucose before meals; ECG for QTc and rate","Replace phosphate, potassium and magnesium per protocol while continuing to feed"],"psych":["Meal support from trained nurses; 30–60 minutes post-meal supervision","Eating disorder team and dietitian review Monday"],"social":["Inform family with consent; plan for visits that support meals"],"safety":["Observations four times a day, including temperature and SUSS test daily","Escalate to on-call medical team if phosphate falls sharply, pulse under 40, chest pain, oedema, confusion or glucose under 3","Capacity assessment for feeding if she refuses; MHA assessment if refusing with serious risk"]},"facts":[{"h":"High-risk criteria","x":"Any one: BMI under 16, more than 15% weight loss in 3–6 months, little intake for over 10 days, or low potassium, phosphate or magnesium before feeding."},{"h":"Phosphate drops on days 2–4","x":"Insulin surges with carbohydrate, pulling phosphate into cells. Check bloods daily the first week; the dip usually comes after a day or two."},{"h":"Replace, don't stop","x":"Falling electrolytes are a reason to replace and monitor more, not to halt feeding. Stopping drives hypoglycaemia and further weight loss."},{"h":"Thiamine before the first meal","x":"Carbohydrate without thiamine can tip someone into Wernicke's. Prescribe it before or with the first meal and continue for 10 days."},{"h":"SUSS shows muscle weakness","x":"Sit Up–Squat–Stand: can she rise from lying and from squatting without using her hands? Failure signals dangerous muscle loss."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Assess refeeding risk, order phosphate and electrolytes, and write a weekend refeeding and monitoring plan."},"c-2-7-3":{"obj":["Clarified binge frequency, size and type of compensatory behaviours, including laxatives and insulin omission","Asked about palpitations, dizziness, dental damage, mood and self-harm","Explained that the first treatment is guided self-help, then CBT-ED, and that tablets alone are not recommended","Arranged potassium and an ECG today and agreed what result means same-day action","Given dental advice and Beat helpline details"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm so ashamed. I've never told anyone this.\"","r":"\"Thank you for trusting me. Bulimia is common and it's an illness, not a weakness. Telling someone is a big first step. Can you tell me what a typical day looks like?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there just a tablet to stop the binges?\"","r":"\"Fair question. Tablets alone don't work well for bulimia. The best evidence is for a therapy focused on eating, starting with guided self-help. If your mood is low too, medication may help alongside it.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"My weight's normal, so it can't be that serious, right?\"","r":"\"I understand why it seems that way. Vomiting can drop your potassium and affect your heart even at a normal weight. That's why I'd like a blood test and heart trace today.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I skip my insulin to lose weight.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. That's really important, because it can cause a dangerous condition called ketoacidosis. I'd like to involve the diabetes team today so they can help safely.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I stop being sick, I'll put on loads of weight.\"","r":"\"That's a very common fear. Vomiting doesn't get rid of most calories, and regular eating usually reduces binges. Most people's weight stays fairly stable in treatment.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should I brush my teeth after? I do it straight away.\"","r":"\"Good that you're thinking about it. Rinse with water or a fluoride mouthwash instead, and wait about an hour before brushing. Brushing straight away rubs acid into the enamel.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Potassium and ECG today; repeat potassium regularly while vomiting; same-day medical review if potassium under 2.5 mmol/L or ECG changes","If an antidepressant is used alongside therapy: fluoxetine is licensed for bulimia at 60 mg once daily in the morning","Reduce laxatives gradually rather than stopping abruptly"],"psych":["Bulimia-focused guided self-help: 4–9 short supportive sessions over 16 weeks","If not effective or not acceptable: individual CBT-ED, at least 20 sessions over 20 weeks, eating disorder service"],"social":["Dental review; Beat helpline and support groups","Discuss stress, finances and relationships linked to binges"],"safety":["Insulin omission: urgent diabetes team involvement","Screen self-harm and suicidal thoughts; give crisis line","Advise reporting fainting to DVLA; GP review in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Normal weight, real danger","x":"Purging can cause hypokalaemia, arrhythmias and sudden death at any weight. Potassium and ECG are routine, not optional."},{"h":"Guided self-help comes first","x":"First line in adults is bulimia-focused guided self-help with brief support sessions. Step up to CBT-ED if it isn't working."},{"h":"Fluoxetine dose is 60 mg","x":"The bulimia dose is higher than for depression: 60 mg once daily. Never the sole treatment; use alongside therapy."},{"h":"Don't brush straight after vomiting","x":"Rinse with water or fluoride mouthwash and wait before brushing to protect enamel. Arrange regular dental checks."},{"h":"Ask about insulin omission","x":"In type 1 diabetes, skipping insulin to lose weight risks ketoacidosis and early complications. Ask every patient with diabetes directly."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Take a bulimia history, assess physical and psychological risk, and explain treatment options."},"c-2-8-1":{"obj":["Found out her relapse history, what she knows about lithium in pregnancy and what she wants","Explained the cardiac malformation risk in absolute numbers alongside the relapse risk of stopping","Advised her not to stop lithium suddenly and to continue contraception until a plan is agreed","Outlined pregnancy monitoring: levels every 4 weeks, weekly from 36 weeks, within 24 hours of birth","Explained the valproate rules in general terms without discussing her sister's care"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to stop my lithium to get pregnant?\"","r":"\"Not necessarily. Some women stay on it, some switch, some stop slowly. It depends on how unwell you've been off it. The perinatal team will help us make a plan together.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I read lithium causes heart defects. I couldn't live with that.\"","r":"\"That's an understandable worry. The risk is real but small: roughly 1 to 2 extra babies in 100 have a heart problem. We'd check the baby's heart with a detailed scan.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Isn't it selfish to take medication while pregnant?\"","r":"\"No. Staying well is good for your baby too. Becoming very unwell during pregnancy or after the birth carries serious risks for both of you.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I actually stopped my pill last month. I didn't think it mattered.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Let's do a pregnancy test today and agree a plan quickly. Please don't stop your lithium on your own.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"My sister's on valproate. Is that safe for her?\"","r":"\"I can't comment on her care, but there are strict UK rules for valproate in women who could become pregnant. She should ask her own doctor about the safety programme.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I breastfeed on lithium?\"","r":"\"It's possible in some cases, but lithium passes into breast milk and the baby would need close monitoring. Many services advise against it. We'll plan this with the perinatal team before the birth.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue lithium for now; if stopping, taper over at least 4 weeks, never abruptly","Folic acid 400 micrograms daily from when trying to conceive until 12 weeks","In pregnancy: lithium level every 4 weeks, weekly from 36 weeks, within 24 hours of birth; detailed anomaly scan and fetal echo","Deliver in hospital; maintain fluids in labour; plan postnatal dose reduction as clearance falls after birth"],"psych":["Preconception counselling with the perinatal mental health team","Relapse signature and early warning signs plan shared with partner"],"social":["Partner involved in planning; written birth and postnatal plan shared with midwife, obstetrics, GP and perinatal team"],"safety":["Continue contraception until plan agreed","Postnatal relapse plan: early warning signs, crisis line, perinatal team contact in first 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Absolute risk, not relative","x":"Lithium roughly doubles heart malformation risk, from about 1 in 100 to about 2 in 100. Ebstein's anomaly itself is rare. Say numbers, not 'doubles'."},{"h":"Postnatal relapse is the big danger","x":"Women with bipolar I face a high relapse risk after birth, including postpartum psychosis. Stopping lithium raises it further."},{"h":"Never stop lithium abruptly","x":"Sudden withdrawal raises relapse risk sharply. If stopping, taper over at least 4 weeks under the perinatal team."},{"h":"Lithium toxicity after birth","x":"Kidney clearance falls back quickly after delivery. Check a level within 24 hours, watch fluids and expect to reduce the dose."},{"h":"Valproate: two specialists, under 55","x":"MHRA rules: valproate must not be started in anyone under 55 unless two specialists agree there is no alternative, with pregnancy prevention in place for women who could conceive."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Counsel her on lithium before and during pregnancy, and explain valproate pregnancy prevention rules."},"c-2-8-2":{"obj":["Explained why a steady methadone dose is safer for her baby than a rapid reduction","Described neonatal withdrawal honestly, including that it is treatable","Discussed social care openly: methadone alone is not a reason for removal","Covered safe storage, naloxone and other children in the home","Arranged joint care with the specialist substance misuse midwife"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I don't want my baby born addicted. Cut my dose now.\"","r":"\"It shows how much you care about your baby. But cutting quickly can cause withdrawal in the womb and relapse. A steady dose is safer. If you want to reduce later, we'll do it slowly.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Social services will take my baby if they see I'm on methadone.\"","r":"\"That's a common fear. Being on treatment is not by itself a reason to remove a baby. Staying stable and engaged shows you're putting your baby first. If anyone needs to be involved, we'll talk first.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will the baby have withdrawals?\"","r":"\"Possibly. Many babies have some symptoms in the first days, like being unsettled or feeding poorly. The neonatal team will watch closely and treat if needed.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I breastfeed on methadone?\"","r":"\"Usually yes, and it can ease withdrawal for the baby. It's safe if you're not using other drugs and your blood tests are clear. We'll check this with your midwife.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've been craving in the evenings lately. I'm scared I'll use.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Late pregnancy often makes methadone wear off faster. That's not your fault. Let's review your dose, possibly split it into two doses.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking naloxone home. I'm clean.\"","r":"\"I'm really glad you're clean. Naloxone is like a fire extinguisher: you hope never to use it. It can also save a child who swallows methadone by accident.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue methadone at the dose that stops withdrawal; supervised consumption as agreed","Third trimester: review for evening withdrawal; consider dose increase or split twice-daily dosing","Offer HIV, hepatitis B and C and syphilis testing; hepatitis B vaccination if negative","ECG if dose over 100 mg daily or other QTc risk"],"psych":["Keyworker sessions and relapse-prevention work at the drug service","Smoking cessation support"],"social":["Specialist substance misuse midwife and obstetric joint care","Pre-birth planning with the midwife; involve her openly in any referral to children's social care"],"safety":["Naloxone kit supplied and demonstrated (IM or nasal)","Methadone stored in a locked box out of children's reach","Birth plan shared with neonatal team for withdrawal monitoring; dose review every 4 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Rapid detox harms the fetus","x":"Sudden withdrawal can cause fetal distress, preterm labour and relapse with overdose risk. Stable methadone is safer than rushing to stop."},{"h":"Late pregnancy needs more methadone","x":"Bigger blood volume and faster metabolism shorten its effect in the third trimester. Evening cravings mean dose review, not drug-seeking."},{"h":"Neonatal withdrawal is expected and treatable","x":"Many exposed babies show irritability, poor feeding or tremor in the first days. Neonatal staff monitor and treat; it is not lasting harm."},{"h":"Breastfeeding is usually encouraged","x":"On stable methadone, without other drug use and with negative HIV status, breastfeeding is generally supported and may ease the baby's withdrawal."},{"h":"A small dose can kill a child","x":"Store methadone locked away. Give naloxone and show how to use it before she leaves."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain methadone in pregnancy, address her fears about the baby and social services, and agree a plan."},"c-2-8-3":{"obj":["Asked directly about suicidal thoughts and thoughts of harming the baby","Distinguished intrusive unwanted thoughts from intent, and excluded postpartum psychosis","Asked about previous bipolar disorder or postpartum psychosis","Explained treatment options: therapy, breastfeeding-compatible antidepressant, perinatal team","Agreed a written safety plan with her and her partner, including when to call 999"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm a terrible mother. My baby deserves someone better.\"","r":"\"I'm so sorry you're feeling this. Depression makes good mothers feel terrible. The fact you're worried shows how much you care. Let's talk about getting you better.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I get pictures of dropping her down the stairs. I'd never do it.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Unwanted thoughts like that are common with postnatal depression, and the fact they horrify you is reassuring. Do you ever feel you might act on them?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will they take my baby if I admit how bad it is?\"","r":"\"That's a common fear. Getting help is what keeps families together. Most mums with depression get support at home with their baby.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I take tablets if I'm breastfeeding?\"","r":"\"Yes, some antidepressants are safe while breastfeeding. Sertraline is often used because very little gets into breast milk. We'll check the details together.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to see a psychiatrist. I just need sleep.\"","r":"\"Sleep really matters. The perinatal team are experts with new mums and can help with sleep too. Would you be willing to meet them once?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Partner: \"What should I watch out for?\"","r":"\"Call straight away if she seems confused, isn't sleeping at all, says strange things, or talks about harming herself or the baby. Otherwise, help with night feeds so she can sleep.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["If antidepressant chosen: sertraline 50 mg once daily, increase by 50 mg after at least a week if needed, max 200 mg","Warn: nausea, early anxiety, 2–4 weeks to work; don't stop suddenly","Bloods: FBC, TFT (postpartum thyroiditis), ferritin"],"psych":["CBT or interpersonal therapy via perinatal team or NHS Talking Therapies (perinatal priority)","Mother-infant bonding work if bonding is impaired"],"social":["Partner to take some night feeds; family help with practical tasks","Health visitor increases visits; peer support (PANDAS)"],"safety":["Urgent referral to specialist community perinatal mental health team","Written safety plan with crisis line and 999 criteria; partner given a copy","Mother and Baby Unit via perinatal team if admission needed; review within 1 week"]},"facts":[{"h":"Intrusive thoughts are not intent","x":"Unwanted, horrifying images of harming the baby are common and rarely acted on. Ask about intent and plans, not just thoughts."},{"h":"Seven weeks is not baby blues","x":"Blues peak around day 3–5 and settle by 2 weeks. Persistent low mood beyond that is depression until proven otherwise."},{"h":"Sertraline suits breastfeeding","x":"Low levels pass into breast milk. Start 50 mg daily; check the baby is feeding and settling normally."},{"h":"Postpartum psychosis is an emergency","x":"Confusion, little sleep, odd beliefs or rapid mood change after birth need same-day assessment. Highest risk in bipolar I or a previous episode."},{"h":"Check the thyroid","x":"Postpartum thyroiditis can mimic depression. Check TFTs and FBC in any new postnatal mood change."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Take a postnatal depression history, assess risk to mother and baby, and explain treatment options."},"c-2-8-4":{"obj":["Asked her, in plain words, about thoughts of harming herself or the baby, and recorded her answers verbatim","Sent a physical screen (FBC, CRP, U&E, calcium, TFT, glucose, urine drug screen) and checked obs for sepsis or delirium","Put her on 1:1 nursing with the baby cared for in sight of a named staff member","Phoned the perinatal or crisis team and the bed manager to request a Mother and Baby Unit bed tonight","Given her husband a clear explanation, what to watch for and a number to call"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not ill. I'm just tired. Every new mum is like this, aren't they?\"","r":"\"You're right that tiredness is normal with a newborn. But going without sleep for two days and feeling the baby has been swapped is different, and it worries me. I'd like to help you get rest and feel like yourself.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He's not mine. They switched him. He keeps sending me signals through his eyes.\"","r":"\"That sounds frightening and confusing. Thank you for telling me. When you feel he's not yours, have you had any thoughts of hurting him, or of something you need to do to him?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to take my baby away?\"","r":"\"Our aim is to keep you and your baby together. There are special units where mums and babies are admitted together. Tonight a nurse will help with his care while you rest.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Husband) \"Is this the baby blues? Will she go back to normal?\"","r":"\"No, it's a more serious illness called postpartum psychosis. It comes on quickly but responds well to treatment, and most women recover fully. She needs specialist care tonight, and you can help by staying close.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going to any unit. I want to go home with my baby now.\"","r":"\"I understand you want to be home with him. Right now I'm worried about your safety and his. Could you stay tonight while we talk it through with your husband and the specialist team?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"(Husband) \"I should have noticed. I let her go two nights without sleep.\"","r":"\"This illness comes on very fast, often within hours, and you called for help, which was the right thing. What matters now is getting her the right care, and you are part of that.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Full physical screen tonight: obs, FBC, CRP, U&E, calcium, LFT, TFT, glucose, urine drug screen; CT head if focal signs, headache or seizure","Antipsychotic after senior review, e.g. olanzapine 5 mg at night, titrate to 10 mg; check breastfeeding plan with the perinatal pharmacist","For acute agitation or no sleep: lorazepam 0.5–1 mg, maximum 4 mg in 24 hours; watch sedation and baby handling","Protect sleep: night feeds by partner or staff with expressed or formula milk if she agrees"],"psych":["Simple, calm explanation repeated often; she may not retain it while unwell","Perinatal team review within 4 hours; later, mother–infant bonding work on the MBU"],"social":["Husband: written information and Action on Postpartum Psychosis contacts","Ward social worker or MBU team to plan family support and care of the baby","Health visitor and GP told before any discharge"],"safety":["1:1 nursing observation; baby never left alone with her until risk is reviewed","If she tries to leave and lacks capacity, consultant and AMHP for MHA assessment","Midwife, obstetric team and trust safeguarding lead informed tonight","Senior review at the morning handover"]},"facts":[{"h":"Onset is fast and early","x":"Most cases start within two weeks of birth, often within days. Symptoms swing hour to hour, so a calm spell at review is not reassurance."},{"h":"Bipolar disorder is the big flag","x":"Around 1 in 4 women with bipolar disorder become unwell after birth; previous postpartum psychosis carries about a 1 in 2 recurrence risk in later pregnancies."},{"h":"Ask about the baby directly","x":"Ask about thoughts of harm to the baby and to herself in plain words. Asking does not plant the idea and is the core of the risk assessment."},{"h":"Mother and baby together","x":"NICE: a woman needing inpatient care within 12 months of birth should normally go to a Mother and Baby Unit, unless there is a specific reason not to."},{"h":"Exclude organic causes first","x":"Sepsis, pre-eclampsia, thyroid disease, low sodium, venous sinus thrombosis and drug withdrawal can all present as psychosis on the postnatal ward."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Assess suspected postpartum psychosis urgently, decide level of care, and keep mother and baby safe."},"c-2-8-5":{"obj":["Explained honestly that quetiapine may have raised her glucose, without blame","Agreed in writing to continue quetiapine with diabetes clinic monitoring and a postnatal relapse plan","Separated the new mother's intrusive harm thoughts from psychotic or depressive ideas by asking specific questions","Referred the new mother for CBT with exposure and response prevention and discussed an SSRI compatible with breastfeeding","Booked a 2-week review with each woman and told them what would prompt earlier contact"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Nobody told me this tablet could give me diabetes. Why wasn't I warned?\"","r":"\"I'm sorry you weren't told clearly; that's a fair complaint. Quetiapine can raise blood sugar. Let's look at what that means now and what we can do safely for you and the baby.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't I just stop it until the baby's born and restart afterwards?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting to protect the baby. Stopping now carries a real risk of a serious relapse, and the weeks after birth are the riskiest. The diabetes team can treat your sugar while you stay well.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will the diabetes harm my baby?\"","r":"\"Diabetes in pregnancy, when well controlled, usually leads to a healthy baby. The diabetes team will check your sugars, may use diet, metformin or insulin, and will scan the baby's growth.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I picture dropping her down the stairs. I've stopped carrying her up. Am I a monster?\"","r":"\"No. These thoughts are horrible precisely because they go against everything you want. That's typical of OCD, and mothers with these thoughts do not act on them. Avoiding the stairs keeps the fear going; we'll work on that together.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I tell the health visitor, will they take her off me?\"","r":"\"No. Unwanted thoughts that horrify you are not a safeguarding concern on their own. Sharing them helps us get you the right treatment quickly.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"I'm breastfeeding. Can I take anything?\"","r":"\"Yes. Sertraline is one of the most used antidepressants in breastfeeding, and very little reaches the baby. We'd start low and pair it with talking therapy.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue quetiapine at current dose pending consultant or perinatal pharmacist review; do not stop abruptly","Glucose monitoring and treatment by the antenatal diabetes team (diet, then metformin or insulin); weight and lipids at review","New mother: sertraline 50 mg in the morning, review at 4 weeks, increase in 50 mg steps up to 200 mg if needed; warn of nausea, loose stools, early anxiety","Ask about suicidal thoughts at each SSRI review in the first weeks"],"psych":["CBT with exposure and response prevention through perinatal psychology or NHS Talking Therapies (perinatal priority)","Psychoeducation for the new mother and partner about intrusive thoughts and avoidance","Pregnant woman: relapse signature and early-warning signs written into the birth plan"],"social":["Health visitor (with consent): support normal care tasks, not removal","Midwife and obstetrician to have the postpartum relapse plan","GP letter for both women with the agreed plan and prescriber"],"safety":["Pregnant woman: early postnatal review within the first week after delivery","New mother: crisis line and NHS 111 option 2 given; return earlier if thoughts feel right or commanded, or mood drops","No children's social care referral unless assessment shows real risk"]},"facts":[{"h":"Postnatal weeks carry peak relapse risk","x":"Bipolar relapse risk is highest in the first weeks after birth, especially after stopping treatment. Plan cover for this window before delivery."},{"h":"Metabolic risk is managed, not avoided","x":"Diabetes arising in pregnancy on an antipsychotic is treated by the diabetes team. Switching drugs mid-pregnancy exposes the baby to two drugs and risks relapse."},{"h":"OCD thoughts are ego-dystonic","x":"The mother finds the thought repugnant and avoids triggers. Thoughts felt as justified, commanded or calmly held point to psychosis or severe depression instead."},{"h":"Avoidance feeds the thought","x":"Stopping bathing, changing or carrying the baby keeps the anxiety alive. ERP gradually brings these tasks back."},{"h":"Sertraline suits breastfeeding","x":"Low milk transfer and wide use make sertraline a common first choice. Start 50 mg daily; full benefit for OCD may take 8–12 weeks."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Advise on quetiapine with gestational diabetes, then assess the new mother's intrusive thoughts."},"c-2-8-6":{"obj":["Listed the risk and protective features in this pregnancy and judged that together they meet the threshold for referral","Agreed with the trust safeguarding lead and the midwife who sends the referral to children's social care today","Told the woman honestly about the referral and why, and offered continued CMHT support","Offered referrals to the drug and alcohol service and to an IDVA","Recorded the rationale, names, times and any information shared"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So you're reporting me. I knew I couldn't trust you lot.\"","r":"\"I can hear how angry and let down you feel. I'm telling you because I want to be honest with you. The aim is support for you and the baby before birth, and I'm not going anywhere.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"They'll take my baby off me the minute it's born.\"","r":"\"I know that's the fear. Most pre-birth assessments end with a support plan, not removal. Working with them early is the best way to show what you can do.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I only used a bit of coke at a party. It's not a big deal.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Even occasional cocaine in pregnancy can affect the placenta and the baby's growth. Would you meet the drug service so they can help you stop?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He does get rough when he's had a drink. But he's never touched my bump.\"","r":"\"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. You and the baby deserve to be safe. Can I ask a few more questions about what happens, and tell you about someone who supports women in this situation?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"(Midwife) \"I don't want to wreck my relationship with her. Isn't this over the top?\"","r":"\"I understand. But substance use, violence at home and missed care together meet the threshold. Let's agree who refers, and tell her together so she hears it's about help.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why didn't anyone help me before it got this bad?\"","r":"\"That's a fair question and I'm sorry. This referral is how we make sure support is in place now, before the baby arrives.\""}],"bps":{"psych":["Increase CMHT contact to weekly with the care coordinator through to the postnatal period","Continue structured psychological work for EUPD (e.g. DBT skills group) where available"],"social":["Children's social care referral for pre-birth assessment today, by phone then in writing via the MASH or front door","Community drug and alcohol service referral; offer a specialist substance misuse midwife where available","IDVA referral and National Domestic Abuse Helpline 0808 2000 247","Single lead professional agreed with GP, midwife and health visitor; attend any strategy meeting or child protection conference"],"safety":["Trust safeguarding lead informed the same day","DASH risk checklist if she discloses abuse; MARAC if high risk","Crisis plan updated to cover labour and the postnatal ward","Review at the next CMHT appointment, within one week"]},"facts":[{"h":"Diagnosis alone is not a referral","x":"Refer on the combination of risks and their effect on care of the baby: substance use, abuse at home, missed care, unstable mental state, little support."},{"h":"Refer before birth, not at birth","x":"Pre-birth assessment lets support start in pregnancy. Waiting risks an emergency decision on the postnatal ward."},{"h":"Tell her unless it raises risk","x":"Usually explain the referral first. Skip this only if telling her could put the baby, her or someone else at greater risk; record why."},{"h":"Abuse often starts or worsens in pregnancy","x":"Ask about it alone, never with the partner present. A violent partner is a risk to the unborn baby in his own right."},{"h":"Data protection does not block safeguarding","x":"You can share relevant information without consent to protect a child. Share only what is needed, with whom, and record the reason."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Assess risk to the unborn baby and decide on safeguarding referral while keeping her engaged."},"c-2-8-7":{"obj":["Taken a cycle-linked mood history and screened both women for past hypomania and suicidal thoughts","Explained suspected PMDD to the 34-year-old and given her a two-cycle daily symptom diary with a review date","Linked the 46-year-old's mood dip to possible perimenopause and recurrent depression, and agreed an antidepressant review","Written a GP letter covering PMDD treatment options, an HRT discussion and TFT/FBC","Given both women crisis contacts"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"My GP says I'm bipolar. Are you saying he's wrong?\"","r":"\"Your GP was right to take your mood swings seriously. Bipolar episodes usually last days to weeks regardless of your cycle. Yours seem tied to your periods, so let's check that properly with a diary.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"For a week every month I hate everyone, including myself. I've thought about not being here.\"","r":"\"That sounds exhausting, and thank you for telling me. Can we talk about those thoughts: when they come, how strong they get, and what keeps you safe?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do I have to fill in a diary? Can't you just give me something now?\"","r":"\"I understand you want relief quickly. Memory blurs the pattern, and two months of daily ratings make the diagnosis sure and the right treatment clear. If things get worse meanwhile, we'll act sooner.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this the menopause? I'm only 46.\"","r":"\"Irregular periods with night sweats at 46 are common signs of perimenopause. Hormone changes can bring back depression in women who've had it before. We can treat both parts.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"HRT gives you breast cancer, doesn't it? My sister said never take it.\"","r":"\"Many women worry about that. The risk depends on the type and how long it's used, and for most women in their 40s it is small. Your GP can talk you through your own figures.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want more tablets. I'm already on an antidepressant.\"","r":"\"That's fine. CBT helps both low mood and menopausal symptoms, and HRT may ease sleep and sweats. Let's check your current dose is right before adding anything.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["34-year-old, if PMDD confirmed: SSRI via GP, e.g. sertraline 50 mg daily, continuous or luteal phase (day 14 to menses); off-label, warn of nausea and early agitation","Alternative: continuous drospirenone-containing combined pill after GP checks VTE risk","Both: TFT and FBC via GP if not done in the last year","46-year-old: review dose and adherence of current antidepressant; GP to discuss HRT under NICE NG23 (no FSH needed over 45)"],"psych":["CBT for PMDD symptoms (NHS Talking Therapies)","CBT for depression and menopausal symptoms for the 46-year-old via NHS Talking Therapies or CMHT psychology"],"social":["GP letter with options and named prescriber","Workplace: suggest occupational health or reasonable adjustments if work is affected"],"safety":["Ask about suicide in the luteal week specifically","Crisis line and NHS 111 option 2 to both","Review the 34-year-old after two cycles (about 8 weeks); the 46-year-old in 4 weeks","Avoid valproate in any woman who could become pregnant"]},"facts":[{"h":"Two cycles of daily ratings","x":"PMDD needs prospective daily ratings across at least two cycles. Symptoms must ease within days of bleeding and be minimal the following week."},{"h":"Cycle-locked, not episodic","x":"Bipolar episodes ignore the menstrual calendar. Mood that resets every month after the period points away from bipolar disorder."},{"h":"Luteal-phase SSRI works fast","x":"In PMDD, SSRIs act within days, so dosing from mid-cycle to menses is an option. This differs from depression, where benefit takes weeks."},{"h":"No FSH test over 45","x":"NICE NG23: diagnose perimenopause clinically in women over 45 with vasomotor symptoms and irregular periods, no blood test needed."},{"h":"Valproate needs a PPP","x":"MHRA: do not start valproate in anyone of childbearing potential unless the pregnancy prevention programme is in place and no alternative suits."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Assess one woman for PMDD and the other for perimenopausal mood change, and agree plans."},"c-2-8-8":{"obj":["Seen her alone, believed her, and named what she describes as abuse","Completed the DASH risk checklist and identified any high-risk features","Agreed a safe way to contact her and kept letters out of the home without her consent","Offered an IDVA referral and the 24-hour National Domestic Abuse Helpline in a safe format","Discussed the children with the safeguarding lead and recorded the decision and rationale"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"He'll be back in a minute. Please don't say anything in front of him.\"","r":"\"I won't. Everything you've told me stays between us for now. Let's agree a quick safe way to contact you, and I'll see you on your own next time too.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It's not really abuse. He's never hit me. Maybe I am useless.\"","r":"\"Controlling your money, phone and friends, and putting you down, is abuse, even without hitting. It isn't your fault, and it can make depression much worse.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want the police involved. It'll make things worse.\"","r":"\"That's your decision, and I respect it. Police aren't the only option. A domestic abuse adviser can help you plan safely at your own pace. Can I refer you?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will social services take the kids if I tell you more?\"","r":"\"The aim is to support you and keep the children safe with you. If I need to speak to children's services, I'll try to tell you first and explain why.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Where would I even go? He controls all the money.\"","r":"\"You don't have to decide now. The helpline is free and open 24 hours, and refuges and advisers can help with money and housing. Is there a safe place to keep the number?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"The kids hear us arguing. I feel like I'm failing them.\"","r":"\"You're telling me because you care about them, and that matters. Children are affected by living with abuse, so getting support is part of protecting them.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Review antidepressant response; abuse may explain non-response","Ask about sleep, alcohol and self-harm as coping"],"psych":["Trauma-informed follow-up with a consistent clinician, always seen alone","Later: trauma-focused therapy via CMHT psychology once safety allows; never couples therapy"],"social":["IDVA or local domestic abuse service referral with her consent","National Domestic Abuse Helpline 0808 2000 247; refuge and housing options via the IDVA","Trust safeguarding lead consulted about the children aged 7 and 10; refer to children's social care if threshold met"],"safety":["DASH checklist; MARAC referral if 14+ ticks, escalation, or professional judgement","Safe contact details and a restricted record flag; no letters home without checking","999 if in immediate danger; Silent Solution (999 then 55) if she cannot speak","Review in 2 weeks or sooner if risk rises"]},"facts":[{"h":"Coercive control is a crime","x":"Coercive or controlling behaviour in an intimate relationship is an offence in England and Wales, even with no physical violence."},{"h":"14 ticks means MARAC","x":"A DASH score of 14 or more, clear escalation, or your professional judgement triggers a MARAC referral."},{"h":"Children are victims too","x":"The Domestic Abuse Act 2021 recognises children who see, hear or experience the effects of abuse as victims in their own right."},{"h":"See her alone, every time","x":"A partner who always attends and answers for her is a warning sign. Build in time alone routinely so it does not look unusual."},{"h":"Silent 999 call","x":"If she cannot speak on a 999 mobile call, pressing 55 tells the operator she needs police."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Safety plan","do":"Respond to the coercive control disclosure, assess risk, and agree a safety plan with her."},"c-2-9-1":{"obj":["Used her chosen name and pronouns throughout and updated the record with her consent","Assessed her mood and suicide risk, including the effect of waiting and discrimination","Asked which hormones she takes, the doses and the source, and asked the GP for safety bloods","Explained the GP referral route to an NHS adult gender dysphoria clinic and realistic waits","Agreed a crisis plan and a 4-week review"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Are you going to tell me this is just my depression talking?\"","r":"\"No. Your gender identity and your depression are separate things. I'll help with your mood and safety, and support your referral at the same time.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can you just sign me off so the gender clinic takes me?\"","r":"\"There's no sign-off from us. Your GP refers you directly to an NHS adult gender clinic. I'll write to support that and say we're treating your depression alongside.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've been taking estradiol pills from a website for four months.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest; I understand why with such long waits. I won't judge you. Can you show me the packet and dose? I'd like your GP to check some bloods to keep you safe.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"The wait is five years. Sometimes I don't see the point of carrying on.\"","r":"\"That wait sounds crushing, and I'm glad you told me. Can we talk about those thoughts: how often they come and whether you've made any plans?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will my old name be on letters to my GP? My family don't know yet.\"","r":"\"I'll use Sophie and she/her, and I won't share your gender history beyond what's needed without asking you. Let's agree what goes in letters.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Last time a nurse called me 'he' three times.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry. That shouldn't have happened. I'll update your record so it shows your name and pronouns clearly for everyone.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue depression treatment, e.g. sertraline 50 mg in the morning, review at 4 weeks","GP blood monitoring for self-sourced oestrogen: oestradiol, testosterone, prolactin, LFTs, lipids, FBC; check blood pressure","Warn: smoking and immobility raise VTE risk on oestrogen; advise against higher-than-recommended doses or abrupt stopping"],"psych":["CBT for depression via NHS Talking Therapies or CMHT psychology","Explore minority stress and supports without questioning her identity"],"social":["Name and pronoun updated on the record with consent","GP letter requesting referral to an NHS adult gender dysphoria clinic","Signpost to local LGBTQ+ and trans peer support groups"],"safety":["Suicide risk assessed and safety plan written","Crisis line and NHS 111 option 2","Review in 4 weeks; track referral progress"]},"facts":[{"h":"Not a mental disorder","x":"ICD-11 places gender incongruence under conditions related to sexual health. Treat co-existing depression; do not treat identity."},{"h":"GP refers, not psychiatry","x":"Referral to an NHS adult gender dysphoria clinic is usually made by the GP. Mental health problems are treated alongside, never a reason to delay."},{"h":"Oestrogen raises clot risk","x":"Unmonitored oral oestradiol increases VTE risk, more so with smoking. Bloods include oestradiol, testosterone, prolactin and LFTs."},{"h":"Cyproterone needs care","x":"MHRA: cyproterone carries a meningioma risk and liver toxicity. Ask if she sources anti-androgens online too."},{"h":"Gender reassignment is protected","x":"Under the Equality Act 2010 it is a protected characteristic. Getting her name and pronouns right is a basic duty."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Assess gender incongruence respectfully, address mental health and safety, and explain the UK referral pathway."},"c-3-1-1":{"obj":["Checked hearing aids, glasses, first language, schooling and mood before starting, and recorded them","Completed the ACE-III in standard order with delayed recall at the right point, without prompting","Scored each of the five domains and named the pattern (e.g. amnestic) with its caveats","Explained to him that the test alone is not a diagnosis and what happens next","Handed the consultant a 30-second summary with total, domain scores and version used"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I know what day it is. Do you think I'm stupid?\"","r":"\"Not at all. I ask everyone the same questions, some very easy and some hard on purpose. It gives us a fair picture of your memory.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't remember the address. This is embarrassing. I'm going to fail.\"","r":"\"Lots of people find that one hard; it's meant to be. There's no pass or fail. Let's carry on with the next part.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So have I got dementia then?\"","r":"\"I can't tell from this test alone. Your memory for new things found it harder. The consultant will put this together with your history, bloods and scan, and talk it through with you today.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"(Daughter at the door) \"Can I come in? He gets flustered, I can help him answer.\"","r":"\"Thank you, I know you want to help. The test only works if he answers alone. I'd really value your view straight after, as what you've noticed at home is just as important.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"My memory's fine. My daughter's just fussing.\"","r":"\"It's good she cares. Sometimes others notice things before we do. These questions help us see whether there's anything to worry about, or put minds at rest.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will you tell the DVLA? I still drive to my allotment.\"","r":"\"Not from today's test. If a diagnosis that affects driving is made, you'd need to tell the DVLA yourself, and we'd help you with that.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Confirm GP bloods: FBC, U&E, calcium, LFT, TFT, B12, folate, HbA1c or glucose","Structural imaging (CT or MRI) via the memory pathway if not done","Note medicines with anticholinergic load for the consultant"],"psych":["Screen mood (e.g. two depression questions) and anxiety; low mood lowers scores","Feedback with consultant same day; repeat ACE-III in 6–12 months using the same version"],"social":["Collateral history from the daughter in a separate room","Interpreter if English is not his first language; family must not interpret"],"safety":["Ask about driving, getting lost, cooking and money now","Delirium screen (4AT) if attention is poor or onset sudden"]},"facts":[{"h":"ACE-III cut-offs: 88 and 82","x":"Below 88 is suggestive; below 82 is more specific for dementia. The domain profile tells you more than the total."},{"h":"MoCA: 26 is normal","x":"Score out of 30, 26 or above normal; add 1 point if 12 years or less of education."},{"h":"Delayed recall needs a delay","x":"Give the name and address early and test recall only after the intervening tasks. Testing too early inflates the memory score."},{"h":"Record the version","x":"Use the licensed form and note the version (A, B or C). Alternate versions reduce practice effects at repeat testing."},{"h":"No relatives in the room","x":"Prompting invalidates the score. Watch for the patient turning to the relative for answers; it suggests poor insight."}],"type":"Cognitive assessment","do":"Administer and explain the ACE-III or MoCA, interpret scores fairly, and plan next steps."},"c-3-1-2":{"obj":["Established handedness and the time course of her symptoms before testing","Tested neglect, dressing and constructional apraxia, Gerstmann features, visual fields and temporal lobe signs systematically","Named the findings correctly and localised them to the right parietal lobe","Escalated to the medical registrar for urgent CT head and stroke review the same day","Put falls precautions and neglect-aware care into the nursing plan"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"There's nothing wrong with my left side. I'm perfectly fine.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you feel well in yourself. I'd like to check a few things with your hands and eyes, which helps us look after you properly. Is that all right?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do you want me to draw a clock? I'm not a child.\"","r":"\"Fair point; it does seem simple. Drawing tests how different parts of the brain work together. Please do your best.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I keep getting lost on the way to the loo. I'm frightened I'm losing my mind.\"","r":"\"That must be frightening. Something may be affecting how your brain handles space and direction. We're going to find out why, and nurses will help you get around safely.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Son on the phone) \"Is this her dementia getting worse?\"","r":"\"It came on over a few days, which makes me want to rule out a stroke first. We've asked the medical team to see her today and arranged a brain scan. I'll call you with the results.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"(Nurse) \"She's agitated tonight. Can we give her something to settle her?\"","r":"\"Let's hold off sedation until we know the cause. Approach her from the right side, keep her calm and safe, and call me if she becomes distressed or unsafe.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Urgent CT head today; if within 4.5 hours of a clear onset time, stroke team per local protocol","Neurological and cardiovascular check, ECG, glucose, FBC, U&E, CRP, clotting","Avoid antipsychotics for confusion until cause is known"],"psych":["Simple explanation repeated; reduce noise and stimulation","Screen mood and check for anosognosia before judging engagement with care"],"social":["Occupational therapy functional assessment once stable","Update the family and consultant the same day"],"safety":["Falls precautions; bed and call bell on her right side","Serve food on her right and turn the plate half-way through","Physical health escalation via the medical registrar; document timings"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask handedness first","x":"In right-handed people the left hemisphere is almost always dominant. Localising signs as dominant or non-dominant depends on it."},{"h":"Right parietal: neglect and denial","x":"Left neglect, dressing and constructional apraxia, getting lost and denial of the deficit (anosognosia) point to the non-dominant parietal lobe."},{"h":"Gerstmann is dominant parietal","x":"Acalculia, agraphia, finger agnosia and right-left confusion together localise to the dominant (usually left) angular gyrus."},{"h":"Quick neglect tests","x":"Line bisection, star or letter cancellation, clock drawing and visual or tactile extinction take two minutes at the bedside."},{"h":"Days, not years, means stroke","x":"New focal signs over hours or days are a stroke or space-occupying lesion until proven otherwise, even on a psychiatric ward."}],"type":"Cognitive assessment","do":"Examine parietal and temporal lobe function, interpret findings, and decide on urgent next steps."},"c-3-1-3":{"obj":["Confirmed consent and explained to the son why his view matters","Mapped onset, course and change across memory, language, orientation, function and behaviour","Screened for Lewy body, frontal, vascular, delirium and depression features","Identified risks: driving, getting lost, fire, money and scams, self-neglect","Asked how the son is coping and offered a carer's assessment"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have done something sooner. I just thought it was old age.\"","r":"\"Changes like this creep up slowly, and lots of families think the same. You've brought him now, and what you tell me today will help a great deal.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will he know I've spoken to you behind his back?\"","r":"\"He agreed to us speaking, and I'll be open with him that I talked to you. I won't share anything you'd rather keep private without asking you first.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Someone rang pretending to be his bank. He gave them his card details. Lost £3,000.\"","r":"\"That's really worrying, and I'm sorry. Let's get the bank told today and raise a safeguarding concern so he's protected from it happening again.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"He still drives. Taking his keys would kill him.\"","r":"\"I understand how much driving means to him. If he's diagnosed with dementia, he must tell the DVLA. Has anything worried you about his driving, like getting lost or scrapes?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm round there every day. I've got my own family. I'm exhausted.\"","r":"\"That's a lot to carry, and it's understandable to feel worn out. You're able to ask for a carer's assessment, which can bring practical help. Can I refer you?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it Alzheimer's?\"","r":"\"It's too early to say. What you've told me helps a lot. We'll put it together with his tests and scan, and the consultant will explain the result to you both.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GP to review anticholinergic and sedative medicines (e.g. oxybutynin, amitriptyline, benzodiazepines)","Bloods (B12, folate, TFT, calcium, glucose) and imaging if not done"],"psych":["Informant scale such as the IQCODE completed by the son","Screen father for depression at his own assessment"],"social":["Carer's assessment for the son via the local authority; Alzheimer's Society contacts","Fire and rescue Safe and Well visit","Discuss Lasting Power of Attorney (health and finance) while he may still have capacity"],"safety":["Safeguarding adults concern to the local authority today for financial abuse; tell the bank","Driving: ask about incidents; DVLA notification needed if dementia is diagnosed","Document the son's account separately and attributed"]},"facts":[{"h":"The informant sees what tests miss","x":"People with dementia often lack insight. The collateral account of change over time, not today's score, carries the diagnosis."},{"h":"Ask the 'pattern' questions","x":"Hallucinations, fluctuation, falls and acting out dreams suggest Lewy body; personality change with good memory suggests frontal; step-wise drops suggest vascular."},{"h":"Money is a top risk","x":"Scams and financial abuse are common early. A safeguarding concern under the Care Act 2014 brings a local authority enquiry."},{"h":"LPA needs capacity now","x":"Lasting Power of Attorney can only be made while the person has capacity to do so. Raise it early."},{"h":"Carers have a legal right","x":"Under the Care Act 2014, any carer who appears to have support needs is entitled to a local authority carer's assessment."}],"type":"Collateral history","do":"Take a collateral history from the son to assess for dementia and identify risks."},"c-3-2-1":{"obj":["Checked what she and her daughter already knew and how much detail they wanted","Used the word 'dementia' and explained mixed Alzheimer's and vascular disease in plain language","Explained why this is not Lewy body dementia or mild cognitive impairment","Covered treatment, vascular risk control, driving, LPA and post-diagnostic support","Checked her emotional reaction and suicide risk, and booked follow-up with written information"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"Dementia? No. I just forget names. Everyone my age does.\"","r":"\"It's a lot to take in, and some forgetting is normal with age. Your tests show changes beyond that, and they're starting to affect daily life. That's why we call it dementia.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there a cure?\"","r":"\"Not at present. But there are tablets that may help memory for a time, and controlling blood pressure may slow the vascular part. Lots of support can keep you living well.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will I end up not knowing my own daughter?\"","r":"\"That's a natural fear. Dementia usually changes slowly over years, and many people stay at home and know their family for a long time. Planning now helps keep you in charge.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Daughter) \"What's the difference between this and MCI? Mum's friend has that.\"","r":"\"In mild cognitive impairment, memory is affected but daily life mostly isn't. Your mum's memory is now affecting things like shopping and bills, so it's dementia.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I don't want to be a burden. What's the point?\"","r":"\"I'm sorry this is so hard to hear. Can I ask: when you say that, have you had thoughts of wishing you weren't here?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm not giving up my car.\"","r":"\"Many people with early dementia can keep driving for a while. You'll need to tell the DVLA and your insurer, and they decide, sometimes after a driving assessment.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Offer an AChE inhibitor for the Alzheimer's component, e.g. donepezil 5 mg at night, increase to 10 mg after 4 weeks; check pulse first","GP to optimise blood pressure, statin, diabetes and smoking"],"psych":["Cognitive Stimulation Therapy group (NICE NG97)","Post-diagnostic follow-up with memory nurse or dementia adviser"],"social":["Carer's assessment for the daughter via the local authority","Alzheimer's Society and dementia adviser contacts","Discuss Lasting Power of Attorney and advance care planning"],"safety":["Check mood and suicidal thoughts after giving the diagnosis","DVLA and insurer notification explained","Plain-language letter copied to the patient; review in 6–8 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Say 'dementia', then define it","x":"Avoiding the word leaves families confused. Name it, explain it in one sentence, then check what they heard."},{"h":"Mixed dementia is common","x":"In older people Alzheimer's and vascular changes often coexist. Treat the Alzheimer's part with medication and the vascular part with risk control."},{"h":"MCI vs dementia: daily life","x":"The dividing line is function. Measurable decline with daily life mostly intact is MCI; decline that affects independence is dementia."},{"h":"The patient tells the DVLA","x":"A person diagnosed with dementia must tell the DVLA and their insurer. Many can continue driving after review."},{"h":"Watch mood after diagnosis","x":"Suicide risk is higher in the months after a dementia diagnosis, especially early in the illness. Ask before they leave."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis","do":"Explain the dementia diagnosis, how it differs from other types and MCI, and next steps."},"c-3-2-2":{"obj":["Given the wife unhurried time and validated her distress before asking questions","Elicited core bvFTD features: disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy, compulsions, sweet craving, executive change","Asked about language change, motor neurone disease symptoms and family history","Assessed financial, driving, sexual, exploitation and her own safety risks","Agreed an urgent young-onset dementia referral and practical money protection steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I don't recognise him. He laughed when our grandson was in hospital.\"","r":"\"That must have been awful to see. Losing his warmth like that is one of the hardest changes for a family. Can you tell me when you first noticed him becoming different?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"The GP said it's depression. Could it still be that?\"","r":"\"Depression can look similar, so it's a fair thought. But loss of empathy, disinhibition and craving sweets with good memory suggests the front of the brain may be affected. We need a specialist assessment to be sure.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He's lost £20,000 gambling online. I can't stop him.\"","r":"\"That's frightening, and it's not your fault. Let's talk now about protecting your money: the bank can add gambling blocks, and we can look at legal options.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I've been shouting at him. I thought he was doing it on purpose.\"","r":"\"Anyone would react like that to such changes. If this is an illness, he isn't choosing it. Understanding that may help, and you deserve support too.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"His father went strange in his fifties too. Will my children get this?\"","r":"\"Some types run in families. If that's the case here, a genetics clinic can talk your children through it later. First we need to find out what is happening to him.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He grabbed my arm hard last week when I hid the card.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Your safety matters too. Has anything else like that happened, and do you feel safe at home tonight?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Urgent young-onset dementia or memory clinic assessment with MRI brain and neuropsychology","Do not start AChE inhibitors or memantine for FTD (NICE NG97)","GP to review the depression diagnosis and current medicines","Neurology referral if weakness, wasting, fasciculations or swallowing problems"],"psych":["Psychoeducation for the wife about bvFTD behaviours and triggers","Behavioural strategies via the older adults or young-onset team"],"social":["Bank gambling blocks and joint account review; look into LPA if he still has capacity, otherwise Court of Protection deputyship","Carer's assessment via the local authority; Rare Dementia Support","Genetics referral if family history suggests inherited FTD"],"safety":["Ask about her safety and any aggression; domestic abuse support if needed","Driving: advise he should not drive until assessed; DVLA notification if diagnosed","Safeguarding adults referral if he is being exploited financially or sexually","Consultant informed today; review within 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"bvFTD spares memory early","x":"Disinhibition, apathy, lost empathy, compulsions and sweet craving appear while memory and orientation look fine. Short cognitive screens can be normal."},{"h":"Often mislabelled as depression","x":"Onset is usually before 65. Apathy looks like low mood, but he lacks sadness and concern. The collateral history is the key test."},{"h":"Ask about motor neurone disease","x":"A minority with bvFTD develop MND. Ask about weakness, muscle twitching, choking and slurred speech."},{"h":"No AChE inhibitors in FTD","x":"NICE NG97: do not offer donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine or memantine for frontotemporal dementia; they can worsen behaviour."},{"h":"Protect money early","x":"Banks can block gambling transactions. LPA is only possible while he has capacity; otherwise the Court of Protection appoints a deputy."}],"type":"Collateral history","do":"Take a frontotemporal dementia collateral history from his wife, assess risk, and offer carer support."},"c-3-2-3":{"obj":["Asked what he and his wife hope the medication will do and corrected any belief that it cures","Explained the benefits and common side effects of AChE inhibitors and when memantine is used","Identified syncope with bisoprolol as a cardiac risk and requested pulse and ECG before starting","Reviewed his medication list for anticholinergic drugs","Agreed a start date, titration plan, 4–6 week review and shared care with the GP"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will these tablets bring my memory back?\"","r":"\"I wish they could. They don't cure Alzheimer's, but in some people they help memory and daily life for a while and slow the decline. It's worth a try if it's safe for your heart.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"(Wife) \"He fainted in the kitchen in March. Could these make that happen again?\"","r":"\"That's an important question. These tablets can slow the heart, especially with bisoprolol. That's why we'll check his pulse and heart tracing first and start low.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"When should I take it, morning or night?\"","r":"\"Donepezil is usually taken at night so you sleep through any sickness. If it gives you vivid dreams or poor sleep, you can switch to the morning.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want more pills. I already take six.\"","r":"\"That's understandable. It's your choice. We can also check whether some of your current tablets can be cut down, and you can try this one and stop if it doesn't suit you.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"(Wife) \"Can we just get the strongest dose straight away?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting the most benefit. Starting low for four weeks lets the body adjust and reduces sickness and heart effects. Then we increase.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What happens when it stops working?\"","r":"\"We don't stop it simply because the illness moves on. Later, a second medicine called memantine can be added or used instead.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Pulse and 12-lead ECG via GP before starting; cardiology advice if HR < 60, heart block or recurrent syncope","Then donepezil 5 mg at night for 4 weeks, increase to 10 mg at night if tolerated","Warn: nausea, diarrhoea, poor appetite, vivid dreams, cramps; stop and seek advice if fainting or dizziness","Stop or reduce anticholinergics (e.g. oxybutynin) with the GP","Memantine later: 5 mg daily, up by 5 mg weekly to 20 mg; 10 mg if eGFR 30–49"],"psych":["Written information sheet; wife keeps a side-effect and function diary","Agree realistic goals with the couple (e.g. keeping up hobbies, daily routine) to judge benefit at review"],"social":["Shared care with the GP once stable","Memory nurse follow-up for pulse, weight and side effects"],"safety":["Review at 4–6 weeks: pulse, weight, side effects, falls","Advise to report blackouts, slow pulse or black stools immediately"]},"facts":[{"h":"Check the pulse before starting","x":"AChE inhibitors are vagotonic. Unexplained syncope, HR < 60 or heart block need an ECG and cardiac review first, especially with beta-blockers."},{"h":"Donepezil goes at night","x":"Start 5 mg at bedtime, increase to 10 mg after 4 weeks. Vivid dreams or insomnia: move it to the morning."},{"h":"Rivastigmine patch option","x":"Start 4.6 mg/24 h, increase to 9.5 mg/24 h after at least 4 weeks. Fewer GI effects than capsules; rotate the site to avoid skin irritation."},{"h":"Memantine: renal check","x":"Titrate 5 mg weekly to 20 mg once daily. Reduce to 10 mg daily if eGFR is 30–49. Side effects: dizziness, headache, constipation."},{"h":"Don't stop for progression","x":"NICE NG97: do not stop an AChE inhibitor just because the disease is more severe. Add memantine in moderate to severe disease."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Discuss cholinesterase inhibitors and memantine, including benefits, side effects and ECG checks, and agree a plan."},"c-3-2-4":{"obj":["Told the care staff clearly not to give haloperidol and explained why","Listed delirium checks for staff: obs, pain, urine, constipation, retention, new medicines","Given three practical non-drug strategies for the hallucinations and night-time shouting","Arranged next-working-day specialist review and a drug sensitivity alert on all records","Described a parkinsonism check (bradykinesia, rigidity, tremor, gait, postural BP) for the review"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"(Care staff) \"The GP said haloperidol. Why won't you just approve it?\"","r":"\"I understand you need help tonight. In Lewy body dementia, haloperidol can cause severe stiffness, falls and even death. Let's look for a cause and calm her another way; call back if that doesn't work.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"(Care staff) \"She's screaming, the other residents are awake, and we're two staff short.\"","r":"\"That sounds really hard. Could one person sit with her in a lit, quiet room? Check for pain, a full bladder or constipation. If she's unwell or unsafe, call 111 or 999.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"(Patient) \"There are children in the garden. They'll fall in the pond!\"","r":"\"That sounds worrying. You're safe, and the garden is quiet now. Let's sit together in here with the light on.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Daughter) \"She hates being stiff and falling. Can she have Parkinson's tablets?\"","r":"\"They can help movement, but they often make hallucinations worse. We'll ask the Parkinson's or neurology team to balance this carefully, starting low.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"(Daughter) \"Why did nobody tell the GP she can't have these drugs?\"","r":"\"You're right to be upset. We'll put a clear sensitivity alert on her GP, care home and hospital records today so it doesn't happen again.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Care staff) \"Should we tell her the children aren't real?\"","r":"\"Arguing usually upsets her more. If she isn't distressed, gently reassure and distract. If she is frightened, comfort her, turn the lights on and stay with her.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No haloperidol or other typical antipsychotics; sensitivity alert on all records","Delirium screen by out-of-hours GP: obs, urine dip, bloods, bowels, bladder scan, medicine review","AChE inhibitor for DLB symptoms (NICE NG97): donepezil 5 mg at night, increase to 10 mg after 4 weeks, or rivastigmine; check pulse first","Lying and standing BP: drop of 20 systolic or 10 diastolic within 3 minutes indicates postural hypotension"],"psych":["Non-drug measures: good lighting at dusk, reduce shadows and clutter, calm reassurance, consistent staff","Hallucinations that do not distress her need reassurance only"],"social":["Care home liaison or older adults CMHT review next working day","Update the daughter and agree a care plan with the home"],"safety":["Falls risk plan: night-time checks, low bed or sensor mat","Escalate via 111 or 999 if physically unwell","Parkinsonism and falls review at the next visit; Parkinson's or neurology input if severe"]},"facts":[{"h":"Haloperidol can kill in DLB","x":"Severe antipsychotic sensitivity causes rigidity, falls, confusion and NMS-like reactions. Many patients react badly; avoid typical antipsychotics completely."},{"h":"AChE inhibitors treat hallucinations","x":"NICE NG97 recommends donepezil or rivastigmine in mild to moderate DLB. They often reduce hallucinations and fluctuation."},{"h":"Check for delirium first","x":"A sudden worsening of hallucinations or night-time agitation often means infection, constipation, urinary retention, pain or a new drug."},{"h":"Parkinsonism check in 2 minutes","x":"Finger taps for slowing and decrement, wrist rigidity with activation, rest tremor, gait and turning, pull test, and lying and standing BP."},{"h":"DaTscan settles doubt","x":"If DLB is uncertain, a dopamine transporter scan (123I-FP-CIT SPECT) can support the diagnosis (NICE NG97)."}],"type":"Management plan + Physical examination","do":"Advise safe DLB symptom management, examine for parkinsonism, and explain antipsychotic sensitivity."},"c-3-3-1":{"obj":["Scored a bedside 4AT and recorded the total with the time","Handed the surgical FY1 a named cause search: bladder scan, bowel chart, sodium, calcium, CRP, cultures, oxygen saturations and an opioid review","Put four non-drug measures in place: glasses and hearing aids in, clock and good lighting, one consistent nurse, wife invited to sit with him","Decided for or against haloperidol and, if used, written the dose, 24-hour ceiling, ECG check and stop date","Recorded that he lacks capacity for treatment decisions tonight and that care is given in his best interests using the MCA"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Get off me! You lot are keeping me prisoner in here.\"","r":"\"You sound frightened, and I would be too waking up somewhere strange. You're in hospital after your hip operation. It's the middle of the night. Your wife is coming back in the morning.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"FY1: \"Can you just section him? He's wrecking the bay.\"","r":"\"I know you're stretched. The Mental Health Act won't help here: this is delirium, so we treat under the Mental Capacity Act. What he needs most tonight is a cause. Could you scan his bladder and check his sodium?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"Wife: \"He was fine last week. Has the operation given him dementia?\"","r":"\"That's a frightening change to see. This looks like delirium, a sudden confusion from the strain of surgery, pain or infection. It usually settles as those are treated. Nobody should call it dementia until he has recovered.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Nurse: \"Can we give him lorazepam so the bay gets some sleep?\"","r":"\"It's been a hard night for you all. Lorazepam usually makes delirium worse and raises his fall risk. Let's try a side room, low light and one nurse with him first; I'll write haloperidol as a last resort.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not having another needle in my arm.\"","r":"\"Fair enough, you've had plenty of needles. We'll leave it for now. Can I feel your tummy and see if you need a wee? Sometimes that's what makes people feel so uncomfortable.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"There are men in the corner watching me.\"","r":"\"That sounds scary. I can't see anyone there, but I believe you're seeing them. Being unwell can play tricks on the eyes. Let's turn the light up so you can see the room properly.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Surgical team tonight: bladder scan, bowel chart, U&E, calcium, glucose, FBC, CRP, chest and wound check; cultures if febrile","Drug chart: regular paracetamol 1 g four times daily, lowest effective opioid, stop anticholinergics (oxybutynin, cyclizine, amitriptyline)","Only if still distressed or dangerous after de-escalation, and no Parkinson's or Lewy body disease: haloperidol 0.5 mg orally, may repeat after 2 hours, max 5 mg in 24 hours; ECG for QTc; review daily, stop within a week","If heavy drinking: chlordiazepoxide by symptom-triggered scale plus IV Pabrinex before any glucose"],"psych":["Reorient at every contact: name, place, time; clock and calendar in view","Same nurse where possible; explain each task before touching him","4AT daily; once clear, liaison team repeats cognitive testing (ACE-III or MoCA) before any dementia label"],"social":["Wife invited to visit or stay at night; she brings glasses, hearing aids and family photos","Nurse completes the 'This is me' leaflet with his wife","Discharge summary to GP: delirium episode, higher future risk, recheck cognition at about 3 months"],"safety":["Enhanced observation (1:1 or bay nurse) instead of bed rails or restraint; bed at lowest height, crash mat","Nurse in charge and surgical registrar told tonight; falls risk on handover","MCA best interests note for any restraint, cannula or medicine given against his wishes","Surgical review before the 09:00 ward round; liaison psychiatry follow-up the same day"]},"facts":[{"h":"4AT needs no training","x":"Four items: alertness, AMT4, months backwards, acute change or fluctuation. 4 or more means likely delirium. Works in drowsy patients who can't do other tests, and takes under two minutes."},{"h":"Quiet delirium is the common one","x":"Hypoactive delirium (sleepy, withdrawn, not eating) outnumbers the agitated kind and has a worse outlook. Ask the nurses who else in the bay has 'gone quiet'."},{"h":"Retention looks like psychosis","x":"Opioids, constipation and anticholinergics cause painful retention that presents as agitation. A two-minute bladder scan can end the crisis."},{"h":"No haloperidol in Lewy body or Parkinson's","x":"Severe, sometimes fatal antipsychotic sensitivity: rigidity, falls, collapse. If sedation is unavoidable, small-dose lorazepam on senior advice is the safer choice."},{"h":"Benzodiazepines only for withdrawal","x":"Outside alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal they prolong delirium, cause falls and depress breathing, especially alongside opioids."},{"h":"Delirium flags future dementia","x":"An episode raises later dementia risk. Ask the GP to recheck cognition about three months after discharge."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Screen with the 4AT, search for delirium causes, set nursing measures, and decide on medication."},"c-3-3-2":{"obj":["Fixed the onset to a date or event and recorded her pre-admission baseline: memory, self-care, money, driving, getting about","Asked the daughter about fluctuation, drowsiness, hallucinations, falls, alcohol and new medicines","Explained delirium in three plain sentences and why dementia cannot be diagnosed today","Asked about any Lasting Power of Attorney, who lives with her and how the daughter herself is coping","Fed back to the medical consultant with a clear plan: treat the infection, cut anticholinergics, recheck cognition after recovery"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"She was doing her own shopping last week. Now she doesn't know me. Has she gone mad?\"","r":"\"That must have been a real shock. She hasn't gone mad. This looks like delirium: the infection is upsetting how her brain works. It usually improves as the infection clears, though it can take some weeks.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So is it dementia or not? The ward says she can't go home.\"","r":"\"I understand you want a straight answer. Right now the infection clouds everything, so a memory test would be unreliable. Once she's recovered we'll test again; that's when we can say whether there's dementia as well.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Honestly, she's been forgetting things for about a year. I didn't want to say.\"","r":"\"Thank you, that's really useful, and you were right to tell me. It doesn't change today's treatment, but it means we should check her memory properly once she's well. What did you notice?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have noticed sooner. I only ring her on Sundays.\"","r":"\"Delirium comes on over hours to days; most families couldn't have spotted it earlier. You're here now, and what you're telling me is exactly what the team needs.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why are you asking me all this? Just ask her.\"","r":"\"I will speak to her too. At the moment her memory comes and goes, so you're the best person to tell us what she's normally like. That's how we'll know when she's back to herself.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is she going to end up in a home?\"","r":"\"That's a big worry. It's far too early to decide; most people improve once the infection is treated. Any decision about where she lives comes later, with her involved.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Medical team continue antibiotics guided by culture; recheck U&E, sodium and CRP at 48 hours","Stop or avoid anticholinergics (oxybutynin, chlorphenamine, amitriptyline); total the anticholinergic burden score","Bowels, fluids and pain charted daily","Before discharge: repeat 4AT; if negative, ACE-III or MoCA by the liaison team"],"psych":["Daughter completes the 16-item IQCODE to record change over the past 10 years","Nurses reorient daily; daughter visits at a set time each day"],"social":["OT functional assessment (kitchen, washing, stairs) before discharge planning","Daughter offered a carer's assessment by the local authority under the Care Act 2014","Copy of any property and financial or health and welfare LPA filed in the notes"],"safety":["Ask about falls, gas left on, getting lost and scams before admission; raise safeguarding if neglect or exploitation is suspected","If she drives: advise no driving until cognition is reassessed","GP letter: delirium episode; memory service referral if cognition is still impaired 6 to 12 weeks after recovery"]},"facts":[{"h":"Onset date is the key question","x":"Hours to days points to delirium; months to years points to dementia. Anchor it: 'Was she like this at Christmas? On her birthday?'"},{"h":"IQCODE captures the baseline","x":"Relative rates change over the past 10 years; the 16-item version takes five minutes and isn't distorted by her current confusion."},{"h":"Delirium can last weeks","x":"In older adults attention and memory may take weeks to months to recover. Testing on the ward often overcalls dementia; repeat once well."},{"h":"Listening is not a breach","x":"Hearing a relative's account discloses nothing. If she lacks capacity to consent, sharing what is needed in her best interests is lawful."},{"h":"Dipsticks mislead over 65","x":"Bacteria in the urine are common without infection in older women. If she isn't improving at 48 hours, look for another cause."}],"type":"Collateral history","do":"Take a collateral history from the daughter to separate delirium from dementia, then explain the plan."},"c-3-4-1":{"obj":["Got an ABC account of the last three incidents: time, task, which carer, trigger, what calmed her","Asked the GP to check pain, constipation, chest, urine symptoms, new drugs and delirium within 48 hours","Given the manager three non-drug changes to start today","Explained antipsychotic risks in one sentence and named the conditions under which one would be used","Checked whether the purse has been found and that the theft claim is logged and looked into"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Manager: \"We need something to calm her down, or we'll have to give notice.\"","r":"\"I hear your staff are under real strain, and I want to keep her placement. Calming drugs carry stroke and death risks in dementia, so first let's find what's upsetting her. I'll get the GP in and we'll visit this week.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Someone's been in my handbag. You're all thieves!\"","r":"\"Losing your purse is horrible, and anyone would be cross. Let's look for it together. Where do you usually keep it safe?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't need washing. I had a bath this morning.\"","r":"\"Okay, you feel fresh already. How about just your hands and face now, and a proper bath later with the music you like?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Daughter: \"Why can't she just have a tablet? Isn't that what they're for?\"","r":"\"Fair question. Calming tablets can help short term, but in dementia they raise the risk of stroke, falls and death. We use them only if distress is severe and other steps have failed, and we review within six weeks.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"Carer: \"She only hits out when the night agency staff do her care.\"","r":"\"That's really helpful. It sounds like unfamiliar faces frighten her. Could regular staff she knows do her care, at the time she prefers? Let's write that into her care plan.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I want to go home. My mother's waiting for me.\"","r":"\"You're thinking about your mum. Tell me about her. What did you two use to do together?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GP review within 48 hours: abdomen for constipation, chest, teeth, ears, skin and pressure areas; urine only if urinary symptoms","Pain trial: regular paracetamol 1 g four times daily for 2 weeks (lower dose if under 50 kg); rescore pain before and after","Medication review: cut anticholinergics and sedatives; check anything started recently","Only if severe risk or distress persists: risperidone 250 micrograms twice daily, up to 500 micrograms twice daily (max 1 mg twice daily); stop or review by 6 weeks; ECG if cardiac history"],"psych":["Care home staff keep ABC charts for 2 weeks; CMHT nurse reviews the patterns","Person-centred care plan from her life history ('This is me'); music or reminiscence at care times","Pain scored with PAINAD or Abbey at each care episode"],"social":["Care home liaison team visits within 1 week and coaches staff on approach to personal care","Daughter involved in the care plan and any antipsychotic decision","Check the DoLS authorisation is in place and in date"],"safety":["Home logs the theft claim, searches her room and tells the family; safeguarding concern if money is really missing","Staff plan: back off and return later; two familiar carers for personal care if needed","CMHT revisit in 2 weeks; any antipsychotic reviewed by 6 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Risperidone: 6 weeks, Alzheimer's only","x":"The only UK-licensed antipsychotic for persistent aggression in moderate to severe Alzheimer's, for up to 6 weeks. Start 250 micrograms twice daily."},{"h":"Antipsychotics raise stroke risk","x":"About threefold stroke risk and a higher death rate in dementia. Tell the family plainly and record target symptoms so you know if it works."},{"h":"Pain drives aggression","x":"People with dementia under-report pain. A fixed two-week paracetamol trial can cut agitation; score pain before and after."},{"h":"Theft claims may be true","x":"Misplacing things does fuel accusations in Alzheimer's, but residents are also robbed. Check before calling it a symptom."},{"h":"Personal care is the flashpoint","x":"Most hitting out happens during washing and dressing. A known face, slower pace and explaining each step reduce it."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Assess the care home behaviour by phone, look for causes, and advise non-drug management first."},"c-3-4-2":{"obj":["Asked him directly where he was going and why","Listed his likely triggers: toilet, pain, boredom, evening restlessness, old work routines","Agreed three least restrictive safety measures (door sensor, GPS device, Herbert Protocol form), with a best interests record for anything he cannot consent to","Referred his wife for a carer's assessment and respite","Explained to the son in one sentence why the Mental Health Act does not apply"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I don't wander. I was going to work. They need me at the depot.\"","r":"\"It sounds like work mattered a lot to you. What did you do there? Could we find a safer way for you to get out and about in the daytime?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"Wife: \"I haven't slept properly in weeks. I lie there listening for the door.\"","r":"\"That sounds utterly exhausting, and you can't keep going like this. Let's get a door alarm so you can sleep, and I'll ask social care to assess you for help and a break.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Son: \"Can't you just section him? At least he'd be safe.\"","r":"\"I can see you're scared for him. Sectioning is for mental illness needing hospital treatment; it wouldn't stop the walking and would take him from home. Alarms, a tracker and daytime activity are safer and keep him with your mum.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Wife: \"Can I lock the front door at night?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd want to. Locking him in is a fire risk and may unlawfully restrict him. A sensor that wakes you when the door opens is safer. Let's ask about one today.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"Wife: \"I feel terrible even thinking about a care home.\"","r":"\"That's a natural feeling, and it doesn't mean you love him any less. Let's get you proper support now; if more care is needed later, we'll plan it together.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not wearing a tag like a criminal.\"","r":"\"Understandable; nobody wants to feel tracked. It's a small device, like a watch, so your wife knows you're safe and you can keep going for walks. Would you try it for a month?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GP review: urine and prostate symptoms, pain, constipation, sleep-disrupting drugs; give any diuretic in the morning","Vascular risk control with the GP: blood pressure, statin, antiplatelet as indicated","No sedatives or antipsychotics for the walking itself"],"psych":["Daytime structure: accompanied walk daily, day centre 2 to 3 days a week","Evening routine: toilet before bed, night light on the route, picture sign on the bathroom door","Build his old routines (work hours, dog walk) into the day"],"social":["Adult social care: care needs assessment for him, carer's assessment for his wife (Care Act 2014)","Telecare via the local authority: door and bed sensors linked to a pager for his wife","Planned respite booked; Alzheimer's Society dementia adviser","Wife completes the Herbert Protocol form with a recent photo"],"safety":["Best interests decision recorded for a GPS device if he lacks capacity to agree","If still driving: advise stop and notify the DVLA","Review in 4 weeks, sooner if he leaves at night again or his wife's health worsens"]},"facts":[{"h":"Herbert Protocol saves search time","x":"Family keep a form with photo, routines, past addresses and favourite places, and hand it to police the moment he goes missing."},{"h":"Walking usually has a purpose","x":"Looking for the toilet, an old workplace or home, escaping noise, pain or boredom. Find the purpose and the walking often eases."},{"h":"Locking in can be unlawful","x":"Locking in someone who can't consent may be a deprivation of liberty and is a fire risk. Sensors and alerts are less restrictive."},{"h":"Carer breakdown ends home care","x":"Exhausted carers are a leading reason people move into care homes. A carer's assessment is a legal right under the Care Act 2014."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Assess wandering risk, causes and carer needs, and agree a least restrictive safety plan."},"c-3-4-3":{"obj":["Been invited in, or agreed a date to come back, without arguing about the clutter","Rated the clutter (Clutter Image Rating) and checked exits, cooker, heaters, smoking and a working smoke alarm","Screened for depression, bereavement, dementia, psychosis and OCD","Recorded a decision-specific capacity judgement about clearing the hallway","Agreed one concrete first step with a date: a clear path to the front door and a fire service visit"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"You're not coming in. Everyone wants to throw my things out.\"","r":"\"I'm not here to take anything, I promise. It makes sense to protect your things. I'd just like to understand how life is for you. Could I come in for ten minutes, or come back another day?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"The council sent you, didn't they? They want me out of this flat.\"","r":"\"I can see why you'd be suspicious. I'm from the health team, not housing. My job is to make sure you're well and safe at home. Keeping you in your home is what I want too.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What's wrong with keeping things? They might come in useful.\"","r":"\"Lots of people find it hard to let things go. My only worry is whether you could get out quickly if there were a fire. Can we look at the hallway together?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"If I throw it away, it's like losing a bit of him.\"","r":"\"It sounds like these things keep you close to someone you've lost. We won't rush anything. Would you tell me about him?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll clear the hall if you leave the rest of the flat alone.\"","r":"\"That's a really good start, thank you. Let's agree the hallway by next Friday, and the fire service will fit smoke alarms. We'll talk about the rest when you're ready.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to put me in a home?\"","r":"\"No. The aim is the opposite: helping you stay in your own home safely. That's why the fire safety matters so much.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GP review: weight, nutrition, alcohol, skin and feet; bloods (FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, B12, folate, glucose)","If depressed: sertraline 50 mg in the morning; sodium at 2 to 4 weeks; review at 2 weeks","Memory testing (ACE-III) once trust is built"],"psych":["CBT for hoarding (sorting and discarding practice in the home, around 20 to 26 sessions) via the psychology team where available","Motivational approach: same worker, regular visits, her goals first","Screen for OCD, ADHD and autistic traits, and unresolved grief"],"social":["Fire and rescue safe and well visit: smoke alarms, fire-retardant bedding, escape plan","Multi-agency hoarding or self-neglect panel: social worker, housing officer, fire service, CMHT","Social worker as lead professional; Age UK befriending for isolation","Environmental health only if pests or a risk to neighbours"],"safety":["Clear exit route agreed by a set date; cooker and heaters checked","Section 42 self-neglect enquiry if risk is serious and she cannot protect herself","Share with housing and fire service with consent, or without it if life is at risk","Same CMHT nurse reviews in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Clutter Image Rating sets the bar","x":"Nine photos per room from clear to packed. 4 or above means clutter is disrupting life and needs action; reuse it to track change."},{"h":"Self-neglect counts under the Care Act","x":"Hoarding with self-neglect can trigger a Section 42 enquiry, but her wishes still lead the plan (Making Safeguarding Personal)."},{"h":"Forced clearance backfires","x":"Clearing without consent causes great distress and the clutter usually returns within months. Small agreed steps last."},{"h":"Fire is the main killer","x":"Hoarded homes burn faster and trap people. Ask about smoking, candles, heaters, the cooker and the smoke alarm at every visit."},{"h":"Unwise is not incapable","x":"She may understand the fire risk and still choose to keep things. Judge capacity for the specific decision, never from the mess."}],"type":"Capacity assessment + Risk assessment","do":"Engage her, assess hoarding causes, capacity and risk, and agree a multi-agency plan."},"c-3-5-1":{"obj":["Asked directly about suicidal thoughts, plans, method, rehearsal and final acts (giving things away, will, letters)","Checked access to means: stockpiled tablets, firearms or shotgun certificate, car, ropes","Written a safety plan with him: warning signs, two coping steps, two named contacts, Samaritans and NHS 111 option 2","Started a named antidepressant with a sodium check date and a review within 1 week","Spoken to his daughter with his agreement and given her three specific tasks"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not depressed, doctor. I'm just old. This is what happens.\"","r":"\"Getting older brings real losses, and you've had big ones. But losing sleep, weight and hope isn't just age. It sounds like depression, and that's treatable at any age.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've sorted my affairs. The kids won't have anything to deal with.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest. When people put things in order like that, sometimes they're planning to die. Have you been thinking about ending your life?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What's the point of treatment? I'm 78 and I've got cancer.\"","r":"\"I can hear how hopeless things feel. Depression makes the future look darker than it is. Treatment works as well at 78 as at 40, and feeling better can make the time you have worth having.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I couldn't save her. I should have seen she was ill.\"","r":"\"That's a heavy thing to carry for two years. Grief often brings guilt like that. It sounds like you loved her very much. I'd like to help with this, not just the sleep.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Don't tell my daughter. She's got enough on her plate.\"","r":"\"I respect that you want to protect her. She's already worried and rang us. Sharing a little helps keep you safe. What would you be comfortable with me telling her?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"Daughter: \"Should I take his tablets away? I don't want him to feel like a child.\"","r":"\"That's a thoughtful worry. You could offer to take spare tablets back to the pharmacy together. Most people accept it, and it makes a real difference to safety.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg in the morning (range 50 to 200 mg); sodium at baseline and at 2 to 4 weeks; add a PPI if on aspirin or an NSAID","Or mirtazapine 15 mg at night if poor sleep and weight loss dominate, up to 30 mg after 2 weeks; warn about sedation and falls","1 to 2 weeks' supply per prescription while risk is raised","GP bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, TSH, calcium, B12, folate, glucose","If psychotic, refusing food or fluids, or not responding: discuss ECT with the consultant"],"psych":["Behavioural activation or CBT, 16 to 20 sessions, via NHS Talking Therapies or CMHT psychology","Bereavement support (Cruse Bereavement Support)","Problem-solving the loss of driving: transport, getting out, contact with friends"],"social":["Daughter: daily contact for 2 weeks, return unused medicines to the pharmacy","Age UK befriending or lunch club; community transport and bus pass","Older adult CMHT allocates a care coordinator"],"safety":["Discuss with the consultant today; crisis and home treatment team if risk is high tonight","Written safety plan with Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2 and the CMHT duty number","Ask about firearms; tell police firearms licensing if there is a concern","Review within 1 week; GP letter within 24 hours"]},"facts":[{"h":"Final acts are a red flag","x":"Giving away tools, making a will, writing letters or saying goodbyes suggest planning. Ask about each one directly."},{"h":"No risk scores","x":"NICE NG225 advises against using risk scales to predict suicide or decide who gets care. Plan around needs and what is happening now."},{"h":"SSRIs lower sodium in older adults","x":"Check sodium before and 2 to 4 weeks after starting. New confusion, falls or nausea in the first month: recheck at once."},{"h":"Late-life attempts are more lethal","x":"Older people plan more, warn less and use deadlier methods. Treat passive death wishes seriously, especially in bereaved, ill men living alone."},{"h":"Small supplies save lives","x":"Prescribe 1 to 2 weeks at a time while risk is raised. Sertraline and mirtazapine are far safer in overdose than tricyclics."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Safety plan","do":"Take a late-life depression history, assess suicide risk carefully, and agree an immediate safety plan."},"c-3-5-2":{"obj":["Confirmed mania (sleep, speech, grandiosity, spending, duration) and asked about past depressions and family history","Requested the organic screen tonight: bloods, urine, ECG, CT head and a drug review including steroids and levodopa","Done a bedside neurological check and a brief cognitive screen","Decided the setting and legal route (informal, MCA, or MHA assessment via an AMHP) and told the on-call consultant","Agreed with her husband how to protect her money tonight"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I've never felt better! Why does everyone want me in hospital?\"","r":"\"I can see you're full of energy, and it's good you feel well. But three nights without sleep is a strain on the body at any age. I'd like to check nothing physical is causing it. Will you let us?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"My money is my business. I can spend what I like.\"","r":"\"It is your money, and you have every right to it. Your husband is worried because this is so unlike you. Could we pause big purchases for a few days while we check you're well?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Husband: \"She's never been like this. Is she losing her mind?\"","r":"\"This is frightening to see. It looks like mania, and when it starts after 70 there's often a physical cause, like an infection, a medicine or a change in the brain. That's why we're scanning her and doing tests first.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"A&E doctor: \"She's obviously manic. Just take her to the psych ward.\"","r":"\"I agree she looks manic. A first episode at 71 needs an organic workup first. Can we get bloods, urine, an ECG and a CT head tonight? I'll sort the right bed once they're back.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not having a scan. There's nothing wrong with my head.\"","r":"\"You know yourself best. The scan is quick and painless, and it rules out anything we'd hate to miss. If it's clear, that's one less worry. Can we do it tonight?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"Husband: \"The GP started her on steroids for her chest a fortnight ago.\"","r":"\"That's really important, thank you. Steroids can trigger mania. I'll ask the prescribing team about reducing them safely; they mustn't just stop suddenly.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Tonight: FBC, U&E, LFT, calcium, glucose, TSH, B12, folate, CRP, urine culture, ECG, CT head; syphilis and HIV serology if indicated","Steroids: prescriber tapers if possible; never stop abruptly","Antimanic: olanzapine 2.5 to 5 mg at night, or quetiapine 25 mg daily rising by 25 to 50 mg a day; ECG, glucose, lipids, weight; watch postural BP and falls","Sleep: lorazepam 0.5 mg at night if needed, for up to 1 week","If lithium later: eGFR, TFT, calcium and ECG first; aim 0.4 to 0.7 mmol/L in older adults, level 12 hours post-dose weekly until stable"],"psych":["Low-stimulus side room; one consistent nurse","Once settled: early warning signs and relapse plan with her and her husband"],"social":["Husband contacts the bank to pause cards and raise fraud flags; cancel recent orders where possible","Older adult ward bed via the bed manager; on-call consultant informed"],"safety":["Capacity assessment for admission: informal if she has capacity and agrees; AMHP for MHA assessment if she refuses and risk is high","Observation level set on arrival; falls, dehydration, sexual and financial exploitation","Advise no driving and tell her she must notify the DVLA","No discharge from A&E tonight"]},"facts":[{"h":"First mania after 50: think brain","x":"Late-onset mania is often secondary: stroke, tumour, frontotemporal dementia, thyroid disease, infection or drugs. Brain imaging is not optional."},{"h":"Know the drug culprits","x":"Corticosteroids, levodopa and dopamine agonists, antidepressants and excess thyroxine. Check what the GP started recently."},{"h":"Start at a quarter to half the dose","x":"Older adults clear drugs slowly and fall easily. Start antipsychotics well below the adult dose and titrate slowly."},{"h":"Lithium hates NSAIDs and diuretics","x":"NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, ARBs and thiazides raise levels. Older adults can be toxic at 'normal' levels: tremor, confusion, unsteadiness, vomiting."},{"h":"Mania must go to the DVLA","x":"She must stop driving and tell the DVLA. Sleeplessness and poor judgement make driving unsafe now."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Assess late-onset mania, exclude organic causes, and decide on safe treatment and setting."},"c-3-5-3":{"obj":["Adapted for her deafness: pocket amplifier or written prompts, facing her in good light, checking she heard","Mapped the belief without arguing: content, conviction, evidence and what she has done about it","Screened for delirium, dementia (including Lewy body features), depression and smell or other hallucinations","Checked the gas and ventilation risk, any plan against the neighbours, and what she is eating and drinking","Agreed one acceptable first step: bloods, a hearing check, or low-dose medicine framed around sleep and stress"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm not mad. You think I'm making it up, don't you?\"","r":"\"I don't think you're making anything up. Something very distressing is happening to you, and you've had to cope alone. I'm here to help you feel safer and sleep better.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will you tell the police to make the neighbours stop?\"","r":"\"I can't control what the police do, and I wish I could make this stop for you. What I can do is help with the stress, the sleep and your hearing, so you feel more in charge. Can we start there?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I take the tape off the vents, the gas will kill me.\"","r":"\"You blocked them to protect yourself, I understand that. The danger is that blocked vents with a gas fire can cause carbon monoxide. Could a gas engineer check, and we fit a carbon monoxide alarm?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking pills. They'll turn me into a zombie.\"","r":"\"That's a fair worry. We'd start with a tiny dose, far lower than younger people take, and check how you feel. If you don't like it, we stop. Would you try it for sleep and stress?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Some nights I hear them laughing about me through the wall.\"","r":"\"That must be horrible, lying there listening. Thank you for telling me. Does it happen when it's quiet and the TV is off? Do you hear voices anywhere else?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I've lived here forty years. Why am I the one seeing a doctor?\"","r":"\"It does feel unfair, and you've done nothing wrong. Seeing me doesn't mean you're to blame. It means you get some support while this is going on.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GP organic screen: FBC, U&E, LFT, calcium, glucose, TFT, B12, folate, CRP, urinalysis; ECG before any antipsychotic","Amisulpride 50 mg once daily (up to 100 mg; lower in renal impairment) or risperidone 0.5 mg at night (up to 1 to 2 mg); review at 2 and 6 weeks for stiffness, tremor, sedation, postural drop, falls","Weight, HbA1c, lipids and prolactin at baseline and 3 months","CT or MRI head if cognitive decline, focal signs or atypical features; EEG if smells or brief spells suggest temporal lobe epilepsy"],"psych":["Same care coordinator visits weekly at first to build trust","CBT for psychosis focused on distress and coping, psychology team, if she engages","ACE-III with hearing corrected"],"social":["Audiology referral via GP; hearing aid batteries and fit checked; local sensory support team visit","Age UK befriending, lunch club or telephone friendship line to cut isolation","With consent, liaise with the local police neighbourhood team about the repeated calls"],"safety":["Gas Safe engineer check, vents cleared, carbon monoxide alarm; fire service safe and well visit","Ask at every visit about plans to confront neighbours; inform police if threats","Check food and fluids if she fears poisoning","Review within 1 week; GP letter the same week"]},"facts":[{"h":"Deafness feeds the belief","x":"Hearing and sight loss strongly predict late-onset psychosis. Getting hearing aids working can ease distress and improves every assessment you do."},{"h":"Partition delusions","x":"People or gas coming through walls, floors or ceilings is classic late-onset psychosis. It can herald dementia, so retest cognition yearly."},{"h":"Low dose works","x":"In very-late-onset psychosis, amisulpride 50 to 100 mg daily has trial evidence. Older women get parkinsonism and falls at small doses."},{"h":"Smells raise a flag","x":"Smell hallucinations should prompt questions about seizures, head injury and temporal lobe disease; consider EEG and imaging."},{"h":"Taped vents can kill","x":"Blocking ventilation near a gas appliance risks carbon monoxide poisoning. A gas check and alarm are quick wins she may accept."},{"h":"Frame care around her goals","x":"Offer help with sleep, stress and hearing, not 'treating delusions'. Agreement grows when the plan matches what she wants."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Assess late-onset psychotic symptoms, screen organic causes and risk, and agree an acceptable plan."},"c-3-5-4":{"obj":["Taken his own account and his wife's separately, covering worry content, checking, mood and suicidal thoughts","Recorded the diazepam in full: dose, frequency, prescriber, alcohol use, falls and driving","Done a cognitive screen and explained why one score today is not a diagnosis","Agreed a written diazepam taper with the GP and a start date for an SSRI and talking therapy","Booked repeat cognitive testing about six months after treatment"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"My father had Alzheimer's. This is how it started with him, isn't it?\"","r":"\"That's an understandable fear after what you saw. Worry itself can make memory much worse, and so can diazepam. Let's treat those first, then test again. I'll tell you honestly what we find.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Wife: \"Why not just do a brain scan and tell us?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting a clear answer. A scan can help, but it can't tell us how much is worry and how much is memory. Treating the anxiety and rechecking gives a far clearer picture.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"The diazepam is the only thing that helps. You're not taking it away.\"","r":"\"I can see it gives you relief, and I won't stop it suddenly; that could be dangerous. We'll come down slowly, in steps you agree, while other treatments start working.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I lie awake checking the doors. I can't switch off.\"","r":"\"That sounds exhausting. The checking is your mind trying to keep you safe, but it keeps the worry going. There are therapies that help with exactly this. Can I tell you about them?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Some nights I have a whisky with the tablet to help me sleep.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that matters. Alcohol and diazepam together slow breathing and worsen falls and memory. Let's cut both down safely. How much whisky is it?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Talking therapy is for young people. I'm too old for that.\"","r":"\"Lots of people think that, but CBT works well at your age, and many find it helps more than tablets. Would you give a few sessions a try?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 25 mg once daily for 1 week, then 50 mg (range 50 to 200 mg); warn about early jitteriness and nausea; sodium at baseline and 2 to 4 weeks","Diazepam taper with the GP: cut by about one-eighth of the daily dose every 2 weeks; one prescriber, short scripts","Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, calcium, HbA1c, TFT, B12, folate; ECG if palpitations","Avoid anticholinergics and any new hypnotic"],"psych":["High-intensity CBT for GAD (12 to 15 sessions) via NHS Talking Therapies; he can self-refer online","Worry diary and sleep routine while waiting"],"social":["Wife offered a carer's assessment by the local authority and details of carers' support","Advise no driving while drowsy on diazepam"],"safety":["Ask about suicidal thoughts directly and record the answer","Falls: night light, check lying and standing BP","Repeat ACE-III at about 6 months; imaging if decline continues","GP letter within 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Diazepam fogs memory","x":"Benzodiazepines blunt attention and new learning and raise falls and hip fracture risk. Daily use can mimic early dementia."},{"h":"Taper by eighths","x":"Reduce by about one-eighth (range one-tenth to one-quarter) of the daily dose every fortnight. Sudden stopping risks seizures and rebound anxiety."},{"h":"Sertraline first for GAD","x":"NICE's first-choice drug for GAD (off-label). Start low in older adults, check sodium, expect benefit over 4 to 6 weeks."},{"h":"Anxiety scores badly","x":"Anxious patients drop points on attention and recall. A score that improves after treatment points away from dementia."},{"h":"Drug-driving law covers diazepam","x":"Driving while impaired by diazepam is an offence even on prescription. Advise no driving if drowsy."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Separate anxiety, depression and early dementia by history, and agree a safe diazepam plan."},"c-3-6-1":{"obj":["Excluded delirium before assessing capacity and recorded how","Given her the key facts about home versus a care home in plain words, with aids, before testing","Recorded two separate assessments, residence and money, each with the functional test and the causal link to dementia","Quoted her own words about home and her cat in the record","Told the team the next legal steps: LPA check, best interests meeting, IMCA if no one to consult, deputyship or appointeeship for money"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm going home to my cat and that's the end of it.\"","r":"\"Your cat clearly matters a great deal, and I want to hear about home. Can you talk me through a normal day there? Who would help if you fell?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I never fell. I just sat down on the floor.\"","r":"\"Okay. The ward says you were on the floor for a while before help came. If that happened at home, what would you do?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Son: \"She's got dementia. Surely she can't make this decision?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd think that. The law says a diagnosis alone doesn't remove her right to decide. I have to test this specific decision. If she can't make it, we decide together in her best interests.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Son: \"So can I sign the care home papers?\"","r":"\"Only if you hold a registered health and welfare Lasting Power of Attorney. Without one, it's a best interests decision by the team, with your views and hers at the centre.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Everyone's ganging up on me. You all want rid of me.\"","r":"\"That sounds frightening, feeling outnumbered. Nobody wants rid of you. My job is to make sure your wishes are heard properly. What would you most want everyone to know?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"The boy next door does my shopping. I give him my card.\"","r":"\"That's kind of him to help. Does he bring back the change and receipts? Do you know roughly how much is in your account?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Exclude delirium first: 4AT, infection treated, bloods back to baseline; reassess capacity if she improves","Hearing aids, glasses and pain relief sorted before testing; see her in the morning when most alert"],"psych":["Two written capacity assessments: understand, retain, use and weigh, communicate; then whether any inability is because of the dementia","Visual aids: photos of home and the care home, a written list of support options"],"social":["Hospital social worker: Care Act needs assessment; trial at home with a care package as the less restrictive option","OT home visit with her: kitchen, stairs, toilet, cat care","OPG register search (form OPG100); if no LPA, family apply to the Court of Protection for deputyship and you complete COP3","If only pension and benefits need managing: DWP appointeeship"],"safety":["Best interests meeting: her, son, social worker, ward team, OT; IMCA if no family or friend appropriate to consult","If a care home will supervise her constantly and she is not free to leave: the home applies for DoLS","Safeguarding concern if the unpaid bills suggest money is being taken","Pendant alarm and key safe if she goes home"]},"facts":[{"h":"Functional test first","x":"The Supreme Court (2021) set the order: can she understand, retain, use and weigh, and communicate? Only then ask whether the dementia causes any inability."},{"h":"One decision at a time","x":"Where to live and managing money are separate decisions. She may manage one and not the other; record each."},{"h":"Weighing is where it fails","x":"Many people with dementia repeat facts but can't apply them to themselves. Ask how she would manage a fall at 2 a.m."},{"h":"Relatives cannot consent","x":"Without a registered LPA or court order, family give views, not consent. The decision-maker is whoever is acting, usually the team or social worker."},{"h":"IMCA for long-term moves","x":"Required for a move of over 28 days in hospital or 8 weeks in a care home when no family or friend is appropriate to consult."},{"h":"Appointee covers benefits only","x":"DWP appointeeship is quicker than deputyship but covers benefits only, not savings or property."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"Assess her capacity separately for residence and finances using the functional test, and advise the team."},"c-3-6-2":{"obj":["Told him plainly, before he left the room, not to drive home today","Explained that he must tell the DVLA, how (online or form CG1), and that the DVLA, not the doctor, decides","Said what you will do if he keeps driving, and recorded the advice and date in the notes and clinic letter","Agreed how he gets home and a phone call in 2 weeks to check he has notified","Offered alternatives: a driving assessment centre, community transport, bus pass"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Fifty-five years driving and never had an accident. You can't stop me.\"","r":"\"That's an impressive record, and I can see how much this matters. I'm not taking your licence; the DVLA decides that. But with this diagnosis the law says you must tell them, and I don't think you should drive today.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"What if I only drive to the shops? It's two miles.\"","r":"\"I understand; it's your independence. Short familiar trips still mean junctions and roundabouts. Until the DVLA decides, please don't drive at all. Could your daughter or a community bus help with shopping?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"That roundabout thing was a one-off. Everyone makes mistakes.\"","r":"\"It may have been, and I'm not judging your driving. Together with today's memory results, it's a warning I can't ignore. The DVLA may arrange a practical assessment, which is the fairest test.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I get my licence back?\"","r":"\"Some people with early Alzheimer's keep driving for a while with yearly reviews. The DVLA decides after reports and sometimes a driving assessment. Telling them yourself puts you in the strongest position.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Without the car I'm stuck. I'll never see my friends.\"","r":"\"Losing the car feels like losing your freedom, and that's a real loss. Let's plan how you keep seeing your friends: bus pass, community transport, lifts. Who do you see most?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Daughter: \"Can't you just not mention it? It'll break him.\"","r":"\"I can see you're protecting him. I can't leave this out; his safety and other people's depend on it. I'll help him through it, and he keeps the chance to tell the DVLA himself.\""}],"bps":{"psych":["Screen mood at the 2-week call; losing driving often triggers low mood","Post-diagnostic support: Alzheimer's Society adviser or local memory support group"],"social":["Daughter drives him home today and helps complete CG1 this week","Older person's bus pass from the council; community transport; taxi account","Driving Mobility accredited centre if the DVLA asks for an on-road assessment"],"safety":["Advice and date recorded in the notes and in the letter to GP and patient","Phone call in 2 weeks to confirm the DVLA has been told","If still driving without notifying: tell him, inform the DVLA medical adviser, then confirm in writing to him","If he insists on driving off today clearly unsafe, consider telling the police"]},"facts":[{"h":"Dementia must be notified","x":"Anyone diagnosed with dementia must tell the DVLA. Bus and lorry licences stop; a car licence may continue with yearly review if driving is judged safe."},{"h":"Not telling has a price","x":"Failing to notify can bring a fine of up to £1,000, and his insurance may be invalid if he crashes."},{"h":"The DVLA decides, not you","x":"Your job is to advise and record. 'The DVLA decides' takes the personal sting out of the conversation."},{"h":"GMC: tell him before and after","x":"If he drives against advice, try to persuade him; if that fails, inform the DVLA medical adviser and tell him before and after you do."},{"h":"No score settles it","x":"No ACE-III cut-off proves unsafe driving. Weigh visuospatial problems, attention, insight and real incidents like the roundabout."},{"h":"Self-reporting counts","x":"The DVLA looks more kindly on drivers who notify themselves than on those found driving against advice."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"Explain his duty to tell the DVLA and what you will do if he does not."},"c-3-6-3":{"obj":["Spoken to her alone and recorded her words, including how she feels about her grandson","Assessed her capacity for the specific decision about giving him her money, and asked about fear or pressure","Raised a safeguarding concern the same day and recorded the reference number","Arranged food, heating and carer support for tonight","Checked the OPG register and agreed who contacts her bank"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"He's a good boy. He needs the money more than I do.\"","r":"\"You clearly love him and want to help him. I'm still worried you've no food or heating in November. Can we find a way to help him that doesn't leave you cold and hungry?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If you get him in trouble he'll leave, and I'll have nobody.\"","r":"\"That's a frightening thought; he's company for you. Our aim isn't to punish anyone. It's to make sure you're safe and have what you need. What would you like to happen?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will the police get involved?\"","r":"\"Not necessarily. A social worker will look at it with you first. If a crime is suspected the police may be told, but your wishes count and we'll keep you informed.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want social workers poking around.\"","r":"\"I hear you, and I'd feel the same. Because I'm worried you're at risk, I do have to tell social care, but they'll go at your pace and keep your wishes in mind.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"Carer: \"He shouts at her if she asks where the money's gone.\"","r":"\"Thank you; that's important, and you did right to tell me. I'll put it in the safeguarding referral. Please note what you see, with dates, and ring the duty number if she's in danger.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm such a burden on everyone.\"","r":"\"You're not a burden. You've every right to warm meals and a warm home. Let's sort out some help today so you're more comfortable.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GP review: weight, hydration, skin and temperature for malnutrition and cold exposure","Confirm she is receiving and taking her prescribed medicines"],"psych":["Decision-specific capacity assessment: giving money away and managing her finances; record any coercion or fear","Screen mood; low mood and isolation raise the risk of exploitation"],"social":["Adult social care: safeguarding concern today; Section 42 enquiry led by a social worker","Emergency food, heating checked, extra carer visits or meals service","Bank's vulnerable customer team: stop or replace the card, review account access","OPG register search (OPG100); OPG investigates if the grandson is an attorney or deputy; DWP checks any appointeeship"],"safety":["Do not confront the grandson; do not promise secrecy","Tell the team safeguarding lead and consultant today","Police if she is in immediate danger or theft is clear","Review within 1 week; attend the strategy meeting; keep a dated chronology"]},"facts":[{"h":"Section 42 is a duty","x":"The council must make enquiries when an adult with care needs may be at risk of abuse or neglect, including financial abuse, and can't protect themselves."},{"h":"Capacity isn't the end","x":"Even with capacity, coercion can make a choice unfree. The High Court's inherent jurisdiction can protect such adults."},{"h":"Banking Protocol","x":"Branch staff can call police at once if they think a vulnerable customer is being exploited during a transaction."},{"h":"It can be a crime","x":"Theft and fraud by abuse of position may apply. Police involvement is decided with her and social care, not by you alone."},{"h":"OPG polices attorneys","x":"If the grandson holds an LPA or deputyship, the Office of the Public Guardian investigates misuse and can seek his removal."},{"h":"Know the signs","x":"Unpaid bills, empty fridge, heating off, a new helper with card and PIN, sudden withdrawals, missing belongings, a changed will."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Assess suspected financial abuse, keep her safe, and make a safeguarding referral respecting her wishes and capacity."},"c-3-6-4":{"obj":["Seen the LPA or checked the OPG register, confirmed it is health and welfare and registered, and noted whether it covers life-sustaining treatment","Explained to his wife that she is the decision-maker for the care home move and must decide in his best interests","Recorded his known past wishes, values and beliefs in the best interests record","Started an advance care plan covering hospital transfer, antibiotics and CPR, to share with the GP and care home","Started the carer's assessment and the NHS Continuing Healthcare checklist"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"I have his power of attorney, so I just decide, don't I?\"","r":"\"If it's a registered health and welfare LPA, yes, this decision is yours. The law asks you to decide in his best interests, thinking about what he'd have wanted. We'll give you everything you need to decide.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I promised him I'd never put him in a home.\"","r":"\"That promise came from love, and you've cared for him for years. Making sure he's safe and well looked after is keeping faith with him too. Choosing good care isn't breaking your promise.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"What if he gets pneumonia? Will they just let him die?\"","r":"\"That's a hard question, and it's good to ask it now. Nobody stops caring. We'd plan whether hospital, or treatment in the home with comfort first, fits him best, and write it down.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Can I insist he's resuscitated whatever happens?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting to do everything. CPR is a medical decision; doctors don't offer it if it wouldn't work, and an attorney can't demand it. But your views and his past wishes shape every decision, and we'll explain our reasons.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm 77. I can't lift him any more. I'm so tired.\"","r":"\"You've given so much, and you're exhausted. Saying you can't manage alone isn't failing him. Let's get your own needs assessed too.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Who pays for the care home?\"","r":"\"It depends on his needs and finances. We'll complete an NHS Continuing Healthcare checklist; if his needs are mainly health needs, the NHS may pay in full. Otherwise social care does a financial assessment.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"His brother says we should keep him at home. Does he get a say?\"","r":"\"His brother's views matter and will be heard at the meeting. As attorney, the decision is yours, made in his best interests. If there's serious disagreement, the Court of Protection can decide.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Medication review before transfer: stop what's not needed; if on an antipsychotic, set a review and reduction date","Treatment escalation plan and ReSPECT form (or local equivalent) agreed with the consultant"],"psych":["Advance care planning with his wife: his values, faith, preferred place of care and of death","Support for her grief and guilt: Admiral Nurse if available, Alzheimer's Society carer groups"],"social":["Ward social worker: Care Act assessment, care home search, financial assessment","NHS CHC checklist; if positive, full CHC assessment; if not eligible and in a nursing home, NHS-funded nursing care","Carer's assessment for his wife from the local authority"],"safety":["Best interests meeting recorded: wife as decision-maker, social worker, nurse, OT, consultant","Care home applies for DoLS on arrival if needed","Discharge summary to GP and care home within 24 hours; care home liaison follow-up in 4 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Two LPAs, different powers","x":"Property and financial affairs can be used, with his consent, while he still has capacity. Health and welfare works only once he lacks capacity for that decision."},{"h":"Life-sustaining needs explicit wording","x":"An attorney can refuse life-sustaining treatment only if the LPA document expressly grants that power. Read the form itself."},{"h":"Unregistered means no power","x":"An LPA has no legal effect until registered at the Office of the Public Guardian. Check the original, a certified copy or the OPG online access code."},{"h":"Attorneys can't demand treatment","x":"They can consent to or refuse what is offered, not insist on treatment clinicians won't offer, including CPR."},{"h":"Advance decision versus LPA","x":"A later health and welfare LPA covering the same treatment overrides an earlier advance decision; an advance decision made after the LPA takes priority."},{"h":"CHC checklist first","x":"A positive checklist triggers a full NHS Continuing Healthcare assessment; if eligible, the NHS pays the whole care home fee."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain her Lasting Power of Attorney, how the care home decision is made, and begin advance care planning."},"c-4-1-1":{"obj":["Named her goal for therapy in her own words as a change in pattern, not just relief","Rated her suitability on four points: psychological mindedness, motivation, resilience between sessions and current crisis","Shared a three-step relationship-pattern formulation in plain words and checked she recognised it","Recommended one therapy type with a reason and one alternative, without promising a start date","Left her with the likely wait, a GP letter copied to her, and three crisis contacts"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So which therapy is the best one? Just tell me and I'll do it.\"","r":"\"Fair question. No single therapy is best for everyone; it depends on what keeps you stuck. Yours sounds like a pattern with people, so a therapy that looks at relationships fits better than one aimed only at mood. Does that match your sense?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I already did the Talking Therapies thing. It didn't work, so what's the point of more?\"","r":"\"That's disappointing, and it makes sense you're wary. Guided self-help is short and aimed at symptoms. What you describe is longer-standing. What did you take from it, even a little? That helps me pick something different.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Every relationship ends the same way. I always pick people who leave.\"","r":"\"That sounds lonely and exhausting. Noticing the pattern is an important first step. Can we walk through the most recent one, from how it started to how it ended, so we can see the pattern together?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"What if I start digging into all this and I fall apart?\"","r":"\"It's wise to ask. Therapy can stir things up for a while. Before we refer, let's check how you'd cope between sessions: who you'd call and what helps. If we need to steady things first, we will.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't you just see me yourself every week? I don't want to start again with a stranger.\"","r":"\"I understand. Telling your story again is tiring. Today is an assessment; the team allocates a therapist trained in the approach that suits you. I'll write it all down so you don't have to repeat everything from scratch.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I have a few glasses of wine most nights. It's the only way I can switch off.\"","r":"\"Thanks for being honest. Lots of people use drink to cope. Roughly how many units a week? It matters because heavy drinking can blunt what therapy can do, so we may need to plan for it alongside.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Ask the GP to confirm the current antidepressant, dose and adherence; continue it through therapy unless reviewed","Screen for an untreated depressive episode (PHQ-9) and alcohol use (AUDIT-C) at baseline"],"psych":["Cognitive analytic therapy, 16–24 weekly sessions, secondary care psychological therapies team (first choice for relational patterns)","Alternative: short-term psychodynamic psychotherapy, about 16–20 sessions, psychotherapy department","Take the formulation to the team allocation meeting before confirming the therapy type"],"social":["Ask about work and childcare clashes now so session times can be planned","Signpost a local peer support or Mind group while waiting"],"safety":["Written safety plan before she leaves: warning signs, two people to call, crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123","GP and care coordinator (if any) told of the plan and the waiting time","Review if she is still waiting at 8 weeks or if crisis contacts rise"]},"facts":[{"h":"Four checks decide suitability","x":"Can she reflect on feelings? Does she want change rather than rescue? Can she cope between sessions? Is there a live crisis? Crisis, active psychosis or heavy drinking: stabilise first."},{"h":"Culture and IQ are not barriers","x":"Never rule out therapy because of ethnicity, language or education. Book an interpreter or adapt the format; the relationship with the therapist predicts outcome more than the model."},{"h":"CAT suits repeating relationship patterns","x":"Cognitive analytic therapy maps the 'roles' a person keeps re-enacting, writes a reformulation letter at about session 4 and runs 16–24 sessions. Good for patterns plus self-criticism."},{"h":"Talking Therapies take self-referrals","x":"NHS Talking Therapies treat common anxiety and depression without a GP letter. Complex, long-standing or high-risk problems go to secondary care psychological therapies instead."},{"h":"Never promise a therapy or date","x":"Allocation is a team decision and waits vary. Say 'I'll recommend' and give a realistic range; a broken promise repeats the rejection she fears."}],"type":"Therapy assessment","do":"Assess her suitability for psychological therapy and agree which therapy, if any, to recommend."},"c-4-1-2":{"obj":["Declined the watch and the friend request in one warm sentence each, giving the reason","Explored what the gift means to him and linked it to his fear of the ending","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts and recorded his answer and what keeps him going","Agreed a plan for the remaining sessions, including a written relapse blueprint","Booked supervision and documented the gift, the refusal and the risk check the same day"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Please take it. I'll be really hurt if you don't. It cost me a lot.\"","r":"\"I can see how much thought went into this, and I'm moved. That's exactly why I want to be honest: accepting something this valuable would change what we have here. The thank-you itself I'm keeping.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So I'm just another case to you. Real people accept presents.\"","r":"\"It hurts to feel pushed away, especially now. You're not just a case; these sessions have mattered. Saying no is how I keep this space safe for you. Can we talk about what it felt like when I said that?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Fine, no watch. But we can still message after therapy ends, can't we? Just now and then.\"","r":"\"I understand wanting to keep a link. I can't stay in touch personally once we finish. What I can do is help you build the links that will last: people, groups and services you can turn to.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"You're the only person who gets me. When this stops I'll have nothing.\"","r":"\"That sounds frightening. When you say you'll have nothing, do thoughts of self-harm or wishing you weren't here come into it? I'd like to understand so we can plan the ending together.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it against the rules or something? Will you get in trouble?\"","r":"\"There are guidelines, yes, but that's not the main reason. Gifts like this can make a therapy relationship feel special in a way that makes endings harder. I'd rather we put that energy into what you'll take with you.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"My dad walked out when I was nine. Everyone leaves in the end.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me that. I wonder if our ending is touching that old hurt. It's worth us using the next sessions to look at it, so this ending can feel different from that one.\""}],"bps":{"psych":["Use the remaining 4 sessions for a planned ending: review progress, name the loss, rehearse coping","Write a 'what helped' blueprint with him by the final session; he keeps a copy","Take your own guilt or flattery to supervision before the next session"],"social":["Map his supports (sister, work, faith group) and agree one extra contact per week","Signpost a peer support group or voluntary sector service to start before therapy ends","Tell his care coordinator the end date and how anxious he is about it"],"safety":["Direct suicide question asked and recorded today","Crisis card: crisis team number, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123","Check your social media privacy settings; do not reply to the request","Gift declined and noted; declare on the trust register only if accepted under policy"]},"facts":[{"h":"Crossing versus violation","x":"A boundary crossing (a card, a small team gift) can be harmless and discussable. A violation exploits the relationship and harms the patient. Repeated crossings nobody discusses slide into violations."},{"h":"Gift value thresholds are local","x":"NHS conflict of interest guidance sets low-value gifts (often under £50) as acceptable to declare; anything costly is declined. Check your trust policy, not your instinct."},{"h":"Never accept patient friend requests","x":"GMC guidance: keep personal and professional online lives apart. Don't accept, don't reply on the platform; raise it in the next session and record it."},{"h":"Endings raise risk","x":"The run-up to the last session can bring back old losses and suicidal thoughts. Ask directly whenever someone says they 'don't know what they'll do'."},{"h":"Your feelings are data","x":"Feeling flattered, guilty or irritated by a gift tells you something about the relationship. Name it in supervision rather than acting on it in the room."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"Address the gift, contact request and dependency while keeping the therapeutic relationship safe."},"c-4-1-3":{"obj":["Checked the cuts for depth, bleeding and infection and arranged wound care if needed","Asked about intent, substances taken and current suicidal thoughts, and recorded the answers","Asked whether she feels safe with her partner and whether any children are at home","Agreed with her what will be shared with her care coordinator, by whom, and when today","Completed a written safety plan with her, giving two named contacts and crisis numbers"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"If you tell anyone, I'm done. I'll never come back.\"","r":"\"I hear how scared you are of this getting out of your hands. I won't do anything behind your back. Can we decide together exactly what gets said and who says it? You can be in the room when I call.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"It's stupid. I'm an adult, I shouldn't be doing this.\"","r":"\"It's not stupid. Self-harm is usually a way of surviving a feeling that's too big in the moment. Let's understand what happened last night, so we can find other ways through it.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It's just scratches. It's not like I was trying to kill myself.\"","r":"\"Thanks for telling me that; it matters. I still take it seriously because it shows how much you were hurting. Did any part of you want to die last night? And right now?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He gets in my face when he's had a drink. Last night he threw my phone.\"","r":"\"That sounds frightening, and it isn't your fault. Do you feel safe going home tonight? There's specialist help for this, and you decide what happens next. Can I tell you what's available?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will they section me or take me off therapy?\"","r":"\"No. Sharing this is about getting you more support, not punishment. Nothing today suggests hospital. Therapy carries on, and we might add skills for coping with strong feelings.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What would you even tell her? What's the point?\"","r":"\"Just the essentials: what happened, that you're safe now, and our plan. That way she isn't working in the dark if things get hard between sessions. What would you want her to know, or not mention?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Clean superficial cuts: practice nurse or GP same day for dressing; A&E only if gaping, deep, infected or any overdose","Ask about tetanus status if the blade was dirty","No medication to reduce self-harm itself (NICE NG225); review her antidepressant with the care team if mood has dipped"],"psych":["Pause the CBT agenda today; build a chain analysis of last night (trigger, feeling, act, aftermath)","Add emotion regulation and distress tolerance skills to the CBT plan, agreed in supervision","If self-harm repeats, consider referral for DBT-informed work via the team"],"social":["Domestic abuse: offer the National Domestic Abuse Helpline (0808 2000 247) and a referral to the local IDVA service","If she consents, complete a DASH risk checklist; high risk goes to MARAC","Identify a safe person she can stay with if arguments escalate"],"safety":["Care coordinator told today, with her knowledge; risk section of the care plan updated","Written safety plan: triggers, delays (cold water, call a friend), two named contacts, crisis team, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123","Same-day phone contact if she misses next week; never discharge for non-attendance","Review in 1 week; discuss in supervision this week"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask every time: did you take anything?","x":"Cuts often come with a hidden overdose. Paracetamol overdose may feel fine for 24 hours yet still needs blood levels in A&E. Ask directly and record the answer."},{"h":"No risk scores to decide care","x":"NICE NG225: do not use risk tools or 'low/medium/high' labels to predict suicide or ration care. Assess needs and make a safety plan with her."},{"h":"Sharing within the team is normal","x":"Information can go to her direct care team without fresh consent, but tell her what and why. Silence breeds mistrust; secrecy is outside your role."},{"h":"Ask about method briefly, not graphically","x":"Record site, depth and tool in one line. Detailed questioning about method adds shame and adds little to the plan."},{"h":"Argument as trigger means ask about abuse","x":"Ask privately: 'Has he ever hurt you or made you afraid?' Children at home turn it into a children's safeguarding referral, not just an adult one."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Respond to the in-session self-harm disclosure, assess risk, and agree what is shared and with whom."},"c-4-2-1":{"obj":["Drawn his own thought–feeling–action cycle from the job loss, with him filling in the gaps","Named three thinking traps he uses, each tied to something he said today","Corrected the 'positive thinking' myth in one sentence while agreeing his problems are real","Given the format: about 16–20 weekly hour-long sessions, homework, collaboration, and a possible early dip","Recorded his choice among CBT, behavioural activation, medication, a combination, or waiting, plus a brief risk check"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So I just think happy thoughts and suddenly I've got a job again?\"","r":"\"No, and you'd be right to walk out if I said that. Your losses are real. CBT looks at how depression bends the way you read them, so you can act in ways that help rather than keep you stuck.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why can't I just have tablets? Talking won't fix my situation.\"","r":"\"Tablets are a real option, and some people do both. Talking therapy tends to lower the chance of relapse later. Shall I lay out each option side by side so you can choose?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm a failure. Thirty and nothing to show for it.\"","r":"\"That sounds heavy to carry. Notice the word 'failure': it sums up a whole person from two hard events. That's the kind of thought CBT would look at with you, gently and with evidence, not by arguing.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I can't talk in groups. Will there be other people there?\"","r":"\"Good to know now. Individual CBT is one-to-one; group CBT is another option for milder depression. I'll ask for individual sessions given what you've said.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Homework? I'm not at school. I won't have time for that.\"","r":"\"Fair reaction. The 'homework' is usually small: noting a thought or doing one planned activity. It matters because change happens between sessions. You and the therapist agree what's realistic each week.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How long until I feel any different?\"","r":"\"Many people notice a change within the first six to eight sessions, especially once they start doing more again. Some feel slightly worse first. If it isn't helping, we review rather than you giving up.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["If he chooses medication: sertraline 50 mg once daily in the morning; range 50–200 mg, up in 50 mg steps no faster than weekly","Warn: nausea, loose stools, sweating, sexual dysfunction, early agitation; review at 2 weeks","Check alcohol intake; advise against using it to sleep"],"psych":["Individual CBT for depression, 16–20 weekly sessions, NHS Talking Therapies (he can self-refer)","Start behavioural activation now: one planned activity a day, logged with mood 0–10","Baseline PHQ-9 today; repeat each session"],"social":["Jobcentre Plus and Universal Credit claim; Citizens Advice for money worries","Encourage one social contact per week (friend, sport, volunteering)"],"safety":["Brief suicide screen today and recorded","Safety plan with crisis team number, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123","GP letter within a week stating the agreed option"]},"facts":[{"h":"Hot cross bun in 60 seconds","x":"Draw four linked circles: thoughts, feelings, body, actions, with the situation above. Fill it with his words. A drawing beats a definition when he has a bus to catch."},{"h":"Three traps cover most depressed thinking","x":"Labelling ('I'm a failure'), overgeneralising ('nothing ever works') and fortune-telling ('I'll never get hired'). Pick the ones he actually used today."},{"h":"Behavioural activation can start today","x":"Withdrawal starves mood of reward. One small, scheduled, valued activity a day, logged with a mood score, is evidence-based and needs no waiting list."},{"h":"PHQ-9 bands","x":"5–9 mild, 10–14 moderate, 15–19 moderately severe, 20–27 severe. Item 9 asks about suicidal thoughts: always follow it up in person."},{"h":"New antidepressant: review at 2 weeks","x":"NICE NG222: review within 2 weeks of starting; within 1 week if under 25 or at raised risk, because suicidal thoughts can rise early. Book it before he leaves."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain CBT for depression and common thinking errors plainly, and help him decide whether to start."},"c-4-2-2":{"obj":["Drawn the fear–avoidance–relief loop with her using one recent dog encounter","Built the first five rungs of a fear ladder with her SUDS rating for each","Taught one relaxation skill in the room (slow breathing or muscle relaxation) and had her practise it once","Screened for blood-injury fainting, panic attacks and low mood","Agreed the first exposure task, how often she will practise it, and how she will record it"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"You're not going to put me in a room with a dog today, are you?\"","r":"\"Absolutely not. Nothing happens without your agreement, and we start where it feels manageable, maybe just a photo. You set the pace; my job is to keep it a stretch, not a shock.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why would facing it help? Every time I see a dog I just panic more.\"","r":"\"Good question. Usually you leave before the fear peaks and falls, so your brain never learns it passes. If you stay with a small step long enough, the fear drops by itself, and next time it starts lower.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't I just have something to calm me down before I go out?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting a shortcut. Calming tablets block the very learning that makes the fear shrink and can be habit-forming. A breathing technique gives you control without that downside. Shall we try one now?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I fainted once when they took my blood. Is that the same thing?\"","r":"\"Really useful to know. That fainting pattern is different and needs a different technique: tensing your muscles to keep blood pressure up, rather than relaxing. Let's check whether it applies to dogs too.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I turned down a job I really wanted because of this. It's ruining my life.\"","r":"\"That's a painful loss, and it shows how much the fear has taken. The good news is this is one of the most treatable problems we see. Let's set a goal around work so we know what we're aiming for.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How many sessions? I can't keep taking time off.\"","r":"\"Phobias often improve in fewer sessions than people expect, sometimes a handful, especially with daily practice at home. Some services offer evening or video sessions; ask when you refer yourself.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No benzodiazepines or routine beta-blockers for specific phobia; state this in the GP letter","Screen for co-existing depression (PHQ-9) and panic disorder"],"psych":["Exposure-based CBT for specific phobia, NHS Talking Therapies (self-referral), typically a short course","Teach paced breathing (in 4, out 6) or progressive muscle relaxation; practise daily for a week before step 1","Daily exposure homework, 20–45 minutes, staying until SUDS falls by about half; diary of SUDS before, peak, after"],"social":["Set a work goal (such as tolerating a dog-friendly office) as the top rung","Recruit a trusted friend with a calm, small dog for later rungs, agreed with the therapist"],"safety":["Plan for a bad step: drop one rung, not stop; who to contact","Brief mood and suicide screen recorded"]},"facts":[{"h":"Stay until the fear falls","x":"Leaving at the peak teaches the brain escape worked. Stay until SUDS drops by roughly half, then repeat. Short, frequent practice beats one long session a week."},{"h":"Safety behaviours sabotage exposure","x":"Clutching keys, holding a friend's arm or checking exits keeps the fear alive. Spot them and drop them one by one as she climbs."},{"h":"Blood-injury phobia: tense, don't relax","x":"Heart rate rises then drops, causing faints. Applied tension (clench arms, legs and trunk for 10–15 seconds, repeat) keeps blood pressure up. Relaxation makes it worse."},{"h":"Desensitisation versus exposure","x":"Systematic desensitisation pairs each rung with relaxation. Graded exposure relies on staying put until anxiety settles. Both climb a ladder; exposure is the active ingredient."},{"h":"Phobias rarely need secondary care","x":"Specific phobia is usually treated by NHS Talking Therapies with brief exposure-based CBT. Refer onward only with complex comorbidity or risk."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain systematic desensitisation and graded exposure for her phobia, and agree a first exposure step."},"c-4-2-3":{"obj":["Got the patient's consent before discussing his symptoms with his mother","Drawn the trigger–thought–anxiety–compulsion–relief cycle using his handwashing","Built the first rungs of an exposure ladder with him and agreed one task without washing","Agreed one specific, gradual change in his mother's cleaning or reassurance, with his agreement","Discussed an SSRI with dose, time to effect and early-review plan, and checked mood and suicidal thoughts"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I don't wash and someone gets ill, it's my fault. I can't risk that.\"","r":"\"That fear feels completely real, and it shows how much you care about your family. OCD uses that care against you. In ERP we test the fear in small steps, so you learn what actually happens.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"(Mother) Have I made him worse by cleaning for him?\"","r":"\"You did it out of love, and anyone would. It's very common. It does keep OCD going, so we'll plan small steps back together, with him, rather than all at once.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So I just touch dirty things and don't wash? That's it?\"","r":"\"Touching is half of it. The key part is not washing afterwards and letting the anxiety rise and fall on its own. We start with something rated about 30 out of 100, not your worst fear.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to take tablets. I'm not crazy.\"","r":"\"You're not crazy; OCD is common and treatable. Tablets are optional. Therapy alone works for many people. If things stay stuck we can revisit it, and you'd still be the one deciding.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"(Mother) What do I say when he asks me again and again if his hands are clean?\"","r":"\"Something kind but non-reassuring, agreed with him: 'I can see you're anxious. I think you can handle not knowing.' Then change the subject. The therapist will help you both script it.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"My hands are killing me. They crack and bleed.\"","r":"\"That sounds really painful. Let's look after your skin now: a gentle soap substitute and plenty of emollient. Your GP can check for infection. Healing skin also takes away one of OCD's arguments.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["If he opts for an SSRI: sertraline 50 mg in the morning, rising in 50 mg steps (at least a week apart) towards 200 mg if needed; or fluoxetine 20 mg up to 60 mg","OCD response is slow: judge at 12 weeks on a tolerated dose; continue at least 12 months after remission","Age 19: review at 1 week for agitation and suicidal thoughts; warn of nausea, sleep change, sexual side effects","Skin: soap substitute and fragrance-free emollient after every wash; GP to check for infection"],"psych":["CBT with ERP: mild, up to about 10 therapist hours in NHS Talking Therapies; moderate–severe, more intensive CBT with ERP via secondary care psychology","Baseline Y-BOCS today; repeat at review","Home ERP diary: trigger, peak anxiety 0–100, minutes until it halves, any washing"],"social":["Written family plan, agreed by all three: reduce one accommodating behaviour per week","Carer's assessment for his mother through adult social care; signpost OCD-UK","Check whether OCD is affecting college or work; letter if adjustments needed"],"safety":["Mood and suicide screen today; repeat at 1-week SSRI review","Crisis numbers to patient and mother","GP letter copied to patient within a week"]},"facts":[{"h":"Response prevention is the active part","x":"Exposure without blocking the wash is just stress. Every task pairs a trigger with not washing, reassuring or neutralising until the anxiety settles."},{"h":"Watch for hidden compulsions","x":"Mental reviewing, silent counting or 'just checking' with family can replace washing. If ERP stalls despite effort, look for these."},{"h":"Y-BOCS severity bands","x":"0–7 subclinical, 8–15 mild, 16–23 moderate, 24–31 severe, 32–40 extreme. Score at baseline so you can show him progress."},{"h":"OCD needs higher SSRI doses, longer","x":"Expect up to 12 weeks for benefit and often higher doses than for depression. If an adequate trial fails: another SSRI or clomipramine, with combined CBT."},{"h":"Taper accommodation, don't stop it dead","x":"A sudden withdrawal of cleaning or reassurance without his agreement spikes distress and conflict. Plan one change at a time, agreed in advance."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain ERP to him and his mother, including family accommodation, and agree next steps."},"c-4-3-1":{"obj":["Linked the start of her low mood to her mother's death and the rift with her sister on a simple timeline","Chosen one or two IPT focus areas with her (grief, dispute) and said why in plain words","Screened for psychosis, heavy drinking, complicated grief and suicidal thoughts, and recorded each","Explained IPT's length, weekly format and that it runs alongside sertraline, and checked her understanding","Agreed a baseline PHQ-9, a safety plan and a GP request to review the sertraline dose and adherence"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"If this IPT is better, why did nobody offer it to me in the first place?\"","r":"\"That's a fair frustration. No therapy is best for everyone; services often start with the most widely available one. Your story points clearly to relationships and loss, which is exactly where IPT focuses. That's why it fits now.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Everyone says I should be over Mum by now. It's been a year.\"","r":"\"There's no deadline on grief, and being told to move on can make it lonelier. Can you tell me what the hardest moments are now? I want to know whether you're stuck in the grief or simply missing her.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"My sister didn't lift a finger when Mum was ill. I'm not making up with her.\"","r":"\"That sounds like a real hurt, and you'd be angry with good reason. IPT doesn't force a reconciliation. It helps you work out what you want from that relationship and how to say it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to stop the sertraline if I start therapy?\"","r":"\"No. You can take both, and together they often work better than either alone. I'll ask your GP to check whether the dose is right, since it hasn't helped yet.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I don't need to talk about feelings. I just want things back to normal.\"","r":"\"Getting back to normal is a good goal. When people say that, sometimes things feel very bleak underneath. Have there been times you've thought life wasn't worth living?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How is this different from the CBT my friend had?\"","r":"\"CBT works mainly on thoughts and habits. IPT works on what's happening in your relationships and how mood and those changes feed each other. Both are recommended for depression; IPT suits your story well.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GP to check sertraline adherence and dose: start 50 mg once daily, range 50–200 mg, increase in 50 mg steps at least a week apart; review 2–4 weeks after any change","If no response at an adequate dose for 4–6 weeks, GP or team to consider a switch","Warn of sertraline side effects: nausea, sleep change, sexual dysfunction"],"psych":["IPT, about 16 weekly sessions, CMHT or psychotherapy service; focus: grief plus role dispute with sister","Baseline PHQ-9 today; repeat every session","Screen for prolonged grief (persistent yearning, avoiding reminders) at assessment"],"social":["Ask about work and money since the bereavement; signpost Cruse Bereavement Support","Map remaining supports: partner, friends, faith or community groups"],"safety":["Written safety plan: crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123","Re-screen suicidal thoughts if guilt about her mother's death surfaces","GP letter copied to her within a week; review if wait exceeds 8 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Four IPT problem areas","x":"Grief, interpersonal disputes, role transitions and interpersonal sensitivity (isolation). Choose one or two, linked to when the depression began. Too many foci dilutes the work."},{"h":"Prolonged grief has a time line","x":"ICD-11 prolonged grief disorder needs intense yearning or preoccupation persisting at least 6 months after the death and clearly beyond cultural norms. A year of yearning plus impairment: name it."},{"h":"IPT is three phases","x":"Early sessions map relationships and agree the focus; middle sessions work on it; final sessions plan the ending and relapse signs. Tell her this so the end isn't a shock."},{"h":"Medication non-response isn't a bar","x":"Combined therapy and antidepressant often outperforms either alone. Treat non-response by checking dose and adherence, not by withholding therapy."},{"h":"Don't rank therapies against each other","x":"Patients hear 'better' as 'I was short-changed'. Say different therapies suit different problems, then explain why this one suits hers."}],"type":"Therapy assessment","do":"Assess her suitability for interpersonal therapy, explain IPT, and agree a plan."},"c-4-3-2":{"obj":["Responded to the accusation with curiosity rather than defence, and checked for any real lapse in your care","Offered one tentative link between her feelings about you and earlier relationships, in everyday words","Declined your mobile number while giving the crisis line and the date and time of the next session","Checked for self-harm and suicidal thoughts this week","Named your irritation and urge to rescue to your supervisor and agreed how to use them"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You're just like the rest. You don't actually care, you're paid to sit there.\"","r":"\"It sounds like you're feeling really let down, and I'm glad you said it here rather than not coming. Help me understand: what happened that left you feeling I don't care?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If you cared, you'd give me your number. Just for emergencies.\"","r":"\"I can hear how alone the gaps between sessions feel. My personal number has to stay private, but I'll see you same time next week, and the crisis line is answered any hour. Let's write it down now.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"My mum was always 'busy'. I'd wait by the window and she wouldn't come.\"","r":"\"That sounds painful. I wonder if being late today and feeling I don't care connects to that waiting by the window. Does that fit at all, or not really?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Don't psychoanalyse me. You were late last week anyway.\"","r":"\"You're right, I was late last week, and I'm sorry. That matters. I don't want to explain your feelings away. Can we talk about how that felt for you?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Sometimes between sessions I just want to disappear.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. When you say disappear, do you mean thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life? I'd like to check how safe you've been this week.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do you need to tell your supervisor about me?\"","r":"\"Fair question. Supervision is where I think about how to help you best. It's confidential within the team, and it's standard for all my patients, not because you've done anything wrong.\""}],"bps":{"psych":["Weekly supportive psychodynamic sessions at a fixed time; give at least 2 weeks' notice of breaks","Bring session 8 to psychotherapy supervision this week, with process notes","Interpret tentatively and late in the session only when the link is clear"],"social":["Check her support between sessions: friends, groups, peer support","Confirm the care coordinator knows the therapy schedule and breaks"],"safety":["Ask about self-harm and suicidal thoughts this session and record the answer","Give crisis team number, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123 in writing","Update the care coordinator if risk has changed; review crisis plan before any break"]},"facts":[{"h":"Rule out a real grievance first","x":"Before naming transference, ask what actually happened: a late start, a cancelled session, a missed call. Apologise for real lapses; only then explore older patterns."},{"h":"Rescue pull signals boundary drift","x":"An urge to give extra time, extra contact or your number is countertransference. Notice it, hold the frame and take it to supervision."},{"h":"Keep interpretations tentative","x":"Offer links as 'I wonder if…' and ask whether they fit. Firm or early interpretations feel like blame and can push a patient out of therapy."},{"h":"Breaks and lateness stir abandonment","x":"Testing behaviour often rises before holidays, rotations and endings. Flag breaks early and give crisis routes for the gap."},{"h":"Process notes stay separate","x":"Personal process notes for supervision are kept apart from the clinical record, following trust policy. The clinical record still holds risk, plan and contacts."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"Identify transference and countertransference, respond safely in session, and take it to supervision."},"c-4-3-3":{"obj":["Confirmed what their son has agreed can be shared before discussing his care","Explained negative symptoms and sedation in a way that reframed 'lazy' as illness","Described family intervention: at least 10 sessions over 3 to 12 months, with their son where possible","Named one critical or over-involved pattern at home and agreed one alternative response","Arranged a carer's assessment, a family intervention referral and a shared relapse plan"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"(Father) He's not ill, he's lazy. I was working at his age.\"","r":"\"It's really hard watching him in bed all day, and I can hear your worry. Schizophrenia can drain motivation and energy, and some medicines sedate. It isn't a choice. Pushing hard usually backfires, but there are things that help.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"(Mother) If I don't check on him every hour, what if something happens?\"","r":"\"That's a frightening thought to live with. Checking every hour will exhaust you and may make him feel watched. Let's agree what the real warning signs are, so you know when to act and can rest otherwise.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this saying we caused his illness?\"","r":"\"No. Families don't cause schizophrenia. What happens at home can affect how often it comes back, which is good news, because it means you can help lower that risk.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why won't anyone tell us anything? We're the ones looking after him.\"","r":"\"That's a real frustration, and your role is vital. With his agreement I can share his plan. Even without it, I can listen, give you general information and make sure you get support of your own.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"(Mother) I haven't slept properly in months. I don't know how much longer I can do this.\"","r":"\"You're carrying a lot. Your health matters too. You're able to ask for a carer's assessment, which can lead to practical support and breaks. Shall I set that up today?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What happens in these sessions? Do we all sit in a circle and argue?\"","r":"\"No. A trained worker meets you as a family, helps you understand the illness, spot early warning signs and solve problems together. Many families find home life calmer afterwards.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Check antipsychotic adherence with him and the care coordinator; if covert non-adherence drove the relapses, offer a long-acting injection","Review sedation: move a sedating dose to night or lower it if possible","Physical health checks at least annually: weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids, smoking; ask about cannabis"],"psych":["Family intervention, at least 10 sessions over 3–12 months, EIP family intervention worker, with the son where possible","Offer him CBT for psychosis, at least 16 sessions, EIP psychology","Teach the family his early warning signs and agree who to call"],"social":["Carer's assessment for both parents through adult social care (Care Act 2014)","Signpost Rethink Mental Illness carer groups","Care coordinator to plan a graded daytime routine, education or work support (e.g. IPS employment specialist)"],"safety":["Relapse prevention plan shared with the family, with his consent","EIP duty number and crisis line given to parents","Review at the next care plan review meeting"]},"facts":[{"h":"Expressed emotion has three parts","x":"Critical comments, hostility and emotional over-involvement. High levels at home raise relapse risk. Calm, low-key, warm responses lower it; teach rather than blame."},{"h":"Negative symptoms look like laziness","x":"Low drive, flat affect, little speech and withdrawal are part of schizophrenia. Sedation and post-psychotic depression add to it. Check both before calling it motivation."},{"h":"Carers have their own rights","x":"Under the Care Act 2014 any adult carer who appears to have support needs can have a carer's assessment, whatever the patient's consent."},{"h":"Listening never breaches confidentiality","x":"You can always hear carers, take information and give general advice about the illness. Only his personal details need his consent."},{"h":"Family intervention cuts relapse","x":"NICE CG178 offers it to all families living with or in close contact with the person. Ten or more sessions, over 3 months to a year."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain family intervention for schizophrenia to the parents and agree how to offer it."},"c-4-3-4":{"obj":["Recommended one first-line therapy for each of the three referrals with a one-line reason","Named an alternative and an interim plan for each in case of long waits","Flagged stabilisation before trauma work and asked about alcohol use in the PTSD referral","Agreed who will check each person's preference and who sends each referral","Recorded the decisions in the MDT minutes and planned GP letters"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"(Colleague) Why not just put them all on the CBT list? It's quicker.\"","r":"\"I understand the pressure on lists. Each one has a specific evidence-based option that general CBT would miss, and the woman who self-harms could deteriorate on a long generic wait. Can we check the specialist waits first?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"(23-year-old) A year of groups? I can't sit with strangers talking about feelings.\"","r":"\"Lots of people feel that before starting. DBT groups teach skills, more like a class than sharing your story. Many find being with others who understand helps them stay with it.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"(41-year-old man) I don't want to relive the crash. I can't go through that.\"","r":"\"That's understandable; avoiding it has been how you've coped. Trauma therapy goes at a pace you can manage, with skills first. The aim is for the memory to feel like the past, not now.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"(50-year-old woman) Therapy won't take my pain away. I want a scan, not a shrink.\"","r":"\"Your pain is real, and I'm not saying it's in your head. ACT doesn't promise to remove it. It helps you get back to things you value despite it, which tablets often can't. Would you hear more?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"(23-year-old) Nine months? I'll have hurt myself again by then.\"","r":"\"Waiting that long sounds unbearable when things are this hard. You won't be left alone: we'll set regular appointments with the same clinician and a safety plan now. Can we start that today?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"(41-year-old man) Will tablets fix this instead?\"","r":"\"Trauma-focused therapy works best for PTSD. If you'd rather have medication, or as well, sertraline or venlafaxine are options. Sleeping tablets and diazepam aren't advised for this. What would you prefer?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Woman, 23: no drug treatment specifically for borderline personality disorder; treat comorbid depression on its own merits; in crisis a short sedative course of no more than 1 week only","Man, 41: if he prefers drugs, sertraline 50 mg in the morning (range 50–200 mg) or venlafaxine; no benzodiazepines; AUDIT-C now","Woman, 50: no opioids, gabapentinoids or benzodiazepines for chronic primary pain; an antidepressant (e.g. amitriptyline or duloxetine) can be considered after discussing benefits and harms"],"psych":["Woman, 23: DBT about 12 months (weekly individual, skills group, phone coaching); MBT if preferred; structured clinical management from the CMHT while waiting","Man, 41: trauma-focused CBT (8–12 sessions) or EMDR, trauma service or NHS Talking Therapies","Woman, 50: ACT via the pain clinic or health psychology team"],"social":["Man, 41: check work, driving and insurance or legal stress after the crash","Woman, 50: check work and benefits; signpost pain support groups","Interpreters and access needs flagged on each referral"],"safety":["Woman, 23: safety plan, one named care coordinator, fixed-time contacts rather than ad-hoc crisis visits","All three: crisis line and NHS 111 option 2 in each care plan","Allocation reviewed at next MDT if any wait exceeds the service standard"]},"facts":[{"h":"DBT has four parts","x":"Weekly individual therapy, weekly skills group, phone coaching for crises and a therapist consultation team. Usually about a year. Programmes without all four are 'DBT-informed'."},{"h":"Stabilise before trauma processing","x":"Repeated self-harm, heavy drinking or ongoing danger come first. Unstabilised trauma work can raise distress and self-harm."},{"h":"No drugs for borderline personality disorder","x":"NICE CG78: don't prescribe for the disorder or its symptoms. In crisis, a short sedative course of no more than a week, then stop."},{"h":"Chronic primary pain: avoid the usual analgesics","x":"NICE NG193: don't start opioids, gabapentinoids, NSAIDs or benzodiazepines. Offer exercise, ACT or CBT, and consider an antidepressant."},{"h":"EMDR uses bilateral stimulation","x":"The person holds the memory in mind while following eye movements or taps. It is a NICE-recommended option for PTSD after non-combat trauma, alongside trauma-focused CBT."}],"type":"Therapy assessment","do":"Match each patient to DBT, MBT, ACT or EMDR and justify each choice to the team."},"c-4-4-1":{"obj":["Owned your part in the 'bored' session without defending yourself, and asked what would help","Found out the reasons for the two missed sessions","Compared today's PHQ-9 with baseline and asked directly about suicidal thoughts and plans","Agreed with him a revised plan for the last four sessions and who takes over after you rotate","Started a relapse prevention blueprint and arranged a medication review"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You were checking the clock. Why would I come back to that?\"","r":"\"You're right to tell me. If I looked distracted, that's on me, and I'm sorry. I'm glad you came back anyway. What would make these sessions feel worth coming to?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have rung. I just couldn't face anyone.\"","r":"\"Not ringing makes sense when everything feels too much. I'm not annoyed. What made it hard to come in? I'd like us to make it easier to stay in touch if it happens again.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I lost the job. Everything's just falling apart.\"","r":"\"That's a big blow on top of feeling low. No wonder your scores have gone up. Have things got so bad that you've thought of ending your life?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"And now you're leaving too. What was the point?\"","r":"\"It's fair to feel let down; the timing is mine, not yours. We'll use our last four sessions well, and I'll make sure you're handed on properly, not just dropped.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I get someone else, or is this it?\"","r":"\"I'll discuss it with my supervisor this week. Given how things have got harder, I'll ask for therapy to continue with a colleague. I'll tell you the answer at our next session.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking pills. I don't want to be a zombie.\"","r":"\"That's a common worry. Most modern antidepressants don't make people feel like that. It's your choice; I'd just like the doctor to talk it through with you so you can decide with the facts.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Medication review by GP or CMHT doctor within 2 weeks: if he agrees, sertraline 50 mg in the morning, range 50–200 mg; review at 2 weeks","Check alcohol use since the job loss"],"psych":["Repeat PHQ-9 each session; if no improvement, supervisor to consider extending or stepping up therapy","Next four sessions: rupture repair, behavioural activation around job seeking, relapse blueprint","Name the ending early; link it to past losses only if he raises them"],"social":["Jobcentre Plus and Universal Credit claim this week","Citizens Advice or StepChange for debt and rent","Encourage one structured activity a day (volunteering, gym, library)"],"safety":["Suicide question asked and recorded today; safety plan updated with crisis team, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans 116 123","Same-day phone call after any further missed session; no discharge for non-attendance","Written handover to the next therapist and GP letter before you rotate"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ruptures come in two kinds","x":"Withdrawal (missing sessions, going quiet) and confrontation (complaints, anger). Repairing either, by owning your part and asking what would help, predicts better outcomes than never having one."},{"h":"Never discharge for non-attendance alone","x":"A missed session may mean deterioration. Phone the same day, check risk, and tell the care team before any decision to close."},{"h":"Rising PHQ-9 means review the plan","x":"NHS Talking Therapies count a rise of 6 or more points as reliable deterioration. Recheck item 9, review risk and discuss stepping up care or adding medication."},{"h":"Rotations are forced endings","x":"Tell patients your rotation date at the start of therapy and again with four sessions left. A surprise ending repeats old abandonments."},{"h":"Blueprint before the last session","x":"One page: what kept the depression going, what helped, early warning signs and who to contact. He keeps it; a copy goes to the GP."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Address missed sessions, repair the rupture, respond to deterioration, and plan a good therapy ending."},"c-5-1-1":{"obj":["Gathered a home, school and clubs picture of attention, activity and impulsivity, with onset before 12, using everyday activities rather than labels","Screened development, hearing, sleep, absences, personal and family cardiac history, and safeguarding, and recorded each one","Told the mother in plain words that ADHD is a brain-development difference, not a parenting failure, and checked she could say it back","Agreed a written plan: school rating scales, parent-training referral, hearing check and a review date with the child present","Explained the pre-medication baseline (height, weight, pulse, BP on centile charts) and what monitoring would follow"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"My husband keeps saying I'm too soft with him. Did I cause this?\"","r":"\"I can hear how much that blame hurts. ADHD comes mostly from genes and how the brain develops, not from how soft or strict you are. Parenting skills can help, but they help with ADHD, they don't explain it.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"He can play his console for three hours without moving. How can he have an attention problem?\"","r":"\"That's a really common puzzle. Games reward him every few seconds, so they hold almost anyone's attention. What matters is boring tasks: homework, getting dressed, listening in class. How does he manage those?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I've read these tablets turn kids into zombies and stunt their growth.\"","r":"\"It's right to worry about that. At the correct dose children should seem more like themselves, not flat. Growth can slow a little, which is why we measure him every six months and adjust or pause if needed.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this something he'll have forever?\"","r":"\"For many children the restlessness eases in the teenage years, while the concentration side tends to hang on longer. Some adults still need support. We'll review him regularly and plan for each stage rather than guess now.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Honestly, I was exactly like him at school. I still lose my keys every day.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that's useful, because ADHD often runs in families. If it's affecting you now, your GP can refer you for your own assessment. Understanding it in yourself can make it easier to help him.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't you just give him something today so school stop phoning me?\"","r":"\"I understand how worn down the calls make you. Starting medicine before we're sure of the diagnosis, and without his heart checks, wouldn't be safe. What I can do today is write to school so they put support in now.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Baseline before any stimulant: height and weight plotted on growth centiles, pulse and BP plotted against age norms, cardiac history and family history of sudden death; ECG only if indicated","If medication follows diagnosis: methylphenidate first line for 5+; immediate release 5 mg once or twice daily (breakfast and lunch), or modified release 18 mg each morning; titrate weekly; usual max 54 mg MR","Give with or after breakfast; avoid late-afternoon doses to protect sleep; warn about reduced appetite, poor sleep, headache, stomach ache, irritability","If a 6-week trial at adequate dose fails: switch to an amfetamine-class stimulant (specialist decision)","GP hearing and vision check now; sleep history for snoring or apnoea"],"psych":["ADHD-focused group parent-training programme via CAMHS or local family service; partner invited","Psychoeducation to mother and child, with written NHS information on ADHD","Screen for co-occurring autism, tics, dyslexia and low self-esteem at the full assessment"],"social":["SENCO: classroom adjustments now (seat near teacher, chunked instructions, movement breaks) without waiting for diagnosis","Teacher rating scale returned before review, with parental consent","EHC needs assessment request by parent or school if SEN support is not enough"],"safety":["Ask directly about physical punishment and stress at home; safeguarding referral if harm suspected","Road and household safety advice for impulsive behaviour","Review with the child present in 4 weeks; shared care with GP only once dose is stable"]},"facts":[{"h":"Games don't test attention","x":"Fast-reward screens hold any child. Ask about boring, effortful tasks: homework, dressing, listening at the table. That is where ADHD shows."},{"h":"Two settings, before twelve","x":"Symptoms must be present before age 12, impair functioning and appear in at least two settings. One setting only: look for a cause in that setting."},{"h":"Stimulants: morning, with food","x":"Give methylphenidate with or after breakfast and allow a big evening meal. Late doses cause insomnia. Weigh at 3 and 6 months, then 6-monthly; height 6-monthly."},{"h":"Pulse and BP at every change","x":"Check before starting, after every dose change and every 6 months. Resting pulse over 120, arrhythmia, or systolic BP above the 95th centile on two readings: reduce dose and seek paediatric advice."},{"h":"Schedule 2: 30-day scripts","x":"Methylphenidate and the amfetamine-class stimulants are Schedule 2 controlled drugs. Supply is usually limited to 30 days; scripts need dose, form, strength and total in words and figures."}],"type":"Collateral history + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Take an ADHD collateral history from the mother, then explain the likely diagnosis and medication monitoring."},"c-5-1-2":{"obj":["Taken a developmental history covering first words, pointing and sharing, response to name, pretend play, routines, sensory responses and any loss of skills","Screened hearing, seizures, sleep, feeding, family history and early adversity, and documented each","Explained autism to the father as a lifelong difference in communication and experience, without blame or doom, and checked understanding in chunks","Agreed concrete next steps: place on the autism assessment pathway, school information request, hearing check, and supports that start now","Corrected any cure or diet myth kindly and given one trusted information source"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"Her brother did all this and he's fine now. She'll grow out of it.\"","r":"\"I'm glad her brother is doing well. Some children do settle, but her school has noticed real struggles, and autism often runs in families. Assessing her doesn't label her for life; it gets her the right help sooner.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will she ever have friends, a job, a normal life?\"","r":"\"That's the question every parent asks. Many autistic people have friends, careers and families. What helps most is people understanding how she thinks. I can't predict her future today, but support now makes a real difference.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Her mum thinks it's because we split up when she was two.\"","r":"\"Separation is hard on children, and it's natural to wonder. Autism isn't caused by family break-up or parenting; it's about how the brain develops from very early on. We'll still ask about her early years so we see the whole picture.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there a tablet that treats autism?\"","r":"\"No medicine treats autism itself. We sometimes use medicine for things that come with it, like poor sleep or severe distress. Most help is practical: communication support, predictable routines and adjustments at school.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Someone online said a gluten-free diet and supplements cured their son.\"","r":"\"I understand wanting to try anything that might help. Good research hasn't shown diets or supplements change autism, and restrictive diets can harm growth. If eating is a problem, let's get a dietitian's view instead.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm doing this on my own most days. I'm exhausted.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Looking after her alone with meltdowns every day is hard. You're able to ask for a carer's assessment from the council, and I'll give you details of local parent groups today.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GP referral for audiology now; paediatric review if any skill loss, seizures, staring spells or abnormal head growth","Sleep: sleep diary and bedtime routine first; if persistent, specialist may start prolonged-release melatonin 2 mg 30 to 60 minutes before bed, up to 5 mg, max 10 mg","Feeding: check range of foods, weight and height; dietitian if very restricted"],"psych":["Autism diagnostic assessment by the multidisciplinary team: history, observation, often a structured tool, school information","Social-communication intervention involving parents and teachers (play-based, speech and language therapy)","Screen at assessment for ADHD, anxiety and learning disability"],"social":["SENCO: visual timetable, warning before changes, quiet space; no diagnosis needed","Carer's assessment from the local authority for the father","Signpost to the local offer, parent groups and the National Autistic Society"],"safety":["Ask about early adversity and harm; consider attachment difficulties","Advise on wandering risk and road safety if she bolts during meltdowns","Written summary to father, GP and SENCO; review after assessment feedback"]},"facts":[{"h":"Support doesn't wait for diagnosis","x":"SEN support and an EHC needs assessment can be requested on need alone. Tell parents this on day one so school acts while the assessment runs."},{"h":"Assessment should start within 3 months","x":"Autism diagnostic assessment should begin within 3 months of referral. Never promise a date; say what the team does and who will contact them."},{"h":"Lost skills means paediatric review","x":"Loss of words or skills is not typical autism. Ask the paediatrician to assess for hearing loss, epilepsy or neurological causes."},{"h":"Girls mask","x":"Girls often copy peers and hold it together at school, then fall apart at home. Ask about exhaustion and meltdowns after school, not only behaviour in class."},{"h":"Melatonin: start 2 mg","x":"Prolonged-release melatonin for autistic children: 2 mg 30 to 60 minutes before bed, can rise to 5 mg, max 10 mg. Only after sleep routine changes fail."}],"type":"Collateral history + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Take a developmental autism history from the father, then explain autism and next steps."},"c-5-1-3":{"obj":["Established his own baseline from the mother and school records and pinned down what is new in the last 3 months, with dates","Separated autistic traits (scripting, literal thinking, social withdrawal) from psychotic symptoms using concrete, literal questions","Screened for catatonia, drugs, depression and physical causes, and ordered the bloods, drug screen and ECG","Explained the reasoning to his mother without dismissing her view, and agreed a low-and-slow treatment plan","Confirmed his capacity for informal admission and checked a C(E)TR has been held"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"He's always talked to himself. This is just his autism. You lot want to drug him.\"","r":"\"You know him better than anyone, so I need your help. Has he always answered back as if someone is speaking to him, or is that new? If it's new, it may be an illness we can treat.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"The newsreader says my name. Not out loud. It's in the way she looks at the camera.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me exactly what happens. That must feel strange. When she looks at the camera like that, what do you think it means? Does it happen with other programmes too?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to talk any more. Too many questions.\"","r":"\"That's fine. You've done well. Would it be easier to write your answers, or to stop now and finish tomorrow at the same time? You choose.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If he takes tablets, will he become a different person?\"","r":"\"That's an important worry. The aim is to calm the frightening experiences, not change who he is. Autistic young people can be sensitive to side effects, so we'd start very low, go slowly and check with you both often.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can someone be autistic and have schizophrenia too?\"","r":"\"Yes, both can happen together. Autism doesn't protect against psychosis. That's why we compare him with his own usual self rather than with other teenagers.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Nurse: \"He's barely eating and just stands by the window for ages. Is that the autism too?\"","r":"\"Good that you flagged it. Standing still for long periods and not eating could be catatonia, which is serious but treatable. Please record food and fluid intake, and I'll do a catatonia rating now and tell the consultant.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, glucose or HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, CK; urine toxicology; ECG; EEG or MRI if neurological signs","Catatonia rating (Bush-Francis) on admission and daily; if positive, consultant considers lorazepam challenge and holds the antipsychotic","If psychosis confirmed and agreed: aripiprazole 2 mg daily for 2 days, then 5 mg for 2 days, then 10 mg; increase slowly only if needed","Antipsychotic monitoring: weight weekly for 6 weeks, then at 12 weeks; BP, pulse, glucose, lipids, prolactin at 12 weeks, then 6-monthly"],"psych":["Communication passport with speech and language therapist or autism practitioner: literal questions, extra time, written options","CBT for psychosis adapted for autism, delivered by the psychology team once settled","Family intervention with mother, involving the ward family therapist"],"social":["Sensory adjustments on the ward: single room, low noise, predictable timetable, named nurse each shift","Hospital school continues education; school sends work","C(E)TR and dynamic support register checked by the ward's learning disability and autism lead"],"safety":["Food and fluid chart; daily weight while intake is low","Observation level set by MDT for withdrawal and risk of absconding","Mother told who to contact; review at MDT within the week"]},"facts":[{"h":"Compare with him, not others","x":"The test is change from his own baseline. Ask mother and school for a concrete picture of 6 to 12 months ago: school, self-care, interests, sleep."},{"h":"Scripting is not voices","x":"Autistic self-talk repeats phrases or rehearses conversations. Answering back, pausing to listen, or describing a voice from outside points to hallucinations."},{"h":"Catatonia hides in autism","x":"New slowing, freezing, mutism or stopping eating in an autistic teenager may be catatonia. Rate it before blaming autism, and before raising an antipsychotic."},{"h":"Start low in autism","x":"Autistic young people often get more side effects. Start at the bottom of the range, titrate slowly and check for akathisia, sedation and weight gain."},{"h":"His capacity governs admission","x":"At 16, if he has capacity, his consent or refusal of informal admission stands. His mother cannot override it either way."}],"type":"Mental state examination","do":"Assess for new psychotic symptoms against his autistic baseline, and explain your reasoning to nurse and mother."},"c-5-2-1":{"obj":["Confirmed with A&E that ingestion time, paracetamol level, LFTs and INR are done and acetylcysteine is running as TOXBASE advises","Explained confidentiality and its limits before she disclosed anything, and promised to tell her first if anything must be shared","Covered the overdose before, during and after, plus mood, self-harm, intent now, harm from others online and at home","Agreed with her exactly what her mother will be told, and by whom","Written up a safety plan jointly with her and her mother, covering locked-away medicines, and a dated CAMHS follow-up"],"snip":[{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If I tell you everything, will you promise not to tell my mum?\"","r":"\"I can't promise everything stays secret, and I don't want to trick you. Most of what you say stays private. If I need to share something to keep you safe, I'll tell you first and we'll decide how together.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I feel completely fine now. Can I just go home?\"","r":"\"I'm glad you feel OK. Paracetamol can damage the liver quietly for a day or two before you feel ill, so you need to stay until the blood tests and treatment are finished. While we wait, can we talk about tonight?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"They made a group chat about me. Everyone was laughing. I just wanted it to stop.\"","r":"\"That sounds humiliating and lonely. No wonder you wanted it to stop. When you took the tablets, did you want to die, or to escape the feeling for a while? Either answer is OK to tell me.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"Mother: \"Why didn't you tell me? Who are these girls? I'm going straight to the school.\"","r":"\"Of course you're upset and want to protect her. What she needs most tonight is to see that you're calm and listening. Let's agree together how the school is contacted, so she doesn't feel things are being done behind her back.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Mum will take my phone off me and then I'll have nobody.\"","r":"\"Your friends online matter a lot. Let's ask Mum together about a plan that keeps you safe without cutting you off: maybe muting the group, saving the messages, and keeping the people who are kind.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Am I going to get sectioned now?\"","r":"\"No, that isn't what usually happens. Tonight you'll stay on the children's ward to finish treatment, and a CAMHS worker will see you again before you go home to make a plan with you.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Paracetamol level at 4 h or later; LFTs, INR, U&E, glucose, venous gas; treat if above the 100 mg/L line at 4 h, or straight away if timing is staggered or unknown","IV acetylcysteine by weight as TOXBASE and the trust protocol direct; repeat LFTs, INR and creatinine before medical discharge","If moderate to severe depression after review: psychological therapy first; fluoxetine 10 mg daily only by CAMHS alongside therapy"],"psych":["CAMHS psychosocial assessment before discharge: needs, triggers, intent, what protects her; no risk scoring","Follow-up with CAMHS for mood; CBT or IPT-A if depression confirmed","Teach a short distress plan: who to message, Childline 0800 1111, Shout text 85258"],"social":["With her consent, school designated safeguarding lead and head of year told about the bullying","Save screenshots; report accounts on the platform; police if threats","Mother given NHS and Young Minds parent information"],"safety":["Overnight on paediatric ward with nursing awareness of risk","All medicines at home locked away or removed; family told before discharge","Safeguarding referral to children's social care if exploitation, abuse or neglect emerges","Dated CAMHS appointment given before she leaves; GP letter same day"]},"facts":[{"h":"One treatment line: 100 mg/L","x":"UK nomogram uses 100 mg/L at 4 hours for everyone. Levels before 4 h are uninterpretable. Staggered or unknown timing: start acetylcysteine without waiting."},{"h":"Feeling well proves nothing","x":"Paracetamol liver injury usually shows at 24 to 72 hours. Early nausea may be the only sign. Never accept 'she looks fine' as a discharge reason."},{"h":"Ask about hurting the bullies","x":"After peer bullying, ask about thoughts of harming the people involved, as well as self-harm. Also ask whether a teacher was told and what happened."},{"h":"No risk scores","x":"Do not use risk tools to decide discharge or treatment after self-harm. Record needs, intent, triggers and what keeps her safe, in your words."},{"h":"Report Remove for images","x":"Under-18s can ask Childline and the Internet Watch Foundation to take down nude images through Report Remove. Online sexual threats go to CEOP or police and a safeguarding referral."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Ensure paracetamol treatment is safe, assess risk and reasons for the overdose, and handle her confidentiality request."},"c-5-2-2":{"obj":["Built a dated timeline from first change to now, covering prodrome, delusions, hallucinations, passivity and functional decline","Asked about cannabis and other drugs, head injury, seizures, recent illness and family history of psychosis or bipolar disorder","Assessed risk to himself, to other people and from other people, including exploitation","Explained first-episode psychosis in plain words to the boy and his father without labelling schizophrenia, and answered the hospital question honestly","Agreed an urgent EIP referral, a physical baseline and a crisis plan with numbers, written down"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not ill. There's nothing wrong with my head. It's the phone.\"","r":"\"I'm not asking you to agree it's an illness. You've been frightened and hardly sleeping for weeks, and that's real. Would you let us help you feel safer and sleep better, even while we disagree about why?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"Father: \"Is he going to be locked up? Is he dangerous?\"","r":"\"I can see how scared you are. Most young people with psychosis are treated at home by the early intervention team. People with psychosis are far more often frightened than dangerous. Hospital is only if home can't keep him safe.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I smoke a bit with the older lads. Helps me sleep. They don't charge me.\"","r":"\"Thanks for being honest. Cannabis, especially the strong stuff, can make these experiences worse. I'm also a bit worried about why older lads give it free. Can you tell me more about them?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"Father: \"I thought he was just being a moody teenager. I should have seen it.\"","r":"\"Early psychosis looks a lot like normal teenage withdrawal; most parents miss it at first. You noticed the foil and brought him in. That's exactly what helps. What matters now is starting treatment.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Father: \"Will the medicine turn him into a zombie?\"","r":"\"That's a common fear. We start with a low dose, choose a medicine together with you both, and adjust if he feels slowed or drowsy. We check weight, bloods and how he feels at every review.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"If I leave my room, they'll get me.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying. You're safe in this room with us right now. Can we work out together what would make it feel safer at home this week, and who you can call if it gets too much?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Baseline: weight, height, BMI, waist, pulse, BP, HbA1c or fasting glucose, lipid profile, prolactin, full blood count, renal, liver and thyroid function, urine toxicology; ECG when indicated; MRI or neurology opinion for atypical features","Antipsychotic chosen with him and father; start at lowest dose (aripiprazole is licensed from 15, so use at 14 is a specialist off-label decision) and titrate slowly","Trial at optimal dose for 6 weeks before judging response","Monitor weight weekly for 6 weeks, then at 12 weeks; pulse, BP, glucose, lipids, prolactin at 12 weeks, then 6-monthly"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis, at least 16 planned sessions, from the EIP psychologist","Family intervention with father (and mother if involved), about 10 or more sessions over 3 to 12 months","Substance-use support from the young people's drug and alcohol service"],"social":["EIP care coordinator named within 2 weeks; home visits","School contacted with consent: reduced timetable, home tuition if needed","Safeguarding lead consulted if older peers are supplying drugs (possible criminal exploitation)"],"safety":["Crisis plan written down: local CAMHS crisis number, 111 (mental health option), 999 for immediate danger","Remove means of self-harm at home; father told warning signs","Tier 4 bed via provider collaborative only if home is unsafe; review within 1 week"]},"facts":[{"h":"Two-week EIP start","x":"First-episode psychosis should start a NICE-recommended care package within 2 weeks of referral. Refer the same day; don't wait for a diagnosis."},{"h":"Don't say schizophrenia yet","x":"Use 'first episode of psychosis'. Drugs, mood disorder and organic causes are still on the list, and a label can stick wrongly."},{"h":"Free cannabis is a red flag","x":"Older peers giving drugs free to a young teenager suggests grooming or county lines. Discuss with the safeguarding lead and consider a referral."},{"h":"Six weeks at the right dose","x":"Judge an antipsychotic after 6 weeks at an adequate dose, taken reliably. Check adherence before calling it a failure."},{"h":"Weight weekly at first","x":"Young people gain weight fast on antipsychotics. Weigh weekly for 6 weeks, then at 12 weeks. Give diet and exercise advice from the start."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Take an early-onset psychosis history and explain an initial management plan to him and his father."},"c-5-2-3":{"obj":["Elicited the core manic features (sleep, speech, ideas, grandiosity, spending, risk-taking) with collateral from the mother and dated onset","Identified online and sexual exploitation risk by asking who she has messaged and whether anyone has asked to meet or for images","Reviewed the antidepressant, drug use and physical causes, and got a pregnancy test before any medicine","Documented her capacity and the lawful authority for holding her on the ward overnight, with a 1:1 nursing plan","Escalated to the on-call CAMHS consultant and explained the plan to her and her mother in plain words"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I've never felt better in my life. You're just jealous I've got a plan.\"","r":"\"I can see you feel brilliant and full of ideas. I'm not trying to take that away. But four nights without sleep wears out anyone's body. Let's get you rested first, then talk about the business.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm 16. You can't keep me here. I'm leaving.\"","r":"\"You're right that your views count a lot at 16. I'm worried that right now you may not be able to weigh up the risks. Please stay while the consultant sees you; I'll explain exactly what we can and can't do.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've met an amazing guy online. He's 24. He's going to pick me up tomorrow.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you told me. A 24-year-old arranging to collect a 16-year-old he's only met online worries me, and I'd say that to anyone. Can you show me the messages so we can check you're safe?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"Mother: \"I pushed for the antidepressant. Did I cause this?\"","r":"\"You were trying to help her when she was very low. Antidepressants can sometimes trigger mania in people already prone to it; no one could know that beforehand. We'll review it tonight with the consultant.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Mother: \"Does this mean she has bipolar disorder for life?\"","r":"\"It's too early to say. A manic episode after depression raises that possibility, so we'll follow her closely. Many young people with bipolar disorder do well with treatment and a good relapse plan.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Stop following me! I don't need a babysitter.\"","r":"\"I know it's irritating to have someone with you all the time. The nurse is here because you've had no sleep and things feel very fast. As soon as you're calmer, we'll step it down.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, calcium, glucose, CRP; urine toxicology; pregnancy test; ECG","Stop the antidepressant with consultant agreement","If agreed by consultant: aripiprazole 2 mg daily for 2 days, 5 mg for 2 days, then 10 mg once daily (licensed 13+, up to 12 weeks); watch for akathisia","Short-term lorazepam for severe agitation or no sleep, per consultant; avoid valproate"],"psych":["Psychoeducation for her and her mother once settled: early warning signs, sleep protection","Relapse-prevention plan and family support from the CAMHS team"],"social":["Phone kept with nursing staff if she agrees; online contacts reviewed","School told of absence with consent; hospital school if prolonged","Mother given carer information and contact numbers"],"safety":["1:1 nursing with the paediatric nurse in charge; doors and exits plan","Capacity assessment documented; if she lacks capacity and tries to leave, consultant considers s5(2) or MHA assessment via AMHP","Safeguarding referral and police if grooming suspected","CAMHS consultant review next morning; Tier 4 bed request if needed"]},"facts":[{"h":"Valproate: two specialists","x":"Valproate is not started in patients below 55 unless two specialists independently document that nothing else is suitable. In girls, a pregnancy prevention programme applies too."},{"h":"Aripiprazole: 12-week licence","x":"Licensed for mania from age 13: 2 mg for 2 days, 5 mg for 2 days, then 10 mg daily, for up to 12 weeks. Higher doses add side effects, not benefit."},{"h":"Parents can't authorise detention","x":"Parents cannot consent to a 16 or 17-year-old being deprived of liberty. If she lacks capacity and must stay, use the MHA or seek legal advice."},{"h":"s5(2) works on paediatric wards","x":"An admitted inpatient on any hospital ward can be held under s5(2) by the doctor in charge or their nominee, for up to 72 hours. Not in A&E."},{"h":"Ask about online contact","x":"Disinhibited teenagers are easy targets. Ask who she messages, whether anyone asked for photos or to meet, and look at the messages with her consent."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Assess possible mania in a 16-year-old, make her safe tonight, and explain initial management."},"c-5-3-1":{"obj":["Engaged the 10-year-old girl without asking her to speak, using nodding, pointing and drawing, and got her view on mornings and school","Mapped where she does and doesn't speak, morning symptoms vs weekends, and triggers at school and at home, including bullying and harm","Ruled in or out hearing loss, language disorder, autism and English as an additional language, with hearing and speech and language checks requested","Explained anxiety and avoidance to the mother in plain words, and why staying home makes it worse","Agreed by phone with the SENCO a graded return starting within days, a key adult and no-pressure speaking rules, and booked a 4-week review"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Mother: \"The tummy aches are real. You're saying she's making it up?\"","r":"\"No, the pain is completely real. Worry releases stress chemicals that really do cause stomach pain and sickness. That's why it's worst on school mornings and gone at weekends. Treating the worry treats the pain.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"Mother: \"The school says they'll fine us. I can't afford that on top of everything.\"","r":"\"That's a lot of pressure. I'll write to the school today explaining that this is an anxiety condition with a treatment plan. That usually moves them from fines towards an attendance support plan. Please bring any letters to our review.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"Mother: \"Can you sign her off school until she's ready to go back?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting to protect her from the upset. The trouble is that 'ready' rarely comes while she's at home: each day off makes the next one harder. A note supporting a small, planned return will help her more.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"Child (whispering to Mum): \"I don't want to go. Everyone will stare.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling Mum. Lots of children feel exactly like that. You won't have to do it all at once, and no one will make you talk. Shall we draw what the first tiny step could look like?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Mother: \"Should the teacher just encourage her to speak? Maybe a reward?\"","r":"\"Pressure usually makes the voice stick harder. It works better if the teacher takes away the expectation, accepts nods or cards, and doesn't make a fuss when she does speak. We build speaking up in tiny steps.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"Mother: \"I was the same as a child. I still panic in crowds.\"","r":"\"Thank you for sharing that. Anxiety often runs in families, and you'll understand her better than most. It's worth seeing your GP for yourself too: when parents get help with their own anxiety, children often improve faster.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Hearing test via GP or school nurse; speech and language therapy view","No routine medication for childhood anxiety or selective mutism; any SSRI is a specialist decision only after therapy fails","Simple sleep and morning routine: same wake time on all days, breakfast before the school run"],"psych":["Parent-led CBT with graded exposure, from the MHST or CAMHS psychology","Selective mutism: graded 'sliding-in' of new speakers by a key adult, starting with the mother in an empty classroom","Screen for low mood and separation anxiety at review"],"social":["SENCO: graded return starting within days (gate, then a short morning), key adult, quiet base, non-verbal answers allowed","Clinic letter to school attendance lead requesting an attendance support plan instead of a penalty","SMIRA resources for the family and school"],"safety":["Ask about bullying, low mood, self-harm and harm at home; safeguarding referral if concerns","Consent recorded for sharing with school and GP","Review in 4 weeks with attendance figures"]},"facts":[{"h":"Days off feed the fear","x":"Every day at home rewards avoidance, so return starts within days, not after the anxiety settles. A partial day beats a perfect plan next month."},{"h":"Weekend test","x":"Pains that vanish at weekends and in holidays point to anxiety. Pains every day, night waking, weight loss or blood in stools need a GP check."},{"h":"Don't push her to speak","x":"Direct questions in front of others make selective mutism worse. Allow nodding, pointing or cards, and don't make a fuss when she speaks."},{"h":"15 days triggers council duty","x":"If health needs will keep a child out for 15 school days or more, the local authority must arrange suitable education. Mention it if the return stalls."},{"h":"Letters, not sick notes","x":"A clinic letter explaining the diagnosis and the graded plan helps the school support her. Open-ended 'unfit for school' notes entrench absence."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Assess school refusal and selective mutism, explain the formulation, and agree a plan with mother and school."},"c-5-3-2":{"obj":["Taken a before-during-after account of meltdowns across home, school and other adults, and found what already works","Screened for ADHD, autism, language disorder, learning difficulty and hearing problems, and sent the SDQ to parent and teacher","Asked the mother directly about violence at home and her own mood, and acted on the answer","Explained why there is no tablet for this, and what a parenting group involves (sessions, length, who goes), without blame","Agreed a referral to a group parenting programme with the practical barriers solved, and a review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Please, just give him something to calm him down. I can't cope another week.\"","r":"\"I can hear how exhausted you are. Calming tablets would sedate him without changing the behaviour, and carry side effects. A parenting group works better. Let's also find you some support this week while you wait.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"So you're saying it's my fault. I'm a bad mum.\"","r":"\"No. Some children are much harder to parent than others, and you've kept going on your own. The group teaches extra tools for children like him. Parents who go aren't failing; they're doing more than most.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not sitting in a room with other parents judging me.\"","r":"\"Lots of parents feel that before the first session. Most say afterwards that meeting others in the same boat was the best part. If a group truly isn't possible, an individual programme exists. Would you try the first session?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"His teacher says he might have ADHD. Could that be it?\"","r":"\"It could be part of it, and we'll check properly. I'll send rating forms to you and school. If he has ADHD, treating it often makes the behaviour easier, and parenting strategies still help either way.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"His dad gets angry. He pushed me last week. My son saw it.\"","r":"\"Thank you for trusting me. That's not your fault, and it matters for you both. Are you safe to go home today? I'd like to talk about getting you support and what needs to happen to keep you and him safe.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"The school just keep excluding him. They've given up on him.\"","r":"\"That's really frustrating, and exclusions often make things worse. With your consent I'll write to the school asking the SENCO to review his needs and draw up a plan for behaviour support, and I'll mention an EHC needs assessment if his needs are high.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No routine medication for conduct problems","Hearing test via GP; speech and language therapy assessment","If ADHD is confirmed: treat it under ADHD guidance alongside parent training","Risperidone only by a specialist, for severe aggression after psychosocial treatment fails; under 50 kg start 0.25 mg once daily; licensed short-term (up to 6 weeks)"],"psych":["Group parent-training programme (Incredible Years or Triple P type): 10 to 16 weekly sessions of 90 to 120 minutes","SDQ and ADHD rating scales from parent and teacher before review","Mother to GP for her own mood and sleep"],"social":["Referral to early help or the Family Hub for practical support","School: SENCO review, behaviour support plan, consider an EHC needs assessment","Solve attendance barriers for the group: crèche, transport, time of day"],"safety":["Ask about domestic abuse every time; if disclosed, DASH risk check and MARAC referral if high risk","Children's social care referral if the child witnesses abuse or is harmed","Supervisor told the same day of any disclosure; review in 6 to 8 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Parent groups first, ages 3 to 11","x":"Offer a group parent-training programme: 10 to 16 weekly sessions of 90 to 120 minutes, about 10 to 12 parents. Ask about crèche and transport, or they won't come."},{"h":"No routine drugs for behaviour","x":"Don't prescribe for conduct problems routinely. Risperidone is a short-term specialist option for severe aggression only after psychosocial help fails."},{"h":"Witnessing abuse counts","x":"A child who sees or hears domestic abuse is a victim in their own right. Ask the mother alone; a disclosure needs a safeguarding response the same day."},{"h":"Before, during, after","x":"Ask what happened just before each meltdown, what he did, and what adults did next. Patterns of triggers and accidental rewards guide the plan."},{"h":"Domestic abuse helpline","x":"National Domestic Abuse Helpline, 0808 2000 247, free and open 24 hours. Give the number discreetly and check it's safe for her to keep it."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a treatment","do":"Assess the conduct problems, screen for other causes, and explain parent training as first-line treatment."},"c-5-3-3":{"obj":["Assessed physical risk by percentage median BMI, lying and standing pulse and BP, temperature, ECG with QTc, bloods and fluid intake, not raw BMI","Asked about purging, laxatives, exercise, fluid restriction, periods, and self-harm and suicidal thoughts","Decided she cannot go home tonight and secured paediatric admission with a clear handover","Explained to the girl and her parents why admission is needed and what family therapy for anorexia involves, without blame","Ensured a refeeding plan with daily phosphate, magnesium and potassium, and a named eating disorder link for the ward"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not even thin. I fainted because it was hot in the hall.\"","r":"\"I hear that you don't feel thin, and that's part of what this illness does. But your heart is beating 42 times a minute and you nearly passed out standing up. Those numbers worry me, whatever your size.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"Parents: \"Can't we take her home and bring her back Monday? We'll make sure she eats.\"","r":"\"I can tell how much you want to look after her yourselves. Her heart rate and fainting mean she could collapse or develop a heart rhythm problem over the weekend. She needs monitoring in hospital tonight; you'll be with her.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I go in, they'll make me eat everything and I'll get fat.\"","r":"\"That sounds really scary. The aim is to make your body safe, not to make you fat. A dietitian will plan meals carefully and increase them step by step, and we'll check your blood every day while that happens.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"Mother: \"I've always been on diets. Did I give her this?\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest. Eating disorders have many causes, including genes and temperament; no parent causes them alone. What we know is that parents are the most powerful part of recovery, so you're exactly who she needs.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Father: \"What is family therapy? Are we on trial?\"","r":"\"Not at all. It's the best-proven treatment for teenagers with anorexia. For a while, you take charge of meals at home with our team coaching you, then hand control back to her as she recovers. It usually runs about a year.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I won't go to the children's ward. You can't force me.\"","r":"\"I understand you don't want this. Right now your body is in danger, so I can't agree to you going home. Let's talk about what would make the ward easier, and I'll ask the consultant to explain your choices too.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["ECG with QTc; lying and standing pulse and BP; temperature; sit-up-squat-stand test; bloods: FBC, U&E, phosphate, magnesium, calcium, glucose, LFT, TFT, coeliac screen","Paediatric admission tonight; dietitian refeeding plan; thiamine and multivitamin before first feeds per protocol","Phosphate, magnesium, potassium and glucose daily for the first days; replace as advised rather than cutting calories","Bone density scan if underweight for a year or more"],"psych":["Family therapy for anorexia nervosa (FT-AN), 18 to 20 sessions over a year, by the community eating disorders service once medically stable","Meal support on the ward by trained nurses; parents involved at meals","Screen for depression, OCD, autism and self-harm"],"social":["School told of absence with consent; hospital school during admission","Parents signposted to Beat carer support","Sibling and family support needs discussed by the family therapist"],"safety":["Nursing observations per paediatric protocol; bathroom supervision after meals if purging suspected","If she refuses admission: consultant reviews; consider parental consent within PR or MHA","Daily CEDS liaison review; consultant informed tonight"]},"facts":[{"h":"Use percentage median BMI","x":"Adult BMI cut-offs mislead in children. Calculate BMI as a percentage of the median for age and sex: below 70% is high risk, 70 to 80% is moderate."},{"h":"Syncope plus bradycardia: admit","x":"Fainting, a resting pulse in the low 40s and postural dizziness together mean same-day paediatric admission. Normal bloods do not make her safe."},{"h":"Don't underfeed","x":"Starting too low prolongs danger and is now a commoner harm than refeeding syndrome. Feed as the dietitian plans and correct phosphate rather than cutting calories."},{"h":"Refeeding: watch phosphate","x":"Phosphate, magnesium and potassium drop in the first days of feeding. Check daily at first; breathlessness, oedema or confusion means urgent paediatric review."},{"h":"Family therapy: about a year","x":"FT-AN is 18 to 20 sessions over a year. Parents take charge of meals first, then gradually hand control back."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Explain a treatment","do":"Assess her medical risk with MEED, decide tonight's setting, and explain family-based treatment."},"c-5-4-1":{"obj":["Received the disclosure calmly with open, non-leading questions, and recorded her words verbatim with times","Told her kindly why the information must be shared, with whom, and what happens next","Checked immediate safety: where the stepfather is, other children at home, and self-harm or suicidal thoughts tonight","Phoned children's social care out of hours and the police the same night, with reference numbers logged","Arranged paediatric admission as a place of safety and told both consultants, without alerting the mother early"],"snip":[{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Please don't tell anyone. I'll say I made it up.\"","r":"\"I can hear how frightened you are. I can't keep this secret, because it's about your safety. I'll only tell the people whose job is to protect you, and I'll tell you each step before it happens.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"He'll kill me if he finds out I told.\"","r":"\"That sounds really frightening. You won't be going home tonight; you'll stay here on the children's ward where staff are with you. The social workers and police know how to keep young people safe in exactly this situation.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Mum's going to hate me. I'll wreck the family.\"","r":"\"You haven't wrecked anything. What's happened is not your fault, and telling was brave. Mums often feel shocked at first. The social worker will help decide how and when she's told, with support for her too.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I just want to disappear. I can't do this.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me how bad it feels. Are you thinking of hurting yourself or ending your life tonight? You don't have to manage this alone; a nurse will stay close, and I'll check on you again before I go.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I have to tell the police everything? I don't want to say it again.\"","r":"\"That's a fair worry. You don't need to tell me any more details tonight. Specially trained officers would talk with you later, in a calm room, at your pace, with someone you trust nearby.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Mother: \"Why can't I take her home? What's going on? I'm her mother.\"","r":"\"I understand this is confusing and upsetting. I can't share everything yet, but she needs to stay on the children's ward tonight. A social worker will speak with you as soon as possible to explain more.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Wound care for the cuts by A&E; tetanus status checked","Paediatric admission tonight as a place of safety","If contact may be recent: police and social care decide on a paediatric SARC referral for forensic, emergency contraception and infection care"],"psych":["Brief risk review tonight; full CAMHS assessment on the ward next day","No detailed questioning: police and social workers interview under ABE","Ongoing therapeutic support via CAMHS once the strategy plan is set"],"social":["Emergency Duty Team phoned now; written MASH referral to follow within the timeframe local policy sets (often 48 hours)","Police informed: 999 if immediate danger, otherwise as social care advise","Strategy discussion led by social care with police and health; siblings' safety included"],"safety":["Stepfather not to visit; ward staff told who may and may not visit","Mother not told details until social care advise","On-call paediatric and CAMHS consultants told tonight; named doctor or nurse for safeguarding next working day","Record verbatim words, times, who told, reference numbers; safeguarding form completed"]},"facts":[{"h":"Never promise secrecy","x":"Say early that you can't keep safety information secret. Promising and then breaking it destroys trust and can make her retract."},{"h":"Listen, don't investigate","x":"Use open prompts like 'tell me more'. Leading or detailed questions can damage a later police case. Specially trained interviewers do that."},{"h":"Write her exact words","x":"Record her words in quotation marks, the time, who was present and what you asked. Note every call, who you spoke to and the reference number."},{"h":"Don't send her home tonight","x":"With an alleged abuser in the home, admit to the paediatric ward as a place of safety, however minor the self-harm."},{"h":"Retraction is common","x":"Many young people take back a disclosure under pressure or fear. Record it verbatim, tell social care, and don't change the plan yourself."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"Respond safely to the abuse disclosure, explain confidentiality limits, and start safeguarding tonight."},"c-5-4-2":{"obj":["Assessed Gillick competence for this one decision: understanding of purpose, benefits, side effects, alternatives, risk of no treatment, and free choice","Confirmed who holds parental responsibility, and documented that a competent 15-year-old's consent stands despite one parent objecting","Phoned the father the same day, heard his worries and offered a meeting, without breaching the boy's confidentiality","Agreed a fluoxetine plan with continued therapy, weekly contact for the first 4 weeks and a written safety plan","Recorded the competence assessment and consultant agreement in the notes"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"Father: \"I'm his father. I refuse permission. You can't give my son mind drugs.\"","r":"\"I can hear how strongly you feel, and your worries matter. Your son understands the benefits and risks and wants to try it, and at 15 the law lets him decide. Could we meet so I can explain the plan and how we'll keep watch?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"Father: \"I read these pills make kids suicidal.\"","r":"\"You're right that in the first weeks a small number of young people get more suicidal thoughts. That's why we see him every week, start low, and give him and his mum a clear plan for who to call. Untreated depression carries risks too.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I have to take it forever? Will I get addicted?\"","r":"\"It isn't addictive. If it helps, we'd usually continue for at least six months after you feel well, then reduce slowly together. Stopping suddenly can cause unpleasant symptoms, so please talk to us first.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I don't want Mum and Dad fighting about me again.\"","r":"\"That sounds hard; it isn't your job to fix their disagreement. You've made a thoughtful choice. I'll speak to your dad myself, and you decide how much he hears about what we talk about.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Father: \"I've got parental responsibility. I'm entitled to know everything he says.\"","r":"\"You do have parental responsibility, and I'd really like you involved. Because he's competent, he also has a right to confidentiality. I'll share what he agrees to, and I'll always tell you if I think he's unsafe.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I've done CBT for weeks and nothing's changed. Maybe I'm just broken.\"","r":"\"You're not broken. You stuck with therapy even when it was hard. Some people need medicine as well to lift the mood enough for therapy to work. We'll keep the therapy going alongside it.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Fluoxetine 10 mg each morning; increase to 20 mg after 1 week if needed; most young people stay at 20 mg","Warn: nausea, headache, sleep change, restlessness, and new or worse suicidal thoughts in the early weeks","Continue at least 6 months after remission, then taper over weeks; no St John's wort"],"psych":["Continue CBT with the same therapist alongside fluoxetine","Repeat mood rating (for instance PHQ-A or MFQ) at each review","Family session with both parents if he agrees"],"social":["Father phoned the same day by the doctor; joint or separate meeting offered","Trust legal team consulted if parents escalate or a court order exists","With consent, school pastoral lead aware for support"],"safety":["Weekly contact for the first 4 weeks, face to face or phone","Written safety plan: CAMHS duty line, NHS 111 mental health option, 999 in emergency; medicines kept by mother","Consultant agreement documented before first prescription"]},"facts":[{"h":"Gillick is decision-specific","x":"Assess competence for this medicine, now. Record what he understood about benefits, side effects, alternatives and no treatment, and that he chose freely."},{"h":"Fluoxetine: 10 mg then 20 mg","x":"Start 10 mg daily, increase to 20 mg after a week if needed. Take in the morning, as it can disturb sleep. Combine with psychological therapy."},{"h":"Watch the first 4 weeks","x":"Suicidal thoughts and agitation can rise early in treatment in young people. Arrange weekly contact and tell the family exactly what to watch for."},{"h":"Fraser is for contraception","x":"Fraser guidelines cover contraceptive advice to under-16s. For psychiatric treatment, use Gillick competence."},{"h":"A parent can't veto competence","x":"If an under-16 is Gillick competent and consents, a parent's objection does not override it. Consent from one person with PR is enough when the child lacks competence."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"Assess his Gillick competence, clarify parental responsibility, and agree a lawful, safe plan."},"c-5-4-3":{"obj":["Named a transition worker and recorded a written transition plan with dates, starting now, four months before 18","Sent referrals to adult ADHD and adult mental health services with a full summary, risk history and current methylphenidate dose","Secured a GP shared care agreement so methylphenidate continues without a gap","Asked her what she wants shared with her father after 18 and recorded her consent","Covered risk, driving, education and living plans, and gave adult crisis contacts and a relapse plan in writing"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"So on my birthday I just get dropped and lose my tablets?\"","r":"\"I understand why it feels like that. We won't discharge you until the adult team or your GP has taken over. We're starting now so your medicine continues without a gap and you meet the new team first.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"Father: \"The adult team says she's not ill enough. So she has to get worse first?\"","r":"\"That's a fair frustration. I'll ask for their decision in writing and discuss it with my consultant. Meanwhile, the GP and adult ADHD service will be in the plan so care doesn't stop.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to tell my uni I've got ADHD?\"","r":"\"It's your choice. Telling the disability service lets you get support like extra time or note-taking, and you may be able to apply for Disabled Students' Allowance. Telling them doesn't go on your academic record.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Father: \"I'm her dad. You can't shut me out just because she's 18.\"","r":"\"You're very important to her, and she's told me she'd like you included, and that's now in her notes. We'll need her agreement to share details. If you're ever worried about her safety, call us; we can always listen.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"When I move to uni, I'm worried I'll get low again and no one will notice.\"","r":"\"Thank you for saying that. Let's write down your early warning signs now and who to contact there. Registering with a GP near uni in the first week is key; I'll write a summary you can take.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"I'm learning to drive. Does the ADHD medicine matter?\"","r":"\"Good question. Taken as prescribed, it often helps concentration. You must tell the DVLA if your ADHD or mood affects safe driving, and never drive if you feel drowsy or unwell. We can talk through it together.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Methylphenidate continued at the current dose via GP shared care before transfer; no gaps","Monitoring in the handover letter: pulse and BP every 6 months and after dose changes; weight every 6 months","Annual specialist medication review by the adult ADHD service"],"psych":["Relapse-prevention plan for depression with early warning signs","Adult talking therapy (NHS Talking Therapies) referral if needed during the wait"],"social":["Named transition worker coordinates; joint CAMHS-adult meeting with her before transfer","If she may have care needs at 18, adult social care does a Care Act transition assessment","University disability service and Disabled Students' Allowance; EHC plan can continue to 25 in education"],"safety":["Adult crisis line and NHS 111 mental health option in her written plan","Self-harm and suicide risk reviewed at each appointment until transfer","Consent for sharing with father documented; written refusals escalated to consultant"]},"facts":[{"h":"Start transition early","x":"Plan transition from year 9 (13 to 14) for young people with ongoing needs. Starting weeks before 18 drives drop-out."},{"h":"Meet adult services before transfer","x":"The young person should meet an adult practitioner before transfer. A joint meeting with CAMHS present lowers the risk of disengagement."},{"h":"No gap in stimulant supply","x":"Methylphenidate is a Schedule 2 controlled drug, usually supplied 30 days at a time. Confirm who writes the next script before CAMHS steps back."},{"h":"Consent changes at 18","x":"Parents lose automatic access to information at 18. Record her wishes about sharing; you can always listen to her father without sharing."},{"h":"Get refusals in writing","x":"If an adult service declines, ask for reasons in writing, tell your consultant and request a professionals meeting. Never discharge into a gap."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Plan a joined-up transition to adult services covering medication handover and information sharing."},"c-6-1-1":{"obj":["Listed at least five named record sources requested (prison healthcare notes, probation reports, CPS papers, convictions list, family collateral) with a date to chase each","Drafted a formulation sentence linking his past wounding to psychosis, non-adherence and cannabis","Written three risk scenarios (repeat, worse case, best case), each with two named warning signs","Agreed with the psychologist a co-rating date before Friday's CPA review","Set out a management plan with a named owner and a review date for every item"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"'Why are you digging up stuff from ten years ago? I've done my time for that.'","r":"'That's fair to ask. The past matters only because it shows what happened when you were unwell. Knowing that helps us spot early signs and plan your leave safely. You'll see what I write.'"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"'I'm not a violent person. That was the illness, not me.'","r":"'I hear that, and it fits with what I've read: you were very unwell then. So the plan is mostly about keeping you well. What were the first signs, looking back, that things were slipping?'"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"'If you write I'm high risk, I'll never get out of here.'","r":"'This isn't a score that locks you in. It's a plan: what raises risk, what lowers it, and what you can do. Taking your depot and staying off cannabis are the parts that count most.'"},{"tag":"Question","p":"'Can I read what you write about me?'","r":"'Yes. I'll go through the draft with you before the CPA meeting, show you the evidence for each point, and write your own view alongside it.'"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"'I'm not talking about the offence. Read the file.'","r":"'Okay, we can leave the details for now. Can you tell me just one thing: what was going on in your head that week? That helps more than any file.'"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"'Sometimes the officer on the wing still looks at me like he's planning something.'","r":"'Thank you for telling me. That sounds frightening. Has that feeling come back more often lately? I'd like to let the nurses know, so they can check in with you when it happens.'"}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue his current depot at the dose that kept him well; check the depot card for missed or late doses since prison","If two antipsychotics have failed at adequate dose, raise clozapine: 12.5 mg once or twice on day 1, titrate to usual 200–450 mg/day; FBC weekly for 18 weeks","Urine drug screen on admission and after every leave; record cannabis and synthetic cannabinoids","Annual physical health check: weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids, ECG if on high-dose or combined antipsychotics"],"psych":["HCR-20 V3 co-rated by ward psychologist and you; formulation agreed at MDT","Substance misuse work (motivational interviewing then relapse prevention), ward psychology or substance misuse worker, weekly","Relapse signature work with his named nurse: he writes his own three early warning signs","Offence-focused work once stable, forensic psychology, typically 12+ months"],"social":["Ward social worker: check MAPPA level and victim issues with the offender manager","Social worker gathers family collateral (with his consent) and maps family support","Occupational therapist: structured daytime activity and vocational plan","RC prepares leave application to MoJ Mental Health Casework Section once plan is agreed"],"safety":["Observation level set by nurse in charge; review at each ward round","Named warning signs (missed depot, withdrawal, accusing staff) shared with all ward staff in the care plan","Restricted patient: leave and transfer need MoJ consent; inform MHCS of serious incidents","HCR-20 review every 6 months or sooner after any incident"]},"facts":[{"h":"Rate presence and relevance separately","x":"Each HCR-20 item is rated for whether it is present and whether it is relevant to his violence. A present but irrelevant item should not drive the plan."},{"h":"No total score","x":"HCR-20 V3 gives no cut-off. Summary judgement is low, moderate or high priority, with a separate view on serious physical harm and imminence."},{"h":"Scenarios make it usable","x":"Write at least: repeat (same as before), escalation (worse), and best case. For each, give the warning sign and who acts."},{"h":"Collateral beats interview","x":"Calm denial on the ward tells you little. Under-reporting is common; the convictions list and witness statements often tell a different story."},{"h":"Section 47/49 leave","x":"Restricted transferred prisoners need MoJ consent for any leave. If treatment is no longer needed, he may be remitted to prison rather than discharged."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Outline a structured HCR-20 violence risk assessment and a linked risk management plan."},"c-6-1-2":{"obj":["Elicited and documented the content of voices or delusions linked to the food refusal and the cellmate assault","Asked directly about suicide, self-harm and spice use, and recorded the answers","Agreed with prison staff a written plan: single cell, observation frequency, food and fluid chart, two-officer approach","Started the transfer process: secure unit referral sent today and second assessment booked","Arranged same-day physical checks (obs, U&E, CK) through the prison GP"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"'I'm not eating their food. They're putting stuff in it.'","r":"'That sounds really frightening. I'm not here to make you eat. Can we find something you'd trust, like sealed water or packet food? I need to keep your body safe while we sort this out.'"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"'You're just another screw in a different uniform.'","r":"'I get why you'd think that. I'm from the NHS mental health team, not the prison. My job is your health. Some things I may need to share for safety, and I'll tell you if I do.'"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"'He was working with them. The voices told me he'd kill me first.'","r":"'Thank you for telling me. That must have been terrifying. Are the voices saying anything like that now, about anyone here?'"},{"tag":"Question","p":"'Why can't you just give me an injection here and sort it?'","r":"'Mental health law doesn't let us force treatment in prison. I can offer medicine you choose to take today, and I'm asking for a hospital bed where you can get full treatment.'"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"'If I go to hospital I'll lose my court case.'","r":"'Going to hospital doesn't decide your case. Your solicitor and the court will be told you're getting treatment. Being well usually helps you take part properly.'"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"'There's nothing wrong with me. It's the food that's the problem.'","r":"'I hear that. You haven't eaten for two days, and that worries me whatever the cause. Can we check your blood and get you some water while we keep talking?'"}],"bps":{"bio":["Offer oral antipsychotic he agrees to: olanzapine 10 mg at night (range 5–20 mg), orodispersible if he will accept it; warn: sleepiness, weight gain","Same-day obs, U&E, creatinine, CK, glucose; ECG before or soon after starting","Food and fluid chart; prison GP reviews daily; send to acute hospital if rising creatinine, low BP or confusion","Urine drug screen including synthetic cannabinoids if he consents"],"psych":["In-reach CPN visits daily until transfer to build trust and monitor mental state","Explain each step to him before it happens; sealed or packaged food offer to reduce fear"],"social":["In-reach team requests transfer: secure unit admission team assessment within days","Second registered doctor (one s12-approved) for s48/49 recommendation; MoJ issues warrant","Liaison and diversion or solicitor told if court date is near","NHS England case manager chases bed if delay exceeds 28-day transfer standard"],"safety":["ACCT opened or reviewed; observations as agreed with the wing manager","Single cell and cell-sharing risk assessment updated: no sharing while psychotic","Two officers and calm explanation for every cell entry","If he lacks capacity about fluids and is deteriorating, acute hospital under MCA best interests"]},"facts":[{"h":"No forced treatment in prison","x":"Under the MHA, prison healthcare does not count as a hospital. Only consent, MCA best interests (if lacking capacity) or emergency common law apply. Real treatment needs transfer."},{"h":"s48 for remand, s47 for sentenced","x":"Unsentenced prisoners in urgent need go under s48/49; sentenced under s47/49. Both need two medical recommendations and a MoJ warrant."},{"h":"28-day transfer standard","x":"National guidance expects transfer within 28 days of first referral. Record dates and escalate delays to the NHS England case manager."},{"h":"Two days without food or fluid","x":"Dehydration and rhabdomyolysis can be fatal fast. Check U&E and CK today, not after the psychiatric plan."},{"h":"Spice mimics psychosis","x":"Synthetic cannabinoids cause agitation, paranoia and violence and are missed on standard screens. Ask directly and request an extended screen."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Assess his mental state and risk, decide on hospital transfer, and agree a plan with prison staff."},"c-6-1-3":{"obj":["Gathered four facts from the nurse: what was passed, how late he was, his state on return, and the drug screen result","Agreed with the RC today a proportionate decision on unescorted leave, with clear steps to restore it","Named one physical, one procedural and one relational security action and who does each","Asked him directly whether anyone is pressuring or exploiting him, and recorded the answer","Ensured the MoJ is informed and the incident is logged before the shift ends"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"'So I'm late once and you take my leave? That's a joke.'","r":"'I understand it feels unfair. Being late, missing psychology and the handover together worry us. Leave is paused while we review it this week, and I'll tell you exactly what gets it back.'"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"'It was just a biscuit. They're making a big deal out of nothing.'","r":"'Maybe it was. We'd rather check than guess. Would you agree to a room search and a urine test so we can clear it up quickly?'"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"'If I tell you, I'm a grass. You don't know what happens to grasses here.'","r":"'You're right to think about your safety. If someone is pressuring you, we can protect you without naming you. Is anyone making you carry or do things?'"},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"'I'll go to every psychology session if you leave my leave alone.'","r":"'I'm glad you want to go back to psychology. That will help. The leave decision is the RC's, made at this week's meeting, and your engagement will count.'"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"'This will wreck my tribunal. You're setting me up.'","r":"'We have to be honest in the report, but one setback handled well can count for you. The tribunal also hears what you did next. Let's make that the good part.'"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"'I owe someone money. I didn't know what else to do.'","r":"'Thank you for telling me. That takes courage. Debt on a ward can get dangerous. Let's talk with your named nurse today about keeping you safe and sorting it.'"}],"bps":{"bio":["Urine drug screen today including synthetic cannabinoids; repeat after every leave for a month","Mental state check for early relapse; check depot or clozapine adherence and levels if relevant","Physical obs if intoxication suspected"],"psych":["Named nurse and psychologist explore what drove the late return and the missed sessions within a week","Restart psychology sessions with a written attendance agreement","Review HCR-20 management plan and relapse signature at MDT"],"social":["Social worker explores debt, family pressures and outside contacts","Security lead and police liaison officer informed if drug supply is suspected","RC informs MoJ Mental Health Casework Section of the incident"],"safety":["Unescorted leave suspended by RC pending MDT; escorted leave can continue if agreed","Room search per policy, with consent where possible; log on Datix","Increase observation to intermittent; review at next ward round","Adult safeguarding referral if exploitation is suspected; protect both patients"]},"facts":[{"h":"RC can suspend, not grant","x":"For s37/41 patients, the RC may suspend leave without MoJ consent but must tell the MoJ. Restoring or extending leave needs MoJ agreement."},{"h":"Three kinds of security","x":"Physical (walls, locks), procedural (searches, testing, leave rules) and relational (knowing the patient, ward climate). Relational security picks up early warning signs first."},{"h":"Small signs come first","x":"Missed sessions, late returns and handovers often appear before a serious incident. Act on clusters, not single events."},{"h":"He may be the victim","x":"Passing items can mean exploitation or debt. Ask privately and consider a safeguarding referral before assuming he is dealing."},{"h":"Tribunal reports must reflect this","x":"Record the incident accurately and how he responded. Leaving it out damages trust with the tribunal and the MoJ."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Review risk with the nurse in charge, decide today's leave, and agree physical, procedural and relational security."},"c-6-2-1":{"obj":["Tested how fixed his belief is and recorded whether it is a delusion or an overvalued idea","Asked directly about violence, weapons, threats, homicidal thoughts and suicide, and recorded each answer","Recorded his weekly alcohol units and whether drinking came before or after the jealousy","Completed a DASH checklist with the wife and given her the domestic abuse helpline number","Made a same-day children's safeguarding referral and agreed disclosure with your consultant"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"'I'm not jealous, I'm right. I've seen the messages.'","r":"'I can see how sure you are and how much it's hurting you. Can you tell me what the messages said? I want to understand it from your side.'"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"'Whose side are you on? She's the one lying.'","r":"'I'm not taking sides. My job is to look after your health and make sure everyone at home is safe. You're clearly under huge strain. When did it start?'"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"'One way or another, she's going to admit it.'","r":"'When you say one way or another, what do you mean? Have you thought about forcing her to tell you?'"},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Wife) 'Is he going to hurt me?'","r":"'I can't predict that for sure, and I'm taking your safety seriously. Has he ever hurt or threatened you? Let's go through some questions together and make a plan today.'"},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"(Wife) 'Maybe it's my fault. I did go for drinks with work once.'","r":"'This is not your fault. This kind of jealousy can be part of an illness, and it can be fed by alcohol. Nothing you did justifies being checked or followed.'"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"(Wife) 'If social services get involved, will they take the kids?'","r":"'The aim is to support you and keep the children safe, not to remove them. Telling them shows you're protecting the children. I'll explain what happens next.'"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"'I'm not stopping drinking. It's the only thing that calms me down.'","r":"'I understand it takes the edge off. It also makes these thoughts and arguments worse. Would you see the alcohol team to cut down safely?'"}],"bps":{"bio":["If delusional: antipsychotic, e.g. risperidone 1 mg twice daily or 2 mg at night, titrate to 4–6 mg/day; warn: stiffness, sexual side effects, high prolactin","Baseline weight, BP, glucose/HbA1c, lipids, prolactin; repeat at 3 months","AUDIT; if dependent, assisted withdrawal (chlordiazepoxide reducing regime) with oral thiamine 200–300 mg daily in divided doses","Screen for organic causes: LFTs, B12/folate, TFTs; brain imaging if late onset or cognitive change"],"psych":["Psychoeducation once engaged; CBT focused on checking and jealousy, psychology team","Alcohol treatment: drug and alcohol service, keyworker, motivational interviewing"],"social":["Wife: DASH with clinician, referral to IDVA; MARAC if high risk","Children's social care referral via MASH; trust safeguarding team informed","Discuss one partner living elsewhere for now; housing officer if needed","Wife told about Domestic Violence Disclosure Scheme (Clare's Law) via police"],"safety":["Consultant agrees same-day risk level and what is shared with whom","Wife given National Domestic Abuse Helpline (0808 2000 247) and told to call 999 if threatened","Weapons asked about and removed where possible","Urgent MHA assessment if risk escalates and he refuses treatment; review in one week"]},"facts":[{"h":"Homicide and suicide go together","x":"Morbid jealousy carries risk of partner homicide followed by suicide. Always ask about both, in the same interview."},{"h":"A real affair doesn't rule it out","x":"The diagnosis rests on how he reaches the belief, not whether it is true. Checking underwear and phones is typical."},{"h":"Separation is a real intervention","x":"Separating the couple, at least for now, is often recommended when risk is high. Treatment alone may not be enough."},{"h":"Drinking can come first or second","x":"Alcohol can cause the jealousy (alcoholic morbid jealousy) or follow it. The timeline changes the treatment plan."},{"h":"Share the minimum to protect","x":"You can tell the wife she may be at risk without disclosing his full history. Record what was shared, with whom and why."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Assess his morbid jealousy and risk, then explain the condition and safety to his wife within confidentiality."},"c-6-2-2":{"obj":["Recorded the full pattern of contact: number of letters, visits, online contact and any gifts","Asked directly about anger at the presenter, his partner and anyone blocking contact, and about self-harm","Screened for mania and schizophrenia and recorded the likely diagnosis","Agreed with police what is shared, and recorded the reason and legal basis","Set a follow-up plan: MHA assessment or CMHT care coordinator, with contact within 72 hours of any discharge"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"'He loves me. Why would I stay away from someone who loves me?'","r":"'I can see how important he is to you. I'm not here to argue. Can you tell me how you first knew he felt this way?'"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"'He winks at me every night on telly. You've seen it, everyone has.'","r":"'I haven't seen what you've seen. I'm interested in what it means to you. What does he want you to do when he does that?'"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"'His girlfriend is the problem. She's keeping him from me.'","r":"'It sounds like she feels like a real barrier. How do you feel about her? Have you ever thought of doing anything to her?'"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"'If he doesn't marry me, there's no point to anything.'","r":"'That sounds very painful. When you say no point, do you mean you've thought of ending your life?'"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"'I'm not taking pills to stop loving someone.'","r":"'That isn't what the medicine is for. You haven't been sleeping and you're getting into trouble. It can help with the stress and the strong thoughts. Can we try a low dose?'"},{"tag":"Question","p":"'Are you going to tell the police what I said?'","r":"'Some of it, yes, if I think someone could be harmed. I'll share only what's needed and I'll tell you what I've said.'"}],"bps":{"bio":["Antipsychotic, e.g. aripiprazole 10 mg once daily in the morning, range 10–30 mg; warn: restlessness (akathisia), nausea, insomnia","Alternative: risperidone 2 mg/day, titrate to 4–6 mg; check prolactin","Baseline weight, BP, glucose, lipids, ECG; repeat at 12 weeks","Rule out mania, organic causes and substance use: urine drug screen, TFTs"],"psych":["Supportive engagement; avoid arguing; work on distress and isolation","CBT for psychosis when stable, psychology team, around 16 sessions"],"social":["AMHP arranges MHA assessment if admission seems needed","Police lead victim support; consider a Stalking Protection Order","Care coordinator in CMHT monitors contact and stress","Refer to specialist stalking clinic if available locally"],"safety":["Police told of risk to the presenter and his partner; record the reason","Care plan clearly states no contact with the presenter","Self-harm risk reviewed at each contact; crisis line given","Follow-up within 72 hours of inpatient discharge"]},"facts":[{"h":"Rejection raises risk","x":"Risk often rises when the belief is challenged or the person feels rejected. Ask what she will do if he says no."},{"h":"Third parties are at risk","x":"Partners and those seen as blocking contact are often the target of violence, not the loved person. Ask about them directly."},{"h":"Stalking is a crime","x":"Persistent unwanted contact can be stalking under the Protection from Harassment Act 1997. Police can apply for a Stalking Protection Order."},{"h":"Treatment is slow","x":"The belief often persists for months on medication. Distress and behaviour usually improve first; measure those."},{"h":"National Stalking Helpline","x":"The presenter and family can call 0808 802 0300 (Suzy Lamplugh Trust) for advice; police lead the safety plan."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Assess her erotomanic beliefs and risk to the presenter and herself, and plan management with police."},"c-6-2-3":{"obj":["Explained limits of confidentiality before asking about the earlier incidents","Recorded the pattern of all three incidents: urge, arousal, victim age, escalation","Asked directly about contact offences, other paraphilias and interest in children","Assessed suicide risk after arrest and recorded a clear observation recommendation","Given written advice to custody on fitness for interview and safe release"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"'I'm disgusting. My parents will never look at me again.'","r":"'I can hear how ashamed you feel. You've told me honestly, and that matters. There is help for this. How bad have things got for you since the arrest?'"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"'It was an accident. I was just adjusting myself.'","r":"'Okay. I need to understand it properly. You mentioned it was the third time. Could you tell me what happened the first time?'"},{"tag":"Question","p":"'Will this go on a register? Will I lose my job?'","r":"'I can't answer legal questions, but your solicitor can. My job is your health and getting you help. Let's focus on how you're coping tonight.'"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"'Are you going to tell the police everything I say?'","r":"'What you tell me is mostly private, but some of it may go into a report to help the court and police understand your health. If someone is at risk, I'd have to share it.'"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"'Honestly, I'd rather not be here than go through this.'","r":"'Thank you for telling me. That's important. Have you thought about how you might end your life? I want to make sure you're safe tonight.'"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"'I get this urge when I'm stressed. It builds and I can't stop it.'","r":"'That's really useful to know. Lots of people describe that. What helps the urge pass, and what makes it stronger?'"}],"bps":{"bio":["If depressed or for compulsive urges: SSRI, e.g. fluoxetine 20 mg once daily in the morning, can increase to 40–60 mg; warn: nausea, agitation in first weeks, sexual side effects","Antilibidinal medication (e.g. cyproterone acetate, GnRH analogues) only via specialist service with informed consent and baseline LFTs, bone health","Check alcohol and drug use; screen for organic causes if late onset or disinhibited"],"psych":["Refer to community forensic or psychosexual service for assessment","CBT-based relapse prevention and urge management, specialist psychology","Treat low mood: psychological support via GP or talking therapies"],"social":["Liaison and diversion share summary with custody, solicitor and court","Check he has somewhere safe to go on release; parents informed only with consent","Police manage safeguarding of the victims"],"safety":["Custody: increased observation level as advised; review before release","Safety plan and crisis line (NHS 111, option 2) given on release","GP informed with consent, or if risk justifies it","Follow-up by community team within days of release"]},"facts":[{"h":"Suicide risk peaks after arrest","x":"People arrested for sexual offences are at high risk of suicide soon after arrest or release. Always ask, and advise on observation."},{"h":"Ask about escalation","x":"Most exposure does not progress, but some offenders go on to contact offences. Ask about fantasies, approaches and touching."},{"h":"Victim age matters","x":"Exposure to children raises concern. Ask if the age of the person watching matters to him."},{"h":"Fitness to interview","x":"He is fit if he understands the caution and questions and his answers will be reliable. Low mood alone does not make him unfit."},{"h":"Antilibidinal drugs need a specialist","x":"They need consent, baseline tests and close follow-up; never start them from custody."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Assess his mental state and indecent exposure risk, and advise custody staff on needs and next steps."},"c-6-2-4":{"obj":["Explained the limits of confidentiality before he gave any details","Recorded onset, age and sex of interest, whether exclusive, and how distressing he finds it","Asked directly about images, any contact with a child, and every child he has access to (family, work, babysitting)","Assessed suicide risk after disclosure and agreed a written safety plan","Spoken to the safeguarding lead the same day and given him the Stop It Now helpline number"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"'If I tell you everything, will you call the police?'","r":"'Having thoughts is not a crime, and you came for help. I'll keep things private unless I think a child or someone else could be at risk. If so, I'll tell you first if I safely can.'"},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"'I'm a monster. I hate myself for this.'","r":"'You're not a monster. You've never acted on these thoughts, and you asked for help. That takes real courage. How low have you been feeling?'"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"'My sister's little girl stays over most weekends.'","r":"'Thank you for telling me. That's important. Are you ever alone with her? Have the thoughts ever been about her?'"},{"tag":"Question","p":"'Can this be cured? Will I always be like this?'","r":"'The interest itself often lasts, but many people learn to manage it and never offend. Treatment and support help a lot. That's what we're starting today.'"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"'Since telling the GP I've thought it'd be better if I wasn't here.'","r":"'I'm really glad you told me. Have you thought about how you'd end your life? Let's make a plan to keep you safe tonight.'"},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"'Please don't write any of this down.'","r":"'I understand why you'd worry. I do have to keep a record, and it's kept securely. I'll write only what's needed for your care and safety.'"}],"bps":{"bio":["For depression and sexual preoccupation: sertraline 50 mg once daily in the morning, increase in 50 mg steps to 100–200 mg; warn: nausea, early agitation, reduced libido","Review at 1–2 weeks: ask about suicidal thoughts and agitation after starting","Antilibidinal medication only via specialist service with consent and baseline tests"],"psych":["Refer to forensic CMHT or specialist sexual behaviour service for CBT-based relapse prevention","Stop It Now helpline (0808 1000 900) for confidential support between appointments","Psychological support for depression via CMHT or talking therapies"],"social":["Same-day call to trust safeguarding lead; if a specific child could be at risk, refer in writing to children's social care","Plan avoids unsupervised contact with children, including family visits and babysitting","Check job and voluntary roles for access to children"],"safety":["Written safety plan: triggers (alcohol, being alone), coping steps, crisis line","GP letter with plan; record what was shared and why","Review within one week; sooner if suicidal thoughts increase"]},"facts":[{"h":"Interest is not offending","x":"Paedophilic disorder is a mental disorder. Offending is a crime. Many people with the interest never offend."},{"h":"Images are an offence","x":"Making or possessing indecent images of children is a crime. If he discloses current use, discuss with the safeguarding lead at once."},{"h":"Suicide risk after disclosure","x":"Risk of suicide is high after disclosure or arrest. Ask directly, and again at the end."},{"h":"Ask about every child","x":"Family, work, neighbours, babysitting. Access to children drives the safeguarding decision more than the interest itself."},{"h":"Public interest disclosure","x":"GMC guidance allows you to share information without consent where this protects others from serious harm. Tell him first if it's safe, and share the minimum needed."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Take a sensitive history of sexual interest in children, assess risk to children and himself, and plan."},"c-6-2-5":{"obj":["Taken him step by step through before, during and after the fire, including what he did once it started","Recorded the likely motive (revenge, distress, psychosis, pleasure) and any previous fire-setting","Screened for psychosis, mood, alcohol and learning disability, and asked about suicide","Given the custody sergeant written advice on fitness to interview, appropriate adult and observation level","Passed on the risk to his ex-partner to the police with a log or reference number"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"'I just dropped a cigarette. It was an accident.'","r":"'Okay. Tell me what happened step by step, from when you got there. Where were you standing, and what happened next?'"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"'She deserves to feel what I felt. She ruined my life.'","r":"'It sounds like you've been really hurt. When you set the fire, what did you want her to feel or do?'"},{"tag":"Question","p":"'Am I going to prison for this?'","r":"'I can't answer that. Your solicitor can. My job is to check your health and make sure you get the right support. How are you feeling right now?'"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"'I don't care what happens to me anymore.'","r":"'That sounds like you're in a lot of pain. Have you had thoughts about ending your life, today or before?'"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"'Are you going to tell them I'm mad?'","r":"'I'm not here to label you. I'm here to see if you need help. I'll tell the police what's needed for your care and safety, and you'll know what I say.'"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"'I've done it before. Bins at school, a shed once.'","r":"'Thank you for being honest. That's important. What was going on in your life each time? How did you feel afterwards?'"}],"bps":{"bio":["Treat alcohol withdrawal if dependent: CIWA-Ar scoring; chlordiazepoxide reducing regime per custody healthcare protocol","If depressed: sertraline 50 mg once daily, titrate to 100–200 mg; warn: early agitation, nausea","Screen for learning disability and head injury"],"psych":["Fire-setter intervention: forensic psychology or fire service programme","Alcohol treatment: drug and alcohol keyworker; brief intervention in custody"],"social":["Liaison and diversion report to solicitor and court by morning","Refer to CMHT or forensic CMHT for full assessment","Fire service Safe and Well visit; housing officer if housing at risk"],"safety":["Police told of risk to ex-partner; DASH and MARAC if domestic abuse","Custody: observation level as advised; appropriate adult if learning needs","Delay interview until sober","Handover to morning liaison and diversion team"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask what he did after","x":"Staying to watch, raising the alarm or fleeing tells you about motive and remorse. It's the question most often missed."},{"h":"Revenge raises the risk","x":"Fires aimed at a named person, especially an ex-partner, point to domestic abuse risk. Pass it on."},{"h":"Pyromania is rare","x":"It needs repeated fire-setting for pleasure or relief, with no other motive and no other cause. Most arson is revenge, distress or intoxication."},{"h":"Appropriate adult rule","x":"Under PACE Code C, a person who may be mentally vulnerable must have an appropriate adult for interview."},{"h":"Intoxicated means not fit now","x":"Advise waiting until sober. Fitness to interview is about now, not his general mental state."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Assess the fire-setting motive and risk, screen for mental disorder, and advise custody staff."},"c-6-2-6":{"obj":["Defined the single decision (CT neck and collar now) and its urgency with the emergency doctor","Tested all four parts of the capacity test (understand, retain, weigh, communicate) and recorded the answer to each","Recorded the impairment of mind or brain, if any (e.g. intoxication, head injury, delirium)","Tried at least two practical ways to support his decision before concluding","Given a written capacity opinion and agreed a time to reassess"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"'I'm fine. I just want to go back to my cell.'","r":"'I hear you. I know A&E isn't a nice place to be. The doctors are worried your neck may be injured. Can I explain what they're worried about?'"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"'You're all in with the police. Just let me go.'","r":"'I'm a doctor, not police. I'm here to make sure you understand what's going on, so you can make your own choice. What do you know about your neck?'"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"'It's just a bit sore. Nothing's broken.'","r":"'It might be just bruising. The risk is that a broken bone can damage the spinal cord if you move. If it is broken, what do you think could happen?'"},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"'I'll do it if I can have a fag first.'","r":"'I can't let you smoke here, but I can ask for a nicotine patch or spray now. Would that help you stay calm for the scan?'"},{"tag":"Question","p":"'Can you make me have it?'","r":"'If you understand the risks and still say no, you can refuse. I need to check you can weigh it up properly first. Can you tell me what we've just talked about?'"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"'I just don't want to be stuck here. I hate hospitals.'","r":"'That makes sense. What is it about hospitals that's hard for you? Is there anything that would make this easier?'"}],"bps":{"bio":["Analgesia per emergency team; nicotine replacement: 21 mg/24 h patch if over 10 cigarettes a day, plus spray or gum for cravings","Screen for head injury (GCS, neuro obs) and delirium (4AT)","Check alcohol level or breath alcohol; reassess capacity when sober"],"psych":["Calm space, simple explanation, time to think","Trusted person by phone if he wants"],"social":["Police stay for safety but do not restrain unless clinically directed","Custody healthcare professional informed before return"],"safety":["If lacking capacity: emergency doctor decides in best interests, least restrictive, proportionate restraint only (MCA s5–6)","If capacity present and refusing: record informed refusal and safety-netting advice","Reassess capacity when sober; record time and findings"]},"facts":[{"h":"Unwise is not incapable","x":"A person can make an unwise decision and still have capacity. Assess the process, not the outcome."},{"h":"MHA cannot treat a neck","x":"The MHA covers mental disorder only. A physical treatment decision goes through the MCA."},{"h":"Capacity changes hour by hour","x":"Intoxication can remove capacity now and it can return when sober. Record the time and plan to reassess."},{"h":"Decision-specific","x":"Capacity is for this decision (CT and collar now), not a general verdict on the person."},{"h":"Treating doctor decides best interests","x":"You give an opinion on capacity. The emergency doctor makes the treatment decision and records it."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"Assess his capacity to refuse neck imaging and immobilisation, and advise the emergency team."},"c-6-3-1":{"obj":["Explained in plain words why the MoJ must agree any community leave for a restricted patient","Assessed readiness: mental state, insight into the offence, early warning signs, substance use and clozapine adherence","Listed the steps before applying: updated risk assessment, victim liaison, MAPPA views, RC application","Agreed a realistic timeline and what he can do now to help his case","Written a leave care plan covering purpose, times, area, conditions and what happens if he does not return"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"'Everyone else gets leave. Why am I treated like a criminal?'","r":"'I can see why that feels unfair. Your order means the Ministry of Justice has to agree your leave. That's the law, not a judgement on you. Let's make the strongest case we can.'"},{"tag":"Question","p":"'How long will this take?'","r":"'It usually takes some weeks once we've sent the application. First we update your risk plan and check with victim liaison. I'll keep you posted at each step.'"},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"'If I promise to come back on time, can I just go once?'","r":"'I believe you mean that. The RC can't grant it without the Ministry agreeing, though. What we can do is show how well you've done on your escorted leave.'"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"'What if I see someone from back then?'","r":"'That's a really good thing to think about. It shows insight. What would you do if it happened? We'll build that into your plan, including places to avoid.'"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"'I've been stable for two years. What more do you want?'","r":"'Two years stable is real progress, and I'll say so. The Ministry also wants to see you know your warning signs and what you'd do. Can you tell me yours?'"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"'Sometimes I skip clozapine at weekends. It makes me sleepy.'","r":"'Thank you for being honest. That's important. Missing doses can make clozapine unsafe to restart. Let's look at the timing and the dose together today.'"}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue clozapine; trough level 12 h post-dose if adherence doubtful (target at least 350 µg/L); FBC monthly after the first year","If 48 hours or more missed, re-titrate from 12.5 mg; tell the pharmacist","Ask about bowel habit at every review; laxative if constipated (MHRA warning on fatal gut hypomotility)","Smoking changes clozapine levels: stopping smoking can double levels; starting can halve them"],"psych":["Relapse signature work with named nurse and psychologist before leave","Offence-focused work and relapse prevention, forensic psychology"],"social":["Victim Liaison Officer contacted before application if a victim is registered; exclusion zones set","MAPPA coordinator views if level 2 or 3; informed when leave is granted","RC sends leave application to MoJ Mental Health Casework Section with reports","OT plans purpose of leave: shops, travel, structured activity"],"safety":["Leave plan includes what to do if he does not return: AWOL policy, police informed, MHCS told","Drug and alcohol screen after leave","Review each leave at ward round and record outcome"]},"facts":[{"h":"s41 leave needs MoJ consent","x":"Any leave from hospital grounds for a restricted patient needs MoJ approval. The RC applies; the RC cannot grant it alone."},{"h":"Clozapine after a missed 48 hours","x":"If 48 hours or more of doses are missed, restart at 12.5 mg and re-titrate. Check FBC monitoring rules with pharmacy."},{"h":"Smoking shifts levels","x":"Tobacco smoke speeds clozapine breakdown. A change in smoking on leave can cause toxicity or relapse; recheck the level."},{"h":"Victims have a voice","x":"Registered victims can request leave conditions such as exclusion zones through the Victim Liaison Officer."},{"h":"MHA 2025 not yet in force","x":"As of November 2026, current leave rules for restricted patients still apply."}],"type":"Mental Health Act process","do":"Explain restricted-patient leave and Ministry of Justice approval, assess readiness, and start the request."},"c-6-3-2":{"obj":["Explained at the start that the report is not confidential and will go to the solicitor and court","Tested each fitness to plead ability with a specific question and recorded his answers","Linked any impairment to a stated diagnosis and current mental state","Recommended treatment and transfer, with a plan to reassess fitness","Explained MAPPA correctly: police, probation and prisons lead; health has a duty to cooperate"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"(Patient) 'Why should I talk to you? You're working for the judge.'","r":"'I've been asked by your solicitor, not the judge. I'll write what I find honestly. You don't have to answer everything, but it helps me give a fair picture of your health.'"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"(Patient) 'The judge is in on it. They all are.'","r":"'That sounds very frightening. Can you tell me more about what they're doing? How does that affect how you'd talk to your solicitor?'"},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Solicitor) 'So is he fit or not? I need a yes or no.'","r":"'Right now, in my opinion, he is not fit. He cannot instruct you because he believes you are part of the plot. With treatment, that could change in weeks.'"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"(Solicitor) 'He's got schizophrenia, surely that makes him unfit?'","r":"'Not on its own. Fitness depends on specific abilities, like following the trial and instructing you. Many people with schizophrenia are fit to plead.'"},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Solicitor) 'Will MAPPA mean he's watched for life?'","r":"'No. MAPPA is how police, probation and prisons manage risk with other agencies. The level depends on risk and can be reduced over time.'"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"(Patient) 'I'm not going to any hospital. I'll handle my own case.'","r":"'I understand you want to stay in control. Treatment could help you deal with the case more clearly. Can we talk about what worries you about hospital?'"}],"bps":{"bio":["Antipsychotic he agrees to in prison: olanzapine 10 mg at night (range 5–20 mg); warn: sleepiness, weight gain","Baseline bloods: glucose/HbA1c, lipids, FBC, U&E, LFT; ECG","Transfer under s48/49 if urgent need; treatment can then be given under MHA"],"psych":["In-reach CPN visits to build engagement","Repeat fitness assessment after treatment"],"social":["Agree report deadline with solicitor; ask early for missing papers","Second doctor (one s12-approved) for fitness finding","Trust informs MAPPA coordinator if he later receives a hospital order"],"safety":["Prison ACCT if any self-harm risk","Consultant reviews and signs report; copy stored securely"]},"facts":[{"h":"Pritchard criteria","x":"Can he understand the charge, decide a plea, challenge a juror, instruct counsel, follow the trial and give evidence? Failing any one may mean unfit."},{"h":"Diagnosis isn't unfitness","x":"The question is about abilities now, not the label. Test each ability with a direct question."},{"h":"Two doctors needed","x":"A finding of unfitness needs evidence from two doctors, one approved under s12. If unfit, the court holds a trial of the facts."},{"h":"Fitness can change","x":"Treatment can restore fitness within weeks. Recommend a review date."},{"h":"MAPPA categories","x":"Category 1: registered sex offenders; 2: violent offenders with 12+ months custody or a hospital order; 3: other dangerous offenders. Levels 1–3 show agency involvement."}],"type":"Documentation and reporting","do":"Assess fitness to plead using the Pritchard criteria, and explain court process and MAPPA to the solicitor."},"c-6-3-3":{"obj":["Explored the threat in detail: named victim, intent, plan, means, timing and access","Asked directly about weapons now, past violence and what has stopped him so far","Sought consent first and explained the limits of confidentiality","Shared the minimum necessary with the police and recorded the log number and reason","Arranged an urgent MHA assessment through the AMHP service the same day"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"'You can't tell anyone. That's my private business.'","r":"'I understand you want this kept private. Most of what you tell me is. But if someone could be seriously hurt, I can't keep it to myself. I'd rather we agree on it together.'"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"'He's poisoning me. Nobody is protecting me.'","r":"'That sounds terrifying. I want to help you feel safe. Would you come into hospital where we can look after you and check what's going on?'"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"'So you're on his side now? Calling the police on me?'","r":"'I'm not on his side. I'm on the side of nobody getting hurt, including you. If you hurt him, your life would change forever. I want to stop that.'"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"'I've got a knife at home. I keep it by the door.'","r":"'Thank you for telling me. That's important. Would you be willing to let someone remove it while we get you help?'"},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"'If I take the depot again, will you leave it?'","r":"'Restarting the depot is a really good step and I want to arrange it. Because of what you said, I still need to share some information to keep your neighbour safe.'"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"'I didn't mean it. I was just angry.'","r":"'I'm glad to hear that. You said it very clearly, though. Have you thought about how or when you might do it?'"}],"bps":{"bio":["Restart previous depot; check product's missed-dose rules (re-loading may be needed after a long gap)","Oral cover if needed: same antipsychotic orally until depot effective","Urine drug screen; check alcohol use"],"psych":["Engage via crisis team; explore poisoning fears without arguing","Relapse prevention once stable, care coordinator"],"social":["AMHP service for urgent MHA assessment; crisis team follows up if he leaves","Police informed (999 if immediate, 101 otherwise); record log number","MAPPA coordinator informed if he is on MAPPA"],"safety":["Consultant or duty senior informed immediately","Record reason for disclosure; Caldicott Guardian if unsure","Update risk assessment and crisis plan; Datix if required","Do not warn the neighbour directly; police decide on warning"]},"facts":[{"h":"Disclosure to prevent serious harm","x":"GMC guidance allows disclosure without consent when the risk of death or serious harm outweighs confidentiality. Share only what's needed."},{"h":"Tell the police, not the neighbour","x":"Police assess and act on the threat. Contacting the neighbour yourself risks over-sharing and making things worse."},{"h":"Seek consent first","x":"Even when you will disclose, ask first and tell him what you will share, unless that would raise the risk."},{"h":"Record every step","x":"Note the threat in his words, who you told, when, the log number and why. This protects him, you and the neighbour."},{"h":"Stopping depot plus threat","x":"Non-adherence with persecutory beliefs about a named person is a high-risk mix. Act today; don't wait for the next appointment."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"Assess the threat to the named person, decide on disclosure without consent, and record your reasoning."},"c-7-1-1":{"obj":["Pinned down the time of the last drink, daily units and past fit or DTs history, and recorded him as high withdrawal risk","Charted chlordiazepoxide, Pabrinex before any glucose, and CIWA-Ar every 1–2 hours with clear bleep triggers","Ruled out hypoglycaemia, head injury and infection with a capillary glucose, NEWS2 and a head-to-toe look","Agreed with him to stay for inpatient withdrawal and told him in writing to stop driving and notify the DVLA","Asked about children at home and recorded any safeguarding action"],"snip":[{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just give me a few tablets and let me go home. I've done it on my own before.\"","r":"\"I hear you want to be home. Last year stopping gave you a fit, and that makes another one more likely, not less. Two or three nights here means we can treat it early. Can we agree to start tonight?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Am I going to have another fit? I can feel it coming on.\"","r":"\"That fear makes sense after last time. The medicine I'm charting now lowers that risk a lot, and the nurses will score your symptoms every hour or two. If anything changes, they call me straight away.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"A litre a day isn't that much. Everyone I know drinks like that.\"","r":"\"It can feel normal when everyone around you drinks the same. A litre of vodka is about 40 units a day, and your shaking now shows your body depends on it. That is why we need to be careful.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do I need injections? Can't I just take vitamin tablets?\"","r":"\"Good question. Heavy drinking blocks vitamin B1 getting into the body, and tablets barely get absorbed. The drip gets it straight to the brain and protects your memory. Tablets come later, once the drip course is done.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I've been sat in A&E for eight hours. Stop asking me questions and give me something.\"","r":"\"Eight hours is a long wait and you are feeling rough. I need two minutes of answers to get the dose right, then I'll chart it straight away. When was your last drink, as close as you can say?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"My daughter's eleven. She's been sorting herself out for school while I'm like this.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. That shows you care about her. While you are here, who is looking after her tonight? I'll need to share this with the social worker so she has support, and I'll keep you involved.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Chlordiazepoxide per trust protocol (BNF range 10–50 mg four times daily, tapered over 7–10 days); extra PRN doses only if CIWA-Ar is 10 or more","Pabrinex IV, 2 pairs three times daily for 3–5 days if Wernicke's suspected, otherwise 1 pair daily; give over 30 minutes with anaphylaxis kit nearby; then oral thiamine 100 mg three times daily","Bloods tonight: FBC, U&E, LFT, GGT, Mg, PO4, glucose, clotting; replace magnesium and phosphate on medical advice","If he has severe liver disease, switch to oxazepam or lorazepam on pharmacist and senior advice"],"psych":["Brief motivational chat on day 2–3 once withdrawal settles: his goals, reasons to stop","Hospital alcohol care team review next working day; plan relapse prevention medicine before discharge"],"social":["Ward social worker to check who cares for his child tonight and whether a children's social care referral is needed","Community drug and alcohol service referral before discharge with a first appointment date","GP letter: detox regimen, thiamine, DVLA advice, relapse prevention plan"],"safety":["CIWA-Ar and NEWS2 every 1–2 hours overnight; bleep for CIWA-Ar over 15, confusion, a fit or NEWS2 of 5 or more","Falls precautions and seizure plan: lorazepam on the chart for a fit, medical registrar for any seizure","Written DVLA advice given and recorded; review the regimen on the morning ward round"]},"facts":[{"h":"Past fit means inpatient detox","x":"A previous withdrawal seizure or DTs is the strongest single predictor of a severe withdrawal. NICE advises inpatient medically assisted withdrawal for these patients."},{"h":"Thiamine before glucose","x":"Glucose without thiamine can tip someone into Wernicke's. Chart Pabrinex first; if a glucose drink or IV dextrose is needed for hypoglycaemia, do not delay it, but give thiamine as soon as possible."},{"h":"Withdrawal clock","x":"Symptoms start 6–12 hours after the last drink. Fits peak at 12–48 hours. DTs usually appear at 48–72 hours. Twelve hours in and shaking means the worst is still ahead."},{"h":"Pabrinex is not harmless","x":"MHRA warns of rare anaphylaxis. Dilute and infuse over 30 minutes, with resuscitation equipment on the ward. Still give it: the benefit far outweighs the risk."},{"h":"Watch the liver","x":"Chlordiazepoxide is long-acting and builds up in liver failure. Jaundice, raised INR or confusion: use a shorter-acting drug such as oxazepam, with senior advice."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Assess withdrawal risk, start chlordiazepoxide and Pabrinex, and explain why inpatient detox is needed."},"c-7-1-2":{"obj":["Recognised DTs from fluctuating confusion, visual hallucinations and autonomic signs, and said so to the medical registrar","Given oral or parenteral lorazepam and high-dose Pabrinex, and checked glucose, temperature and for head injury","Put him on 1:1 observation with hazards removed and a capacity assessment recorded","Arranged medical transfer with a structured SBAR handover and completed a Datix for the window attempt"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Get them off me! They're all over the walls, can't you see them?\"","r":"\"I can see how frightened you are. You are in hospital and you are safe. Your body is reacting to coming off alcohol, and that is making you see things. I'm going to give you something to calm it.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not taking anything from you. You're trying to poison me.\"","r":"\"I understand you don't trust us right now. This medicine is to stop the shaking and the things you are seeing. Will you take it as a tablet, with the nurse you know here with you?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Where am I? I need to get home, my mum's waiting for me.\"","r":"\"You're in hospital, it's Thursday evening, and I'm one of the doctors. You've been unwell. Let's sit down here together. A nurse will stay with you the whole time.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Healthcare assistant) \"Should we just give him haloperidol? That's what we normally use.\"","r":"\"Good thought, but this is alcohol withdrawal. Benzodiazepines treat the cause; haloperidol alone can lower the fit threshold. Lorazepam first, please, and Pabrinex. I'll consider an antipsychotic only if he is still very disturbed after that.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"(Medical registrar) \"Can't you manage him on your ward? We're full.\"","r":"\"I know you're stretched. His pulse is 128, temperature 38.1, and he is delirious. He may have sepsis or a head injury, and we have no overnight medical cover here. He needs a medical bed tonight.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"(Wife, on the phone) \"I brought the vodka in on Sunday. Is this my fault?\"","r":"\"I can hear how worried you are. This is a medical complication of stopping drinking. It is not something you caused. Right now he's being treated and is safe. Can I tell you what happens next?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Oral lorazepam per protocol (often 1–2 mg, repeated to effect); IM or IV if he cannot take it; haloperidol or olanzapine only as add-on if still disturbed","Pabrinex IV 2 pairs three times daily for 3–5 days (treatment dose), infused over 30 minutes","Capillary glucose, temperature, NEWS2 now; bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, Mg, PO4, CRP, clotting, blood cultures if febrile; ECG","IV fluids and electrolyte replacement led by the medical team; CT head if any fall or focal signs"],"psych":["Structured MSE recorded: level of consciousness, orientation, perception, mood, risk","Once recovered, explain what happened and start relapse prevention planning with the alcohol care team"],"social":["Update next of kin with consent or in his best interests; nurse in charge to arrange escort for transfer","Hand over to the day team; alcohol care team and community alcohol service once medically recovered"],"safety":["1:1 observation; remove ligature points and lock the window; low-stimulus room with soft light","Assess and record capacity; treat and transfer under MCA ss4–6, restraint only if proportionate","Datix for the window attempt; inform on-call consultant; call 999 if on a standalone site"]},"facts":[{"h":"DTs is a medical emergency","x":"Untreated DTs can kill through arrhythmia, seizures, dehydration and infection. It needs a medical bed, not a psychiatric one, especially on sites without resident medical cover."},{"h":"Benzodiazepines first, not haloperidol","x":"NICE recommends oral lorazepam first line for DTs. Antipsychotics alone lower the fit threshold and do not treat withdrawal; add them only if agitation persists."},{"h":"Delirium, not psychosis","x":"Fluctuating attention and disorientation point to delirium. Primary psychosis usually leaves orientation intact. Test both and record the result."},{"h":"Look for the second problem","x":"Confused drinkers often fall. Check for scalp wounds, unequal pupils and focal signs; a subdural can look identical to withdrawal. Low threshold for CT."},{"h":"MCA, not MHA","x":"Treating delirium from a physical cause sits under the MCA. Assess capacity, act in best interests and record proportionate restraint. Use the MHA only with senior advice."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Recognise delirium tremens, describe the mental state, and manage it safely with urgent medical transfer."},"c-7-1-3":{"obj":["Confirmed clear consciousness and full orientation, and separated hallucinosis from DTs on the record","Mapped when the voices started against when he cut down","Asked directly about weapons, 'self-defence' plans, a named target and suicide, and recorded a risk formulation","Agreed informal admission for assisted withdrawal, thiamine and a short antipsychotic course, with a review after 2–4 weeks off alcohol"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"They're outside right now. They're going to break in and kill me tonight.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying, and I believe the voices feel completely real. You are safe in here. Have you thought about protecting yourself if they come? Do you have anything at home you could use as a weapon?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"This has nothing to do with drink. They're real people, I've seen their van.\"","r":"\"I understand it feels like real people. I'm not saying you imagined everything. I do notice the voices started soon after you cut down, and that pattern is common. Can we treat both and see what happens?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"They're calling me a paedophile. I'm not one. I'll sort them out myself.\"","r":"\"Being called that would make anyone furious. I want to keep you and others safe. When you say 'sort them out', what have you thought about doing?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Does this mean I've got schizophrenia now?\"","r":"\"Not necessarily. Voices like this often come from heavy drinking and stopping, and they usually settle within weeks once you are off alcohol. If they don't, we will look again. You won't be labelled today.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want those tablets that make you a zombie.\"","r":"\"That's a fair worry. I'd suggest a low dose at night, for a short time only, to quieten the voices and help you sleep. We'll review it within a few weeks and stop it if the voices have gone.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If I just stop drinking completely, will the voices go away?\"","r":"\"Staying off alcohol gives you the best chance they will. But stopping suddenly on your own is risky after 15 years. Let us manage the withdrawal safely here, then plan how to stay off it.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Chlordiazepoxide per trust protocol if CIWA-Ar shows withdrawal; Pabrinex 1 pair daily for 3–5 days then thiamine 100 mg three times daily","Short antipsychotic course if distressed: olanzapine 5 mg at night (max 20 mg) or low-dose haloperidol with a baseline ECG; review at 2–4 weeks","Urine drug screen; LFT, GGT, MCV, U&E, glucose"],"psych":["Brief motivational work on abstinence once settled; alcohol care team review","Review the diagnosis after 2–4 weeks of abstinence; refer to early intervention in psychosis if voices persist"],"social":["Community drug and alcohol service referral before discharge","Check housing and neighbour conflict; housing officer if he feels unable to return home"],"safety":["Risk plan: no weapons access, named target, suicidality; general observation, increase if threats escalate","If he refuses admission and meets criteria, AMHP and section 12 doctor for an MHA assessment","If a specific person is threatened, discuss disclosure with the consultant (GMC public interest)"]},"facts":[{"h":"Clear head, loud voices","x":"Alcoholic hallucinosis: vivid, usually threatening, often third-person voices with clear consciousness and full orientation. That clear consciousness is what separates it from DTs."},{"h":"Psychosis counts under the MHA","x":"Dependence alone is not a mental disorder under the MHA, but an alcohol-induced psychosis is. If he needs admission and refuses, an MHA assessment is lawful."},{"h":"Set a review date","x":"Most hallucinosis settles within days to weeks of abstinence. A minority persist; still hearing voices after about a month without alcohol, think primary psychosis and re-assess."},{"h":"Persecuted people defend themselves","x":"Threatening voices drive 'pre-emptive' violence. Always ask about weapons and plans to confront the neighbours, not just about suicide."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Distinguish alcoholic hallucinosis from DTs and primary psychosis, assess risk, and plan management."},"c-7-1-4":{"obj":["Gained consent, offered a chaperone and completed a structured cerebellar check: gait, heel-shin, finger-nose, rapid alternating movements, nystagmus and speech","Looked for asterixis, jaundice, spider naevi, ascites and fetor, and named the likely hepatic encephalopathy","Held all sedatives, given Pabrinex and sent bloods including INR and glucose","Presented the findings in one clear sentence to a senior and arranged same-day medical review"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why are you poking my fingers and watching me walk? I came in for my nerves.\"","r":"\"Fair question. The nurses noticed you're unsteady and a bit muddled. Alcohol can affect the balance centre of the brain and the liver, and both can cause this. These checks tell us which, so we treat the right thing.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want you looking at my stomach.\"","r":"\"That's fine to say. I'd like to check for fluid, because it changes how we treat you. A female nurse can be here with you, and we can stop at any point. Would that be alright?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'm just tired. Give me something to help me sleep and I'll be fine tomorrow.\"","r":"\"I can see you're exhausted. Sleeping tablets could make this much worse, because your liver can't clear them well right now. The medical team can give you treatment that helps clear your head instead.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I've done this to myself, haven't I? My liver's gone.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry you're feeling that. Your liver is struggling, but this drowsiness often improves with treatment. Today is about getting you the right care, not about blame.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"(Nurse) \"She's had diazepam as needed all week for withdrawal. Shall I give her another?\"","r":"\"Thanks for checking. Please hold it. Her drowsiness and flapping tremor look like liver-related confusion, and more sedative could make it worse. Keep her on regular NEWS2 and call me if she gets drowsier.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"(Daughter) \"She was talking fine on Monday. Why is she so confused now?\"","r":"\"It is upsetting to see her like this. When the liver struggles, toxins build up and affect the brain. Something often sets it off, such as an infection or constipation. The medical team are coming to find and treat it.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Stop benzodiazepines and other sedatives; if withdrawal cover is essential, use short-acting oxazepam or lorazepam on senior and pharmacist advice","Pabrinex IV 2 pairs three times daily if Wernicke's cannot be excluded","Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, INR, glucose, CRP, ammonia if available, cultures if febrile","Medical team: lactulose titrated to 2–3 soft stools a day; treat the trigger (infection, bleeding, constipation, electrolytes); rifaximin 550 mg twice daily to prevent recurrence"],"psych":["Revisit low mood once encephalopathy clears; screen for depression and suicide risk","Alcohol care team and hepatology input on abstinence and relapse prevention"],"social":["Update the daughter with consent; ward social worker to plan discharge support","Community alcohol service referral when medically stable"],"safety":["Falls plan: lying and standing BP, bed low, footwear, enhanced observation","NEWS2 at least 4-hourly; escalate any drop in GCS; 999 transfer if unstable on a standalone site","Reassess capacity daily as confusion fluctuates; record it"]},"facts":[{"h":"Truncal versus limb ataxia","x":"Alcohol damages the cerebellar vermis first: wide-based gait and truncal ataxia, with finger-nose often near normal. Limb signs point more to a lateral cerebellar lesion."},{"h":"Asterixis is not tremor","x":"Ask her to hold arms out, wrists cocked back, for 30 seconds. A coarse, irregular flap means metabolic encephalopathy. Withdrawal tremor is fine, fast and constant."},{"h":"Find the trigger","x":"Hepatic encephalopathy is usually set off by infection, GI bleeding, constipation, low potassium, dehydration or sedatives. Check bowels, stool colour and temperature."},{"h":"Sedatives can tip her over","x":"Benzodiazepines can trigger or deepen hepatic encephalopathy. A drowsy patient with liver disease gets fewer sedatives, not more."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Perform a cerebellar examination, look for chronic liver disease and encephalopathy signs, and escalate correctly."},"c-7-1-5":{"obj":["Checked LFTs, U&E, pregnancy, opioid use and her drinking goal before choosing a medicine","Explained acamprosate, naltrexone and disulfiram in plain words, with how each feels and what can go wrong","Agreed one medicine, its dose, start date, duration and who prescribes it after the first month","Booked a community alcohol service appointment within 2 weeks and given mutual-aid and crisis contacts","Agreed with her that a lapse means calling the team, not dropping out"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I've let everyone down three times already. What's the point?\"","r":"\"It takes real strength to come back a fourth time. Most people need more than one go before it sticks. Each attempt teaches us something. This time we can add medicine and support, which many people never get.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Which one's the best? Just tell me what to take.\"","r":"\"Acamprosate and naltrexone work about equally well. Acamprosate eases cravings and your kidneys are fine for it. Naltrexone takes the pleasure out of a drink, but needs liver checks. Which matters more to you: cravings, or a slip turning into a binge?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"The one that makes you sick if you drink. That's what I need. I'll start it tonight.\"","r":"\"I like that you want something strong. Disulfiram can work, but it needs someone to watch you take it daily and at least a day without alcohol first. Do you have someone who would do that?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will these tablets mess up my liver even more?\"","r":"\"That's a sensible worry, given your blood results. Acamprosate is not processed by the liver, so it suits you well. If you choose naltrexone, we'll recheck your liver tests regularly.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I take co-codamol most days for my back. Is that a problem?\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me, that matters. Naltrexone would block the codeine and could put you into withdrawal, so it's not right for you now. Acamprosate is safe alongside it.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"AA didn't work for me. I don't want to sit in a church hall talking about God.\"","r":"\"That's fine. There are other options: SMART Recovery uses practical, non-religious methods, and the community alcohol team offers one-to-one work. Medicine works better with some kind of support. Which sounds more like you?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Acamprosate: 666 mg three times daily if 60 kg or more (under 60 kg: 666 mg morning, 333 mg midday, 333 mg night); start straight after detox; up to 6 months; avoid if creatinine over 120 micromol/L or severe liver disease","Naltrexone alternative: 25 mg on day 1, then 50 mg daily; LFTs before starting and during treatment; no opioids","Disulfiram only if supervised: start at least 24 hours after last drink, 200 mg daily; LFTs at baseline, monitor every 2 weeks for 2 months, then monthly for 4 months","Pregnancy test before prescribing; oral thiamine 100 mg three times daily continues"],"psych":["Community alcohol service: CBT-based relapse prevention or behavioural couples therapy if she has a partner, within 2 weeks","Mutual aid: AA or SMART Recovery; write a relapse-warning plan with her before discharge"],"social":["GP letter: drug, dose, duration, monitoring, GP takes over prescribing after the first month","Ask about children at home and partner's drinking; Carers' assessment by the local authority for any supporting relative"],"safety":["Follow-up within 7 days of discharge (72 hours if suicide risk); crisis line or NHS 111 option 2","Naltrexone: opioid-blocker alert card and a record flag","DVLA advice after assisted withdrawal, recorded in the notes"]},"facts":[{"h":"Acamprosate depends on kidneys","x":"Cleared by the kidneys, not the liver. Good when LFTs are raised; avoid if creatinine is over 120 micromol/L. Main side effect is diarrhoea."},{"h":"Naltrexone blocks opioid painkillers","x":"It blocks codeine, tramadol and morphine, and can trigger withdrawal in anyone using opioids. Give an alert card, and stop it a few days before planned surgery."},{"h":"Disulfiram reactions can be severe","x":"Even alcohol in mouthwash, sauces or hand gel can cause flushing, vomiting, palpitations and collapse. Rare hepatitis: tell her to stop and seek help if jaundiced."},{"h":"Start soon after detox","x":"NICE: start acamprosate or naltrexone as soon as withdrawal is complete, alongside psychological support, for up to 6 months or longer if helpful."},{"h":"A lapse is not a discharge","x":"Treat a slip as information, not failure. Keep the medicine going unless drinking becomes heavy and sustained, and keep her on the caseload."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain acamprosate, naltrexone and disulfiram, help her choose one, and arrange community follow-up."},"c-7-2-1":{"obj":["Taken a structured drug history: what, how much, route, injecting practice, other sedatives and overdoses","Confirmed dependence with a COWS score and urine drug screen, and phoned his drug service and pharmacy before prescribing","Explained methadone and buprenorphine in plain words and agreed a starting plan with the addiction team","Offered blood-borne virus testing, hepatitis B vaccination and a take-home naloxone kit","Kept him on the ward with a withdrawal plan he accepts, without misusing his section"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Give me methadone now or I'm walking out. I'll get gear on the street.\"","r":"\"I hear you, and I want to stop you feeling like this. Leaving to use would be risky: you are on a section, and street gear is unpredictable. Give me an hour to check your test and your drug service, then we can start treatment.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You think I'm lying about how much I use? I'm rattling, look at me.\"","r":"\"I can see you're in withdrawal, and I believe you. Everyone gets the same checks, because the amount in street heroin varies so much that a guessed dose can kill. Checking protects you.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What's the difference between methadone and the other one, Subutex?\"","r":"\"Both stop withdrawal and cravings. Methadone is a liquid, very effective, but riskier in overdose. Buprenorphine is a tablet or film, safer if you mix drugs, but you must be in withdrawal before the first dose. We'll choose together.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've shared needles a couple of times. Never been tested.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest. That's really useful. Can we test your blood for hepatitis B, C and HIV while you're here? If anything is positive, there is good treatment now. We can also vaccinate you against hepatitis B.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can I just have some diazepam to take the edge off?\"","r":"\"I understand you want relief now. Mixing diazepam with opioids is one of the main causes of overdose, so I won't add that. I can give medicine for the cramps, diarrhoea and sickness while we sort your substitute medicine.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I tell you all this, will social services take my kids?\"","r":"\"That is a real worry for many parents. Using drugs doesn't mean your children will be taken. My job is to make sure they're safe and you get help. Who looks after them, and where is any medicine stored at home?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Methadone oral solution 1 mg/mL: start 10–30 mg once daily (lower if tolerance unclear), supervised; increase by 5–10 mg every few days, no more than 30 mg above start in week 1; usual maintenance 60–120 mg","Or buprenorphine: first dose 4–8 mg once COWS shows moderate withdrawal (at least 8–12 hours after heroin); usual maintenance 12–16 mg daily, max 32 mg","Symptom relief meanwhile: loperamide 4 mg then 2 mg after each loose stool (max 16 mg a day), metoclopramide 10 mg up to three times daily","Baseline ECG; repeat if methadone over 100 mg or other QTc risks; urine drug screen; BBV tests with consent; hepatitis B vaccine course"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing on treatment goals; addiction liaison team to agree titration within 24 hours","Treat the mania in parallel; review risk of using on leave"],"social":["Phone community drug service and dispensing pharmacy to confirm no current script and last collection","Ask about children; ward social worker to check safe storage and any safeguarding referral","Discharge: confirmed community prescription starting the next day, pharmacy told in writing"],"safety":["COWS every 4 hours; naloxone on the chart; bleep if drowsy, pinpoint pupils or breathing under 10","Before leave: tolerance warning, take-home naloxone kit, search on return under ward policy","Record on CD chart and controlled drug register"]},"facts":[{"h":"No safe heroin conversion exists","x":"Street purity varies too much to convert reported heroin to methadone. Start low and titrate against objective withdrawal. A confident 'I use a gram a day' is not a dose."},{"h":"Methadone builds up slowly","x":"It takes about 5 days to reach steady state. Most early methadone deaths happen in the first 2 weeks, often from increasing the dose too fast."},{"h":"Buprenorphine needs real withdrawal","x":"Given too early, it displaces heroin and causes sudden, severe withdrawal. Wait until COWS shows moderate withdrawal, usually 8–12 hours after heroin and longer after methadone."},{"h":"Methadone lengthens the QTc","x":"MHRA: ECG for doses over 100 mg, heart or liver disease, low potassium or magnesium, or other QTc-prolonging drugs such as citalopram or some antipsychotics."},{"h":"Tolerance drops within days","x":"After even a few days in hospital, tolerance falls. Most overdoses happen on leave or after discharge. Every patient leaves with naloxone and a warning."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a treatment","do":"Assess and confirm opioid dependence and withdrawal, then explain opioid substitution treatment options."},"c-7-2-2":{"obj":["Gone to the drowsy patient first, run ABCDE, and given IM or IV naloxone with the time of each dose recorded","Arranged medical transfer and at least 6 hours of observation, because naloxone wears off before long-acting opioids","Declined to give unconfirmed methadone after four missed days, explained why in plain words, and charted symptom relief","Phoned or planned to phone the community pharmacy to confirm dose and last collection, and suspend the community script","Completed a Datix, a search under ward policy and a take-home naloxone plan for both patients"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I've been on 80 mil for two years. Why won't you just give me my dose?\"","r":"\"I believe you, and I'm sorry you're feeling this rough. After four days off, your body has lost some tolerance. Your old dose tonight could stop your breathing. I'll treat the symptoms now and confirm everything with your pharmacy in the morning.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just give me half, then. Forty. That's safe, isn't it?\"","r":"\"I can see you're trying to meet me halfway. Without knowing your real dose or what else you've taken, I can't call any amount safe tonight. Withdrawal is horrible, but it won't kill you. I'll make you as comfortable as I can.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Fine. I'll go out and score, then. Your choice.\"","r":"\"It is your choice, and I'd really rather you didn't. Using tonight, with lower tolerance, is when people die. Stay, let me get you medicine for the cramps and sleep, and we'll restart methadone properly tomorrow. Will you give me tonight?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"(Woman, after naloxone) \"I only had a bit of gear and a couple of pregabalin. Same as always.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. After a few days on the ward your body can't take what it used to, and pregabalin makes breathing worse. You need to be watched for several hours, because this can come back.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"(Woman) \"I'm fine now. I'm not going to A&E. Leave me alone.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you're awake, and I understand you want to rest. The injection I gave wears off within an hour, but what you took lasts much longer. You could stop breathing again. Please let us take you where you can be monitored.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"(Woman) \"Am I going to get kicked off the ward or arrested for this?\"","r":"\"I can hear you're scared. My first job is keeping you alive, not punishing you. We will need to talk about leave and safety, but you won't be thrown out for being honest with us.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Naloxone 400 micrograms IM or IV, repeat every 2–3 minutes to effect (BNF), aiming for breathing, not full wakefulness; infusion via medical team if long-acting opioid","Oxygen, airway support, glucose check, ECG; consider other sedatives (pregabalin, benzodiazepines, alcohol, nitazenes needing larger naloxone doses)","Methadone patient: COWS, then loperamide 4 mg then 2 mg per loose stool, metoclopramide 10 mg, paracetamol 1 g up to four times daily; no opioid until dose confirmed","Next day: addiction team re-titrates from a reduced dose if 3 or more days missed; ECG before restarting"],"psych":["Once recovered, assess whether the overdose was intended; review mood and suicide risk","Addiction liaison team review for both patients next working day"],"social":["Pharmacy: confirm dose, last collection, supervision; suspend the community script during admission","Discharge: confirmed restart date with the community prescriber, pharmacy told in writing, GP letter"],"safety":["Call 2222 or 999 if unresponsive; minimum 6 hours monitored observation after the last naloxone dose","Search on return from leave under ward policy; review leave conditions at the MDT","Datix; inform on-call consultant; take-home naloxone kit and training for both patients"]},"facts":[{"h":"Naloxone wears off first","x":"Naloxone lasts about an hour; methadone acts for 24 hours or more. A patient who wakes can stop breathing again. Observe for at least 6 hours, often longer."},{"h":"Missed days mean lost tolerance","x":"Three or more missed methadone doses: do not give the usual dose. Re-assess and restart lower, usually after confirming with the prescriber. Five or more: treat as a new start."},{"h":"Withdrawal hurts, overdose kills","x":"Opioid withdrawal in an adult is miserable but rarely dangerous. Withholding an unconfirmed dose overnight is safe; giving a guessed dose is not."},{"h":"Anyone can give naloxone","x":"UK law allows anyone to give naloxone injection to save a life. Take-home kits: Prenoxad (IM, 400-microgram doses) or Nyxoid (1.8 mg nasal spray)."},{"h":"Nitazenes need more naloxone","x":"These synthetic opioids are far stronger than heroin and are increasingly found in street supply. Expect to need repeated naloxone doses and longer monitoring."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Decide whether to give methadone after missed doses, then manage possible opioid overdose with naloxone."},"c-7-2-3":{"obj":["Listed every benzodiazepine source (GP, online, street), daily totals, alcohol use, past fits and suicide risk","Converted lorazepam and diazepam to one total diazepam dose, checked with a pharmacist against the BNF","Agreed a written, patient-led reduction schedule with weekly dispensing from a single prescriber","Referred her for CBT for anxiety and insomnia and given driving advice, recorded in the letter to the GP"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I can't come off them. Last time I tried I thought I was dying.\"","r":"\"That sounds frightening, and it makes sense you're scared. Last time was probably too fast. This time you set the pace, with small steps, and if a step is too hard we pause. You won't be stopped suddenly.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I feel like an addict buying them off the internet. I'm so ashamed.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Many people end up there after years on prescribed tablets. You're not a bad person. Online tablets can contain anything, though, so getting everything from one prescription will make you safer.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"My GP gave me these for nine years. Now you're saying it's my problem?\"","r":"\"You have every right to feel let down. Long-term prescribing was common, and it wasn't your fault. What matters now is that we sort it out together, slowly and safely.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why switch to diazepam? The lorazepam works better for me.\"","r":"\"Lorazepam wears off in a few hours, so you get mini-withdrawals between doses. Diazepam lasts much longer and evens things out. That makes the reduction smoother and easier to do in small steps.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can I just keep a few lorazepam for bad days?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting a safety net. Extra doses on top restart the cycle and make the plan harder. Let's build in other help for bad days instead: a breathing technique, a call to the team, and pausing a step if needed.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Some nights I wish I just wouldn't wake up.\"","r":"\"I'm really glad you told me. Can you tell me more about those thoughts? Have you ever thought about taking your tablets all at once?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Convert to diazepam (lorazepam 1 mg is about diazepam 10 mg; BNF table), stabilise 1–2 weeks, then reduce by about one-tenth every 2–4 weeks","Below 20 mg slow to 1–2 mg steps, below 5 mg to 0.5–1 mg steps; pause any step rather than go back up","Screen for alcohol (AUDIT) and depression; treat underlying anxiety, e.g. sertraline 50 mg daily, review at 4 weeks"],"psych":["CBT for generalised anxiety and CBT for insomnia via NHS Talking Therapies (self-referral possible in England)","Bereavement-focused work if grief is unresolved; written reduction chart she keeps"],"social":["GP to issue weekly prescriptions and block other sources; one named prescriber","Community drug and alcohol service if online buying continues or other substances are used"],"safety":["Suicide risk assessed and recorded; crisis contacts given (NHS 111 option 2)","Driving: do not drive if drowsy; DVLA notification if dependence or misuse","CMHT review in 4 weeks; GP to contact CMHT if withdrawal becomes severe"]},"facts":[{"h":"Lorazepam 1 mg = diazepam 10 mg","x":"BNF lists lorazepam 0.5 mg as roughly diazepam 5 mg. Add all sources together, convert, and get a pharmacist to check before writing it down."},{"h":"Slow wins","x":"Reduce by about a tenth every 2–4 weeks; the whole taper often takes months. NICE NG215 says let the patient guide the pace and review regularly."},{"h":"Sudden stop can cause fits","x":"Abrupt withdrawal after long use can cause seizures, confusion and psychosis. Never stop both drugs at once, especially if she also drinks."},{"h":"Online pills are unknown","x":"Tablets bought online may be counterfeit or contain much stronger drugs such as bromazolam. Overdose risk is unpredictable; ask about it directly."}],"type":"Medication review","do":"Assess benzodiazepine dependence and agree a safe, patient-led diazepam reduction plan."},"c-7-2-4":{"obj":["Established the time of the last GBL dose and how often he dosed, and recognised withdrawal delirium, not primary psychosis","Told the A&E registrar it is a medical emergency needing high-dose benzodiazepines, close monitoring and critical care review","Used the MCA, not the MHA, and recorded capacity and any restraint","Covered chemsex, sexual health, consent and the friends' harm-reduction information with his permission"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"They're coming for me. I can hear them outside the curtain.\"","r":"\"You're in hospital and I'm a doctor. You're safe here. Your body is reacting to stopping something suddenly, and that can make the mind play tricks. We have medicine that will calm this down.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"(A&E registrar) \"He's psychotic. Can't you just take him to your ward?\"","r":"\"I see why it looks like psychosis. But he dosed GBL every hour or two and stopped 8 hours ago; he's hot, fast and delirious. This is withdrawal, and it can kill. He needs medical care and big benzodiazepine doses.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"(Friend) \"He uses it at parties too, with crystal meth. Should we have said?\"","r":"\"Thank you, that really helps. Meth changes how we treat him and what we watch for. Do you know when he last used it, or if he's taken anything else, like alcohol?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"(Friend) \"We should've taken him in sooner. We thought he'd sleep it off.\"","r":"\"You brought him in, and that was the right call. Most people don't know how quickly GBL withdrawal turns serious. He's getting the treatment he needs now.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Patient, next day) \"Can I just stop on my own next time? I know what to expect now.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you're thinking ahead. Stopping GBL suddenly is one of the most dangerous withdrawals there is. Next time, stop with the addiction team, usually in hospital, with medicine planned in advance. Can I refer you?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"(Patient) \"Some of the parties got out of hand. I'm not sure everything was what I wanted.\"","r":"\"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. What happened to you matters. Would you like to talk about it now, or with someone from the sexual health team? You can decide what happens next.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Medical team, guided by TOXBASE: early high-dose diazepam (often 10–20 mg repeated to calm, light sedation), large totals may be needed; IV lorazepam if oral not possible","Baclofen 10 mg three times daily on specialist advice for benzodiazepine-resistant withdrawal","Monitor NEWS2, temperature, CK, U&E, glucose; ECG; HDU/ITU if needing very high doses or deteriorating","Avoid antipsychotics as first line; slow, symptom-led benzodiazepine reduction with the addiction team"],"psych":["Addiction liaison review next morning; plan elective detox and relapse prevention","Explore chemsex context, mood and suicide risk once delirium clears"],"social":["Sexual health or chemsex service referral: HIV and STI testing, PEP or PrEP","Harm reduction leaflet and service contacts for friends, with his consent"],"safety":["Assess and record capacity; best-interests treatment and proportionate restraint under MCA s5–6","Close nursing observation; falls and seizure precautions","Safeguarding or IDVA input if sexual assault or exploitation disclosed"]},"facts":[{"h":"GBL withdrawal starts within hours","x":"Heavy round-the-clock users can withdraw within 1–6 hours of the last dose. Delirium, seizures and autonomic instability can follow within a day. Ask 'when, and how often?'"},{"h":"Big benzodiazepine doses needed","x":"Doses far above usual alcohol-withdrawal amounts are often needed. Undertreating early is the classic error. Titrate to symptoms on a monitored ward, guided by TOXBASE."},{"h":"Antipsychotics are second line","x":"They do not treat withdrawal, lower the seizure threshold and worsen temperature control. Use them only on specialist advice if agitation persists despite benzodiazepines."},{"h":"GBL is now Class B","x":"GHB, GBL and 1,4-butanediol are Class B drugs. Reassure him that his care is confidential and that the priority is treating him, not reporting him."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Recognise GBL withdrawal as a medical emergency and arrange acute medical care, not psychiatric admission."},"c-7-2-5":{"obj":["Checked temperature, pulse, BP and NEWS2 in the s136 suite and named stimulant toxicity as a medical emergency","Arranged A&E transfer for cooling, fluids, ECG, CK and benzodiazepines, and told the AMHP with the detention time recorded","Known the 24-hour s136 limit and the 12-hour extension rule, and recorded the reason if used","Asked the 19-year-old about bladder pain, frequency and blood in urine, and arranged GP and drug service follow-up"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Get off me! I've done nothing wrong. Let me out of here!\"","r":"\"I'm a doctor and I'm here to help. You're very hot and your heart is racing, and that's dangerous. Can you sit down with me for a moment and have some water while we check you?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"(Police officer) \"Can't we just hold him face down until he settles?\"","r":"\"I know you want to keep everyone safe. Face-down holds on someone this hot and agitated can kill. Please avoid prone restraint. We need to get him to A&E now for cooling and medicine.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(AMHP) \"Can we finish the MHA assessment first? I've got the doctors arranged.\"","r":"\"I'd like to, but he is medically unwell: temperature 39 and pulse 140. He goes to A&E first; the s136 still applies there. Once he's stable, we can assess properly. His psychosis may settle by then.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"(Woman) \"Ketamine's not addictive. I just do a bit most nights.\"","r":"\"Lots of people think that. Daily use can become a habit that is hard to stop, and the pain you describe may be ketamine affecting your bladder. That's worth checking now, while it can still improve.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"(Woman) \"Will my bladder be damaged forever?\"","r":"\"That's a fair worry. If you stop early, the bladder often recovers. Continuing can cause lasting damage. Your GP can check your urine and kidneys, and the drug service can help you cut down.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"(Woman) \"I'm not going to a drug service. I'm not a junkie.\"","r":"\"You're right, you're not. The service sees students and young professionals all the time, and it's confidential. Could we start with your GP checking your bladder, and you decide about the rest?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["A&E: diazepam 10–20 mg oral or IV, repeated as needed for agitation (benzodiazepines first, per TOXBASE); avoid antipsychotics without senior advice","Active cooling if temperature is 38.5 or higher; IV fluids; ECG; CK, U&E, LFT, glucose, troponin if chest pain","Ketamine user: urine dip, MSU, U&E and eGFR via GP; urology referral if haematuria, severe pain or poor kidney function"],"psych":["MHA assessment once medically fit; reassess for ongoing psychosis","Woman: brief motivational chat on ketamine use; screen mood and suicide risk"],"social":["Tell the AMHP and s136 coordinator; record time detention started and any extension","Refer both to the local drug service; trust dual diagnosis lead if needed; GP letters"],"safety":["Continuous observation; no prone restraint; record use of force under the Use of Force Act","Report any new or unusual substance through the local drug alert system","Datix for restraint or incidents; review s136 time limit at every handover"]},"facts":[{"h":"Hot and agitated means medical","x":"Temperature over 38.5, tachycardia and agitation after stimulants can progress to rhabdomyolysis, kidney failure and death. Treat it as a medical emergency first and a mental health question second."},{"h":"s136: 24 hours plus 12","x":"Detention lasts up to 24 hours from arrival. The doctor responsible for the assessment can extend it by up to 12 hours if the person's condition prevents assessment. Record why."},{"h":"Negative screen means nothing","x":"Most standard urine drug screens miss synthetic cathinones, synthetic cannabinoids and other new substances. Treat what you see, not the result."},{"h":"Ketamine damages the bladder","x":"Regular use causes painful, frequent urination, blood in urine and a shrunken bladder. Stopping early often helps; ask every young ketamine user about bladder symptoms."},{"h":"Restraint can kill","x":"Prone restraint in a hot, exhausted patient risks sudden cardiac arrest. Keep holds short, avoid pressure on the chest and back, and watch breathing throughout."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Recognise stimulant toxicity in the 136 suite as a medical emergency, then give brief ketamine advice."},"c-7-3-1":{"obj":["Agreed one realistic substance goal with each patient using motivational language, not ultimatums","Planned alcohol withdrawal for the woman with AUDIT, bloods, thiamine and a decision on where to detox","Chosen a low-misuse ADHD option with cardiovascular checks and a GP shared care plan","Written joint care plans with the dual diagnosis worker, with crisis contacts and DVLA advice in each GP letter"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"(Man with psychosis) \"Weed is the only thing that calms me down. You can't make me stop.\"","r":"\"You're right, I can't, and I'm not trying to. I'm curious: every time you've used heavily, the voices came back. What do you make of that? What would cutting down even a bit look like for you?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Man with psychosis) \"Isn't cannabis natural? How can it make me ill?\"","r":"\"Natural doesn't always mean safe. Today's strong cannabis has much more THC, which can trigger voices and paranoia. It also makes your medicine work less well. Lower-strength or less often would already help.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"(Woman with depression) \"A bottle of wine isn't drinking. I'm not an alcoholic.\"","r":"\"I'm not putting a label on you. A bottle is about 10 units: most of the 14-unit weekly limit, every night. It can make low mood worse and stop antidepressants working. How would you feel about trying to cut down with support?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"(Woman with depression) \"If I stop drinking I won't sleep at all. It's the only thing that helps.\"","r":"\"Lots of people feel that. Alcohol helps you drop off but wrecks deep sleep, so you wake tired and low. We'll plan stopping safely and give you other help with sleep, so you're not left with nothing.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"(Man with ADHD) \"So because I do a bit of coke, I don't get my meds? That's discrimination.\"","r":"\"I understand why it feels unfair. I do want to treat your ADHD. Cocaine plus a stimulant strains your heart, so I'll choose a longer-acting option, check your heart and review regularly. Would that work for you?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"(Man with ADHD) \"Give me the old methylphenidate and I promise I'll stop the coke.\"","r":"\"I appreciate that promise, and I'd like to support it. Let's start with a once-daily option, small supplies and a review in a month. If you're off cocaine and doing well, we can look at changes then.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Psychosis: continue antipsychotic; consider a long-acting injection if adherence is poor; annual physical health checks","Alcohol: AUDIT, LFT, GGT, FBC, U&E; community or inpatient withdrawal by risk; thiamine 100 mg three times daily; acamprosate or naltrexone after detox; antidepressant review after 3–4 weeks abstinent","ADHD: atomoxetine 40 mg daily for 7 days then 80 mg (max 100 mg), or Elvanse (long-acting stimulant prodrug) 30 mg in the morning, up by 20 mg weekly (max 70 mg); baseline and each change: pulse, BP, weight"],"psych":["CBT for psychosis (at least 16 sessions) and family intervention via the early intervention team","Motivational interviewing with the dual diagnosis worker; CBT for depression once drinking is stable","ADHD: psychoeducation and coaching through the adult ADHD service"],"social":["Dual diagnosis worker and community drug and alcohol service join each CPA review","GP shared care agreement for ADHD prescribing; weekly or fortnightly controlled drug supplies","Carers' assessment by the local authority for the young man's family"],"safety":["Crisis plans with named contacts and NHS 111 option 2 for each patient","Woman: suicide risk review (alcohol plus depression); man with psychosis: 72-hour follow-up after discharge","DVLA advice recorded in each GP letter"]},"facts":[{"h":"Never 'come back when sober'","x":"NICE says people with severe mental illness must not be excluded from mental health services because of substance use. Treat both conditions at the same time, in the same plan."},{"h":"High-strength cannabis and relapse","x":"Daily high-potency cannabis is linked to about five times the risk of psychosis and predicts relapse and readmission. Cutting down or switching to lower-strength products still reduces harm."},{"h":"Let mood settle off alcohol","x":"Low mood often lifts after 3–4 weeks of abstinence. Unless depression is severe or risk is high, review the need for an antidepressant after detox."},{"h":"ADHD plus cocaine: lower misuse risk","x":"Prefer atomoxetine or a long-acting stimulant such as lisdexamfetamine (Elvanse) over immediate-release methylphenidate. Small supplies, regular reviews and cardiovascular checks."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Write a joint dual-diagnosis plan for each patient without excluding anyone for substance use."},"c-8-1-1":{"obj":["Screened pain, bowels, seizures, medicines and environment with the house manager and named the most likely cause","Declined a risperidone increase and given the reason in one sentence the manager accepted","Booked a GP review within 48 hours (abdomen, ears, mouth, urine, bloods) with reasonable adjustments flagged","Started daily bowel, ABC and DisDAT pain charts with a named staff member responsible","Given red-flag safety-netting (vomiting, hard swollen tummy, drowsiness, seizure = 999) and a 2-week review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"We've tried everything. Just put the risperidone up, my staff are getting hurt.","r":"Staff getting hurt is serious and I'm taking it seriously. If he's in pain, more risperidone just quietens the signal while the cause carries on. Give me 48 hours to find it, and we'll agree what staff do meanwhile."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"He goes to the toilet most days, so it can't be his bowels.","r":"That helps, thank you. Going daily doesn't rule it out: small hard stools or soiling can leak round a blocked bowel. Can someone check his chart and see if his tummy looks swollen or feels hard today?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"How would we even know he's in pain? He can't tell us.","r":"You know his normal better than anyone. Grimacing, guarding one spot, rubbing his jaw or ear, refusing food or new poor sleep are all clues. The DisDAT tool helps you record his distress signs against his content signs."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"If he hurts someone again, the placement will end. We can't keep him.","r":"I understand the pressure on the house. Let's protect his home: the CLDT behaviour specialist will review the PBS plan with you this week, and you'll have the duty number if things escalate."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"He won't let a GP near him. Last time he bit the nurse.","r":"That's really useful to know. We'll ask for the first or last slot, a quiet room, his favourite staff member, or a home visit. The CLDT nurse can prepare him with pictures beforehand."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"My night staff are exhausted and two have phoned in sick this week.","r":"That sounds hard on the whole team. Let's write down two things staff do the moment he starts hitting his head, and I'll flag the staffing to his care manager so the house gets support."}],"bps":{"bio":["No change to risperidone. GP review within 48 h: abdomen, ears, teeth, urine dip; bloods FBC, CRP, U&E, LFT, TFT, valproate level; ammonia if drowsy or confused","If constipated (GP-led): macrogol 1–3 sachets daily; for impaction up to 8 sachets daily for no more than 3 days; daily bowel chart with Bristol stool type","Suspected pain: regular paracetamol 1 g four times daily (reduce if under 50 kg) for 1 week; see if ABC incidents fall","Dental review via community special care dentistry; sedation for treatment if needed, decided in best interests"],"psych":["ABC chart for every incident for 2 weeks, analysed by the CLDT behaviour specialist","PBS plan refreshed: predictable routine and familiar staff (proactive), early warning signs, agreed reactive steps"],"social":["House manager: keep the same 2–3 familiar staff on his shifts where possible","Care manager (local authority) told of staffing strain; package review if needed","Health action plan and hospital passport updated with findings"],"safety":["999 or A&E for vomiting, hard swollen abdomen, fever, drowsiness or a seizure","Unexplained injuries or fear of one staff member: Care Act s42 concern to the local authority the same day","Physical intervention only as written in the PBS plan; every use recorded","CLDT medical review in 2 weeks with a STOMP check on risperidone"]},"facts":[{"h":"Constipation kills in this group","x":"Faecal impaction and obstruction recur in LeDeR death reviews. Overflow can look like loose stools. Ask the stool type, last hard stool and whether the tummy is distended."},{"h":"Where he hits points to the pain","x":"Hitting one side of the head: ear or tooth. Punching the abdomen: bowel, bladder or reflux. Refusing food: mouth or swallowing pain. Map the target before treating the act."},{"h":"Antipsychotics can cause the agitation","x":"Akathisia from risperidone looks like restless distress and worsens with a higher dose. Ask: pacing, unable to sit, worse since a dose rise?"},{"h":"Valproate: level, liver and ammonia","x":"Toxicity brings drowsiness, tremor and confusion; raised ammonia causes encephalopathy even at normal levels. Low levels mean breakthrough seizures. Ask for level, LFT and ammonia if drowsy."},{"h":"PRN sedation hides the diagnosis","x":"A sedated man cannot show where it hurts. Benzodiazepines add falls and aspiration risk. Treat the cause; keep PRN for the red phase of the PBS plan."}],"type":"Collateral history","do":"Take a history from the house manager, check pain, constipation, epilepsy and medication, and plan without extra medication."},"c-8-1-2":{"obj":["Explained the reduction plan as three short ideas, each checked with teach-back in her own words","Agreed the first dose step and the next review date with her directly, not only with her key worker","Recorded her consent before anything was shared with her key worker","Given her an easy read plan with a named contact and what to do if she feels upset","Booked weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids and prolactin with dates"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"If I stop the tablet, will I get angry again?","r":"That's a good worry to tell me. You've been doing really well for a long time. We'll take away a tiny bit, then wait and see. If you feel cross, we can slow down."},{"tag":"Acquiescence","p":"Yes. Yes, okay, doctor.","r":"Thank you. I want to check I explained it well. Can you show me or say back what's going to happen with your tablet?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"Will I lose the weight if I take less?","r":"Some people do lose some weight, but it can be slow. Healthy food and walking help too. We'll weigh you at every visit so you can see how you're doing."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"My periods stopped and sometimes milk comes out of my boobs.","r":"Thank you for telling me, that's really important and not your fault. The tablet can do that. We'll do a blood test, and taking less tablet may help it stop."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"Honestly, I'd rather not rock the boat. She's settled now.","r":"I understand, nobody wants her unsettled. But six years on this tablet has cost her 14 kg and raises her diabetes risk. Small, slow steps with you watching closely is the safest way to find out if she still needs it."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"If she has a bad day, can we just give her the old dose?","r":"Please don't change it yourselves. Some upset days are her body getting used to less tablet, not illness coming back. Write it down and ring the CLDT; we'll decide together, usually the same day."}],"bps":{"bio":["Risperidone: cut by about a quarter of the current dose (e.g. 2 mg to 1.5 mg) every 4–6 weeks, keeping the same time of day","Baseline then each review: weight, BMI, waist, BP; HbA1c, lipids and prolactin now and 3 months after the last step","Warn about withdrawal in the 1–2 weeks after a step: poor sleep, nausea, irritability, new abnormal movements"],"psych":["CLDT psychologist or PBS practitioner keeps her coping strategies going: what she does when she feels cross","Easy read feelings diary (faces) filled in weekly with her key worker"],"social":["Key worker records sleep, mood and incidents weekly and brings the chart to clinic","Accessible Information Standard flag: easy read letters, longer appointments","Support provider helps with healthy meals and a regular activity such as a walking group"],"safety":["Named CLDT contact and duty number printed in her easy read plan","If upset lasts beyond 2 weeks after a step: hold the dose, look for a life event, then review","Clinic review every 4–6 weeks during reduction; GP copied into each letter"]},"facts":[{"h":"Risperidone raises prolactin","x":"Missed periods, breast milk and low libido are rarely volunteered. Long-term high prolactin thins bone. Ask directly and check prolactin before and after reducing."},{"h":"Teach-back beats 'Do you understand?'","x":"Many people with learning disability say yes to please. Ask her to tell or show you the plan. If she can't, change the explanation, not the conclusion."},{"h":"One idea per sentence","x":"Short sentences, one fact each, everyday words: 'less tablet, slowly', not 'taper'. Pause and check before the next idea. Pictures help her keep it."},{"h":"Withdrawal is not relapse","x":"Poor sleep or irritability in the first weeks after a cut usually settles. Hold the step and look for a trigger before restoring the old dose."},{"h":"No licence for adult behaviour","x":"Risperidone is not licensed for behaviour that challenges in adults with learning disability. Record the reason, the benefit seen and the plan to review or reduce."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain the medicine reduction plan in easy-read terms, check her understanding, and agree next steps."},"c-8-1-3":{"obj":["Moved him to a quiet, dim room within minutes and agreed a 'stop' signal for breaks","Separated new psychotic symptoms from his autistic baseline using his mother's account, with an onset date","Screened for catatonia, delirium, substances and physical illness (obs, glucose, urine drug screen)","Assessed risk to neighbours, self-neglect and access to weapons, and recorded it","Agreed home treatment or a planned admission with the crisis team and alerted the CLDT for an urgent C(E)TR"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"The wires are in the walls. They're listening. I can't go back there.","r":"That sounds really scary. You are safe in this room right now. I want to help. Can you tell me when you first noticed the wires?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"Too loud. Too loud. Make it stop.","r":"I'll turn the light down and close the door. Only one person will talk. Keep your hands on your ears if it helps. Say 'stop' whenever you need a break."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"He's always been odd about things. It's just his autism getting worse.","r":"You know him best, so that matters. What you've described is new, though: voices, fear of the neighbours, barricading, missing college. A change like that from his usual self points to an illness we can treat."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"I'm not taking tablets. They'll poison me.","r":"Thank you for telling me. You don't have to decide now. Let's talk about what's been happening first. If we do use a tablet, it would be a very small dose and Mum can check it with you."},{"tag":"Question","p":"Is he going to be locked up on a ward?","r":"Not if we can avoid it. My plan is support at home from the home treatment team, plus a quick meeting of everyone who knows him. A ward is only if home isn't safe."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"I smoke weed with a lad at college. It stops my thinking.","r":"Thanks for being honest, I'm not cross. Cannabis can make frightening thoughts and voices stronger. Can you tell me how much and how often?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Before any antipsychotic: weight, BMI, waist, BP, pulse, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, FBC, U&E, LFT, ECG; urine drug screen","If agreed with consultant: risperidone 0.5 mg at night, increase by 0.5 mg every 3–7 days to the lowest effective dose (adult range 2–6 mg)","Catatonia screen (Bush-Francis) each review; if present, lorazepam 1–2 mg challenge on consultant advice and hold the antipsychotic","Weight weekly for 6 weeks then at 12 weeks; HbA1c and lipids at 12 weeks; watch for stiffness and akathisia"],"psych":["Easy read psychoeducation on psychosis and cannabis for him and his mother","CBT for psychosis adapted for learning disability once settled, CLDT psychology","Family intervention offered to him and his mother"],"social":["Home treatment team: daily visits by the same 1–2 staff, with his autism communication plan","CLDT and Dynamic Support Register keyworker arrange an urgent community C(E)TR","College contacted only with his consent; phased return planned later"],"safety":["Mother given the crisis line, NHS 111 option 2 and 999 for immediate danger","Weapons and threats to neighbours asked about directly and recorded","If admission is needed: autism-friendly side room, hospital passport shared, LD liaison nurse involved","CLDT psychiatrist review on Monday"]},"facts":[{"h":"Catatonia hides inside 'more autistic'","x":"Freezing, mutism, food refusal or new posturing are often put down to autism. Score Bush-Francis. Lorazepam usually helps fast; antipsychotics can worsen it."},{"h":"Start at a quarter of the usual dose","x":"People with learning disability get more stiffness, sedation and weight gain. Start risperidone at 0.5 mg, not 2 mg, and titrate every few days."},{"h":"Literal is not thought disorder","x":"Concrete replies, monologues about interests and self-talk may be his baseline. New fears, voices and lost skills compared with his usual self point to psychosis."},{"h":"Admission should trigger a C(E)TR","x":"For an autistic person or someone with learning disability, a community C(E)TR should come before admission where possible. Ring the CLDT or crisis team early, even at weekends."},{"h":"Autism alone still counts under the 1983 Act","x":"Autism is a mental disorder for section 3 now. The 2025 Act will remove detention for autism alone but is not yet in force."}],"type":"Mental state examination + Risk assessment","do":"Distinguish psychosis from his autistic baseline, manage immediate risk, and plan least restrictive care."},"c-8-2-1":{"obj":["Given decision-specific information on sertraline, talking help and no treatment, using easy read and pictures","Tested understanding, retention, weighing and communication with open questions and teach-back, not yes/no","Recorded a capacity outcome for this single decision, quoting her own words as evidence","Asked directly about thoughts of death or self-harm and recorded the answer","Agreed the start date, what to do about side effects and a 2-week check with her"],"snip":[{"tag":"Acquiescence","p":"Yes. Yes, okay.","r":"Thank you. I want to make sure I explained it well. What do you think the tablet is for? Say it any way you like."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"I should be over it. Dad would be cross I'm still crying.","r":"Missing your dad this much shows how much you loved him. Crying isn't wrong. I think something called depression has come on top of the sadness, and that's something we can help with."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"Will the tablet change me? Make me a different person?","r":"No, it won't change who you are. Over a few weeks it can make the sad feelings smaller. If you don't like it, tell us and we'll stop it slowly together."},{"tag":"Question","p":"Do I have to take it forever?","r":"No. If it helps, people usually keep taking it for about six months after they feel better. Then we lower it slowly together."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"Sometimes I think I want to be with my dad.","r":"Thank you for telling me, that's really important. Do you mean you've thought about dying, or about hurting yourself? I want to help keep you safe."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"I don't want tablets. I want to talk to someone.","r":"That's your choice, and talking help is a good option. Can you tell me why you prefer it? Then we'll make a plan together, and you can change your mind later."}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 25 mg once each morning with breakfast for 1 week, then 50 mg; usual adult range 50–200 mg, rises in 50 mg steps at least a week apart","Warn: nausea, loose stools, headache and poor sleep in weeks 1–2, usually settling; mood lifts after 2–4 weeks","GP baseline bloods: TFT, FBC, B12, folate, U&E","Continue for at least 6 months after she feels well, then reduce gradually over weeks"],"psych":["Bereavement support adapted for learning disability (CLDT psychology or local service with easy read materials)","Behavioural activation with her support worker: one valued activity each day"],"social":["Support worker prompts the morning dose, with a blister pack if helpful","Keep her day activities and contact with friends going"],"safety":["CLDT nurse phone check at 2 weeks: side effects, mood, suicidal thoughts","Easy read crisis card: CLDT duty number, NHS 111 option 2, 999","Clinic review at 4 weeks; GP letter the same day"]},"facts":[{"h":"Capacity is one decision at a time","x":"Assess only the sertraline decision. Record which decision, which ability (if any) failed, and the evidence. A learning disability alone proves nothing."},{"h":"Grief or depression?","x":"Grief comes in waves linked to reminders, with self-worth intact. Depression is constant: no pleasure, worthlessness, early waking. Both can follow a death."},{"h":"Sertraline: nausea first, mood later","x":"Nausea peaks in the first week, so take it with breakfast. Benefit takes 2–4 weeks. Warn her so she doesn't stop early, thinking it has failed."},{"h":"A carer's signature means nothing","x":"Only the patient, a health and welfare attorney, a deputy appointed by the court, or a properly made best interests decision can authorise treatment for an adult. A support worker cannot consent."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"Assess her capacity to decide about sertraline using the four-part test, and act lawfully on it."},"c-8-2-2":{"obj":["Pinned down the exact decision (laparoscopic cholecystectomy now) and the surgeon's key facts before seeing him","Heard his answers directly, having agreed with the support worker to prompt rather than answer","Found and recorded the reason for his refusal, separating fear from inability to decide","Advised the surgical team in writing on outcome, decision-maker, IMCA need and a best interests meeting date","Listed at least three reasonable adjustments that could make the operation acceptable to him"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"No hospital. No hospital.","r":"Okay. I can hear you don't want to be in hospital. You're not in trouble. Can you tell me what is scary about hospital?"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"He never understands these things. I'll just answer for him.","r":"You know him well and I need your help. The law says I have to hear from him first. Afterwards, tell me what helps him understand and whether his answers match what you see at home."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"Needles hurt. They held me down.","r":"That sounds frightening, and I'm sorry it happened. We can use cream so needles don't hurt, and someone you trust can stay with you. Nobody will hold you without talking to you first."},{"tag":"Question","p":"Can't his mum just sign the form?","r":"Not for an adult, unless she is his attorney under a health and welfare LPA. If he can't decide, the surgeons decide in his best interests after hearing from him, her and you."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"Can I have tablets instead? I'll take the tablets.","r":"Tablets can help the pain for a while, but they don't take the stones out. The pain will probably come back, and you could get very poorly. Let's look at these pictures together."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"He's said no, so we'll discharge him, yes?","r":"Not yet. A 'no' isn't a capacity outcome. He needs supports and another try; if he still lacks capacity, you make a best interests decision. Can we get the LD liaison nurse in today?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Pain control per surgical team; repeat the capacity assessment once pain and opioids have settled","4AT delirium screen before each capacity attempt","Anaesthetic plan: lidocaine-prilocaine cream at least 1 hour before cannulation, first on the list, oral premedication if agreed in best interests"],"psych":["LD liaison nurse: easy read pictures of the operation, a visit to theatre and recovery, graded desensitisation over days","Past hospital trauma explored and triggers written into his care plan"],"social":["Family traced via the support provider; if none, IMCA instructed through the local IMCA service","Support worker or family member to go with him to the anaesthetic room","Hospital passport and reasonable adjustment flag updated"],"safety":["Best interests meeting within days: surgeon (decision-maker), anaesthetist, family or IMCA, LD liaison nurse","Any restraint planned, necessary and proportionate (MCA s6); significant restraint or sedation goes to the trust legal team for a possible Court of Protection application","DoLS application if he lacks capacity to agree to staying and is under continuous supervision and control"]},"facts":[{"h":"The surgeon owns this assessment","x":"The doctor proposing treatment assesses capacity and makes any best interests decision. Psychiatry advises on method and mental state; write advice, not the decision."},{"h":"Refusal is not incapacity","x":"A fearful or unwise 'no' stands if he can understand, retain, weigh and communicate. Find out what he fears before you test."},{"h":"Supports first, verdict second","x":"Simple words, pictures, a familiar person, a better time of day, a second visit. Record each support tried; one rushed attempt won't stand up."},{"h":"Paid carers don't count for IMCA","x":"If the only people to consult are paid staff, surgery under general anaesthetic needs an IMCA. Instruct early so the meeting isn't delayed."},{"h":"Numbing cream needs an hour","x":"Lidocaine-prilocaine cream needs at least 60 minutes under a dressing; tetracaine gel about 30–45. Apply on the ward, not in the anaesthetic room."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"Assess his capacity to decide about surgery, manage the support worker's role, and advise the team."},"c-8-2-3":{"obj":["Acknowledged the mother's exhaustion before stating the law","Explained that no parent can consent for an adult and that any sterilisation without her consent needs a court","Asked directly about coercion, abuse or exploitation and arranged to see the daughter alone","Offered at least two less restrictive options (relationships education, LARC) and named who provides them","Referred the mother for a carer's assessment and a GP check of her own mood"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"I can't do this any more. If she gets pregnant, I'll end up raising the baby.","r":"You've carried a huge load for years, and that fear makes sense. You shouldn't be doing this alone. Let's look at what support you need, and at keeping your daughter safe."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"I'm her mother. I know what's best. Why can't I decide?","r":"You know her better than anyone, and your views will count. But she's an adult, so the law won't let anyone consent for her. Only she, or a court, can decide on sterilisation."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"What if he's taking advantage of her?","r":"That's an important worry and I take it seriously. I'll see her on her own with a nurse she trusts, and speak to the day centre. If anyone is hurting her, we act straight away."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"Then can she just have the injection without knowing what it's for?","r":"I understand why that's tempting. Giving contraception without telling her wouldn't be lawful and would break her trust. We can explain it simply; many young women choose an implant or injection themselves."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"Am I a terrible mother for even asking?","r":"No. You asked because you're worried and worn out. I hear it as a request for help, and I'm glad you came. Let's get you some support."},{"tag":"Question","p":"What's a carer's assessment? Will they judge me?","r":"It's your right from the council, and it's about your needs, not a test of you. It can lead to breaks, practical help or a carers' group. I can refer you today."}],"bps":{"bio":["If she wants contraception: GP or sexual health clinic to discuss LARC (implant lasts 3 years; injection every 13 weeks), with her capacity for that decision assessed","Pregnancy test and STI screen offered, with her consent, if she is sexually active"],"psych":["Relationships and sex education adapted for learning disability, CLDT nurse or psychology, easy read, around 6–8 sessions","Separate capacity assessments for sexual relations and for contraception, CLDT psychiatrist with LD nurse","Mother: GP review of her mood; NHS Talking Therapies self-referral"],"social":["Adult social care: carer's assessment for mother (Care Act s10), needs review for daughter","Day centre manager: supervision arrangements and staff awareness","Local carers' centre and learning disability family support details given"],"safety":["Daughter seen alone this week with CLDT nurse: ask about touching, secrets and fear","Trust safeguarding lead told today; Care Act s42 referral if any concern; police if a crime is disclosed","Any permanent procedure: trust legal team and Court of Protection","Review with mother in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Three separate capacity decisions","x":"Sex, contraception and sterilisation are each assessed separately. For sex she must understand that her partner has to consent and can stop at any time."},{"h":"Sterilisation without consent goes to court","x":"For an adult lacking capacity, sterilisation for contraception should come before the Court of Protection, even when family and doctors all agree."},{"h":"Demands for permanence signal carer crisis","x":"Behind a request for sterilisation there is often exhaustion, isolation and fear of the future. Meet the carer's needs and the request often softens."},{"h":"Reversible options come first","x":"An implant lasts 3 years and hormonal coils last several years. Both are reversible and need no daily action, so they suit many women with learning disability."},{"h":"Care workers: consent is no defence","x":"Sexual activity by a care worker with a person with mental disorder in their care is an offence under the Sexual Offences Act 2003, whatever she appears to agree to."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"Respond empathically to the sterilisation request, explain the law, explore concerns, and prioritise the daughter."},"c-8-3-1":{"obj":["Built a picture of her 'best' from her sister, old reports or videos, with the date decline began","Ordered the treatable-mimic screen: thyroid, blood count, B12 and folate, calcium, glucose, plus hearing and sight tests","Explained to the sister in plain words why Alzheimer's is likely but not yet diagnosed","Arranged an epilepsy review for the new seizure","Raised a Lasting Power of Attorney and advance care planning with her while she can still decide"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"Is this dementia? Just tell me straight.","r":"I'll be honest: it might be. People with Down syndrome get Alzheimer's more often and younger, and the seizure worries me. But thyroid problems, low mood and hearing loss can look the same, so we check those first."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"How long has she got?","r":"That's a really hard question, and nobody can give an exact time. If it is Alzheimer's, it usually progresses over several years. We'll plan step by step so she has the right help at each stage."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"I should have noticed sooner. I thought she was just being lazy.","r":"Lots of families think that at first. Early changes are subtle and look like losing interest. You've brought her now, and that's what matters."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"I don't want a blood test.","r":"That's okay to say. Can you tell me what you don't like about it? We can use cream so it doesn't hurt, and your sister can hold your hand."},{"tag":"Question","p":"Is there a tablet for it?","r":"If it is Alzheimer's, a tablet like donepezil may help memory a little for a while. It isn't a cure. We'd check her heart first."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"I'm 60. Who'll look after her when I can't?","r":"That's a big worry to carry. Let's start now: a carer's assessment for you, a needs assessment for her, and talking with her about where she'd like to live in future."}],"bps":{"bio":["GP bloods: TFT, FBC, B12, folate, calcium, glucose, U&E, LFT","Audiology (wax, hearing loss) and optometry (cataract, keratoconus); sleep study if snoring or pauses in breathing","If hypothyroid: levothyroxine 50 micrograms each morning before breakfast (25 micrograms if heart disease), TSH at 6–8 weeks","If Alzheimer's confirmed: donepezil 5 mg at night, 10 mg after 4 weeks if tolerated; pulse and ECG first","Epilepsy team chooses the antiseizure medicine; if levetiracetam, watch for low mood and irritability"],"psych":["CLDT psychology: Down syndrome-specific cognitive and adaptive assessment, repeated every 6–12 months","Life story work; keep her at her craft group with extra support"],"social":["Adult social care: needs assessment for her, carer's assessment for her sister","Down syndrome and dementia support groups signposted for her sister","OT home safety visit: getting lost, kitchen and door safety"],"safety":["ID card or locator for getting lost, with her agreement; safeguarding if she is exploited","LPA and advance care plan started while she has capacity","SALT referral at the first cough or choking with meals","Review in 6 weeks with results"]},"facts":[{"h":"New seizures in mid-life signal Alzheimer's","x":"In Down syndrome, seizures starting in mid-life, especially jerks on waking, strongly suggest Alzheimer's. Ask about morning jerks and dropped cups, not just fits."},{"h":"Personality changes before memory","x":"Early Alzheimer's in Down syndrome often shows as apathy, irritability or lost daily skills before forgetfulness. Ask what she has stopped doing."},{"h":"Thyroid every year","x":"Hypothyroidism is common in Down syndrome and mimics dementia: slowing, weight gain, low mood. Yearly TFT is standard; recheck before any dementia diagnosis."},{"h":"General cognitive scores mislead","x":"MMSE and MoCA cut-offs don't apply. Use tools designed for learning disability and compare her with herself over time."},{"h":"Check the pulse before donepezil","x":"Cholinesterase inhibitors slow the heart, and heart disease is common in Down syndrome. Check pulse and ECG; warn about nausea, diarrhoea, vivid dreams and dizziness."}],"type":"Collateral history","do":"Take a history from the sister, outline dementia work-up excluding treatable causes, and explain next steps."},"c-8-3-2":{"obj":["Checked temperature, rigidity, fluids and alertness first, and ruled NMS in or out","Stopped haloperidol today and told the GP by phone","Elicited fluctuation, visual hallucinations, acting out dreams and parkinsonism, compared with his own baseline","Placed an 'avoid antipsychotics' alert on his record and hospital passport","Given the support worker clear 999 triggers and a 2-week review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"The GP said it would calm him. Shouldn't he stay on it?","r":"I can see why it was tried. Stiffness and sleepiness after two days point to a dangerous sensitivity that some people with this pattern have. Stopping it today is safest, and I'll ring the GP myself."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"The children are in my room at night.","r":"That sounds frightening. Thank you for telling me. I can't see them, but I believe you see them. I think your brain is making pictures. We'll find out why and help you feel safe."},{"tag":"Question","p":"So when he sees things, do we argue with him?","r":"No arguing, and no playing along. Stay calm, say you can't see them but you're with him, switch a light on, and move him gently if he's scared. Note it on the chart."},{"tag":"Question","p":"Why is he fine one day and lost the next?","r":"Big swings between good and bad days are a typical sign of one kind of dementia, called Lewy body dementia. Your daily chart will help us confirm it."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"He's not the man he was. It's like losing him.","r":"That's painful to watch. Some of what you're seeing is the medicine and should ease within days. Some may be dementia, and we'll plan support so he stays as well as he can."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"No more tablets. They make me bad.","r":"You're right, that tablet made you stiff and sleepy. We're stopping it. We won't start a new one without talking to you first."}],"bps":{"bio":["Stop haloperidol today. GP: temperature, pulse, BP; bloods CK, CRP, FBC, U&E, LFT, glucose, TFT, B12; urine dip","Same-day A&E if temperature over 38°C, CK markedly raised, worsening rigidity, confusion or not drinking (possible NMS)","Specialist may start rivastigmine 1.5 mg twice daily, rising by 1.5 mg twice daily every 2 weeks (max 6 mg twice daily), or donepezil 5 mg at night; pulse and ECG first","Cut anticholinergic burden; no antipsychotic of any kind unless specialist-led"],"psych":["Hallucinations without distress: reassurance, good lighting, night light, no arguing, no drug","CLDT psychology: ID-specific dementia assessment, repeated at 6–12 months"],"social":["Support worker keeps a daily chart: good or bad day, sleep, falls, hallucinations","CLDT OT and physio: falls plan and home layout; SALT if coughing with meals","Sister involved in best interests decisions; carer meeting in 2 weeks"],"safety":["Alert on record and hospital passport: 'Antipsychotic sensitivity, avoid'","999 if very stiff, hot, newly confused or not drinking","Falls: sensor mat or hip protectors considered in best interests","CLDT consultant review in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Antipsychotics can be lethal in DLB","x":"Many people with Lewy body dementia react severely to antipsychotics: sudden rigidity, sedation, falls, NMS-like states. A small dose of haloperidol is enough."},{"h":"Fluctuation is the key clue","x":"Hours or days of muddle alternating with clear spells, staring episodes and daytime sleepiness suggest DLB. Delirium also fluctuates, so screen infection, bowels and drugs."},{"h":"Acting out dreams comes first","x":"Shouting, punching or kicking in sleep (REM sleep behaviour disorder) can appear years before dementia. Ask the night staff specifically."},{"h":"Cholinesterase inhibitors are first-line","x":"NICE recommends donepezil or rivastigmine for mild to moderate DLB; they can reduce hallucinations and fluctuation. Check pulse first; warn about nausea and slow heart rate."},{"h":"DaTscan needs planning","x":"Reduced dopamine transporter uptake supports DLB. The scan follows an injection by a few hours and he must lie still for about half an hour; plan adjustments ahead."}],"type":"Collateral history","do":"Establish change from baseline with the support worker, identify possible DLB, and agree a safe plan."},"c-8-3-3":{"obj":["Timed the behaviour against seizures (before, during, after, between) from the seizure diary","Checked for pain sources: teeth, ears, bowels, periods and injuries","Reviewed the lamotrigine change and the rescue medication plan, and confirmed staff know when to use it","Requested her annual health check and health action plan in writing to the GP","Discussed SUDEP and night-time monitoring with her mother"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Two years without a health check. Nobody listens to me.","r":"You're right to be angry; two years is far too long. I'll write to the GP today asking for her check and a health action plan, and I'll copy you in."},{"tag":"Question","p":"Can we give her the midazolam when she's kicking off?","r":"No. Buccal midazolam is only for seizures, exactly as her plan says. Using it for distress is unsafe and would be chemical restraint. Let's use the ABC chart and calming steps instead."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"Could she die from a seizure at night?","r":"It's a fair question. Sudden death in epilepsy is rare, but more likely with night seizures and missed doses. Reliable doses and good night monitoring lower the risk, and we'll review her plan."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"Her teeth are fine. She'd let us know.","r":"She may show pain by holding her face or refusing food rather than telling you. A dental check this week will settle it either way."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"The GP wants to give her something to calm her. Will that fix it?","r":"It might make her sleepy, but it wouldn't fix why she's upset. Let's check for pain and seizure changes first. If nothing's found, we'll talk through other options together."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"Honestly, the night staff don't really know her seizure plan.","r":"Thank you for telling me, that matters. Let's get the epilepsy nurse to train the night team this week, and keep the plan and in-date midazolam where night staff can reach them."}],"bps":{"bio":["Dental check this week (special care dentistry); GP: abdomen, ears, urine, constipation, bloods","Lamotrigine: compare the date of the increase with the behaviour change; check for rash, dizziness, double vision, unsteadiness; levels only if toxicity or missed doses suspected","Rescue plan: buccal midazolam (adult dose usually 10 mg) given only as her written plan says; 999 if the seizure continues after it","No new psychotropic until the physical and seizure review is done"],"psych":["ABC chart and seizure diary for 2 weeks, analysed by CLDT nurse or psychologist","SALT: communication passport and DisDAT pain profile"],"social":["Home manager: night staff trained in epilepsy and rescue medication this week","GP: confirm LD register, reasonable adjustment flag, annual health check and health action plan","Mother invited to reviews and copied into letters as part of best interests"],"safety":["Night monitoring reviewed with epilepsy nurse (e.g. bed seizure alarm)","SUDEP discussion with mother recorded","Safeguarding concern if missed checks or untreated pain reflect neglect","CLDT review in 3 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Rash on lamotrigine is urgent","x":"A rash, especially in the first 8 weeks or after a dose rise, can herald Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Stop and arrange same-day medical review."},{"h":"Map behaviour to the seizure clock","x":"Irritability hours before, automatisms during, confusion and lashing out after. Ask staff to put seizure times on the ABC chart to see the link."},{"h":"Some antiseizure drugs cause aggression","x":"Levetiracetam and perampanel are known for irritability and aggression; topiramate for low mood and slowed thinking. Check what changed and when."},{"h":"No register, no invite","x":"Annual health checks from age 14 only reach people on the GP learning disability register. Ask the practice to confirm she is on it."},{"h":"Midazolam has one job","x":"Buccal midazolam follows a named written plan stating dose and timing. Using it for distress is an unplanned restrictive practice and risks slowed breathing."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Link epilepsy and behaviour by history, check physical causes, and set up an annual health check."},"c-8-4-1":{"obj":["Acknowledged the staff injuries and agreed an interim crisis plan before discussing medicine","Taken a functional history of the assaults: setting, trigger, what happens after","Explained STOMP in two plain sentences and declined the increase without stopping risperidone abruptly","Requested weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin and ECG, and referred for a PBS functional assessment","Set a best interests meeting and a 6-week review to plan a slow reduction"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"If you won't increase it, are you coming to get hit instead?","r":"I hear how frightening this is for your team, and staff safety matters. Today we'll agree a crisis plan and who to ring. A higher dose may sedate him without stopping the cause."},{"tag":"Question","p":"What's STOMP? Sounds like you just want to take his meds off him.","r":"It's an NHS programme to stop people getting medicine for behaviour when it isn't helping. We won't stop anything suddenly. We'll find the reason for the hitting first, then decide about the dose."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"We've done ABC charts before. They don't work.","r":"That's fair if nobody went through them with you. This time the PBS practitioner will analyse them with you in 4 weeks and turn them into a plan."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"Since the day centre closed he's been stuck at home most days.","r":"That's really useful, thank you. Losing his day centre could explain a lot. Let's ask his care coordinator about replacement activities this week."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"Don't like new staff. Don't like.","r":"Thank you for telling me, Jake. New people are hard. We'll try to keep the staff you know with you."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"If something happens, it's my registration on the line.","r":"I understand. Writing the crisis plan and today's reasons in his notes protects you and him. I'll send it to you and the GP today."}],"bps":{"bio":["Risperidone stays at 2 mg for now. GP: weight, BMI, waist, BP, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin, ECG","Physical screen for pain, teeth, constipation and sleep","Later reduction, after the best interests meeting: about 25% every 4–8 weeks (2 mg → 1.5 → 1 → 0.5 mg → stop), holding a step if distress rises"],"psych":["PBS practitioner: functional assessment and written PBS plan within 6–8 weeks","ABC charts for 4 weeks, reviewed jointly with staff","SALT: visual timetable and Now/Next board"],"social":["Care coordinator: replacement day activities and a consistent staff team","Support provider: PBS training for staff on top of Oliver McGowan training","Mother invited to the best interests meeting"],"safety":["Crisis plan: early signs, de-escalation steps, CLDT duty number, out-of-hours crisis line","Incidents logged on the provider's system; safeguarding concern if anyone is injured","Best interests meeting in 4–6 weeks; STOMP review at 6 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Akathisia can pass for aggression","x":"Inner restlessness from risperidone can show as pacing and lashing out, and a higher dose worsens it. Look for it before calling it behaviour."},{"h":"Six years means overdue checks","x":"Antipsychotic monitoring: weight, BP, HbA1c and lipids at baseline, 12 weeks, then yearly. A 14 kg gain needs a GP plan for diabetes risk."},{"h":"The function decides the plan","x":"Escape, attention, getting something, sensory: pain can sit behind any of them. A sedative addresses none, so find the function first."},{"h":"Stopping suddenly backfires","x":"Abrupt withdrawal brings rebound agitation, poor sleep and withdrawal dyskinesia, convincing everyone he 'needs' it. Reduce in steps and hold a step when needed."},{"h":"Chemical restraint is a restrictive practice","x":"Medicine used mainly to control behaviour must have a recorded reason, a review date and a plan to reduce."}],"type":"Medication review","do":"Respond to the risperidone request, explain STOMP, and agree a positive behaviour support plan first."},"c-8-4-2":{"obj":["Explained Antecedent, Behaviour, Consequence in plain words and filled in one real entry with the parents","Agreed how long to keep the chart (2–4 weeks) and when to fill it in (within the hour)","Checked her hand and arranged a physical and dental check","Offered the parents a carer's assessment and a link to day services","Set a 2-week review to turn the pattern into a written green-amber-red plan"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"Why does she do it? She never did this at school.","r":"Something has changed for her, and this is her way of telling us. School gave her a busy routine; afternoons may now feel empty or loud. The chart will help us find out which."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"Is it our fault? Are we doing something wrong?","r":"No. You've been doing your best with very little help since school ended. The chart isn't about blame; it's about understanding her."},{"tag":"Question","p":"What do I actually write? I'm not a nurse.","r":"Just three things: what came before, what she did, what came after, plus the time. A few words each. Let's fill one in together now from today."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"Can't we just hold her hands or put gloves on her?","r":"I know it's hard to watch her hurt herself. Holding or gloves can upset her more and count as restraint. Let's find the reason first, and plan what to do safely if she's injured."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"We haven't had a night off since she was born.","r":"That's exhausting, and you deserve support. You can ask the council for a carer's assessment of your own. I'll ask her social worker to arrange it and to look at breaks for you."},{"tag":"Question","p":"She only does it in the afternoons. Does that mean anything?","r":"It could mean a lot: boredom, tiredness, noise or hunger at that time. Note what's going on then, and we'll spot the pattern together."}],"bps":{"bio":["GP: physical check, dental review, ears, bowels, periods; confirm her annual health check is booked","Hand: clean and dress; GP if redness, swelling, pus or fever","No sedative; only revisit medication if NICE NG11 criteria are met after psychological approaches"],"psych":["ABC chart for 2–4 weeks, filled in within the hour, plus a time-of-day scatter chart","PBS practitioner or psychologist: functional assessment and written PBS plan","Proactive steps: visual timetable, planned afternoon activity, ear defenders and a quiet space"],"social":["Local authority social worker: carer's assessment, short breaks and a day service place","Transition review: whether her EHC plan can continue in education or training up to 25","Family support groups and local carers' centre signposted"],"safety":["Traffic-light plan: green (keeps her calm), amber (first signs), red (safe response)","If the family is near crisis: Dynamic Support Register and C(E)TR","Review with CLDT nurse in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Write it within the hour","x":"Details fade fast. Record time, place, who was there, before and after. Describe what you saw ('bit her left hand'), not a guess at why ('angry')."},{"h":"The consequence keeps it going","x":"If biting ends a demand or brings company, it works for her and repeats. The chart shows what she gains; the plan gives her a safer way to get it."},{"h":"Scatter charts show the clock","x":"A grid of time slots across the week shows clusters, like every day around 3 pm. It often reveals triggers faster than long notes."},{"h":"Leaving school is a crisis point","x":"Leaving school removes structure, peers and support overnight. Behaviour that challenges often starts in the months after. Ask about her EHC plan and day services early."},{"h":"Restriction needs a plan","x":"Holding, mittens or locked doors without a best interests plan may breach the MCA. If ever needed, write them into the PBS plan with a review date."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain ABC charts to her parents and start a simple positive behaviour support plan."},"c-g-01":{"obj":["Recorded a full set of obs with a NEWS2 score and a 12-lead ECG, with QTc noted","Completed a cardiovascular examination including BP in both arms, JVP, heart sounds, lung bases and calves","Stated whether acute coronary syndrome, PE or dissection is likely, with the findings that support it","Arranged troponin and same-day medical review, or an emergency ambulance with aspirin 300 mg given","Told her honestly what you found and what happens next"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I think I'm having a heart attack. Please don't just tell me it's nerves again.\"","r":"\"I won't. Chest pain needs checking properly, whatever else is going on. I'm going to examine you and do a heart tracing now, and I'll tell you what I find.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You're a psychiatrist. What do you know about hearts?\"","r":"\"Fair question. I'm a medical doctor first, and I'm trained to check for heart problems. If I find anything worrying, I'll get the medical team or an ambulance straight away.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't breathe, I can't breathe, it's happening again.\"","r":"\"I'm here with you. Your oxygen level is normal. Breathe out slowly with me, longer out than in. When it settles a little, I'll carry on checking your heart.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If the tracing's normal, does that mean my heart is fine?\"","r":"\"It's a good sign, but not the whole answer. A blood test called troponin shows heart strain, and sometimes we repeat it. When both are clear, we can be much more confident.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going in an ambulance. I'll lose my bed here.\"","r":"\"I understand you want to stay. Your tracing shows changes that need a hospital tonight. I'll ask the ward to keep your bed and phone the hospital ahead. Can I explain the risks of staying?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've had this pain walking up the stairs for a month. I didn't tell anyone.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that's important. Pain on effort is a reason to see a heart specialist. I'll make sure the medical team knows today.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["12-lead ECG now and with any new pain; high-sensitivity troponin at 0 h and repeat per local pathway; FBC, U&E, glucose, HbA1c, lipids","Suspected ACS: 999, aspirin 300 mg chewed (unless allergic), GTN 400 micrograms sublingual if systolic BP over 90; oxygen only if sats below 94%","Keep citalopram 20 mg if QTc under 450 ms; 40 mg is the maximum under 65","Smoking: nicotine patch 21 mg/24 h plus a short-acting product, offered today"],"psych":["Once the heart is cleared, explain the panic cycle: breathing, chest tightness, fear of a heart attack, more breathing","CBT for panic disorder (NICE CG113), weekly sessions with the ward or community psychology team"],"social":["With her consent, phone her daughter to explain the plan and the results","If transferred: nurse escort, copy of drug chart, ECG and notes summary go with her"],"safety":["Obs with NEWS2 4-hourly for 24 h; nurses to repeat ECG and call the doctor if pain returns","Escalation written in notes: new pain with ECG change or NEWS2 5 or more, call 999","Day team to review at handover and chase the troponin"]},"facts":[{"h":"Normal ECG does not exclude ACS","x":"A normal resting ECG lowers the odds but does not rule out acute coronary syndrome. Wait for the troponin and repeat the ECG with any further pain."},{"h":"Aspirin 300 mg, chewed","x":"For suspected acute coronary syndrome give a single 300 mg loading dose as soon as possible unless she is allergic; note the time on the chart and in the handover."},{"h":"Oxygen is not routine","x":"Give oxygen in suspected ACS only if sats are below 94% (88 to 92% target if she has COPD with CO2 retention)."},{"h":"Both arms, both calves","x":"A BP difference over 20 mmHg between arms suggests dissection; a swollen tender calf with tachycardia suggests PE. Thirty seconds each, and they change the plan."},{"h":"Citalopram and the QT","x":"Citalopram lengthens QTc with dose. Maximum 40 mg daily, 20 mg over 65. Check the QTc on every ECG you request."}],"type":"Physical examination + ECG and investigations","do":"Take a chest pain history, do a cardiovascular examination, and decide disposition with an ECG and troponin plan."},"c-g-02":{"obj":["Completed a respiratory examination front and back and named the affected lobe","Scored NEWS2 and CRB-65 and stated the transfer triggers in the notes","Sent FBC with neutrophils, CRP, troponin and a clozapine level, and done an ECG","Agreed tonight's clozapine dose with the nurse in charge and chosen a non-interacting antibiotic","Explained to the patient why the blood test matters tonight"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"No more blood tests. I had one on Monday.\"","r":"\"I know, you've had lots. This one is different: with a fever on clozapine I need to check your infection-fighting cells tonight. It's one tube and it keeps you safe.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is it the clozapine? Will you take me off it? It's the first thing that's worked.\"","r":"\"I hear how much it's helping. Most likely this is a chest infection. I may hold tonight's dose until the blood results are back, then we'll restart it carefully.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do you need a heart tracing for a cough?\"","r":"\"Good question. Early on, clozapine can rarely inflame the heart, and it can look like flu. The tracing and a blood test let me rule that out.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just give me paracetamol and let me sleep it off.\"","r":"\"Paracetamol will help the fever, and you can have it. But your chest sounds infected, so you also need an antibiotic. Then sleep, and the nurses will check on you.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I stopped smoking when I came in. Haven't had one for a week.\"","r":"\"That's really useful to know, and well done. Stopping smoking raises clozapine levels, so I'll check your level and the pharmacist may adjust the dose.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Amoxicillin 500 mg three times a day for 5 days (low severity); penicillin allergy: doxycycline 200 mg on day 1 then 100 mg daily","Avoid ciprofloxacin and erythromycin or clarithromycin with clozapine unless the pharmacist agrees; they can raise levels","Hold clozapine pending FBC and troponin; if neutrophils below 1.5, stop and treat as possible neutropenic sepsis in the acute hospital","Clozapine level once settled; if more than 48 h of doses are missed, re-titrate from 12.5 mg","Paracetamol 1 g up to four times a day; hyoscine for hypersalivation only after review (adds constipation)"],"psych":["Reassure that clozapine is likely to continue; watch for relapse if doses are held","Nurses to record mental state each shift while doses are reduced or held"],"safety":["Obs hourly overnight; nurse to call if NEWS2 7 or more, sats below 92% or new confusion","Transfer to A&E with escort if any trigger is met","Ward pharmacist and day team to review clozapine at handover"]},"facts":[{"h":"Fever on clozapine: three questions","x":"Is he neutropenic? Is it myocarditis? Is the level rising? FBC, troponin with CRP and ECG, and a clozapine level answer them."},{"h":"Red means stop","x":"UK clozapine monitoring: neutrophils below 1.5 or white cells below 3.0 is red. Stop clozapine and seek specialist advice before any restart."},{"h":"Infection pushes levels up","x":"Inflammation and stopping smoking both slow clozapine breakdown. Levels can climb sharply in days; watch for sedation, hypotension and seizures."},{"h":"48 hours missed: start again","x":"If clozapine is missed for more than 48 hours, re-titrate from 12.5 mg once or twice daily to avoid hypotension and collapse."},{"h":"CRB-65 on the ward","x":"Confusion, RR 30 or more, low BP, age 65 or over: one point each. 0 is low risk; 2 or more means hospital assessment."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Do a respiratory examination, grade severity, and decide on bloods, antibiotics, clozapine dosing and transfer."},"c-g-03":{"obj":["Examined the abdomen in full, including hernial orifices, liver signs and an offered rectal examination","Named obstruction or ileus as the working diagnosis and listed the signs that support it","Stopped laxatives, enemas and oral intake, and held clozapine and hyoscine","Arranged same-day surgical review with a written summary and an escort","Written a bowel prevention plan for his return"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It's just constipation. I always get it. Give me something and I'll be fine.\"","r":"\"You've been very patient with it. Twelve days, with vomiting and a swollen tummy, is more than usual constipation on clozapine. I need a surgeon to check today.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are they going to cut me open?\"","r":"\"Most people with this get better with rest for the bowel, a drip and close watching. The surgeons decide after a scan. I'll tell them everything so you don't have to repeat it.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going to that hospital. Last time I waited 12 hours.\"","r":"\"That sounds miserable, and I'm sorry. This time I'm phoning ahead and writing a letter, and a nurse will go with you. Waiting here could make this much more dangerous.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I stopped taking the laxatives. They made me run to the toilet.\"","r":"\"Thanks for being honest. That's common, and it's not your fault. When you're back, we'll find a regimen that works without rushing you to the toilet.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why does clozapine do this?\"","r":"\"It slows the muscles in the gut wall, so things move very slowly. It's common and can be serious, which is why we ask about your bowels every week.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Now: nil by mouth, IV fluids at the acute hospital; hold clozapine and hyoscine; levels and bloods as above","On return: senna 15 mg at night plus docusate 200 mg twice daily; add macrogol 1 to 2 sachets daily if no bowel movement for 2 days","Avoid bulk-forming laxatives; stop hyoscine; agree another approach to drooling with the pharmacist","If clozapine is held more than 48 h, re-titrate from 12.5 mg with the pharmacist","Alcohol: AUDIT, LFT, thiamine 100 mg three times a day if drinking heavily"],"psych":["Explain why bowel checks matter so he reports early; agree a phrase he is comfortable using","Monitor mental state while clozapine is held; relapse can follow within days"],"social":["Care coordinator to add bowel monitoring to his care plan for leave and discharge","Supported accommodation staff (if any) given the bowel plan and red flags in writing"],"safety":["Bowel chart daily; nurse to escalate no bowel movement for 3 days, vomiting or distension","Weekly clozapine review includes bowel habit","RC informed; incident report if harm"]},"facts":[{"h":"Clozapine constipation can kill","x":"Clozapine slows the whole gut. Obstruction, impaction and ileus have caused deaths in the UK. Ask about bowels at every review; patients rarely raise it."},{"h":"Prevent from day one","x":"Start a softener plus stimulant, such as docusate and senna, when clozapine starts. Avoid bulk-forming laxatives in a slow gut."},{"h":"No enema before examination","x":"Distension, vomiting, no flatus, tinkling or absent bowel sounds: stop laxatives and enemas, keep him nil by mouth, and get a surgical review."},{"h":"Overflow fools people","x":"Watery leakage around a hard faecal mass looks like diarrhoea. Examine before you treat 'loose stools' in anyone on clozapine."},{"h":"Anticholinergics add up","x":"Hyoscine for drooling, procyclidine, tricyclics and opioids all slow the gut further. Review each one after an event."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Do an abdominal examination, recognise obstruction or ileus, and decide on surgical transfer and clozapine."},"c-g-04":{"obj":["Examined the thyroid including swallow, tongue protrusion, nodes, retrosternal percussion and a bruit","Looked for and recorded pulse, tremor, eye signs and ankle reflexes","Sent TSH, free T4, calcium, U&E, eGFR and a 12-hour lithium level","Explained lithium-induced hypothyroidism and that lithium can continue","Written a monitoring plan with dates for TSH and lithium bloods"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is it cancer? My aunt had a lump in her neck.\"","r":"\"I can understand that worry. The swelling feels smooth and even on both sides, which fits lithium rather than cancer. A blood test will confirm it, and if anything changes we'll scan it.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Nobody told me lithium could do this. Why wasn't it checked?\"","r":"\"You're right to be annoyed; the test was overdue. I'm sorry. I've sent it today, and I'll make sure the dates for your next checks are written down for you and your GP.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to stop lithium now?\"","r":"\"No. Lithium has kept you well for years. An underactive thyroid is treated with a daily tablet, and you carry on with lithium. We just check both more closely.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can I just eat more seaweed or take a supplement instead?\"","r":"\"I can see why you'd want something natural. Extra iodine won't fix this and can sometimes make it worse. The hormone tablet replaces exactly what your thyroid isn't making.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've been feeling worse than my usual depressions. Heavier, slower.\"","r":"\"That's really helpful. An underactive thyroid can do exactly that. Treating it may lift some of the heaviness, alongside your other treatment.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Levothyroxine about 1.6 micrograms/kg daily (e.g. 100 micrograms at 65 kg), on an empty stomach 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast; 25 to 50 micrograms if over 65 or heart disease","TSH every 3 months after starting or any dose change until stable, then yearly","Continue lithium; aim for a level of 0.6 to 0.8 mmol/L taken 12 hours after the dose","Lithium bloods: level every 3 months in year one then every 6 months; TFT, U&E, eGFR and calcium every 6 months","Calcium raised: send PTH; refer to endocrinology if hyperparathyroidism"],"psych":["Review the depression once thyroid is treated; avoid adding drugs for symptoms that may be thyroid-driven","Repeat PHQ-9 or a structured mood rating in 4 weeks to track change"],"social":["Give her a written lithium record (levels and test dates); GP and community team copied into the monitoring plan","Discharge letter to the GP lists levothyroxine dose and the next TSH and lithium test dates"],"safety":["Ultrasound and suspected cancer referral if a nodule, hard or fixed lump, nodes, hoarseness or rapid growth","Warn about lithium toxicity signs: vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness, confusion: stop and get a level that day"]},"facts":[{"h":"Swallow lifts a goitre","x":"Thyroid swellings rise on swallowing; a thyroglossal cyst rises on tongue protrusion. Lymph nodes do neither."},{"h":"Treat the thyroid, keep the lithium","x":"Lithium-induced hypothyroidism is managed with levothyroxine. Stopping lithium risks relapse, especially mania, within weeks."},{"h":"Levothyroxine before breakfast","x":"Take on an empty stomach, 30 to 60 minutes before food or caffeine. Separate from iron and calcium supplements by at least 4 hours."},{"h":"Lithium raises calcium too","x":"Lithium can cause hyperparathyroidism. Check calcium every 6 months; if raised, send PTH."},{"h":"Thyroid can drive low mood","x":"Hypothyroidism mimics and worsens depression and is linked to rapid cycling. Check TFT before changing mood treatment."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Examine the thyroid and signs of dysfunction, interpret results, and plan treatment while continuing lithium."},"c-g-05":{"obj":["Recorded acuity, colour vision, pupil reactions including the swinging light test, fields and fundi for each eye","Stated whether an RAPD is present and what it means","Recommended a specific same-day review (ophthalmology, neurology, imaging) when signs are organic","Screened mood and suicide risk and recorded protective factors","Advised her not to drive until her vision is assessed"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"They think I'm making it up. Why else would they send a psychiatrist?\"","r":"\"That must feel awful. I'm not here because anyone thinks you're pretending. I want to check your eyes carefully myself, and I'll tell you honestly what I find.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can stress really make you go blind?\"","r":"\"Stress can affect the body in real ways, and sometimes vision. But I don't assume that. First I check the eye and the nerve, because those findings guide what we do.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is it a brain tumour?\"","r":"\"I can hear how scared you are. What I found points more to inflammation of the eye nerve, which often improves. A scan will help us be sure, and I'll stay involved.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"A few years ago my legs went numb for a fortnight. I never told anyone.\"","r":"\"Thank you. That's important, and I'll tell the neurology team. Two episodes affecting different nerves is something they'll want to look at closely.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"First my job, then him, now this. I can't cope with any more.\"","r":"\"That's a huge amount at once, and it makes sense you feel overwhelmed. Can I ask how low things have got? Have you had thoughts about ending your life?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So you're saying it is all in my head, then?\"","r":"\"No. Your pupil reaction shows a real problem with the nerve in that eye. That's a physical sign, and it needs the eye and nerve specialists today.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Same-day ophthalmology review; neurology review and MRI brain and orbits if optic neuritis","Bloods per medical team: FBC, CRP, ESR, glucose; BP and urine if retinal vascular signs","Any steroid decision rests with neurology or ophthalmology, not liaison"],"psych":["Liaison follow-up this week: adjustment, health anxiety about MS, mood","If a functional component is later confirmed, explain it as a real, common problem and refer to neuro-ophthalmology or functional neurology services"],"social":["Fit note from the medical team; ward social worker or Citizens Advice for benefits after job loss","With consent, involve her sister for support at diagnosis and after discharge"],"safety":["Low suicide risk recorded with protective factors; give crisis line number (NHS 111, option 2)","Not to drive until cleared; documented","Liaison review again before discharge"]},"facts":[{"h":"RAPD cannot be faked","x":"A relative afferent pupillary defect on the swinging light test means optic nerve or retinal disease. Its presence ends any 'purely functional' discussion."},{"h":"Pupils before drops","x":"Check pupil reactions and record them before anyone dilates the eyes. Dilated pupils hide an RAPD and block neurological obs."},{"h":"Pain on moving the eye","x":"Vision loss over hours to days with pain on eye movement and washed-out red in a young woman: think optic neuritis."},{"h":"Stress is not a diagnosis","x":"Recent stress is common in everyone admitted to hospital. Functional vision loss is diagnosed on positive examination signs, never on life events alone."},{"h":"Right eye with right eye","x":"Hold the ophthalmoscope in your right hand to your right eye for her right eye, and vice versa, so your noses do not meet."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Check acuity, colour vision, pupils, fields and fundi, and exclude an eye or brain emergency."},"c-g-06":{"obj":["Screened for cauda equina and cord compression red flags and recorded the answers","Completed gait, Romberg, and a full upper and lower limb examination in a fixed order","Named the pattern of findings and the likely level or cause","Sent B12, folate, FBC and homocysteine or MMA and started IM hydroxocobalamin","Made an urgent neurology referral with MRI spine requested"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It's just laughing gas. It's harmless, everyone does it.\"","r":"\"Lots of people think that. Heavy use blocks vitamin B12, which the spinal cord needs, and your examination shows signs of that. The good news is that treatment started early can help.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Am I going to end up in a wheelchair?\"","r":"\"That's a frightening thought. Many people improve a lot with B12 injections and stopping the gas, especially when caught early, as yours has been. We'll get the neurologists involved this week.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do you want me to close my eyes and stand still?\"","r":"\"It tests the nerves that tell your brain where your feet are. If they're not working, you rely on your eyes, so you sway when they close. I'll stand right next to you.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I was using it every day by the end. It was the only thing that helped my mood.\"","r":"\"Thanks for telling me straight. That helps us understand your depression and your nerves. Let's treat both, and find you something that helps without these effects.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I hate needles. Can't I just take a tablet?\"","r":"\"I understand. When nerves are affected, injections work more reliably at first. It's a small needle in the arm, and the nurse can use numbing cream. Tablets may be possible later.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Hydroxocobalamin 1 mg IM on alternate days until no further improvement, then every 2 months (BNF regimen for neurological involvement); neurology may adjust","Do not give folic acid alone before B12 is replaced","Bloods: B12, folate, FBC, homocysteine or MMA before the first injection if possible; repeat FBC in 10 days","MRI spine via neurology; emergency MRI the same day if saddle numbness, retention or a sensory level appears"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing about nitrous oxide; link use to mood and coping in the formulation","Continue depression treatment; explain that low B12 can worsen mood and thinking"],"social":["Community drug and alcohol service referral before discharge","Physiotherapy and occupational therapy assessment for gait and home safety"],"safety":["Falls risk assessment; supervised showering; night light","Nurses to report new bladder symptoms, back pain or worsening weakness the same shift","Neurology review date recorded in notes"]},"facts":[{"h":"Gas switches off B12","x":"Nitrous oxide inactivates B12, so serum B12 can look normal. A raised homocysteine or methylmalonic acid confirms functional deficiency."},{"h":"Romberg tests the dorsal columns","x":"Steady with eyes open, falls with eyes closed: loss of joint position sense. Stand close, arms ready, before asking him to close his eyes."},{"h":"B12 before folate","x":"Giving folic acid alone in B12 deficiency can worsen the spinal cord damage. Start hydroxocobalamin first."},{"h":"Saddle numbness is an emergency","x":"Back pain with saddle numbness, new retention or incontinence needs emergency MRI the same day for cauda equina."},{"h":"Absent ankles, up-going toes","x":"Combined UMN and LMN signs in the legs point to B12 deficiency, among a short list. Write the pattern, not just the findings."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Do an upper and lower limb neurological examination, localise the lesion, and plan bloods, imaging and referral."},"c-g-07":{"obj":["Recorded a witness account covering onset, eyes, responsiveness, movements, duration and recovery","Asked about aura, awareness, frequency, nocturnal episodes, injuries and risk factors","Done an ECG and neurological examination and checked her drug chart for threshold-lowering drugs","Referred to the first seizure clinic with MRI and EEG requested","Advised no driving, informing the DVLA and bath and height safety, and documented it"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How can it be epilepsy? I've never had a proper fit.\"","r":"\"Good question. Most people picture falling and shaking, but some seizures start in one small area and just change awareness for a minute. What you describe fits that kind. A specialist will confirm it.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So I can't drive? I'll lose my job.\"","r":"\"That's a real blow, and I'm sorry. Driving during a spell could kill you or someone else. I can write to your employer, and many people return to driving once seizures are controlled.\""},{"tag":"Relief","p":"\"So I'm not going mad? I thought it was all in my head.\"","r":"\"No, you're not. These spells come from the brain's electrical activity, not from your imagination. You've also had anxiety, and we'll keep helping with that too.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will I have to take tablets forever?\"","r":"\"I understand that worry. The specialist will talk you through options; many people take one medicine and become seizure-free. Some can stop after years without seizures, with advice.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not having them film me. That's creepy.\"","r":"\"That's completely your choice. A short video helps the specialist a lot, but we can rely on careful notes instead. If you change your mind, only the medical team would see it.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I had one at the wheel last month. I pulled over just in time.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; I know that wasn't easy to say. It's exactly why driving has to stop now. Let's talk about how to tell the DVLA today.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Antiseizure medicine started by neurology: lamotrigine or levetiracetam are first choice for focal seizures","Lamotrigine: 25 mg daily for 2 weeks, 50 mg daily for 2 weeks, then up slowly to 100 to 200 mg daily; stop and seek help for any rash; combined pill lowers levels","Levetiracetam: can cause irritability, low mood and suicidal thoughts; monitor mood weekly","Keep sertraline; avoid bupropion and high-dose tricyclics; review any threshold-lowering drug"],"psych":["Explain the diagnosis in plain terms and give the Epilepsy Action or Epilepsy Society leaflet","Continue CBT for anxiety; screen for depression at each review"],"social":["Letter for employer about driving; Access to Work if her job needs travel","Epilepsy specialist nurse contact once diagnosed"],"safety":["Showers rather than baths; no swimming or heights alone; ward seizure chart","If a seizure lasts 5 minutes or longer: emergency treatment per ward protocol and call 999","Driving advice recorded; review at next ward round"]},"facts":[{"h":"Déjà vu and lip smacking","x":"Rising stomach feeling, déjà vu or fear, then staring with chewing or picking, then confusion: classic temporal lobe focal seizure."},{"h":"Normal EEG does not exclude","x":"A routine EEG is often normal between seizures. Use it to support a specialist diagnosis, never to rule epilepsy out."},{"h":"ECG for every blackout","x":"Do a 12-lead ECG for any unexplained episode of lost awareness; look at QTc and conduction."},{"h":"The patient tells the DVLA","x":"Advise her not to drive and to inform the DVLA, and record it. If she refuses and keeps driving, tell her, then inform the DVLA yourself."},{"h":"No valproate under 55","x":"Valproate is not started in anyone under 55 unless two specialists agree there is no alternative and pregnancy prevention is in place for women."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Take a history from her and the witness, explain possible focal epilepsy, and arrange EEG, MRI and driving advice."},"c-g-08":{"obj":["Taken a history covering intake, weight trend, purging, exercise, body image, periods and suicidal thoughts","Recorded BMI, rate of loss, HR, lying and standing BP, temperature, SUSS score, QTc and electrolytes","Rated her medical risk using MEED markers and stated it clearly to the medical team","Written specific advice on refeeding, thiamine, daily bloods and supervised meals","Assessed capacity to refuse admission and stated the legal route if she tries to leave"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not anorexic. I just got busy with coursework and forgot to eat.\"","r":"\"University can be hugely pressured. Your weight has dropped a lot and your heart is now very slow. Whatever we call it, your body needs help. Can we talk about what eating is like for you?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll eat everything if you let me go home tomorrow.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you want to eat. The trouble is your heart and salts need watching while you start, because the first days of eating carry their own risks. Let's set a goal for the next week together.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I eat what they give me I'll just balloon.\"","r":"\"That fear is real and really common. The plan is measured and the dietitian adjusts it. Your body will use most of it to repair your heart and muscles first.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I make myself sick after most meals. Nobody knows.\"","r":"\"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. It explains your low potassium, which affects your heart. We'll help you through the time after meals so you're not on your own with it.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You can't keep me here. I'm an adult.\"","r":"\"You're right that adults make their own choices, and I respect that. My job is to check whether this illness is making the decision for you. Right now I'm worried it is.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Mother on the phone) \"Should I make her eat when she comes home?\"","r":"\"You clearly want to help. For now the team will manage meals here. The eating disorder service will guide you on meals at home; forcing food alone tends to backfire.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Refeeding plan by dietitian; MEED suggests around 1400 to 2000 kcal a day for most adults rather than very low starts","Thiamine 200 to 300 mg daily orally plus vitamin B compound strong 1 to 2 tablets three times a day, starting before feeding; multivitamin","Phosphate, potassium, magnesium and glucose daily for the first week, then twice weekly; replace as needed","ECG daily while QTc prolonged or potassium low; avoid QTc-prolonging drugs"],"psych":["Eating disorder service review and motivational work on the ward","Later, one of NICE's options for adult anorexia: CBT-ED, MANTRA or SSCM, delivered by the eating disorder team"],"social":["With consent, involve family and the university welfare team; extenuating circumstances for coursework deadlines","Carers' assessment and family support from the eating disorder service"],"safety":["Supervised meals and 1 hour post-meal observation; restrict exercise","Suicide risk reviewed daily; nurses aware of passive death wishes","If she tries to leave: medical and psychiatric senior review, capacity assessment, MHA assessment if indicated"]},"facts":[{"h":"SUSS shows hidden weakness","x":"Sit up from lying and stand from squatting without using hands. Failure means serious muscle loss and higher medical risk, even if she looks well."},{"h":"Don't underfeed","x":"Very low starting calories prolong medical danger. MEED advises a higher start with daily electrolytes and phosphate replacement."},{"h":"Phosphate falls in days 2 to 5","x":"Refeeding syndrome usually shows within the first week. Check phosphate, potassium and magnesium daily and replace early."},{"h":"Low potassium means purging","x":"Unexplained low potassium in restrictive eating suggests vomiting or laxatives. Ask directly; it changes cardiac risk."},{"h":"Feeding is treatment","x":"Under the MHA 1983, feeding for anorexia nervosa, including by nasogastric tube, can count as treatment for the mental disorder."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Take an eating disorder history, grade medical risk with MEED, and advise on refeeding and legal options."},"c-g-09":{"obj":["Told him clearly about the court order and allowed time for his reaction","Recorded his abilities with cooking, money, medicines and self-care, and his drinking","Offered at least three realistic accommodation options and recorded his preference","Agreed referrals for a Care Act assessment, housing, alcohol support and repeat cognitive testing","Shared discharge details with police and his wife's IDVA as part of the safeguarding plan"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"She wouldn't do that. That's my house. I paid for it.\"","r":"\"I can see this is a huge shock, and I'm sorry to be the one telling you. The court has said you can't return for now. Let's think about where you can be settled and safe.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"She's been carrying on behind my back. This is her and her fancy man.\"","r":"\"You sound very hurt and angry. I'd like to understand how long you've had those worries about her. Whatever has happened, the order stands, and going near her would get you into serious trouble.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I've never cooked a meal in my life. I'll starve.\"","r":"\"That's an honest worry, and an important one. That's exactly why we'd arrange support, whether meals delivered, carers, or a place with staff. You won't be left to manage alone.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going in a home. That's where you go to die.\"","r":"\"I hear that. A care home isn't the only option. There are flats with staff on site, where you have your own front door. What would matter most in a new place?\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If I stop drinking, can I go home?\"","r":"\"Stopping drinking would be good for you, and we'll help. But the court order isn't up to us, so it won't change by itself. Let's plan for now, and you can get legal advice about the order.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I didn't mean to hurt her. I don't even remember.\"","r":"\"It sounds like that weighs on you. Not remembering is something we should look into, along with the drinking. Taking that seriously is part of making sure it doesn't happen again.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue mirtazapine 30 mg at night; check sodium, weight and falls risk","Oral thiamine 100 mg three times a day while drinking risk remains; LFT and GGT before discharge","Relapse prevention: acamprosate or naltrexone discussed with the alcohol service, checking renal and liver function","Repeat ACE-III after six weeks' abstinence; memory service referral if impaired"],"psych":["Explore jealous beliefs over time; record them in the risk formulation","Alcohol liaison brief intervention now, then community alcohol service key worker"],"social":["Ward social worker: Care Act needs assessment and interim placement if needed","Housing referral to the local authority under the duty to refer, with consent","OT: kitchen, money and medication assessments; blister pack and meals service if needed","Discuss Lasting Power of Attorney for finance; Citizens Advice for benefits including Attendance Allowance"],"safety":["DASH risk assessment; MARAC referral if high risk; IDVA informed of discharge plan","Police informed of discharge date and address under bail or order conditions","Community older adult team follow-up within 7 days of discharge; review risk at the discharge meeting"]},"facts":[{"h":"Duty to refer","x":"Under the Homelessness Reduction Act 2017, hospitals must refer people who may be homeless to the local housing authority, with consent. Do it early, not on discharge day."},{"h":"Care Act assessment is a right","x":"Anyone who appears to need care and support must be assessed by the local authority. The ward social worker requests it."},{"h":"Presume capacity","x":"He is presumed to have capacity to decide where to live. Assess only if there is a reason to doubt it, and record the decision-specific assessment."},{"h":"Jealousy is a risk marker","x":"Persistent beliefs that a partner is unfaithful, with past violence, raise the risk of further harm. Record them and share within the safeguarding plan."},{"h":"Check memory after abstinence","x":"Retest cognition after several weeks without alcohol. Alcohol-related impairment can improve; persistent deficits change the level of support needed."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Explain why he cannot go home and agree a discharge plan covering accommodation, alcohol and safeguarding."},"c-p-01":{"obj":["Established her relapse risk: number and severity of episodes and what happened when she last stopped sertraline","Explained the risks of sertraline in pregnancy in absolute numbers, alongside the risks of relapse","Started folic acid 400 micrograms daily and checked whether she needs 5 mg","Agreed what she will do if she becomes pregnant, including not stopping sertraline suddenly","Recorded her decision and arranged a GP letter, perinatal advice and a review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I've read SSRIs cause heart defects. I'd never forgive myself.\"","r":"\"That worry shows how much you care already. Some early studies raised that question, but larger, better studies of sertraline haven't shown a clear rise in birth defects. Let me give you the real numbers.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't I just stop for the pregnancy and go back on after?\"","r":"\"I can see why that's tempting. Last time you stopped, you were unwell within three months, and pregnancy and the newborn months are hard times to relapse. If you want to try, we'd taper slowly with a clear plan.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will the baby be born addicted?\"","r":"\"No. Some babies are a bit jittery, sleepy or slow to feed for the first few days, and it settles on its own. The midwives just keep a closer eye after birth.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"What kind of mother takes pills while pregnant?\"","r":"\"A mother who's looking after her health so she can look after her baby. Treating depression is part of caring for the pregnancy, just like treating asthma or diabetes.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I breastfeed on it?\"","r":"\"Yes. Very little gets into breast milk, and sertraline is one of the antidepressants we prefer when women breastfeed.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"My mum had a breakdown after she had me.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that matters. It raises your chance of becoming unwell after a birth, so I'd like the perinatal team involved early to plan with you.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue sertraline 100 mg once daily (morning); do not stop abruptly; keep the lowest dose that keeps her well, reviewed each trimester","Folic acid 400 micrograms daily from now until 12 weeks of pregnancy; 5 mg only if a high-risk group applies","Vitamin D 10 micrograms daily in pregnancy and while breastfeeding (NHS advice)","Advise no alcohol when trying to conceive or in pregnancy, as the safest approach"],"psych":["Written relapse signature agreed today: early waking, withdrawing from friends, losing interest","If low mood returns in pregnancy: fast-track CBT through NHS Talking Therapies, which prioritise perinatal women"],"social":["Invite her husband to the next review; give him the bumps leaflet and the warning signs","GP to flag the plan; midwife told at booking so maternity can plan care after birth"],"safety":["If pregnant: keep taking sertraline, contact GP and midwife, CMHT review within 2 weeks","Crisis: CMHT duty line in hours, NHS 111 mental health option out of hours, 999 if in danger","Perinatal team advice requested now because her last episode was severe; review in 3 months"]},"facts":[{"h":"Don't stop on a positive test","x":"Stopping sertraline suddenly brings discontinuation symptoms and relapse. Tell every woman of childbearing age on an antidepressant: if pregnant, keep taking it and ring us."},{"h":"PPHN in numbers","x":"Persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn: about 2 in 1000 babies normally, about 3 in 1000 after late-pregnancy SSRI exposure. Give absolute numbers, never 'doubles the risk'."},{"h":"Bleeding risk before birth","x":"MHRA 2021: SSRIs in the month before delivery slightly raise postpartum haemorrhage risk. The maternity team needs to know; it is not a reason to stop."},{"h":"Who needs folic acid 5 mg","x":"BMI 30 or more, diabetes, antiepileptic or HIV medicines, sickle cell or thalassaemia, or a personal or family history of neural tube defect. Everyone else: 400 micrograms."},{"h":"Don't switch for pregnancy","x":"If a medicine works, switching to another 'safer' one risks relapse and exposes the baby to two drugs. NICE advises weighing this before any change."}],"type":"Medication review + Explain a treatment","do":"Weigh continuing sertraline against relapse with her, start folic acid, and record a shared preconception plan."},"c-p-02":{"obj":["Confirmed quetiapine adherence, her early warning signs and family history","Explained her postpartum psychosis risk as about 1 in 5, highest in the first 2 weeks","Agreed to continue quetiapine 300 mg and arranged glucose screening at 24 to 28 weeks","Set out a birth and postnatal plan that protects sleep, with named contacts in the first 2 weeks","Referred her to the specialist perinatal team and agreed who receives the written plan by 32 weeks"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you saying I'm going to go mad after the birth?\"","r":"\"No. Most women with bipolar disorder stay well after birth. About 1 in 5 become unwell, and knowing that early lets us plan. Planning is the best protection there is.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I want to come off the quetiapine. I don't want her on drugs.\"","r":"\"I understand; it's a protective instinct. Quetiapine hasn't been linked with a clear rise in birth defects, and stopping makes relapse much more likely, which carries real risks for you both. Can we go through it?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will they take the baby off me if I get ill?\"","r":"\"Having bipolar disorder doesn't mean that. If you did need hospital, there are Mother and Baby Units where you and your baby stay together. The plan we're writing is about keeping you together and well.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Partner) \"What do I actually look for?\"","r":"\"Her own early signs: sleeping much less but not tired, talking fast, irritability, spending, or saying things that don't make sense. If you see them, ring the perinatal team that day.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why would I need a single room? I want to be on the ward like everyone else.\"","r":"\"That's fair, and it's your choice. Sleep is the biggest trigger for you, and open bays are noisy at night. A side room with your partner helps you get a proper block of sleep.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I was in hospital for six weeks last time. I can't go through that again with a baby.\"","r":"\"That sounds like a really hard time, and it makes sense you're scared. This time we know your risk in advance, and we'll have a plan ready long before the baby arrives.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue quetiapine 300 mg at night; no dose change unless symptoms emerge; review each trimester with the perinatal psychiatrist","Weight and BP at every antenatal visit; oral glucose tolerance test at 24 to 28 weeks","Vitamin D 10 micrograms daily; folic acid 400 micrograms until 12 weeks","Agree in advance, in the plan, what the perinatal psychiatrist will offer if sleep fails after birth","Paediatric team told in the third trimester; baby observed after birth for withdrawal or movement signs"],"psych":["Written relapse signature with her and her partner: sleep under 5 hours, racing thoughts, spending, irritability","Perinatal team community nurse as her named contact through pregnancy and the first year"],"social":["Partner to take paternity leave for the first 2 weeks and do one night feed (expressed milk or formula)","Health visitor antenatal contact from 28 weeks, copied into the plan","Mother or another named relative to stay for the first fortnight if possible"],"safety":["Written perinatal plan by 32 weeks, shared with her, midwife, obstetrician, labour ward, GP and health visitor","Crisis contacts in the plan: perinatal team in hours, crisis team or NHS 111 mental health option out of hours; nearest MBU named","Reviews at 20, 28 and 32 to 36 weeks; seen before discharge after birth and regularly for the first 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"About 1 in 5 after birth","x":"Women with bipolar disorder have about a 1 in 5 risk of postpartum psychosis; about 1 in 2 after a previous episode. Most cases start within 2 weeks of birth."},{"h":"Plan written by 32 weeks","x":"The perinatal mental health plan must be in the maternity notes before labour: early signs, medicines, birth plan, feeding, postnatal contacts and crisis numbers."},{"h":"Sleep is the trigger","x":"Protect sleep: early pain relief, a side room, partner staying, and one night feed done by someone else so she gets a 5 to 6 hour block."},{"h":"Quetiapine and glucose","x":"Antipsychotics raise the risk of diabetes in pregnancy. Check weight and BP each visit and offer a glucose tolerance test at 24 to 28 weeks."},{"h":"Never valproate in pregnancy","x":"Valproate is contraindicated in pregnancy for bipolar disorder because of birth defects and developmental harm; MHRA rules restrict starting it in anyone under 55."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Explain her postpartum relapse risk, keep quetiapine going, and start a written perinatal and birth plan with referrals."},"c-p-03":{"obj":["Took the first birth history, including the worst moment and what she believed would happen","Screened for PTSD symptoms, depression and thoughts of self-harm, suicide or ending the pregnancy","Explained both birth options fairly and her right to a planned caesarean under NICE guidance","Referred her for fear-of-childbirth therapy and a consultant obstetric review within two weeks","Rebooked missed antenatal checks and agreed a review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I thought I was going to die on that table and nobody would tell me anything.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying, and being left without information made it worse. Thank you for telling me. This time, being told what's happening will be written into your plan.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Everyone keeps telling me the second one's easier. Are you going to say that too?\"","r":"\"No. You've been through something frightening and you deserve to be heard first. Then we'll look at your options properly, including a caesarean.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If they say no to a caesarean, I'll just not come in.\"","r":"\"I hear how desperate you feel. The guidance says if you still want a caesarean after support, you should be offered one. I'll make sure that's discussed with a consultant soon.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I wish I wasn't pregnant. What kind of mother says that?\"","r":"\"A frightened one, and that's understandable. Can I ask what goes through your mind with that? Have you had thoughts of ending the pregnancy or harming yourself?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is a caesarean actually safer for me?\"","r":"\"Each route has different risks. A caesarean avoids labour but means a longer recovery and affects future pregnancies. The obstetrician will give you your own figures so you can choose.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I haven't been to my son's swimming lessons. The pool smells like the hospital.\"","r":"\"That's a really important detail. Avoiding reminders like that is part of how trauma works, and it can be treated. It tells me therapy should start soon.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medicine needed for fear alone; if depression or PTSD is moderate to severe, consider sertraline 50 mg in the morning (compatible with pregnancy and breastfeeding)","Planned caesarean, if chosen, at 39 weeks or later with a pre-operative anaesthetic meeting; or vaginal birth with consultant-led care and early epidural available","Rebook missed checks today: BP, urine, growth assessment as the midwife advises"],"psych":["Maternal mental health service psychologist: fear-of-childbirth sessions (about 6), starting within 2 weeks","If PTSD criteria are met: trauma-focused CBT or EMDR, which can be given in pregnancy","Birth reflections appointment with a senior midwife to go through her first birth notes"],"social":["Named continuity midwife; quieter clinic times or community appointments to reduce hospital exposure","Partner at the obstetric appointment and on the labour ward visit","Childcare for her 3-year-old for appointments, via family or the health visitor's local support"],"safety":["Midwife phones within 24 hours of any missed appointment; GP copied in","Crisis contacts: perinatal team in hours, NHS 111 mental health option out of hours, 999 if unsafe","Review at 28 weeks; written birth plan in the notes by 32 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"NICE says offer the caesarean","x":"Anxiety-based request: offer perinatal mental health support first. If she still wants a caesarean, offer a planned one. An unwilling obstetrician should refer her to a colleague who will."},{"h":"Planned caesarean at 39 weeks","x":"NICE advises planned caesarean birth from 39 weeks unless there is a medical reason for earlier, because babies born earlier have more breathing problems."},{"h":"Control heals","x":"Feeling unheard or powerless in labour drives birth trauma. Write in consent before each examination, a stop word, a named team and clear explanations."},{"h":"Maternal mental health services","x":"NHS services in England for moderate to severe birth trauma, fear of childbirth and perinatal loss, combining psychology with maternity care; refer from midwife, GP or perinatal team."},{"h":"Ask what the wish means","x":"'I wish I wasn't pregnant' may mean fear, ambivalence, thoughts of ending the pregnancy, or suicidal thoughts. Ask each directly and record her words."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Assess her fear of childbirth and trauma symptoms, check risk, and agree birth options with obstetrics."},"c-p-04":{"obj":["Corrected the wrong advice and explained that both medicines are generally compatible with breastfeeding","Offered breastfeeding, mixed feeding and formula as real choices and supported her decision","Named the baby's warning signs and who will check feeding, weight and alertness","Kept both medicines unchanged and explained why lamotrigine must not be stopped and restarted","Agreed a night-feed plan that protects her sleep"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Why did she tell me I couldn't? I've wasted a whole day.\"","r":"\"You've every right to be upset, and I'm sorry. That advice wasn't correct. I'll raise it with the ward manager. Let's get you feeding her today if that's what you want.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm poisoning her by taking these, aren't I?\"","r":"\"No. You're keeping yourself well so you can care for her. A small amount does reach the milk, which is why we watch her, but for a healthy baby the benefits of breastfeeding usually outweigh that.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Could I just stop the lamotrigine for a few months while I feed?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd think that. The months after birth are when relapse is most likely. And if lamotrigine stops for about a week, it has to be built up again slowly because of rash risk.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What exactly am I watching for?\"","r":"\"If she's unusually sleepy, not waking for feeds, not gaining weight, has pauses in her breathing or a rash. Any of those, tell the midwife or health visitor that day.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I don't breastfeed, will I bond with her?\"","r":"\"Yes. Bonding comes from holding, talking and responding to her, however she's fed. Breast, bottle or both are all fine choices.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I haven't slept since she was born. I'm scared I'll go high.\"","r":"\"That's a really sensible worry, and I'm glad you said it. Let's plan a night feed your partner can do with expressed milk, so you get a proper block of sleep.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue lamotrigine 200 mg daily and sertraline 100 mg in the morning, unchanged; no missed doses","If lamotrigine stops for more than 5 half-lives (about a week), restart at the titration start dose, not 200 mg","If the dose was raised in pregnancy, plan a step-down after birth with the perinatal pharmacist; watch for dizziness, double vision, unsteadiness","Baby: midwife checks feeding and alertness daily; routine weighing around day 5 and day 10 to 14; paediatrician if concerns"],"psych":["Perinatal team review on day 7 and regularly through the first 6 weeks, checking sleep and early signs of hypomania or depression","Teach-back of the baby's warning signs before discharge"],"social":["Infant feeding lead to support the first feed today","Partner to give one expressed-milk feed at night; breast pump provided or loaned before discharge","Health visitor new birth visit (day 10 to 14) to include feeding and weight; GP 6 to 8 week check"],"safety":["Written plan to midwife, health visitor and GP with the baby's warning signs","Her own early signs: sleeping less without tiredness, racing thoughts; perinatal team same day, NHS 111 mental health option out of hours","Ward manager informed of the incorrect advice"]},"facts":[{"h":"Sertraline is a first choice","x":"Very little sertraline reaches breast milk and babies' blood levels are usually undetectable. It is a preferred antidepressant for breastfeeding."},{"h":"Lamotrigine: breastfeed and watch","x":"More lamotrigine reaches the baby than sertraline. A healthy term baby can usually breastfeed; watch for sleepiness, poor feeding, poor weight gain, breathing pauses and rash."},{"h":"Never restart at full dose","x":"If lamotrigine stops for more than 5 half-lives (about a week), restart from the titration start dose. A full-dose restart raises the risk of serious rash."},{"h":"Doses can need cutting after birth","x":"Lamotrigine clearance rises in pregnancy and falls back within weeks of birth. If the dose was raised, plan a reduction with the pharmacist to avoid toxicity."},{"h":"Use the lactation service","x":"For any medicine in breastfeeding, check the SPS UK Drugs in Lactation Advisory Service pages or phone their advisers, and record what you used."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain feeding choices on lamotrigine and sertraline, agree one with her, and set out the baby's monitoring."},"c-p-05":{"obj":["Established that the thoughts are unwanted, distressing, resisted and recognised as her own","Asked directly about intent, urges, psychotic symptoms, mood and suicidal thoughts","Explained that OCD-type intrusive thoughts do not increase the risk of harm to the baby","Made and recorded a reasoned safeguarding decision, with honest limits of confidentiality","Offered CBT with exposure and response prevention, sertraline, and signs that need same-day contact"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to take him off me?\"","r":"\"I can see how frightened you are. Having these thoughts is not a reason to take a baby away. I'll be honest throughout: from what you've told me, he's safe with you.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"What kind of mother thinks about stabbing her own baby?\"","r":"\"A frightened, loving one. These thoughts stick precisely because they're the opposite of what you want. Many new mums have them, and they don't make harm more likely.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've hidden all the knives. My partner thinks I'm mad.\"","r":"\"That makes sense as a way of feeling safer. The trouble is, hiding them tells your brain the thought is dangerous, so it comes back stronger. Treatment helps you undo that, step by step.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How do you know I won't do it?\"","r":"\"Because of how you are with these thoughts: you hate them, fight them and protect him. That's the pattern of OCD, not of someone who'll act. I'd be concerned if you wanted to act or felt it was right.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can you just tell me again that I'm not dangerous?\"","r":"\"I understand that need. Reassurance helps for a minute, then the doubt returns. I've told you clearly once; therapy will help you live with the doubt without needing it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I take tablets if I'm breastfeeding?\"","r":"\"Yes. For breastfeeding mums, sertraline is usually our antidepressant of choice; very little reaches the baby. It's an option alongside therapy, and the choice is yours.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Offer sertraline 50 mg in the morning; review at 2 to 4 weeks; increase by 50 mg steps up to 200 mg if needed (OCD often needs higher doses)","Warn: early nausea, loose stools, jitteriness; anxiety can rise in the first fortnight","Compatible with breastfeeding; baby's feeding and settling checked by the health visitor"],"psych":["CBT with exposure and response prevention, weekly, perinatal psychology or NHS Talking Therapies perinatal pathway","Psychoeducation today with an RCPsych perinatal OCD leaflet; partner invited to the next session","Partner coached to stop giving repeated reassurance, kindly"],"social":["Health visitor to continue visits and avoid reassurance loops; GP informed with consent","Peer support: local perinatal peer group or Maternal OCD resources"],"safety":["No safeguarding referral; reasoning recorded in the notes","Same-day contact if she feels urges to act, believes the thoughts are right, hears voices or stops sleeping","Crisis: perinatal team in hours, NHS 111 mental health option out of hours; review in 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Horror means OCD","x":"OCD harm thoughts are unwanted, resisted and recognised as her own; she avoids and checks to protect the baby. These thoughts are not linked to acting on them."},{"h":"Red flags for psychosis","x":"Thoughts feel justified or come from outside, beliefs about the baby being evil or swapped, voices, perplexity, little distress, severe sleep loss. Same-day senior review."},{"h":"Ask plainly, record verbatim","x":"Ask about intent, urges and past near-misses in plain words. Record her exact phrases; they show your reasoning if anyone later asks."},{"h":"Reassurance feeds OCD","x":"Repeated reassurance and avoidance give short relief and keep the cycle going. Say it clearly once, then treat with exposure and response prevention."},{"h":"Wrong referral does harm","x":"An unneeded child protection referral for OCD thoughts adds shame and teaches women to hide symptoms. Record why you did or did not refer."}],"type":"Risk assessment + History to a diagnosis","do":"Characterise the harm thoughts, exclude psychosis and intent, judge risk, and explain the diagnosis and treatment."},"c-p-06":{"obj":["Elicited re-experiencing, avoidance and sense of threat, with duration and impact, and made the diagnosis of PTSD","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts and thoughts of harming the baby, and explored bonding","Explained PTSD after birth in plain words, without blame","Explained trauma-focused CBT and EMDR, with number of sessions and who delivers them","Agreed practical steps: birth reflections, parent-infant support and the baby's vaccinations"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"My body failed her. A proper mum would have given birth normally.\"","r":"\"I'm really sorry you're carrying that. Nothing you did caused her heart rate to drop. Your body got her here safely with help. That guilt is part of the trauma, and therapy helps with it.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I woke up and she was gone. Nobody told me if she was alive.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying. Waking up alone, not knowing, is one of the hardest things a mother can go through. Thank you for telling me. Take all the time you need.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I say I don't feel like her mum, will you report me?\"","r":"\"No. It's common after being separated and frightened, and it's a symptom we can treat. You're caring for her well. Telling me helps me help you.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Everyone says I should just be grateful she's alive.\"","r":"\"You can be grateful and still be hurt by what happened. Both are true. Being told to be grateful can make it harder to ask for help.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Won't talking about it make it worse?\"","r":"\"It's a fair worry. In therapy you go back to the memory in a planned, safe way, at your pace. That's what makes it lose its power. Avoiding it keeps it raw.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going back into that hospital for anything.\"","r":"\"That makes sense with how you're feeling. We can see you here, away from the maternity unit. As therapy goes on, we'll plan any visits with you, at your pace.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medicine first line; if she prefers medication or depression is significant: sertraline 50 mg in the morning, review at 2 to 4 weeks, up to 200 mg","Physical check at the GP: wound healing, pain, iron, thyroid if fatigue is marked","Sleep: regular routine; avoid alcohol and over-the-counter sedatives"],"psych":["Trauma-focused CBT or EMDR, 8 to 12 weekly sessions, maternal mental health service psychologist; start within 4 weeks","Birth reflections appointment with a consultant obstetrician or senior midwife to go through her notes","Parent-infant work (for example video interaction guidance) via the perinatal team if bonding remains hard","Baseline PCL-5 and PHQ-9, repeated at session 4 and at the end"],"social":["Health visitor to give the baby's vaccinations at home or arrange a quiet first slot","Partner invited to a session; signpost him to support, as partners can be traumatised too","Birth trauma charity and local peer support group details given"],"safety":["No current risk to self or baby; recorded","Contact the team the same day if suicidal thoughts, thoughts of harming the baby, or not coping with her care","Crisis: NHS 111 mental health option out of hours, 999 if unsafe; review in 4 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Three clusters, then impact","x":"ICD-11 PTSD: re-experiencing now (flashbacks, nightmares), avoidance, and a sense of current threat, lasting several weeks with impairment. Ask each cluster in turn."},{"h":"Therapy first, not tablets","x":"NICE: trauma-focused CBT or EMDR is first line, 8 to 12 sessions. Drugs (an SSRI such as sertraline, or venlafaxine) only if she prefers them."},{"h":"Bonding follows the trauma","x":"Not feeling like the baby's mother is common after separation and fear, and usually improves with treatment. Explore it gently; it is not a safeguarding trigger alone."},{"h":"Birth reflections helps","x":"Going through the notes with a senior clinician fills gaps in memory and answers 'why'. Offer it to every woman with birth trauma who wants it."},{"h":"Fix the avoidance now","x":"Avoidance can hit the baby's care, such as missed vaccinations. Solve it practically: home visits or quiet slots, while therapy starts."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a treatment","do":"Take a trauma history to a PTSD diagnosis, screen risk and bonding, and explain trauma-focused therapy."},"c-p-07":{"obj":["Explained postpartum psychosis in plain words, including that it is nobody's fault and that most women recover fully","Gave an honest outlook: weeks for the acute illness, months for full recovery, and possible low mood afterwards","Stated the risk of recurrence after a future pregnancy as about 1 in 2 and named two ways to lower it","Agreed three concrete ways he can help on the unit and at home, and how to contact the team","Checked his own mood, sleep and risk, and offered a carer's assessment and partner peer support"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have seen it. She was up all night talking and I just thought she was excited.\"","r":"\"Lots of partners say exactly that. It came on fast, and early signs look like new-baby excitement. You noticed, and you got her help. That's why she's here now, getting better.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is she going to hurt Alfie?\"","r":"\"That's a natural fear. The staff are with them all the time, and Alfie is cared for safely here. As she gets better, those risks fall. Tell us anything she says that worries you.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will she ever be the same Hannah again?\"","r":"\"Most women make a full recovery. The worst usually settles in weeks; getting back to her usual self can take some months, and she may feel low on the way. That's part of recovery, not a relapse.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"She's not dangerous. Why is she locked up on a Section?\"","r":"\"I understand it feels wrong. She's very unwell and can't see it right now, so the Section lets us treat her safely. It's for up to 28 days, and it's reviewed all the time.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can we have another baby?\"","r":"\"Yes, many couples do. The honest answer is there's about a one in two chance it could happen again. Planning before pregnancy, with the perinatal team, lowers that and means help starts the day she gives birth.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I haven't slept in a week. I'm meant to be holding it all together.\"","r":"\"You've had a huge shock, and you're running on empty. Holding it together includes looking after yourself. Can we talk about who can help you this week?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Olanzapine 5 mg at night, increasing to 10 mg after 2 to 3 days if tolerated; check weight, BMI, fasting glucose and lipids at baseline and at 12 weeks","Lorazepam 0.5 to 1 mg orally as needed for severe agitation, maximum 4 mg in 24 hours; review daily","Bloods already done: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFTs, calcium, glucose, CRP; repeat TFTs at 6 weeks to catch postpartum thyroiditis","MBU pharmacist checks every medicine for breastfeeding; Alfie watched for sleepiness and poor feeding if breastfed"],"psych":["Family meeting with the consultant and named nurse, Friday 14:00, for both of them","Psychoeducation for Hannah and Tom about early warning signs once she is calmer","Offer partner sessions with the MBU psychologist if Tom has intrusive memories of the admission"],"social":["Carer's assessment request to the local authority (Care Act 2014), made by the MBU social worker","Tom given Action on Postpartum Psychosis partner peer support details","Tom to take statutory paternity leave and see his GP about further time off or a fit note","Nursery nurse to support Tom and Hannah with Alfie's care during visits"],"safety":["Tom told how to reach the nurse in charge 24 hours a day and to report any change in her talk or behaviour","Tom's GP told, with his consent, that he is under strain; he knows to ring 111 (mental health option) if he feels unsafe","Health visitor and GP informed of admission; future-pregnancy plan to be written into her discharge summary"]},"facts":[{"h":"About 1 to 2 in 1,000 births","x":"Postpartum psychosis is rare but well known. Onset is usually within two weeks of birth; risk is highest with bipolar disorder, a past episode, or a mother or sister who had it."},{"h":"Recurrence: about 1 in 2","x":"After one episode, about half of women become unwell after a later birth. A pre-conception plan, specialist antenatal care and protected sleep after birth lower the risk."},{"h":"Recovery is slow after fast onset","x":"The acute illness usually settles over weeks. Full recovery takes months, and low mood or anxiety afterwards is common; warn the family so they do not read it as failure."},{"h":"Husband is nearest relative","x":"He can order discharge from Section 2 with 72 hours' written notice; the responsible clinician can bar it. Explain his rights early so he trusts the process."},{"h":"Ask about her mother","x":"A mother or sister with postpartum psychosis raises the risk sharply. Partners often know family history the patient cannot give while unwell."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Family meeting","do":"Explain postpartum psychosis, outlook and 1-in-2 recurrence risk to her husband, and agree three ways he helps."},"c-p-08":{"obj":["Rechecked suicidal ideas, intent, thoughts of harm to the baby and psychotic features before recommending admission","Explained why admission is needed now, naming the failure of home treatment and the rising risk","Described what happens on the MBU, including that the baby stays with her and feeding is supported","Answered her fear of social services and of being judged as a bad mother honestly","Agreed informal admission today with capacity recorded, transport and the husband informed"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I go in, they'll take her off me. Social services will be involved.\"","r":"\"I can see why you'd worry about that. Going to the unit is about getting you well, with Lina beside you. It isn't a social services referral. We tell the health visitor and GP, as we always would.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"A proper mum wouldn't need to go to hospital.\"","r":"\"Depression after a baby is an illness, not a test of being a good mum. You noticed you weren't right and let us help. That is exactly what a good mum does.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't the crisis team just come three times a day instead?\"","r":"\"I wish more visits would do it. They've come twice a day for a week and you're feeling worse. The unit gives you help through the night, which is when it's hardest.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if I say no?\"","r":"\"It's your decision and I'd respect your view. I'd be honest though: I'm worried enough about your safety that if things got worse, we might have to think about the Mental Health Act. I'd much rather we agree this together.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I just feel she'd be better off with someone else.\"","r":"\"That sounds such a painful thought to live with. It's the depression talking, and it's one of the reasons I'm worried. When you're better, most mums find that feeling goes.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I still breastfeed there?\"","r":"\"Yes. The staff will help you feed her, and you can sleep while they settle her between feeds if you express. The pharmacist checks your medicine is fine for feeding.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg to 100 mg once daily in the morning from today; compatible with breastfeeding; review mood and side effects weekly","Admission bloods: FBC, ferritin, B12, folate, TFTs, U&E, LFT, glucose, CRP","Weigh her on admission and weekly; food and fluid chart for 72 hours","No routine hypnotic: protect sleep with night-feed support first; any hypnotic is agreed with the MBU pharmacist because she is breastfeeding"],"psych":["Named MBU nurse to agree a sleep plan: nursery nurse covers night feeds with expressed milk for two nights","Mother-infant interaction work with the MBU nursery nurse and psychologist once mood lifts","CBT for depression with the perinatal psychologist when concentration allows"],"social":["CPN to escort Amira and Lina by car at 15:00; husband Karim told by phone with her consent","MBU social worker to help with travel costs for Karim's evening visits","Health visitor, GP and community midwife informed of admission by the perinatal team today"],"safety":["Within-eyesight observation until transfer; she is not left alone with Lina before admission","MBU admission risk assessment on arrival, then nursing observation level set by the MBU team","If she withdraws consent before transfer: senior review and consider MHA assessment"]},"facts":[{"h":"Three perinatal red flags","x":"Recent big change in mental state, new thoughts or acts of violent self-harm, and new persistent feelings of failing as a mother or being estranged from the baby. Any one needs urgent senior review."},{"h":"MBU is the default","x":"NICE: a woman needing admission within 12 months of birth should normally go to a mother and baby unit, unless there are specific reasons not to."},{"h":"Sertraline 50 to 200 mg","x":"Start 50 mg in the morning, increase in 50 mg steps no faster than weekly. Low levels in breast milk; usually the first choice when breastfeeding."},{"h":"Not a safeguarding referral","x":"Admission is health care, not a child protection action. Say so plainly; fear of losing the baby is the commonest reason women refuse help."},{"h":"Ask about harm to the baby","x":"Ask directly about thoughts of harming the baby. Intrusive unwanted thoughts are common and frightening; thoughts she agrees with or plans are not."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + Risk assessment","do":"Recheck risk, explain why a mother and baby unit admission is needed, answer her fears, and agree admission today."},"c-p-09":{"obj":["Established core depressive symptoms, duration and severity, separating depressive sleep loss from baby-related waking","Quantified alcohol use in units and asked about the birth and past depression","Asked about anger, coping with crying and thoughts of harming the baby, and praised safe coping","Completed a suicide risk enquiry with protective factors","Agreed sertraline 50 mg, NHS Talking Therapies, an alcohol goal and a review in 2 weeks"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm probably wasting your time. All new dads are knackered.\"","r":"\"You're not wasting my time. Tiredness is normal; what you've told me about mood, sleep and drinking is more than that. Let's look at it properly.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Efua had the operation. I've got no right to feel like this.\"","r":"\"Her recovery matters, and so do you. Watching an emergency birth is frightening for partners too. Feeling low isn't taking anything away from her.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I shouted at her at three in the morning. A baby. What kind of dad does that?\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest. Lots of parents feel that rage at night. You put her in her cot and walked out. That was the right thing to do. Can we talk about it?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want tablets. I'll just push through.\"","r":"\"That's your choice, and therapy alone is an option. Pushing through has been going on for two months, though. Sertraline helped you before. Could we try it for six weeks and review?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will they think I'm a danger to the baby if I tell the health visitor?\"","r":"\"No. Telling her that you're getting help for depression is a strength. If I ever thought Ama wasn't safe, I'd tell you myself before anything else.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"The drinking is the only thing that helps me switch off.\"","r":"\"It feels like it helps at first. Then it wakes you at four and makes the mood worse the next day. Could we aim for no drinking on work nights?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Sertraline 50 mg once daily in the morning; review at 2 weeks; increase in 50 mg steps to 100 mg if partial response at 4 to 6 weeks; continue at least 6 months after recovery","Warn: nausea, loose stools, headache and early agitation in the first 2 weeks; sexual side effects; do not stop suddenly","Alcohol: from about 50 units a week to no drinking on work nights and under 14 units a week; check for withdrawal (morning shakes, sweats) before cutting down; repeat AUDIT-C at review","Bloods via GP: FBC, LFT, GGT, TFTs"],"psych":["Self-referral to NHS Talking Therapies today: CBT for depression, about 12 to 16 sessions with a high-intensity therapist","Ask Talking Therapies to screen for birth-related PTSD symptoms","Sleep plan agreed with Efua: alternate night feeds, one protected night each a week"],"social":["Health visitor informed with his consent; she can support both parents and check Efua's mood","GP fit note for two weeks if needed; consider occupational health and a phased return","Local dads' peer support group details and the NHS website page on fathers' mental health"],"safety":["Crisis plan: NHS 111 mental health option, Samaritans 116 123, and 999 if he feels he may act","Coping with crying: put Ama safely in her cot, walk away, breathe, ask Efua or a friend to take over (ICON message)","GP review in 2 weeks; CMHT review if worse, if suicidal thoughts become active, or if any thought of harming Ama appears"]},"facts":[{"h":"About 1 in 10 fathers","x":"Paternal depression after a birth is common and peaks around 3 to 6 months. It is more likely if the mother is depressed or he has had depression before."},{"h":"Men present differently","x":"Irritability, anger, long hours at work, drinking and physical symptoms often replace obvious sadness. Ask about them directly."},{"h":"Sleep when the baby sleeps","x":"Waking because the baby cries is normal. Lying awake while the baby sleeps, or waking early, points to depression."},{"h":"Praise safe coping","x":"Putting a crying baby in the cot and walking away is the ICON advice. Saying so lowers shame and makes honest disclosure more likely."},{"h":"Ask how the mother is","x":"Depression in one parent raises the risk in the other. One question about her mood can prevent a second missed diagnosis."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Diagnose his depression, assess risk to himself and the baby, and agree medication, therapy and review."},"c-p-10":{"obj":["Used the baby's name and invited her to talk about him and the birth","Distinguished grief from depression by asking about enjoyment, self-worth and how pervasive the low mood is","Screened for post-traumatic symptoms: intrusions, nightmares and avoidance","Clarified a wish to be with the baby versus active suicidal thoughts, plans and intent","Agreed support (bereavement midwife, Sands, optional therapy) and a review in 4 weeks with signs to return sooner"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Everyone wants me to be getting better. It's only been six weeks.\"","r":"\"Six weeks is no time at all. There's no timetable for losing Leo. I'm not here to hurry you, just to make sure you have the right support.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"My body failed him. It's my fault.\"","r":"\"I hear how much you're blaming yourself. We don't know the cause yet. When the results come, the consultant will go through them with you. Can we look at what's helping you carry this?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I told them he wasn't moving as much. They sent me home.\"","r":"\"That sounds incredibly painful to live with. Your questions matter. The hospital reviews every stillbirth, and you can send your questions to be part of it. I'll help you do that.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I just want to be with him.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Many parents feel that. Can I ask more? Have you had thoughts about ending your life, or made any plans?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Am I depressed? Should I be on tablets?\"","r":"\"From what you've told me, this sounds like grief, which is painful but normal. Tablets don't treat grief. If it turns into depression, we'd talk about treatment, and I'll keep checking with you.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How do I tell people at work?\"","r":"\"You choose how much to say. Some parents ask a manager to tell the team before they go back. You still have your full maternity leave, so there's no rush.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No antidepressant now: no depressive episode. Reassess at 4 weeks with PHQ-9 and a PTSD screen","GP to check physical recovery: bleeding, wound or perineum, contraception wishes, and iron if she was anaemic","Advise against using alcohol or sleeping tablets to sleep; simple sleep routine and daylight walks"],"psych":["Continued weekly contact with the bereavement midwife until the post-mortem results meeting","Offer counselling or NHS Talking Therapies now (her choice); trauma-focused CBT or EMDR if PTSD symptoms persist at 3 months","Offer the same support to Ben, separately or as a couple"],"social":["Sands: helpline, online community and local parent groups, for her and Ben","Bereavement midwife to support PMRT questions and the post-mortem results appointment with the consultant obstetrician","PALS details if she wants to raise concerns about care","Maternity leave and pay continue in full; GP fit note if needed after that"],"safety":["Crisis plan: NHS 111 mental health option, Samaritans 116 123, and contact the clinic if the wish to be with Leo becomes a thought of ending her life","Expect hard days: Leo's due date, the results meeting and anniversaries; plan support for each","Review in 4 weeks; GP informed by letter today"]},"facts":[{"h":"Grief keeps self-worth","x":"In grief the world feels empty; in depression the self feels worthless. Waves, moments of relief and preserved self-esteem point to grief."},{"h":"Prolonged grief needs 6 months","x":"ICD-11 prolonged grief disorder needs persistent longing or preoccupation for at least 6 months, with marked impairment. Do not diagnose it at 6 weeks."},{"h":"Register within 42 days","x":"A stillbirth after 24 weeks must be registered within 42 days in England and Wales. The bereavement midwife usually helps parents with this."},{"h":"Parents can shape the review","x":"Every stillbirth is reviewed with the PMRT. Parents are invited to send questions and receive the findings; tell them how."},{"h":"A wish to be with the baby","x":"Common in grief. Ask openly, then clarify any active thoughts, plans and intent. Do not assume it is suicidal, and do not ignore it."}],"type":"Risk assessment + History to a diagnosis","do":"Separate grief from depression and trauma, assess suicide risk, and agree support with a 4-week review."},"c-p-11":{"obj":["Validated her fear and anger before explaining the pre-birth assessment and conference","Explained possible conference outcomes honestly without promising one","Told her what your report will say, including at least two strengths, and offered to show it to her","Assessed self-harm, suicide, substance use and domestic abuse, with her seen alone","Agreed three concrete actions she controls before the conference, and a review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You're all building a case to take my baby.\"","r":"\"I can hear how frightened you are. I'm not here to build a case. I'm here to help you, and to be honest with you about everything, including what I write.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If I do everything you say, will I keep her?\"","r":"\"I wish I could promise that. I can't, and I won't lie to you. What I can say is that what you do now really counts, and I'll make sure it's in my report.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I was in care. I know how this ends.\"","r":"\"Your experience makes complete sense of how scared you are. Your story isn't decided yet. You've already cut down a lot, and that shows what you can do.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Ryan pushed me once. But he was stressed.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that was brave. Being pushed is not OK, whatever the stress. I'd like to ask a few more questions so we can keep you and the baby safe.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going to that conference. They'll just judge me.\"","r":"\"It will be hard, I know. If you're there, they hear your side from you. You can bring your personal adviser, and you'll see the reports first. Would that make it more possible?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I want to cut right now. I can't do this.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me rather than hiding it. Let's stay with this for a minute. What helped last time the urge was this strong? Shall we ring Jade together?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No psychotropic medicine needed now; avoid benzodiazepines; do not start valproate in pregnancy","Nicotine replacement: 16-hour patch 15 mg plus inhalator, via the stop smoking in pregnancy service","Weekly urine drug screen with the substance misuse midwife; growth scans as the obstetric team plans","Self-harm wounds checked by GP or midwife; tetanus status checked"],"psych":["DBT-informed skills group or individual skills work with the perinatal psychologist, weekly, starting before birth","Crisis and distress-tolerance plan written with her: ice, cold water, call personal adviser, crisis line","Parent-infant relationship work booked for after birth (perinatal team and health visitor)"],"social":["Social worker leads the pre-birth assessment; your written report is shared with Chloe before the conference","IDVA referral with her consent; DASH completed; MARAC if the score reaches the threshold (14 or more, or professional judgement)","Personal adviser (leaving care team) to attend the conference with her and help with a solicitor if pre-proceedings start","Family group conference offered to identify any family support"],"safety":["Social worker told of the domestic abuse disclosure today; Chloe knows and agreed the wording","Crisis numbers: perinatal team in hours, 111 mental health option out of hours, 999 if in danger","Review in 1 week, then fortnightly until birth; midwife and health visitor in the core group"]},"facts":[{"h":"Parents see the reports","x":"Under Working Together, parents are invited to conferences and should see reports in advance. Write factually, include strengths, and show her before you send it."},{"h":"Never promise the outcome","x":"You do not decide where the baby lives. Promises you cannot keep destroy trust; honest uncertainty with concrete actions keeps her engaged."},{"h":"Ask about abuse alone","x":"Domestic abuse often starts or worsens in pregnancy. Ask with the partner out of the room; use the DASH checklist and refer to an IDVA."},{"h":"Care leavers keep a personal adviser","x":"Care leavers are entitled to a personal adviser up to 25. Invite them; they are often the steadiest adult in her life."},{"h":"Strengths are evidence","x":"Reduced use, attendance, insight and support are real evidence. A balanced report is fairer and more useful to the conference."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Breaking bad news","do":"Explain the pre-birth conference and your report honestly, assess risks including domestic abuse, and agree concrete actions."},"c-p-12":{"obj":["Took a panic history and screened for worry, avoidance, checking and fear of childbirth","Asked about breathlessness at rest, chest pain, calf swelling and palpitations, and arranged FBC, ferritin and TFTs","Assessed mood, self-harm and thoughts of harm to the baby","Explained panic in plain words and reassured her honestly that attacks do not harm the baby","Agreed high-intensity CBT, self-help and a 2-week review, respecting her choice not to take medicine"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Every time it happens I think I'm going to die and the baby will die with me.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying. Panic makes your body feel in danger when it isn't. Your heart and breathing speed up, but attacks pass and don't hurt you or the baby.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want any tablets. I've read they cause problems for the baby.\"","r":"\"I hear you, and that's your choice. The best first treatment is talking therapy anyway. If you ever want to talk about medicines, I'll give you the real facts, and you decide.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How do you know it's not my heart or something with the pregnancy?\"","r":"\"Good question. Let me ask some specific things and check your pulse and oxygen. I'll also do blood tests for anaemia and thyroid, which can cause similar feelings.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I keep thinking the stress will damage the baby.\"","r":"\"Many mums-to-be worry about that. Everyday stress and panic attacks don't damage the baby. Getting help now is good for both of you, and you're doing that.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't even get on a bus any more. I feel pathetic.\"","r":"\"You're not pathetic. Avoiding is what everyone does with panic, and it keeps the fear going. Therapy helps you take those steps back, a little at a time.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I'm terrified of the birth. My friend nearly died.\"","r":"\"No wonder you're frightened, after seeing that. Let's make sure your midwife knows. You can talk through birth options with the team, and there's a specialist service if the fear stays strong.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medication: informed choice recorded. If she later chooses one, sertraline 50 mg once daily is commonly used in pregnancy; avoid benzodiazepines","Bloods: FBC, ferritin, TFTs; ECG if palpitations occur outside panic or are irregular","Cut caffeine to one cup a day (pregnancy limit 200 mg/day)","Red flags for same-day review: breathlessness at rest, chest pain, calf swelling, severe headache or visual change"],"psych":["High-intensity CBT for panic disorder via NHS Talking Therapies perinatal pathway (priority), about 12 to 14 weekly sessions","While waiting: NHS panic self-help, slow breathing (in 4, out 6), and planned graded steps back onto the bus with her partner","Referral to the maternal mental health service if fear of childbirth stays severe after initial therapy"],"social":["Midwife: early birth-plan conversation and options discussion with the obstetric team if fear persists","Partner invited to a session so he can help with graded exposure and know the plan","Health visitor told before birth: higher risk of postnatal anxiety or depression"],"safety":["Review in 2 weeks in perinatal clinic; GAD-7 at each contact","If mood drops or thoughts of self-harm appear: contact the perinatal team in hours, 111 mental health option out of hours","Postnatal plan in her notes: perinatal team review within 2 weeks of birth"]},"facts":[{"h":"Therapy first for anxiety","x":"NICE recommends high-intensity psychological therapy for anxiety disorders in pregnancy: assessment within 2 weeks of referral, therapy within 1 month of assessment."},{"h":"No benzodiazepines in pregnancy","x":"NICE: only short-term for severe anxiety and agitation. Risks include dependence and neonatal sedation or withdrawal if used near birth."},{"h":"Think PE before panic","x":"Pregnancy raises clot risk. Breathlessness at rest, pleuritic pain, a swollen calf or low sats need same-day medical assessment, not reassurance."},{"h":"Panic does not harm the baby","x":"Attacks are frightening but short and self-limiting. Saying this clearly and honestly lowers the fear that fuels the next attack."},{"h":"Anxiety predicts postnatal illness","x":"Antenatal anxiety raises the risk of postnatal depression and anxiety. Write a postnatal plan and tell the health visitor."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Diagnose her panic and anxiety, exclude physical causes and risk, and agree a CBT-first plan without medication."},"c-s-01":{"obj":["Established today's overdose: trigger, amount, intent, alcohol and how help was called","Elicited the lasting pattern with examples: fast mood shifts, anger, emptiness, shifting self-image, intense relationships, fear of being left, impulsivity and repeated self-harm","Excluded a depressive episode, hypomania and psychosis, and recognised stress-linked paranoia for what it is","Assessed current suicidal intent, future risk and violence in relationships","Explained the working diagnosis in plain, non-blaming words and agreed a crisis plan and referral"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Seriously, another doctor? I've told my story five times. Just let me go home.\"","r":"\"That's fair, and I'm sorry you've had to repeat yourself. I'm asking because this keeps happening, and I want the help to fit. Give me fifteen minutes and then we plan getting you home.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You lot just think I'm attention-seeking.\"","r":"\"I don't. Taking sixteen tablets tells me you were in real pain this morning. I want to understand that pain, not judge it.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm fine now. It was a one-off.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you feel safer now. It's the fourth time this year, though, so let's look at what sets it off, so the next row doesn't end the same way.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"So I've got a personality disorder. That means I'm a psycho, doesn't it?\"","r":"\"No. It's an awful name for something common. It means your emotions hit harder and faster than most people's. It isn't about being bad, and it often gets much better with the right therapy.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Everyone leaves me. My mum left. Why would anyone stay?\"","r":"\"That sounds incredibly painful, and it makes sense that being left hurts so much now. We don't have to go into your childhood today. Can I ask what helps when that feeling hits?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't you just give me a tablet to stop the mood swings?\"","r":"\"I wish it were that simple. There isn't a tablet for this pattern itself. What works best is therapy that teaches skills for strong feelings, like DBT. I'll refer you today.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"When I'm really stressed I think people are talking about me.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Does it pass when things calm down, and do you know deep down they're not? That helps me tell stress-linked worries apart from other problems.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No new psychotropic for the personality pattern itself (NICE); review any existing prescriptions for overdose danger","Alcohol: brief intervention today; give the drink-line number; GP to recheck LFTs in 2 weeks if abnormal today"],"psych":["CMHT referral for assessment for DBT (individual therapy, skills group and phone coaching over about a year) or MBT","Interim: structured clinical management by the care coordinator; written crisis plan agreed today"],"social":["Friend collects her today and takes the remaining paracetamol home","Signpost to local self-harm support and to the domestic abuse helpline in case past violence recurs"],"safety":["Crisis plan: triggers (rows, alcohol), what helps, who to call (friend, crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, 999)","GP letter today: small prescriptions only; CMHT duty worker to phone her within 72 hours"]},"facts":[{"h":"Duration separates EUPD from bipolar","x":"Mood shifts lasting minutes to hours, set off by people, point to EUPD. Hypomania lasts days, with little need for sleep and no tiredness."},{"h":"No tablet for the disorder itself","x":"NICE advises against drug treatment for borderline personality disorder or its individual features. Treat a coexisting depressive episode or psychosis on its own merits."},{"h":"Paracetamol pack limits","x":"UK shops sell paracetamol in packs of up to 16 tablets, pharmacies up to 32. Removing stockpiles at home is a simple, effective safety step."},{"h":"Risk tools do not predict suicide","x":"NICE NG225: do not use risk scales to predict suicide or decide who is discharged. Base the plan on a full psychosocial assessment."},{"h":"Plenty of people recover","x":"Many people with this diagnosis no longer meet the criteria within a few years, especially with structured therapy. Say this out loud: hope is part of the treatment."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Explain a diagnosis","do":"Elicit the emotionally unstable pattern, exclude mood and psychotic disorders, assess risk, and explain the working diagnosis kindly."},"c-s-11":{"obj":["Checked his cardiovascular risk factors: smoking, family history, blood pressure, diet, activity and symptoms of high sugar","Explained the results as metabolic syndrome linked to clozapine, in plain words, and confirmed clozapine will continue","Agreed a safe plan to stop smoking with a clozapine level before the quit date and weekly afterwards","Agreed lifestyle support, metformin and atorvastatin, with the checks each needs and the GP's role","Gave warning signs of very high blood sugar and closed with a written plan and review date"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"You're going to take my clozapine away, aren't you?\"","r":"\"I can hear how much it matters to you, and it should. No, that's not the plan. Clozapine has kept you well for 15 years. We'll work on the side effects around it.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"The paper said I'll die 20 years early. Nobody ever told me.\"","r":"\"That's a shocking thing to read, and I'm sorry no one talked it through with you. The gap is real, but much of it comes from things we can change: smoking, weight, blood pressure, sugar. That's what today is for.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm stopping smoking Monday. Cold turkey. That's good, isn't it?\"","r":"\"It's a brilliant decision. One catch: smoking keeps your clozapine level down, so stopping suddenly can push it up within days. Let's set the date together and check your level first.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want more tablets. I feel fine.\"","r":"\"That's fair; nobody wants a bag of pills. High sugar and cholesterol don't make you feel ill until they do damage. Could we go through what each one does, then you decide?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why am I hungry all the time?\"","r":"\"That's the clozapine; it turns up appetite for many people. It's not weakness. Eating at set times, more protein and fewer sugary snacks helps, and metformin can take the edge off.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I can't cook. It's crisps and microwave meals mostly.\"","r":"\"Thanks for being honest; lots of people are in the same spot. The dietitian can show you cheap, simple meals, and your support workers can help with shopping and cooking.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue clozapine 400 mg/day; level before any smoking change, then weekly for 4 weeks after quitting; team to cut the dose gradually if the level rises","Metformin 500 mg once daily with breakfast for 1 week, then 500 mg twice daily with meals, up to 1 g twice daily; eGFR before and yearly; B12 if long-term or symptoms; warn about nausea and loose stools","Atorvastatin 20 mg at night; lipids and LFTs before, LFTs at 3 and 12 months; lipids at 3 months aiming for a 40% drop in non-HDL; report unexplained muscle pain","GP to confirm BP with 7 days of home readings and offer treatment if 135/85 or more on average","Repeat weight, waist, BP, HbA1c and lipids at 3 months, then at the annual check"],"psych":["Motivational approach: he chooses one food change and one activity change today","Stop Smoking Service: weekly support plus combination NRT (24-hour patch plus a short-acting form); quit date set with the team"],"social":["Dietitian referral and NHS Diabetes Prevention Programme (Healthier You) via GP","Supported accommodation key worker: help with shopping lists and two cooked meals a week","Care coordinator: free or low-cost walking group or leisure-centre access scheme"],"safety":["Warning signs of very high sugar: severe thirst, passing lots of urine, vomiting, drowsiness; same-day GP or 111, 999 if very unwell","Signs of a high clozapine level after quitting: more sleepy, more drooling, jerks, confusion; ring the team that day","Written plan to him and to his key worker; GP letter; clinic review in 4 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Quitting smoking raises clozapine levels","x":"Tobacco smoke (not nicotine) speeds clozapine breakdown. Stopping can raise levels by up to half within a week. NRT does not have this effect. Check a level first and weekly after."},{"h":"Metabolic syndrome in one line","x":"Waist 94 cm or more in a man (80 cm in a woman) plus two of: triglycerides 1.7 or more, low HDL, BP 130/85 or more, fasting glucose 5.6 or more."},{"h":"Prediabetes numbers","x":"HbA1c 42–47 mmol/mol or fasting glucose 5.5–6.9 mmol/L is non-diabetic hyperglycaemia. HbA1c 48 or more means diabetes. It qualifies for the NHS Diabetes Prevention Programme."},{"h":"Statin threshold","x":"QRISK3 counts severe mental illness and atypical antipsychotics. At 10% or more, offer atorvastatin 20 mg after discussing lifestyle (NICE NG238)."},{"h":"Clozapine and ketoacidosis","x":"Clozapine can rarely trigger sudden severe hyperglycaemia or DKA, even without known diabetes. Tell every patient the warning signs."},{"h":"Switch last","x":"In treatment-resistant illness, switching away from clozapine risks relapse. Treat each metabolic problem first; consider a switch only if all else fails, with specialist advice."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Explain his results as metabolic syndrome, plan safe smoking cessation on clozapine, and agree metformin, statin and GP actions."},"c-s-12":{"obj":["Took a brief illness and medication history, linking the weight gain in time to olanzapine","Screened for health effects of the weight and other cardiovascular risk factors, and checked his monitoring","Explained why olanzapine causes weight gain, its health risks and why a switch is not advised","Agreed specific diet and activity changes and a referral to a structured weight programme","Discussed metformin and aripiprazole add-on with honest benefits and risks, and booked follow-up monitoring"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I hate what I see in the mirror. I look like a different person.\"","r":"\"That sounds really hard, especially when you've worked so hard to get well. Your feelings about your body matter as much as the voices. Let's make a plan for both.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Could I just come off it again for a bit to lose the weight?\"","r":"\"I understand the pull. Last time you stopped, you became unwell within months. Let's keep you well and work on the weight at the same time.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it the tablet or is it me?\"","r":"\"Mostly the tablet. Olanzapine turns your appetite up, so you feel hungry when you've eaten enough. It's not a lack of willpower; it just means we need a plan that fights the hunger.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going to a group. Everyone will stare.\"","r":"\"Lots of people feel that before the first session. The groups are small and everyone's there for the same reason. If it still feels too much, there's a free NHS app you can use at home.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there a tablet that just stops the weight?\"","r":"\"Not a magic one, sadly. Metformin helps people lose about 3 kg on average, and adding aripiprazole about 2 kg. They work best on top of food and activity changes.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"My girlfriend says I snore like a train now.\"","r":"\"Thanks for mentioning that; it's important. With the weight gain and long sleep, it could be sleep apnoea, where breathing pauses at night. I'll ask your GP to look into it.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue olanzapine 10 mg at night; no dose change for weight","Weight monthly for 3 months; HbA1c and lipids today and at 3 months, then yearly; BP and pulse at each review","If still gaining at 3 months: metformin 500 mg once daily with food, increase weekly by 500 mg to 1 g twice daily if tolerated; eGFR before and yearly, B12 if long-term","Alternative add-on: aripiprazole 5 mg each morning; warn about restlessness, nausea and poor sleep; review mental state after 2 weeks","STOP-BANG score today; GP referral for sleep study if high"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing: he picks two changes now (no snacks after 8 pm; walk 30 minutes 5 days a week)","Structured group programme covering eating, activity and emotional eating, through the trust's healthy-living group or GP weight service"],"social":["Care coordinator: help join a council leisure-centre discount scheme","Partner invited to next review to help with shopping and cooking changes"],"safety":["Do not stop olanzapine without talking to us; early signs of relapse written in his plan","Review in 3 months; earlier if weight rises more than 2 kg a month"]},"facts":[{"h":"Appetite drives olanzapine weight gain","x":"Most gain comes from increased hunger and bigger portions, fastest in the first months. Explain this so the patient blames the drug, not himself, and targets the hunger."},{"h":"Add-on medicines: honest numbers","x":"Average extra loss: metformin about 3 kg, aripiprazole add-on about 2 kg. Both work best on top of lifestyle change. Never promise more."},{"h":"Metformin checks","x":"Check eGFR first; avoid if eGFR is under 30. Start low with food to limit stomach upset. Check B12 with long-term use or if numbness or tiredness develops."},{"h":"Weight gain makes people stop","x":"Distress about weight is a leading reason people stop antipsychotics. A weight plan is relapse prevention."},{"h":"When not to switch","x":"If several other antipsychotics failed and stopping this one led to relapse, a switch for weight puts his mental health at risk. Work around the effective drug."}],"type":"Medication review + Management plan","do":"Link his weight gain to olanzapine, screen his physical health, and agree a weight plan with follow-up."},"c-s-14":{"obj":["Let her voice her anger and worries, apologised for the poor communication and confirmed Ryan's consent","Explained what NMS is, its features and why it may have happened, in plain words","Explained the treatment on the medical unit, the possible complications and an honest outlook","Explained how and when an antipsychotic will be restarted safely","Agreed a named contact, daily updates and a meeting with the consultant"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You poisoned him. He came here to get better.\"","r":"\"I can understand why it feels like that, and I'm so sorry this has happened. You have every right to answers. Let me tell you exactly what we know.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Nobody told me anything. They kept saying 'confidentiality'.\"","r":"\"That should never have happened, and I'm sorry. Ryan has said we can share everything with you, so I'll answer every question you have.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is he going to die?\"","r":"\"That's the most frightening question there is, and I'll be honest. NMS can be dangerous, which is why he was moved so quickly. He's responding well, and the medical team expect a good recovery.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Did you put the dose up too fast?\"","r":"\"It's a fair question. A quick increase is one known risk factor, and we're reviewing exactly how his doses were given. I'll make sure you hear what we find.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So he can never take these tablets again?\"","r":"\"Most people can, safely. We'd wait until he's fully better, choose a different medicine, start very low and go slowly, with daily checks. You'd be part of that decision.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Who do I ring? I can't go through another night like that.\"","r":"\"You won't have to. His named nurse will ring you at 4 pm every day, and here's the ward number for any time. You'll meet his consultant on Monday.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Haloperidol stopped; recorded as a serious adverse reaction on the drug chart and in the GP letter","Medical unit: IV fluids, cooling, lorazepam for agitation and stiffness; CK, U&Es and temperature daily; VTE prophylaxis","Restart no sooner than 2 weeks after full recovery (normal CK, no fever or stiffness); a different, lower-potency antipsychotic, low dose, slow increase","On restart: temperature, pulse and BP 4 times a day, CK twice weekly for the first weeks; no depot"],"psych":["Liaison psychiatry review on the medical unit daily; lorazepam for distress while off antipsychotics","Psychoeducation for Ryan and his mother about NMS warning signs when restarting"],"social":["Named nurse to ring mother daily at 16:00; consultant meeting Monday 14:00","PALS details given; incident review outcome shared with her"],"safety":["Section 2 continues; Section 17 leave to the general hospital with a mental health nurse","Yellow Card report and incident report submitted today","Warning signs on restart: fever, stiffness, sweating, confusion, fast pulse; stop the drug and call the doctor"]},"facts":[{"h":"NMS in four words","x":"Rigidity, fever, autonomic instability, confusion. Add a high CK. Usually starts within days of starting or raising an antipsychotic; any antipsychotic can cause it."},{"h":"Know the risk factors","x":"Rapid dose increase, high-potency drugs, IM or depot use, dehydration, agitation, restraint and previous NMS. Name them honestly if a family asks why."},{"h":"Stop, cool, fluids, transfer","x":"Stop the antipsychotic, give IV fluids and cooling, use benzodiazepines, and move to medical care. Dantrolene or bromocriptine are for severe cases under the medical team."},{"h":"Kidneys are the danger","x":"Muscle breakdown releases myoglobin and can damage the kidneys. Watch CK, creatinine and urine output. Clots and chest infection are the other main complications."},{"h":"Rechallenge rules","x":"Wait at least 2 weeks after full recovery. Choose a different, lower-potency drug, start low, go slow, avoid depots, and check temperature, pulse, BP and CK closely."},{"h":"Apology is not liability","x":"Saying sorry for harm or poor communication is expected under the duty of candour and is not an admission of legal liability."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Handling a complaint","do":"Explain NMS, its cause, treatment, outlook and safe restart to his angry mother, and agree updates."},"c-s-22":{"obj":["Read the ECG in order and recalculated the QTc (465 ms over the root of 0.8 s, giving 520 ms)","Explained the slow recharge and its risk in plain words, without frightening her","Checked symptoms, cardiac and family history, every drug including clarithromycin, extra doses, and the low potassium and calcium","Agreed to hold quetiapine tonight and attend same-day medical assessment, with the consultant and medical registrar informed","Agreed the restart dose, the aripiprazole fallback, a repeat ECG after a week, CPN relapse checks and a 999 safety net"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I feel perfectly well. What's all the fuss about?\"","r":"I'm really glad you feel well. This kind of rhythm change gives no warning until it causes trouble, which is why we act while you feel fine."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Please don't take my tablet off me. The voices nearly finished me last year.\"","r":"That sounds terrifying, and I don't want that for you either. We're pausing it to make your heart safe, then restarting at a dose your heart was happy on."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do you think it's that tablet and not the others?\"","r":"Good question. It's probably a mix: the antibiotic and low potassium add to it. But your tracing was nearly normal before the increase, so the higher quetiapine dose is the biggest change."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going to hospital. I've got the cat to feed.\"","r":"I understand; the cat matters. Could Karen feed her later? This check is a few hours, and at this level it really is safest done today."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it my blood test that's done it?\"","r":"Partly, yes. Your potassium and calcium are low, and both lengthen the recharge. The hospital will top them up today, and the GP will look at the water tablet that lowers potassium."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Did I do this? I took those antibiotics without asking anyone.\"","r":"Not at all. Your GP prescribed them for a real infection. Nobody could expect you to know about the interaction. That's our job, and we've caught it."}],"bps":{"bio":["Hold quetiapine MR from tonight (consultant agreed); same-day SDEC assessment: repeat 12-lead ECG, cardiac monitoring, potassium, magnesium and calcium replacement.","Restart quetiapine MR 400 mg at night only when QTc < 470 ms and potassium is normal; ECG 1 week after restart and after any dose change.","If QTc stays long on 400 mg: switch to aripiprazole, started low (e.g. 5 mg each morning) and titrated by the consultant, with ECG after each change.","GP: review indapamide (consider an alternative antihypertensive), and check magnesium, calcium and vitamin D; avoid macrolides in future where possible."],"social":["Daughter Karen drives her to SDEC today and holds a copy of the clinic letter."],"safety":["999 for palpitations, fainting, blackouts or chest pain.","CPN visits weekly during the change to watch for return of voices; consultant review within 1 week.","Alert added to her record: QTc prolongation; check before any new QT-prolonging drug. Yellow Card submitted."]},"facts":[{"h":"Check the machine's numbers","x":"Count the rate yourself (QRS complexes in 10 seconds × 6) and measure the QT by eye. A misread rate or T wave gives a wrong QTc."},{"h":"How to calculate QTc","x":"Bazett: QT ÷ √RR (seconds). At 75 bpm, RR is 0.8 s, so 465 ÷ 0.894 ≈ 520 ms. Above 90 bpm prefer Fridericia (cube root), as Bazett overcorrects."},{"h":"The 500 ms line","x":"Normal is up to about 440 ms in men and 460–470 ms in women. Above 500 ms, or a rise of more than 60 ms, act the same day: stop the cause, get medical advice."},{"h":"Antipsychotics differ","x":"Haloperidol and pimozide carry high QTc risk; quetiapine and chlorpromazine moderate; olanzapine, risperidone and clozapine low; aripiprazole and lurasidone very little."},{"h":"Fix the electrolytes","x":"Keep potassium above 4 mmol/L and correct magnesium and calcium in anyone with a long QTc. Diuretics, vomiting and diarrhoea are the usual culprits."}],"type":"ECG and investigations + Medication review","do":"Calculate and explain her QTc, check risk factors, hold quetiapine and arrange same-day medical assessment and follow-up."},"c-s-23":{"obj":["Named the ECG as an anterior STEMI from ST elevation in V1–V4 and I with reciprocal depression in II, III and aVF","Took a brief focused history: site, character, onset, radiation, sweating and previous heart disease","Explained in plain words that she is having a heart attack and must go to hospital now, not home","Had 999 called, gave aspirin 300 mg after checking allergy and bleeding, and monitored her with a defibrillator at hand","Handed over the ECG, medicines and history to paramedics and informed her husband, GP and consultant"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"It's only indigestion, doctor. I get it all the time.\"","r":"I can see why you'd think that, and you know your body. But today's tracing shows a heart attack, and in diabetes it often feels like indigestion. We need to act now."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Am I going to die?\"","r":"That's a frightening thought, and it's natural to be scared. The ambulance is coming, the treatment works best when it's early like this, and I'm staying with you."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'll just go home and rest. Alan will drive me to the hospital later if it's no better.\"","r":"I understand you want to be with Alan. But resting won't unblock the artery, and every minute counts. I'll call Alan and he can meet you there."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it my schizophrenia tablets that did this?\"","r":"Fair question. Mainly it's diabetes, blood pressure and cholesterol. Quetiapine can add to blood fats and sugar, so we'll review it together once you're recovering. Don't stop it today."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What will they do to me at the hospital?\"","r":"Usually they pass a thin tube from your wrist to your heart, while you're awake, and open the artery with a small mesh tube. Most people go home within a few days."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have rung someone this morning instead of getting the bus.\"","r":"You did the right thing by coming in, and that's how we found it. Many people think it's indigestion. What matters is that you're getting help now."}],"bps":{"bio":["Aspirin 300 mg chewed once, after checking allergy and active bleeding; paramedics give GTN and opioid pain relief and pre-alert the cath lab.","Oxygen only if SpO2 < 94%; obs every 5 minutes until handover: BP, HR, SpO2, RR, consciousness.","After discharge: secondary prevention from cardiology (dual antiplatelet, high-intensity statin, ACE inhibitor, beta-blocker); psychiatry reviews quetiapine's metabolic effects and repeats ECG and lipids."],"social":["Clinic nurse calls 999; doctor phones husband Alan to meet her at the heart attack centre.","Cardiac rehabilitation team informed of her schizophrenia so the programme can be adapted."],"safety":["Defibrillator at her side; do not leave her alone; she must not drive or travel by public transport.","Handover to paramedics with ECG copy, medication list and mental health history; GP and consultant informed the same day."]},"facts":[{"h":"STEMI needs territory and reciprocity","x":"ST elevation in two adjacent leads of one territory, plus ST depression in the opposite leads, means STEMI. Widespread concave elevation without reciprocity suggests pericarditis."},{"h":"Diabetes hides chest pain","x":"Older people, women and those with diabetes often describe indigestion, breathlessness, nausea or tiredness. Take any new 'indigestion' with ECG changes seriously."},{"h":"Aspirin 300 mg, chewed","x":"Give once if not allergic and no active bleeding. Chewing speeds absorption. Record the time and tell the paramedics so it isn't repeated."},{"h":"Oxygen only if low","x":"Give oxygen in suspected heart attack only if saturations are below 94%. Routine oxygen does not help and may harm."},{"h":"Primary PCI within two hours","x":"Ambulance crews take STEMI patients straight to a heart attack centre. Primary PCI is preferred if delivered within 120 minutes of when fibrinolysis could have been given."}],"type":"Emergency management + ECG and investigations","do":"Recognise anterior STEMI, check her symptoms, explain simply, give aspirin and arrange 999 transfer with a full handover."},"c-s-24":{"obj":["Opened with patient details, indication and calibration before reading the tracing","Presented rate, rhythm, axis, PR, QRS, ST and T in a fixed order with measured values","Calculated the QTc by Bazett and Fridericia and explained when each misleads","Gave a one-line summary and compared it with last year's ECG","Explained pre-clozapine checks, myocarditis, cardiomyopathy and when to repeat the ECG, and told infarct and pericarditis apart"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why bother with calibration? It's always the same.\"","r":"Usually it is, and that's exactly why it catches people out. If the speed or gain was changed, every number I give you would be wrong. It takes two seconds."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is Bazett good enough here?\"","r":"At 84 it's acceptable, but it overcorrects as the rate rises. Clozapine patients are often fast, so I'd also give Fridericia, which is about 380 here."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I lost a patient to myocarditis. His ECG looked almost normal.\"","r":"I'm sorry; that must stay with you. It's a strong reminder that a near-normal tracing doesn't exclude it, so his symptoms and the troponin and CRP matter just as much."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So he's past the first months. We're safe now, aren't we?\"","r":"Mostly, for myocarditis. But cardiomyopathy can come later, and his metabolic risk adds up, so new breathlessness or swelling still needs an ECG and an echo."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What's your exact troponin schedule during titration?\"","r":"Most UK protocols test weekly for the first four weeks. I'd want to check our trust's version before quoting exact thresholds."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Anything else we should know about ECGs?\"","r":"Always compare with the last one, and never file one you're unsure about. If in doubt, I'd ask the medical team the same day."}],"bps":{"bio":["Clozapine 400 mg/day continues; ECG at baseline, after dose increases, annually and with any cardiac symptoms.","During titration: troponin and CRP at baseline and weekly for 4 weeks (local protocol); pulse, BP and temperature at each dose step.","Annual: weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids, ECG; consider statin and lifestyle support if risk is raised."],"safety":["Patient told to report chest pain, breathlessness, palpitations, fever or fainting at once.","Any raised troponin or CRP with symptoms: hold clozapine and arrange urgent medical review the same day."]},"facts":[{"h":"Rate in two seconds","x":"Count QRS complexes on the 10-second rhythm strip and multiply by 6. For a regular rhythm, 300 divided by large squares between R waves also works."},{"h":"Axis from two leads","x":"Leads I and aVF both positive means a normal axis. I positive, aVF negative and lead II negative means left axis deviation."},{"h":"Bazett over, Fridericia under","x":"Bazett (QT ÷ √RR) overestimates QTc at fast rates. On clozapine, where tachycardia is common, quote Fridericia (QT ÷ cube root of RR) too."},{"h":"Myocarditis hides early","x":"Usually within 4–8 weeks of starting clozapine. Fever, persistent tachycardia, chest pain, flu-like illness. ECG often non-specific; rising troponin and CRP make the diagnosis."},{"h":"Pericarditis versus infarct","x":"Pericarditis: widespread saddle-shaped ST elevation, PR depression, no reciprocal change. Infarct: one territory with reciprocal ST depression opposite."}],"type":"ECG and investigations + Handover and escalation","do":"Present the ECG in a fixed order with measured intervals and QTc, then explain clozapine's cardiac risks and monitoring."},"c-s-25":{"obj":["Confirmed his mother's consent to share, and drew out his doubts about infection and his fears","Explained dementia and vascular dementia in plain words, linking them to her stroke, diabetes, blood pressure and cholesterol","Showed how history, normal bloods and urine, HADS, ACE-III 68/100 and the MRI rule out infection and depression and support the diagnosis","Explained the stepwise course, risk-factor control, why memory tablets are not used, and that it is not directly inherited","Raised the DVLA duty and wandering safety, offered CST, a dementia adviser, a carer's assessment and LPA, and checked understanding"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"She's fine. She still drives and goes to her club. I don't buy it.\"","r":"It's hard to hear when she seems so well to you. Many people keep up routines early on. Can I show you what the tests found, and you tell me what you think?"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"It's a water infection. I see it every day at work.\"","r":"That's a sensible thought, and we checked. Her urine and bloods were clear, and infection comes on over days, not months. So we can be confident it isn't that."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have nagged her about her blood pressure tablets.\"","r":"That shows how much you care. But this built up over many years, from many causes. You didn't cause it. What helps now is supporting her from here."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can she have donepezil, like my mate's dad?\"","r":"Good question. Those tablets help in Alzheimer's, but not in vascular dementia alone, and they have side effects. Her blood pressure, sugar and cholesterol tablets protect her brain more."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will I get it? Will my kids?\"","r":"It's natural to worry. Vascular dementia isn't usually passed down directly, but diabetes and blood pressure can run in families, so keeping yours in check lowers your risk."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You're taking her car? That's all she's got left.\"","r":"I know how much her independence means. The law says she must tell the DVLA, and getting lost makes driving risky for her and others. Let's think together about other ways she can stay independent."}],"bps":{"bio":["GP letter: BP target under 140/90 (individualised), HbA1c review, atorvastatin dose review, clopidogrel 75 mg adherence; consider a dosette box or pharmacy blister pack.","No acetylcholinesterase inhibitor or memantine (pure vascular dementia, NICE NG97); review if features of mixed Alzheimer's emerge.","Urine and bloods if she suddenly worsens, to catch delirium on top of dementia."],"psych":["Group cognitive stimulation therapy, 14 sessions, memory service.","Advice to the family on irritability: routine, calm tone, distraction rather than argument; check for pain or infection if behaviour changes suddenly."],"social":["Alzheimer's Society dementia adviser contacts the family within 2 weeks.","Carer's assessment for Ron by the local authority; Attendance Allowance and council tax reduction information.","Lasting Power of Attorney (health and welfare, and property and financial affairs) while she has capacity."],"safety":["Driving: advise her to stop now and notify the DVLA; doctor informs the DVLA if she continues against advice.","Wandering: GPS tracker; Herbert Protocol form with local police.","Review in the memory clinic in 6 weeks; team number given."]},"facts":[{"h":"Delirium versus dementia","x":"Delirium: hours to days, fluctuating attention, often drowsy, physical cause. Dementia: months to years, steady progression. Clear bloods and urine plus a slow onset point away from infection."},{"h":"No donepezil in pure vascular","x":"NICE NG97: do not offer cholinesterase inhibitors or memantine for vascular dementia alone. Consider them only when it is mixed with Alzheimer's, Lewy body or Parkinson's disease dementia."},{"h":"Stepwise, but not always","x":"Vascular dementia often worsens in steps after new strokes, but small vessel disease can progress gradually. Good blood pressure, glucose and lipid control can slow it."},{"h":"Dementia means telling the DVLA","x":"Car drivers with dementia must notify the DVLA. Getting lost while driving or walking is a red flag. If she drives against advice, the GMC allows you to tell the DVLA."},{"h":"Herbert Protocol for wanderers","x":"A police form kept by the family with a recent photo, habits and places she might go. Handing it over saves time if she goes missing."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis","do":"Explain vascular dementia, its evidence, course, risk-factor care, driving and support to her son, checking understanding throughout."},"c-s-26":{"obj":["Found out what she already knew and her main worry before explaining anything","Explained dementia with Lewy bodies in plain words, linking each core feature and the DaT scan to his own symptoms","Given an honest account of the likely course, without false hope","Explained rivastigmine and the danger of antipsychotics, and agreed to flag his sensitivity on his records","Agreed a falls and home-safety plan, checked understanding with teach-back and set a family meeting"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So it's Parkinson's? He doesn't shake.\"","r":"\"Good question. It's a close cousin of Parkinson's: the same protein clumps, but here they hit thinking first. Many people with Lewy bodies never get a tremor; the stiffness and falls are the movement part.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I kept telling myself he was just getting old. I should have pushed the GP.\"","r":"\"You noticed, and you got him help. The good days in between make this illness very hard to spot, even for doctors. You've done nothing wrong.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"He nearly hit my little girl with that stick. What if it happens again?\"","r":"\"That must have been terrifying. Keeping the children safe is part of the plan: we'll look at the stick, the lighting and what to do when he sees things, before he comes home.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Why can't you just give him something to stop the hallucinations?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting them gone. The usual medicines for that, antipsychotics, can make people with Lewy bodies stiff, fall, or become dangerously unwell, as happened on the medical ward. Rivastigmine often helps the hallucinations more safely.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"How long has he got?\"","r":"\"I'm sorry; that's a hard question to ask. It does shorten life, and it gets worse over time, but the pace varies a lot. Many people live several years, and good care makes those years better.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"When he sees the dogs, should I tell him they're not real?\"","r":"\"Arguing tends to upset him. Stay calm, ask what he sees, reassure him he's safe, and switch on a brighter light. If he's not frightened by them, you don't need to do anything.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Rivastigmine 1.5 mg twice daily with breakfast and evening meal; increase by 1.5 mg twice daily every 2 weeks to 3–6 mg twice daily as tolerated (or patch 4.6 mg/24 h, then 9.5 mg/24 h after 4 weeks)","Before starting: pulse and ECG; hold if heart rate under 60 or heart block. Warn: nausea, vomiting, poor appetite, weight loss, dizziness, slow pulse","No antipsychotics; alert on drug chart, electronic record and GP summary. If severe distressing psychosis despite rivastigmine, consultant decision only","Lying and standing BP; regular laxative (macrogol) and review of constipation; stop drugs that worsen confusion or falls"],"psych":["Hallucinations: calm reassurance, good lighting, glasses on, no arguing; record frequency and distress","REM sleep behaviour disorder: move sharp or hard objects from the bedside; consider separate bed if injuries"],"social":["Ward OT home visit before discharge: rugs, glass panels, lighting, grab rails; physio falls assessment and stick review","Carer's assessment for Abena by the local authority; ward social worker to discuss care package and day centre","Encourage Lasting Powers of Attorney now; Alzheimer's Society leaflet and Dementia Connect support line"],"safety":["Children not left alone with him during frightened or confused spells; stick kept by his chair, not used as a weapon at night","Discharge plan: GP and community older adult team told of antipsychotic sensitivity; memory clinic review in 4–6 weeks","Crisis: community team duty line in hours, NHS 111 option 2 out of hours, 999 if anyone is in danger"]},"facts":[{"h":"Four core features","x":"Fluctuating attention and alertness, recurrent detailed visual hallucinations, REM sleep behaviour disorder, parkinsonism. Two core features, or one plus an abnormal DaT scan, make DLB probable."},{"h":"The one-year rule","x":"Dementia preceding parkinsonism, or starting within a year of it: dementia with Lewy bodies. Parkinson's for over a year before dementia: Parkinson's disease dementia."},{"h":"Antipsychotic sensitivity can kill","x":"Up to half of people with DLB react badly: sudden rigidity, falls, drowsiness, confusion, sometimes an NMS-like state. Flag it everywhere; haloperidol is the classic culprit."},{"h":"Check the pulse first","x":"Cholinesterase inhibitors slow the heart. Check pulse and ECG before starting; caution with heart rate under 60, heart block or a history of fainting."},{"h":"Rivastigmine or donepezil","x":"NICE: offer either for mild to moderate DLB; galantamine only if both are not tolerated; memantine if cholinesterase inhibitors are unsuitable."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Explain a treatment","do":"Explain DLB through his own symptoms and scan, warn about antipsychotics, and agree a falls plan."},"c-s-27":{"obj":["Gained consent and asked briefly about shaking, stiffness, slowness, falls, sleep and autonomic symptoms","Examined face, tremor, tone with activation and bradykinesia with decrement, comparing both sides","Examined posture, gait, turning and postural instability safely","Measured lying and standing blood pressure correctly and identified orthostatic hypotension","Explained the findings to him and presented them to the consultant as probable DLB, with safety actions"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to walk. I'll only fall over again.\"","r":"\"That's a sensible worry after three falls. I'll walk right beside you, and you can hold my arm to start. Seeing how you walk helps us stop the falls.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why are you twisting my wrist like that?\"","r":"\"Sorry if it feels odd. I'm feeling for stiffness in the muscles. Some illnesses that affect memory also make muscles stiff, and that changes which medicines are safe for you.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Have I got Parkinson's, then?\"","r":"\"I can see you're worried. Some of what I've found is like Parkinson's. The consultant and I will put it together with your memory tests and talk it through with you and June today.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"June says I punch the pillow in my sleep. Is that relevant?\"","r":"\"Thank you for mentioning it; it's very relevant. Acting out dreams is linked with the same group of conditions. How long has it been happening?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I used to make furniture. Now I can't even write my name properly.\"","r":"\"That must be hard for someone who's worked with his hands all his life. Smaller writing is one of the signs I'm looking for, and there's help for it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why lie down for blood pressure? It was fine at the GP's.\"","r":"\"Good question. Sitting readings can look normal. Some people's pressure drops when they stand, which makes them dizzy and fall. Lying first then standing shows that.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Postural drop: GP to review amlodipine 5 mg and stop tamsulosin if possible; repeat lying and standing BP in 4 weeks","Advise: stand up slowly, sit on the bed edge first, 1.5–2 litres fluid daily unless restricted","If DLB confirmed: donepezil 5 mg at night after pulse and ECG check, 10 mg after 4 weeks if tolerated","Antipsychotic sensitivity alert on record; avoid metoclopramide and prochlorperazine (dopamine blockers)"],"psych":["Ask June about fluctuation and dream enactment; record in collateral history"],"social":["Falls clinic referral; community physio for gait and balance; OT home assessment","Carer's assessment for June by the local authority"],"safety":["Pendant alarm while falls continue; walking aid assessed by physio","Results discussed with consultant today; diagnosis given to him and June in clinic"]},"facts":[{"h":"Decrement makes it bradykinesia","x":"Ten finger taps or hand openings: true bradykinesia gets smaller and slower as it goes. Slow but steady is not enough."},{"h":"Activate to find rigidity","x":"Ask him to open and close the other hand while you rotate the wrist. Hidden cogwheel rigidity often appears only then."},{"h":"Postural BP done properly","x":"Lie 5 minutes, measure. Stand, measure at 1 and 3 minutes. Drop of 20 systolic or 10 diastolic, or symptoms, is significant."},{"h":"Tremor is optional in DLB","x":"Parkinsonism in DLB is often symmetrical and stiff-slow without much tremor. No tremor never rules it out."},{"h":"Look up, look down","x":"Check vertical eye movements. Limited downgaze with early backward falls suggests progressive supranuclear palsy, not DLB."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Examine for tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, gait and postural instability, measure postural BP, and link findings to DLB."},"c-s-28":{"obj":["Let him express his anger and reflected it back before giving information","Explained BPSD as a sign of distress or unmet need, and named likely causes to check: pain, constipation, infection and grief","Explained why risperidone was used, its limits and its risks, including stroke and death, without underplaying them","Agreed a specific non-drug plan using what he knows about his brother","Agreed no dose increase, a stop date, a review date with him, and how to raise concerns"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You've turned him into a zombie so your staff have an easy life.\"","r":"\"I can hear how angry you are, and if I thought that about my brother I'd feel the same. Nobody should be drugged for convenience. Let's sit down and look at exactly what's happening.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"The paper said these pills kill people with dementia.\"","r":"\"You've read it right: they do raise the risk of stroke and death. That's why we use them only when someone is at real risk, at the lowest dose, for weeks not months.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"He keeps calling for Margaret. He never got over losing her.\"","r":"\"That's heartbreaking, and thank you for telling me. Grief can show up as agitation in dementia. It's something we can work with, and it isn't something a tablet fixes.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Who gave permission? Nobody asked me.\"","r":"\"That's a fair question. You should have been consulted. If he can't decide himself, we make a best-interests decision and talk to family first. I'm asking you now, and I'll record your views.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just take him off it today, then.\"","r":"\"I understand. Stopping suddenly can bring the hitting straight back. I'd rather halve it from tomorrow while we treat the likely causes, and aim to stop it completely by the end of November.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So what will you actually do instead of pills?\"","r":"\"Good question. Treat pain and constipation, track what sets him off, change how carers approach washing, and use things he loves. What music did Leonard enjoy?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No increase. Reduce risperidone to 250 micrograms at night from tomorrow; stop date 6 weeks from start unless serious risk returns","Paracetamol 1 g four times daily for 2 weeks (check weight over 50 kg and liver function), then review with PAINAD","Macrogol 1–2 sachets daily; bowel chart","Bloods today: FBC, U&E, CRP, LFT, TFT, glucose, calcium; no urine dipstick in over 65s, send urine culture only if urinary symptoms or fever"],"psych":["ABC chart for 2 weeks by care home staff; review patterns at the next visit","Personalised care: his music during washing, photos of his wife, same two carers, approach from the front and explain each step","Screen for depression and grief (Cornell Scale for Depression in Dementia) at the 2-week review"],"social":["Brother to bring photographs and a life story; care home activities coordinator to plan daily occupation","Check hearing aids and glasses are in and working"],"safety":["Watch for sedation, falls, swallowing problems and stiffness; tell GP or team the same day if they appear","If he hits out: step back, give space, try again later; document on ABC chart","Review with brother in 2 weeks; best-interests decision recorded; how to contact PALS given"]},"facts":[{"h":"Risperidone: 6 weeks, not forever","x":"Licensed only for persistent aggression in moderate to severe Alzheimer's with risk of harm, up to 6 weeks. Start 250 micrograms twice daily; usual best dose 500 micrograms twice daily."},{"h":"Stroke and death, honestly","x":"Antipsychotics in dementia raise stroke risk about threefold and add roughly 1 death per 100 treated over 12 weeks. Say it plainly; relatives respect honesty."},{"h":"Pain looks like aggression","x":"People who can't say 'my knee hurts' hit out during washing. Use PAINAD or the Abbey Pain Scale, and try regular paracetamol before any antipsychotic increase."},{"h":"No dipsticks over 65","x":"UKHSA: don't use urine dipsticks to diagnose UTI in people over 65; bacteria in urine are common and harmless. Send a culture only with symptoms."},{"h":"Who decides","x":"Next of kin can't consent for an adult. Health and welfare LPA can; otherwise the clinician decides in his best interests after consulting family, and records it."}],"type":"Explain a treatment + Management plan","do":"Calm the brother, explain BPSD and risperidone risks honestly, and agree a non-drug plan with a stop date."},"c-s-29":{"obj":["Checked consent and found out what he and Margaret want to know","Explained mild cognitive impairment as not dementia, and what each test result ruled out","Given an honest chance of progression in natural frequencies, including that many people stay stable","Explained why no medication is offered and agreed concrete risk-reduction steps","Arranged a 12-month review and a plan to see Margaret herself, and checked his understanding"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is this how it started with her mum? Just be honest.\"","r":"\"I will be. I can see how frightening that is after watching her mum. This isn't Alzheimer's or any dementia. It does mean we keep an eye on her, and there's a lot she can do now.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If it's not dementia, why is it called impairment?\"","r":"\"Fair point; it's a clumsy name. It means her memory tests a bit lower than expected for her age, but not enough to affect her daily life. That's the line between this and dementia.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't she just try the dementia tablets in case?\"","r":"\"I understand wanting to do something. Studies show those tablets don't help at this stage and do cause side effects. What does help is exercise, blood pressure and hearing, and those are in her hands.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should our children get tested for the Alzheimer's gene?\"","r":"\"Good question. For Alzheimer's starting in later life, like her mum's, gene tests don't give useful answers and aren't recommended. Healthy habits matter more.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I've been so short with her when she forgets things.\"","r":"\"That's a very human reaction, and it shows how worried you've been. Now you know what's going on, it may feel easier. Prompts like a shared calendar help you both.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can she keep driving?\"","r":"\"Her driving isn't affected, and that's what matters. If you ever notice getting lost, near misses or new dents, tell us or the GP, and we'll look at it again.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No cholinesterase inhibitor or memantine (NICE)","GP: blood pressure check and treat to target; HbA1c yearly; review any anticholinergic medicines","Hearing test at the GP or NHS audiology; hearing aids if needed, worn daily","Alcohol under 14 units a week; stop smoking if she smokes"],"psych":["Memory aids: shared calendar, phone reminders, a fixed place for keys and the shopping list","Treat low mood or anxiety early if it appears; HADS repeated at review"],"social":["Exercise 150 minutes of moderate activity a week, such as brisk walking, plus strength twice weekly","Keep up social and mentally active roles: groups, volunteering, learning","Suggest Lasting Powers of Attorney for health and welfare and for finances"],"safety":["Repeat ACE-III and review in 12 months; earlier if daily function slips (money, cooking, getting lost, driving)","Tell her to stop driving and contact us or the GP if near misses or getting lost occur","GP letter with results and plan; copy to patient"]},"facts":[{"h":"The line is daily function","x":"MCI: tests show a deficit but daily life is intact. Dementia: decline in thinking that affects everyday function. Ask about money, cooking, appointments and driving."},{"h":"Natural frequencies","x":"Say 'about 1 in 10 a year in memory clinics', and add that some stay stable or improve. Never say 'it will turn into Alzheimer's'."},{"h":"No tablets for MCI","x":"NICE: don't offer cholinesterase inhibitors or memantine for MCI. They don't stop progression and cause nausea, diarrhoea and slow pulse."},{"h":"What lowers the risk","x":"Treat hearing loss and high blood pressure, keep active, avoid smoking and heavy drinking, stay socially connected, manage diabetes and depression. Make it personal."},{"h":"Explain tests by what they rule out","x":"Normal HADS: not depression. Normal bloods: not thyroid, B12 or folate. CT: no stroke, tumour or bleed. ACE-III 81: real but mild deficit."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis","do":"Explain MCI and her results to her husband, give honest progression risk, risk-reduction steps and follow-up."},"c-s-30":{"obj":["Named the specific decision and gave her the relevant information in plain words","Tested understanding, retention, using and weighing, and communication with open questions","Screened for falls causes, mood, nutrition and isolation","Told her the conclusion clearly and that the decision is hers","Agreed a discharge safety plan she accepts and kept the offer of carers open"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Another doctor? I've told everyone. I don't need strangers washing me.\"","r":"\"I hear that, and I'm sorry you've had to keep saying it. I'm not here to force carers on you. I want to make sure the decision is properly yours and properly recorded.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I only fell because the hospital floor's slippery.\"","r":"\"That may well have played a part. You've also been very poorly and your eyes aren't at their best. What would you do if you fell at home?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I stay here any longer, this place will kill me.\"","r":"\"After the pneumonia, I'm not surprised you feel that. You're medically ready to go, and nobody can keep you here because of this decision.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"It does get lonely at home, since Arthur died.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me that. Lots of people feel that way living alone. Would you like someone from a befriending service to visit or ring each week?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So are you saying I'm not all there?\"","r":"\"Not at all. Everyone starts from the position that they can make their own decisions. It's my job to check that properly and write it down, so nobody questions it later.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll have the OT come round, but not carers every day.\"","r":"\"That sounds like a sensible middle way. Let's add a pendant alarm and the physio too, and you can ask for carers any time if things get harder.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Lying and standing BP before discharge; if drop of 20/10 or more, ask the medical team to reduce amlodipine 10 mg to 5 mg","Expedite cataract surgery: GP to chase ophthalmology","Falls clinic referral; vitamin D and bone health review by GP","Diabetes: HbA1c at GP review; metformin sick-day advice"],"psych":["Brief mood screen today (low mood, interest, sleep, appetite): no depression; recheck at GP review"],"social":["Community OT home visit within a week: perching stool, bath board, rails, kitchen trolley","Community physio for strength and balance (6–8 sessions)","Neighbours to help with shopping and meals in the first fortnight; Age UK befriending referral","Adult social care number given; carers or free reablement can start whenever she asks"],"safety":["Pendant alarm before discharge; how to get up from the floor taught by physio","GP review within 2 weeks; discharge letter includes capacity conclusion and falls plan","If she falls and can't get up, is hurt, or becomes confused again: press the pendant or ring 999"]},"facts":[{"h":"Name the decision","x":"Capacity is decision-specific. 'Capacity to refuse twice-daily carers on discharge' is assessable; 'capacity to go home' is too vague and invites error."},{"h":"Unwise is not incapable","x":"MCA s1(4): a person is not treated as unable to decide merely because the decision is unwise. Risky choices by capacitous adults are respected."},{"h":"Test weighing with scenarios","x":"Ask 'What would you do if you fell at night?' and 'How will you wash in the first week?' Answers show whether she uses the information, not just repeats it."},{"h":"Delirium first","x":"Check 4AT on the day. If delirium persists, capacity may fluctuate; if the decision can wait, reassess when she is clearer."},{"h":"Reablement is free","x":"Short-term reablement after hospital is free for up to six weeks. Some people who refuse 'carers' accept 'help to get back on your feet'."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"Assess her capacity to refuse carers, tell her the conclusion, and agree a safe discharge plan she accepts."},"c-s-31":{"obj":["Confirmed that his mental state is not distorting his decisions about money","Tested understanding, retention, use and weighing, and communication using his own income, rent and arrears figures","Told him the outcome in plain words, separating disorganisation from incapacity","Agreed at least two support measures he chose, such as rent paid direct and debt advice","Gained his consent for exactly what will be shared with the housing officer"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm not ill. Why does everyone think I'm mad because I forgot the rent?\"","r":"\"That's a fair complaint. Your diagnosis doesn't mean you can't run your money, and the law says I start by assuming you can. I just need to ask about this one decision.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Are you going to take my money off me?\"","r":"\"I can hear that worries you. No. If you can make this decision, it stays yours. Even if you needed help, we'd use the lightest help possible. Let's go through it together.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"The housing lot have wanted me out for ages. They're out to get me.\"","r":"\"That sounds stressful. Tell me more: what makes you feel they want you out? Is it the letters, or something else?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"A mate's been staying. He borrows money and doesn't always pay it back.\"","r":"\"Thanks for telling me. That happens to a lot of people and it isn't your fault. How much has he had, and do you feel able to say no to him?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If I've got capacity, why would I need anyone helping me?\"","r":"\"You don't have to have help; it's your call. Some people use help as a tool, like a direct debit, so the rent can't slip. What would suit you?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want my mum involved. She treats me like a kid.\"","r":"\"That's your right, and I won't contact her without your agreement. Is there someone else you'd trust, like a support worker, to help set things up?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue quetiapine MR 400 mg at night; ask about morning sedation, which can make post and bills slip","Annual physical health check: weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids"],"psych":["Care coordinator: short monthly budget review for 3 months, using his banking app and rent payment alerts"],"social":["Universal Credit managed payment: housing costs paid straight to the landlord, requested through his online journal","Debt adviser (Citizens Advice or StepChange): repayment plan for the £2,600 arrears; ask about Breathing Space","Support worker: four weekly sessions to set up direct debits for gas, electric and phone","With consent, housing officer told the outcome and the plan only"],"safety":["Relapse signature in the care plan: if paranoia about the landlord returns, reassess capacity then","Ask about exploitation at each review; adult safeguarding referral if money is being taken"]},"facts":[{"h":"Decision-specific, time-specific","x":"Capacity is about this decision, now. Day-to-day money differs from signing a tenancy or a large purchase: write down exactly which decision you tested."},{"h":"Unwise is not incapacity","x":"MCA s1(4): a bad outcome proves nothing on its own. If he understands and weighs it but forgot to pay, he has capacity and needs practical help."},{"h":"Abilities first, then the cause","x":"Since JB (2021), ask first whether he can understand, retain, use or weigh, and communicate; only then whether any inability is caused by his mental disorder."},{"h":"Rent paid straight to the landlord","x":"Universal Credit can pay the housing element directly to the landlord (a managed payment). He can ask through his online journal; landlords can request it once arrears build up."},{"h":"Appointee or deputy: only without capacity","x":"A DWP appointee manages benefits only; a Court of Protection deputy covers wider property and affairs. Neither applies to someone who can decide for himself."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"Test all four capacity abilities against his real money figures, tell him the outcome, and agree support he chooses."},"c-s-32":{"obj":["Elicited core and biological symptoms of depression, including weight loss, early waking and diurnal variation","Established that the 'rotten insides' belief is a delusion by testing certainty and evidence, and screened for other psychotic symptoms","Quantified food and fluid intake over three days and checked for dehydration and delirium","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts, plans, stockpiled tablets and self-neglect","Decided on same-day hospital care and explained it to her in plain words"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Don't make me eat. It'll only rot with the rest of me.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying, and I won't force anything on you now. Could you manage a few sips of water while we talk? Your body really needs it.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"There's nothing wrong with my mind. It's my body that's dying.\"","r":"\"I can see it feels completely real, and I won't argue. Severe depression can make the body feel like this, and that can be treated. Would you let us look after both?\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I'm just a burden on Susan. She'd be better off without me.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry you feel that. Feeling a burden is part of how this illness talks. When you feel like that, do you ever think about ending your life?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going into hospital. I want to die in my own bed, like Gerald.\"","r":"\"Your home matters, and so does Gerald. You're not dying, but you're very short of fluid, and that is dangerous. A short stay could make you strong enough to come home.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this dementia, doctor? She keeps losing track of what she's saying.\" (daughter)","r":"\"That's a fair worry. Severe depression often slows thinking, and lack of fluid can confuse her too. We'll check her bloods now and test her memory properly once she's better.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will she have to have electric shock treatment?\" (daughter)","r":"\"I understand that sounds frightening. ECT is an option if she still can't eat or drink; it's done under a short anaesthetic and often works quickly in this illness. Nothing happens without careful discussion.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Same-day bloods: U&E, FBC, glucose, calcium, LFT, TFT, B12, folate, CRP; ECG","Fluids: oral if she will drink; IV on the medical ward if dry or kidney function is impaired. Fluid and food charts, weight twice weekly","Mirtazapine 15 mg at night continues; the ward team plans an increase to 30 mg plus an antipsychotic (NICE: antidepressant with antipsychotic for psychotic depression), with ECG","ECT if intake stays dangerously poor or life is at risk"],"psych":["Bereavement support (e.g. Cruse) once the psychotic depression has lifted"],"social":["With her consent, Susan told the plan; ward and bed manager informed","Ward OT to assess daily living skills before discharge home"],"safety":["Not left alone today: Susan stays until transfer","Ward observation level set on arrival; any stored tablets at home removed by Susan","If she withdraws agreement: AMHP contacted the same day for MHA assessment"]},"facts":[{"h":"Fluid first, mood second","x":"An older adult on sips for three days may already have kidney injury or high sodium. Check pulse and lying and standing BP at home and get bloods today; antidepressants take weeks."},{"h":"Test the belief, don't fight it","x":"Ask how she knows, how certain she is, and whether anything could change her mind. Full certainty against the evidence, in keeping with her mood, means a mood-congruent delusion."},{"h":"Cotard-type beliefs are a red flag","x":"Nihilistic delusions (organs rotting, being dead) are rare and mostly seen in severe depression in later life. They often drive refusal to eat and raise suicide risk."},{"h":"Two drugs or ECT","x":"For psychotic depression NICE advises an antidepressant combined with an antipsychotic. ECT is considered when the illness is life-threatening or a rapid response is needed."},{"h":"Slowed thinking is not always dementia","x":"Severe depression and dehydration both impair attention and memory. Screen for delirium now; formal memory testing waits until mood and fluids recover."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Elicit severe depression with nihilistic delusions, quantify intake and suicide risk, and arrange same-day hospital care."},"c-s-33":{"obj":["Confirmed handedness, vision, hearing and limb strength before any test","Tested visual fields, visual inattention and all four Gerstmann features","Tested ideomotor, ideational, dressing and constructional praxis","Tested stereognosis, graphaesthesia, two-point discrimination and sensory inattention with her eyes closed","Localised the findings to left, right or both parietal lobes and stated the practical impact"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Are you the one with my scan results? Nobody tells me anything.\"","r":"\"Waiting for that is frustrating, I know. Dr Shah will go through the scan with you tomorrow, and I'll tell her you're asking. Today is just some short tests.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I used to do the books at the Co-op. Now I can't take seven from a hundred.\"","r":"\"That must be really upsetting, when numbers were your job. Thank you for sticking with it. That tells me something useful. Let's move to something different.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"What's the point of drawing pictures? I'm not a child.\"","r":"\"Fair question. Copying a shape shows how your brain judges space, which affects things like dressing and finding your way. It takes seconds and helps us plan the right help.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Am I going mad? The girls laugh when I get my cardigan wrong.\"","r":"\"You're not going mad. Some brain changes make it hard to match clothes to your body. That's not your fault, and nobody should laugh. We can make dressing easier.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not closing my eyes. I don't like not seeing what you're doing.\"","r":"\"That's fine; you're in charge. I'll show you each object first, then you can close your eyes just for a moment. You can open them whenever you want.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I get my numbers back?\"","r":"\"I can't promise that. Some people improve, and we can make things easier now: the nurses can read the menu with you and help with money.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Correlate with the CT (small vessel disease, old left parietal infarct); vascular risk review: BP, lipids, HbA1c, antiplatelet as advised by the stroke or medical team"],"psych":["Explain findings to her in plain words; graded, errorless prompts rather than correction when she struggles"],"social":["Ward OT: functional assessment of dressing, money handling and kitchen tasks within the week","Nurses: clothes laid out in order with verbal prompts; menu read aloud","With consent, family told which tasks need help before discharge planning"],"safety":["Large, clear signage to the toilet and her bed space; night light","Flag cognitive profile for any later capacity decision about money or discharge"]},"facts":[{"h":"Fields before neglect","x":"A field cut can look like neglect. Test each eye in four quadrants first, then both hands moving together; ignoring one side only when both move is visual inattention."},{"h":"Gerstmann has four parts","x":"Dyscalculia, agraphia, finger agnosia and left-right confusion point to the dominant angular gyrus. Two or three is common; record each one separately."},{"h":"Dressing apraxia is usually right-sided","x":"Trouble orienting a garment, with normal strength and understanding, suggests non-dominant parietal damage. OT can teach laying clothes out in order."},{"h":"Weakness and comprehension come first","x":"Apraxia means failing a learned action despite intact strength, sensation and understanding. If she can't move the arm or didn't follow the instruction, it isn't apraxia."},{"h":"Cortical sensation needs basic touch","x":"Test light touch first. Astereognosis or agraphaesthesia with normal touch points to the parietal cortex opposite the affected hand."}],"type":"Cognitive assessment","do":"Test fields, neglect, Gerstmann features, praxis, drawing and cortical sensation, then localise the findings for the team."},"c-s-34":{"obj":["Asked about focal seizure experiences, including auras, déjà vu and lost time","Tested registration, recent, remote and semantic memory and delayed recall of a name and address","Tested comprehension, repetition, graded naming, reading and writing","Tested visual fields including the upper quadrants and face recognition","Localised the findings, named postictal effects as a caveat and planned repeat testing"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I've never had a fit in my life. Am I going to have more?\"","r":"\"That's a frightening thing to wake up to. The neurology team will talk with you about that. My part is checking your memory and language, which helps them find the cause.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't read it. I was a teacher. This is stupid.\"","r":"\"It isn't stupid; it's the seizure and whatever caused it, not you. Thank you for keeping going. This tells us something important.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I can't remember the address. Please just move on.\"","r":"\"Of course. Can I give you a couple of hints first? It helps us see whether the memory is stored but hard to reach.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this the start of dementia?\"","r":"\"That's a natural worry. It's too early to say. Seizures can affect memory for a while afterwards, so we'll repeat these tests in a day or two, and you'll have a scan.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Actually, I've had funny smells and déjà vu for months.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that's really important. Those can be small seizures. I'll make sure the neurology team knows today.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Can't I just drive home on Friday? I feel fine now.\"","r":"\"I understand you want to get back to normal. After a seizure the law says you must stop driving and tell the DVLA. The neurology team will advise how long.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["MRI brain and EEG requested by the medical team; first seizure clinic within two weeks","Check sodium, calcium, glucose and a drug history; antiseizure treatment decided by neurology"],"psych":["Repeat temporal lobe testing in 48 hours, once postictal effects have settled"],"social":["With consent, explain to her son tonight what the tests showed and what is temporary"],"safety":["Not to drive; tell the DVLA; documented in the notes","Seizure safety advice before discharge: no baths alone, care with heights and cooking"]},"facts":[{"h":"Give the address early","x":"Register a seven-part name and address at the start and ask for it at the end. Free recall first, then cues: recognition with cues suggests retrieval rather than storage failure."},{"h":"Grade the naming","x":"Name the whole object, then its parts (watch, strap, buckle). Mild dominant temporal damage spares common words and shows up on low-frequency ones."},{"h":"Pie in the sky","x":"Temporal lesions damage Meyer's loop, causing an upper quadrant field defect on the opposite side. Parietal lesions affect the lower quadrant."},{"h":"Ask about auras","x":"Months of déjà vu, strange smells or a rising stomach feeling before a 'first' fit usually mean earlier focal seizures. It changes the diagnosis and the driving advice."},{"h":"Postictal is temporary","x":"Confusion and language problems can persist for hours after a seizure. Test now as a baseline, but repeat before you call any deficit fixed."}],"type":"Cognitive assessment","do":"Test memory, language, naming, reading, writing and upper visual fields after her seizure, then localise and plan repeat testing."},"c-t-13":{"obj":["Explained the tasks, gained consent and asked about smell and changes others have noticed","Tested timed letter and category fluency, proverbs and similarities","Tested a cognitive estimate, Luria's sequence and an alternating pattern, demonstrating first","Tested conflicting instructions, go/no-go and the grasp, palmomental and pout reflexes","Summarised the findings as a dysexecutive pattern and named the next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"This is stupid. Why are you asking me about chickens?\"","r":"\"Fair question; it does sound odd. These tasks look at how the front of the brain handles ideas, which can change after an injury like yours. Give it your best guess.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm totally fine. My mum just worries too much.\"","r":"\"It's good that you feel well in yourself. After head injuries, changes are often easier for others to spot. These tasks help us see it from your side too.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Did I fail that one?\"","r":"\"There's no pass or fail here, Callum; some are meant to be hard. What matters is the overall pattern, and I'll explain it at the end.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Ha, doc, you look like my old PE teacher. Bet you were rubbish at sport.\"","r":"\"You might be right! Let's keep going, as we've got a few more to do. Next, watch my hand carefully.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is my brain damaged for good?\"","r":"\"That's a really important question. The crash did injure the front of your brain, and some changes may last, but many people keep improving for years, and support makes a big difference.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["FAB (out of 18) and ACE-III at the next visit; review MRI with the neuroradiology report","Smell testing via ENT or neurology if anosmia affects safety (gas, smoke, spoiled food)","Screen mood (PHQ-9) and alcohol (AUDIT); both worsen disinhibition after brain injury"],"psych":["Formal neuropsychology assessment to map executive function and guide rehabilitation","Community brain injury team: goal-setting, routines and strategies for impulsivity"],"social":["Collateral from his parents with his consent; Headway family support group","Budgeting support and bank spending limits agreed with him; capacity for finances assessed only if a specific decision arises","Vocational rehabilitation referral when ready for work"],"safety":["Advise not to drive and to tell the DVLA about the head injury; record the advice","Smoke and gas alarms at home because of anosmia"]},"facts":[{"h":"The MMSE misses frontal problems","x":"A normal MMSE does not exclude a dysexecutive syndrome. Use the FAB (six subtests, scored out of 18) and the ACE-III fluency items."},{"h":"Lost smell points to orbitofrontal injury","x":"Anosmia after head injury suggests damage to the orbitofrontal cortex or sheared olfactory nerves. Advise smoke and gas alarms and food safety."},{"h":"Under ten F words is low","x":"Most young adults give well over ten F words in a minute. Record every word: repeats and proper nouns show poor monitoring."},{"h":"Demonstrate Luria silently","x":"Show fist, edge, palm five times without speaking, then ask him to copy. Verbal cues hide the problem."},{"h":"Grasp reflex is a frontal release sign","x":"Stroke the palm lightly; an involuntary grip suggests frontal damage. The palmomental reflex can be present in healthy adults, so weigh it less."}],"type":"Physical examination + Cognitive assessment","do":"Test fluency, abstraction, estimates, sequencing, inhibition and frontal release signs, then summarise the pattern."},"c-t-14":{"obj":["Gained consent, offered a chaperone, checked for pain, exposed with dignity and asked about red flags","Inspected and tested tone at hip, knee and ankle, comparing sides","Tested power by movement, including Hoover's and hip abductor signs","Tested reflexes, plantars, coordination and all sensory modalities, and observed gait safely","Summarised the positive signs kindly to her and clearly to neurology"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"It's worse today. What if it's a stroke they've missed?\"","r":"\"That's a really understandable worry. I'll check your legs carefully now, and if I find anything new, I'll tell the neurology team straight away.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Are you a psychiatrist? So they think I'm making it up.\"","r":"\"No one thinks you're making this up. The weakness is real. Neurology asked me to help because we work together on problems like this. Can I examine your legs?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't even lift it. I'm scared I'll never walk again.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying. Many people with this kind of weakness do get back to walking, often with specialist physiotherapy. Let's see exactly what the leg can do.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why did my leg push down when I lifted the other one?\"","r":"\"Good spot. It means the wiring to that leg works when it's automatic. The trouble is sending the signal on purpose. That's a positive finding, and it can be retrained.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"The divorce has been hell. Is that why?\"","r":"\"I'm sorry it's been so hard. Stress can be one factor, but it's not the whole story and it doesn't mean it's in your head. We can talk about that whenever you'd like.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Findings to the neurology team today; any new sign (sensory level, bladder change, reflex change) means urgent re-review","No new medication for the weakness; review sleep, pain and mood medicines with the ward team"],"psych":["Liaison follow-up this week: explanation, mood screen (PHQ-9), stressors including the divorce","Outpatient psychological therapy for FND (often CBT-based) if she wishes, after neurology's explanation"],"social":["Specialist FND physiotherapy on the ward, then outpatient; occupational therapy for home and work","Point her to neurosymptoms.org and FND Hope UK"],"safety":["Falls risk assessment; stand-by assistance for walking","Nurses to escalate new numbness, back pain, bladder or bowel change at once"]},"facts":[{"h":"Hoover's sign: test the right way","x":"Hand under the weak heel; ask her to lift the good leg against resistance. Strong downward push from the weak leg means involuntary hip extension is intact."},{"h":"Diagnose on positive signs","x":"Functional weakness is diagnosed by signs like Hoover's and the hip abductor sign, not by normal scans or by finding stress."},{"h":"Worse today: check for red flags","x":"New back pain, saddle numbness, bladder or bowel change, a sensory level or reflex change need urgent neurology review, whatever the working diagnosis."},{"h":"Words matter","x":"Say 'functional', and explain it as a problem with how the brain sends signals. Avoid 'nothing wrong', 'all in your head' or 'hysterical'."},{"h":"Normal tone and reflexes, profound weakness","x":"Normal tone, symmetrical reflexes and flexor plantars with severe weakness do not fit an upper or lower motor neurone lesion."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Examine both lower limbs fully, including Hoover's sign, explaining each step, and summarise for neurology."},"c-t-15":{"obj":["Established what Claire knew and feared before giving any results","Explained the results and the diagnosis of moderate Alzheimer's disease in plain words, pausing when she was overwhelmed","Explained donepezil: how it works, the benefit, side effects and pulse check, with memantine as a later option","Covered non-drug help and safety at home: cooking, medicines, falls, scams and driving","Planned ahead with LPA, social care and a carer's assessment, checked understanding and gave written information"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Stop, please. It's too much. I can't take it all in.\"","r":"\"Of course. Let's pause; take your time. That was a lot at once. When you're ready, I'll go back over it more simply, one bit at a time.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"My neighbour's on a memory tablet. Can Mum have that?\"","r":"\"Good question. There is a tablet, donepezil, which boosts a brain chemical used for memory. It doesn't cure it, but can help for a while. I'd like to start it after checking her pulse.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have noticed sooner. I've been so busy.\"","r":"\"It's very common to look back and feel that. Changes creep in slowly and people cover them well. You're here now, and that matters a great deal for her.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is she safe living on her own?\"","r":"\"That's the right thing to worry about. Many people stay at home safely for a good while with the right support. Let's go through cooking, medicines, falls and money, and see what would help.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it going to get worse? How long has she got?\"","r":"\"I'm sorry, it is a condition that gets worse over time, though the pace varies a lot. Many people live several years with it, and planning now gives her more say.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"She'll never give up driving. It's her independence.\"","r":"\"I understand; it's a huge loss for her. With her scan and memory results, driving isn't safe now. I'll talk to her myself, and she needs to tell the DVLA.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Donepezil 5 mg at night; increase to 10 mg after 4 weeks if tolerated. Check pulse first; ECG if under 60, faints or heart block","Warn: nausea, diarrhoea, poor appetite, vivid dreams (move to morning), dizziness; slow pulse plus osteoporosis raises fracture risk from falls","If not tolerated: memantine 5 mg daily, increased weekly by 5 mg to 20 mg; it can be added later as the illness progresses","Pharmacy blister pack; keep anticholinergic load low; GP to review medicines yearly"],"psych":["Cognitive stimulation therapy: 14 group sessions, twice weekly, run by the memory service","Dementia adviser: routines, calendar clock, labelled cupboards, reminiscence with photos and music","Carers' support group and information sessions for Claire"],"social":["OT home safety visit: cooker cut-off, falls sensor, pendant alarm (telecare)","Adult social care needs assessment (Care Act 2014); carer's assessment for Claire","Lasting powers of attorney for health and welfare and for property and financial affairs while she has capacity","Bank fraud protection and call blocking; advise stopping driving and telling the DVLA"],"safety":["Daily check-in call; agreed plan if she is found lost or confused","Fire and rescue home safety visit and smoke alarms","Follow-up with Margaret in 4 weeks to review donepezil and the plan"]},"facts":[{"h":"Donepezil goes at night","x":"Start 5 mg at bedtime, 10 mg after 4 weeks. Nausea and diarrhoea are common; vivid dreams mean switch to morning. Check pulse first; caution if below 60."},{"h":"Memantine is for moderate to severe","x":"Offer memantine if cholinesterase inhibitors are not tolerated, consider adding it in moderate disease, and offer it in severe disease (NICE NG97)."},{"h":"ACE-III below 82 needs explaining","x":"The ACE-III is scored out of 100; scores below about 82 suggest dementia. A score of 46 with daily-life effects fits moderate dementia."},{"h":"LPA only while capacity remains","x":"Lasting powers of attorney need the person to have capacity when made. Raise both types early; registration with the Office of the Public Guardian takes weeks."},{"h":"Dementia must be told to DVLA","x":"A person with dementia who drives must tell the DVLA. If they continue against advice, the doctor may need to inform the DVLA after telling them."},{"h":"Cognitive stimulation therapy has evidence","x":"NICE recommends group cognitive stimulation therapy for mild to moderate dementia: usually 14 sessions, twice a week."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Explain a treatment","do":"Explain Alzheimer's disease, its treatment and home safety plainly to her daughter, and check understanding."},"c-t-16":{"obj":["Settled Angela, acknowledged her injury and checked she was comfortable and safe to talk","Established this morning's assault: trigger, how long, black-out, voice or swallowing symptoms, and where Clive is now","Elicited a gradual two-year change with examples of disinhibition, lost empathy, apathy, overeating, compulsions and spending","Screened for memory, language, mood, psychosis, alcohol, head injury, stroke and a family history of early dementia","Assessed risk to Angela, the grandchildren and the public, plus driving and money, and agreed where she will sleep tonight"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't... I'm sorry. My neck. I just keep seeing his face.\"","r":"\"There's nothing to be sorry for. You've had a terrifying morning. Take a breath; I'm not going anywhere. When you're ready, tell me as much or as little as you like.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"It's my fault. I hid his keys. I knew it would set him off.\"","r":"\"You hid them because you were worried he'd crash. That was a sensible thing to do. Nothing you did makes it OK for him to hurt you.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"He's not a violent man. He'd never really hurt me.\"","r":"\"I hear how out of character this is, and that matters. But hands round the neck is one of the most dangerous things that can happen, so I need to take it seriously, for you.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will they lock him up? He wouldn't cope in a cell.\"","r":"\"That worry shows how much you still care for him. The police decide about charges, but I'll make sure the team in custody know he may be unwell, so he's assessed properly.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it dementia? But his memory's fine.\"","r":"\"It's a fair question. One type of dementia affects the front of the brain first. It changes manners, eating and empathy, while memory often stays good early on. It needs tests to be sure.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"His dad went strange too. They called it Pick's disease.\"","r":"\"Thank you, that's really important. Pick's disease is an older name for a type of frontotemporal dementia, and it can run in families. I'll make sure the specialists know.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'll be fine at home. I don't want a fuss.\"","r":"\"I understand wanting your own bed. But he could be released today, and I'd feel much happier if you weren't alone with him. Could you stay with Sophie while we sort the next steps?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["For Angela: ED senior review of the neck before discharge; CT angiogram if there is persisting pain, voice change, swallowing difficulty, neurological symptoms or petechiae","For Clive: urgent psychiatric assessment; bloods (FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, B12, folate, calcium, glucose, syphilis serology); MRI brain; referral to a cognitive disorders or neurology clinic","If FTD is confirmed: no cholinesterase inhibitors or memantine (NICE NG97); sertraline 50 mg once daily may reduce disinhibition and compulsive eating; avoid routine antipsychotics"],"psych":["Plain explanation to Angela now; fuller diagnostic discussion with both of them once tests are done","Trauma support for Angela: GP and NHS Talking Therapies self-referral; IDVA for emotional and practical support","Later: carers' education on calm responses, distraction and removing triggers such as car keys"],"social":["DASH checklist today; refer to MARAC and the IDVA service if high risk","Adult safeguarding referral to the local authority; tell the police and custody Liaison and Diversion team what you found","Clive must not drive; DVLA to be told once dementia is diagnosed; daughter to hold the car keys","Money: limit his cards and online accounts; raise Lasting Power of Attorney while he may still have capacity"],"safety":["Angela stays with her daughter tonight, not with Clive","Call 999 if he comes to the house or threatens her, or if she develops voice change, swallowing or breathing problems, or weakness","Grandchildren not left alone with Clive until he has been assessed"]},"facts":[{"h":"Frontal dementia spares memory early","x":"Behavioural-variant FTD presents with disinhibition, apathy, lost empathy, compulsions and sweet cravings. Memory and orientation are often fine at first, so a normal memory test does not rule it out."},{"h":"Strangulation is a high-risk marker","x":"Hands round the neck sharply raises the risk of later homicide. It is a DASH high-risk item and, since 2022, a specific offence in England and Wales."},{"h":"Neck injury can appear late","x":"After strangulation, swelling or arterial injury can emerge hours or days later. Warn about voice change, swallowing trouble, breathlessness and stroke symptoms."},{"h":"No dementia drugs for FTD","x":"NICE NG97: do not offer cholinesterase inhibitors or memantine for frontotemporal dementia. They do not help and may worsen agitation."},{"h":"Ask about family history","x":"Around a third of FTD is familial. Ask about early dementia, motor neurone disease, or a parent who 'changed' in middle age; 'Pick's disease' is an older name."}],"type":"Collateral history + Risk assessment","do":"Elicit Clive's frontal-type change from Angela, screen other causes, and assess her risk and the wider risks."},"c-t-17":{"obj":["Reassured Leah and agreed a quick pace with short questions","Established tonight's events before, during and after, and whether it had happened before","Elicited a gradual decline over months with examples, and screened for delirium, physical causes, medicines, falls and alcohol","Assessed function, mood and grief, and physical, social and psychiatric risks including gas, driving, money and exploitation","Contained Leah's distress and explained a clear plan: tests tonight, not home alone, and support to follow"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is he alright? Mum said the police found him.\"","r":"\"He's safe and warm on the ward, and the nurses are with him. Thank you for coming so quickly. I'd like to ask some quick questions so we can understand what happened.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"I don't know how long he was out. Nobody does.\"","r":"\"That's fine; you're not expected to know everything. Tell me what you do know, and we'll fill the gaps with the police and neighbours.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have done something about the gas last week.\"","r":"\"You noticed it and you were worried, which is exactly what matters. Tell me more about that day; it helps us plan.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Nan only died in September. Is this the beginning of the end for him?\"","r":"\"You've lost your nan and now this; that's a lot to carry. It isn't necessarily the beginning of the end. There may be a treatable cause, and there is a lot we can do.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it dementia?\"","r":"\"It's a fair question. I can't say tonight. Infections or other illnesses can make older people suddenly confused, so we test for those first. Memory checks come once he's well.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"He'll want to go home. He hates hospitals.\"","r":"\"I understand, and his wishes matter. But tonight it isn't safe for him to be alone. We'll keep him here, find out what's going on, and plan home with you.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Tonight: 4AT, observations, capillary glucose, FBC, U&Es, CRP, LFTs, calcium, TFTs, B12, folate, urine dip, bladder scan, bowel chart and ECG","Review amlodipine and atorvastatin and any over-the-counter sleep or anticholinergic medicines; nurse-supervised medication on the ward","Once delirium is excluded: cognitive testing (ACE-III or MoCA) and CT or MRI brain via the memory clinic"],"psych":["Ward delirium care: orientation, glasses and hearing aids, lighting, sleep routine, familiar faces","Bereavement support for Harold and family (Cruse); assess for depression if mood stays low","Leah: space to talk now; GP or NHS Talking Therapies if her grief overwhelms her"],"social":["Ward social worker: Care Act needs assessment before discharge; reablement or home care","Gas cut-off valve, telecare door sensor and pendant alarm","Herbert Protocol form completed by family with local police","No driving during assessment; family to look after the car keys; check bills and protect against doorstep callers"],"safety":["Not discharged home alone tonight","Falls risk assessment and bed near the nurses' desk; if he tries to leave, assess capacity and act under the MCA","Family given ward number; if he goes missing in future, call 999 and share the Herbert Protocol form"]},"facts":[{"h":"Sudden or gradual is the key question","x":"Hours to days with fluctuation suggests delirium. Months of slow decline suggests dementia. Both can coexist, and dementia is the biggest risk factor for delirium."},{"h":"4AT takes two minutes","x":"Alertness, AMT4 (age, birth date, place, year), months backwards, and acute change or fluctuation. A score of 4 or more suggests delirium."},{"h":"Grief can hide behind confusion","x":"Older people after a spouse's death may stop eating, sleeping and caring for themselves. Depression can mimic dementia; ask about mood, hopelessness and suicidal thoughts."},{"h":"The Herbert Protocol","x":"A police form held by the family with photo, routines, past addresses and favourite places. It saves vital time if a person with memory problems goes missing."},{"h":"Spouses hide decline","x":"A partner often quietly takes over meals, money and tablets. After a death, the gaps suddenly appear; ask what the late spouse used to do."}],"type":"Collateral history + Risk assessment","do":"Take Leah's account of tonight and recent months, screen for delirium, and assess Harold's physical, social and psychiatric risks."},"c-t-18":{"obj":["Built a respectful, curious rapport and explained the referral without judgement","Established what he collects, how many, since when, its link to his wife, and the impact on each room","Explored difficulty discarding, acquiring and insight, and screened for OCD, autism, psychosis, depression and dementia","Assessed fire, falls and other risks at home, including smoke alarms, the cooker and exits","Completed a mental state assessment and explained hoarding disorder with a safe next step he agreed to"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"My daughter put the GP up to this, no doubt.\"","r":"\"It sounds like she cares a lot. I'd like to hear your side, in your words. What's life at home like?\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"How many? Enough.\"","r":"\"Fair enough; I'm not counting to judge. It helps me picture your home. Are we talking hundreds, or more?\""},{"tag":"Pride","p":"\"They're probably worth a fortune. Complete runs from the sixties.\"","r":"\"That's a remarkable collection, and clearly important to you. Where do you keep them all now?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"So you want me to throw them all out, I suppose?\"","r":"\"No. Nobody will clear your house without you. The aim is to make it safe while respecting what the journals mean to you.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"They were hers. Reading them, I feel she's still here.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. That makes a lot of sense. Losing her was huge, and the journals keep her close.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"The house is perfectly fine. Just as she kept it.\"","r":"\"I hear it feels right to you. Can I ask about the practical side: can you cook, sit and sleep in the rooms you'd like to?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No medication first line; consider an SSRI only if depression or OCD emerges","ACE-III and bloods (FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs, B12, folate, calcium); brain imaging if frontal signs or decline","GP review of falls risk and eyesight"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing to build his own reasons for change","CBT for hoarding with the psychology team, often around 20 or more sessions, including sorting practice at home","Bereavement support (Cruse) if grief resurfaces"],"social":["Fire and Rescue Service Safe and Well visit, with his consent","Home visit by the care coordinator using the Clutter Image Rating to measure progress","Family meeting with his daughter, with his agreement, to agree a realistic pace","Local hoarding panel or Hoarding UK peer support; donation options for duplicates"],"safety":["Gas oven stays off until the kitchen is clear; working smoke alarm fitted","Clear paths to the front and back doors and a torch by the bed","Review in four weeks; safeguarding concern to the local authority only if risk escalates and he declines help"]},"facts":[{"h":"Hoarding is its own diagnosis","x":"ICD-11 places hoarding disorder in the obsessive-compulsive group but separate from OCD. Core: difficulty discarding and excessive acquiring, causing clutter that stops rooms being used."},{"h":"OCD hoarding feels unwanted","x":"In OCD, keeping things is driven by intrusive fears, such as harm if something is thrown away, and feels distressing. In hoarding disorder, collecting usually feels pleasurable and meaningful."},{"h":"Fire is the biggest danger","x":"Ask about smoking, cookers, heaters, smoke alarms and blocked exits. Fire services offer free home visits and will fit alarms."},{"h":"Late onset needs a brain check","x":"Hoarding starting late in life, or with disinhibition or memory change, can be an early sign of dementia, especially frontotemporal. Screen cognition."},{"h":"Forced clear-outs backfire","x":"Clearances without consent cause great distress, and homes usually refill. CBT for hoarding with sorting practice works better."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"Establish hoarding disorder, screen OCD, psychosis, depression, autism and dementia, assess fire risk, and present a mental state."},"c-t-29":{"obj":["Engaged her despite her irritation without arguing about the neighbours","Elicited the persecutory and partition delusions, their onset, evidence and conviction","Screened hallucinations in all five senses, with content, timing and how she responds","Screened thought interference and passivity, and checked hearing, sight, orientation and recall","Asked about risk to herself and to the neighbours and agreed a follow-up with her"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I shouldn't be here. It's them next door you should be seeing.\"","r":"\"You're clearly fed up, and I'm glad you came anyway. I'd like to understand what's been going on, in your words.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You think I'm going senile, don't you?\"","r":"\"No, I haven't drawn any conclusions. I ask everyone your age a few memory questions, so I don't miss anything. Is that alright?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I can't sleep in my own bed. The smell comes through the wall.\"","r":"\"That sounds exhausting and frightening, not even feeling safe in your own bedroom. What do you notice about the smell? When is it worst?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If you won't stop them, what are you suggesting I do?\"","r":"\"I can't promise to change the neighbours, but I can help with how unsettled and unsafe you're feeling at night. Let's start there.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not wearing those hearing aids. They whistle.\"","r":"\"Lots of people find that annoying. A proper refit usually stops the whistling. Good hearing would help me check everything carefully. Could we arrange that?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"My own children think I'm making it up.\"","r":"\"That must feel very lonely. I don't think you're making anything up; I can see this is real to you. Would you be happy for me to talk with them, with you there?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Organic screen: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, calcium, B12, folate, glucose, CRP, urine dip; MRI head (CT if MRI not possible)","If she agrees: amisulpride 50 mg once daily, or risperidone 0.5 mg at night, titrated slowly","Baseline ECG and postural BP; warn about stiffness, drowsiness and falls; review at 4-6 weeks"],"psych":["Engagement first: same clinician visiting regularly, not challenging the beliefs","CBT for psychosis adapted for older adults if she is willing","Practical coping at night: radio, routine, planned daytime activity"],"social":["Older adults CMHT care coordinator; home visits","Audiology refit of hearing aids; optician check","Family meeting with her consent; social prescribing to reduce isolation"],"safety":["If she feels in danger at home: call family, GP, or NHS 111 option 2; 999 in an emergency","Review in 2 weeks; reassess risk if conflict with neighbours escalates"]},"facts":[{"h":"Partition delusions are typical","x":"Late-onset psychosis often features beliefs that people, gas or noise pass through walls, floors or ceilings. Ask specifically how it gets in."},{"h":"Hearing loss is a risk factor","x":"Sensory impairment and social isolation are linked to very-late-onset psychosis. Get the hearing aids in and refer to audiology."},{"h":"Check every sense","x":"Late-onset psychosis is often multimodal: voices plus smells and tastes. Missing smell and taste misses half the picture."},{"h":"Exclude organic causes first","x":"New psychosis after 65 needs bloods, brain imaging and a cognitive screen: delirium, dementia, stroke and medicines can all present this way."},{"h":"Low and slow","x":"Older people need much lower antipsychotic doses. Amisulpride 50-100 mg daily has evidence in very-late-onset psychosis; watch for parkinsonism and falls."}],"type":"Mental state examination","do":"Elicit delusions and hallucinations in every sense, check thought interference, senses, cognition and risk, without arguing."},"c-t-31":{"obj":["Explained what galantamine does and does not do, with an honest picture of likely benefit and a clear review point","Explained how to take it: XL 8 mg with breakfast, rising to 16 mg after four weeks, and what to do after missed doses","Described the common and serious side effects, including the slow-pulse risk with bisoprolol, with a clear action for each","Answered his concerns about the liver, addiction, cost and herbal supplements accurately","Agreed monitoring and follow-up and gave written information, carer support and a contact number"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I've read these drugs don't really do much. Why bother?\"","r":"\"That's a fair question, and you're right that the effect is modest. Some people stay steadier for a while, and some don't benefit. We'll know within three months, and we'll stop it if it isn't helping.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"His heart's already slow on that bisoprolol. Could this make it worse?\"","r":"\"You've spotted something important. It can slow the pulse a little more, so we'll do a heart tracing before he starts and his GP will check his pulse. Fainting or a pulse under 50 means we stop and review.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it addictive? I don't want him hooked on something.\"","r":"\"Good to ask. It isn't addictive, and he won't crave it. If it's stopped, any benefit fades over a few weeks, which is why we plan any stopping together.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Someone at work said daffodil extract does the same thing. Could he take that instead?\"","r":"\"There's a link: galantamine was first found in snowdrop and daffodil bulbs. But garden extracts aren't safe or tested, and some bulbs are poisonous. The tablet gives a known, measured dose.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have noticed sooner. He got lost driving twice before I did anything.\"","r":"\"Lots of families only piece it together afterwards; the changes creep in slowly. You've acted now, and you're here asking the right questions. That matters more.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"So he's just going to get worse whatever we do?\"","r":"\"It's a lot to take in, and I'm sorry. The illness does progress, usually over years. But there's a lot we can do to keep him well, safe and at home for as long as possible.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Galantamine XL 8 mg once daily with breakfast for 4 weeks, then 16 mg; up to 24 mg after a further 4 weeks if helpful and tolerated","ECG before the first dose (on bisoprolol, pulse 58); GP to check pulse at each dose step; hold and call clinic if pulse below 50, fainting or falls","Weight at baseline and each review; warn about nausea, poor appetite, diarrhoea and dizziness; stop and seek help for any blistering or spreading rash","If more than a few days' doses are missed, restart at 8 mg; avoid new anticholinergic medicines where possible","Memory clinic review at 3 months (cognition, daily function, side effects), then 6-monthly; GP continues under shared care"],"psych":["Group cognitive stimulation therapy: 14 sessions, twice weekly, through the memory service","Carer education for Neil: what to expect and how to handle repeated questions without arguing"],"social":["Carers' assessment for Neil from the local authority; Admiral Nurse or memory service dementia adviser as named contact","Harold to make health and finance Lasting Powers of Attorney while he has capacity","Harold to tell the DVLA; refer for driving assessment or agree to stop; Attendance Allowance and council tax discount"],"safety":["Same-day GP or NHS 111 if fainting, falls, black stools, severe vomiting or new breathlessness","Pill organiser or blister pack checked by Neil weekly"]},"facts":[{"h":"Not a cure, but often worth trying","x":"Cholinesterase inhibitors boost acetylcholine and treat symptoms. A share of people improve or stay steadier for some months; the illness itself still progresses."},{"h":"XL once daily with food","x":"Galantamine XL: 8 mg each morning with food for 4 weeks, then 16 mg. Food and a slow start cut nausea. Missed for several days: restart at 8 mg."},{"h":"Watch the pulse with beta-blockers","x":"Cholinesterase inhibitors slow the heart. With bisoprolol or a resting pulse in the 50s, get an ECG first and check the pulse at each step."},{"h":"Rare serious skin reactions","x":"Galantamine can rarely cause serious skin reactions. Tell families: any blistering, peeling or spreading rash means stop and seek urgent medical advice."},{"h":"Dementia and driving","x":"After a dementia diagnosis the licence holder must tell the DVLA. Advise it, record it, and suggest a driving assessment if he wishes to keep driving."}],"type":"Explain a treatment","do":"Explain galantamine's benefits, dosing, side effects and monitoring to the son, and answer each of his concerns honestly."},"c-t-34":{"obj":["Calmed her by listening fully, acknowledging her anger and apologising for the lack of contact","Explained why clozapine was chosen, why progress is slow, and the white cell risk with how monitoring protects him","Explained his earlier abnormal liver tests and that all his results are now normal","Discussed discharge home honestly, including her husband's drinking, the distance and the brother's financial abuse","Agreed next steps: family meeting, weekly contact, carer support and how to complain"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Nobody tells me anything. I have to find out from the internet.\"","r":"\"You're right, and I'm sorry. You should have been kept informed. Let's go through everything now, and I'll make sure his nurse rings you every week.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"You're experimenting on him with a drug that destroys bone marrow.\"","r":"\"I can hear how frightening that is to read. Clozapine can lower white blood cells, which is why he has weekly blood tests. Every one of his has been normal.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Three months and he still can't wash himself. What are you doing?\"","r":"\"I understand why that's upsetting to see. This medicine works slowly in illness like his. Nurses and the occupational therapist are working on washing and dressing with him each day.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What happened with his liver tests? Is that the clozapine?\"","r":"\"Good question. They were raised when he arrived, before clozapine, likely from his life at the flat. They've been normal on every test since, and we keep checking.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm taking him home. I'll look after him better than you.\"","r":"\"You clearly care deeply about him and want him well. I want him home too. Let's plan it so it's safe: leave visits first, with his bloods arranged near you.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I want to make a complaint.\"","r":"\"That's absolutely your right, and it won't affect his care. Here's the PALS number and the complaints leaflet. I'll still make sure the things we agreed today happen.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue clozapine (currently 300 mg/day in divided doses); FBC weekly to week 18, fortnightly to week 52, then 4-weekly, via the monitoring service","Clozapine plasma level now, aiming for at least 350 micrograms/L; recheck if smoking changes, e.g. on leave","Daily bowel check and regular laxative: clozapine slows the gut and constipation can be fatal (MHRA alert)","Report fever, sore throat or mouth ulcers the same day for urgent FBC; LFTs, weight, glucose, lipids and ECG as scheduled"],"psych":["Family intervention with the psychology team: about 10 sessions over 3 to 12 months","Psychoeducation for Mrs Hussain on clozapine, warning signs of relapse and why stopping suddenly is dangerous","OT daily living skills programme: washing, laundry and simple cooking, with weekly goals"],"social":["Safeguarding concern to the local authority today about the brother taking Jamal's money; DWP appointee or new bank account","Care coordinator and ward social worker: discharge options including supported accommodation","Graded leave plan: day visits first, then overnight, with clozapine supplies and bloods arranged","Carer's assessment for Mrs Hussain; named nurse to call her weekly; PALS and complaints details given"],"safety":["If clozapine is missed for more than 48 hours, it must be restarted from a low dose by the team","Family meeting with the consultant on Monday to agree the discharge plan"]},"facts":[{"h":"Blood monitoring is mandatory","x":"Clozapine FBC: weekly for 18 weeks, fortnightly to one year, then every 4 weeks. No valid blood result, no supply."},{"h":"The real risk, honestly","x":"Severe loss of white cells (agranulocytosis) affects under 1 in 100 people, mostly in the first 18 weeks. Monitoring catches it early so clozapine can be stopped."},{"h":"Clozapine is the step after two","x":"NICE: offer clozapine when schizophrenia has not responded to two adequate antipsychotic trials, one non-clozapine second-generation. Benefit often builds over months."},{"h":"Missed 48 hours means retitrate","x":"After more than 48 hours without clozapine, restart at a low dose and build up again. Tell families: never restart the old dose at home."},{"h":"Constipation can kill","x":"Clozapine slows the gut. Ask about bowels at every review, prescribe a laxative early, and treat new abdominal pain or vomiting as urgent."}],"type":"Handling a complaint + Explain a treatment","do":"Calm the mother, explain clozapine monitoring and his results honestly, discuss safe discharge, and give the complaints route."},"c-t-38":{"obj":["Elicited core and biological depressive symptoms with duration and severity, adapting questions to his slowness","Explored physical causes of weight loss, medicines and alcohol","Separated depression from dementia with a brief cognitive check, and money worries from a delusion of poverty","Assessed suicide risk, self-neglect, means and driving","Named the diagnosis plainly to Reg and Pat and agreed immediate next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm no use to anyone now. Forty years driving and then nothing.\"","r":"\"That's a huge loss, Reg, the job, the routine, the people. It makes sense you feel lost. Feeling useless every day like this is also a sign of depression, which we can treat.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I've lost all this weight. It's cancer, isn't it?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd worry. Depression often causes weight loss, but we must check properly, so I'll ask your GP to do the right tests. Have you had any pain, bowel changes or a cough?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"We'll run out of money. The pension's half what I earned.\"","r":"\"That's a real change, and money worries are hard. Can we look at it together? What comes in each month, and what goes out?\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Sometimes I think Pat would be better off without me.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me, that takes courage. Do you ever think about ending your life? Have you thought how?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is it my memory going? I can't think straight.\"","r":"\"Depression often slows thinking and concentration, and that usually improves as the mood lifts. Let me do a few quick checks now, and we'll recheck once you feel better.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"(Pat) \"Is there anything that can get him back to himself?\"","r":"\"Yes. This looks like depression, which is very treatable at any age. We'll check his physical health, start treatment, and see him every week. You'll know who to call.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["GP to complete physical work-up: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, calcium, glucose, HbA1c, B12, folate, CRP, and chest X-ray given weight loss and smoking history","Mirtazapine 15 mg at night, rising to 30 mg after 2 weeks if needed; helps sleep and appetite; warn about drowsiness, especially with alcohol","Sodium at baseline and at 2-4 weeks: antidepressants can cause hyponatraemia in older people","If psychotic features, refusal to eat or drink, or rising suicide risk: urgent consultant review, possible admission, consider ECT"],"psych":["Behavioural activation or CBT, around 16 weekly sessions, via older adults' psychological therapies","Daily small activities planned with Pat, starting with short trips to the allotment","Work on loss of role after retirement once mood starts to lift"],"social":["CMHT care coordinator, weekly contact while treatment starts; consultant review next week","Age UK befriending, a men's shed or volunteering to rebuild routine","Carer's assessment for Pat via the local authority","Alcohol: cut to no more than 14 units a week, with alcohol-free days"],"safety":["Pat holds and gives medicines weekly; no stockpiles; no firearms in the house","Not to drive for now; crisis plan: CMHT duty line in hours, crisis team or NHS 111 option 2 out of hours, 999 in emergency"]},"facts":[{"h":"Depression in later life hides","x":"Older adults may deny sadness but report weight loss, aches, poor sleep or poor memory. Ask about enjoyment and mornings, not just mood."},{"h":"Test the money belief","x":"Ask what comes in and goes out. Worry shifts with facts; a delusion of poverty stays fixed against clear evidence and changes treatment."},{"h":"Depression or dementia?","x":"Depression: rapid onset, distress about memory, 'don't know' answers, effortful success. Dementia: gradual, unaware of deficits, confabulation. Recheck cognition when mood improves."},{"h":"Mirtazapine suits poor sleep and appetite","x":"15 mg at night, up to 45 mg. Sedation and weight gain are useful here. Check sodium at 2-4 weeks in older people."},{"h":"Older men carry high suicide risk","x":"Passive death wishes, loss of role, alcohol, physical illness and living with access to means all raise risk. Ask about firearms and stockpiled medicines."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Mental state examination","do":"Establish depression severity, exclude physical causes and psychosis, check cognition and risk, and explain the diagnosis to both."},"c-t-40":{"obj":["Set up safely: consent, handedness, glasses, hearing, pain and a chaperone","Demonstrated left neglect and inattention, separating extinction from a field defect","Tested constructional and dressing apraxia, awareness of the weakness, and cortical sensation after confirming basic touch","Tested dominant parietal signs, visual fields by quadrant and praxis","Explained the findings plainly and agreed what goes to the team"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"What's the point of all this? It's my arm that's the problem.\"","r":"\"Fair question. Your family noticed you miss things on your left, and these tests show the therapists exactly where to help. Each one takes under a minute.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"There's nothing wrong with my left arm. It's just a bit lazy.\"","r":"\"I hear you. Strokes can make it hard to sense how much an arm has changed; that's part of the stroke, not you being stubborn. Can you lift it for me?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't even draw a clock. I feel like an idiot.\"","r":"\"You're not, Harry. This test is hard after a stroke on this side, and it's showing me something useful. Let's move on to something different.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not closing my eyes. I don't like it.\"","r":"\"That's fine, we'll do it your way. I can cover your hand with a towel instead, so you can't see what I'm putting in it. Is that OK?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Am I going doolally? Is this dementia?\"","r":"\"No. This is the stroke affecting one part of the brain that handles space and the left side. It often improves with therapy over the coming months.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I be able to drive again?\"","r":"\"Not for now. With the stroke and the gap in your lower left vision, the law says you mustn't drive and the DVLA must be told. We'll recheck as you recover.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Stroke team to continue secondary prevention as prescribed; formal visual field testing by orthoptics","Screen mood at the next visit (post-stroke depression is common) and recheck cognition in 6-8 weeks"],"psych":["Explain findings to Harry and family in plain words; give the Stroke Association leaflet on neglect"],"social":["OT: visual scanning training, dressing practice starting with the weak arm first, kitchen and home assessment before discharge","Family and staff: approach from the right, then draw attention gradually to the left; tray turned halfway through meals"],"safety":["Falls risk flagged: bumps into things on the left; bed rails and call bell positioning reviewed by nursing","No driving; DVLA to be informed if field defect or neglect persists beyond a month"]},"facts":[{"h":"Single first, then double","x":"Test each side alone, then both together. Missing the left only when both move is extinction (inattention); missing it alone is a field defect or sensory loss."},{"h":"Neglect is not a field cut","x":"A man with a left hemianopia turns his head to compensate. A man with neglect does not notice the left exists, even with full fields."},{"h":"Check basic touch first","x":"Astereognosis and agraphaesthesia only mean cortical loss if light touch and joint position are good enough in that hand."},{"h":"Which side, which signs","x":"Non-dominant parietal: neglect, dressing apraxia, anosognosia. Dominant parietal: finger agnosia, left-right confusion, acalculia, agraphia (Gerstmann)."},{"h":"Parietal field loss is inferior","x":"Parietal damage affects the upper optic radiation, producing a contralateral inferior quadrantanopia: 'pie on the floor'."}],"type":"Physical examination + Cognitive assessment","do":"Complete a structured parietal assessment, record each test result, and report a right parietal pattern to the team."},"c-t-43":{"obj":["Explained the findings and named neuroleptic malignant syndrome as a rare, unpredictable reaction, not an allergy, with its risk factors","Set out the immediate plan: stop antipsychotics, urgent medical transfer, fluids, cooling, monitoring and other causes excluded","Gave an honest account of complications and outlook, including that it can be life-threatening","Responded to his anger with an apology and complaint routes, and explained how psychosis will be treated safely later"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"I brought him here to get better, not to be poisoned by your injections.\"","r":"\"I understand why you're angry, and I'm truly sorry. Kofi's medicine has caused a serious reaction. We've stopped it, and the medical team is taking over his care right now.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is he allergic to it, then?\"","r":"\"Good question. It's not an allergy. It's a rare reaction where the medicine upsets temperature and muscle control. Higher doses and injections make it more likely, so we'll record it clearly in his notes.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Could he die from this?\"","r":"\"I won't pretend it isn't serious; it can be life-threatening, which is why we're moving fast. With prompt treatment most people recover completely, usually within one to two weeks.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Somebody made a mistake. Who's responsible?\"","r":"\"You have every right to ask. This is being reported and reviewed, and his consultant will meet you to go through it. I'll also give you the PALS details if you want to raise it formally.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So what about his mind? Does he just stay ill now?\"","r":"\"That's a really important worry. He'll still get treatment. Once he's fully recovered, at least two weeks, we'd try a different medicine at a low dose and increase it slowly with close checks.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I persuaded him to come in. This is my fault.\"","r":"\"I can hear how much you're blaming yourself. Bringing him in was right; he was very unwell. This reaction is unpredictable, and you couldn't have known.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Stop haloperidol and all antipsychotics now; no antipsychotic in any rapid tranquillisation plan","Urgent transfer to the acute medical team; IV fluids, active cooling, paracetamol; HDU or ICU if worsening","Observations every 15 minutes until transfer; CK, U&Es, LFTs, clotting and blood cultures; CK and U&Es at least daily","Lorazepam 1-2 mg for agitation and rigidity; dantrolene or bromocriptine if severe, as physicians decide; VTE prophylaxis","After full recovery (at least 2 weeks): a different antipsychotic, e.g. aripiprazole 5 mg, slow increase with temperature, pulse and CK checks"],"psych":["Ward nurse escort and liaison psychiatry review on the medical ward","Psychoeducation for Kofi and family about psychosis and this reaction once he is well","Family intervention through the early intervention team later"],"social":["Consultant meeting with Mr Asante; duty of candour letter","Incident report today; PALS details given","Carers' assessment and information pack for Mr Asante"],"safety":["Adverse reaction recorded on the drug chart and in the notes; Yellow Card report to the MHRA","Family to tell staff at once about new fever, confusion or stiffness"]},"facts":[{"h":"NMS: four features to look for","x":"Fever, lead-pipe rigidity, altered consciousness and autonomic instability (fast pulse, labile BP, sweating), with raised CK and white cells. Onset usually over days."},{"h":"Risk factors you can change","x":"High-potency drugs, rapid dose increases, intramuscular doses, dehydration and agitation. Avoid stacking oral and intramuscular haloperidol in a newly started patient."},{"h":"Not serotonin syndrome","x":"Serotonin syndrome starts within hours, with clonus, hyperreflexia, diarrhoea and dilated pupils. NMS develops over days with rigidity and slow reflexes."},{"h":"Rechallenge is usually possible","x":"Wait at least two weeks after full recovery. Use a different, lower-potency antipsychotic at a low dose, increase slowly, and check temperature, pulse and CK."},{"h":"Apology is not admission of liability","x":"Under the duty of candour, say sorry for harm promptly, explain what happened and what will be done, and follow up in writing."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Explain NMS honestly to his father, set out the medical plan and future treatment, and respond to his anger."},"c-t-51":{"obj":["Heard what Ellis has seen at home, what he understands and what he hopes for, including his wish for a tablet to stop the visions","Checked for a new cause of the recent decline: infection, constipation, pain or a new medicine","Explained dementia with Lewy bodies, the DaT scan, why it is not Parkinson's disease dementia, and that it will progress","Explained the medicine options honestly: rivastigmine increase with pulse checks, the limits of levodopa and the danger of antipsychotics","Agreed home, falls and carer support, future planning, a review date and who to call"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"He talks to my mum's mum and his own mum like they're sitting there. I never know what to say to him.\"","r":"\"That must be painful to watch. Those visions are part of the illness, and they feel real to him. If they comfort him, you don't need to correct him; sit with him or gently change the subject.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there a tablet that'll make the visions stop?\"","r":"\"I wish there were one that simply switches them off. Raising his rivastigmine often makes them less frequent. Light, routine and calm reassurance help too. We avoid the usual tablets for hallucinations, and I'll explain why.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I read online that antipsychotics could kill him. Is that true?\"","r":"\"You've read right, and I'm glad you asked. People with this illness can react badly, even to tiny doses. We'd only consider one for severe distress or danger, with a specialist, at the lowest dose.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Does this mean he'll have to go into a home?\"","r":"\"Not now. Lots of people stay at home a long time with the right help. Let's make the house safer, get you and your mum support, and talk honestly as things change.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Mum shouted at him when he called her an intruder. She feels awful.\"","r":"\"That's completely understandable at three in the morning. Please tell her that. He likely didn't recognise her in the dark. Better lighting and a calm voice may stop it happening again.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why isn't it just Parkinson's? He's stiff and shaky.\"","r":"\"Good question. They're cousins. In Parkinson's disease, movement problems come first, and memory years later. In your grandad, memory went first and stiffness came within the year. That order points to Lewy body dementia.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"If he takes all his tablets properly, will he get back to how he was?\"","r":"\"I'm sorry, no. The tablets can help for a while, but the illness slowly gets worse. That's why planning now, while he can still have his say, matters so much.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Rivastigmine 3 mg BD to 4.5 mg BD today after pulse (above 60) and ECG; review at 2 weeks, then 6 mg BD if tolerated; warn about nausea, diarrhoea, weight loss, dizziness","If nausea or forgotten capsules: switch to rivastigmine patch 4.6 mg/24 h, then 9.5 mg/24 h after 4 weeks; change daily, rotate sites","No haloperidol or other antipsychotic; if severe distress or risk persists, specialist-led quetiapine 12.5 mg at night with close review","GP to check urine, bloods and bowels if sudden worsening; stop or avoid anticholinergics; lying and standing BP for postural drop","Memantine 5 mg daily, rising weekly to 20 mg, if rivastigmine is not tolerated"],"psych":["Psychoeducation for the family by the memory nurse: do not argue with visions, reassure, distract, keep a calm routine","Cognitive stimulation therapy, 14 sessions twice weekly, on his better days"],"social":["Occupational therapist home visit: lighting, night lights, rails, remove trip hazards, pendant alarm","Community physiotherapy falls programme; pharmacy blister pack or carer to give tablets","Carers' assessment from adult social care for Ellis's mother; respite and sitting service; Lewy Body Society and Admiral nurse"],"safety":["Sudden confusion, drowsiness or high temperature: GP same day or NHS 111; fall with head injury or cannot get up: 999","Confirm he no longer drives and that DVLA is told; LPA information given; review in memory clinic in 4 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"Antipsychotic sensitivity can kill","x":"Many people with Lewy body dementia react severely to antipsychotics: sudden rigidity, drowsiness, falls, confusion, sometimes death. Avoid haloperidol completely."},{"h":"The one-year rule","x":"If memory trouble starts first, or within twelve months of parkinsonism, call it Lewy body dementia. Parkinsonism for a year or more before dementia means Parkinson's disease dementia."},{"h":"Check the pulse before every increase","x":"Cholinesterase inhibitors slow the heart. Check pulse before each step; get an ECG if it is under 60, irregular, or he faints."},{"h":"Restart low after a gap","x":"If rivastigmine is missed for more than three days, restart at 1.5 mg twice daily and build up again to avoid severe vomiting."},{"h":"The DaT scan shows dopamine loss","x":"Reduced uptake in the basal ganglia supports Lewy body dementia; it is usually normal in Alzheimer's disease."},{"h":"Sudden decline means look for delirium","x":"A worse week is often infection, constipation, pain or a new tablet, not the dementia. Check before changing psychiatric treatment."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Explain Lewy body dementia and its treatment to the grandson, address his concerns, and agree a safe plan."},"c-t-60":{"obj":["Explored the nurse's concerns and expectations, including the consultant's view on lithium","Gathered the presentation, treatment so far, adherence, PRN use and physical health","Assessed risk to Connor and to other patients","Explained clearly why lithium today is unsafe and why olanzapine has not yet had a fair trial","Agreed a plan for today: olanzapine increase, PRN, observations, environment, physical monitoring and handover"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Dr Hollis said lithium if the olanzapine didn't work. Why can't we start it today?\"","r":"\"That's a fair question, and lithium may well be right later. But without blood tests we can't start it safely or check its levels. Let's give olanzapine a proper dose today.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So we just wait while he winds everyone up? My staff are exhausted.\"","r":"\"No, you shouldn't just wait, and I can see how stretched you are. Let's change things today: higher dose tonight, PRN, closer observation and a quieter space for him.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't we just give the lithium and do bloods when he lets us?\"","r":"\"I understand the logic. The trouble is lithium can be toxic, especially if he's not drinking much, and we'd have no way of knowing. It needs kidney and thyroid checks first.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Someone's going to hit him. Yesterday it was close.\"","r":"\"That sounds frightening for everyone. Let's put him on within-eyesight observations and nurse him away from the patients he's been provoking.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if he refuses the higher dose?\"","r":"\"Good thinking. Offer it with a calm explanation. If he refuses and is very agitated, follow the rapid tranquillisation policy, oral first. Call me if you need to.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can we at least prescribe it so it's ready for tomorrow?\"","r":"\"I'd rather not have it on the chart until the baseline tests are back; it risks being given by mistake. I'll hand over clearly to Dr Hollis tomorrow.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Olanzapine 15 mg at night from tonight; 20 mg within the week if tolerated and needed (BNF maximum 20 mg); judge response after 1 to 2 weeks at an adequate dose","Lorazepam 1 to 2 mg oral PRN for agitation or sleep, maximum 4 mg in 24 hours; IM options only under the rapid tranquillisation policy with physical monitoring after","Before lithium: U&E, eGFR, TFTs, calcium, FBC, weight or BMI, ECG; then a level 12 hours after the dose, 5 to 7 days after starting and each change, aiming 0.6 to 0.8 mmol/L","Physical health: food and fluid chart; visual observations (breathing rate, colour, sweating) if hands-on refused; retry NEWS2 and bloods daily"],"psych":["Calm, consistent, low-stimulus approach with short, clear explanations","Named nurse sessions to build trust around blood tests and medication","Structured quiet activities and planned rest periods to channel energy"],"social":["Within-eyesight observations to manage intrusive behaviour; review each shift","Nurse him away from vulnerable or provoked patients; use a separate lounge area","Safeguarding record of yesterday's incident; inform family with his consent","Bleep holder to review staffing if observations increase"],"safety":["Call the duty doctor urgently if violence, signs of dehydration, refusing all fluids, or physical deterioration; consultant review Monday"]},"facts":[{"h":"Olanzapine for mania starts at 15 mg","x":"BNF: mania as monotherapy starts at 15 mg daily (10 mg in combination), range 5 to 20 mg. Ten milligrams for three days is not a fair trial."},{"h":"Lithium needs bloods before the first dose","x":"Baseline U&E, eGFR, TFTs, calcium, weight and ECG if cardiac risk. Then levels 12 hours post-dose, weekly until stable. Without bloods, toxicity can be missed."},{"h":"NICE order in acute mania","x":"Start an antipsychotic (haloperidol, olanzapine, quetiapine or risperidone). If it fails at an adequate dose, switch antipsychotic; then consider adding lithium or valproate (avoid valproate in women who could become pregnant)."},{"h":"Dehydration makes lithium dangerous","x":"A manic patient who is overactive and not drinking concentrates lithium. Toxicity: coarse tremor, vomiting, diarrhoea, ataxia, confusion."},{"h":"Risk runs both ways","x":"On PICU, an intrusive manic patient is at real risk of assault. Observation, environment and safeguarding protect him as well as others."}],"type":"Management plan + Medication review","do":"Gather the picture, explain why lithium is unsafe today, and agree a concrete plan for medication, observation and safety."},"c-t-61":{"obj":["Elicited core, biological and cognitive symptoms of depression with duration and severity","Explored the caring role, including nights, help available and his feelings of guilt and frustration","Covered pain, falls, senses, memory, alcohol and medicines, and excluded psychotic features","Assessed suicide risk, including means at home and protective factors","Asked directly about joint-death thoughts and harm or neglect towards Margaret, and agreed immediate safety steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm just tired. Anyone would be, looking after her.\"","r":"\"You're right, anyone would be worn out. I'd also like to check whether it has turned into something more, like depression, because that's treatable and it would make caring easier.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I promised her I'd never put her in a home. I'm failing her.\"","r":"\"That promise shows how much you love her. You're not failing; you're doing a job meant for a team, alone. Getting help is a way of keeping that promise.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Some nights I wonder if we'd both be better off gone.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me; that takes courage. Can I ask more? Have you thought about how that might happen, or done anything towards it?\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I tell you I'm struggling, you'll take her away.\"","r":"\"That's not what this is about. My aim is to keep you both at home, safely, with more help around you. Being honest with me makes that more likely, not less.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Nobody's lifted a finger for us in a year.\"","r":"\"You're right to be angry; you should have had support long before now. I'll start that today, with a carer's assessment and help for Margaret.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't you just give me something to make me sleep?\"","r":"\"I understand; you're exhausted. Sleeping tablets would raise your risk of falls at night with Margaret. Treating the low mood is what usually brings sleep back, and I'll explain how.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Consultant to consider sertraline 25 mg daily for 1 week, then 50 mg; review at 2 and 4 weeks","Check sodium at baseline and 2-4 weeks after starting (hyponatraemia, falls)","Stop OTC ibuprofen with an SSRI or add a PPI; GP to review arthritis pain relief","Avoid hypnotics and tricyclics; reduce whisky with brief alcohol advice"],"psych":["Behavioural activation or CBT via older adults psychology, about 12-16 weekly sessions, once engaged","Admiral Nurse or local carers' centre for carer coaching and emotional support"],"social":["Carers' assessment under the Care Act 2014 by adult social care, requested today","Needs assessment for Margaret: night sitting, day centre, respite","Age UK benefits check: Attendance Allowance for Margaret, carer's element"],"safety":["Saved co-codamol handed over today; future prescriptions in weekly packs","Daughter informed with consent; CMHT review within 48 hours","Crisis plan: Samaritans 116 123, NHS 111 option 2, 999 in an emergency"]},"facts":[{"h":"Ask about dying together","x":"Homicide-suicide in older couples is rare but usually involves a depressed husband caring for an ill wife. Ask about 'both going' directly; nobody volunteers it."},{"h":"A carer's assessment is a right","x":"Under the Care Act 2014 the council must assess any carer who appears to need support, whatever the cared-for person's finances. Request it the same day."},{"h":"SSRIs drop sodium in older adults","x":"Hyponatraemia is commoner over 65. Check sodium before and 2-4 weeks after starting. Confusion, falls or nausea mean recheck urgently."},{"h":"Check the drawers, not the prescription","x":"Older people often save tablets after courses end. Ask what is kept at home, including a spouse's medicines, and arrange removal."},{"h":"Psychotic depression changes treatment","x":"Fixed guilt, poverty or nihilistic beliefs mean psychotic depression. It needs antipsychotic augmentation or ECT, and usually urgent senior review."}],"type":"Risk assessment + History to a diagnosis","do":"Elicit depressive symptoms, caring strain and physical factors, then assess suicide risk and risk to his wife."},"c-t-68":{"obj":["Confirmed consent and drew out Stephen's concerns, including driving incidents and drinking","Explained the ACE-III result, the alcohol intake and liver tests, and the possible causes in plain words","Stated she must stop driving now and explained her DVLA duty and the clinic's next step if she refuses","Agreed longer-term steps: safe alcohol treatment, further tests, transport, LPA and carer support","Checked Stephen's understanding with teach-back and safety-netted"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"She's livid. She says you lot are taking her car away.\"","r":"\"I can hear how hard these few days have been. Only the DVLA decides on licences, not us. Let me explain what we found and why we're worried.\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"Surely it's just the whisky. If she stops, she'll be back to normal?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd hope so, and the drink may be part of it. But a score of fifty is very low. We'll treat the drinking safely and test again to see how much returns.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have noticed. I thought it was just her getting older.\"","r":"\"Lots of families think that; changes creep in slowly. You've noticed now and you're acting. That's what matters.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't she just do a driving test to prove she's fine?\"","r":"\"I see why that sounds fair. With her test results and the scrapes, she isn't safe to drive while we wait. She needs to stop now.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"She'll never forgive me if I'm the one who stops her.\"","r":"\"That's a real fear. You don't have to carry it alone. We can meet her together, so she hears it from us, not just you.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What if she just ignores everyone and drives anyway?\"","r":"\"Then please tell us. We'd inform the DVLA. If she drives once her licence is gone, or someone is in danger, the police may need to know, as a last resort.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Thiamine 100 mg three times daily from now (GP), for Wernicke's risk","No sudden stop: supervised reduction via the community alcohol team; if detox is needed, a short-acting benzodiazepine such as oxazepam, given age and liver results","Bloods: B12, folate, TFT, calcium, glucose, FBC, LFT, clotting; MRI head (CT if MRI unsuitable)","Repeat ACE-III after about 2-3 months of abstinence; no anti-dementia drug until the diagnosis is clearer"],"psych":["Motivational interviewing with an older adults' alcohol worker, building on her wish to stay independent","Joint meeting this week with the consultant, Mrs Doyle and Stephen to explain driving and the plan"],"social":["Driving: she stops now and tells the DVLA; if not, the clinic tells her it will inform the DVLA, does so, and writes to her","Car sold or kept elsewhere with her agreement; bus pass, taxi account, community transport, lifts from family","Adult social care assessment for self-care and meals, with her consent; carers' assessment for Stephen","Lasting Power of Attorney for health and finances while she has capacity; Age UK and Alzheimer's Society support"],"safety":["999 if she drives and someone is in danger; police only as a last resort","Call GP or 111 for shakes, sweating, confusion or a fit while cutting down; consultant review within 2 weeks"]},"facts":[{"h":"ACE-III cut-offs","x":"Scored out of 100. Cut-offs of 88 (sensitive) and 82 (specific) are common. A score of 50 suggests dementia of moderate severity, pending other causes."},{"h":"Half a bottle is 14 units","x":"A 70 cl bottle of 40% whisky holds 28 units. Half a bottle a day is the weekly low-risk limit, every day."},{"h":"GGT and ALT mean different things","x":"GGT rises with heavy drinking; ALT rises with liver cell injury. Together they support heavy, harmful drinking."},{"h":"GMC driving sequence","x":"Advise stopping, explain her duty to tell the DVLA, try again if she refuses, tell her you will inform the DVLA, do so, then write to her."},{"h":"Never stop drinking suddenly","x":"Abrupt stopping after months of heavy drinking risks fits and delirium tremens, worse in older people. Give thiamine and plan supervised reduction."}],"type":"Explain a diagnosis + Management plan","do":"Explain the memory and alcohol findings to her son, then agree the driving plan in the GMC and DVLA order."},"c-t-69":{"obj":["Gained consent, offered a chaperone, exposed both arms and checked for pain","Inspected and tested tone and power joint by joint, comparing sides with MRC grades","Tested reflexes with reinforcement, Hoffmann's sign and coordination","Tested light touch, pin prick, temperature, vibration and joint position across C5-T1","Summarised findings to him in plain words with next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Everyone thinks it's in my head. Why bother checking?\"","r":"\"I'm sorry it's felt like that. Your symptoms are real, and that's exactly why I want to check the nerves properly, both arms, so we know.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is it MS? I read pins and needles can be MS.\"","r":"\"That's a scary thing to read. It's one thing doctors think about, and this check helps. I'll tell you what I find at the end, honestly.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why are you doing the left arm? It's the right that's numb.\"","r":"\"Good question. Comparing with your good arm is how I spot small differences. So you'll do everything twice.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Ow, that's sore near my shoulder.\"","r":"\"Thanks for telling me. I'll stop that bit. Just push as much as feels comfortable, and tell me if anything hurts.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So if it's all normal, what is it then?\"","r":"\"The nerves and muscles are working well, which is reassuring. Tingling can come from tension, anxiety or things like low B12, so we'll check bloods and keep an eye on it.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods today: B12, folate, HbA1c, TFT, FBC, U&E, calcium; record in the admission physical health check","Re-examine if symptoms spread, weakness appears, or bladder or bowel change; then discuss with the medical team and consider nerve conduction studies or MRI"],"psych":["Include bodily symptoms in his CBT (psychology team), with a shared, non-dismissive explanation","Avoid repeated unnecessary tests once causes are excluded; one named doctor reviews physical complaints"],"safety":["Nurses to report new weakness, clumsiness, falls, neck pain or bladder change the same day","Findings handed to the ward round and documented today"]},"facts":[{"h":"Root values for power","x":"Shoulder abduction C5, elbow flexion C5-6, elbow extension C7, wrist extension C6-7, finger flexion C8, finger abduction T1."},{"h":"Reflex roots","x":"Biceps and supinator C5-6, triceps C7. If a reflex seems absent, ask him to clench his teeth (reinforcement) before calling it absent."},{"h":"Dermatomes to test","x":"C5 outer shoulder, C6 thumb, C7 middle finger, C8 little finger, T1 inner elbow. Compare each point right with left."},{"h":"Two sensory pathways","x":"Dorsal columns carry light touch, vibration and joint position; spinothalamic tracts carry pain and temperature. Test both."},{"h":"MRC grades","x":"0 no movement, 1 flicker, 2 moves with gravity removed, 3 against gravity, 4 against some resistance, 5 normal."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Examine both upper limbs in order, comparing sides, and state whether there is any neurological deficit."},"c-t-70":{"obj":["Gained consent from Des for the examination and the student, offered a chaperone and positioned him","Examined general signs, hands and pulse, naming what would suggest atrial fibrillation","Measured BP in both arms and lying and standing, linking a postural drop to his falls","Examined neck, heart, lung bases, ankles and foot pulses, explaining each step to the student","Summarised findings and next tests to Des and the student"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What's my heart got to do with my memory?\"","r":"\"Good question. The brain needs a steady blood supply. High pressure, an irregular heartbeat or furred arteries can starve small parts of it, and that can affect memory.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is this to see if I've had a stroke?\"","r":"\"That's a natural worry. Partly, yes: I'm looking for things that raise the risk of small strokes, so we can treat them and protect your brain.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm a bit dizzy standing up.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Sit straight back down; I've got you. That dizziness may be part of why you've been falling, so it's useful to know.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"Student: \"Why both arms for blood pressure?\"","r":"\"A difference over 15 suggests narrowing in one arm's artery. We use the higher arm for future readings.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Do I have to have the student here?\"","r":"\"Not at all. It's entirely your choice, and it won't change your care. Would you prefer she waits outside?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["ECG today for rhythm and LVH; 24-hour ECG if falls are unexplained or the pulse was irregular","Bloods: lipids, HbA1c, U&E, eGFR, FBC, TFT, B12, folate; urine albumin:creatinine ratio","MRI brain for infarcts and white matter change; echocardiogram if a murmur is heard","Drug review with the medical team: stop or reduce BP-lowering drugs causing postural drop; avoid hypoglycaemia-prone agents; statin per NICE"],"psych":["Explain to Des, in short steps, how blood vessel health links to memory, with a written summary"],"social":["Falls assessment by physiotherapy and OT; diabetic foot review by podiatry","GP informed for long-term BP, diabetes and smoking cessation follow-up"],"safety":["Lying and standing BP daily for a week; falls care plan; nurses to report dizziness on standing","Capillary glucose monitoring; treat and report any reading below 4 mmol/L"]},"facts":[{"h":"Postural hypotension threshold","x":"Measure lying, then after at least one minute standing. A systolic fall of 20 mmHg or more suggests postural hypotension (NICE NG136); review medicines."},{"h":"Both arms first","x":"If BP differs by more than 15 mmHg between arms, repeat, then use the higher arm for all future readings."},{"h":"AF and dementia","x":"AF raises stroke and dementia risk. Feel the pulse for at least 30 seconds; confirm any irregularity with an ECG."},{"h":"Carotids before the heart","x":"Listen over each carotid with breath held. A bruit suggests stenosis and needs a duplex scan if symptoms fit."},{"h":"Feet in diabetes","x":"Check foot pulses, skin, ulcers and sensation. Peripheral arterial disease signals widespread vascular disease, including the brain."}],"type":"Physical examination","do":"Examine the cardiovascular system fully, explaining each step's link to vascular dementia and falls to the student."},"c-u-11":{"obj":["Took a history of the attempt covering planning, the note, method, lethality, how he survived and his regret now","Asked about current suicidal intent, a plan for leaving, means at home and what he would do if his children refused","Asked directly about risk to others (wife and children) and risk from others, and screened for depression and psychosis","Told him he should stay, with three reasons, and honestly explained Sections 5(4) and 5(2) and what each means","Set continuous observation, a belongings search and a neck check, and named the senior told and the 9 a.m. review"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I came in voluntarily. That means I can walk out whenever I like.\"","r":"\"You're right that you came in voluntarily, and I respect that. Can I tell you honestly where I stand, and why, before you decide?\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You lot don't care. You just want to lock me up.\"","r":"\"I can hear how trapped you feel, and I'd be angry too. I don't want to lock anyone up. I want you alive by the morning, and talking to the consultant.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I'm not even glad the rope broke, if I'm honest.\"","r":"\"Thank you for being honest; that matters. It tells me how much pain you're in right now. Are those thoughts still with you tonight?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not mentally ill. My life just isn't worth living any more.\"","r":"\"Losing your family feels unbearable, and that's real. Two months of no sleep, no enjoyment and no hope is also what depression looks like, and depression can be treated.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So are you going to let me leave, or not?\"","r":"\"Not tonight, Gareth. I'm worried you'd die. I'd like you to stay voluntarily until nine. If you tried to leave, we'd hold you briefly for a full assessment. That's the honest answer.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Let me go and see the kids, and I'll come straight back.\"","r":"\"I can see how much you miss them; that's a strong reason to stay alive. Tonight isn't safe. Let's plan with the team how and when you see them properly.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["No new medication at 4 a.m.; if distressed or unable to sleep, lorazepam 1 mg orally as a one-off, per the chart","Neck check now: voice, swallowing, breathing and bruising; any hoarseness, stridor or difficulty swallowing means urgent medical review","Morning: FBC, U&Es, LFTs, TFTs; consultant to consider an antidepressant such as sertraline 50 mg once depression is confirmed"],"psych":["Named nurse to spend time with him through the night and again on the day shift","Collaborative safety plan before any leave; CBT for depression later in the admission","Consultant review at 09:00 with the plan and his views fed back"],"social":["With his consent, contact his brother for support and collateral in the morning","Ward social worker to help with separation, housing and arrangements to see his children","AMHP and a second doctor if a Mental Health Act assessment is needed"],"safety":["Continuous observation within eyesight tonight; belongings searched with consent; belts, cords and laces removed","Nurse in charge may use Section 5(4) if he leaves before the doctor arrives; doctor uses 5(2) (Form H1) after ringing a senior","If he goes missing: ring police as a high-risk missing person and inform the on-call manager; consider warning his wife, as he plans to go to the children"]},"facts":[{"h":"5(4): nurse holds up to six hours","x":"A registered mental health nurse can hold an informal inpatient for up to 6 hours on Form H2. It ends when the doctor or approved clinician arrives."},{"h":"5(2): doctor holds up to 72 hours","x":"For the doctor in charge or one nominated deputy. Complete Form H1 and get it to the hospital managers. It allows assessment, not enforced treatment."},{"h":"Hanging is a high-lethality method","x":"Survival often depends on chance, as here. Judge intent by planning, the note, timing to avoid rescue, and his feelings about surviving, not by the injury."},{"h":"Neck injury can worsen later","x":"After a hanging, swelling can threaten the airway hours later. Ask about voice change, swallowing and breathing; any change needs urgent medical review."},{"h":"Do not use risk scores to decide","x":"NICE NG225: risk scales and tools must not be used to predict suicide or decide who goes home. Base the decision on a full assessment and formulation."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Mental Health Act process","do":"In ten minutes: assess suicide and other risks, decide he stays, explain holding powers, set observations."},"c-u-13":{"obj":["Asked for a 12-lead ECG and NEWS2 within the first minute and confirmed whether pain was present now","Asked onset, site, character, radiation, associated symptoms and at least three cardiac risk factors","Completed hands, radial pulse, both-arm blood pressure, face, JVP, carotids, apex, four heart areas, lung bases and legs, explaining each step","Gave Brenda the findings in plain words and named the troponin and medical review still needed","Made an SBAR call to the medical on-call and gave named 999 triggers to the nurse in charge"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"It's my heart. Everyone keeps saying it's just my nerves.\"","r":"\"That must be frustrating and frightening. I'm taking it seriously: I'm going to do a heart tracing and examine you properly right now.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't breathe properly. I think I'm dying.\"","r":"\"You're safe, and I'm right here. Breathe slowly with me for a moment. Your oxygen level is good. Now, show me where it hurts.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why are you checking both arms?\"","r":"\"Good question. Comparing both arms helps me rule out a problem with the big blood vessel from the heart. It's routine with chest pain.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"The ECG's normal, so you'll say it's anxiety again.\"","r":"\"I understand why you expect that. A normal tracing is reassuring but not the whole story, so I'm asking the medical doctor to check a heart blood test too.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to take my top off with the door open.\"","r":"\"Of course not. Let's close the door and pull the curtain. You can keep a sheet over you and I'll only uncover what I need.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["12-lead ECG within 10 minutes; repeat if pain recurs or changes","High-sensitivity troponin as advised by the medical team; FBC, U&E, lipids, HbA1c, magnesium","If the medical team suspect ACS: aspirin 300 mg chewed once (if not allergic) and GTN as prescribed; transfer to ED","Review psychotropics for QT effects; avoid adding QT-prolonging drugs until reviewed"],"psych":["Explain clearly that her heart has been checked and what the tests show, to lower health anxiety","Once the heart is cleared: CBT for panic disorder from the ward psychologist"],"social":["With her consent, update her daughter after the medical review","Cardiology outpatient referral by the medical team if they advise it"],"safety":["Observations hourly for 4 hours; urgent medical review if NEWS2 is 5 or more, or 3 in one parameter","Call 999 or the emergency team if pain persists beyond 15 minutes, ECG changes, collapse or SpO2 under 94%","Nurse in charge told the triggers and that PRN sedatives wait until the medics have reviewed"]},"facts":[{"h":"ECG within ten minutes","x":"Any chest pain on the ward: 12-lead ECG within 10 minutes and full NEWS2. Show the ECG to the medical on-call if you are unsure."},{"h":"A normal ECG isn't the all-clear","x":"Acute coronary syndrome can have a normal ECG. NICE CG95: a high-sensitivity troponin guides the decision; the medical team decides on timing."},{"h":"Check BP in both arms","x":"A difference of more than 20 mmHg between arms with tearing pain to the back suggests aortic dissection. Call 999 or the emergency team at once."},{"h":"Psychiatric patients die of heart disease","x":"People with severe mental illness die years earlier, mostly from physical illness. Chest pain in a patient with anxiety needs the same work-up as anyone else."},{"h":"Listen in four areas, timed","x":"Aortic, pulmonary, tricuspid and mitral areas, timing with the carotid. Roll left for mitral stenosis; sit forward in expiration for aortic regurgitation."}],"type":"Physical examination + Emergency management","do":"In ten minutes: get an ECG and NEWS2, take a pain history, examine the heart fully, escalate by SBAR."},"c-u-14":{"obj":["Asked about onset, growth, pain and the three red flags: swallowing, breathing and voice","Asked at least six hypothyroid symptoms, including periods and libido, plus lithium and thyroid test history","Examined hands, pulse, eyes, and the neck from front with swallow and from behind, including nodes and trachea","Checked for a bruit, ankle reflexes and proximal power, and offered to check pretibial skin","Explained likely lithium-related hypothyroidism, named four blood tests, and told her to continue lithium"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is the lithium doing this? Will I have to come off it?\"","r":"\"That's a really fair worry. Lithium can slow the thyroid, but it's very treatable with a daily tablet, and most people stay on lithium. Please don't stop it.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I thought I was just getting fat and lazy.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry you've been blaming yourself. Tiredness and weight gain like this can come straight from an underactive thyroid. It isn't laziness.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Could it be cancer?\"","r":"\"I understand why that thought comes up. A smooth swelling on both sides that's grown slowly is much more likely to be the thyroid enlarging from lithium. If anything changes, we'll scan it.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Could this be why I got so depressed?\"","r":"\"It may well have played a part. An underactive thyroid can drag your mood down, so treating it could help your recovery too.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want people staring at my neck.\"","r":"\"That's completely understandable. It's just me and the nurse, door closed. I'll explain everything before I do it, and we can stop whenever you like.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods today: TSH, free T4, thyroid peroxidase antibodies, U&E and eGFR, adjusted calcium; lithium level 12 hours after the evening dose","Continue lithium; aim for a 12-hour level of 0.6–0.8 mmol/L; never stop it suddenly","If hypothyroid: levothyroxine, e.g. 50 micrograms each morning on an empty stomach, adjusted by TSH; recheck about every 3 months until stable (NICE NG145)","Thyroid ultrasound if nodular, asymmetric or growing; urgent ENT if stridor, dysphagia or voice change"],"psych":["Explain that treating the thyroid may lift her mood and energy","Ward nurse goes through the lithium record book with her: toxicity and thyroid symptoms"],"social":["GP letter for shared care: thyroid tests and levothyroxine after discharge","Community pharmacist alerted to avoid NSAIDs and ACE inhibitors without checking"],"safety":["Report hoarse voice, difficulty swallowing or breathing at once","Lithium toxicity signs to report urgently: vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness, confusion"]},"facts":[{"h":"Lithium slows the thyroid","x":"Hypothyroidism and goitre are common on lithium. Treat with levothyroxine and usually continue lithium. NICE CG185: check TFTs, U&Es and calcium every 6 months."},{"h":"Lithium levels at 12 hours","x":"Take the level 12 hours after the last dose; usual maintenance target 0.6–0.8 mmol/L. Levels every 3 months in the first year, then every 6."},{"h":"Fine tremor: lithium, not always thyroid","x":"A fine tremor is common on lithium. A coarse tremor with vomiting, diarrhoea or unsteadiness suggests toxicity: hold the dose and check a level now."},{"h":"Swallow tells you it's thyroid","x":"A thyroid swelling rises on swallowing. A midline lump that rises when the tongue sticks out suggests a thyroglossal cyst instead."},{"h":"Check calcium too","x":"Lithium can cause hyperparathyroidism and raised calcium, which can also cause low mood, thirst and constipation. Check adjusted calcium with the thyroid tests."}],"type":"Physical examination + Medication review","do":"In ten minutes: take a focused hypothyroid history, examine the thyroid fully, explain lithium link and blood tests."},"c-u-16":{"obj":["Stated a NEWS2 score of 6 and named it an urgent response within the hour","Asked about onset, sputum, pleuritic pain, haemoptysis, calf pain, smoking and swallowing","Checked expansion, percussion, breath sounds and vocal resonance on both sides, front and back, and named right basal consolidation","Checked for rigidity and calf swelling, and named neuroleptic malignant syndrome and a clot as alternatives","Agreed urgent medical transfer with Section 17 leave, named bloods including cultures and CK, and set a NEWS2 trigger for 999"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Am I going to die? I can't get my breath.\"","r":"\"That sounds really frightening. Your oxygen level is safe right now and you're talking well. I'm going to find out what's causing this and get you the right treatment tonight.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to take my top off. Not with men around.\"","r":"\"Of course. It's just me and Nurse Okafor, curtains closed. You can keep your bra on and I'll only uncover one part at a time.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"It's that new tablet, isn't it? You lot have poisoned me.\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd wonder. The tablet can make chest infections a bit more likely, and I'll tell your consultant. But stopping it suddenly tonight could make you unwell, so let's treat the infection first.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not going to another hospital. They'll keep me forever.\"","r":"\"That's a fair worry after everything. This is only for your chest: an X-ray, blood tests and antibiotics. A nurse from here comes with you, and you'll come back once you're better.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why do you keep tapping my back?\"","r":"\"Good question. The sound tells me whether the lung underneath is full of air or something thicker, like infection. Your right side sounds different, which helps me find it.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I just want to sleep. Leave me alone.\"","r":"\"You're exhausted, I can see that. Two more minutes listening to your back and I'll let you rest while we sort the transfer.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods before antibiotics: FBC, U&E, CRP, LFT, CK, venous lactate; two sets of blood cultures; sputum for culture","Antibiotics by the medical team per local pneumonia guideline, ideally within the hour if sepsis is confirmed","Oxygen if SpO2 falls below 94%, target 94-98%; chest X-ray at the acute hospital","Continue olanzapine unless the medical team advise otherwise; responsible clinician to review dose and sedation in the morning","Speech and language therapy swallow assessment once she is back on the ward"],"psych":["Short, calm explanations at each step; check what she understands about the transfer","Escort nurse who knows her, to reduce fear and paranoia in a strange setting"],"social":["Nurse in charge to phone her named next of kin in the morning, with her agreement","Ward manager to book the escort and handover to the AMU nurses"],"safety":["NEWS2 every 30 minutes until transfer; 999 if 7 or more, SpO2 below 94% or new drowsiness","Section 17 leave form completed and copied; risk summary sent with her","Hand over to the day team and the responsible clinician at 09:00 handover"]},"facts":[{"h":"NEWS2 sets the clock","x":"Score 5-6, or 3 in one parameter: urgent review within the hour. Score 7 or more: emergency response; in a standalone unit, that usually means 999."},{"h":"Antipsychotics raise pneumonia risk","x":"Sedation, a dry mouth or drooling, and a slower swallow all raise aspiration risk. Risk is highest early in treatment. Ask about choking on drinks."},{"h":"Fever plus antipsychotic: check NMS","x":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome: fever, lead-pipe rigidity, confusion, sweating, unstable pulse and BP, and a raised CK. Check muscle tone and send a CK."},{"h":"Consolidation: four signs at one spot","x":"Dull percussion, bronchial breathing, coarse crackles and louder vocal resonance over the same zone. Effusion is stony dull with quiet breath sounds and reduced vocal resonance."},{"h":"Detained patients need Section 17 leave","x":"A patient on Section 2 or 3 needs Section 17 leave to go to a general hospital. At night, ring the on-call consultant and record it."}],"type":"Physical examination + Emergency management","do":"In ten minutes: score NEWS2, check the chest front and back, and arrange urgent medical transfer safely."},"c-u-17":{"obj":["Asked about last bowel action, wind, vomiting, overflow, laxatives taken and clozapine doses","Inspected, palpated all nine regions light then deep, percussed and listened, leaving the painful area until last","Offered hernia and rectal checks with a chaperone and documented his answer","Named suspected clozapine-related obstruction and arranged same-day surgical review with nil by mouth and named bloods","Agreed a clozapine plan: hold, hyoscine stopped, consultant and pharmacist told, re-titration if over 48 hours"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Please don't stop my discharge. I've waited months.\"","r":"\"I know how much this matters to you. Let's get your tummy safe first. Telling us was the right thing, and it's why we can sort it before you go home.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have said something on leave. I'm an idiot.\"","r":"\"You're not. Lots of people keep quiet when something important is at stake. What matters is that you told us today.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not having a finger up my backside.\"","r":"\"That's completely your choice. It helps check for hard stool low down, but the surgeons can decide if it's needed. I'll note that you said no for now.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't you just give me a strong laxative?\"","r":"\"Fair question. If the bowel is blocked, pushing it with strong laxatives could make things worse. The surgeons need to check first, then we choose the right treatment.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So you're taking my clozapine off me now?\"","r":"\"I understand that worries you; it's kept you well. We may pause a dose while your bowel recovers. If it's paused over two days, we restart slowly and build back up, so you stay safe.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"It really hurts when you press there.\"","r":"\"Sorry, I'll stop pressing. Thank you for telling me. That helps me know where the problem is.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Nil by mouth until surgical review; IV fluids at the acute hospital if dehydrated","Bloods now: FBC with neutrophils, U&E, CRP, LFT, amylase, venous lactate, clozapine level","Hold evening clozapine pending consultant and pharmacist advice; if over 48 hours missed, re-titrate from 12.5 mg","Stop hyoscine; review all anticholinergics. After obstruction is excluded: Laxido and senna daily, no bulk-forming laxatives"],"psych":["Explain why bowels matter on clozapine; agree he tells staff about any two days without a bowel action","Reassure him that discharge is delayed, not cancelled, so he stays open with the team"],"social":["Care coordinator and community pharmacist told of the bowel plan before discharge","Family or supported-living staff shown the bowel diary and warning signs"],"safety":["Same-day surgical review by ambulance with nurse escort; 999 if he vomits, develops guarding or NEWS2 rises","Daily bowel chart with Bristol stool chart for every clozapine patient, including on leave","Incident report; consultant and ward manager informed today"]},"facts":[{"h":"Clozapine constipation can kill","x":"Clozapine slows gut transit in most patients. Impaction, ileus and bowel ischaemia can follow. The MHRA has warned about deaths. Ask about bowels at every review."},{"h":"Over 48 hours missed: re-titrate","x":"If clozapine has been stopped for more than 48 hours, restart at 12.5 mg and build up again. Restarting at the old dose risks collapse and seizures."},{"h":"Hyoscine adds to the problem","x":"Hyoscine hydrobromide for drooling is anticholinergic and slows the gut further. Stop it if constipation is severe; review any other anticholinergic drugs."},{"h":"Softener plus stimulant, not bulk","x":"Prevent with a macrogol and a stimulant such as senna. Avoid bulk-forming laxatives like ispaghula: with slow transit, they can form a blockage."},{"h":"NEWS2 can be falsely reassuring","x":"Obstruction or early perforation may show only a fast pulse. Distension, no wind, vomiting or guarding mean surgical review today, whatever the score."}],"type":"Physical examination + Emergency management","do":"In ten minutes: ask the bowel red flags, check his abdomen systematically, and arrange same-day surgical review."},"c-u-18":{"obj":["Established a 12-month, gradual and steady decline with at least one dated example","Asked about memory, word-finding, recognising people, orientation and at least three daily tasks","Screened for vascular, Lewy body, frontotemporal, depressive, alcohol and medicine causes","Identified driving, cooker, scam and carer-strain risks and agreed a named action for each","Summarised a likely Alzheimer's-type picture and named bloods, memory testing and a brain scan as next steps"],"snip":[{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I thought it was just old age. I feel awful we didn't notice sooner.\"","r":"\"That's so understandable; early changes are easy to explain away. You noticed, and you're here now. That's what counts. Can you tell me what changed first?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this dementia? Just tell me straight.\"","r":"\"I can hear you want a clear answer. What you describe fits a memory illness like Alzheimer's, but we need bloods, memory tests and a scan before we can say for sure.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"He'll never stop driving. It's his whole life.\"","r":"\"I know, he drove for a living. But getting lost while driving worries me. I'll explain it to him myself, so it doesn't have to come from you.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Mum's exhausted. She won't ask for help.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. Caring like this is hard. She's able to ask for a carer's assessment from adult social care; I'll give you the number today.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will he forget who we are?\"","r":"\"That's a frightening thought. Right now he knows you all well. Memory illnesses change slowly, and there's a lot we can do to keep him well at home for longer.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He paid a man on the phone £300 for boiler cover we didn't need.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you mentioned it. That's a real risk. Let's talk to his bank about card limits and a trusted contact, and think about power of attorney.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, B12, folate, HbA1c, calcium; review medicines for anticholinergic effects","ACE-III in memory clinic and MRI head (CT if MRI not possible)","If Alzheimer's is confirmed: donepezil 5 mg at night for 4 weeks, then 10 mg; check pulse first, ECG if slow or faints","GP to keep blood pressure well controlled"],"psych":["Post-diagnostic support for Tariq and family from the dementia adviser","Cognitive stimulation therapy group, 14 sessions over 7 weeks, via the memory service"],"social":["Stop driving until assessed; if diagnosed, Tariq tells the DVLA","Carer's assessment for Farida through adult social care","Lasting power of attorney for finance and for health and welfare while he can still decide","Occupational therapy home safety visit"],"safety":["Herbert Protocol form with recent photo, held by the family","Bank: card limits and a trusted contact; call blocker for cold callers","Cooker: timer or automatic gas shut-off via OT"]},"facts":[{"h":"Steady decline suggests Alzheimer's","x":"Gradual onset, steady progression, early memory loss, word-finding trouble and getting lost suggest Alzheimer's. Sudden steps and vascular risk factors suggest vascular dementia."},{"h":"Lewy body clues change drug safety","x":"Visual hallucinations, fluctuating alertness, parkinsonism, falls and acting out dreams suggest Lewy body dementia. Antipsychotics can cause severe reactions: avoid them where possible."},{"h":"Dementia and driving","x":"With a dementia diagnosis, the patient must tell the DVLA. If he keeps driving against advice, a doctor may inform the DVLA after telling him (GMC)."},{"h":"Look for anticholinergic burden","x":"Over-the-counter sleep remedies, bladder drugs like oxybutynin, and older antidepressants can worsen memory. Ask the family what he takes, including from the chemist."},{"h":"Herbert Protocol","x":"A form the family keeps, with a photo, routines and places he might go. They hand it to police if he goes missing, saving vital time."}],"type":"Collateral history + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes: take a structured collateral history, screen other causes, and act on driving, cooker, money and carer risks."},"c-u-19":{"obj":["Established when Gordon was last well and that the change was acute and fluctuating","Asked about attention, orientation, sleep reversal, hallucinations and eating","Screened for infection, new medicines, alcohol, falls, bowels and bladder, and identified the productive cough","Confirmed an independent baseline (driving, money, shopping, childcare) and asked about bereavement and mood","Explained delirium versus dementia in plain words, gave Rhys two practical tasks and checked his understanding"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is this dementia? He was fine last week.\"","r":"\"That's a really natural fear. Dementia creeps on over months or years. A change in days, with drowsiness that comes and goes, points to delirium, which usually gets better.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Why has nobody told me anything? He's getting worse in here.\"","r":"\"I'm sorry you've felt left out; that's hard when you're this worried. I'll explain what I think is going on, and make sure the ward keeps you updated.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I told him to see the GP about that cough. I should have taken him.\"","r":"\"You did the right thing telling him. Lots of people put off the GP. What you've told me today is exactly what helps us treat him.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"The consultant said psychotic. Will he need to go to a mental hospital?\"","r":"\"I understand why that word worried you. I don't think he has a psychotic illness. This is delirium from a physical cause, treated here on the medical ward.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What can I actually do?\"","r":"\"A lot. Visit at regular times, bring his glasses and family photos, and gently tell him where he is and why. Familiar faces help the brain settle.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"He's been on his own since Mum died. I should have been there more.\"","r":"\"Losing your mum was hard on all of you. You're here for him now. Once he's better, we'll check how he's coping with the loss too.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Delirium screen: 4AT, chest X-ray, FBC, CRP, U&E, glucose, calcium, LFT, blood and sputum cultures, urine dip","Treat the chest infection per local guideline; oxygen if needed; fluid and food charts","Drug chart review: stop sedatives and anticholinergics; check for constipation and urinary retention (bladder scan)","Avoid antipsychotics; only if severely distressed or dangerous, haloperidol 0.5 mg orally after an ECG, lowest dose, usually no more than a week (NICE CG103)","Repeat cognitive testing about 6-8 weeks after recovery"],"psych":["Reorientation each shift: clock, calendar, glasses and hearing aids, the same nurses where possible","Rhys visits at regular times with photos, calmly telling him where he is and why","Once well: screen for depression; offer bereavement support via Cruse or NHS Talking Therapies"],"social":["Ward OT and physiotherapy assessment before discharge; short-term reablement if needed","No driving until fully recovered and the GP has reviewed him","Carer's assessment for Rhys if he takes on more care"],"safety":["Falls bundle and enhanced observation while wandering; bed near the nurses' desk","If he tries to leave and lacks capacity: best-interests action under the MCA; DoLS application if restrictions continue"]},"facts":[{"h":"Delirium: acute, fluctuating, inattentive","x":"Onset over hours to days, fluctuating course, poor attention and altered alertness. Dementia develops over months with clear consciousness. Delirium can sit on top of dementia."},{"h":"Hypoactive delirium is easily missed","x":"Drowsy, withdrawn, eating little: often mistaken for depression or tiredness. It carries worse outcomes than the agitated form. Use the 4AT."},{"h":"Antipsychotics are a last resort","x":"NICE CG103: treat the cause and use non-drug measures first. Short-term haloperidol only for severe distress or danger. Avoid in Parkinson's or Lewy body dementia."},{"h":"Look for the hidden causes","x":"Infection, drugs, alcohol withdrawal, dehydration, sodium, calcium, glucose, constipation, urinary retention, pain, hypoxia and sensory loss. Often several combine."},{"h":"Recheck memory after recovery","x":"Delirium can take weeks to clear and may unmask dementia. Ask the GP to recheck cognition and mood at about two months."}],"type":"Collateral history + Explain a diagnosis","do":"In ten minutes: establish baseline and onset, screen causes, explain delirium to Rhys, and give him a role."},"c-u-20":{"obj":["Elicited reduced sleep need, raised energy, racing thoughts, irritability and grandiose beliefs, and stated a manic episode","Asked about steroids, head injury, headaches, memory and substances, and named at least two tests","Asked about past depression, past highs and family history of mood disorder","Assessed risk to self (including COPD and the crystal), to others, and from others (debts, exploitation)","Explained mania in plain words, named the next step, and checked her response"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"That doctor poisoned the room. I won't sit where he sat.\"","r":"\"That's fine, sit wherever feels right. It sounds like today has been really frustrating. Can you tell me what happened?\""},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm not ill. I've never felt better in my life.\"","r":"\"I'm glad you feel well in yourself. What worries me is three hours' sleep, not eating, and the money. Those can wear anyone down, even when they feel wonderful.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You young doctors don't understand energy healing.\"","r":"\"You may well know more about it than me. I'm not here to argue. I want to understand what's been happening to your sleep and your health.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"When I find the right crystal I'll swallow it. My lungs will heal.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me. I can see how much you want your breathing better. Swallowing a crystal could seriously hurt you, so I need to talk about keeping you safe.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"My husband would have understood. Nobody listens now.\"","r":"\"It sounds like you've missed him a great deal. I am listening. I want to help you feel heard and safe.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not staying here. I've far too much to do.\"","r":"\"I hear you're busy. I'd like a little more of your time to check your body isn't causing this. Some blood tests and a scan first, then we decide together.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Physical work-up: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, calcium, glucose, CRP, B12, folate, syphilis serology, urine drug screen, ECG; CT or MRI head","Check all medicines: steroids, antidepressants, levodopa; avoid further steroid courses without psychiatric advice","Antipsychotic first (NICE CG185), e.g. olanzapine 5 mg at night, lower than the usual adult start; baseline weight, lipids, glucose, ECG","Warn of sedation, dizziness and falls; continue COPD inhalers","If bipolar is confirmed, discuss lithium once settled: levels 12 hours post-dose; renal, thyroid and calcium every 6 months"],"psych":["Low-stimulation care: quiet space, short calm conversations, consistent staff","Psychoeducation for her and Martin on early warning signs once she is settling","Structured psychological therapy for bipolar disorder after recovery; relapse plan written when well"],"social":["Admission to the older adult ward, or home treatment team if she accepts treatment and Martin feels safe","Protect finances: card limits, cancel online accounts with her agreement; Lasting Power of Attorney when well","Carer's assessment for Martin; no driving while unwell"],"safety":["If she refuses help and risks remain high: Mental Health Act assessment via the AMHP service","Martin to call 999 if she becomes aggressive; crisis line number given"]},"facts":[{"h":"Mania needs a week, or admission","x":"Elevated or irritable mood with more energy for at least a week (or any length if admitted), plus symptoms such as less sleep, pressured speech, racing thoughts, grandiosity and recklessness."},{"h":"Steroids can trigger mania","x":"Corticosteroids can cause mania, psychosis or depression, usually within days to weeks and more often at higher doses. Ask about recent courses for COPD or asthma."},{"h":"Later-life mania: scan and test","x":"Mania first recognised after about 50 is more often linked to stroke, tumours, thyroid disease, medicines or dementia. Do bloods and brain imaging."},{"h":"Ask about the forgotten high","x":"A past spell of sleeplessness, spending or sudden travel suggests an earlier manic or hypomanic episode. With past depression, that points to bipolar disorder."},{"h":"Risk in mania is wide","x":"Ask about exhaustion, eating, physical illness, money, debts, exploitation, sexual risk, driving, aggression and suicide, especially as mood falls."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Risk assessment","do":"In ten minutes: elicit manic symptoms, screen physical causes and history, explain mania, and assess all risks."},"c-u-21":{"obj":["Acknowledged her anger within the first minute and offered her a choice about how the talk would go","Recorded the belief's content, onset, evidence and a conviction rating, and asked about one other explanation","Screened smell, taste, voices, visions and touch, and asked about seizures or funny turns","Recorded mood, sleep, eating, orientation and insight, each with a specific finding","Asked about confronting the neighbours, weapons, self-harm and food refusal, and gave the nurse in charge a stated risk level"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Get away from me! I've done nothing wrong and you lot dragged me in here.\"","r":"\"You've had a frightening night and you didn't choose to be here; I'd be angry too. I'll sit over here. Would you tell me what's been happening at home?\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm not mad! Why are you asking me about voices?\"","r":"\"I'm sorry, I'm not saying you are. I ask everyone the same things so nothing gets missed. Let's leave that and come back to the smell, which clearly matters most.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"They're putting something in my food. I'm not eating anything you give me either.\"","r":"\"That sounds terrifying, and it makes sense you'd be careful. Would you eat something still sealed in its packet? I can ask the nurses for that today.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Are you going to tell the police I'm making it up?\"","r":"\"No. I believe you've been really frightened, and that's what I'm writing down. The police brought you here because they were worried about you, not because you did anything wrong.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't need any blood tests. There's nothing wrong with me.\"","r":"\"I hear that you feel well in yourself. A smell that won't go away can sometimes come from the body or brain, and a blood test and scan would rule those out. Would you think about it?\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"They took Misty. She was all I had.\"","r":"\"I'm so sorry. Losing her must have been awful, especially living on your own. That matters. And how have you been in yourself since then?\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Organic screen today: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, calcium, B12, folate, glucose; ECG; CT head (MRI if available)","If seizures or funny turns are described: EEG and neurology advice before any antipsychotic","If she agrees after the screen: amisulpride 50 mg once daily, review at 2 weeks, slowly up to 100-200 mg; or risperidone 0.5 mg at night","Baseline weight, lipids, HbA1c, prolactin; warn about stiffness, dizziness on standing and falls"],"psych":["One named nurse each shift; no arguing about the belief, talk about her fear, sleep and food","Later: CBT for psychosis adapted for older adults, at least 16 sessions, focusing on distress rather than proof","Ward activities and, after discharge, a day centre or befriending to reduce isolation"],"social":["Care coordinator to arrange a gas safety check at her home (Gas Emergency line 0800 111 999 if a leak is suspected)","Audiology and optician checks: poor hearing or sight make late-onset psychosis more likely","With her consent, one agreed plan with the police and council for future calls","Rights under s132 repeated and IMHA referral made by the ward"],"safety":["General observations; food, fluid and sleep charts for 72 hours","Sealed, packaged food offered at every meal","Before any leave: agree what she will do instead of confronting the neighbours"]},"facts":[{"h":"Rule out the real smell first","x":"Rotten eggs can be a gas leak or a drain fault. Ask for a gas safety check before calling it a hallucination."},{"h":"Smell hallucinations mean ask about seizures","x":"Olfactory hallucinations raise temporal lobe epilepsy or a lesion. Ask about funny turns, déjà vu, lost time and tongue biting; arrange imaging and EEG if any are present."},{"h":"Low doses in late-onset psychosis","x":"Older adults respond to low antipsychotic doses. Amisulpride 50-100 mg daily is commonly used; adjust in poor kidney function. Start low, increase slowly, watch for falls."},{"h":"Deafness and isolation matter","x":"Sensory loss and social isolation are linked with very late-onset psychosis. Check hearing and sight and plan contact, not just tablets."},{"h":"Section 2 rights on day one","x":"Explain s132 rights aloud and in writing, the 14-day window to apply to the Tribunal, and the right to an IMHA. Record that you did."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis + Mental state examination","do":"In ten minutes, map her persecutory belief, screen all perceptions, mood, orientation, insight and risk, then record a targeted MSE."},"c-u-47":{"obj":["Told him in one clear sentence that a court order stops him going home, then paused and validated his reaction","Recorded what he can and cannot do across washing, dressing, tablets, cooking, shopping and bills","Elicited and recorded his preferred place to live and his main worry","Offered at least two realistic accommodation options, each with one benefit and one barrier, and recorded his preference","Named the next actions and owners: social worker (Care Act and housing), OT assessment, police and solicitor about bail"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"That's rubbish. Forty-eight years and I've never laid a finger on her. One argument.\"","r":"\"I can hear how hard this is to take in. I'm not here to judge what happened; that's for the court. What I can do is make sure you have somewhere safe and the right help when you leave.\""},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"It's my house! She can't stop me going to my own house.\"","r":"\"I understand why that feels so unfair. The court order does mean you can't go back for now, and going there could get you into serious trouble. Your solicitor can explain your rights about the house.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"I don't want to die in some home full of old people.\"","r":"\"That's a really common worry, and nobody is going to force you. There are other options, like a flat with staff nearby. Let's look at what fits what you need.\""},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"She's been seeing someone, you know. That's what this is really about.\"","r":"\"Thank you for telling me that. It sounds like it's been on your mind a lot. I'll make sure the team knows, and we can talk about it more another time. Today I want to focus on where you'll live.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Who's going to cook for me? Halina did all that.\"","r":"\"That's exactly the kind of thing we need to plan. Our occupational therapist can see what you can manage in the kitchen, and carers or ready meals can fill the gaps.\""},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't I just go to my son's for a bit?\"","r":"\"It's natural to want to be with family. Tomasz has told us he supports his mum's decision, so that isn't possible right now. I'll make sure you can keep in touch with him if he agrees.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue his current antidepressant (mirtazapine 30 mg at night or trazodone as charted) unchanged; GP review 4 weeks after discharge","Oral thiamine 100 mg three times a day while drinking risk remains; LFTs, GGT and MCV before discharge","ACE-III once settled and sober; memory clinic referral if impaired","Compliance aid (blister pack) via hospital pharmacy and community pharmacist"],"psych":["Alcohol liaison nurse before discharge; community alcohol team follow-up","Support with loss of his marriage and home through the older adults CMHT"],"social":["Ward social worker: Care Act needs assessment and Duty to Refer to the housing authority this week","OT: kitchen, medication and money-handling assessment on the ward","Options: extra-care or sheltered housing with a care package (his preference), or residential care if needs are higher","Benefits check (pension credit, attendance allowance) and help with banking; discuss a property and financial affairs LPA while he has capacity","Police officer in the case and solicitor to agree a new bail address before discharge"],"safety":["No contact with his wife except as the injunction allows; consultant and MDT told of his belief about her fidelity","Discharge planning meeting in 1 week; CMHT follow-up within 72 hours of discharge; crisis line number on discharge"]},"facts":[{"h":"Hospitals have a duty to refer","x":"Under the Homelessness Reduction Act 2017, NHS hospitals must refer patients at risk of homelessness to a local housing authority, with consent. Start early: it takes weeks."},{"h":"Care Act assessments are by request","x":"The local authority must assess anyone who appears to need care and support. The ward social worker refers; the OT report on daily living strengthens it."},{"h":"Bail to hospital is a condition","x":"If bail requires residence at hospital, discharge elsewhere needs the bail address changed. Tell the officer in the case and his solicitor before setting a date."},{"h":"Extra-care housing sits in between","x":"Own flat, own front door, care staff on site. Suits someone who manages personal care but needs help with meals, tablets and money."},{"h":"LPA needs capacity to make","x":"A property and financial affairs LPA is valid only if he has capacity when he signs it. Raise it early if memory is a concern."}],"type":"Breaking bad news + Management plan","do":"In 10 minutes, tell him he cannot go home, map his daily living and money needs, and agree discharge options."},"c-u-51":{"obj":["Established the admission story, Section 3 status, reason for seclusion, and the threats and refusals since","Quantified intake (about 600 mL, 300 mL, then sips) and 12 hours without urine, and asked about at least four dehydration features","Agreed a staffed plan: sealed fluids first, then NEWS2 observations, U&E, CK and ECG, with IM lorazepam (not an antipsychotic) if needed","Stated named triggers for A&E transfer and how to do it safely: escorts, Section 17 leave, medical handover","Informed the consultant, set the next medical review time and documented in the seclusion record"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"How are we meant to check him or treat him when he won't let anyone near?\"","r":"\"That's the real problem, and it's fair to ask. We try sealed drinks and talking first. If he still refuses, we bring in enough trained staff and use lorazepam so we can examine him safely.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What signs should I be looking for if he's dry?\"","r":"\"Good question. Thirst, headache, dry lips, sunken eyes, dizziness on standing, a fast pulse and little dark urine. New confusion or drowsiness is the one that would make me act straight away.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"There are only two of us trained in restraint today. I'm not going in like that.\"","r":"\"You're right not to. Nobody goes in short-staffed. I'll ring the bleep holder now for three more trained staff, and we'll plan it together before anyone opens the door.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I feel like we've let him get this bad.\"","r":"\"I can hear that, and it shows how much you care. You've kept him and everyone else safe, and you called at the right time. Let's put it right now.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"If he has to go to A&E, how on earth do we get him there?\"","r":"\"Sensible worry. He'd go by ambulance with two trained escorts, Section 17 leave from his consultant, his papers and drug chart, and I'll ring the medical team first. Police only if risk is very high.\""},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"He says if anyone touches him he'll get his DNA taken.\"","r":"\"That fear makes sense of all his refusals. Let's tell him exactly what we're doing and why, open the bottles in front of him, and keep one familiar face doing the talking.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Now: sealed bottled water, oral rehydration sachets in sealed bottles and packaged food, opened in front of him; fluid chart from today","Planned check: pulse, BP, temperature, respiratory rate, SpO2, NEWS2, capillary glucose; bloods U&E, CK, FBC, LFT, glucose, CRP; 12-lead ECG","If he cannot be assessed safely: IM lorazepam 1-2 mg, repeated only per the local rapid tranquillisation policy; no IM antipsychotic until ECG and CK are known; flumazenil available","After RT: pulse, BP, RR, temperature, hydration and consciousness every 15 minutes for the first hour, then hourly until stable","Clozapine stopped over 48 hours ago: once he accepts oral medication, restart at 12.5 mg and retitrate; inform pharmacy and the clozapine monitoring service"],"psych":["One familiar nurse leads all talking; short, calm, honest explanations of what will happen and why","Acknowledge his fear about DNA and tampering rather than arguing with it","Offer drinks at every nursing check, not only when he asks"],"social":["Bleep holder or site manager: three extra restraint-trained staff to PICU within 30 minutes","Consultant (RC) informed today; Section 17 leave agreed in advance for A&E if needed","Next of kin told of the physical health concern if Marcus agrees, or if it becomes serious","Incident report for the assault on day 1 checked as done; staff debrief offered"],"safety":["Transfer to A&E if NEWS2 5 or more, new confusion, AKI on bloods or CK markedly raised","999 if he becomes drowsy, collapses or fits; nursing review every 2 hours and medical review on the seclusion schedule"]},"facts":[{"h":"Lorazepam first when he is dry","x":"Dehydration, exhaustion and restraint raise the risk of neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Use IM lorazepam; avoid IM antipsychotics until fluids, CK and an ECG are known."},{"h":"Monitor every 15 minutes after RT","x":"NICE NG10: after rapid tranquillisation, check pulse, BP, respiratory rate, temperature, hydration and consciousness at least hourly; check every 15 minutes if he is physically unwell, sedated or was restrained."},{"h":"Seclusion reviews run to a timetable","x":"Code of Practice: medical review within an hour, then 4-hourly until the first MDT review, then at least twice daily; nursing review every 2 hours; independent MDT review after 8 hours."},{"h":"NEWS2 tells you when to escalate","x":"Score 5 or more, or 3 in one parameter: urgent medical review. Score 7 or more: emergency response, usually transfer to an acute hospital."},{"h":"Clozapine break over 48 hours","x":"Do not restart at the old dose: begin again at 12.5 mg and retitrate. Tell pharmacy and the clozapine monitoring service, which reset the blood-test schedule."}],"type":"Handover and escalation + Emergency management","do":"In ten minutes, take the seclusion handover, name likely dehydration, and agree a staffed plan for checks, fluids and transfer."},"c-u-55":{"obj":["Established gradual onset over about a year, with recent memory lost and older memories kept","Screened language, recognition, gadgets, money and behaviour, including hallucinations, falls and personality change","Identified wandering, the gas hob, driving and the phone scam as risks, and gave at least two named safety steps","Took vascular history, medicines (flagging oxybutynin), alcohol and family history","Asked about Mrs Harper's own strain and explained next steps: testing today, bloods, scan, diagnosis appointment, carer's assessment and a contact number"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"So is it dementia?\"","r":"\"It's a fair question. What you describe sounds like a memory illness, possibly Alzheimer's. We'll know more once his tests and scan are done, and we'll go through the results with you both.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I'm so tired. I'm up half the night listening for the door.\"","r":"\"That sounds exhausting, and you've been doing it alone. You matter in this too. A carer's assessment and a door sensor could help you sleep. Can I help arrange both?\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Should I just lock him in when I pop to the shops?\"","r":"\"I understand why you'd want to. The trouble is the fire risk, and it restricts him. Safer options are a door sensor, a GPS tracker, and someone with him while you're out.\""},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have noticed sooner. I thought he was just getting old.\"","r":"\"That's very common. These changes creep in slowly and are easy to explain away. You've noticed now, and you've brought him in at a good time.\""},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"He won't stop driving. It's the last thing he's got.\"","r":"\"I hear how hard that is for him. For now, it's safest not to drive until the assessment is done. We'll talk to him about it today, so it isn't left to you.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is there a tablet that can help?\"","r":"\"If it is Alzheimer's, there are medicines that can help memory for a while, like donepezil. We'd discuss them once we've confirmed the diagnosis.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Check GP bloods: FBC, U&E, LFT, TFT, B12, folate, calcium, glucose, HbA1c; ECG if a cholinesterase inhibitor is planned","CT or MRI head as part of the assessment","GP to review oxybutynin (anticholinergic) and consider an alternative such as mirabegron if suitable","If Alzheimer's is confirmed: donepezil 5 mg at night, increased to 10 mg after 4 weeks; check pulse first; warn about nausea, diarrhoea and vivid dreams","GP to keep blood pressure controlled"],"psych":["Post-diagnostic support sessions for Leonard and Gwen","Cognitive stimulation therapy group, usually 14 weekly sessions, if mild to moderate dementia is confirmed","Memory aids: daily whiteboard, key safe, labelled cupboards"],"social":["Adult social care referral: needs assessment for Leonard and a carer's assessment for Gwen (Care Act 2014)","Herbert Protocol form with local police; telecare door sensor and GPS device","Gas isolation valve or cooker safety device; occupational therapy home safety check","Lasting power of attorney while he has capacity; Alzheimer's Society dementia adviser"],"safety":["No driving until assessment complete; if dementia is confirmed he must tell the DVLA","If he goes missing: call 999 immediately and give the Herbert Protocol form"]},"facts":[{"h":"The course points to the type","x":"Gradual, steady decline with early recent-memory loss suggests Alzheimer's. Stepwise drops suggest vascular. Early hallucinations, falls or parkinsonism suggest Lewy body; early personality change, frontotemporal."},{"h":"Look for anticholinergic drugs","x":"Oxybutynin, tricyclics and sedating antihistamines worsen memory in older people. NICE NG97 advises reviewing anticholinergic burden during dementia assessment."},{"h":"Herbert Protocol for wanderers","x":"A form held by the family with a recent photo, habits and places he may go. If he goes missing, it is handed to police to speed up the search."},{"h":"Dementia must be reported to DVLA","x":"Once diagnosed, he must tell the DVLA, which decides on licensing. Advise not driving until assessed if there are safety concerns like a scrape or getting lost."},{"h":"Carers have a legal right to assessment","x":"Under the Care Act 2014, any carer who appears to need support can have a carer's assessment from adult social care, whatever their income."}],"type":"Collateral history","do":"In ten minutes, take a collateral history from his wife, identify likely Alzheimer's and key risks, and explain next steps."},"c-u-64":{"obj":["Asked at least six hypothyroid symptoms, including low mood, plus neck swelling, swallowing, voice change and family history","Explained in plain words that a high TSH with low T4 means an underactive thyroid, commonly caused by lithium, treatable, and that lithium usually continues","Examined in order: hands, skin and pulse; neck inspection with swallow and tongue out; palpation from behind; nodes and trachea; bruit; eyes; ankle reflexes","Told him the findings: slow pulse of 56, small smooth goitre, slow-relaxing ankle reflexes","Agreed next steps: lithium level, U&E, calcium, TPO antibodies, ECG, GP letter on levothyroxine, and a lithium toxicity safety net"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is it the lithium? Have I got to come off it?\"","r":"\"Good question. Lithium is a common cause, yes. But usually we treat the thyroid and keep the lithium, because it's what keeps you well.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is this why I've been so tired?\"","r":"\"Very likely, yes. A slow thyroid causes tiredness, weight gain and feeling cold. Once it's treated, most people find their energy comes back over a few weeks.\""},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"A lump in my neck? Is it cancer?\"","r":"\"I can see why that's frightening. What I feel is a smooth, even swelling of the whole gland, which is typical of lithium. We'll check bloods, and scan it if it changes.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will I need the thyroid tablet forever?\"","r":"\"Probably for as long as you're on lithium, and sometimes afterwards. It's one tablet a morning, and the blood test tells us the right dose.\""},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I thought I was sliding into depression again. I've been dreading it.\"","r":"\"That must have been worrying. A slow thyroid can make you feel low and flat. Treating it may lift a lot of that, and we'll keep an eye on your mood too.\""},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can I keep taking ibuprofen for my knee?\"","r":"\"Better not. Ibuprofen can push lithium levels up. Paracetamol is safer. Ask the pharmacist if you're unsure about any medicine.\""}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue lithium; recommend GP starts levothyroxine on an empty stomach each morning (NICE NG145: about 1.6 micrograms/kg/day if no heart disease; lower start if older or cardiac disease)","Today: lithium level 12 hours after the dose (aim 0.6 to 0.8 mmol/L), U&E, eGFR, calcium, TPO antibodies; ECG for pulse 56","Recheck TSH about every 3 months until stable, then TFTs, U&E and calcium every 6 months with lithium levels","Thyroid ultrasound if the goitre grows, becomes nodular or causes pressure symptoms"],"psych":["Explain that low thyroid can cause low mood and fatigue; review mood at the next clinic before changing any mood medicine","Refresh lithium safety: fluids, avoid NSAIDs, toxicity signs"],"social":["Same-day letter to GP under shared care; update his lithium record book","Tell his community pharmacist about the new medicine for interaction checks"],"safety":["Seek urgent help for vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness or confusion (possible lithium toxicity)","Urgent review if the neck swelling grows quickly, or swallowing or breathing becomes difficult"]},"facts":[{"h":"Lithium commonly causes hypothyroidism","x":"Lithium reduces thyroid hormone release. Hypothyroidism and goitre are common, more so in women and with thyroid antibodies. Treat with levothyroxine; lithium usually continues."},{"h":"Six-monthly bloods on lithium","x":"NICE CG185: check thyroid function, renal function and calcium every 6 months, and lithium levels every 3 months in year one, then 6-monthly if stable."},{"h":"Levothyroxine on an empty stomach","x":"Take 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast; separate from calcium, iron and antacids by 4 hours. Recheck TSH about every 3 months until stable."},{"h":"Slow-relaxing reflexes are the sign","x":"In hypothyroidism the ankle jerk contracts normally but relaxes slowly. Test with him kneeling on a chair for the clearest view."},{"h":"Thyroid moves with swallowing","x":"A goitre rises on swallowing. A thyroglossal cyst also rises with tongue protrusion. Palpate from behind with the neck slightly flexed."}],"type":"Physical examination + Explain a diagnosis","do":"In 10 minutes: ask focused thyroid symptoms, explain the results, examine the thyroid and related signs, and state the findings."},"wc-01":{"obj":["Triaged the seven-patient list before 09:00, flagging the overdue lithium level and the dependent adult who was not brought","Sent a 12-hour post-dose lithium level with U&E/eGFR, TFTs and calcium, and set a date to check the result","Recorded the 10:00 slot as was-not-brought and handed same-day follow-up to a named duty worker","Kept each slot to 30 minutes by agreeing the patient's top priority in the first two minutes","Dictated plain-English letters to patients, copied to GPs, with any dose change written in words and figures"],"snip":[{"tag":"Question","p":"\"I feel fine, so why do I need a blood test? I hate needles.\"","r":"Feeling fine is exactly what we want, and the level helps keep it that way. Lithium can creep up without warning. One test, about 12 hours after your evening dose, and we can settle into regular checks."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not hanging around for bloods today. I've got work.\"","r":"Your time matters, I understand. Could we book the first slot tomorrow morning, roughly 12 hours after tonight's dose? I'll write it on a card now and ring you with the result."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've been taking ibuprofen most days for my back.\"","r":"Thanks for telling me, that's really important. Ibuprofen can push lithium up to unsafe levels. Let's swap to paracetamol for now, and I'll ask your GP about other options for your back."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"My hands have been shaky and I've had the runs this week.\"","r":"I'm glad you mentioned it, because that can mean lithium is running high. I want the level checked today. If you start vomiting, feel unsteady or muddled, go to A&E the same day."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"He just didn't want to come. That's his choice, isn't it?\"","r":"It might be, and his choices matter. But he relies on you to get here, so I need to know he was told and offered help. Could you check with him today and ring me back?"},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"We're short-staffed. Nobody was free to bring him.\"","r":"That sounds really stretched, and I'm not blaming you. It does mean he missed care through no fault of his own. Our duty worker will call this afternoon to rebook and agree who brings him."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can't the GP just do everything? Coming here is a hassle.\"","r":"I hear that the trip is a hassle. Your GP can take the bloods under a shared-care plan, and I'll set out clearly in my letter who checks what and when, so nothing slips."}],"bps":{"bio":["Lithium level 12 h post-dose (usual target 0.6–0.8 mmol/L); U&E/eGFR and TFTs; calcium and weight at least yearly","Levels every 3 months in year one, then 6-monthly (stay 3-monthly if older, on interacting drugs or with renal or thyroid risk)","Stop regular ibuprofen; paracetamol instead; ask GP to avoid ACE inhibitors, ARBs and thiazides where possible"],"psych":["Agree his personal early-warning signs (eg sleep loss, overspending) and write them into the clinic letter","Offer bipolar psychoeducation (group or individual) through the CMHT if he has not had it"],"social":["CMHT duty worker phones the supported housing manager today and rebooks within 2 weeks with a named staff escort","Ask whether his social worker's Care Act review is due and whether anything has changed at home"],"safety":["Was-not-brought recorded and the GP told in writing","Adult safeguarding concern to the local authority if neglect or exploitation is suspected; inform the trust safeguarding team","Lithium patient told toxicity signs and to attend A&E the same day if they appear","Brief the consultant on return: risks, dose changes, the missed patient"]},"facts":[{"h":"Lithium level: 12 hours post-dose","x":"Once-daily evening dose means a morning sample about 12 hours later. Usual target 0.6–0.8 mmol/L. Toxic symptoms or a level of 1.5 or more need urgent medical review."},{"h":"Three drug groups push lithium up","x":"NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors or ARBs, and thiazide diuretics all cut renal lithium clearance, as do vomiting, diarrhoea and hot weather. Ask about over-the-counter ibuprofen every time."},{"h":"Monitoring rhythm for lithium","x":"Level every 3 months in the first year, then every 6 months. U&E/eGFR and thyroid function every 6 months. Keep 3-monthly if older or on interacting drugs."},{"h":"Was not brought is not a choice","x":"An adult who depends on carers to attend has not opted out. Chase the carer the same day and think safeguarding before any discharge back to the GP."},{"h":"Write doses in words and figures","x":"Write to the patient in plain English and copy in the GP: drug, dose, timing, what changed, who prescribes and who monitors. Skip abbreviations like OD and BD."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Plan and run the clinic safely, act on the missed appointment, and agree what each letter contains."},"wc-02":{"obj":["Recorded his main goal for the next six months in his own words","Listed what he dislikes about the injection and agreed one change to try","Written his three early warning signs and who to ring into the crisis plan","Referred him to IPS employment support and his mother for a carer's assessment","Confirmed s117 aftercare on the record and set a dated review in 3 months"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I've been well for a year. I don't need the jab any more.\"","r":"A year well is a real achievement, and the injection is part of that. Whether to stop is your decision. Can we look first at what bothers you about it, then plan any change together?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will the injection stop me holding down a job?\"","r":"Plenty of people work while on an injection. If tiredness is the problem, we can look at the dose, the timing or a different medicine. An employment specialist can help you find and keep work."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Last time it was the stress that made me ill, not stopping the tablets.\"","r":"Stress definitely played a part. Your notes also show you became unwell a few months after stopping. Can we agree the early signs, so we catch it fast if it happens again?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't keep doing this. I'm not sleeping, I'm worn out.\"","r":"You've been carrying a lot, and it shows how much you care. You have your own right to an assessment from social services. Can I refer you today and give you the carers' centre number?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He goes quiet and stops eating before he goes downhill.\"","r":"That's really useful, thank you. You often notice first. With his agreement, I'll put those signs into the plan, with the number to ring if you see them."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"If I tell you about side effects, you'll just put me back in hospital.\"","r":"I can see why you'd worry. Side effects are a reason to adjust treatment, not to admit you. Telling us helps us get the medicine right for you."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to pay for all this help now I'm off the section?\"","r":"No. Because you were on section 3, your aftercare is free under section 117. It carries on until health and social care agree with you that you no longer need it."}],"bps":{"bio":["Side-effect check: sedation, weight, sexual function, movement; weight, BP, HbA1c, lipids, prolactin","Consultant medication review within 2 weeks: lower dose, longer interval, or switch to a less sedating long-acting injection (eg aripiprazole 400 mg monthly)","If he chooses to stop: planned gradual reduction, never abrupt, with monthly contact for at least 6 months"],"psych":["Relapse-prevention plan with the care coordinator: written relapse signature and actions","Offer family intervention with his mother (NICE: at least 10 sessions over 3–12 months)"],"social":["Care coordinator refers to the IPS employment specialist this week","Carer's assessment for his mother via adult social care, plus local carers' centre","Welfare benefits check before he starts work"],"safety":["Crisis plan with crisis line number shared with GP and mother (with consent)","s117 status confirmed with the local authority","Review in 3 months, sooner if early signs appear"]},"facts":[{"h":"Section 117 aftercare is free","x":"Applies after s3, s37, s45A, s47 or s48. No charges for services meeting needs arising from the disorder. It ends only by joint ICB and local authority decision."},{"h":"Off section, no CTO: he decides","x":"A capacitous informal patient can stop his injection. Your task is an informed choice: past relapse pattern, alternatives, and a monitored plan, all recorded."},{"h":"IPS: place first, then train","x":"Individual Placement and Support gets people into real paid jobs quickly and supports them in work. NICE recommends supported employment for people with psychosis who want to work."},{"h":"Carers have their own legal right","x":"Care Act 2014 s10: any adult carer who appears to need support is entitled to a local authority assessment, whatever the cared-for person's eligibility."},{"h":"Check the body every year","x":"Weight, waist, BP, HbA1c, lipids, smoking and alcohol at least yearly. People with severe mental illness die 15–20 years earlier, mostly from physical illness."}],"type":"Family meeting","do":"Run a person-centred CPA review covering goals, medication, risk, carer needs and section 117 aftercare."},"wc-03":{"obj":["Logged the visit with base: address, time in, call-back time and a code word for trouble","Asked directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and stored tablets","Checked the home for self-neglect, means of suicide, fire risk and dependants","Agreed a same-day plan (crisis team referral or next contact date) before leaving the street","Updated the risk record and alert with what you found"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Why are you here? I never asked anyone to come.\"","r":"Fair enough, it's a surprise to see us. We came because we hadn't heard from you and we were worried. Ten minutes to check you're alright, then we'll go if you want."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm fine. I just haven't felt like answering the phone.\"","r":"Thanks for telling me. Not wanting to answer can be a sign things feel heavy. How have your days been, and what's been getting you through them?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Some days I think everyone would be better off without me.\"","r":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that; it sounds really painful. Have you thought about how you might end your life, or done anything to get ready?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"You're not taking my tablets. I need them.\"","r":"You do need them, and I'm not taking them away. I'm worried about a big supply in the house while you feel this low. Could we keep a week here and let the pharmacy hold the rest?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is the dog a problem? He's all I've got.\"","r":"He clearly means a lot to you. Could you shut him in the kitchen while we talk, so we can concentrate? Then I'd like to hear more about what keeps you going."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"A few cans help me sleep. It's not a problem.\"","r":"It makes sense to reach for something that helps. Alcohol also deepens low mood and makes acting on dark thoughts more likely. How many cans most days? Would you see the alcohol team?"}],"bps":{"bio":["GP to review antidepressant and issue 7-day prescriptions while risk is raised","GP bloods: FBC, LFTs, GGT, U&E, B12/folate, TFTs given drinking and self-neglect","Oral thiamine (often 100 mg three times a day) if drinking heavily with poor diet"],"psych":["Crisis resolution and home treatment team daily visits if risk is raised","Co-written safety plan; behavioural activation or CBT via NHS Talking Therapies once stable"],"social":["Care coordinator requests a Care Act needs assessment if he cannot manage food, heating or hygiene","Refer to the local drug and alcohol service; food bank voucher if the fridge is empty"],"safety":["Lone-working: visit in pairs, call base at agreed times, leave at once if unsafe","Means restriction with consent: pharmacy holds supply, weekly dispensing","Raise self-neglect with the local authority if care needs leave him unable to protect himself","Next contact within 24–72 hours; risk alert updated"]},"facts":[{"h":"Never let anyone block the door","x":"Sit nearest the exit, keep phone and keys on you, ask for pets to be shut away. If voices rise or others turn up, end the visit and leave."},{"h":"Police can enter to save life","x":"PACE s17(1)(e) lets police force entry to save life or limb. If entry is refused for a Mental Health Act assessment, an AMHP applies to a magistrate for a s135(1) warrant."},{"h":"Read the fridge and the post","x":"Out-of-date food, piles of unopened letters, a cold house and empty bottles tell you more about self-neglect and drinking than any rating scale."},{"h":"Ask about stored tablets every time","x":"Stockpiled paracetamol and antidepressants are a common means. Ask where, how many, and agree safe storage or weekly dispensing with the GP."},{"h":"Self-neglect counts as safeguarding","x":"Care Act guidance lists self-neglect, including hoarding and neglecting hygiene or health. A s42 enquiry follows if he has care needs and cannot protect himself."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Plan a safe lone home visit, assess risk and the home environment, and decide next steps."},"wc-04":{"obj":["Made a duty-to-refer to housing with his consent before confirming the discharge date","Agreed a written safety plan naming two people he can call and the crisis line","Booked face-to-face follow-up within 72 hours with a named team, date and time","Limited take-home medicines to 7 days and asked the GP for weekly prescriptions","Got the discharge summary to the GP inside 24 hours"],"snip":[{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Can I just go today? I feel loads better.\"","r":"I'm really glad you feel better; that's real progress. The first days home are the riskiest, so I want housing and follow-up sorted first. Give me until Friday to set it up properly."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"She wants me out. I've got nowhere to go.\"","r":"That's a huge blow on top of everything. We won't send you out with nowhere to go. With your agreement I'll refer you to the council's housing team today, and our social worker will help."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why only a week of tablets? It's a pain going back to the GP.\"","r":"I know it's a hassle. After an overdose we keep supplies small for a while to keep you safe. Your GP will do weekly scripts, and we'll review that once you're steadier."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"Those thoughts have gone. I wouldn't do that again.\"","r":"I'm glad they've eased. Thoughts can return under stress, like moving out. Let's write down what you'd notice first and what you'd do, just in case."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"Don't tell my partner anything. It's over.\"","r":"That's your choice and I'll respect it. Is there someone else, a friend or relative, you'd like to know your plan and be someone you can call?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"What if I end up back in here?\"","r":"That fear makes sense. Coming back wouldn't be failure; it would mean you got help. Your plan shows who to ring early, so things are less likely to get that bad."}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue the antidepressant at the current dose (eg sertraline 100 mg each morning); 7 days' take-home supply only","GP weekly prescriptions for at least the first month; avoid tricyclics; no large paracetamol stocks at home"],"psych":["Safety plan completed with his named nurse before discharge and copied to him, GP and community team","Refer for CBT for depression (NHS Talking Therapies or CMHT psychology)"],"social":["Ward social worker: duty to refer to his chosen housing authority; accommodation confirmed before he leaves","Debt and benefits advice (Citizens Advice); involve his chosen friend or relative with consent"],"safety":["Face-to-face follow-up within 72 hours, named team and date in the record","Crisis line and NHS 111 (option 2) given in writing","If follow-up is missed: phone the same day, then welfare check by police if not found"]},"facts":[{"h":"The first week home is riskiest","x":"Suicide risk after psychiatric discharge peaks in the first days and stays high for weeks. National guidance expects follow-up within 72 hours."},{"h":"Duty to refer needs his consent","x":"Hospitals must refer anyone homeless, or facing it within 56 days, to a housing authority he chooses, but only with consent. Record the referral or his refusal."},{"h":"Expected discharge date by day 3","x":"Statutory discharge guidance expects an expected discharge date shared within 72 hours of admission and planning from day one, with carers involved."},{"h":"Small supplies after overdose","x":"Give 7 days' take-home medicines and ask the GP for weekly scripts. Prefer drugs safer in overdose; avoid tricyclics after an overdose."},{"h":"GP summary within 24 hours","x":"Send it the same or next day: diagnosis, medicines with changes, risk, safety plan, and who follows up and when."}],"type":"Management plan","do":"Plan discharge covering housing, medication, crisis plan and communication, with follow-up within 72 hours."},"wc-05":{"obj":["Fixed a protected weekly supervision hour in both diaries","Listed the WPBAs needed before ARCP with named assessors and dates","Launched the mini-PAT with 10–12 nominated assessors from mixed roles","Agreed who to tell (college tutor, then TPD) if supervision is cancelled again","Chosen a Paper A sitting and a weekly study plan"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"I'm swamped. Can we just catch up on the ward instead?\"","r":"I know how busy you are, and the ward chats help. I do need an hour a week recorded for my training. Could we hold Tuesday at 14:00, and rebook within the same week if it moves?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What evidence have you actually got so far?\"","r":"Three WPBAs and some reflections. I've mapped the gaps against the HLOs and drafted a plan: two CbDs, one ACE and the mini-PAT this fortnight. Does that cover what you need?"},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"ARCP is ages away. Don't worry about it yet.\"","r":"Thanks, that's reassuring. Six weeks goes quickly, and the mini-PAT alone can take weeks to fill. Could we launch it today so it isn't a last-minute problem?"},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Send me the forms and I'll sign them all off in one go.\"","r":"I appreciate that. Panels look for assessments spread across the post, with feedback I've acted on. Could we do one CbD now on Monday's patient and book the next two?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Have you started your psychotherapy case yet?\"","r":"Not yet, and I know it's a core requirement. Could you put me in touch with the medical psychotherapy tutor, so I can start a short case and join a Balint group this term?"},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"Honestly, I'm exhausted and behind on everything. I think I'm failing.\"","r":"That sounds really hard, and lots of residents feel this in their first post. You're not failing; you've spotted the problem. Shall we write a plan together and look at support like NHS Practitioner Health?"}],"facts":[{"h":"One protected hour every week","x":"Core residents are entitled to an hour of weekly consultant supervision. Keep a log of dates and cancellations; if the pattern continues, raise it with the college tutor."},{"h":"Mini-PAT needs a head start","x":"Nominate 10–12 colleagues from mixed roles to reach the minimum six valid responses. Launch early: replies trickle in over weeks."},{"h":"Outcome 5 means missing evidence","x":"It is not a fail. Upload what is missing within the time the panel sets and the outcome is reviewed."},{"h":"Tag every piece to an HLO","x":"The 2022 curriculum is outcome-based. Link each WPBA and reflection to a High Level Outcome so the panel can see coverage at a glance."},{"h":"MRCPsych before ST4","x":"All parts must be passed before higher training. Check the college's application windows months ahead; they close well before each sitting."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"Fix supervision gaps, map portfolio evidence for ARCP, and set a dated study plan for MRCPsych papers."},"wc-06":{"obj":["Confirmed the van is under 3.5 tonnes (Group 1) and whether he holds any Group 2 entitlement","Advised him to stop driving today and to notify the DVLA himself, recorded in the notes","Explained the usual 3-month well period before relicensing","Agreed a date by which he will show confirmation he has notified","Set out kindly the steps you will take if he keeps driving"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"So I lose my job because of you?\"","r":"I can hear how frightening that is. It's the DVLA's rule, not my personal decision, and it's there to keep you and others safe. Let's think about your employer, other duties, and getting your licence back."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"What if I only drive short trips round town?\"","r":"I understand wanting to keep some independence. The rule covers all driving after mania, not just long trips. Most people are back driving after about three months well. Let's aim for that."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do you tell the DVLA, or do I?\"","r":"It's your legal duty to tell them, and you can do it online. I'll support you, and I'll complete any medical report they send me promptly."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He drove the kids to school this morning after no sleep.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me; I know that wasn't easy to say. I'm worried about that too. I'll talk with him about it now, and with his agreement keep you involved."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I'm completely well now. The mania's gone.\"","r":"You do seem much better, and that's great. The DVLA asks for a period of stability, usually three months, because relapse is most likely early on. It's about time well, not how you feel today."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm not telling them. They'll never find out.\"","r":"I understand the worry. If anything happened, you could be prosecuted and your insurance may not cover you. If you keep driving I'd have to tell the DVLA myself, and I'd tell you first. I'd much rather you did it."}],"bps":{"bio":["Continue his mood stabiliser; check for sedation (eg quetiapine, olanzapine) before any return to driving","Advise no alcohol before driving; prescribed drugs fall under drug-driving law if they impair him"],"psych":["Care coordinator: written relapse signature and a regular sleep routine","Offer a bipolar psychoeducation group; agree what his wife watches for"],"social":["Discuss non-driving duties with his employer or occupational health; GP fit note if needed","Welfare benefits advice if income falls"],"safety":["Advice recorded in the notes and the clinic letter to him and the GP","Care coordinator checks DVLA confirmation within 2 weeks","If still driving: consultant discussion, then DVLA medical adviser, telling him before and writing to him after"]},"facts":[{"h":"A small van is still Group 1","x":"Vans up to 3.5 tonnes driven on a car (category B) licence are Group 1. Lorries and buses are Group 2, with much longer off-road periods after mania or psychosis."},{"h":"After mania: off the road, then 3 months","x":"Stop driving and notify. Group 1 relicensing usually follows 3 months of stability, or 6 months if he has had four or more mood episodes in 12 months."},{"h":"Not notifying can cost £1,000","x":"Failing to tell the DVLA about a relevant condition risks a fine of up to £1,000, and prosecution if he is involved in an accident."},{"h":"Advise, persuade, warn, then disclose","x":"Tell him his duty; try to persuade; say you will inform the DVLA if he continues; do so promptly with senior input; then write to him confirming it."},{"h":"Ask about drowsiness on tablets","x":"Antipsychotics and sedating mood stabilisers slow reaction times. Even once the waiting period ends, advise no driving while drowsy, especially after a dose rise."}],"type":"Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"Advise him on driving and DVLA notification, and decide what to do if he keeps driving."},"wc-07":{"obj":["Scored withdrawal with a named scale (COWS for opioids, CIWA-Ar for alcohol or benzodiazepines)","Completed a full self-harm risk assessment before deciding on prescribing","Declined controlled-drug replacement clearly and offered a concrete alternative","Checked the Summary Care Record and flagged multiple attendances to the GP and drug service","Recorded the reasoning so the next clinician can uphold the decision"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"This is a joke. I'll go and get it off the street then.\"","r":"I can see how angry you are, and I don't want you buying street drugs tonight; that's dangerous. Let me check you properly for withdrawal, treat what I find, and get your drug team to see you tomorrow."},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"Just give me enough for tonight. Ten milligrams, that's all.\"","r":"I hear that you want to get through tonight. Without checking with your prescriber, even one dose could stack on what you've already taken. I can ease sickness and sweats now and sort the rest first thing."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"If I walk out with nothing, I'm going to cut myself.\"","r":"I'm taking that seriously. Tell me what's been happening that makes you feel like hurting yourself. That matters whatever we decide about tablets."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"My GP's away. Who else can prescribe for me?\"","r":"In the morning I'll ask your pharmacy and GP practice to confirm your dose, and the duty doctor there can prescribe. I'll put it in my notes so they're expecting you."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Truth is, I've been buying extra pregabalin and taking it with heroin.\"","r":"Thank you for being honest; that helps me keep you safe. That mix can stop your breathing. Can we give you a naloxone kit tonight and link you with the drug service tomorrow?"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Other hospitals always give me diazepam.\"","r":"I know that's happened before, and it's frustrating when places differ. Tonight I can only give what I can safely check. Here's what I can do instead."}],"bps":{"bio":["Score withdrawal; treat objective signs only. Stat symptom relief: loperamide 4 mg for diarrhoea, metoclopramide 10 mg for vomiting; no take-home supply","Objective alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal: treat under the trust protocol and keep him for observation (seizure risk)","No controlled drugs or opioid substitution until dose and last dispensing are confirmed","Take-home naloxone and overdose advice"],"psych":["Brief motivational conversation about his use and what he wants to change","Offer a named keyworker and psychosocial support at the drug service"],"social":["Next-day drug and alcohol service referral plus self-referral number","Ask him to report the theft to police and bring the crime reference number"],"safety":["Risk assessment documented; written safety plan and crisis line","GP and dispensing pharmacy told about repeated attendances","Incident report if diversion is suspected; liaison consultant informed in the morning"]},"facts":[{"h":"Opioid withdrawal: miserable, rarely deadly","x":"Treat the symptoms and arrange next-day prescribing. A methadone dose on an unverified history can kill, because tolerance falls within days."},{"h":"Benzo and alcohol withdrawal can fit","x":"Unlike opioids, these can cause seizures and delirium. Look for tremor, sweating, tachycardia and agitation; score it and treat objective withdrawal under protocol."},{"h":"Pregabalin is a controlled drug","x":"Pregabalin and gabapentin became Class C, Schedule 3 drugs in April 2019. Taken with opioids they sharply raise the risk of fatal breathing depression."},{"h":"Verify before anything controlled","x":"Check the Summary Care Record, ask for the crime reference, and in hours ring the dispensing pharmacy for the dose and last pick-up."},{"h":"State the limit, then offer something","x":"Present the refusal as a rule you cannot override, not a personal choice. Then offer something real: symptom relief, a booked drug-service slot, naloxone and a written plan."}],"type":"Medication review","do":"Assess him, treat genuine withdrawal or risk safely, and decline unsafe prescribing without escalating conflict."},"wc-08":{"obj":["Reassessed suicide risk once he was sober enough to engage","Screened for depression, alcohol dependence, head injury and cognitive problems","Made the duty-to-refer with consent and spoken to the out-of-hours housing team","Asked whether any children or dependants are affected","Agreed a written plan for tonight and follow-up within 72 hours"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"So you're just going to throw me back out in the cold?\"","r":"No. I can hear how scary tonight feels. A psychiatric ward won't fix housing, but I'm calling the council's night team now to push for a bed tonight. Will you let me make that referral?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I've had nothing to eat in two days, just cider.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. Let's get you food and a hot drink now. How much cider most days, and when was your last? I want to make sure you don't go into withdrawal."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I didn't mean it about ending it. I was drunk.\"","r":"I'm glad you feel steadier. Things said when drunk can still matter. Now you're sober, can we talk about how bad things have got and whether those thoughts come at other times?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want the council knowing my business.\"","r":"That's fair; it's your information. I can only send the referral with your agreement. It just tells them you need housing and how to reach you. Without it, a bed tonight is much harder."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I lost the flat because of my drinking. It's all my fault.\"","r":"You sound really hard on yourself. Drinking is a health problem, and help works. Would you see the alcohol team this week if I set it up?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can't you just say I'm mental so I get a flat?\"","r":"I understand why you'd ask. I'll write honestly about your mental health and how vulnerable you are, which does count when the council decides. I can't overstate it, though."}],"bps":{"bio":["CIWA-Ar 4-hourly; chlordiazepoxide reducing regimen only if objective withdrawal and he stays under supervision","Oral thiamine; IV Pabrinex if confusion, ataxia or eye signs","Look for head injury, hypothermia and infection before calling him medically fit"],"psych":["Brief alcohol intervention tonight using his own reasons to cut down","Community mental health review for depression once he is sober and housed"],"social":["Duty to refer with consent; out-of-hours housing for interim accommodation or a severe-weather bed","Homeless health team or Pathway where available; GP registration without a fixed address","Any affected child: out-of-hours children's social care tonight"],"safety":["Full risk reassessment once sober, documented","If risk persists, crisis team sees him within 72 hours","Care Act safeguarding concern if exploitation or self-neglect"]},"facts":[{"h":"No address, still gets a GP","x":"Practices cannot refuse registration for lack of an address or ID. The practice or a day centre address can be used for letters."},{"h":"Mental illness can mean priority need","x":"Councils must offer interim housing where they have reason to think he is homeless, eligible and in priority need. Being significantly more vulnerable through mental illness can qualify; your letter matters."},{"h":"Freezing nights open emergency beds","x":"Councils activate the Severe Weather Emergency Protocol when freezing temperatures are forecast, offering beds regardless of usual eligibility. Ask the out-of-hours line if it is running tonight."},{"h":"Reassess once sober","x":"Intoxication distorts mood and intent. Ask about suicide again when he is clinically sober; a retracted threat still needs a full risk review."},{"h":"Thiamine for every heavy drinker","x":"Malnourished dependent drinkers need oral thiamine. Confusion, unsteadiness or eye signs mean IV Pabrinex straight away."}],"type":"Risk assessment","do":"Assess his mental health and risk, then plan tonight using housing and social care, not a bed."},"wc-09":{"obj":["Scored catatonia on the Bush-Francis scale before and after lorazepam","Checked temperature, pulse, BP, CK and U&E for malignant features or NMS","Held all antipsychotics and given a lorazepam challenge, recording the response","Started fluid balance, NEWS2, a VTE assessment and pressure-area care","Escalated to the consultant and medical team with an ECT plan if lorazepam fails"],"snip":[{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"She's just being awkward. She spoke fine yesterday.\"","r":"I can see why it looks that way. Staring, posturing and resisting being moved are signs of catatonia, which is treatable. Her pulse of 112 worries me. Can we do a full set of obs now?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is she going to stay like this forever?\"","r":"I can see how frightening this is. Catatonia usually responds well to treatment, often within days. We're starting a medicine called lorazepam today and watching her physical health closely."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Why stop her antipsychotic? Isn't that what she needs?\"","r":"That's a sensible question. In catatonia, antipsychotics can make things worse or trigger a dangerous reaction. We're pausing it while lorazepam works, and the consultant will decide when to restart."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I couldn't move. I was so scared.\"","r":"That sounds terrifying, and thank you for telling me. Your body got stuck, and the medicine is helping. Can you take a sip of water with me now?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want her having electric shocks.\"","r":"Many people worry about that, often from films. ECT is done under anaesthetic and is one of the most effective treatments for catatonia. It's only if lorazepam isn't enough, and we'll talk it through fully."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can we give lorazepam if she won't swallow?\"","r":"Yes, by injection. She's detained and this treats her mental disorder. Give 2 mg IM, then rescore with the scale at 30 minutes and ring me with the change."}],"bps":{"bio":["Lorazepam challenge 1–2 mg IM or IV; rescore after 15–30 minutes; if she responds, regular lorazepam (eg 1–2 mg three or four times daily), titrated up under consultant advice","Bloods: CK, U&E, FBC, LFTs, glucose, CRP; ECG; ask medics about MRI, EEG, LP and NMDA-receptor antibodies if atypical","Hold all antipsychotics; IV fluids via the medical team; VTE assessment and prophylaxis if indicated; NEWS2 at least 4-hourly"],"psych":["Calm, low-stimulus nursing with one familiar nurse at a time","Explain each step to her aloud even though she is mute; she may hear and remember"],"social":["Update her nearest relative and family today; explain catatonia and the plan","MHA office alerted early in case a SOAD or s62 is needed"],"safety":["Enhanced observation; falls and pressure-area care; fluid and food charts","Temperature 38 or above, rising CK or swinging BP: urgent medical transfer","Early ECT referral if no response within days or any malignant feature"]},"facts":[{"h":"A lorazepam response confirms it","x":"Improvement within hours of a challenge supports catatonia. Most patients respond; little change after adequate dosing over a few days means ECT referral."},{"h":"Fever plus rigidity: think NMS","x":"Fever, rigidity, autonomic swings and high CK may be NMS or malignant catatonia. Stop antipsychotics, call the medics and treat it as an emergency."},{"h":"Mute does not mean stable","x":"Two days without food or fluid brings dehydration, AKI, VTE and pressure damage. Start fluid and food charts and NEWS2 within the first hour."},{"h":"s62 for urgent ECT","x":"ECT without consent under s58A needs a SOAD certificate. If her life is at risk or serious deterioration is imminent, s62 allows treatment meanwhile; record why."},{"h":"Young woman, new catatonia: think encephalitis","x":"Fever, seizures, dyskinesia or falling consciousness suggest anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis. Ask the medics about MRI, EEG, LP and serum and CSF antibodies."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Recognise catatonia, exclude medical causes and malignant features, start a lorazepam challenge, and protect physical health."},"wc-10":{"obj":["Separated both patients and placed the alleged perpetrator on 1:1 on another corridor","Responded to her disclosure supportively without questioning her for details","Sealed her room and bagged her clothing, and asked her not to wash until police advise","Contacted police and the SARC with her agreement and raised a safeguarding concern","Recorded her own words, the times and who was told"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Nobody's going to believe me. They'll say I'm mad.\"","r":"I'm treating what you've told me as serious, and your diagnosis doesn't change that. He is being kept well away from you, and you're safe now."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I just want a shower. I feel disgusting.\"","r":"That feeling makes complete sense. Waiting a little while may help the police keep evidence, but it's your choice. Can I get you a fresh blanket and someone to sit with you?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will he be moved? I can't be on the same ward as him.\"","r":"You won't have to be near him. Tonight he has a member of staff with him at all times on another corridor, and the consultant is reviewing a move. I'll tell you what's decided."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want the police. I just want it to stop.\"","r":"I hear you, and you have a say. Can I explain what police involvement would mean, and other support, so you can decide? If others might be at risk, I may still need to share information, and I'd tell you."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"She's lying. I never went near her room.\"","r":"I'm not deciding what happened; others will look into that. For everyone's safety you'll have staff with you tonight. How are you feeling right now?"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"How could this happen on a locked ward?\"","r":"You're right to be angry, and I'm so sorry this happened while she was in our care. It has been reported to safeguarding and the police. With her permission we'll keep you updated."}],"bps":{"bio":["SARC advice on forensic timing (sooner is better; evidence can last up to 7 days)","Emergency contraception: levonorgestrel 1.5 mg within 72 h or ulipristal 30 mg within 120 h","HIV PEP as soon as possible, ideally within 24 h and no later than 72 h, if penetration; STI screen"],"psych":["Trauma-informed support tonight; review her mental state and self-harm risk","ISVA via the SARC; psychology follow-up on the ward"],"social":["Tell family only with her consent","Safeguarding concern to the local authority; trust safeguarding lead informed"],"safety":["Alleged perpetrator: 1:1, consider ward move or PICU, review s17 leave, his own risk review","Female staff with her; observation level reviewed for support","Incident report and on-call manager; review same-sex accommodation; staff debrief"]},"facts":[{"h":"Listen, don't interview","x":"Write down her exact words, the time and who was present. Avoid probing or leading questions; repeated retelling can harm her and any prosecution."},{"h":"Paper bags, sealed room","x":"Put worn clothing and bedding into separate paper bags, not plastic. Stop cleaning of the room. Ask her not to wash or change until police advise."},{"h":"Emergency contraception clocks","x":"Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg works within 72 hours; ulipristal 30 mg within 120 hours. The SARC or sexual health clinic advises on the best option."},{"h":"PEP: within 72 hours","x":"After penetrative assault, HIV post-exposure prophylaxis should start as soon as possible, ideally within 24 hours and never later than 72. The SARC or A&E assesses need."},{"h":"Both patients need care tonight","x":"The alleged perpetrator also needs a mental state and risk review and staff with him. Do not discuss the allegation's details with him."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Make everyone safe, preserve evidence, and start the police and safeguarding response to the allegation."},"wc-11":{"obj":["Started CPR and got the AED on within the first minute","Allocated roles: compressions, airway, defibrillator, door, scribe and other patients","Handed over to paramedics with times, rhythm, shocks and his MHA status","Preserved the ligature, the room and the observation records","Arranged care for other patients and a staff debrief before the end of the shift"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I checked him fifteen minutes ago. This is my fault.\"","r":"I can see how shaken you are. You did your checks and you helped get him down. Right now, can you meet the ambulance at the door? We'll talk properly afterwards; you won't be on your own."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Shouldn't we keep his neck still before starting?\"","r":"Good thought, but no delay. Start compressions now and keep his head in line as best you can. Circulation and breathing come first."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Is he dead? Is someone going to do that to me?\"","r":"Something serious has happened and staff are helping him. You are safe. Can you go with this nurse to the lounge? We'll tell you more when we can."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Is he on a section? Can he come with us?\"","r":"Yes, he's on section 3, and yes, take him now. A nurse is coming with you with his papers. Please don't let that slow you down."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"You were supposed to be watching him!\"","r":"You have every right to be angry, and I'm so sorry this happened. I'll tell you what we know so far. There will be a full review, and we'll share what it finds with you."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Should we have had him on one-to-one?\"","r":"That's a fair question, and the review will look at it. For now, check in with each other and take the support offered. The aim is learning, not blame."}],"bps":{"bio":["Survivor: A&E for airway assessment, neck imaging and chest X-ray; watch for delayed airway swelling and pulmonary oedema","Same night: ligature-point and observation-level review for every patient"],"psych":["Hot debrief for staff within hours; wellbeing or TRiM follow-up","Community meeting for patients the next day; screen others for copycat risk"],"social":["Senior clinician contacts the family; named family liaison","MHA office told in the morning"],"safety":["Scene secured for police; ligature bagged","Incident report and PSIRF response","If he dies: coroner and CQC notification without delay"]},"facts":[{"h":"No collar before compressions","x":"Neck injury is uncommon in low-drop ward hangings. Start CPR at once and keep the head in line by hand if someone is free."},{"h":"Cut away from the knot","x":"Keep the knot intact for police and the coroner. Cut the ligature elsewhere, bag it, and note who cut it and when."},{"h":"Survivors still go to A&E","x":"Even if he wakes quickly, airway swelling, aspiration and pulmonary oedema can appear hours later. Send him with an escort and his MHA papers."},{"h":"Name a scribe in minute one","x":"Record times found, cut down, CPR started, shocks and arrivals. Your statement, the inquest and the review all depend on it."},{"h":"Compressions: rate and depth","x":"Adults: 100–120 per minute, 5–6 cm deep, full recoil, minimal pauses. Swap compressors every 2 minutes at rhythm checks."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Lead the first minutes of resuscitation after hanging, then manage reporting, debrief and family contact."},"wc-12":{"obj":["Confirmed death after five minutes' observation and recorded the time","Reported the death to the coroner and did not issue a death certificate","Asked the MHA office to notify CQC","Told the nearest relative, with a senior, using his name and plain words","Secured notes, drug chart, observation records and clozapine bloods, and booked a staff debrief"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"No, he was fine yesterday. You've got the wrong person.\"","r":"I'm so sorry. It is your dad, and I know this is a terrible shock. He was seen settled at five and found not breathing just after six. Is someone with you right now?"},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Why wasn't anyone checking on him?\"","r":"I understand you want answers, and you deserve them. He was on hourly checks. The coroner will investigate, and the hospital will review what happened and share it with you."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can we see him? Can the funeral be this week?\"","r":"Yes, we'll help you see him. Because he was detained, the coroner must be involved, which can affect timing. The coroner's officer will call you and can advise on religious needs."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What did he die of?\"","r":"I honestly don't know yet, and I won't guess. The coroner will arrange tests, usually a post-mortem, to find out. We'll tell you as soon as we know more."},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Did he die from that medicine? I'm on it too.\"","r":"I can see why that would worry you. We don't know the cause yet. Your own medicine is closely monitored; we can check your bloods and talk it through today."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"I should have checked him at half five.\"","r":"I can hear how much you're carrying. He was on hourly checks and you followed them. The review is about learning, not blame. Let's get you a break and some support."}],"bps":{"bio":["Bowel review for every ward patient on clozapine: bowel chart, stimulant plus softener laxative prescribed proactively","Check other clozapine patients' latest blood counts and any chest pain, fever or breathlessness"],"psych":["Staff debrief by 08:00 and wellbeing follow-up over the next weeks","Patients' meeting with time to talk; extra checks on anyone close to him"],"social":["Senior contact with the family: bereavement leaflet, coroner's officer and PALS details","Offer a meeting with the consultant and help with cultural or religious needs"],"safety":["Coroner (via police out of hours), CQC via the MHA office, incident report under PSIRF","Records, drug chart and monitoring results secured for the coroner"]},"facts":[{"h":"Detained death: always the coroner","x":"Any death during MHA detention is a death in state detention, whatever the cause. Report it to the coroner; do not write an MCCD or refer to the medical examiner service."},{"h":"Confirming death takes five minutes","x":"Observe 5 minutes for no central pulse, heart or breath sounds. Then check fixed pupils, absent corneal reflexes and no response to supraorbital pressure. Record the time."},{"h":"Leave the body as found","x":"Do not remove lines, tubes or pads, or tidy the room, until the coroner or police agree. Close the room."},{"h":"Clozapine slows the gut","x":"Clozapine can cause fatal bowel obstruction. Ask about bowels at every review and prescribe a stimulant plus softener laxative early; avoid bulk-forming laxatives."},{"h":"Say 'died' and use his name","x":"Avoid euphemisms. Warn, give the news in one sentence, then pause. Do not guess the cause or say 'suicide' before the coroner decides."}],"type":"Documentation and reporting","do":"Confirm death, notify the coroner and CQC for a detained patient, inform the family, and support staff."},"wc-13":{"obj":["Asked directly about intent, plan, means, timing and the named man","Checked past violence, alcohol withdrawal and psychotic drivers such as morbid jealousy","Discussed disclosure with the on-call consultant and shared only the minimum with police","Made a plan for if he tries to leave, including s5(4) and s5(2)","Supported the threatened nurse and logged the incident"],"snip":[{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"She's moved on with that loser. He's got what's coming.\"","r":"I can hear how hurt and angry you are; losing a relationship like that is painful. When you say he's got what's coming, what are you planning to do?"},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You can't tell anyone. That's confidential.\"","r":"Most of what you tell me stays private. If I believe someone could be badly hurt, I may need to pass on some details to protect them. I'd rather be open with you about that now."},{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"I was just letting off steam. I wouldn't actually do anything.\"","r":"I'm glad to hear that. You did mention his name and where he lives, so I need to understand more. Have you hurt anyone before, or been in trouble with police for it?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm leaving tonight. You can't stop me, I'm voluntary.\"","r":"You are informal, and I respect that. I'm worried about what might happen tonight. If you try to leave now, I'd need to consider the Mental Health Act to keep you here until you're assessed."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Am I going to get arrested?\"","r":"I can't promise what the police will do. If I share anything, it will be the minimum needed to keep someone safe, and I'll tell you what I've shared."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"He said I'd get what's coming. I don't feel safe here.\"","r":"That's frightening and not acceptable. Thank you for telling me. You won't work with him tonight. Let's log it, and you'll be offered support and the option to report it to police."}],"bps":{"bio":["CIWA-Ar; chlordiazepoxide reducing regimen if in withdrawal; oral thiamine","If agitation builds: oral lorazepam 1–2 mg first; IM only per rapid tranquillisation protocol","Screen for psychosis, including morbid jealousy; review antidepressant"],"psych":["De-escalation by a known nurse in a low-stimulus area","Structured violence risk formulation by the MDT; alcohol relapse-prevention work"],"social":["Safeguarding team: MARAC referral if domestic abuse; check MAPPA status","Police via 101 (999 if imminent); log the reference number"],"safety":["Enhanced observation; staff in pairs; never seen alone in a closed room","Leaving plan: nurse s5(4), doctor s5(2), MHA assessment","Incident report; support and debrief for the threatened nurse","MDT review on Monday; Caldicott Guardian informed"]},"facts":[{"h":"No duty to warn, but you may disclose","x":"UK law has no Tarasoff duty. GMC guidance permits disclosure without consent where there is risk of death or serious harm. Share minimum facts, with senior input, and record why."},{"h":"Holding powers: 6 and 72 hours","x":"Nurses' s5(4) holds an informal inpatient for up to 6 hours until a doctor arrives. Doctor's s5(2) lasts up to 72 hours for an MHA assessment."},{"h":"Specific threats carry weight","x":"A named victim, known address, plan, access and past violence matter far more than vague anger. Ask about each directly and write down his answers."},{"h":"Jealousy plus drink is a red flag","x":"Recent separation, sexual jealousy and heavy drinking together raise the risk of partner violence. Ask whether he checks on, follows or contacts his ex."},{"h":"Rapid tranquillisation: oral first","x":"Offer oral medication first. If IM is needed, use lorazepam alone or haloperidol with promethazine; haloperidol needs a prior ECG. Then check vital signs at least hourly."}],"type":"Risk assessment + Professional and ethical dilemma","do":"Assess the threats, secure ward safety, and decide whether and to whom to disclose to protect the named person."},"wc-14":{"obj":["Assessed capacity for the specific decision: transfer and IV antibiotics now","Screened for delirium with attention and orientation tests","Checked for an advance decision or health and welfare LPA and spoken to family","Used the MCA, not s63, for the infection and arranged escorted transfer under s17","Started what he would accept on the ward while transfer was organised"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"They'll poison me at that hospital. I know it.\"","r":"That sounds really frightening, and I can see why you'd want to stay away. Can I explain what the doctors are worried about, and what might happen if the leg isn't treated?"},{"tag":"Bargaining","p":"\"I'll take tablets here, but I'm not going there.\"","r":"Thank you, that's a real step and we can start tablets now. The medical team thinks you need medicine into a vein to beat this quickly. Would it help if a nurse you know came with you?"},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"What happens if I don't go?\"","r":"Good question. The infection could spread into your blood and make you very unwell, even kill you, possibly within a day. Can you tell me in your words what you understand of that?"},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"No. Leave me alone.\"","r":"I hear you, and I don't want to force anything. I need to check you can weigh up the risks. If these fears stop you doing that, we may need to act in your best interests."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"You can't make me. I know my rights.\"","r":"You're right that you have rights, and I respect them. If you can understand and weigh this decision, it's yours to make. My job is to check that, and to keep you safe if you can't."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"He's always hated hospitals. What would he want?\"","r":"That's exactly what we need to know. When he's been well, what has he said about medical treatment? Your view helps us decide what's in his best interests."}],"bps":{"bio":["NEWS2 hourly on the ward; bloods: FBC, CRP, U&E, lactate, blood cultures; mark the edge of the redness","Sepsis pathway in the acute hospital within the hour: cultures, IV antibiotics, IV fluids, lactate, urine output","If he accepts oral treatment meanwhile: flucloxacillin 500 mg to 1 g four times daily (clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily if penicillin-allergic)"],"psych":["Continue his antipsychotic as prescribed","Familiar staff and simple, repeated explanations to ease the poisoning fear"],"social":["Consult family or carers about his past wishes","IMCA if nobody suitable and serious decisions are non-urgent"],"safety":["RC or on-call consultant authorises s17 leave; RMN escorts with papers","Restraint only if necessary and proportionate (MCA s6), recorded","Acute team reassess capacity as he improves; written handover to the day team"]},"facts":[{"h":"s63 does not cover cellulitis","x":"s63 treats the mental disorder and its direct consequences. An unrelated infection needs consent or the MCA, with a decision-specific capacity assessment."},{"h":"Delusions can block weighing","x":"He may understand and repeat the facts yet be unable to weigh them because of a fixed belief about poison. Record the belief and exactly how it stops weighing."},{"h":"Rule out delirium first","x":"Sepsis causes delirium, which can remove capacity on its own. Use the 4AT: alertness, orientation, months backwards, acute change."},{"h":"Detained patients need s17 to transfer","x":"Going to an acute hospital needs s17 leave from the RC or on-call consultant, with escorts and papers. An emergency does not pause the MHA."},{"h":"Cellulitis: flucloxacillin first","x":"NICE: flucloxacillin 500 mg to 1 g four times daily for 5–7 days; clarithromycin if penicillin-allergic. Signs of sepsis mean IV treatment in hospital."}],"type":"Capacity assessment","do":"Assess his capacity to refuse urgent physical treatment, choose the legal framework, and agree a plan with medics."},"wc-15":{"obj":["Moved him to a quiet, low-stimulus room with fewer staff","Used his mother's calming strategies before considering any medication","Asked paediatrics to look for pain or physical illness","Confirmed parental responsibility and the right legal framework for a 13-year-old","Agreed CAMHS follow-up and a safeguarding plan for him and anyone else at home"],"snip":[{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't take him home. I'm scared of him.\"","r":"Thank you for being honest; that takes courage. Your safety matters too. Let's get him calm first, then plan tonight together, including whether he stays on the children's ward."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Are you going to sedate him? I don't want him drugged.\"","r":"I hear you, and medication isn't my first step. Quiet, space and your ways of calming him usually work better. If we did need medicine, it would be the lowest dose and with your agreement."},{"tag":"Anger","p":"\"Get away from me! I hate it here!\"","r":"OK. I'm stepping back. You're safe. It's very noisy here. Do you want to go to a quiet room with Mum?"},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"He's had toothache all week and won't let me look.\"","r":"That's a really important clue. Pain often shows up as anger in autistic children. I'll ask the children's doctor to check him once he's calmer."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"Maybe I've done something wrong. It's my fault.\"","r":"It isn't your fault. You've been coping with a lot, often on your own. Let's look at what support you and the family need, including help at home."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"He's dangerous. Just section him.\"","r":"I understand the pressure on your department. He's autistic and overwhelmed, and from what I've seen he doesn't meet detention criteria. Let's try a quiet room and fewer people first; CAMHS are on the phone."}],"bps":{"bio":["Paediatric review for pain (teeth, ears, constipation), infection, sleep loss and seizures","Medication only if de-escalation fails: oral first, lowest dose, agreed with paediatrics and his mother"],"psych":["CAMHS follow-up within 24–48 hours","Positive behaviour support plan with the learning disability or autism team, built on his mother's strategies"],"social":["MASH referral if risk to him or siblings; hospital safeguarding team told","Carer support and short breaks via children's social care; school SENCO and EHCP review"],"safety":["Dynamic support register check; C(E)TR if admission is likely","Written safety plan with crisis numbers for his mother","Datix the kick to the nurse and offer her a debrief"]},"facts":[{"h":"Under 16: not the MCA","x":"The MCA applies from 16. At 13, rely on parental consent within the zone of parental control, or his own Gillick competence. The MHA is a last resort."},{"h":"Aggression may be pain","x":"In autistic children, sudden aggression often signals pain, sensory overload or a broken routine. Check teeth, ears, bowels and sleep before reaching for sedation."},{"h":"Never a police cell","x":"Since 2017, police cells cannot be a place of safety for anyone under 18. If he needs admission for safety tonight, a paediatric ward is usually best."},{"h":"Get on the dynamic support register","x":"Autistic young people at risk of admission should be on the local register. A Care, Education and Treatment Review should happen before, or soon after, any admission."},{"h":"Fewer people, fewer words","x":"Dim lights, cut noise and reduce staff in the room. Use short, concrete phrases and follow his mother's lead. Repeated demands escalate distress."}],"type":"Emergency management","do":"Make A&E safe, assess the young person with his mother, and agree a lawful CAMHS and safeguarding plan."},"wc-16":{"obj":["Asked about sedation, weight, periods, sexual function and bowels in a structured way","Done a pregnancy test and checked constipation red flags","Sent HbA1c, lipids and prolactin, and recorded weight, waist and BP","Agreed a cross-taper she accepts instead of stopping abruptly","Told the GP in writing who monitors what and when"],"snip":[{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I'm stopping these tablets. Look what they've done to me.\"","r":"I completely understand; the weight and tiredness are real and they matter. Stopping suddenly risks you becoming ill again. Let's change to something kinder on weight and energy, and do it safely together."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Could I be pregnant? My periods have stopped.\"","r":"Important question, and we'll check today with a simple test. Some antipsychotics raise a hormone called prolactin that can stop periods. If you're sexually active, let's talk about contraception too."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"In the bedroom... I just don't feel like it any more.\"","r":"Thank you for telling me. Lots of people feel this on these medicines but don't mention it. It's a common side effect and often improves with a switch."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"I can't fit into my clothes. I hate looking in the mirror.\"","r":"That sounds really upsetting, and it isn't your fault: olanzapine drives appetite. We'll plan a switch, get you support with food and activity, and check your blood sugar."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"How long until the weight comes off?\"","r":"It varies. Many people lose some weight over months after switching, especially with diet and activity support. I can't promise it all goes, but we'll track it together."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"I haven't been for five days and my tummy hurts.\"","r":"Thank you, that needs sorting today. I'll check your tummy now. If you start vomiting or the pain becomes severe, go straight to A&E. Let's start a laxative today."}],"bps":{"bio":["Cross-taper to aripiprazole: start 5–10 mg each morning, aim for 10–15 mg daily (max 30 mg); reduce olanzapine 15, 10, 5 mg then stop over 2–4 weeks","Constipation: senna 7.5–15 mg at night plus docusate 100 mg twice daily; review in one week","Monitoring: weight weekly for 6 weeks, then at 12 weeks; HbA1c and lipids at 12 weeks; prolactin if periods do not return","Pregnancy test before the switch; ECG if cardiac risk"],"psych":["Shared decision on the switch, with written information she takes home","Healthy-living group through the CMHT"],"social":["Weight-management referral via the GP","Employment or education support once her energy improves"],"safety":["Care coordinator weekly contact during the switch for early relapse signs","Constipation red flags (vomiting, distension, severe pain) mean same-day review"]},"facts":[{"h":"Never stop olanzapine abruptly","x":"Sudden stopping risks rebound insomnia, nausea and early psychotic relapse. Cross-taper over 2–4 weeks with weekly contact."},{"h":"Aripiprazole: kind to weight, watch akathisia","x":"Lowest risk of weight gain and prolactin rise. Warn about restlessness in the first weeks, nausea and insomnia; take it in the morning."},{"h":"Monitoring after a switch","x":"Weight weekly for 6 weeks, then at 12 weeks; HbA1c and lipids at 12 weeks, at 1 year, then yearly. Check BP and pulse at the same points."},{"h":"Ask about sex at every review","x":"Few people volunteer sexual side effects. Ask plainly about desire, arousal and orgasm; unspoken sexual problems are a major reason people stop antipsychotics."},{"h":"No periods: pregnancy first","x":"Rule out pregnancy before blaming the drug. Then check prolactin; persistently high prolactin with amenorrhoea risks bone loss."}],"type":"Medication review","do":"Review sedation, sexual dysfunction, weight gain and constipation systematically, check physical health, and agree an acceptable plan."},"wc-17":{"obj":["Assessed and recorded her capacity to consent to ECT","Checked for a valid advance decision or LPA covering ECT","Requested a SOAD and explained the s58A route to her daughter","Completed anaesthetic pre-assessment, bloods, ECG and a baseline cognitive score","Planned a capacity check before each treatment and regular cognitive tracking"],"snip":[{"tag":"Denial","p":"\"There's no point. I'm already dead.\"","r":"I hear how real that feels, and how frightening. This illness is making you feel that way, and treatment can lift it. Can I tell you what ECT involves?"},{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will it wipe her memory?\"","r":"That's a common worry. Some people lose memories from around the treatment period, and a few have longer gaps. We test her memory before and during the course and adjust if needed."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can she say no if she's sectioned?\"","r":"Yes. If she has capacity and refuses, ECT can't be given except in an emergency. If she lacks capacity, an independent doctor must agree, and a valid advance refusal must be respected."},{"tag":"Challenge","p":"\"Isn't ECT barbaric? It's from the 1950s.\"","r":"I understand that picture; it's what many people imagine. Today it's done under general anaesthetic with a muscle relaxant, so the body barely moves. It's one of the most effective treatments for severe depression."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Will it hurt?\"","r":"You'll be asleep, so you won't feel it. Afterwards some people have a headache or aching muscles for a few hours, and we can give painkillers."},{"tag":"Disclosure","p":"\"Mum once said she'd never have ECT after her sister had it.\"","r":"That's really important, thank you. Do you know if she wrote it down or signed anything? We need to check for an advance decision, and the SOAD will also want to hear her past wishes."}],"bps":{"bio":["Pre-ECT: FBC, U&E, ECG, anaesthetic airway and dental check; fasting as the anaesthetist directs","Review drugs: benzodiazepines and anticonvulsants raise seizure threshold; lithium can worsen post-ECT confusion","Usually twice weekly; a course is often 6–12 treatments, reviewed after each","Food and fluid charts, daily weight; medical team for IV fluids if intake becomes critical"],"psych":["Explain each session again; record her views every time","Daughter invited to ward rounds and ECT reviews"],"social":["Keep her daughter updated after each treatment","Carers' support information and a carer's assessment offer"],"safety":["SOAD requested via CQC; s62 only if life is at risk or serious deterioration imminent","Capacity reassessed before every treatment; MoCA at baseline and through the course","Escort nurse and recovery observations after each treatment"]},"facts":[{"h":"Detention alone never allows ECT","x":"Under s58A a capacitous refusal stands. If she lacks capacity, a SOAD must certify, and ECT cannot conflict with a valid advance decision, LPA or deputy decision."},{"h":"s62 for ECT is narrow","x":"Urgent ECT without a certificate is allowed only to save life or prevent serious deterioration. Dangerous refusal of food and fluid can qualify; document the risk."},{"h":"Seizure-threshold drugs matter","x":"Benzodiazepines and anticonvulsants make seizures harder to induce; lithium can worsen confusion afterwards. Agree changes with the ECT team before the first session."},{"h":"Measure memory before session one","x":"A baseline MoCA plus questions about recent personal events let you spot cognitive side effects early and adjust frequency or electrode placement."},{"h":"Psychotic depression responds well","x":"NICE supports ECT for severe depression that is life-threatening or needs a rapid response. Psychotic features and older age predict a good response."}],"type":"Capacity assessment + Mental Health Act process","do":"Assess her capacity to consent to ECT, follow the section 58A pathway, and prepare her safely."},"wc-18":{"obj":["Booked a professional interpreter matched for language and dialect and checked understanding","Assessed suicide intent and confirmed paracetamol treatment was complete","Screened for PTSD symptoms without asking for trauma details","Confirmed his free NHS entitlement and referred to the trauma service","Linked him with asylum support and crisis follow-up within 72 hours"],"snip":[{"tag":"Fear","p":"\"Will you tell the Home Office about this?\"","r":"That's a very understandable worry. Your health information is confidential and used for your care. It isn't passed to the Home Office for your claim unless you ask us to, such as for a letter."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Do I have to pay for this treatment?\"","r":"No. People who have applied for asylum get NHS care free, including mental health care. You won't receive a bill for this."},{"tag":"Refusal","p":"\"I don't want to talk about what happened back home.\"","r":"That's completely fine. You don't need to tell me any details today. I'd like to understand how you're sleeping and coping now, so we can help with that."},{"tag":"Distress","p":"\"If they send me back, I'll die anyway.\"","r":"That sounds terrifying, and it makes sense you feel desperate. Can you tell me about any thoughts of dying since the overdose? I want to keep you safe while your claim continues."},{"tag":"Guilt","p":"\"My family are still there. I shouldn't have left them.\"","r":"It sounds like you carry a lot of pain for them. Many people who flee feel this way. Those feelings are something we can work on together in treatment."},{"tag":"Question","p":"\"Can you write a letter for my interview?\"","r":"I can write a factual letter about your health and treatment if you want. For a detailed medical report, your solicitor can ask a specialist service. Do you have a solicitor?"}],"bps":{"bio":["Confirm paracetamol level and acetylcysteine course are complete with the medics before psychiatric discharge","If he prefers medication for PTSD: sertraline 25 mg each morning for a week, then 50 mg (max 200 mg); 7-day supplies","GP registration and new-patient health check"],"psych":["Trauma-focused CBT or EMDR via the trauma or refugee service, with an interpreter (typically 8–12 sessions)","Stabilisation and sleep work first if trauma is complex"],"social":["Support worker: Migrant Help for asylum support and housing; solicitor for the interview; Refugee Council","Freedom from Torture referral if torture is disclosed"],"safety":["Crisis team contact within 72 hours; safety plan translated into his language","National Referral Mechanism via a first responder if trafficking is suspected","GP letter within 24 hours"]},"facts":[{"h":"Asylum seekers get free NHS care","x":"Anyone with an asylum claim, including refused claimants on Home Office support, is exempt from hospital charges. GP and A&E care are free to everyone."},{"h":"Interpreter: professional and matched","x":"Book by language and dialect, ask about gender preference and brief the interpreter first. Never use relatives, friends or support workers."},{"h":"Trauma-focused therapy first","x":"NICE: trauma-focused CBT or EMDR for adults with PTSD, usually 8–12 sessions. Sertraline or venlafaxine if he prefers drugs or therapy is not possible."},{"h":"Don't dig on day one","x":"Ask about current symptoms, safety and sleep. Detailed trauma recall belongs in therapy once he feels safe; pushing now can worsen symptoms."},{"h":"Medical evidence for his claim","x":"A factual clinic letter can help. Detailed medico-legal reports on torture come from specialist services, requested through his solicitor."}],"type":"History to a diagnosis","do":"Assess his mental state and risk with a professional interpreter, trauma-informed, and link him to UK services."}};
